window.GIQ={"meta":{"lede":"","feat":1,"fold":1,"tier":1,"top":328,"clk":{"n":328,"n26":261,"nk":21,"nsrc":24}},"lectures":[{"id":"ANAT-G1","t":"GI development (embryology)"},{"id":"ANAT-G2","t":"Anterior abdominal wall, inguinal canal, spermatic cord, scrotum & testes"},{"id":"ANAT-G3","t":"Peritoneum, esophagus, stomach & celiac trunk"},{"id":"ANAT-G4","t":"Small & large intestines, SMA & IMA"},{"id":"ANAT-G5","t":"Liver, biliary tree, gallbladder, portal vein, pancreas & spleen"},{"id":"ANAT-G6","t":"Autonomic nervous system"},{"id":"ANAT-G7","t":"Radiology of thorax & abdomen"},{"id":"ANAT-Kahoot","t":"GI development Kahoot (2026)"}],"groups":[{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":0,"label":"Intraperitoneal = still hangs on a mesentery","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G001-8.jpg","cap":"Cross-section of gut hanging on dorsal mesentery; retroperitoneal definitions","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 8","cu":"../sl/G001-8c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G004-13.jpg","cap":"Fist in balloon: intraperitoneal organ wrapped by visceral peritoneum","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 13","cu":"../sl/G004-13c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/D042-51.jpg","cap":"Table: intraperitoneal, primary, secondary retroperitoneal and subperitoneal organs","where":"2025 TA review, slide 51"},{"src":"../tb/G4_7.jpg","cap":"Panel A: gut hanging on a mesentery and wrapped all around by visceral peritoneum is intraperitoneal.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 4.7","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"G079-6-1","s":"A 58-year-old man arrives at the hospital with lower back pain and trouble urinating. He is diagnosed with the rare condition of retroperitoneal fibrosis, which is compressing his ureter. Immunosuppressants are prescribed. “Retroperitoneal” means","o":["A. the organs are surrounded with two layers of serous membrane","B. the organs are surrounded with one layer of serous membrane","C. the organs were wrapped by serous membrane, but then were pushed behind the membrane during development","D. the organs developed outside the serous membrane"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"the organs developed outside the serous membrane","a":"D","why":"<b>D. The organs developed outside the serous membrane.</b> The peritoneum is a serous membrane; a (primary) retroperitoneal organ such as the kidney forms behind it from the start, never has a mesentery, and ends with peritoneum only on its front.<br>A. Two layers of serous membrane: that is a mesentery, the double fold an intraperitoneal organ hangs from.<br>B. One surrounding layer: visceral peritoneum wrapping an intraperitoneal organ.<br>C. Wrapped, then pushed behind: secondary retroperitoneal (duodenum parts 2–4, pancreas except the tail, ascending and descending colon).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 8 (Retroperitoneal Organs)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Montana interactive","n":"Montana (Great Falls) GI embryology interactive session 2026 (B. Chinnery)","sl":6,"f":"G079"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"1"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":0,"label":"Secondary retroperitoneal: duodenum, pancreas, ascending/descending colon","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G001-8.jpg","cap":"Secondary retroperitoneal: duodenum parts 2-4, pancreas except tail, ascending/descending colon","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 8","cu":"../sl/G001-8c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G001-26.jpg","cap":"Ascending and descending colon mesenteries fuse to the posterior wall","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 26","cu":"../sl/G001-26c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/D042-51.jpg","cap":"Table sorting organs into intra-, primary and secondary retroperitoneal","where":"2025 TA review, slide 51"},{"src":"../tb/M5_25.jpg","cap":"Descending colon starts on a mesentery, which then fuses to the back wall: secondarily retroperitoneal.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.25","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/G4_7.jpg","cap":"Panels A to C: the mesentery swings over and fuses with the back wall, turning gut secondarily retroperitoneal.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 4.7","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-269-1","s":"Which of the following organs is secondary retroperitoneal?","o":["A. Kidney","B. Tail of the pancreas","C. Appendix","D. Ascending colon","E. Spleen"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Ascending colon","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Ascending colon.</b> It starts on a mesentery; once the midgut returns and rotates, that mesentery fuses to the back wall, so it is secondary retroperitoneal. Same group: duodenum parts two to four, pancreas except the tail, descending colon.<br>A. Kidney: primarily retroperitoneal.<br>B. Tail of pancreas: intraperitoneal, in the splenorenal ligament.<br>C. Appendix: keeps its mesentery (mesoappendix).<br>E. Spleen: intraperitoneal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 8 (Retroperitoneal Organs)","GI Development slide 26 (Mesenteries)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":269,"f":"F3903"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"2"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":1,"label":"Pylorus and first duodenal part come from foregut","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G001-9.jpg","cap":"Foregut list: stomach and upper (first) part of the duodenum","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 9"},{"src":"../sl/G001-16.jpg","cap":"Duodenum drawing: foregut-midgut junction at the bile duct opening","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 16","cu":"../sl/G001-16c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/D045-126.jpg","cap":"Foregut derivatives include stomach and proximal duodenum","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 126"},{"src":"../tb/M5_33.jpg","cap":"Panel C: celiac trunk (to foregut) supplies stomach and pylorus, and via the gastroduodenal artery the first duodenum.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.33","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"G075-4-1","s":"A 5-week-old male infant is brought to the emergency department because of progressively worsening vomiting after feedings over the past week. His parents report that vomiting is forceful, occurs shortly after feeding, and does not contain bile. The infant appears hungry immediately after vomiting. On physical examination, a small, firm, olive-shaped mass is palpated in the upper abdomen. An abdominal ultrasound demonstrates thickening of the pyloric muscle. The physician explains that this condition results from abnormal development of the distal foregut. Which of the following best describes the anatomical structure affected by this congenital condition?","o":["A. The distal stomach, which develops from the midgut and contributes to rotation of the primitive intestinal loop.","B. The duodenum, which develops from the junction of the foregut and midgut and regulates passage of stomach contents into the jejunum.","C. The esophagus, which develops from the cranial portion of the foregut and transports food from the pharynx to the stomach.","D. The proximal jejunum, which develops from the midgut and regulates passage of stomach contents into the small intestine.","E. The pylorus, which develops from the caudal portion of the foregut and regulates passage of stomach contents into the duodenum."],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"The pylorus, which develops from the caudal portion of the foregut and regulates passage of stomach contents into the duodenum","a":"E","why":"<b>E. The pylorus, from the caudal foregut.</b> Nonbilious projectile vomiting at a few weeks with an olive-shaped mass is hypertrophic pyloric stenosis. The pylorus is the stomach outlet, caudal foregut, and controls emptying into the duodenum.<br>A. Distal stomach from midgut: the whole stomach is foregut.<br>B. Duodenum: lies beyond the block; a block below the bile duct opening gives bilious vomiting.<br>C. Esophagus: cranial foregut; atresia gives drooling and choking at the first feed.<br>D. Proximal jejunum: midgut, and it does not control gastric emptying.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 14 (Pyloric Stenosis)","GI Development slide 9 (Foregut)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice","n":"GI #1 practice questions Fall 2026 (Lecture #1 embryology, Lecture #2 abdominal wall)","sl":null,"f":"G075"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"3"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":1,"label":"Foregut organs: esophagus, stomach, liver, gallbladder, pancreas","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G001-9.jpg","cap":"Foregut list: esophagus, stomach, liver, gallbladder, pancreas, first duodenum","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 9"},{"src":"../sl/D045-126.jpg","cap":"Foregut, midgut and hindgut derivatives beside a labelled embryo","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 126"},{"src":"../sl/G001-22.jpg","cap":"Midgut list: distal duodenum to two-thirds of transverse colon","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 22","cu":"../sl/G001-22c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/E10_9.jpg","cap":"Liver, gallbladder and both pancreatic buds all sprout from the foregut right beside the stomach.","where":"BRS Embryology 5e, Figure 10-9","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/M5_33.jpg","cap":"Panel C: celiac trunk (to foregut) supplies the foregut organs: liver, gallbladder, stomach and upper duodenum.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.33","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-261-1","s":"A new mother is worried about her son’s health. At 3-weeks-old he still has not gained much weight. A set of exams reveal that his stomach is underdeveloped. Which of the following organs would you expect to also be underdeveloped if the underlying problem was the improper development of the neonate’s foregut?","o":["A. Spleen","B. Jejunum","C. Descending colon","D. Gallbladder","E. Anus"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Gallbladder","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Gallbladder.</b> The stomach and gallbladder both come from the foregut; the gallbladder buds off it as part of the hepatic diverticulum.<br>A. Spleen: mesoderm in the dorsal mesogastrium, not a gut-tube organ.<br>B. Jejunum: midgut.<br>C. Descending colon: hindgut.<br>E. Anus: upper anal canal is hindgut; lower part is ectoderm.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 9 (Foregut)","GI Development slide 15 (Spleen)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":261,"f":"F3903"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"4"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":1,"label":"Duodenum: foregut-midgut and celiac-superior mesenteric junction","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G001-16.jpg","cap":"Duodenum: transition foregut to midgut and celiac trunk to superior mesenteric","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 16","cu":"../sl/G001-16c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G001-7.jpg","cap":"Gut arteries: celiac trunk, superior and inferior mesenteric to each region","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 7","cu":"../sl/G001-7c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/M5_33.jpg","cap":"Panel C: the duodenum gets celiac (foregut) branches above and superior mesenteric (midgut) branches below.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.33","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"G077-17-1","s":"Which gastrointestinal structure delineates the transition from foregut blood supply to midgut blood supply?","o":["A. Common Bile Duct","B. Pancreatic Duct","C. Major Duodenal Papilla","D. Ampulla of Vater","E. Ampulla of Darth Vader"],"g":["C","D"],"k":["C","D"],"rv":false,"at":"Major Duodenal Papilla | Ampulla of Vater","a":"C,D","why":"<b>C. Major duodenal papilla (D, ampulla of Vater, is the same spot).</b> The foregut–midgut and celiac trunk–superior mesenteric artery boundary lies at the bile duct opening in the second part of the duodenum. The papilla is the bump on the inner wall; the ampulla is the dilated duct end just behind it.<br>A. Common bile duct: runs down to that point but is not itself the boundary.<br>B. Pancreatic duct: joins the bile duct at the ampulla; the landmark is the opening.<br>E. Ampulla of Darth Vader: joke option.","ex":"","fl":["The major duodenal papilla (C) and the ampulla of Vater (D) mark the same spot, the bile duct opening, so both are accepted."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 16 (Duodenum and Recanalization)","GI Development slide 22 (Midgut)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown SMA, IMA, small & large intestines interactive 11/12/26 (R. Connington)","sl":34,"f":"G083"},{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown SMA, IMA, small & large intestines interactive 2026, re-posted version (R. Connington)","sl":34,"f":"G108"},{"t":"2026 Middletown TA weekly","n":"Embryology 7 GI system TA supplemental session 11/3/26","sl":17,"f":"G077"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"5"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":2,"label":"Blocked fetal gut: can't swallow, polyhydramnios","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G001-11.jpg","cap":"Fetus swallows amniotic fluid; esophageal or duodenal block gives polyhydramnios","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 11","cu":"../sl/G001-11c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/F3900-247.jpg","cap":"Polyhydramnios: impaired swallowing or more fetal urine; gut causes table","where":"2023 TA review, slide 247","cu":"../sl/F3900-247c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"G079-9-1","s":"A pregnant woman arrives at her OBGYN for a routine sonogram. Results indicate polyhydramnios. This means that","o":["A. there is a lack of fetal blood","B. there is too much fetal blood","C. there is a lack of amniotic fluid","D. there is too much amniotic fluid","E. her blood pressure is too low"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"there is too much amniotic fluid","a":"D","why":"<b>D. There is too much amniotic fluid.</b> Polyhydramnios means excess amniotic fluid. The fetus normally swallows the fluid and absorbs it in its gut, so an upper gut block (esophageal or duodenal atresia) or extra fetal urine (maternal diabetes) raises the volume.<br>A. Lack of fetal blood: fetal anemia, not a fluid-volume term.<br>B. Too much fetal blood: polycythemia, not a fluid-volume term.<br>C. Too little amniotic fluid: oligohydramnios (bilateral renal agenesis).<br>E. Low maternal blood pressure: unrelated to the word.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 11 (Polyhydramniose)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Montana interactive","n":"Montana (Great Falls) GI embryology interactive session 2026 (B. Chinnery)","sl":9,"f":"G079"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"6"},{"id":"G077-56-1","s":"A 26-year-old G1P0 woman at 32 weeks' gestation has a fundal height of 38 cm. Ultrasound shows an amniotic fluid index of 30 cm and a persistently small, nearly absent fetal stomach bubble. Fetal kidneys appear normal, and her glucose tolerance test was normal. Which of the following best explains the excess amniotic fluid?","o":["A. Increased fetal urine output from fetal hyperglycemia","B. Inability of swallowed amniotic fluid to reach the fetal intestine for absorption","C. Decreased fetal urine output from bilateral renal agenesis","D. Leakage of amniotic fluid through ruptured membranes","E. Overproduction of amniotic fluid by the placenta"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Inability of swallowed amniotic fluid to reach the fetal intestine for absorption","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Swallowed fluid cannot reach the intestine to be absorbed.</b> A tiny or absent stomach bubble means fluid is not getting past the esophagus (esophageal atresia), so it piles up: high fundal height and amniotic fluid index.<br>A. More fetal urine from hyperglycemia: ruled out by the normal glucose tolerance test.<br>C. Bilateral renal agenesis: no urine, so too little fluid (oligohydramnios); the kidneys are normal here.<br>D. Ruptured membranes: fluid leaks out and the volume falls.<br>E. Placental overproduction: not how amniotic fluid builds up.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 11 (Polyhydramniose)","GI Development slide 10 (Esophagus)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Montana interactive","n":"Montana (Great Falls) GI embryology interactive session 2026 (B. Chinnery)","sl":12,"f":"G079"},{"t":"2026 Middletown TA weekly","n":"Embryology 7 GI system TA supplemental session 11/3/26","sl":56,"f":"G077"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"7"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":3,"label":"Esophageal atresia with TEF: faulty tracheoesophageal septum","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G076-20.jpg","cap":"Lung bud in front of the esophagus; the septum divides them","where":"2026 GI supplemental session (Nov 6), slide 20","cu":"../sl/G076-20c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G001-10.jpg","cap":"Occlusion and recanalization of the foregut, weeks 5 to 9","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 10","cu":"../sl/G001-10c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"G075-3-1","s":"A 29-year-old pregnant woman presents for a prenatal ultrasound at 32 weeks gestation. The ultrasound demonstrates significant polyhydramnios and a persistently small fetal stomach despite repeated imaging. Following delivery, the newborn develops copious oral secretions, coughing, and episodes of cyanosis during the first feeding attempt. A nasogastric tube is inserted but cannot be advanced into the stomach. A chest radiograph reveals the tube coiled in the upper thoracic esophagus. The neonatology team suspects esophageal atresia with a distal tracheoesophageal fistula. This congenital abnormality is associated with abnormal development of which of the following embryologic structures or processes?","o":["A. Fusion of the lateral body folds during transverse folding to close the ventral abdominal wall.","B. Incorporation of the cranial yolk sac into the embryo during sagittal folding.","C. Formation and partitioning of the primitive foregut by the tracheoesophageal septum.","D. Return of the physiologically herniated midgut into the abdominal cavity during the tenth week.","E. Regression of the vitelline duct connecting the developing midgut to the yolk sac."],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Formation and partitioning of the primitive foregut by the tracheoesophageal septum","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Partitioning of the foregut by the tracheoesophageal septum.</b> The septum splits the front of the foregut into trachea in front and esophagus behind; a faulty (posteriorly deviated) septum leaves a blind upper pouch and a fistula. The coiled nasogastric tube marks the blind pouch.<br>A. Lateral body folds failing to fuse: gastroschisis.<br>B. Taking in the cranial yolk sac: forms the foregut itself, not the trachea–esophagus split.<br>D. Midgut failing to return: omphalocele.<br>E. Vitelline duct persisting: Meckel diverticulum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 10 (Esophagus)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice","n":"GI #1 practice questions Fall 2026 (Lecture #1 embryology, Lecture #2 abdominal wall)","sl":null,"f":"G075"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"8"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":4,"label":"Duodenal atresia/stenosis = failed recanalization","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G001-17.jpg","cap":"Duodenal plug: partial recanalization gives stenosis, none gives atresia","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 17","cu":"../sl/G001-17c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/D042-64.jpg","cap":"Gut tube occlusion and recanalization, weeks five to nine; stenosis, atresia","where":"2025 TA review, slide 64"}],"q":[{"id":"G075-5-1","s":"A newborn is evaluated in the neonatal intensive care unit on the first day of life after developing recurrent bilious vomiting following feedings. The infant has mild abdominal distension but remains clinically stable. An abdominal radiograph shows a \"double bubble\" sign, with air in the distal small intestine. An upper gastrointestinal study reveals a narrowed duodenal lumen with a small amount of contrast passing into the jejunum. The physician explains that this congenital abnormality results from incomplete restoration of the duodenal lumen during embryologic development. Which of the following best explains the underlying developmental mechanism?","o":["A. Abnormal fusion of the lateral body folds, preventing closure of the anterior abdominal wall.","B. Complete failure of duodenal recanalization following temporary occlusion by proliferating endodermal cells.","C. Failure of the midgut to return to the abdominal cavity following physiological herniation.","D. Partial recanalization of the duodenal lumen following temporary occlusion by proliferating endodermal cells.","E. Failure of the vitelline duct to regress, resulting in persistent communication between the intestine and yolk sac."],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Partial recanalization of the duodenal lumen following temporary occlusion by proliferating endodermal cells","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Partial recanalization of the duodenal lumen.</b> A double bubble with some air beyond it and contrast trickling through a narrowed duodenum is duodenal stenosis: the epithelial plug reopened only partly.<br>A. Lateral body folds failing to fuse: gastroschisis.<br>B. Complete failure of recanalization: duodenal atresia, with no gas or contrast beyond the block.<br>C. Midgut failing to return: omphalocele.<br>E. Vitelline duct persisting: Meckel diverticulum or umbilical fistula.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 17 (Duodenal Stenosis and Atresia)","GI Development slide 16 (Duodenum and Recanalization)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice","n":"GI #1 practice questions Fall 2026 (Lecture #1 embryology, Lecture #2 abdominal wall)","sl":null,"f":"G075"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"9"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":4,"label":"Projectile non-bilious vomiting + olive mass: pyloric stenosis","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G001-14.jpg","cap":"Pyloric stenosis: thickened pylorus and projectile vomiting","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 14","cu":"../sl/G001-14c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/E10_8.jpg","cap":"Barium study: curved arrows mark the long, thin pyloric channel squeezed by thickened pyloric muscle.","where":"BRS Embryology 5e, Figure 10-8","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-283-1","s":"A 14-day-old male is brought to the physician because he has been projectile vomiting. The mother explains that he has had some small regurgitation issues but nothing to this effect. Examination shows a hard bump at the right costal margin of the infant. What anomaly would you suspect?","o":["A. Esophageal atresia","B. Pyloric stenosis","C. Duodenal atresia","D. Tracheoesophageal fistula","E. Esophageal stenosis"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Pyloric stenosis","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Pyloric stenosis.</b> Vomiting that turns projectile at about two weeks old, with a firm lump at the right costal margin, is a thickened pylorus.<br>A. Esophageal atresia: drooling and choking from the first feed.<br>C. Duodenal atresia: bilious vomiting on day one.<br>D. Tracheoesophageal fistula: coughing and cyanosis with feeds.<br>E. Esophageal stenosis: trouble swallowing, no abdominal mass.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 14 (Pyloric Stenosis)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":283,"f":"F3903"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"10"},{"id":"D041-88-1","s":"A 14-day-old male is brought to the physician because he has been progressively vomiting and recently the vomiting has been projectile. The infant has failed to gain weight and the mother reports no green color in the vomitus. Examination shows a hard bump at the right costal margin of the infant. What anomaly would you suspect?","o":["A. Esophageal atresia","B. Pyloric stenosis","C. Duodenal atresia","D. Tracheoesophageal fistula","E. Esophageal stenosis"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Pyloric stenosis","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Pyloric stenosis.</b> Vomit without bile puts the block above the bile duct opening. Projectile vomiting, poor weight gain and a hard lump at the right costal margin (the thick pylorus) clinch it.<br>A. Esophageal atresia: drooling and choking from the first feed.<br>C. Duodenal atresia: usually bilious vomiting on day one.<br>D. Tracheoesophageal fistula: coughing and cyanosis with feeds.<br>E. Esophageal stenosis: trouble swallowing, no mass.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 14 (Pyloric Stenosis)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":77,"f":"F0008"},{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":88,"f":"D041"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"11"},{"id":"G002-5-1","s":"A 3-week-old male is brought to the physician due to progressive, non-bilious projectile vomiting after feeding over the past 4 days. The infant appears dehydrated and has failed to gain weight since birth. Physical examination reveals an olive-shaped, firm mass palpable at the right costal margin. Which of the following embryological anomalies is the most likely cause of this patient's condition?","o":["A. Esophageal atresia","B. Hypertrophic pyloric stenosis","C. Duodenal atresia","D. Tracheoesophageal fistula","E. Annular pancreas"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Hypertrophic pyloric stenosis","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Hypertrophic pyloric stenosis.</b> At about three weeks, non-bilious vomiting that becomes projectile, poor weight gain and a firm olive-shaped mass mean a thickened pyloric muscle blocking the stomach outlet.<br>A. Esophageal atresia: drooling and choking from birth.<br>C. Duodenal atresia: bilious vomiting on day one.<br>D. Tracheoesophageal fistula: coughing and cyanosis with feeds.<br>E. Annular pancreas: pancreatic ring narrows the duodenum from birth; no olive mass.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 14 (Pyloric Stenosis)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown clicker","n":"","sl":5,"f":"G002"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"12"},{"id":"G079-15-1","s":"A two-month-old baby was taken to the doctor after having increasing vomiting episodes. The parents were worried because the episodes have increased in frequency over the last few weeks and now the baby is projectile vomiting. A diagnosis of hypertrophy of the pyloric sphincter muscles is confirmed by ultrasound, and surgery is scheduled. The term for this condition (also if a failure of recanalization occurs) is","o":["A. Occlusion of the stomach","B. Polyhydramnios","C. Pyloric stenosis","D. Idiopathic diverticulum","E. Annular pancreas"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Pyloric stenosis","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Pyloric stenosis.</b> Vomiting that grows projectile at a few weeks, with a thick pyloric sphincter on ultrasound, is hypertrophic pyloric stenosis; the pylorus is caudal foregut and the vomit has no bile.<br>A. Occlusion of the stomach: not a named defect; the plug-and-reopen errors are esophageal and duodenal atresia or stenosis.<br>B. Polyhydramnios: too much amniotic fluid during pregnancy, not an infant disease.<br>D. Idiopathic diverticulum: not a pyloric problem; the gut diverticulum of children is Meckel's, on the ileum.<br>E. Annular pancreas: a pancreatic ring around the second part of the duodenum, with symptoms from birth.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 14 (Pyloric Stenosis)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Montana interactive","n":"Montana (Great Falls) GI embryology interactive session 2026 (B. Chinnery)","sl":15,"f":"G079"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"13"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":4,"label":"Bilious vomiting + polyhydramnios: duodenal atresia/stenosis","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G001-17.jpg","cap":"Duodenal stenosis and atresia with a dilated proximal duodenum","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 17","cu":"../sl/G001-17c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G001-11.jpg","cap":"Failure of the duodenum to recanalize also causes polyhydramnios","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 11","cu":"../sl/G001-11c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/F3900-247.jpg","cap":"Polyhydramnios plus bile in vomit: block below the bile duct opening","where":"2023 TA review, slide 247","cu":"../sl/F3900-247c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-249-1","s":"A 28-year-old female is diagnosed with polyhydramnios during her pregnancy. After birth, her son is placed in the NICU due to repeating episodes of vomiting. Examination of the contents indicates presence of bile on all occasions. The neonate has also failed to gain any significant weight. What congenital anomaly explains these symptoms?","o":["A. Pyloric stenosis","B. Esophageal stenosis","C. Duodenal atresia","D. Meckel’s diverticulum","E. Biliary duct atresia"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Duodenal atresia","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Duodenal atresia.</b> Polyhydramnios plus vomit that always contains bile means a complete block in the duodenum past the bile duct opening (failed recanalization).<br>A. Pyloric stenosis: block above the bile duct, so no bile; starts at two to six weeks.<br>B. Esophageal stenosis: no bile; trouble swallowing.<br>D. Meckel diverticulum: painless rectal bleeding, not vomiting at birth.<br>E. Biliary atresia: jaundice and pale stool, because bile can't reach the gut.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 17 (Duodenal Stenosis and Atresia)","GI Development slide 11 (Polyhydramniose)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":249,"f":"F3903"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"14"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":5,"label":"Hepatic diverticulum: liver, bile ducts, gallbladder","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G001-18.jpg","cap":"Hepatic diverticulum grows into septum transversum: bile duct, gallbladder, cystic duct","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 18","cu":"../sl/G001-18c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/D042-56.jpg","cap":"Hepatic diverticulum from foregut into septum transversum; gallbladder beside pancreatic buds","where":"2025 TA review, slide 56","cu":"../sl/D042-56c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/E10_9.jpg","cap":"The hepatic diverticulum buds from the foregut and forms the liver; its stalk gives the gallbladder and bile ducts.","where":"BRS Embryology 5e, Figure 10-9","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"G075-6-1","s":"A newborn is evaluated for persistent jaundice and an enlarged liver during the first week of life. Further investigation reveals a congenital abnormality involving the development of the hepatic biliary system. The physician explains to the parents that the liver begins developing early in embryogenesis as an outgrowth of the primitive gut tube. Which of the following best describes the embryologic origin of the liver?","o":["A. The liver develops from the hindgut endoderm, which migrates cranially and forms the hepatic parenchyma.","B. The liver develops from the midgut endoderm, which grows into the dorsal mesentery and differentiates into hepatocytes.","C. The liver develops from the ventral mesoderm, which forms the hepatic diverticulum and differentiates into bile duct epithelium.","D. The liver develops from the hepatic diverticulum, an endodermal outgrowth of the distal foregut that grows into the septum transversum.","E. The liver develops from the yolk sac mesoderm, which differentiates into hepatocytes and hepatic sinusoids."],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"The liver develops from the hepatic diverticulum, an endodermal outgrowth of the distal foregut that grows into the septum transversum","a":"D","why":"<b>D. The hepatic diverticulum, an endodermal outgrowth of the distal foregut into the septum transversum.</b> The bud forms hepatocytes, bile ducts and gallbladder; septum transversum mesoderm adds fibrous tissue and Kupffer cells.<br>A. Hindgut endoderm: forms colon, rectum and upper anal canal.<br>B. Midgut endoderm into the dorsal mesentery: the liver is foregut and grows into the ventral mesentery.<br>C. Ventral mesoderm: mesoderm forms the framework, not the bud or bile duct epithelium.<br>E. Yolk sac mesoderm: does not form hepatocytes.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 18 (Liver and Gallbladder)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice","n":"GI #1 practice questions Fall 2026 (Lecture #1 embryology, Lecture #2 abdominal wall)","sl":null,"f":"G075"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"15"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":5,"label":"Kupffer cells: septum transversum mesoderm","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G001-18.jpg","cap":"Septum transversum mesoderm forms liver fibrous tissue and Kupffer cells","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 18","cu":"../sl/G001-18c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G002-18.jpg","cap":"Liver bud in septum transversum; Kupffer cells lining liver sinusoids","where":"2026 Middletown clicker, slide 18"}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-293-1","s":"A patient is having his blood work done. The test reveals he is not having proper breakdown of his red blood cells. Further investigation shows he has issues with his Kupffer cells. Embryologically, these cells derive from which of the following germ layers?","o":["A. Endoderm","B. Mesoderm","C. Ectoderm","D. Neuroectoderm"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Mesoderm","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Mesoderm.</b> Kupffer cells, the liver macrophages that break down old red blood cells, come from mesoderm (septum transversum mesoderm forms the liver's fibrous tissue and Kupffer cells).<br>A. Endoderm: forms liver cells and bile ducts from the hepatic diverticulum.<br>C. Ectoderm: skin and nervous system.<br>D. Neuroectoderm: brain and spinal cord.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 18 (Liver and Gallbladder)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":293,"f":"F3903"},{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":87,"f":"F0008"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"16"},{"id":"G002-17-1","s":"A researcher studying the development of the hepatic sinusoidal architecture tracks the lineage of specialized tissue macrophages responsible for clearing cellular debris and spent erythrocytes from portal circulation. These cells, known as Kupffer cells, originate from which of the following embryological germ layers?","o":["A. Endoderm","B. Mesoderm","C. Surface ectoderm","D. Neuroectoderm","E. Neural crest"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Mesoderm","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Mesoderm.</b> Liver cells and bile ducts come from endoderm (the hepatic bud), but Kupffer cells, the macrophages lining the sinusoids, come from mesoderm.<br>A. Endoderm: liver cells and bile ducts only.<br>C. Surface ectoderm: skin and lens.<br>D. Neuroectoderm: brain, spinal cord, retina.<br>E. Neural crest: ganglia, melanocytes and others; not liver macrophages.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 18 (Liver and Gallbladder)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown clicker","n":"","sl":17,"f":"G002"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"17"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":6,"label":"Bilobed ventral bud: annular pancreas","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G001-21.jpg","cap":"Bilobed ventral bud wraps around the duodenum: annular pancreas","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 21","cu":"../sl/G001-21c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/D045-145.jpg","cap":"Bilobed ventral bud encircling the duodenum to form annular pancreas","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 145","cu":"../sl/D045-145c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/E10_9.jpg","cap":"Normally one ventral pancreatic bud swings around the duodenum; a bilobed bud goes both ways and makes a ring.","where":"BRS Embryology 5e, Figure 10-9","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/E10_15.jpg","cap":"Barium study: the arrow marks the duodenum narrowed where a ring of pancreas wraps around it.","where":"BRS Embryology 5e, Figure 10-15","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"G075-7-1","s":"A 3-day-old male newborn is brought to the emergency department after developing recurrent bilious vomiting and abdominal distension shortly after his first feedings. His mother reports that prenatal ultrasounds showed polyhydramnios. An abdominal radiograph demonstrates a double bubble sign. An upper gastrointestinal contrast study reveals narrowing of the second portion of the duodenum. During surgical evaluation, pancreatic tissue partially encircles the duodenum and compresses its lumen. The surgical team explains that this congenital abnormality developed because of an error in the normal rotation of the pancreatic buds during embryogenesis. Which of the following best explains the developmental abnormality responsible for this newborn's condition?","o":["A. Failure of the dorsal pancreatic bud to develop, resulting in incomplete formation of the pancreatic body and tail.","B. Abnormal migration of the ventral pancreatic bud around the duodenum, resulting in pancreatic tissue encircling and compressing the duodenal lumen.","C. Failure of the duodenal lumen to recanalize following temporary occlusion by proliferating endodermal cells.","D. Failure of the ventral and dorsal pancreatic buds to fuse, resulting in separate pancreatic duct systems.","E. Abnormal development of the hepatic diverticulum, resulting in compression of the duodenum by an enlarged liver."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Abnormal migration of the ventral pancreatic bud around the duodenum, resulting in pancreatic tissue encircling and compressing the duodenal lumen","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Abnormal migration of the ventral bud around the duodenum.</b> A bilobed ventral bud sends one lobe behind and one in front of the duodenum; they meet as a ring that narrows the second part, giving bilious vomiting, polyhydramnios and a double bubble.<br>A. No dorsal bud: missing neck, body and tail; no ring.<br>C. Failed recanalization: duodenal atresia or stenosis; no pancreatic tissue around the duodenum.<br>D. Buds failing to fuse: pancreas divisum, two duct systems.<br>E. Hepatic diverticulum defect: liver and biliary problems, not a pancreatic ring.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 21 (Clinical Correlate)","GI Development slide 20 (Pancreas)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice","n":"GI #1 practice questions Fall 2026 (Lecture #1 embryology, Lecture #2 abdominal wall)","sl":null,"f":"G075"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"18"},{"id":"G079-22-1","s":"An annular pancreas occurs when a bifid ventral pancreas forms and the two parts surround the digestive tract when they rotate to the left. This can initially disrupt movement of food in the","o":["A. stomach","B. esophagus","C. duodenum","D. ileum","E. jejunum"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"duodenum","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Duodenum.</b> A bilobed ventral bud sends one lobe behind and one in front of the second part of the duodenum; they meet as a ring that narrows it, so food stops there (double bubble, polyhydramnios).<br>A. Stomach: lies above the ring; it only distends behind the block.<br>B. Esophagus: blocked by esophageal atresia, not by pancreas.<br>D. Ileum: distal midgut, far from the pancreas.<br>E. Jejunum: begins past the duodenojejunal flexure, beyond the ring.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 21 (Clinical Correlate)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Montana interactive","n":"Montana (Great Falls) GI embryology interactive session 2026 (B. Chinnery)","sl":22,"f":"G079"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"19"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":7,"label":"Midgut loop rotates around the superior mesenteric artery","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G001-24.jpg","cap":"Midgut loop wrapped around the superior mesenteric artery, its rotation axis","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 24","cu":"../sl/G001-24c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G001-23.jpg","cap":"Herniation at week six; superior mesenteric artery is the rotation axis","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 23"},{"src":"../tb/E10_16.jpg","cap":"The loop rotates around the superior mesenteric artery, its axis; extra twisting can choke this artery.","where":"BRS Embryology 5e, Figure 10-16","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-265-1","s":"During the herniation of the midgut of a 6-week-old embryo, faulty cell signaling causes the upper arm to rotate 180° counterclockwise instead of the usual 90°. Blood flow of which of the following would be impeded by the extra rotation of the midgut?","o":["A. Celiac trunk","B. Renal arteries","C. Superior mesenteric artery","D. Inferior mesenteric artery","E. Common iliac arteries"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Superior mesenteric artery","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Superior mesenteric artery (SMA).</b> The midgut loop turns around the SMA as its axis. Too much turning <b>twists the mesentery around the SMA</b> and kinks it, like a volvulus.<br>A. Celiac trunk: supplies foregut, which is not in the herniated loop.<br>B. Renal arteries: go to the kidneys, behind the peritoneum.<br>D. Inferior mesenteric artery: supplies hindgut, which stays in the abdomen.<br>E. Common iliac arteries: go to the pelvis and legs.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 23 (Herniation and Rotation)","GI Development slide 7 (Blood supply)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":265,"f":"F3903"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"20"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":8,"label":"Nonrotation: small bowel right, colon left","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G001-24.jpg","cap":"Normal two-hundred-seventy-degree rotation ends with cecum low on the right","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 24","cu":"../sl/G001-24c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G002-21.jpg","cap":"Nonrotation (small bowel right, colon left) versus reversed clockwise rotation","where":"2026 Middletown clicker, slide 21"},{"src":"../tb/E10_20.jpg","cap":"Nonrotation at surgery: small intestine (SI) on the patient's right, large intestine (LI) on the left.","where":"BRS Embryology 5e, Figure 10-20","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/E10_16.jpg","cap":"Normal 270-degree rotation ends with the cecum low on the right; in nonrotation the loop never turns this far.","where":"BRS Embryology 5e, Figure 10-16","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"G002-19-1","s":"A 15-year-old male undergoes surgery for an unrelated acute abdominal condition. Intraoperatively, the surgeon observes that the entire small intestine is located on the right side of the abdominal cavity, the entire large intestine is located on the left side, and the cecum is positioned in the lower midline suprapubic region. Which of the following developmental aberrations best accounts for these findings?","o":["A. Failure of the midgut loop to rotate after returning to the abdomen (Nonrotation)","B. Clockwise rotation of the midgut loop around the superior mesenteric artery (Reversed rotation)","C. Hypermobility of the hepatic flexure","D. Failure of the lateral body folds to fuse","E. Incomplete recanalization of the duodenal lumen"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Failure of the midgut loop to rotate after returning to the abdomen (Nonrotation)","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Nonrotation.</b> The loop made only its first ninety-degree turn and skipped the last one hundred eighty on return. So the <b>small bowel stays on the right, the colon on the left</b>, and the cecum ends low in the midline.<br>B. Reversed (clockwise) rotation: the transverse colon ends up behind the superior mesenteric artery.<br>C. Hepatic flexure mobility: cannot move all the bowel.<br>D. Failed lateral body folds: gastroschisis.<br>E. Failed duodenal recanalization: duodenal stenosis or atresia.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 24 (Herniation and Rotation)","GI Development slide 27 (Clinical Correlates)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown clicker","n":"","sl":19,"f":"G002"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"21"},{"id":"F0008-89-1","s":"A 15-year-old male was treated surgically for a strangulated indirect inguinal hernia. The surgeon noticed that the small intestine was located in the right half of the abdominal cavity and the large intestine on the left side. The cecum and appendix were suprapubic in position. Which of the following would cause this anomaly?","o":["A. nonrotation of the midgut","B. reversed rotation of the midgut","C. midgut volvulus","D. mixed rotation of the midgut","E. duplication of the intestine"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"nonrotation of the midgut","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Nonrotation of the midgut.</b> Normally the loop turns two hundred seventy degrees counterclockwise, ending with the cecum low on the right. If it stops after the first ninety, the <b>small bowel lies on the right and the colon on the left</b>.<br>B. Reversed rotation: clockwise turn; transverse colon passes behind the superior mesenteric artery.<br>C. Midgut volvulus: a twist that cuts off blood, not a layout.<br>D. Mixed rotation: cecum sits just below the pylorus, tied down by bands.<br>E. Duplication: an extra cyst or tube of bowel.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 24 (Herniation and Rotation)","GI Development slide 23 (Herniation and Rotation)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":89,"f":"F0008"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"22"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":8,"label":"Reversed rotation: clockwise turn, transverse colon behind SMA","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G002-21.jpg","cap":"Absent secondary rotation (A); reversed 90° clockwise rotation (B)","where":"2026 Middletown clicker, slide 21"},{"src":"../sl/G001-27.jpg","cap":"Reverse rotation puts the transverse colon behind the superior mesenteric artery","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 27","cu":"../sl/G001-27c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"G075-8-1","s":"A newborn presents with bilious vomiting and abdominal distension. Imaging reveals an abnormal intestinal arrangement, and surgical exploration demonstrates that the transverse colon is positioned posterior to the superior mesenteric artery (SMA), causing intestinal obstruction. The surgeon explains that this congenital abnormality resulted from an error in midgut rotation. Which of the following best describes the embryologic defect?","o":["A. Clockwise rotation of the midgut around the SMA.","B. Failure of the midgut to complete its 270° rotation.","C. Failure of the midgut to return to the abdominal cavity.","D. Excessive counterclockwise rotation of the midgut.","E. Failure of the midgut to elongate during development."],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Clockwise rotation of the midgut around the SMA","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Clockwise rotation of the midgut around the superior mesenteric artery.</b> In reversed rotation the loop first turns 90° clockwise instead of counterclockwise, so the transverse colon ends up behind the superior mesenteric artery.<br>B. Incomplete 270° rotation: malrotation, with a high cecum and bands over the duodenum; the colon stays in front.<br>C. Failed return: omphalocele.<br>D. Excess counterclockwise rotation: twists the mesentery (volvulus) rather than putting the colon behind the artery.<br>E. Failed elongation: not a described rotation error.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 27 (Clinical Correlates)","GI Development slide 24 (Herniation and Rotation)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice","n":"GI #1 practice questions Fall 2026 (Lecture #1 embryology, Lecture #2 abdominal wall)","sl":null,"f":"G075"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"23"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":9,"label":"Omphalocele: midgut fails to return to abdomen","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G001-27.jpg","cap":"Omphalocele photo: bowel kept in an amnion sac at the umbilicus","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 27","cu":"../sl/G001-27c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/F3903-255.jpg","cap":"Side by side: omphalocele sac at umbilicus versus uncovered gastroschisis bowel","where":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker, slide 255","cu":"../sl/F3903-255c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/E10_17.jpg","cap":"Omphalocele: bowel stays outside in a membrane sac at the umbilicus, with the umbilical cord arising from the sac.","where":"BRS Embryology 5e, Figure 10-17","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"G002-15-1","s":"During the 6th week of embryonic development, the midgut loop normally herniates through the primitive umbilical ring. By the 11th week, these intestinal loops fail to return to the abdominal cavity, remaining covered by a sac of peritoneum and amnion. Which of the following anomalies has occurred?","o":["A. Omphalocele","B. Gastroschisis","C. Umbilical hernia","D. Meckel's diverticulum","E. Duodenal malrotation"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Omphalocele","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Omphalocele.</b> The loops herniated normally but never came back. They stay at the umbilicus inside a sac of <b>peritoneum and amnion</b>, often with a trisomy.<br>B. Gastroschisis: wall gap beside the umbilicus; bowel bare, no sac.<br>C. Umbilical hernia: loops returned, then pushed out later through a weak ring; covered by skin.<br>D. Meckel's diverticulum: leftover vitelline duct.<br>E. Duodenal malrotation: abnormal turning, not failure to return.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 27 (Clinical Correlates)","GI Development slide 23 (Herniation and Rotation)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown clicker","n":"","sl":15,"f":"G002"},{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":291,"f":"F3903"},{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":85,"f":"F0008"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"24"},{"id":"G075-2-1","s":"A newborn is delivered with a congenital midline abdominal wall defect at the base of the umbilical cord. Examination reveals protrusion of the liver and intestines into a translucent membranous sac. The infant's physician explains that this condition results from an abnormality in the normal developmental process in which the primitive gut temporarily moves outside the abdominal cavity and then returns. Which of the following embryologic events most likely failed to occur?","o":["A. Fusion of the right and left lateral body folds at the ventral midline during the fourth week of development.","B. Normal return of the physiologically herniated midgut into the abdominal cavity during the 10th week of development.","C. Regression of the vitelline duct connecting the developing midgut to the yolk sac.","D. Incorporation of the cranial portion of the yolk sac into the embryo during cranio-caudal folding.","E. Rotation of the midgut around the superior mesenteric artery during intestinal development."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Normal return of the physiologically herniated midgut into the abdominal cavity during the 10th week of development","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Return of the herniated midgut in week 10.</b> A midline defect at the base of the cord with liver and intestine in a translucent sac is omphalocele: the loop never came back from the cord.<br>A. Lateral body folds failing to fuse: gastroschisis, bare bowel beside the umbilicus.<br>C. Vitelline duct persisting: Meckel diverticulum.<br>D. Taking in the cranial yolk sac by cranio-caudal folding: affects foregut formation.<br>E. Rotation around the superior mesenteric artery: malrotation or volvulus, not a sac at the umbilicus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 27 (Clinical Correlates)","GI Development slide 24 (Herniation and Rotation)","GI Development slide 23 (Herniation and Rotation)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice","n":"GI #1 practice questions Fall 2026 (Lecture #1 embryology, Lecture #2 abdominal wall)","sl":null,"f":"G075"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"25"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":9,"label":"Gastroschisis: lateral body folds fail to fuse","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G001-5.jpg","cap":"Lateral folding: somatic lateral plate mesoderm wraps around to close body wall","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 5","cu":"../sl/G001-5c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G001-27.jpg","cap":"Gastroschisis photo and cause: bilateral somatic lateral plate mesoderm fails to fuse","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 27","cu":"../sl/G001-27c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G002-9.jpg","cap":"Prenatal ultrasound: bowel floating free outside the fetal abdomen","where":"2026 Middletown clicker, slide 9","cu":"../sl/G002-9c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/E10_18.jpg","cap":"Gastroschisis: bare bowel outside the abdominal wall with no sac, from failed fusion of the lateral body folds.","where":"BRS Embryology 5e, Figure 10-18","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"G002-7-1","s":"Ultrasound examination of a pregnant woman at 32 weeks gestation reveals fetal bowel loops freely floating in the amniotic cavity without a covering membrane. This condition is diagnosed as gastroschisis, which results from a defect in the formation and fusion of which of the following embryonic structures?","o":["A. Head fold","B. Tail fold","C. Neural folds","D. Lateral body folds","E. Primitive umbilical ring"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Lateral body folds","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Lateral body folds.</b> They carry the somatic lateral plate mesoderm forward to close the front abdominal wall. If they fail to fuse, bowel spills out beside the umbilicus with <b>no covering membrane</b>: gastroschisis.<br>A. Head fold: closes the upper chest wall; failure can leave the heart outside the chest (ectopia cordis).<br>B. Tail fold: closes the lower wall; failure is linked to bladder exstrophy.<br>C. Neural folds: form the neural tube.<br>E. Primitive umbilical ring: midgut failing to return through it gives omphalocele.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 27 (Clinical Correlates)","GI Development slide 5 (Lateral Folding)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown clicker","n":"","sl":7,"f":"G002"},{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":79,"f":"F0008"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"26"},{"id":"G075-1-1","s":"A newborn is delivered at 37 weeks gestation with loops of small and large intestine protruding through a defect in the anterior abdominal wall, typically located to the right of the umbilicus. The exposed intestines are not covered by a membranous sac. Prenatal ultrasound identified the abnormality during the second trimester. The physician explains to the parents that this congenital defect resulted from abnormal embryonic folding during development. Which of the following developmental processes most likely failed?","o":["A. Failure of lateral body folds to migrate and fuse at the ventral midline, preventing complete closure of the anterior abdominal wall.","B. Failure of cranio-caudal folding to incorporate the cranial portion of the yolk sac, preventing normal foregut formation.","C. Failure of the midgut to undergo physiological herniation into the umbilical cord during normal intestinal development.","D. Failure of the vitelline duct to regress, resulting in persistent communication between the ileum and umbilicus.","E. Failure of the septum transversum to develop, preventing separation of the thoracic and abdominal cavities."],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Failure of lateral body folds to migrate and fuse at the ventral midline, preventing complete closure of the anterior abdominal wall","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Lateral body folds failing to meet and fuse in the ventral midline.</b> Bowel without a sac, to the right of the umbilicus, is gastroschisis: the bilateral somatic lateral plate mesoderm did not fuse, leaving a full-thickness wall gap.<br>B. Cranio-caudal folding failing to take in the yolk sac: would disturb foregut formation.<br>C. No physiologic herniation: not a described defect; failed return gives omphalocele.<br>D. Vitelline duct persisting: Meckel diverticulum or umbilical fistula.<br>E. Septum transversum failing: a diaphragm defect.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 27 (Clinical Correlates)","GI Development slide 5 (Lateral Folding)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice","n":"GI #1 practice questions Fall 2026 (Lecture #1 embryology, Lecture #2 abdominal wall)","sl":null,"f":"G075"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"27"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":10,"label":"Bare bowel beside umbilicus means gastroschisis","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G001-27.jpg","cap":"Gastroschisis photo: bare bowel beside umbilicus; omphalocele sac above for contrast","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 27","cu":"../sl/G001-27c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/F3903-255.jpg","cap":"Omphalocele (sac, midline, trisomy) versus gastroschisis (no sac, lateral)","where":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker, slide 255","cu":"../sl/F3903-255c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/E10_18.jpg","cap":"Gastroschisis: bowel loops lie bare outside the abdominal wall with no covering sac.","where":"BRS Embryology 5e, Figure 10-18","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/E10_17.jpg","cap":"Compare omphalocele: bowel sits inside a membrane sac at the umbilicus.","where":"BRS Embryology 5e, Figure 10-17","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"G079-28-1","s":"A baby is born with its intestines on the outside of the body. The intestines are not covered with peritoneum. The condition this baby has is","o":["A. omphalocoele","B. reversed gut tube rotation","C. gastroschisis","D. improper cranio-caudal folding"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"gastroschisis","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Gastroschisis.</b> Bowel outside the body with no covering at all, not even peritoneum or amnion, through a gap beside the umbilicus, comes from failed fusion of the lateral body folds.<br>A. Omphalocele: the midgut fails to return; the bowel stays in a sac of amnion lined by peritoneum.<br>B. Reversed rotation: a clockwise turn that leaves the transverse colon behind the superior mesenteric artery; the bowel stays inside.<br>D. Faulty head-to-tail folding: head fold failure leaves the heart outside (ectopia cordis); tail fold failure is linked to bladder exstrophy.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 27 (Clinical Correlates)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Montana interactive","n":"Montana (Great Falls) GI embryology interactive session 2026 (B. Chinnery)","sl":28,"f":"G079"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"28"},{"id":"F3903-253-1","s":"A plastic surgeon is performing reconstructive surgery on the anterior abdominal wall of a female neonate who was born with gastroschisis. The loops of intestines are not severely strangulated and can be fully saved. The surgeon asks one of the residents which of the following statements is true about gastroschisis:","o":["A. The intestines herniate into the umbilical cord","B. It is usually associated with a trisomy","C. It is caused due to improper resorption of the midgut","D. The intestines are covered by a membrane","E. The intestines herniate lateral to the umbilicus"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"The intestines herniate lateral to the umbilicus","a":"E","why":"<b>E. The intestines herniate lateral to the umbilicus.</b> Gastroschisis is a wall gap beside the umbilicus, usually on the right, from failed fusion of the lateral folds.<br>A. Into the umbilical cord: omphalocele.<br>B. Trisomy: goes with omphalocele, not gastroschisis.<br>C. Improper 'resorption' of the midgut: not a real cause; failed return of the midgut is omphalocele.<br>D. Covered by a membrane: omphalocele.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 27 (Clinical Correlates)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":253,"f":"F3903"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"29"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":11,"label":"Meckel's diverticulum: leftover vitelline duct on the ileum","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G001-28.jpg","cap":"Ileal diverticulum: free pouch, fibrous cord, or open umbilical fistula","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 28","cu":"../sl/G001-28c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G001-24.jpg","cap":"Panel A: omphaloenteric (vitelline) duct joins midgut loop to yolk sac","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 24","cu":"../sl/G001-24c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/E10_19.jpg","cap":"Arrow: Meckel's diverticulum, a blind pouch on the ileum (IL) left over from the vitelline duct.","where":"BRS Embryology 5e, Figure 10-19","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/E10_16.jpg","cap":"The vitelline duct joins the tip of the midgut loop to the yolk sac; if it persists you get Meckel's.","where":"BRS Embryology 5e, Figure 10-16","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"G075-9-1","s":"A 3-year-old boy is brought to the emergency department by his parents after passing painless, dark red blood in his stool. His parents reported no history of trauma, fever, or abdominal pain. Laboratory testing reveals decreased hemoglobin. A technetium-99m pertechnetate scan demonstrates ectopic gastric tissue in the distal small intestine. The physician explains that this congenital abnormality results from persistence of an embryologic structure that normally regresses during development. Which of the following best describes the embryologic defect responsible for this condition?","o":["A. Failure of the midgut to return to the abdominal cavity.","B. Failure of the duodenal lumen to recanalize.","C. Failure of the midgut to complete its normal rotation.","D. Failure of the dorsal pancreatic bud to develop.","E. Failure of the vitelline duct to completely regress."],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Failure of the vitelline duct to completely regress","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Failure of the vitelline duct to regress completely.</b> Painless dark red rectal bleeding in a small child, with ectopic gastric tissue in the distal small intestine on a technetium-99m pertechnetate scan, is a Meckel diverticulum: the persistent gut end of the vitelline duct.<br>A. Failed midgut return: omphalocele.<br>B. Failed duodenal recanalization: atresia or stenosis with vomiting.<br>C. Incomplete rotation: malrotation and volvulus.<br>D. No dorsal pancreatic bud: missing pancreatic neck, body and tail.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 28 (Meckel’s Diverticulum)","GI Development slide 23 (Herniation and Rotation)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice","n":"GI #1 practice questions Fall 2026 (Lecture #1 embryology, Lecture #2 abdominal wall)","sl":null,"f":"G075"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"30"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":11,"label":"Stool or discharge from umbilicus means Meckel's fistula","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G001-28.jpg","cap":"Panel C: open omphaloenteric fistula from ileal diverticulum to umbilicus","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 28","cu":"../sl/G001-28c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-289-1","s":"A 3-month-old baby girl presents with a swollen umbilicus that has failed to heal normally. The umbilicus drains secretions, and there is passage of fecal material through the umbilicus at times. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["A. Omphalocele","B. Gastroschisis","C. Anal agenesis","D. Meckel's diverticulum","E. Intestinal stenosis"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Meckel's diverticulum","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Meckel's diverticulum.</b> When the vitelline duct stays open from the ileum to the umbilicus (a vitelline fistula), stool and secretions drain from an <b>umbilicus that will not heal</b>.<br>A. Omphalocele: bowel in a sac at birth.<br>B. Gastroschisis: bare bowel beside the umbilicus at birth.<br>C. Anal agenesis: no anus; stool may leave by a fistula to the perineum or urinary tract, not the umbilicus.<br>E. Intestinal stenosis: a narrowing; vomiting and bloating.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 28 (Meckel’s Diverticulum)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":289,"f":"F3903"},{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":83,"f":"F0008"},{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":218,"f":"F3902"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":31,"f":"F3901"},{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":185,"f":"F1252"},{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":307,"f":"F1228"},{"t":"? tutor review","n":"old Exam 2: abdomen part only","sl":203,"f":"F1243"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"31"},{"id":"F3903-257-1","s":"While feeding her one month old neonate, a mother observes a small lump in the baby’s lower right hand quadrant. When she gently palpates it, there seems to be some discharge from the umbilicus. She rushes the baby to the pediatrician. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["A. Meckel’s diverticulum","B. Megacolon","C. Gastroschisis","D. Colic atresia","E. Omphalocoele"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Meckel’s diverticulum","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Meckel's diverticulum.</b> A lump in the right lower quadrant plus <b>discharge from the umbilicus</b> fits an ileal pouch still joined to the umbilicus by the vitelline duct.<br>B. Megacolon (Hirschsprung disease): constipation and a swollen belly.<br>C. Gastroschisis: bare bowel at birth.<br>D. Colic (colon) atresia: blocked colon; no stool.<br>E. Omphalocoele: bowel in a sac at the umbilicus at birth.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 28 (Meckel’s Diverticulum)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":257,"f":"F3903"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"32"},{"id":"G079-31-1","s":"2 to 3% of the population have a connection between their small intestine and their anterior body wall and a fistula between the small intestine and the umbilicus. This may allow chyme to be released through the belly button, but also causes a range of symptoms including rectal bleeding, pain, and inflammation. This is called","o":["A. Meckels diverticulum","B. gastroschisis","C. omphalocoele","D. hemorrhoids","E. cloacal hernia"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Meckels diverticulum","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Meckel diverticulum.</b> A persistent gut end of the vitelline duct on the ileum, in 2–3% of people; when the whole duct stays open (vitelline fistula), chyme or stool drains from the umbilicus, and ectopic gastric tissue can cause rectal bleeding.<br>B. Gastroschisis: bowel outside the body at birth through a wall gap.<br>C. Omphalocele: bowel in an amnion sac at the umbilicus at birth.<br>D. Hemorrhoids: swollen anal veins; they bleed but have no link to the umbilicus.<br>E. Cloacal hernia: not a recognized defect; cloacal errors give rectourinary or rectovaginal fistulas.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 28 (Meckel’s Diverticulum)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Montana interactive","n":"Montana (Great Falls) GI embryology interactive session 2026 (B. Chinnery)","sl":31,"f":"G079"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"33"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":12,"label":"Urorectal septum divides cloaca; defect causes fistula","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G001-30.jpg","cap":"Urorectal septum splits cloaca into bladder in front, rectum behind","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 30","cu":"../sl/G001-30c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G001-32.jpg","cap":"Urorectal fistula diagram from abnormal urorectal septum","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 32","cu":"../sl/G001-32c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G002-14.jpg","cap":"Normal cloacal partition by urorectal septum versus rectovaginal fistula","where":"2026 Middletown clicker, slide 14","cu":"../sl/G002-14c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/E10_26.jpg","cap":"Top panels: the urorectal septum grows down and splits the cloaca into urogenital sinus in front, rectum behind.","where":"BRS Embryology 5e, Figure 10-26","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/E10_30.jpg","cap":"Rectovaginal fistula: a faulty urorectal septum leaves the rectum opening into the vagina.","where":"BRS Embryology 5e, Figure 10-30","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"G002-13-1","s":"A newborn female is examined in the neonatal intensive care unit shortly after delivery. The delivery nurse notes that the infant has passed meconium, but it appears to be emerging entirely from the vaginal vestibule. Physical examination confirms the absence of a normal anal opening at the perineum. Which of the following developmental processes most likely failed during embryogenesis to cause this presentation?","o":["A. Recanalization of the anorectal canal lumen","B. Fusion of the lateral mesonephric ducts","C. Partitioning of the cloaca by the urorectal septum","D. Migration of neural crest cells into the hindgut wall","E. Invagination of the proctodeum surface ectoderm"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Partitioning of the cloaca by the urorectal septum","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Partitioning of the cloaca by the urorectal septum.</b> Meconium coming out of the vaginal vestibule with no anus means the rectum still opens into the front (urogenital) part. That is a <b>rectovestibular fistula</b> from incomplete septum growth.<br>A. Anorectal recanalization: not how the anus opens; the anal membrane ruptures.<br>B. Mesonephric ducts: male genital ducts; they do not fuse.<br>D. Neural crest migration: Hirschsprung disease; anus normal.<br>E. Proctodeum invagination: explains a missing anus, but not a rectum opening into the vestibule.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 32 (Clinical Correlates)","GI Development slide 30 (Cloaca and Cloacal Division)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown clicker","n":"","sl":13,"f":"G002"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"34"},{"id":"G075-11-1","s":"A full-term newborn is evaluated shortly after delivery because the infant has not passed meconium. Physical examination reveals an absent anal opening and abdominal distension. Further imaging demonstrates abnormal communication between the rectum and the urinary tract. The pediatric surgeon explains that this congenital abnormality developed because the embryonic cloaca did not separate properly during development. Which of the following best describes the normal embryologic process that was disrupted?","o":["A. Failure of the vitelline duct to regress during embryonic development.","B. Failure of the hindgut to recanalize following temporary epithelial occlusion.","C. Failure of neural crest cells to migrate into the developing distal colon.","D. Division of the cloaca by the urorectal septum into the urogenital sinus and anorectal canal.","E. Abnormal rotation of the midgut around the superior mesenteric artery."],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Division of the cloaca by the urorectal septum into the urogenital sinus and anorectal canal","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Division of the cloaca by the urorectal septum.</b> No anus plus a link between the rectum and urinary tract means the septum did not fully separate the urogenital sinus (front) from the anorectal canal (back), leaving a urorectal fistula.<br>A. Vitelline duct persisting: Meckel diverticulum.<br>B. Failed hindgut recanalization: the anus opens by membrane rupture, not recanalization.<br>C. Neural crest failure: Hirschsprung disease, with a normal anus.<br>E. Midgut malrotation: changes bowel position, not a fistula.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 32 (Clinical Correlates)","GI Development slide 30 (Cloaca and Cloacal Division)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice","n":"GI #1 practice questions Fall 2026 (Lecture #1 embryology, Lecture #2 abdominal wall)","sl":null,"f":"G075"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"35"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":12,"label":"Anal membrane must rupture; else imperforate anus","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G001-30.jpg","cap":"Panel E labels the anal membrane closing the anal canal","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 30","cu":"../sl/G001-30c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G001-32.jpg","cap":"Imperforate anus: anal membrane stays between rectum and anal pit","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 32","cu":"../sl/G001-32c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/D045-170.jpg","cap":"Urorectal septum splits cloacal membrane into urogenital and anal membranes","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 170","cu":"../sl/D045-170c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-278-1","s":"A 3-day-old neonate has not excreted any meconium (first feces). Feeding has been normal and there has been no regurgitation or projectile vomiting. His appetite is normal but his abdomen seems slightly distended. A manual examination would reveal which of the following anomalies?","o":["A. Urorectal fistula","B. Anal stenosis","C. Imperforate anus","D. Appendicitis"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Imperforate anus","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Imperforate anus.</b> No meconium at all, normal feeding, no vomiting and a mildly swollen belly point to a <b>closed anal opening</b>. A finger exam finds no anus, or only a membrane.<br>A. Urorectal fistula: meconium passes out through the urethra.<br>B. Anal stenosis: some meconium would pass through the narrow opening.<br>D. Appendicitis: fever and pain; rare in newborns.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 32 (Clinical Correlates)","GI Development slide 30 (Cloaca and Cloacal Division)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":278,"f":"F3903"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"36"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":13,"label":"Anal canal: upper two-thirds endoderm, lower third ectoderm","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G001-31.jpg","cap":"Anal canal: hindgut above pectinate line, proctodeum (ectoderm) below","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 31","cu":"../sl/G001-31c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/F3903-277.jpg","cap":"Upper two-thirds endoderm with autonomic nerves; lower third ectoderm, somatic","where":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker, slide 277","cu":"../sl/F3903-277c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/E10_26.jpg","cap":"Photo: upper anal canal from hindgut (endoderm), lower anal canal from proctodeum (ectoderm), split at pectinate line.","where":"BRS Embryology 5e, Figure 10-26","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-285-1","s":"The simple columnar and stratified columnar epithelia lining the lower part of the anal canal above the pectinate line is derived from","o":["A. Mesoderm","B. Endoderm","C. Ectoderm","D. Neuroectoderm","E. Neural crest cells"],"g":["B"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Ectoderm","a":"C","why":"<b>B. Endoderm.</b> Lining <b>above the pectinate line</b> is hindgut endoderm, which is why it is columnar like the rest of the gut. (Ectoderm lines only the zone below the pectinate line.)<br>A. Mesoderm: muscle and connective tissue.<br>C. Ectoderm: below the pectinate line, from the proctodeum.<br>D. Neuroectoderm: brain and spinal cord.<br>E. Neural crest cells: gut nerve cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 31 (Anal Canal)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":285,"f":"F3903"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"37"},{"id":"G075-12-1","s":"A 2-day-old newborn is brought to the pediatric surgeon because of an abnormal anal opening and difficulty passing meconium. Physical examination reveals an imperforate anus. Further evaluation identifies an abnormality involving the junction between the upper and lower portions of the anal canal. The surgeon explains that the anal canal develops from two different embryologic structures, and abnormal development can result in congenital anorectal malformations. Which of the following correctly identifies the embryologic origins of the anal canal?","o":["A. The upper anal canal develops from the hindgut, while the lower anal canal develops from the proctodeum.","B. The upper anal canal develops from the proctodeum, while the lower anal canal develops from the hindgut.","C. Both portions of the anal canal develop from the hindgut.","D. Both portions of the anal canal develop from the cloaca.","E. The upper anal canal develops from the midgut, while the lower anal canal develops from the proctodeum."],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"The upper anal canal develops from the hindgut, while the lower anal canal develops from the proctodeum","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Upper canal from the hindgut, lower canal from the proctodeum.</b> The superior two-thirds is hindgut (cloacal) endoderm; the inferior third is ectoderm of the proctodeum (anal pit). They meet at the pectinate line, where the anal membrane was.<br>B. Reversed: the proctodeum forms the lower part.<br>C. Both from hindgut: the lower third is ectoderm.<br>D. Both from cloaca: the cloaca gives only the upper part.<br>E. Upper from midgut: the anal canal is hindgut, not midgut.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 31 (Anal Canal)","GI Development slide 3 (Trilaminar Germ Disc)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice","n":"GI #1 practice questions Fall 2026 (Lecture #1 embryology, Lecture #2 abdominal wall)","sl":null,"f":"G075"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"38"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":13,"label":"Hemorrhoid pain: endoderm painless above, ectoderm painful below","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G001-31.jpg","cap":"Pectinate line divides hindgut endoderm (above) from proctodeum ectoderm (below)","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 31","cu":"../sl/G001-31c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/F3903-277.jpg","cap":"Above pectinate: endoderm, autonomic nerves; below: ectoderm, somatic nerves","where":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker, slide 277","cu":"../sl/F3903-277c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/E10_26.jpg","cap":"Photo: above the pectinate line is hindgut endoderm (visceral nerves, painless); below is ectoderm (painful).","where":"BRS Embryology 5e, Figure 10-26","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-273-1","s":"A 48-year-old male rushes to the ER after noticing blood in his fecal matter. A physical exam reveals no portal hypertension and no history of localized pain. The patient does mention feeling slight discomfort while sitting. A rectal examination confirms the diagnosis of internal hemorrhoids. Which of the following explains the lack of localized pain in this diagnosis?","o":["A. Hemorrhoids are located in the endodermal aspect of the anal canal","B. Hemorrhoids are located in the ectodermal aspect of the anal canal","C. The area where internal hemorrhoids can develop is innervated by somatic nerves","D. The hindgut failed to properly fuse with the surface ectoderm","E. Hemorrhoids are located in the mesodermal aspect of the anal canal"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Hemorrhoids are located in the endodermal aspect of the anal canal","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Endodermal part of the anal canal.</b> Internal hemorrhoids lie above the pectinate line, in hindgut endoderm. It has <b>autonomic nerves only</b>, so there is no sharp, localized pain.<br>B. Ectodermal part: below the line; external hemorrhoids, painful.<br>C. Somatic nerves: supply below the line; they would cause pain.<br>D. Hindgut not fusing with ectoderm: imperforate anus.<br>E. Mesodermal part: the lining is endoderm or ectoderm, not mesoderm.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 31 (Anal Canal)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":273,"f":"F3903"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"39"},{"id":"G079-34-1","s":"After having 2 babies, a woman went to see her primary doctor about a painful, bleeding, itchy anus. Her doctor diagnosed her with external hemorrhoids. Why not internal hemorrhoids?","o":["A. internal hemorrhoids don’t exist","B. external hemorrhoids are the only ones associated with childbirth","C. internal hemorrhoids have a different blood supply","D. internal hemorrhoids don’t itch because of the nervous supply","E. internal hemorrhoids itch more because of the nervous supply"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"internal hemorrhoids don’t itch because of the nervous supply","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Internal hemorrhoids don't itch because of their nerve supply.</b> Internal hemorrhoids lie above the pectinate line in endoderm with only visceral nerves, so they bleed painlessly; external ones lie below it, under skin with somatic (inferior rectal) nerves, so they hurt and itch.<br>A. Internal hemorrhoids do exist, above the pectinate line.<br>B. Straining, pregnancy and childbirth raise venous pressure and cause both types.<br>C. Their blood supply does differ (superior versus inferior rectal vessels), but that does not decide pain or itch.<br>E. Reversed: internal ones have visceral nerves and do not itch.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 31 (Anal Canal)","Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 54 (Porto systemic anastomosis)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Montana interactive","n":"Montana (Great Falls) GI embryology interactive session 2026 (B. Chinnery)","sl":34,"f":"G079"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"40"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":13,"label":"Below the pectinate line: inferior rectal (pudendal) nerve","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G076-36.jpg","cap":"Anal canal innervation: autonomic above pectinate line, pudendal branch below","where":"2026 GI supplemental session (Nov 6), slide 36","cu":"../sl/G076-36c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G001-31.jpg","cap":"Anal canal: hindgut above the pectinate line, proctodeum below","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 31","cu":"../sl/G001-31c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"G075-13-1","s":"A 35-year-old man visits his physician because of severe pain during bowel movements and bright red blood on the toilet paper. Physical examination reveals a painful anal fissure located below the pectinate line. The physician explains that the patient's intense pain is related to the sensory innervation of this region of the anal canal. Which of the following nerves is primarily responsible for transmitting pain sensations from this area?","o":["A. Superior rectal plexus.","B. Pelvic splanchnic nerves.","C. Hypogastric nerves.","D. Vagus nerve.","E. Inferior rectal branches of the pudendal nerve."],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Inferior rectal branches of the pudendal nerve","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Inferior rectal branches of the pudendal nerve.</b> Below the pectinate line the canal is ectoderm with somatic sensory nerves from the inferior rectal nerve, so a fissure there is sharply painful.<br>A. Superior rectal plexus: autonomic fibers above the line; they sense stretch, not sharp pain.<br>B. Pelvic splanchnic nerves: parasympathetic, above the line.<br>C. Hypogastric nerves: sympathetic, visceral, above the line.<br>D. Vagus nerve: parasympathetic to foregut and midgut only; it does not reach the anal canal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 31 (Anal Canal)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice","n":"GI #1 practice questions Fall 2026 (Lecture #1 embryology, Lecture #2 abdominal wall)","sl":null,"f":"G075"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"41"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":14,"label":"Hirschsprung: neural crest fails, ganglion cells absent","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G001-32.jpg","cap":"Megacolon: no postganglionic parasympathetic cell bodies; poor neural crest migration","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 32","cu":"../sl/G001-32c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G002-11.jpg","cap":"Hirschsprung disease: narrow distal segment with dilated colon above","where":"2026 Middletown clicker, slide 11"},{"src":"../sl/G002-12.jpg","cap":"Gut wall layers showing submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses","where":"2026 Middletown clicker, slide 12","cu":"../sl/G002-12c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"G002-10-1","s":"A 4-day-old newborn male has not passed meconium since birth despite normal feeding toleration. Digital rectal examination reveals normal anal anatomy, but a tight anal canal. Upon withdrawal of the examiner's finger, there is an immediate, forceful release of flatus and liquid stool. This condition is caused by a failure of neural crest cells to migrate into which of the following structures?","o":["A. Internal anal sphincter","B. Urachus","C. Ventral mesodermal folds","D. Linea alba","E. Submucosal and myenteric plexuses (Hirschsprung disease)"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Submucosal and myenteric plexuses (Hirschsprung disease)","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Submucosal and myenteric plexuses.</b> Neural crest cells must reach the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses. If they stop short, the end segment cannot relax: no meconium, a tight canal and an explosive 'squirt' of stool after the exam.<br>A. Internal anal sphincter: smooth muscle from mesoderm; it fails to relax because its plexus lacks neurons.<br>B. Urachus: leftover allantois.<br>C. Ventral mesodermal folds: body wall.<br>D. Linea alba: midline tendon of the abdominal wall.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 32 (Clinical Correlates)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown clicker","n":"","sl":10,"f":"G002"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"42"},{"id":"F3903-287-1","s":"A 4-day-old baby boy has not defecated since coming home from the hospital even though feeding has been normal without any excessive vomiting. Rectal examination reveals a normal anus, anal canal, and rectum. However, a large fecal mass is found in the colon, and a large release of flatus and feces follows the rectal examination. Which of the following conditions would be suspected?","o":["A. Imperforate anus","B. Anal agenesis","C. Anorectal agenesis","D. Rectal atresia","E. Agangliononic megacolon"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Aganglionic megacolon","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Aganglionic megacolon (Hirschsprung disease).</b> A normal anus, anal canal and rectum, a big stool mass in the colon, and a gush of gas and stool after the exam fit a <b>narrow segment without nerve cells</b>.<br>A. Imperforate anus: no anal opening.<br>B. Anal agenesis: no anal canal.<br>C. Anorectal agenesis: rectum ends high; no anus.<br>D. Rectal atresia: anus present, but the finger hits a blind end.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 32 (Clinical Correlates)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":287,"f":"F3903"},{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":81,"f":"F0008"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"43"},{"id":"G075-10-1","s":"A full-term newborn is brought to the emergency department at 48 hours of life because he has not passed meconium since birth. The infant has progressive abdominal distension, bilious vomiting, and difficulty tolerating feedings. A contrast enema demonstrates narrowing of the distal colon with dilation of the proximal colon. A rectal biopsy reveals an absence of ganglion cells in the submucosal and myenteric plexuses. The physician explains that this congenital condition is associated with abnormal development of the hindgut and enteric nervous system. Which of the following best explains the embryologic abnormality responsible for this newborn's condition?","o":["A. Failure of the hindgut endoderm to recanalize during intestinal development.","B. Failure of neural crest cells to migrate into the distal hindgut and form enteric ganglia.","C. Failure of the midgut to complete its normal counterclockwise rotation.","D. Failure of the vitelline duct to regress during embryologic development.","E. Failure of the cloaca to divide into the urogenital sinus and anorectal canal."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Failure of neural crest cells to migrate into the distal hindgut and form enteric ganglia","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Failure of neural crest cells to migrate into the distal hindgut.</b> No meconium, distension, a narrow distal colon with dilated colon above, and no ganglion cells in the submucosal and myenteric plexuses is Hirschsprung disease.<br>A. Failed recanalization: atresia or stenosis, with ganglion cells present.<br>C. Incomplete midgut rotation: malrotation; biopsy normal.<br>D. Vitelline duct persisting: Meckel diverticulum.<br>E. Cloaca not dividing: rectourinary or rectovaginal fistula.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 32 (Clinical Correlates)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice","n":"GI #1 practice questions Fall 2026 (Lecture #1 embryology, Lecture #2 abdominal wall)","sl":null,"f":"G075"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"44"},{"id":"G079-38-1","s":"A newborn is brought to the hospital after 2 days due to not having any bowel movements since birth. The baby is diagnosed with Hirschsprung Disease (congenital aganglionic megacolon; HSCR). This condition is the result of","o":["A. failure of the cloaca to divide properly","B. abnormal formation of the urorectal septum","C. imperforate anus","D. failure of sympathetic ganglion cells to develop","E. failure of parasympathetic ganglion cells to develop"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"failure of parasympathetic ganglion cells to develop","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Failure of parasympathetic ganglion cells to develop.</b> Neural crest cells stop short of the distal colon, so its submucosal and myenteric plexuses lack ganglion cells, the postganglionic parasympathetic cell bodies; the segment cannot relax and no meconium passes.<br>A. Cloaca not dividing: rectourinary or rectovaginal fistula.<br>B. Abnormal urorectal septum: the same fistula problem.<br>C. Imperforate anus: the anal membrane fails to rupture, leaving no opening; here the anus is normal.<br>D. Sympathetic ganglion cells: they sit outside the gut wall (celiac and mesenteric ganglia) and are normal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 32 (Clinical Correlates)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Montana interactive","n":"Montana (Great Falls) GI embryology interactive session 2026 (B. Chinnery)","sl":38,"f":"G079"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"45"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":15,"label":"Referred pain map: stomach → epigastrium and mid-back","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G003-7.jpg","cap":"Epigastric = foregut pain; ulcer epigastric, cholecystitis right hypochondrium, appendicitis right inguinal","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 7","cu":"../sl/G003-7c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/F1252-51.jpg","cap":"Referred pain map: stomach to epigastrium and mid-upper back; gallbladder to right shoulder","where":"2020 TA review, slide 51","cu":"../sl/F1252-51c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G003-24.jpg","cap":"Incisions placed over the region giving best access to the target organ","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 24","cu":"../sl/G003-24c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"F0008-56-1","s":"You were asked to assist in a surgical operation on a young patient to treat an ulcer in the posterior wall of the stomach. You would expect that the surgeon will approach the ulcer by doing an anterior abdominal wall incision in the following region:","o":["a. Epigastric","b. Left inguinal","c. Left lumbar","d. Right hypochondrial","e. Hypogastric"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Epigastric","a":"A","why":"<b>Epigastric.</b> The stomach lies mainly in the epigastric region. Incisions are made over the region that gives the best access to the target organ, so a posterior gastric ulcer is still reached from the front through the epigastric region.<br>B. Left inguinal: lower left groin; sigmoid colon area.<br>C. Left lumbar: left flank; descending colon area.<br>D. Right hypochondrial: liver and gallbladder area.<br>E. Hypogastric: pubic region above the bladder; hindgut pain area.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 7 (Referred Pain and Palpations)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 6 (9 regions of the Abdomen)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":56,"f":"F0008"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"46"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":15,"label":"Appendicitis RLQ, cholecystitis right hypochondrium","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G078-5.jpg","cap":"Visceral pain follows embryologic origin; appendix is midgut, then localizes to right lower quadrant","where":"2026 TA session: GI week 1 part 2, slide 5","cu":"../sl/G078-5c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G003-7.jpg","cap":"Palpation: appendicitis right inguinal, cholecystitis right hypochondrium, ulcer epigastrium","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 7","cu":"../sl/G003-7c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G003-24.jpg","cap":"McBurney's point: two-thirds of the way from umbilicus to anterior superior iliac spine","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 24","cu":"../sl/G003-24c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"G075-14-1","s":"A 21-year-old male presents to the emergency department with abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, and loss of appetite. The physician suspects acute appendicitis based on the patient's symptoms and prepares to perform an abdominal examination to identify the area of greatest tenderness. In which abdominal quadrant should the physician primarily palpate to assess for tenderness associated with appendicitis?","o":["A. Right upper quadrant (RUQ).","B. Left upper quadrant (LUQ).","C. Right lower quadrant (RLQ).","D. Left lower quadrant (LLQ).","E. Epigastric region."],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Right lower quadrant (RLQ)","a":"C","why":"<b>Right lower quadrant (RLQ).</b> The appendix hangs from the cecum in the right lower quadrant, so once its inflammation reaches the parietal peritoneum the tenderness is greatest there (the right inguinal region of the nine-region map, around McBurney's point).<br>A. Right upper quadrant (RUQ): gallbladder and liver; cholecystitis.<br>B. Left upper quadrant (LUQ): stomach and spleen.<br>D. Left lower quadrant (LLQ): sigmoid colon; diverticulitis.<br>E. Epigastric region: foregut pain such as a peptic ulcer; early appendicitis pain is periumbilical, not epigastric.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 7 (Referred Pain and Palpations)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 11 (Organs in Quadrants)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice","n":"GI #1 practice questions Fall 2026 (Lecture #1 embryology, Lecture #2 abdominal wall)","sl":null,"f":"G075"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"47"},{"id":"G075-15-1","s":"A 42-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with right-sided abdominal pain, nausea, and vomiting following a meal high in fat. On physical examination, the physician notes tenderness beneath the right costal margin and a positive Murphy's sign. The physician suspects acute cholecystitis and performs further diagnostic testing. In which abdominal region should the physician primarily palpate to assess for tenderness associated with gallbladder inflammation?","o":["A. Epigastric region.","B. Right hypochondriac region.","C. Left lumbar region.","D. Right iliac region.","E. Umbilical region."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Right hypochondriac region","a":"B","why":"<b>Right hypochondriac region.</b> The gallbladder lies under the right costal margin. Its fundus touches the wall where the right linea semilunaris meets the 9th costal cartilage, so cholecystitis is tender there and a deep breath on palpation catches (Murphy sign).<br>A. Epigastric region: stomach and duodenum (peptic ulcer).<br>C. Left lumbar (flank) region: descending colon or left kidney.<br>D. Right iliac (inguinal) region: appendicitis.<br>E. Umbilical region: midgut pain, such as early appendicitis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 7 (Referred Pain and Palpations)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 6 (9 regions of the Abdomen)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice","n":"GI #1 practice questions Fall 2026 (Lecture #1 embryology, Lecture #2 abdominal wall)","sl":null,"f":"G075"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"48"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":15,"label":"Gallbladder fundus at right 9th costal cartilage","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G006-39.jpg","cap":"Gallbladder fundus lies at the tip of the ninth costal cartilage","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 39","cu":"../sl/G006-39c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G003-8.jpg","cap":"Right linea semilunaris meets the costal margin at the transpyloric plane","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 8","cu":"../sl/G003-8c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/N244.jpg","cap":"The green gallbladder fundus sits where the right linea semilunaris meets the costal margin (9th cartilage) at the transpyloric plane.","where":"Netter Atlas 6e, Plate 244","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-246-1","s":"A 47-year-old man came into the emergency room with a complaint of severe pain felt on the right upper quadrant of his abdominal wall. On examination, the physician noted that the patient experienced pain while inhaling and the pain was localized at the junction of the linea semilunaris and the right ninth costal cartilage. Which of the following organs is the most likely involved in the origination of the pain?","o":["A. Duodenum","B. Gall bladder","C. Kidney","D. Liver","E. Transverse colon"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Gall bladder","a":"B","why":"<b>Gall bladder.</b> The <b>fundus</b> of the gallbladder touches the front wall where the right <b>linea semilunaris</b> (lateral edge of rectus abdominis) meets the <b>right 9th costal cartilage</b>. Pain there when breathing in is Murphy sign of an inflamed gallbladder.<br>A. Duodenum: deeper, near the midline.<br>C. Kidney: on the back wall; felt in the flank and back.<br>D. Liver: mostly under the ribs; pain is not focused at this point.<br>E. Transverse colon: hangs lower, across the umbilical region.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 9 (Transpyloric planes)","Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 39 (Gallbladder)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":246,"f":"F3903"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"49"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":16,"label":"Which organ lies in which quadrant","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G003-11.jpg","cap":"Organs by quadrant: gallbladder right upper, spleen left upper, ovary left lower","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 11"},{"src":"../sl/F1252-51.jpg","cap":"Referred pain: gallbladder to right shoulder, spleen to left side","where":"2020 TA review, slide 51","cu":"../sl/F1252-51c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G069-4.jpg","cap":"Organs in each quadrant","where":"2026 Middletown interactive: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 4","cu":"../sl/G069-4c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G078-3.jpg","cap":"Organs by quadrant: pancreatic tail with spleen and stomach in the left upper","where":"2026 TA session: GI week 1 part 2, slide 3","cu":"../sl/G078-3c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"G069-5-1","s":"A 47-year-old man came into the emergency room with a complaint of severe pain felt in the left lower quadrant of his abdominal wall. On examination, the physician noted that the patient’s pain radiated into his pelvis and was experiencing abdominal bloating even though he hadn’t eaten for hours. Which of the following organs is the most likely involved in the origination of the pain?","o":["A. Gall bladder","B. Liver","C. Jejunum","D. Sigmoid colon","E. Appendix"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Sigmoid colon","a":"D","why":"<b>Sigmoid colon.</b> The sigmoid colon lies in the left lower quadrant (LLQ). Pain there that spreads into the pelvis, with bloating, in an older adult points to disease of the left colon such as diverticulitis.<br>A. Gall bladder: right upper quadrant (RUQ).<br>B. Liver: mostly the RUQ.<br>C. Jejunum: mostly the left upper quadrant (LUQ).<br>E. Appendix: right lower quadrant (RLQ).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 11 (Organs in Quadrants)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 10 (Quadrants)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Anterior abdominal wall interactive Q&A Fall 2026 (Dr. Connington)","sl":5,"f":"G069"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"50"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":17,"label":"Abdominal wall layers, superficial to deep","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G003-13.jpg","cap":"Wall layers from skin, Camper, Scarpa, three muscles, to parietal peritoneum","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 13","cu":"../sl/G003-13c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/D045-24.jpg","cap":"Numbered layers: skin, Camper, Scarpa, three muscles, transversalis fascia, peritoneum","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 24"},{"src":"../sl/G073-3.jpg","cap":"Longitudinal section: fatty Camper layer above membranous Scarpa layer, then the muscles","where":"2026 Harlem clickers: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 3","cu":"../sl/G073-3c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G078-7.jpg","cap":"Layers from skin to peritoneum: Camper, Scarpa, investing fascia, three muscles","where":"2026 TA session: GI week 1 part 2, slide 7","cu":"../sl/G078-7c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-220-1","s":"Camper's and Scarpa's fascia refer to the:","o":["A) fascial layers of the abdominal wall that are deep to the peritoneum.","B) superficial and deep (respectively) fascial layers of the posterior abdominal wall.","C) superficial and deep layers (respectively) of the rectus sheath.","D) fatty and membranous layers (respectively) of the superficial fascia of the inferior anterior abdominal wall.","E) fatty and membranous fascial layers (respectively) of the perineum."],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"fatty and membranous layers (respectively) of the superficial fascia of the inferior anterior abdominal wall","a":"D","why":"<b>Fatty (Camper) and membranous (Scarpa) layers of the superficial fascia</b> (subcutaneous tissue) of the lower anterior abdominal wall. Both lie between the skin and the external oblique. Scarpa is distinct mainly below the umbilicus.<br>A. They are superficial, not deep to the peritoneum.<br>B. They belong to the anterior wall, not the posterior wall.<br>C. The rectus sheath is made of muscle aponeuroses, not these.<br>E. In the perineum the membranous layer is called Colles fascia, not Scarpa.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 13 (Anterolateral Abdominal Wall)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Harlem clicker","n":"Harlem anterior abdominal wall & inguinal canal clickers 11/5/26","sl":2,"f":"G073"},{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":220,"f":"F3903"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"51"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":17,"label":"External oblique fibers run inferomedially","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G003-14.jpg","cap":"External oblique fibers run down and medially; aponeurosis forms inguinal ligament","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 14","cu":"../sl/G003-14c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G073-6.jpg","cap":"External oblique dissection: fibers run down and medially, like hands in pockets","where":"2026 Harlem clickers: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 6","cu":"../sl/G073-6c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/M5_6.jpg","cap":"In layer 1, external oblique fibers run down and forward (inferomedially), like hands in front pockets.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.6","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-222-1","s":"Most muscle fibers of the external oblique muscle:","o":["A) Run transversely.","B) Run inferomedially from their superior attachment.","C) Run inferolaterally from their superior attachment.","D) Pass deep to the linea alba.","E) Pass deep to the inguinal ligament."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Run inferomedially from their superior attachment","a":"B","why":"<b>Inferomedially.</b> External oblique fibers run down and toward the midline, like hands slid into front pockets. They end in an aponeurosis that joins the linea alba; its free lower edge folds over to form the inguinal ligament.<br>A. Transverse fibers belong to transversus abdominis.<br>C. Inferolateral is the internal oblique's slope, at right angles to the external oblique.<br>D. The aponeurosis inserts into the linea alba, not deep to it.<br>E. The aponeurosis itself forms the inguinal ligament.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 14 (External Oblique)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Harlem clicker","n":"Harlem anterior abdominal wall & inguinal canal clickers 11/5/26","sl":5,"f":"G073"},{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":222,"f":"F3903"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"52"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":17,"label":"Abdominal wall muscles: raise pressure, move trunk","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G003-15.jpg","cap":"Internal oblique flexes and rotates trunk, raises intra-abdominal pressure","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 15","cu":"../sl/G003-15c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G003-14.jpg","cap":"External oblique actions: trunk flexion and rotation, intra-abdominal pressure","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 14","cu":"../sl/G003-14c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/M5_6.jpg","cap":"Internal oblique (layer 2) runs up and forward; the obliques flex and rotate the trunk and squeeze the abdomen.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.6","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"G075-20-1","s":"A 28-year-old competitive weightlifter is performing a heavy deadlift. Before lifting weight, he takes a deep breath, contracts his abdominal muscles, and braces his core to stabilize his trunk. His instructor explains that coordinated contraction of the abdominal wall muscles increases intra-abdominal pressure, helping stabilize the lumbar spine during heavy lifting. Which of the following muscle groups contributes most directly to increasing intra-abdominal pressure during this maneuver?","o":["A. Rectus abdominis, external oblique, internal oblique, and transversus abdominis.","B. Latissimus dorsi, trapezius, and rhomboid major.","C. Iliopsoas, sartorius, and rectus femoris.","D. Erector spinae, multifidus, and quadratus lumborum.","E. Pectoralis major, serratus anterior, and intercostal muscles."],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Rectus abdominis, external oblique, internal oblique, and transversus abdominis","a":"A","why":"<b>Rectus abdominis, external oblique, internal oblique and transversus abdominis.</b> Contracting together, the anterolateral wall muscles compress the abdomen and raise intra-abdominal pressure, which braces the lumbar spine for lifting, coughing and the Valsalva maneuver.<br>B. Latissimus dorsi, trapezius, rhomboid major: move the arm and scapula.<br>C. Iliopsoas, sartorius, rectus femoris: hip and thigh muscles.<br>D. Erector spinae, multifidus, quadratus lumborum: back muscles that extend and steady the spine but do not compress the abdomen.<br>E. Pectoralis major, serratus anterior, intercostals: chest and arm muscles.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 14 (External Oblique)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 15 (Internal OBlique)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 26 (Summary)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice","n":"GI #1 practice questions Fall 2026 (Lecture #1 embryology, Lecture #2 abdominal wall)","sl":null,"f":"G075"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"53"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":18,"label":"Linea alba vs linea semilunaris","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G069-13.jpg","cap":"Linea alba and linea semilunaris","where":"2026 Middletown interactive: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 13","cu":"../sl/G069-13c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G003-17.jpg","cap":"Rectus abdominis, separated by the linea alba","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 17","cu":"../sl/G003-17c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"G069-12-1","s":"What structure separates the rectus abdominis from the lateral abdominal wall musculature?","o":["A. Linea alba","B. Linea semilunaris","C. Arcuate line","D. Tendinous intersections","E. Iliopubic tract"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Linea semilunaris","a":"B","why":"<b>Linea semilunaris.</b> The linea semilunaris is the curved line on each side where the lateral flat muscles become aponeurosis and meet the lateral edge of the rectus sheath.<br>A. Linea alba: the midline seam that separates the right and left rectus sheaths.<br>C. Arcuate line: a horizontal line on the back of the sheath below the umbilicus, where the posterior sheath ends.<br>D. Tendinous intersections: bands across rectus abdominis itself (the six-pack lines).<br>E. Iliopubic tract: a thickening of transversalis fascia along the inguinal ligament.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 17 (Rectus Abdominis and pyramidalis)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Anterior abdominal wall interactive Q&A Fall 2026 (Dr. Connington)","sl":12,"f":"G069"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"54"},{"id":"G069-12-3","s":"What structure separates the right and left rectus sheaths?","o":["A. Linea semilunaris","B. Arcuate line","C. Tendinous intersections","D. Conjoint tendon","E. Linea alba"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Linea alba","a":"E","why":"<b>Linea alba.</b> The linea alba is the midline seam where the aponeuroses of both sides interweave, so it separates the right rectus sheath from the left.<br>A. Linea semilunaris: the curved lateral edge of each rectus, where the flat muscles meet the sheath.<br>B. Arcuate line: the lower edge of the posterior sheath, below the umbilicus.<br>C. Tendinous intersections: bands across each rectus muscle (the six-pack lines).<br>D. Conjoint tendon: the fused internal oblique and transversus aponeuroses at the pubic crest, behind the inguinal canal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 17 (Rectus Abdominis and pyramidalis)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Anterior abdominal wall interactive Q&A Fall 2026 (Dr. Connington)","sl":12,"f":"G069"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"55"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":18,"label":"Rectus sheath: the flat muscles' aponeuroses around rectus","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G003-18.jpg","cap":"Cross sections: rectus sheath wraps rectus abdominis in front and behind","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 18","cu":"../sl/G003-18c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/M5_7.jpg","cap":"In the section above the umbilicus, the rectus sheath wraps rectus abdominis both in front and behind.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.7","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"G069-12-2","s":"What creates the rectus sheath?","o":["A. Transversalis fascia and parietal peritoneum alone","B. The aponeuroses of the lateral abdominal wall muscles (external oblique, internal oblique, transversus abdominis)","C. Camper and Scarpa fascia","D. The thoracolumbar fascia","E. The epimysium of rectus abdominis"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"The aponeuroses of the lateral abdominal wall muscles","a":"B","why":"<b>The aponeuroses of the lateral abdominal wall muscles.</b> The flat tendons of external oblique, internal oblique and transversus abdominis wrap rectus abdominis to form its sheath, then meet in the linea alba. Transversalis fascia lines the sheath from behind, and below the arcuate line it is the only layer behind the muscle.<br>A. Transversalis fascia and parietal peritoneum alone: these lie deep to the sheath.<br>C. Camper and Scarpa fascia: the fatty and membranous layers of the subcutaneous tissue, outside the muscles.<br>D. Thoracolumbar fascia: on the back, where internal oblique and transversus abdominis attach.<br>E. Epimysium: the thin coat of the muscle itself.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 18 (rectus sheath)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 19 (rectus sheath)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Anterior abdominal wall interactive Q&A Fall 2026 (Dr. Connington)","sl":12,"f":"G069"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"56"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":18,"label":"Below arcuate line: rectus lies on transversalis fascia","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G003-19.jpg","cap":"Below arcuate line: no posterior sheath; rectus lies on transversalis fascia","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 19","cu":"../sl/G003-19c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G003-20.jpg","cap":"Sagittal view: arcuate line; lower posterior wall is transversalis fascia only","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 20","cu":"../sl/G003-20c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G078-9.jpg","cap":"Above versus below arcuate line; below, rectus lies on transversalis fascia","where":"2026 TA session: GI week 1 part 2, slide 9","cu":"../sl/G078-9c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/M5_10.jpg","cap":"Sagittal section: below the arcuate line the posterior sheath is gone, so rectus lies on transversalis fascia.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.10","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/M5_15.jpg","cap":"From inside, the posterior rectus sheath ends at the curved arcuate line, leaving only transversalis fascia below.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.15","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F0008-60-1","s":"The inferior border of the rectus sheath posteriorly is called the:","o":["a. Falx inguinalis","b. Inguinal ligament","c. Internal inguinal ring","d. Arcuate line","e. Linea alba"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Arcuate line","a":"D","why":"<b>Arcuate line.</b> It is the curved lower edge of the posterior rectus sheath, about one-third of the way from the umbilicus to the pubic crest. Below it, rectus abdominis lies directly on transversalis fascia.<br>A. Falx inguinalis (conjoint tendon): inserts on the pubic crest.<br>B. Inguinal ligament: folded lower edge of the external oblique aponeurosis.<br>C. Internal inguinal ring: opening in transversalis fascia.<br>E. Linea alba: midline seam between the two recti.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 18 (rectus sheath)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 19 (rectus sheath)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":60,"f":"F0008"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"57"},{"id":"F3903-238-1","s":"During abdominal surgery through the anterior abdominal wall it is important to maintain the integrity of the rectus sheath. Which of the following is in contact with the posterior aspect of the rectus abdominis muscle – below the arcuate line?","o":["A. External oblique","B. Fascia transversalis","C. Inguinal ligament","D. Internal oblique","E. Transverse abdominis"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Fascia transversalis","a":"B","why":"<b>Fascia transversalis.</b> Below the arcuate line, all three flat-muscle aponeuroses pass in front of rectus abdominis. There is no posterior sheath, so the back of the muscle touches transversalis fascia.<br>A. External oblique: its aponeurosis is in front of the rectus.<br>C. Inguinal ligament: in the groin, not behind the rectus.<br>D. Internal oblique: its aponeurosis is in front below the line.<br>E. Transversus abdominis: its aponeurosis is also in front below the line.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 19 (rectus sheath)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Anterior abdominal wall interactive Q&A Fall 2026 (Dr. Connington)","sl":12,"f":"G069"},{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":238,"f":"F3903"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"58"},{"id":"F0008-58-1","s":"An obstetrician decides to do a Caesarean section on a 25-year-old pregnant woman. A transverse suprapubic incision is chosen for that purpose. All of the following abdominal wall layers will be encountered during the incision EXCEPT the:","o":["a. Skin and subcutaneous tissue","b. Anterior rectus sheath","c. Rectus abdominus muscle","d. Posterior rectus sheath","e. Transversalis fascia, extraperitoneal fat, and peritoneum"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Posterior rectus sheath","a":"D","why":"<b>Posterior rectus sheath.</b> A suprapubic incision is below the arcuate line. There all three aponeuroses run in front of the rectus, so there is <b>no posterior sheath</b>. Behind the muscle lie only transversalis fascia, extraperitoneal fat and peritoneum.<br>A. Skin and subcutaneous tissue: always cut.<br>B. Anterior rectus sheath: present and cut.<br>C. Rectus abdominis: met, then split or pulled aside.<br>E. Transversalis fascia, fat and peritoneum: crossed to enter the abdomen.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 19 (rectus sheath)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":58,"f":"F0008"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"59"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":18,"label":"Rectus sheath layers above vs below arcuate line","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G003-19.jpg","cap":"Anterior and posterior sheath layers above and below the arcuate line","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 19","cu":"../sl/G003-19c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/D045-27.jpg","cap":"Sheath layers above versus below arcuate line, transverse sections","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 27"},{"src":"../sl/D042-11.jpg","cap":"Above and below arcuate line: epigastric vessels lie just behind rectus","where":"2025 TA review, slide 11","cu":"../sl/D042-11c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G069-15.jpg","cap":"Rectus sheath above and below the arcuate line (cross-sections)","where":"2026 Middletown interactive: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 15"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-25-1","s":"During an abdominal wall reconstruction, a surgeon points out to the resident that the composition of the rectus sheath changes depending on its position relative to the arcuate line. At a point just below the umbilicus but above the arcuate line, which of the following correctly describes the layers forming the posterior portion of the rectus sheath?","o":["A. External oblique aponeurosis only","B. Internal oblique aponeurosis and transversus abdominis aponeurosis","C. Internal oblique aponeurosis only","D. Transversus abdominis aponeurosis only","E. No posterior rectus sheath is present at this level"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Internal oblique aponeurosis and transversus abdominis aponeurosis","a":"B","why":"<b>Internal oblique and transversus abdominis aponeuroses.</b> Above the arcuate line, the internal oblique aponeurosis splits around the rectus. Its posterior leaflet joins the transversus abdominis aponeurosis to form the posterior sheath.<br>A. External oblique: always in the anterior sheath.<br>C. Internal oblique only: leaves out transversus abdominis.<br>D. Transversus abdominis only: leaves out the internal oblique leaflet.<br>E. No posterior sheath: true only below the arcuate line.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 19 (rectus sheath)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Anterior abdominal wall interactive Q&A Fall 2026 (Dr. Connington)","sl":12,"f":"G069"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":25,"f":"D045"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"60"},{"id":"G069-17-1","s":"During abdominal surgery through the anterior abdominal wall it is important to maintain the integrity of the rectus sheath. Which of the following is in contact with the posterior aspect of the rectus abdominis muscle – superior to the arcuate line?","o":["A. External oblique aponeurosis","B. Fascia transversalis","C. Inguinal ligament","D. Internal oblique aponeurosis","E. Transverse abdominis aponeurosis"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Internal oblique aponeurosis","a":"D","why":"<b>Internal oblique aponeurosis.</b> Above the arcuate line, the internal oblique aponeurosis splits around rectus abdominis. Its posterior layer, backed by the transversus abdominis aponeurosis, forms the posterior rectus sheath, so the back of the muscle touches the internal oblique aponeurosis. Below the arcuate line every aponeurosis passes in front, and the muscle lies on transversalis fascia instead.<br>A. External oblique aponeurosis: always in front of the rectus (anterior sheath).<br>B. Fascia transversalis: touches the muscle only below the arcuate line; above it, it lies deep to the posterior sheath.<br>C. Inguinal ligament: the rolled lower edge of the external oblique aponeurosis in the groin; not part of the sheath.<br>E. Transverse abdominis aponeurosis: part of the posterior sheath above the line, but it lies just deep to the internal oblique layer, not on the muscle.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 19 (rectus sheath)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 20 (rectus sheath)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Anterior abdominal wall interactive Q&A Fall 2026 (Dr. Connington)","sl":17,"f":"G069"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"61"},{"id":"G075-17-1","s":"A 48-year-old woman undergoes laparoscopic abdominal surgery. During the procedure, the surgeon identifies a difference in the arrangement of the abdominal wall layers above and below a specific anatomical landmark. Below this landmark, the rectus abdominis muscle no longer has a posterior aponeurotic covering from the three flat abdominal muscles. Instead, it rests directly on the transversalis fascia. Which of the following best explains the anatomical arrangement below this landmark?","o":["A. The aponeuroses of the external oblique, internal oblique, and transversus abdominis muscles pass anterior to the rectus abdominis.","B. The aponeuroses of the three flat abdominal muscles pass posterior to the rectus abdominis.","C. The internal oblique aponeurosis splits to form anterior and posterior layers around the rectus abdominis.","D. The transversalis fascia divides to form the anterior and posterior walls of the rectus sheath.","E. The external oblique aponeurosis passes posterior to the rectus abdominis to form the posterior sheath."],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"The aponeuroses of the external oblique, internal oblique, and transversus abdominis muscles pass anterior to the rectus abdominis","a":"A","why":"<b>The aponeuroses of the external oblique, internal oblique and transversus abdominis pass anterior to the rectus.</b> Below the arcuate line, about one-third of the way from the umbilicus to the pubis, all three aponeuroses form the anterior sheath, so the muscle lies directly on transversalis fascia.<br>B. Reversed: none passes behind the muscle there.<br>C. The internal oblique aponeurosis splits around the rectus only above the arcuate line.<br>D. Transversalis fascia does not split; it lines the back of the muscle.<br>E. The external oblique aponeurosis is always in front of the rectus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 19 (rectus sheath)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Anterior abdominal wall interactive Q&A Fall 2026 (Dr. Connington)","sl":12,"f":"G069"},{"t":"2026 faculty practice","n":"GI #1 practice questions Fall 2026 (Lecture #1 embryology, Lecture #2 abdominal wall)","sl":null,"f":"G075"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"62"},{"id":"G069-12-4","s":"Above the arcuate line, which layers form the anterior layer of the rectus sheath?","o":["A. Aponeurosis of the external oblique only","B. Aponeurosis of the external oblique and the anterior leaflet of the internal oblique aponeurosis","C. Aponeuroses of the external oblique, internal oblique and transversus abdominis","D. Posterior leaflet of the internal oblique aponeurosis and the transversus abdominis aponeurosis","E. Transversalis fascia"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Aponeurosis of the external oblique and the anterior leaflet of the internal oblique aponeurosis","a":"B","why":"<b>External oblique aponeurosis and the anterior leaflet of the internal oblique aponeurosis.</b> Above the arcuate line the internal oblique aponeurosis splits around rectus; its front leaflet joins the external oblique aponeurosis in front of the muscle.<br>A. External oblique only: leaves out the front leaflet of the internal oblique.<br>C. All three aponeuroses: the anterior layer below the arcuate line.<br>D. Back leaflet of the internal oblique + transversus aponeurosis: the posterior layer above the arcuate line.<br>E. Transversalis fascia: lies behind the sheath, and is all that backs rectus below the arcuate line.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 19 (rectus sheath)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Anterior abdominal wall interactive Q&A Fall 2026 (Dr. Connington)","sl":12,"f":"G069"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"63"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":19,"label":"Inferior epigastric: behind rectus, enters at arcuate line","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G003-20.jpg","cap":"Inferior epigastric artery rising behind rectus to the arcuate line","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 20","cu":"../sl/G003-20c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/D042-11.jpg","cap":"Epigastric vessels just deep to rectus, above and below arcuate line","where":"2025 TA review, slide 11","cu":"../sl/D042-11c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G073-33.jpg","cap":"Inferior epigastric artery behind rectus; posterior sheath ends at the arcuate line","where":"2026 Harlem clickers: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 33"}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-232-1","s":"A transverse incision through the rectus abdominis muscle and both layers of the rectus sheath at the arcuate line would transect the:","o":["A) inferior epigastric artery.","B) thoracoepigastric vein.","C) first lumbar artery.","D) ilioinguinal nerve.","E) T12 intercostal nerve."],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"inferior epigastric artery","a":"A","why":"<b>Inferior epigastric artery.</b> It runs vertically behind rectus abdominis and enters the rectus sheath at the arcuate line. A transverse cut through the muscle and both sheath layers there crosses it.<br>B. Thoracoepigastric vein: in the subcutaneous fat of the lateral wall.<br>C. First lumbar artery: on the posterior wall.<br>D. Ilioinguinal nerve: lower, in the inguinal canal.<br>E. T12 (twelfth thoracic) is the subcostal nerve; wall nerves run roughly sideways, parallel to a transverse cut.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 20 (rectus sheath)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 18 (rectus sheath)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Harlem clicker","n":"Harlem anterior abdominal wall & inguinal canal clickers 11/5/26","sl":32,"f":"G073"},{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":232,"f":"F3903"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"64"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":19,"label":"Superior and inferior epigastric anastomose in rectus sheath","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G003-20.jpg","cap":"Superior epigastric (internal thoracic) meets inferior epigastric (external iliac) behind rectus","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 20","cu":"../sl/G003-20c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G003-21.jpg","cap":"Abdominal wall arteries: epigastric pair joining along the rectus","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 21","cu":"../sl/G003-21c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G078-16.jpg","cap":"Superior and inferior epigastric arteries joining behind rectus abdominis","where":"2026 TA session: GI week 1 part 2, slide 16"},{"src":"../tb/M5_14.jpg","cap":"Superior epigastric (from internal thoracic) and inferior epigastric (from external iliac) meet inside rectus abdominis.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.14","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F0008-62-1","s":"The internal thoracic artery is sometimes surgically cut near the caudal end of the sternum and used to supply blood to a region of the heart. In these cases, maintenance of adequate blood flow to the rectus abdominis may be dependent on increased flow through which artery?","o":["a. Superficial circumflex iliac","b. Superficial epigastric","c. Umbilical","d. Inferior epigastric","e. Deep circumflex iliac"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Inferior epigastric","a":"D","why":"<b>Inferior epigastric.</b> Cutting the internal thoracic artery cuts off the parent artery of the superior epigastric artery. The rectus then relies on the inferior epigastric artery (from the external iliac), which feeds the superior epigastric through their anastomosis in the rectus sheath.<br>A. Superficial circumflex iliac: subcutaneous, toward the hip bone.<br>B. Superficial epigastric: subcutaneous lower wall.<br>C. Umbilical: pelvic artery whose far end is obliterated.<br>E. Deep circumflex iliac: lateral wall along the iliac crest.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 18 (rectus sheath)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 20 (rectus sheath)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 21 (vasculature)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":62,"f":"F0008"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"65"},{"id":"G075-16-1","s":"A 65-year-old man undergoes abdominal surgery and develops reduced blood flow through the external iliac artery due to vascular injury. The surgeon explains that an arterial connection between branches of the internal thoracic artery and external iliac artery may help maintain blood supply to the anterior abdominal wall. Which of the following arteries form this important anastomosis?","o":["A. Superior and inferior epigastric arteries.","B. Superior and deep circumflex iliac arteries.","C. Inferior epigastric and superficial epigastric arteries.","D. Superior epigastric and superficial circumflex iliac arteries.","E. Deep circumflex iliac and superior epigastric arteries."],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Superior and inferior epigastric arteries","a":"A","why":"<b>Superior and inferior epigastric arteries.</b> The superior epigastric (end branch of the internal thoracic) and the inferior epigastric (from the external iliac) meet inside the rectus sheath, behind rectus abdominis, because the posterior sheath ends at the arcuate line.<br>B. Superior and deep circumflex iliac: the circumflex iliac arteries run along the iliac crest, not in the rectus sheath.<br>C. Superficial epigastric: subcutaneous, from the femoral artery.<br>D. Superficial circumflex iliac: subcutaneous, toward the hip bone.<br>E. Deep circumflex iliac: links with the musculophrenic in the lateral wall, not with the superior epigastric.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 20 (rectus sheath)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 18 (rectus sheath)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 21 (vasculature)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice","n":"GI #1 practice questions Fall 2026 (Lecture #1 embryology, Lecture #2 abdominal wall)","sl":null,"f":"G075"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"66"},{"id":"G069-12-8","s":"What is the importance of the arcuate line?","o":["A. It marks the lateral border of the rectus abdominis","B. It is where the deep inguinal ring opens through the transversalis fascia","C. It is the midline seam where the lateral abdominal wall aponeuroses insert","D. Below it the posterior rectus sheath is absent, which lets the inferior epigastric vessels enter the sheath and anastomose with the superior epigastric vessels","E. It marks the border between the T10 and L1 dermatomes"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"It allows the superior and inferior epigastric arteries to anastomose","a":"D","why":"<b>Below it the posterior sheath is absent, so the inferior epigastric vessels enter the sheath and anastomose with the superior epigastric vessels.</b> The arcuate line is the lower edge of the posterior sheath. The inferior epigastric artery slips into the sheath there and meets the superior epigastric artery behind rectus abdominis.<br>A. Lateral border of rectus: the linea semilunaris.<br>B. Deep inguinal ring: an opening in the transversalis fascia above the middle of the inguinal ligament.<br>C. Midline seam: the linea alba.<br>E. Dermatomes: T10 is the umbilicus and L1 the groin; the arcuate line marks neither border.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 18 (rectus sheath)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 20 (rectus sheath)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Anterior abdominal wall interactive Q&A Fall 2026 (Dr. Connington)","sl":12,"f":"G069"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"67"},{"id":"G081-9-2","s":"The superior epigastric artery (from the internal thoracic artery) anastomoses inside the rectus sheath with which artery?","o":["A. Deep circumflex iliac artery","B. Musculophrenic artery","C. Inferior epigastric artery","D. Superficial epigastric artery","E. Subcostal artery"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Inferior epigastric artery","a":"C","why":"<b>Inferior epigastric artery.</b> The superior epigastric artery, an end branch of the internal thoracic, runs down inside the rectus sheath and meets the inferior epigastric artery coming up from the external iliac. This links the subclavian and external iliac arteries.<br>A. Deep circumflex iliac: runs along the iliac crest and meets the musculophrenic artery in the lateral wall.<br>B. Musculophrenic: the other end branch of the internal thoracic, along the costal margin.<br>D. Superficial epigastric: stays in the subcutaneous fat.<br>E. Subcostal: from the aorta, runs in the lateral wall below the 12th rib.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 18 (rectus sheath)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 20 (rectus sheath)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 21 (vasculature)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive 11/11/26 (J. LaBarge)","sl":9,"f":"G081"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"68"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":20,"label":"Lymph: above umbilicus axillary, below superficial inguinal","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G003-23.jpg","cap":"Above umbilicus drains to axillary nodes; below to superficial inguinal nodes","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 23","cu":"../sl/G003-23c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G078-23.jpg","cap":"Lymph follows the blood supply; the umbilicus divides upper and lower drainage","where":"2026 TA session: GI week 1 part 2, slide 23","cu":"../sl/G078-23c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"F0008-64-1","s":"The normal pattern of venous and lymphatic drainage of the superficial tissues of the anterior abdominal wall is arranged around a horizontal plane. Above that plane, drainage is in a cranial direction; below the plane drainage is in a caudal direction. This reference plane corresponds to:","o":["a. Level of umbilicus","b. Level of anterior superior iliac spines","c. Transtubercular line","d. Level of arcuate line","e. Transpyloric plane"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Level of umbilicus","a":"A","why":"<b>Level of the umbilicus.</b> Superficial veins and lymph above the umbilicus run up toward the axilla. Below it they run down toward the femoral vein and superficial inguinal nodes.<br>B. ASIS (anterior superior iliac spine) level: too low.<br>C. Transtubercular line: through the iliac tubercles at L5 (fifth lumbar); too low.<br>D. Arcuate line: rectus sheath landmark below the umbilicus.<br>E. Transpyloric plane: L1 (first lumbar); too high.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 23 (Lymphatics)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 21 (vasculature)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":64,"f":"F0008"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"69"},{"id":"G075-19-1","s":"A 46-year-old woman presents to her physician with a small, infected wound on the anterior abdominal wall just inferior to the umbilicus. During the physical examination, the physician evaluates the regional lymph nodes to determine whether the infection has spread through the lymphatic system. Which of the following lymph node groups would most likely receive lymphatic drainage from the affected area?","o":["A. Axillary lymph nodes.","B. Superficial inguinal lymph nodes.","C. Parasternal lymph nodes.","D. Deep cervical lymph nodes.","E. Popliteal lymph nodes."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Superficial inguinal lymph nodes","a":"B","why":"<b>Superficial inguinal lymph nodes.</b> Skin below the umbilicus drains downward with the superficial epigastric and superficial circumflex iliac vessels, branches of the femoral artery, to the superficial inguinal nodes.<br>A. Axillary nodes: skin above the umbilicus.<br>C. Parasternal nodes: lie along the internal thoracic vessels and take some lymph from the upper wall, none from below the umbilicus.<br>D. Deep cervical nodes: head and neck.<br>E. Popliteal nodes: leg and foot.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 23 (Lymphatics)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice","n":"GI #1 practice questions Fall 2026 (Lecture #1 embryology, Lecture #2 abdominal wall)","sl":null,"f":"G075"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"70"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":20,"label":"Paraumbilical veins → caput medusae","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G078-17.jpg","cap":"Caput medusae: portal blood backs up through paraumbilical veins into wall veins","where":"2026 TA session: GI week 1 part 2, slide 17","cu":"../sl/G078-17c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G003-21.jpg","cap":"Paraumbilical veins drain to the hepatic portal vein; blockage gives caput medusae","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 21","cu":"../sl/G003-21c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"G080-8-1","s":"A 55-year-old patient complains of abdominal pain. The patient has a history of heavy alcohol consumption. Upon physical examination a series of protruding structures are found around the patient’s umbilicus. What condition is this patient experiencing?","o":["A. Umbilical hernia","B. Inguinal hernia","C. Femoral hernia","D. Caput medusae","E. Appendisidus"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Caput medusae","a":"D","why":"<b>Caput medusae.</b> Heavy drinking points to cirrhosis and portal hypertension. Portal blood backs up through the paraumbilical veins into the superficial wall veins, which swell and radiate from the umbilicus.<br>A. Umbilical hernia: one soft bulge through the navel, not a ring of veins.<br>B. Inguinal hernia: a groin bulge above the inguinal ligament.<br>C. Femoral hernia: a groin bulge below the inguinal ligament.<br>E. Appendisidus: not a real diagnosis; appendicitis gives right lower quadrant pain, not veins around the umbilicus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 21 (vasculature)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Montana interactive","n":"Montana (Great Falls) anterior abdominal wall, inguinal canal & scrotum interactive 2026 (D. Lawver)","sl":8,"f":"G080"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"71"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":20,"label":"Para- means next to","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G003-52.jpg","cap":"Para-aortic nodes sit next to the aorta and receive testis lymph","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 52","cu":"../sl/G003-52c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"G069-7-5","s":"What does the prefix para- mean, as in the paraumbilical region or the para-aortic lymph nodes?","o":["A. Around","B. Below","C. Through","D. Next to","E. Above"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Next to","a":"D","why":"<b>Next to.</b> Para- means beside: the paraumbilical veins run next to the umbilicus, along the round ligament of the liver, and the para-aortic nodes lie beside the aorta.<br>A. Around: peri-, as in pericardium.<br>B. Below: sub- or infra-, as in subcostal.<br>C. Through or across: trans-, as in the transumbilical plane.<br>E. Above: supra-, as in suprapubic.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 21 (vasculature)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 52 (Lymphatics)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Anterior abdominal wall interactive Q&A Fall 2026 (Dr. Connington)","sl":7,"f":"G069"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"72"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":21,"label":"Wall nerves run between internal oblique and transversus","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G003-16.jpg","cap":"Neurovascular bundles lie between internal oblique and transversus abdominis","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 16","cu":"../sl/G003-16c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G003-20.jpg","cap":"Thoracoabdominal nerves crossing the lateral wall to the rectus sheath","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 20","cu":"../sl/G003-20c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G073-9.jpg","cap":"Thoracoabdominal, subcostal and L1 nerves crossing between internal oblique and transversus","where":"2026 Harlem clickers: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 9","cu":"../sl/G073-9c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G073-10.jpg","cap":"Transversus abdominis: neurovascular bundles lie between it and the internal oblique","where":"2026 Harlem clickers: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 10","cu":"../sl/G073-10c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/M5_10.jpg","cap":"Left side: with internal oblique cut open, thoracoabdominal nerves run on transversus abdominis, just under internal oblique.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.10","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/M5_12.jpg","cap":"In the CT, the nerve path curves between internal oblique and transversus abdominis toward rectus.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.12","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-224-1","s":"The nerve supply to the muscles of the anterior abdominal wall:","o":["A) pierces the peritoneum immediately prior to entering the deep surface of the muscle.","B) is derived from the sympathetic trunk.","C) travels between the internal oblique and transverse abdominis muscles.","D) also innervates the quadriceps femoris.","E) is derived from sacral ventral rami."],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"travels between the internal oblique and transverse abdominis muscles","a":"C","why":"<b>Travels between the internal oblique and transversus abdominis.</b> The lower thoracic (thoracoabdominal and subcostal) nerves and the L1 (first lumbar) nerves run in this plane, just as intercostal nerves run between the inner muscle layers of the chest.<br>A. They stay outside the peritoneum.<br>B. They are spinal nerves, not sympathetic trunk branches.<br>D. Quadriceps is supplied by the femoral nerve (L2–L4, second to fourth lumbar).<br>E. They come from thoracic and first lumbar ventral rami, not sacral.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 16 (Transversus Abdominis)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 22 (Neurovasculature)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Harlem clicker","n":"Harlem anterior abdominal wall & inguinal canal clickers 11/5/26","sl":8,"f":"G073"},{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":224,"f":"F3903"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"73"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":21,"label":"Wall nerves: thoracoabdominal (T7–T11), subcostal (T12)","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G069-8.jpg","cap":"Cutaneous nerves: thoracoabdominal T7–T11, subcostal T12, iliohypogastric and ilioinguinal L1","where":"2026 Middletown interactive: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 8"},{"src":"../sl/G003-14.jpg","cap":"External oblique: innervated by T7–T12","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 14","cu":"../sl/G003-14c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"G069-7-2","s":"What is another name for the 7th–11th intercostal nerves, which run on from the chest into the anterior abdominal wall?","o":["A. Subcostal nerves","B. Thoracoabdominal nerves","C. Iliohypogastric nerves","D. Intercostobrachial nerves","E. Splanchnic nerves"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Thoracoabdominal nerves","a":"B","why":"<b>Thoracoabdominal nerves.</b> The 7th–11th intercostal nerves leave their intercostal spaces at the costal margin and run on into the abdominal wall, between internal oblique and transversus abdominis. They serve both the chest and the abdomen, hence the name.<br>A. Subcostal nerve: only one on each side, the anterior ramus of T12, below the 12th rib.<br>C. Iliohypogastric nerves: from L1, to the skin above the pubis.<br>D. Intercostobrachial nerves: a branch of the 2nd intercostal nerve to the armpit and arm.<br>E. Splanchnic nerves: autonomic nerves to the gut, not to the body wall.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 14 (External Oblique)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 22 (Neurovasculature)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Anterior abdominal wall interactive Q&A Fall 2026 (Dr. Connington)","sl":7,"f":"G069"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"74"},{"id":"G069-7-3","s":"What is another name for the anterior ramus of the T12 spinal nerve?","o":["A. Iliohypogastric nerve","B. Ilioinguinal nerve","C. Subcostal nerve","D. Genitofemoral nerve","E. 11th intercostal nerve"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Subcostal nerve","a":"C","why":"<b>Subcostal nerve.</b> The anterior ramus of T12 runs below the 12th rib, so it is called the subcostal nerve. It supplies the band of abdominal wall just above the L1 area.<br>A. Iliohypogastric nerve: from L1, to the skin above the pubis.<br>B. Ilioinguinal nerve: from L1, through the inguinal canal.<br>D. Genitofemoral nerve: from L1–L2; supplies the cremaster and the front of the thigh.<br>E. 11th intercostal nerve: the anterior ramus of T11, the last thoracoabdominal nerve.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 14 (External Oblique)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 22 (Neurovasculature)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Anterior abdominal wall interactive Q&A Fall 2026 (Dr. Connington)","sl":7,"f":"G069"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"75"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":21,"label":"Dermatomes: T7 xiphoid, T10 umbilicus, L1 groin","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G003-22.jpg","cap":"Dermatomes: T7 xiphoid, T10 umbilicus, L1 iliohypogastric above pubis and groin","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 22","cu":"../sl/G003-22c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G069-9.jpg","cap":"Dermatomes: T4 nipple, T10 umbilicus, L1 inguinal region","where":"2026 Middletown interactive: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 9","cu":"../sl/G069-9c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G078-29.jpg","cap":"Dermatome landmarks: T7 xiphoid, T10 umbilicus, L1 inguinal ligament and groin","where":"2026 TA session: GI week 1 part 2, slide 29","cu":"../sl/G078-29c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-236-1","s":"A 35-year-old woman has acute pain, localized around the umbilical region. The dermatome level involved in the origination of this pain is:","o":["A. T7","B. T8","C. T10","D. L1","E. S1"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"T10","a":"C","why":"<b>T10 (tenth thoracic).</b> The umbilicus lies in the T10 dermatome (the skin strip supplied by one spinal nerve). Midgut pain is referred to the umbilical region at this level.<br>A. T7 (seventh thoracic): xiphoid process.<br>B. T8 (eighth thoracic): upper abdomen, above the umbilicus.<br>D. L1 (first lumbar): groin and pubic region.<br>E. S1 (first sacral): lateral foot and sole.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 22 (Neurovasculature)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":236,"f":"F3903"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"76"},{"id":"G069-10-1","s":"A 42-year-old female is prepared for a laparoscopic cholecystectomy (gallbladder removal). The paraumbilical region needs to be anesthetized. Which of the following dermatomes is involved in the area of anesthesia?","o":["A. L1","B. L2","C. T8","D. T10","E. T12"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"T10","a":"D","why":"<b>T10.</b> The T10 dermatome is the band of skin around the umbilicus, so the paraumbilical region is T10.<br>A. L1: the groin (inguinal region).<br>B. L2: the front of the upper thigh.<br>C. T8: above the umbilicus, in the epigastric region (T7 is at the xiphoid process).<br>E. T12: just above the pubic bone (suprapubic region).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 22 (Neurovasculature)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Anterior abdominal wall interactive Q&A Fall 2026 (Dr. Connington)","sl":10,"f":"G069"},{"t":"2026 faculty practice","n":"GI #1 practice questions Fall 2026 (Lecture #1 embryology, Lecture #2 abdominal wall)","sl":null,"f":"G075"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"77"},{"id":"G069-7-1","s":"Which spinal nerves provide cutaneous (skin) innervation to the anterior abdominal wall?","o":["A. T1–T6","B. T4–T10","C. T7–L1","D. T10–L3","E. L1–L4"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"T7–L1","a":"C","why":"<b>T7–L1.</b> The anterior rami of T7–T11 (thoracoabdominal nerves), T12 (subcostal nerve) and L1 (iliohypogastric and ilioinguinal nerves) supply the skin of the anterior abdominal wall: T7 at the xiphoid process, T10 at the umbilicus, L1 at the groin. (The wall muscles get T5–L1; the skin range is T7–L1.)<br>A. T1–T6: the upper chest and arm.<br>B. T4–T10: from the nipple (T4) down only to the umbilicus; misses the lower abdomen.<br>D. T10–L3: misses the upper abdomen; L2–L3 supply the thigh.<br>E. L1–L4: the groin and lower limb.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 22 (Neurovasculature)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 14 (External Oblique)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Anterior abdominal wall interactive Q&A Fall 2026 (Dr. Connington)","sl":7,"f":"G069"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"78"},{"id":"G069-7-6","s":"Which set correctly pairs key dermatomes of the trunk with their surface landmarks?","o":["A. T2 – nipple; T8 – umbilicus; T12 – inguinal region","B. T4 – nipple; T12 – umbilicus; L3 – inguinal region","C. T4 – nipple; T10 – umbilicus; L1 – inguinal region","D. T6 – nipple; T10 – umbilicus; L4 – inguinal region","E. T4 – xiphoid process; T7 – umbilicus; L1 – inguinal region"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"T4 – nipple; T10 – umbilicus; L1 – inguinal region","a":"C","why":"<b>T4 – nipple; T10 – umbilicus; L1 – inguinal region.</b> These are the anchor dermatomes of the trunk, with T7 at the xiphoid process between the nipple and the umbilicus.<br>A. T2 lies above the nipple, T8 above the umbilicus, and T12 just above the groin.<br>B. T12 lies below the umbilicus; L3 is on the thigh.<br>D. T6 lies below the nipple, near the xiphoid; L4 is on the leg.<br>E. The xiphoid is T7 and the umbilicus T10, not T4 and T7.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 22 (Neurovasculature)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Anterior abdominal wall interactive Q&A Fall 2026 (Dr. Connington)","sl":7,"f":"G069"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"79"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":21,"label":"Trunk dermatomes even bands; limb ones irregular","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G080-10.jpg","cap":"Trunk dermatomes run as even bands; arm dermatomes are stretched and irregular","where":"2026 Montana interactive, slide 10"}],"q":[{"id":"G069-7-4","s":"How do the dermatomes of the thorax differ in shape from the dermatomes of the upper limb?","o":["A. Thoracic dermatomes are irregular; upper limb dermatomes are uniform horizontal bands","B. Thoracic dermatomes are fairly uniform horizontal bands; upper limb dermatomes are irregular because they are stretched out along the arm and digits","C. Thoracic dermatomes run vertically; upper limb dermatomes run horizontally around the arm","D. Both are uniform horizontal bands of the same width","E. Thoracic dermatomes cross the midline to the opposite side; upper limb dermatomes do not"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Thorax: fairly uniform bands; upper limb: irregular (stretched along the arm and digits)","a":"B","why":"<b>Thoracic dermatomes are fairly uniform horizontal bands; upper limb dermatomes are irregular.</b> On the trunk each spinal nerve supplies an even band that wraps from the back around to the front. In the limb the bands were stretched out along the arm and digits as the limb grew, so their shapes are irregular.<br>A. Reversed: the trunk bands are the regular ones.<br>C. Trunk bands run horizontally around the body, not vertically.<br>D. Limb dermatomes are not even bands.<br>E. Neither crosses the midline; a shingles band stops there.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 22 (Neurovasculature)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Anterior abdominal wall interactive Q&A Fall 2026 (Dr. Connington)","sl":7,"f":"G069"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"80"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":22,"label":"Hesselbach triangle borders; direct hernias medial","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G003-25.jpg","cap":"Posterior view: Hesselbach triangle medial to inferior epigastric vessels; deep ring lateral","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 25","cu":"../sl/G003-25c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/D045-73.jpg","cap":"Hesselbach triangle bounded by rectus, inferior epigastric vessels, inguinal ligament","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 73"},{"src":"../sl/G003-37.jpg","cap":"Direct hernia medial to inferior epigastric vessels; indirect lateral via deep ring","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 37","cu":"../sl/G003-37c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G073-24.jpg","cap":"Hesselbach triangle: inferior epigastric vessels, inguinal ligament, rectus edge","where":"2026 Harlem clickers: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 24"}],"q":[{"id":"G075-23-1","s":"A 62-year-old man presents to the clinic with a bulge in his right groin that becomes more noticeable when coughing or lifting heavy objects. The surgeon suspects a direct inguinal hernia and explains that abdominal contents are protruding through a weakened region of the posterior abdominal wall known as Hesselbach's triangle. During surgical repair, the medical student is asked to identify the triangle's anatomical boundaries. Which of the following correctly identifies the boundaries of Hesselbach's triangle?","o":["A. Rectus abdominis medially, inferior epigastric vessels laterally, and inguinal ligament inferiorly.","B. External oblique medially, femoral artery laterally, and inguinal ligament superiorly.","C. Rectus abdominis laterally, inferior epigastric vessels medially, and inguinal ligament inferiorly.","D. Internal oblique medially, spermatic cord laterally, and inguinal ligament inferiorly.","E. Rectus abdominis medially, deep inguinal ring laterally, and linea alba inferiorly."],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Rectus abdominis medially, inferior epigastric vessels laterally, and inguinal ligament inferiorly","a":"A","why":"<b>Rectus abdominis medially, inferior epigastric vessels laterally, inguinal ligament inferiorly.</b> These are the borders of Hesselbach (inguinal) triangle, the weak area through which direct hernias push.<br>B. External oblique and femoral artery are not borders, and the inguinal ligament is inferior, not superior.<br>C. Medial and lateral are swapped.<br>D. Internal oblique and spermatic cord are not borders.<br>E. The deep ring lies lateral to the triangle, and the linea alba is the midline seam.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 36 (Deep Inguinal ring)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 25 (Internal Surface of Abdominal Wall)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Anterior abdominal wall interactive Q&A Fall 2026 (Dr. Connington)","sl":29,"f":"G069"},{"t":"2026 faculty practice","n":"GI #1 practice questions Fall 2026 (Lecture #1 embryology, Lecture #2 abdominal wall)","sl":null,"f":"G075"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"81"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":22,"label":"Umbilical folds: urachus, umbilical arteries, epigastric vessels","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G003-25.jpg","cap":"Five folds: median (urachus), medial (umbilical arteries), lateral (inferior epigastric)","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 25","cu":"../sl/G003-25c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G003-18.jpg","cap":"Below-arcuate section: median umbilical ligament (urachus) and medial umbilical fold","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 18","cu":"../sl/G003-18c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/M5_15.jpg","cap":"Median fold covers the urachus, medial folds the umbilical arteries, lateral folds the inferior epigastric vessels; medial inguinal fossa lies between.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.15","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/N249.jpg","cap":"Labels name each fold's contents: obliterated urachus in the median fold, obliterated umbilical artery in the medial folds.","where":"Netter Atlas 6e, Plate 249","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F0008-72-1","s":"During a laproscopic examination of the deep surface of the lower anterior abdominal wall (using a lighted scope on a thin tube inserted through the wall), the attending physician noted something of interest and asked the young resident to look at the medial inguinal fossa. To do so, the young doctor would have to look at the area between the:","o":["a. obliterated umbilical artery and inferior epigastric artery","b. obliterated umbilical artery and urachus","c. inferior epigastric artery and lateral umbilical fold","d. inferior epigastric artery and urachus","e. median umbilical ligament and medial umbilical ligament"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"obliterated umbilical artery and inferior epigastric artery","a":"A","why":"<b>Between the obliterated umbilical artery and the inferior epigastric artery.</b> The medial inguinal fossa (Hesselbach triangle area) lies between the medial umbilical fold and the lateral umbilical fold. Direct hernias come through it.<br>B. Umbilical artery and urachus: the supravesical fossa.<br>C. The inferior epigastric artery is inside the lateral fold; same structure.<br>D. Inferior epigastric and urachus: spans two fossae.<br>E. Median and medial umbilical ligaments: the supravesical fossa.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 25 (Internal Surface of Abdominal Wall)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":72,"f":"F0008"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"82"},{"id":"G069-29-1","s":"Which structure on the internal surface of the anterior abdominal wall is used to tell a direct from an indirect inguinal hernia?","o":["A. Median umbilical fold (urachus)","B. Lateral umbilical fold (inferior epigastric vessels)","C. Medial umbilical fold (obliterated umbilical artery)","D. Linea semilunaris","E. Arcuate line"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Lateral umbilical fold (inferior epigastric vessels)","a":"B","why":"<b>Lateral umbilical fold (inferior epigastric vessels).</b> The lateral fold covers the inferior epigastric vessels. A hernia medial to it, through Hesselbach triangle, is direct; one lateral to it, through the deep ring, is indirect.<br>A. Median umbilical fold: covers the urachus in the midline.<br>C. Medial umbilical fold: covers the obliterated umbilical artery, on the medial side of Hesselbach triangle.<br>D. Linea semilunaris: the lateral edge of rectus, a surface line.<br>E. Arcuate line: the lower edge of the posterior rectus sheath, well above the groin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 25 (Internal Surface of Abdominal Wall)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 26 (Summary)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Anterior abdominal wall interactive Q&A Fall 2026 (Dr. Connington)","sl":29,"f":"G069"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"83"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":23,"label":"Deep ring sits above the mid-inguinal point","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G003-30.jpg","cap":"Midclavicular line crossing the inguinal ligament marks the deep inguinal ring","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 30","cu":"../sl/G003-30c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G003-6.jpg","cap":"Midclavicular line meets the inguinal line at the midinguinal point","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 6","cu":"../sl/G003-6c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G069-22.jpg","cap":"Direct vs indirect hernia; pressure over the deep ring","where":"2026 Middletown interactive: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 22"},{"src":"../tb/G4_43.jpg","cap":"The deep ring sits just above the middle of the inguinal ligament, right over the femoral artery.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 4.43","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-242-1","s":"A patient comes into the clinic with a bulge in the groin, which his physician diagnosed as an inguinal hernia. To determine whether it is an indirect or direct inguinal hernia, the physician applies pressure over the deep inguinal ring, with his left thumb. The patient is then asked to cough. The physician notes that the hernia does not push out of the abdomen and concludes that it is an indirect inguinal hernia. Where did the physician apply pressure?","o":["A. Immediately lateral to the pubic tubercle","B. Immediately lateral to the lacunar ligament","C. Half an inch above the mid inguinal point","D. One inch below the mid inguinal ligament","E. Immediately medial to the femoral vein"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Half an inch above the mid inguinal point","a":"C","why":"<b>Half an inch above the mid-inguinal point.</b> The deep inguinal ring lies just above the middle of the inguinal ligament, where the midclavicular line crosses it. Pressing there blocks an indirect hernia, which starts at the deep ring.<br>A. Just lateral to the pubic tubercle: the superficial ring.<br>B. Lateral to the lacunar ligament: the femoral ring.<br>D. Below the ligament: the thigh.<br>E. Medial to the femoral vein: the femoral canal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 30 (Inguinal ligament)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 36 (Deep Inguinal ring)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Anterior abdominal wall interactive Q&A Fall 2026 (Dr. Connington)","sl":20,"f":"G069"},{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":242,"f":"F3903"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"84"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":24,"label":"Deep ring: hole in transversalis fascia","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G003-31.jpg","cap":"Internal spermatic fascia starts from transversalis fascia at the deep ring","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 31","cu":"../sl/G003-31c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/D045-58.jpg","cap":"Deep ring in transversalis fascia; superficial ring in external oblique aponeurosis","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 58"},{"src":"../sl/G073-16.jpg","cap":"Deep inguinal ring in transversalis fascia, beside the inferior epigastric vessels","where":"2026 Harlem clickers: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 16"},{"src":"../sl/G073-17.jpg","cap":"Canal walls; the deep ring is the opening in transversalis fascia","where":"2026 Harlem clickers: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 17","cu":"../sl/G073-17c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/G4_43.jpg","cap":"The deep ring is a hole in transversalis fascia, just lateral to the inferior epigastric artery.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 4.43","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/M5_17.jpg","cap":"At the deep ring transversalis fascia becomes internal spermatic fascia; the superficial ring is an opening in external oblique aponeurosis.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.17","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-226-1","s":"Which of the following is incorrect pertaining to the deep (internal) inguinal ring?","o":["A) It is an opening in the peritoneum.","B) It is located lateral to the inferior epigastric artery.","C) It transmits the spermatic cord in males.","D) It transmits the round ligament of the uterus in females.","E) It forms the deep entrance to the inguinal canal."],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"It is an opening in the peritoneum (it is an opening in the transversalis fascia)","a":"A","why":"<b>A is the false statement.</b> The deep ring is an opening in the <b>transversalis fascia</b>, not in the peritoneum. Peritoneum covers it from inside.<br>B. True: it lies lateral to the inferior epigastric artery.<br>C. True: the spermatic cord enters here in males.<br>D. True: the round ligament enters here in females.<br>E. True: it is the inner (deep) entrance of the canal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 36 (Deep Inguinal ring)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 29 (Intro to Inguinal region/recap)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Harlem clicker","n":"Harlem anterior abdominal wall & inguinal canal clickers 11/5/26","sl":15,"f":"G073"},{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":226,"f":"F3903"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"85"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":24,"label":"Superficial ring = gap in external oblique aponeurosis","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G003-31.jpg","cap":"Superficial inguinal ring in external oblique aponeurosis, with intercrural fibers","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 31","cu":"../sl/G003-31c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/D045-58.jpg","cap":"Superficial ring in external oblique aponeurosis; deep ring in transversalis fascia","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 58"},{"src":"../tb/M5_16.jpg","cap":"The superficial ring is the slit between medial and lateral crura of external oblique aponeurosis, above the pubic tubercle.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.16","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F0008-66-1","s":"The superficial inguinal ring is an opening in which structure?","o":["a. Scarpa's fascia","b. Falx inguinalis","c. Internal abdominal oblique muscle","d. External abdominal oblique aponeurosis","e. Transversalis fascia"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"External abdominal oblique aponeurosis","a":"D","why":"<b>External abdominal oblique aponeurosis.</b> The superficial inguinal ring is a triangular gap in this aponeurosis, just above and lateral to the pubic tubercle. It is the exit of the inguinal canal.<br>A. Scarpa fascia: subcutaneous; it has no ring.<br>B. Falx inguinalis (conjoint tendon): medial part of the posterior wall.<br>C. Internal oblique: forms part of the anterior wall and roof, and the cremaster.<br>E. Transversalis fascia: holds the deep (internal) ring.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 14 (External Oblique)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 34 (Inguinal canal)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":66,"f":"F0008"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"86"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":24,"label":"Inferior epigastric vessels: medial edge of deep ring","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G003-25.jpg","cap":"Inner abdominal wall: inferior epigastric vessels between deep ring and Hesselbach's triangle","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 25","cu":"../sl/G003-25c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/D045-73.jpg","cap":"Inferior epigastric vessels: deep ring lateral, Hesselbach's triangle (pink) medial","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 73"},{"src":"../tb/M5_15.jpg","cap":"Right side: the inferior epigastric artery and vein run up just medial to the deep inguinal ring.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.15","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/G4_50.jpg","cap":"Close-up from inside: the inferior epigastric vessels hook along the medial edge of the deep ring.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 4.50","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"G069-29-4","s":"Where is the deep inguinal ring located in relation to the inguinal (Hesselbach's) triangle?","o":["A. Immediately medial to it","B. Within the triangle, at its apex","C. Directly inferior to it, below the inguinal ligament","D. Immediately lateral to it","E. Directly superior to it, at the arcuate line"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Immediately lateral","a":"D","why":"<b>Immediately lateral to it.</b> The inferior epigastric vessels form the lateral border of Hesselbach triangle, and the deep ring opens just lateral to them, in the lateral inguinal fossa. Direct hernias go through the triangle; indirect hernias start just outside it.<br>A. Medial to the triangle lies rectus abdominis.<br>B. The triangle contains no ring; its floor is weak transversalis fascia.<br>C. Below the inguinal ligament lie the femoral ring and vessels.<br>E. The arcuate line lies higher, about a third of the way from the umbilicus to the pubis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 36 (Deep Inguinal ring)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 25 (Internal Surface of Abdominal Wall)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Anterior abdominal wall interactive Q&A Fall 2026 (Dr. Connington)","sl":29,"f":"G069"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"87"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":24,"label":"Inguinal canal is about 4 cm long","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G003-33.jpg","cap":"Development of the inguinal canal: about 1½ inches long","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 33","cu":"../sl/G003-33c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G069-35.jpg","cap":"Inguinal canal and its layers","where":"2026 Middletown interactive: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 35"}],"q":[{"id":"G069-23-1","s":"How long is the inguinal canal?","o":["A. About 1 cm (under ½ inch)","B. About 4 cm (1½ inches)","C. About 8 cm (3 inches)","D. About 12 cm (5 inches)","E. About 20 cm (8 inches)"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"About 4 cm (1½ inches)","a":"B","why":"<b>About 4 cm (1½ inches).</b> The canal runs from the deep ring, just above the middle of the inguinal ligament, to the superficial ring, just above and lateral to the pubic tubercle. It is about 4 cm long, and wider in males because it carries the spermatic cord.<br>A. About 1 cm: too short; the two rings are a few centimeters apart.<br>C. About 8 cm, D. About 12 cm, E. About 20 cm: too long; the whole inguinal ligament is only about 12 cm.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 33 (Development of inguinal Canal)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Anterior abdominal wall interactive Q&A Fall 2026 (Dr. Connington)","sl":23,"f":"G069"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"88"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":24,"label":"Gubernaculum → scrotal ligament (male)","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G003-32.jpg","cap":"The gubernaculum ties each gonad to the future scrotum or labium majus","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 32","cu":"../sl/G003-32c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G080-21.jpg","cap":"The gubernaculum (green line) remains as the scrotal ligament","where":"2026 Montana interactive, slide 21","cu":"../sl/G080-21c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"G080-15-1","s":"In the adult male, the gubernaculum that guided the testis into the scrotum remains as which structure?","o":["A. Processus vaginalis","B. Scrotal ligament","C. Round ligament of the uterus","D. Cremaster muscle","E. Median umbilical ligament"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Scrotal ligament","a":"B","why":"<b>Scrotal ligament.</b> The gubernaculum ties the testis to the floor of the scrotum and guides its descent. In the adult it remains as the small scrotal ligament, which anchors the lower pole of the testis to the scrotum.<br>A. Processus vaginalis: the peritoneal pouch; its lower end becomes the tunica vaginalis.<br>C. Round ligament of the uterus: the female remnant, with the ovarian ligament.<br>D. Cremaster muscle: comes from the internal oblique.<br>E. Median umbilical ligament: the remnant of the urachus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 32 (Development of inguinal Canal)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Montana interactive","n":"Montana (Great Falls) anterior abdominal wall, inguinal canal & scrotum interactive 2026 (D. Lawver)","sl":15,"f":"G080"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"89"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":25,"label":"Canal walls change along it because it runs obliquely","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G003-31.jpg","cap":"Inguinal canal: oblique, walls change along its thirds","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 31","cu":"../sl/G003-31c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G069-28.jpg","cap":"MALT mnemonic for the walls","where":"2026 Middletown interactive: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 28"},{"src":"../sl/G003-34.jpg","cap":"Walls, roof and floor by thirds","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 34","cu":"../sl/G003-34c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"G069-23-2","s":"Why do the structures that make up the walls, roof and floor of the inguinal canal change depending on which third of the canal you are looking at?","o":["A. It runs obliquely from the deep ring, above the inguinal ligament, to the superficial ring, so it passes different layers of the wall along its course","B. It widens medially to hold the testis, so each third needs a different support","C. Each third develops from a different part of the gubernaculum","D. It turns at a right angle at the deep ring, so the walls swap places","E. Each third is supplied by a different spinal nerve"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"It runs obliquely through the layers of the abdominal wall","a":"A","why":"<b>It runs obliquely through the layered wall.</b> The canal starts at the deep ring in transversalis fascia and runs medially and downward, above the inguinal ligament, to the superficial ring in the external oblique aponeurosis. On the way it passes under the arching lower fibers of internal oblique and transversus abdominis, so different layers form its walls in each third. Mnemonic MALT: roof = Muscles (internal oblique, transversus abdominis); anterior wall = Aponeuroses (external oblique, plus internal oblique laterally); floor = Ligaments (inguinal, lacunar medially); posterior wall = Transversalis fascia (plus the conjoint Tendon medially).<br>B. It does not widen to hold the testis; the testis passes through to the scrotum.<br>C. The gubernaculum guides the descent of the gonad but does not form separate thirds.<br>D. It has no right-angle turn; it runs at a slant.<br>E. One nerve (ilioinguinal) runs along the whole canal; nerves do not explain the walls.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 31 (Inguinal canal)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 34 (Inguinal canal)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Anterior abdominal wall interactive Q&A Fall 2026 (Dr. Connington)","sl":23,"f":"G069"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"90"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":25,"label":"Posterior wall: transversalis fascia + conjoint tendon","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G003-34.jpg","cap":"Canal walls table: posterior wall transversalis fascia, conjoint tendon medially","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 34","cu":"../sl/G003-34c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G003-37.jpg","cap":"Direct hernia pushes through peritoneum and transversalis fascia in Hesselbach triangle","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 37","cu":"../sl/G003-37c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G069-28.jpg","cap":"MALT: roof Muscles, anterior wall Aponeuroses, floor Ligaments, posterior wall Transversalis fascia + conjoint Tendon","where":"2026 Middletown interactive: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 28"},{"src":"../sl/G069-24.jpg","cap":"Inguinal canal walls, roof and floor by thirds","where":"2026 Middletown interactive: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 24","cu":"../sl/G069-24c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/M5_8.jpg","cap":"Lower panel: the inguinal falx (conjoint tendon) with transversalis fascia forms the posterior wall of the canal.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.8","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/G4_49.jpg","cap":"A direct hernia bulges through the weak posterior wall beside the conjoint tendon, medial to the inferior epigastric vessels.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 4.49","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-240-1","s":"During surgical repair of inguinal hernias it is important to recognize the boundaries of the inguinal canal. Which of the following structures forms part of the posterior boundary of the inguinal canal?","o":["A. Aponeurosis of external oblique","B. Conjoint tendon","C. Inguinal ligament","D. Lacunar ligament","E. Pectineal ligament"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Conjoint tendon","a":"B","why":"<b>Conjoint tendon.</b> The posterior wall of the inguinal canal is transversalis fascia, reinforced medially by the conjoint tendon (internal oblique plus transversus abdominis aponeuroses, inserting on the pubic crest).<br>A. External oblique aponeurosis: the anterior wall.<br>C. Inguinal ligament: the floor.<br>D. Lacunar ligament: floor of the medial third.<br>E. Pectineal ligament: on the pubic bone, below the canal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 34 (Inguinal canal)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":240,"f":"F3903"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"91"},{"id":"G069-26-1","s":"During surgical repair of inguinal hernias it is important to recognize the boundaries of the inguinal canal. Which of the following structures forms part of the posterior wall of the inguinal canal?","o":["A. Aponeurosis of external oblique","B. Lacunar ligament","C. Inguinal ligament","D. Transversalis fascia","E. Pectineal ligament"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Transversalis fascia","a":"D","why":"<b>Transversalis fascia.</b> Transversalis fascia forms the posterior wall along the whole canal, and the conjoint tendon (falx inguinalis) adds to it in the medial third. (MALT: posterior wall = Transversalis fascia and conjoint Tendon.)<br>A. Aponeurosis of external oblique: the anterior wall.<br>B. Lacunar ligament: the floor, medial third.<br>C. Inguinal ligament: the floor.<br>E. Pectineal ligament: on the pecten pubis below and behind the canal; not one of its walls.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 34 (Inguinal canal)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 31 (Inguinal canal)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Anterior abdominal wall interactive Q&A Fall 2026 (Dr. Connington)","sl":26,"f":"G069"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"92"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":25,"label":"Inguinal canal contents: spermatic cord / round ligament","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G003-35.jpg","cap":"Canal contents: spermatic cord (male) or round ligament (female), plus ilioinguinal nerve","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 35"},{"src":"../sl/G003-42.jpg","cap":"Spermatic cord contents: ductus deferens, testicular artery, pampiniform plexus","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 42","cu":"../sl/G003-42c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/M5_17.jpg","cap":"In males the spermatic cord, carrying ductus deferens and testicular vessels, runs through the inguinal canal to the testis.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.17","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/M5_19.jpg","cap":"In females the round ligament of the uterus runs through the deep ring, canal and superficial ring to the labium majus.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.19","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"G069-23-3","s":"What is the function of the inguinal canal?","o":["A. It lets the spermatic cord (in males) or the round ligament of the uterus (in females) pass from the abdominal cavity to the external genitalia","B. It carries the femoral artery and vein from the abdomen into the thigh","C. It carries the inferior epigastric vessels into the rectus sheath","D. It lets the ductus deferens (in males) or the ovarian ligament (in females) pass down into the pelvis","E. It carries the iliohypogastric nerve to the suprapubic skin"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Passage for the spermatic cord (males) or round ligament of the uterus (females) to the external genitalia","a":"A","why":"<b>It lets the spermatic cord (males) or the round ligament of the uterus (females) pass from the abdomen to the external genitalia.</b> The canal is the track the testis left on its way to the scrotum.<br>B. Femoral vessels: pass under the inguinal ligament, below the canal.<br>C. Inferior epigastric vessels: climb the inside of the wall, medial to the deep ring, and enter the rectus sheath at the arcuate line.<br>D. Ovarian ligament: stays in the pelvis; the ductus deferens runs in the canal inside the cord.<br>E. Iliohypogastric nerve: runs above the canal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 31 (Inguinal canal)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 35 (Inguinal Canal Contents)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Anterior abdominal wall interactive Q&A Fall 2026 (Dr. Connington)","sl":23,"f":"G069"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"93"},{"id":"G069-23-4","s":"The inguinal canal connects the abdominal cavity with which structure in males and which in females?","o":["A. Scrotum in males; labia minora in females","B. Penis in males; clitoris in females","C. Perineal body in males; mons pubis in females","D. Scrotum in males; labia majora in females","E. Femoral triangle in both sexes"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Scrotum in males; labia majora in females","a":"D","why":"<b>Scrotum in males; labia majora in females.</b> The spermatic cord leaves the canal for the scrotum, and the round ligament of the uterus ends in the labium majus, the female counterpart of the scrotum.<br>A. Labia minora: the inner folds; the round ligament ends in the labium majus.<br>B. Penis and clitoris: erectile organs that the canal does not reach.<br>C. Perineal body and mons pubis: the canal ends in the scrotum or labium majus instead.<br>E. Femoral triangle: reached under the inguinal ligament, not through the canal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 31 (Inguinal canal)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Anterior abdominal wall interactive Q&A Fall 2026 (Dr. Connington)","sl":23,"f":"G069"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"94"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":25,"label":"Female inguinal canal: round ligament of uterus","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G003-35.jpg","cap":"Canal contents: spermatic cord in males, round ligament of uterus in females","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 35"},{"src":"../sl/D042-25.jpg","cap":"Female inguinal canal drawing: round ligament of uterus with ilioinguinal nerve","where":"2025 TA review, slide 25"},{"src":"../sl/G078-33.jpg","cap":"Inguinal canal in female and male: round ligament runs with the ilioinguinal nerve","where":"2026 TA session: GI week 1 part 2, slide 33"},{"src":"../tb/M5_19.jpg","cap":"Panel C: the round ligament of the uterus passes through the deep ring and inguinal canal to the labium majus.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.19","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F0008-68-1","s":"Which structure passes through the deep inguinal ring?","o":["a. Round ligament of the uterus","b. Iliohypogastric nerve","c. Ilioinguinal nerve","d. Inferior epigastric artery","e. umbilical ligament"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Round ligament of the uterus","a":"A","why":"<b>Round ligament of the uterus.</b> It runs from the uterus through the deep inguinal ring, along the canal, and out the superficial ring to the labium majus.<br>B. Iliohypogastric nerve: pierces the muscles above the canal; it is not in it.<br>C. Ilioinguinal nerve: is in the canal, but enters from the side through the internal oblique, not through the deep ring.<br>D. Inferior epigastric artery: lies just medial to the deep ring, outside it.<br>E. Umbilical ligament: a fold on the inner abdominal wall near the midline.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 35 (Inguinal Canal Contents)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 31 (Inguinal canal)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":68,"f":"F0008"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"95"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":26,"label":"Direct hernia → through Hesselbach's triangle","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G003-37.jpg","cap":"Direct hernia passes medial to inferior epigastric vessels through the inguinal triangle","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 37","cu":"../sl/G003-37c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/D045-73.jpg","cap":"Hesselbach's triangle bordered by rectus abdominis, inferior epigastric vessels, inguinal ligament","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 73"},{"src":"../sl/F1228-124.jpg","cap":"Table: direct hernias are acquired, older, medial, rarely reach the scrotum","where":"2019 review, slide 124"},{"src":"../sl/G069-36.jpg","cap":"Inguinal (Hesselbach's) triangle, inferior epigastric vessels and deep ring","where":"2026 Middletown interactive: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 36"}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-244-1","s":"A 54-year-old man has pain in the groin. On examination, a palpable mass is felt in the region of the superficial inguinal ring which is diagnosed as a direct inguinal hernia. The hernial sac most likely:","o":["A. Is covered by all three layers of the spermatic fascia","B. Passes lateral to the inferior epigastric artery","C. Passes through linea alba of the anterior abdominal wall","D. Passes through the deep inguinal ring","E. Passes through the inguinal (Hesselbach’s) triangle"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Passes through the inguinal (Hesselbach’s) triangle","a":"E","why":"<b>Passes through the inguinal (Hesselbach's) triangle.</b> That is where every direct hernia comes through, medial to the inferior epigastric artery.<br>A. All three spermatic fasciae: an indirect sac, which runs inside the cord.<br>B. Lateral to the inferior epigastric artery: indirect.<br>C. Linea alba: the midline, site of epigastric and umbilical hernias.<br>D. Deep inguinal ring: indirect.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 37 (Direct Vs. Indirect Inguinal Hernias)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 36 (Deep Inguinal ring)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Anterior abdominal wall interactive Q&A Fall 2026 (Dr. Connington)","sl":33,"f":"G069"},{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":244,"f":"F3903"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"96"},{"id":"G075-22-1","s":"A 68-year-old man presents to his physician with a bulge in his left groin that becomes more prominent when coughing or straining. He reports that the bulge has gradually increased in size over several years. During the physical examination, the physician suspects a direct inguinal hernia. The physician explains that this type of hernia occurs because of weakness in the posterior wall of the inguinal canal. Which of the following best describes the anatomical location of a direct inguinal hernia?","o":["A. Lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels, through the deep inguinal ring.","B. Medial to the inferior epigastric vessels, through Hesselbach's triangle.","C. Through the femoral canal, below the inguinal ligament.","D. Through the deep inguinal ring and into the spermatic cord.","E. Through the superficial inguinal ring without entering the inguinal canal."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Medial to the inferior epigastric vessels, through Hesselbach's triangle","a":"B","why":"<b>Medial to the inferior epigastric vessels, through Hesselbach's triangle.</b> A direct hernia pushes straight through the weak posterior wall of the canal in this triangle, bordered by rectus abdominis, the inferior epigastric vessels and the inguinal ligament.<br>A. Lateral, through the deep ring: indirect hernia.<br>C. Femoral canal below the inguinal ligament: femoral hernia.<br>D. Into the deep ring and spermatic cord: indirect hernia.<br>E. A direct sac comes through the canal's posterior wall into its medial part; it does not reach the superficial ring by skipping the canal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 37 (Direct Vs. Indirect Inguinal Hernias)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 29 (Intro to Inguinal region/recap)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice","n":"GI #1 practice questions Fall 2026 (Lecture #1 embryology, Lecture #2 abdominal wall)","sl":null,"f":"G075"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"97"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":26,"label":"Direct sac never gets internal spermatic fascia","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G073-20.jpg","cap":"Why a direct sac lies beside the cord, outside the internal spermatic fascia","where":"2026 Harlem clickers: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 20"},{"src":"../sl/G073-25.jpg","cap":"Indirect sac enters the deep ring; direct sac pushes through the wall","where":"2026 Harlem clickers: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 25"}],"q":[{"id":"G073-19-1","s":"A 45-year-old porter develops a direct inguinal hernia. If the hernia extended through the superficial inguinal ring, it would be surrounded by all of the abdominal wall layers EXCEPT the:","o":["A. External spermatic fascia","B. Internal spermatic fascia","C. Peritoneum and extraperitoneal connective tissue","D. Weak fascia of the transversus abdominis muscle lateral to the falx"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Internal spermatic fascia","a":"B","why":"<b>Internal spermatic fascia.</b> This inner cord coat is the sleeve of transversalis fascia taken at the deep ring, so only structures that enter the deep ring get it. A direct sac pushes through the posterior wall beside the cord, so it lies outside the internal spermatic fascia.<br>A. External spermatic fascia: a sac that comes out through the superficial ring picks it up.<br>C. Peritoneum and extraperitoneal connective tissue: cover every hernia sac.<br>D. Weak fascia of transversus abdominis lateral to the falx (conjoint tendon): the transversalis fascia that a direct sac pushes ahead of it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 37 (Direct Vs. Indirect Inguinal Hernias)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 44 (Layers of the Cord and Layers of the abdominal  Wall)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 43 (Coverings of the Spermatic Cord)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Harlem clicker","n":"Harlem anterior abdominal wall & inguinal canal clickers 11/5/26","sl":19,"f":"G073"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"98"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":26,"label":"Older man, medial bulge → direct hernia","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G003-37.jpg","cap":"Direct sac pushes medial to epigastric vessels, beside the cord, not into scrotum","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 37","cu":"../sl/G003-37c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/F1228-124.jpg","cap":"Table: indirect versus direct by age, scrotal descent, cause, vessel relation","where":"2019 review, slide 124"},{"src":"../sl/G073-22.jpg","cap":"Finger in superficial ring: impulse on the tip is indirect, against the side direct","where":"2026 Harlem clickers: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 22"},{"src":"../sl/G073-26.jpg","cap":"Diagram: finger pressure over the superficial ring, direct versus indirect hernia","where":"2026 Harlem clickers: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 26","cu":"../sl/G073-26c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-228-1","s":"In examining a male patient you place your finger in his superficial inguinal ring and ask him to cough. You then feel a sudden impulse medial to your finger. You conclude that your patient most likely has:","o":["A) Femoral hernia.","B) Direct inguinal hernia.","C) Enlarged lymph node.","D) Indirect inguinal hernia.","E) Testicular cancer."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Direct inguinal hernia","a":"B","why":"<b>Direct inguinal hernia.</b> A direct hernia pushes straight forward through Hesselbach's triangle, which lies medial to the deep ring. So the cough impulse hits the side of your finger, medially, not its tip.<br>A. Femoral hernia: bulges below the inguinal ligament, not at the superficial ring.<br>C. Enlarged lymph node: has no cough impulse.<br>D. Indirect hernia: comes down the canal from the deep ring and hits the fingertip.<br>E. Testicular cancer: a hard scrotal mass with no cough impulse.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 36 (Deep Inguinal ring)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 37 (Direct Vs. Indirect Inguinal Hernias)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Harlem clicker","n":"Harlem anterior abdominal wall & inguinal canal clickers 11/5/26","sl":21,"f":"G073"},{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":228,"f":"F3903"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"99"},{"id":"G080-24-1","s":"A 65-year-old patient experiences intense pain in their groin after attempting to lift a heavy stone while working on a landscaping project. What is the most likely injury that this patient has suffered?","o":["A. Indirect inguinal hernia","B. Direct inguinal hernia","C. Umbilical hernia","D. Slipped disk","E. Swollen lymph nodes"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Direct inguinal hernia","a":"B","why":"<b>Direct inguinal hernia.</b> An older patient straining to lift a heavy load points to an acquired hernia: the posterior wall in Hesselbach triangle has weakened with age, and the raised pressure pushes bowel straight through it. Direct hernias are mostly a problem of older men.<br>A. Indirect: usually congenital, through the deep ring along a patent processus vaginalis; typical of infants and young men.<br>C. Umbilical: a bulge at the navel, not the groin.<br>D. Slipped disk: back pain running down the leg, no groin bulge.<br>E. Swollen lymph nodes: firm, tender lumps, not set off by lifting.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 36 (Deep Inguinal ring)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 37 (Direct Vs. Indirect Inguinal Hernias)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Montana interactive","n":"Montana (Great Falls) anterior abdominal wall, inguinal canal & scrotum interactive 2026 (D. Lawver)","sl":24,"f":"G080"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"100"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":27,"label":"Lateral to inferior epigastric vessels → indirect","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G003-37.jpg","cap":"Indirect hernia passes lateral to inferior epigastric vessels into the deep ring","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 37","cu":"../sl/G003-37c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/D045-69.jpg","cap":"Cross-sections: indirect lateral to epigastric vessels, direct medial through the wall","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 69","cu":"../sl/D045-69c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G078-41.jpg","cap":"Indirect versus direct: route, deep ring, inferior epigastric vessels, cause, age","where":"2026 TA session: GI week 1 part 2, slide 41"}],"q":[{"id":"F0008-74-1","s":"A man is moving into a new house and during the process lifts a large chest of drawers. As he lifts he feels a severe pain in the lower right quadrant of his abdomen. He finds that he can no longer lift without pain and the next day goes to see his physician. Surgery is indicated and during the surgery the surgeon opens the inguinal region and finds a hernial sac with a small knuckle of intestine projecting through the abdominal wall just above the inguinal ligament and lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels. The hernia was diagnosed as:","o":["a. An indirect inguinal hernia","b. A direct inguinal hernia","c. A femoral hernia","d. An incisional hernia","e. Umbilical hernia"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"An indirect inguinal hernia","a":"A","why":"<b>Indirect inguinal hernia.</b> The sac is above the inguinal ligament and lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels, which is exactly where the deep inguinal ring is.<br>B. Direct: would be medial to these vessels, in Hesselbach's triangle.<br>C. Femoral: would be below the inguinal ligament.<br>D. Incisional: comes through an old surgical scar.<br>E. Umbilical: at the navel.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 36 (Deep Inguinal ring)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 37 (Direct Vs. Indirect Inguinal Hernias)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":74,"f":"F0008"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"101"},{"id":"F3902-69-1","s":"A 65 year old male patient presents to the ER with a mass in the groin area. He states that the mass is painful upon palpation. The patient was diagnosed with an inguinal hernia requiring surgical intervention. During the surgery, the surgeon noticed that the loop of intestine lies lateral to the inferior epigastric/lateral umbilical fold. Which of the following type of hernia was this?","o":["A. Umbilical hernia","B. Femoral Hernia","C. Lumbar hernia","D. Direct inguinal hernia","E. Indirect inguinal hernia"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Indirect inguinal hernia","a":"E","why":"<b>Indirect inguinal hernia.</b> The inferior epigastric vessels raise the lateral umbilical fold. A hernia lateral to them enters the deep inguinal ring, which makes it indirect.<br>A. Umbilical hernia: at the navel.<br>B. Femoral hernia: below the inguinal ligament.<br>C. Lumbar hernia: in the back of the abdominal wall.<br>D. Direct hernia: medial to the inferior epigastric vessels.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 25 (Internal Surface of Abdominal Wall)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 36 (Deep Inguinal ring)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Anterior abdominal wall interactive Q&A Fall 2026 (Dr. Connington)","sl":29,"f":"G069"},{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":69,"f":"F3902"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"102"},{"id":"G075-21-1","s":"A 24-year-old male presents to his physician with a painless bulge in his right groin that becomes more noticeable when coughing or lifting heavy objects. On physical examination, the physician suspects an indirect inguinal hernia and explains that the hernia sac enters the inguinal canal through a natural opening in the abdominal wall. Which of the following best describes the anatomical location of an indirect inguinal hernia?","o":["A. Medial to the inferior epigastric vessels, through Hesselbach's triangle.","B. Lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels, through the deep inguinal ring.","C. Directly through the posterior wall of the inguinal canal.","D. Through the femoral canal, below the inguinal ligament.","E. Medial to the femoral artery, through the femoral ring."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels, through the deep inguinal ring","a":"B","why":"<b>Lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels, through the deep inguinal ring.</b> An indirect hernia uses the natural opening, the deep ring in transversalis fascia, which lies lateral to these vessels, and then follows the spermatic cord down the canal.<br>A. Medial, through Hesselbach triangle: direct hernia.<br>C. Straight through the posterior wall of the canal: direct hernia.<br>D. Femoral canal below the inguinal ligament: femoral hernia.<br>E. Femoral ring, medial to the femoral vein: femoral hernia.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 36 (Deep Inguinal ring)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 37 (Direct Vs. Indirect Inguinal Hernias)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice","n":"GI #1 practice questions Fall 2026 (Lecture #1 embryology, Lecture #2 abdominal wall)","sl":null,"f":"G075"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"103"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":28,"label":"Child's hernia → indirect, open processus vaginalis","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G003-41.jpg","cap":"Processus vaginalis follows the descending testis; if it stays open, congenital hernia","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 41","cu":"../sl/G003-41c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/F1228-124.jpg","cap":"Table: indirect hernias are congenital, tied to processus vaginalis, younger patients","where":"2019 review, slide 124"},{"src":"../tb/M5_18.jpg","cap":"The processus vaginalis leads the testis through the canal; if its stalk stays open, an indirect hernia follows it.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.18","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"G080-27-1","s":"Soon after birth doctors notice a slight bulge in the groin on the right side of a baby boy. The mother is not a smoker and she abstained from alcohol during pregnancy. What is the most likely diagnosis for this condition?","o":["A. Indirect inguinal hernia","B. Direct inguinal hernia","C. Umbilical hernia","D. Slipped disk","E. Swollen lymph nodes"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Indirect inguinal hernia","a":"A","why":"<b>Indirect inguinal hernia.</b> A groin bulge in a newborn boy is almost always an indirect, congenital hernia: the processus vaginalis has stayed open, so bowel slides through the deep ring into the canal.<br>B. Direct: an acquired weakness of the posterior wall in older men.<br>C. Umbilical: a bulge at the navel, not the groin.<br>D. Slipped disk: a spine problem of adults, with no groin bulge.<br>E. Swollen lymph nodes: need an infection or tumor, and nothing here suggests one.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 41 (Recap)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 37 (Direct Vs. Indirect Inguinal Hernias)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Montana interactive","n":"Montana (Great Falls) anterior abdominal wall, inguinal canal & scrotum interactive 2026 (D. Lawver)","sl":27,"f":"G080"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"104"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":29,"label":"Cord coverings ← abdominal wall layers","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G003-44.jpg","cap":"Color-matched: each cord covering continues one abdominal wall layer","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 44","cu":"../sl/G003-44c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/D041-49.jpg","cap":"Table: each scrotal and cord layer with its abdominal wall origin","where":"2025 TA review, slide 49"},{"src":"../sl/G078-45.jpg","cap":"Cord coats: external spermatic, cremasteric, internal spermatic fascia, contents inside","where":"2026 TA session: GI week 1 part 2, slide 45","cu":"../sl/G078-45c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G080-22.jpg","cap":"Each wall layer the testis crosses becomes a coat; peritoneum becomes the processus vaginalis","where":"2026 Montana interactive, slide 22","cu":"../sl/G080-22c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/M5_17.jpg","cap":"External oblique aponeurosis becomes external spermatic fascia, internal oblique becomes cremaster, transversalis fascia becomes internal spermatic fascia.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.17","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/G4_47.jpg","cap":"Follow each colored abdominal wall layer into the covering it forms around the cord and testis.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 4.47","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F3902-73-1","s":"You are scheduled to assist your surgical attending during a hernia repair and you are trying to recall that the transversalis fascia contributes to the formation of which of the following structures?","o":["A. Inguinal ligament","B. Linea alba","C. Internal spermatic fascia","D. Cremaster muscle","E. Ductus deferens"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Internal spermatic fascia","a":"C","why":"<b>Internal spermatic fascia.</b> The deep ring is a hole in the transversalis fascia. As the cord passes through, it takes a sleeve of that fascia: the internal spermatic fascia.<br>A. Inguinal ligament: the rolled lower edge of the external oblique aponeurosis.<br>B. Linea alba: the midline seam of the flat muscles' aponeuroses.<br>D. Cremaster muscle: from the internal oblique.<br>E. Ductus deferens: a duct from the epididymis, not a fascial layer.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 44 (Layers of the Cord and Layers of the abdominal  Wall)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Montana interactive","n":"Montana (Great Falls) anterior abdominal wall, inguinal canal & scrotum interactive 2026 (D. Lawver)","sl":21,"f":"G080"},{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":73,"f":"F3902"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"105"},{"id":"G075-24-1","s":"A 32-year-old male undergoes surgery to repair an indirect inguinal hernia. During the procedure, the surgeon follows the spermatic cord through the inguinal canal and identifies its surrounding fascial coverings. The surgeon explains that these coverings are derived from the layers of the anterior abdominal wall as the testes descend during embryologic development. Which of the following correctly matches the fascial covering of the spermatic cord with its embryologic origin?","o":["A. Internal spermatic fascia – transversalis fascia.","B. External spermatic fascia – transversus abdominis muscle.","C. Cremasteric muscle and fascia – external oblique aponeurosis.","D. Internal spermatic fascia – internal oblique muscle.","E. Cremasteric fascia – transversalis fascia."],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Internal spermatic fascia – transversalis fascia","a":"A","why":"<b>Internal spermatic fascia – transversalis fascia.</b> Each cord coat continues the wall layer at the same depth: external spermatic fascia from the external oblique aponeurosis, cremaster muscle and fascia from the internal oblique, internal spermatic fascia from transversalis fascia at the deep ring.<br>B. External spermatic fascia comes from the external oblique aponeurosis; transversus abdominis gives no coat.<br>C. Cremasteric muscle and fascia come from the internal oblique.<br>D. Internal spermatic fascia comes from transversalis fascia, not the internal oblique.<br>E. Cremasteric fascia comes from the internal oblique.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 44 (Layers of the Cord and Layers of the abdominal  Wall)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 43 (Coverings of the Spermatic Cord)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice","n":"GI #1 practice questions Fall 2026 (Lecture #1 embryology, Lecture #2 abdominal wall)","sl":null,"f":"G075"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"106"},{"id":"G080-21-1","s":"As the testis descends through the anterior abdominal wall, which covering of the spermatic cord comes from the external abdominal oblique aponeurosis?","o":["A. Internal spermatic fascia","B. Cremaster muscle and fascia","C. External spermatic fascia","D. Dartos fascia","E. Tunica vaginalis"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"External spermatic fascia","a":"C","why":"<b>External spermatic fascia.</b> Each coat of the cord continues the wall layer at the same depth. The external oblique aponeurosis, the outermost layer, gives the outer coat, picked up at the superficial ring.<br>A. Internal spermatic fascia: from the transversalis fascia, at the deep ring.<br>B. Cremaster muscle and fascia: from the internal oblique.<br>D. Dartos fascia: continues Scarpa fascia into the scrotum.<br>E. Tunica vaginalis: from the processus vaginalis (peritoneum).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 44 (Layers of the Cord and Layers of the abdominal  Wall)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 43 (Coverings of the Spermatic Cord)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Montana interactive","n":"Montana (Great Falls) anterior abdominal wall, inguinal canal & scrotum interactive 2026 (D. Lawver)","sl":21,"f":"G080"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"107"},{"id":"G080-21-2","s":"As the testis descends through the anterior abdominal wall, which covering of the spermatic cord comes from the internal abdominal oblique?","o":["A. Cremaster muscle and fascia","B. External spermatic fascia","C. Internal spermatic fascia","D. Dartos muscle","E. Tunica albuginea"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Cremaster muscle and fascia","a":"A","why":"<b>Cremaster muscle and fascia.</b> The lowest fibers of the internal oblique loop down around the cord in the canal as the cremaster, the middle coat of the cord.<br>B. External spermatic fascia: from the external oblique aponeurosis.<br>C. Internal spermatic fascia: from the transversalis fascia.<br>D. Dartos muscle: smooth muscle from Scarpa fascia, in the scrotal wall.<br>E. Tunica albuginea: the testis's own fibrous capsule.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 44 (Layers of the Cord and Layers of the abdominal  Wall)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 45 (Cremaster Muscle)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Montana interactive","n":"Montana (Great Falls) anterior abdominal wall, inguinal canal & scrotum interactive 2026 (D. Lawver)","sl":21,"f":"G080"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"108"},{"id":"G080-21-4","s":"As the testis descends through the abdominal wall, the parietal peritoneum it carries with it forms which structure?","o":["A. Internal spermatic fascia","B. Gubernaculum","C. Dartos fascia","D. Cremasteric fascia","E. Processus vaginalis"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Processus vaginalis","a":"E","why":"<b>Processus vaginalis.</b> The testis pushes a pouch of parietal peritoneum ahead of it through the wall. The lower end of this pouch stays around the testis as the tunica vaginalis; the rest normally closes.<br>A. Internal spermatic fascia: from the transversalis fascia.<br>B. Gubernaculum: the cord that guides the testis down; it becomes the scrotal ligament.<br>C. Dartos fascia: continues Scarpa fascia into the scrotum.<br>D. Cremasteric fascia: from the internal oblique.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 41 (Recap)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 48 (Testis)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Montana interactive","n":"Montana (Great Falls) anterior abdominal wall, inguinal canal & scrotum interactive 2026 (D. Lawver)","sl":21,"f":"G080"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"109"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":30,"label":"Cremasteric reflex: testis rises; tests L1–L2","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G003-45.jpg","cap":"Cremaster tested by stroking the upper inner thigh; it lifts the testis","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 45","cu":"../sl/G003-45c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G073-36.jpg","cap":"Reflex: ilioinguinal sensory limb, genital branch of genitofemoral motor limb","where":"2026 Harlem clickers: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 36"},{"src":"../tb/M5_17.jpg","cap":"The cremaster muscle loops down the cord around the testis; when it contracts it lifts the testis.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.17","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/N253.jpg","cap":"Bottom: genital (motor to cremaster) and femoral (upper inner thigh skin) branches of the genitofemoral nerve, both L1–L2.","where":"Netter Atlas 6e, Plate 253","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-234-1","s":"One of the standard tests of a newborn infant is to stroke the inside of its thigh to elicit the cremaster reflex. What would be the response you would expect to see?","o":["A) twitching of the skin in the hypogastric region","B) closing of the labia","C) contraction of the dartos muscle","D) penile rigidity","E) testicular retraction"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"testicular retraction","a":"E","why":"<b>Testicular retraction.</b> Stroking the inner thigh makes the cremaster contract and pull the testis on that side upward.<br>A. Skin twitch over the lower abdomen: that is the abdominal reflex.<br>B. Closing of the labia: no such reflex.<br>C. Dartos contraction: a slow smooth-muscle response to cold, run by sympathetic fibers, not this reflex.<br>D. Penile rigidity: erection, a parasympathetic response.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 45 (Cremaster Muscle)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Harlem clicker","n":"Harlem anterior abdominal wall & inguinal canal clickers 11/5/26","sl":35,"f":"G073"},{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":234,"f":"F3903"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"110"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":31,"label":"Ductus deferens starts at epididymis tail","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G003-49.jpg","cap":"Epididymis head, body and tail; ductus deferens leaves the tail","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 49","cu":"../sl/G003-49c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G003-48.jpg","cap":"Testis: tunica albuginea, rete testis, efferent ductules to epididymis head","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 48"},{"src":"../sl/G078-55.jpg","cap":"Ductus deferens leaves the tail of the epididymis and runs up toward the prostate","where":"2026 TA session: GI week 1 part 2, slide 55"}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-230-1","s":"Which of the following associations is incorrect?","o":["A) rete testis—opening of the ductus deferens","B) testicular arteries—arise from abdominal aorta","C) varicocele—dilation of the pampiniform plexus","D) epididymis—sperm maturation","E) anterior scrotum—innervated by branches from the ilioinguinal and genitofemoral nerves"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"rete testis—opening of the ductus deferens","a":"A","why":"<b>A is wrong.</b> The <b>rete testis</b> is a network of channels inside the testis. It sends sperm through efferent ductules to the <b>head of the epididymis</b>. The ductus deferens starts at the <b>tail</b> of the epididymis.<br>B. True: the testicular arteries come off the abdominal aorta.<br>C. True: a varicocele is a widened pampiniform venous plexus.<br>D. True: sperm mature in the epididymis.<br>E. True: the ilioinguinal nerve and genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve supply the front of the scrotum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 48 (Testis)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 50 (Blood Supply)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 53 (Clinical Correlates)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Harlem clicker","n":"Harlem anterior abdominal wall & inguinal canal clickers 11/5/26","sl":29,"f":"G073"},{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":230,"f":"F3903"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"111"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":32,"label":"Testis → para-aortic (lumbar) nodes","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G003-52.jpg","cap":"Solid arrow: testis lymph along testicular vessels to lumbar (para-aortic) nodes","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 52","cu":"../sl/G003-52c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/D041-58.jpg","cap":"Testis summary: lymph drains to lumbar (para-aortic) nodes","where":"2025 TA review, slide 58","cu":"../sl/D041-58c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G078-56.jpg","cap":"Testis lymph to para-aortic nodes; scrotal lymph to superficial inguinal nodes","where":"2026 TA session: GI week 1 part 2, slide 56"},{"src":"../tb/M5_21.jpg","cap":"Solid arrow: testis lymph follows the testicular vessels up to the lumbar (para-aortic) nodes.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.21","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"D045-96-1","s":"A 30-year-old man presents with a painless testicular mass. Ultrasound confirms a solid intratesticular tumor. Which group of lymph nodes would most likely show metastatic spread of this cancer?","o":["A. Superficial inguinal nodes","B. Deep inguinal nodes","C. External iliac nodes","D. Internal iliac nodes","E. Para-aortic (lumbar) nodes"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Para-aortic (lumbar) nodes","a":"E","why":"<b>Para-aortic (lumbar) nodes.</b> A tumor inside the testis spreads along the testicular vessels to the nodes beside the aorta, near the kidneys, where the testis first formed.<br>A. Superficial inguinal: scrotal skin, not the testis.<br>B. Deep inguinal: deep thigh and glans of the penis.<br>C. External iliac: lower limb and some pelvic organs.<br>D. Internal iliac: pelvic organs.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 52 (Lymphatics)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice","n":"GI #1 practice questions Fall 2026 (Lecture #1 embryology, Lecture #2 abdominal wall)","sl":null,"f":"G075"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":96,"f":"D045"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"112"},{"id":"G080-33-1","s":"A 18-month-old male patient is diagnosed with testicular cancer. Additionally, this cancer has metastasized to the patients tracheobronchial lymph nodes. A test for hCG (human chorionic gonadotropin) is positive. What is most likely pathway that the cancer cells have take to get to the lungs?","o":["A. Through the surficial inguinal lymph nodes","B. Through the para-aortic lymph nodes","C. Through the pampiniform plexus","D. Through the testicular artery","E. Along the genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Through the para-aortic lymph nodes","a":"B","why":"<b>Through the para-aortic lymph nodes.</b> Testicular lymph runs up the spermatic cord with the testicular vessels to the para-aortic (lumbar) nodes. From there cancer cells travel up the lumbar trunks and thoracic duct into the chest.<br>A. Superficial inguinal nodes: drain the scrotal skin, not the testis.<br>C. Pampiniform plexus: a venous route; cells in it reach the lungs through the blood, not through lymph nodes.<br>D. Testicular artery: carries blood toward the testis, not away from it.<br>E. Genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve: a nerve, not a drainage route.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 52 (Lymphatics)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 53 (Clinical Correlates)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Montana interactive","n":"Montana (Great Falls) anterior abdominal wall, inguinal canal & scrotum interactive 2026 (D. Lawver)","sl":33,"f":"G080"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"113"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":33,"label":"Undescended testis: cryptorchidism, along descent path","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G003-53.jpg","cap":"Cryptorchid testes: failed to fully descend into the scrotum","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 53","cu":"../sl/G003-53c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G003-32.jpg","cap":"Testis descent path: abdomen, deep ring, canal, superficial ring, scrotum","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 32","cu":"../sl/G003-32c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/M5_18.jpg","cap":"Testis path: posterior abdominal wall, deep ring, inguinal canal, scrotum; an undescended testis stops along this route.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.18","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F0008-70-1","s":"A pediatrician has diagnosed a newborn baby of having right-sided cryptorchidism (undescended testis). The testis may have been trapped in any site EXCEPT:","o":["a. Perineum","b. Just outside the superficial inguinal ring","c. Pelvic brim","d. At the deep inguinal ring","e. Somewhere in the inguinal canal"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Perineum","a":"A","why":"<b>Perineum.</b> An undescended testis stops somewhere on its normal path: abdomen, pelvic brim, deep ring, inguinal canal, or just past the superficial ring. A testis in the perineum is off that path; that is an ectopic testis, not cryptorchidism.<br>B, C, D and E. All are points on the normal path of descent, so a testis can get stuck at each.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 53 (Clinical Correlates)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 32 (Development of inguinal Canal)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":70,"f":"F0008"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"114"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":33,"label":"Torsion: sudden pain, no cremasteric reflex","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G003-53.jpg","cap":"Torsion twists the spermatic cord and cuts off the testis's blood supply","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 53","cu":"../sl/G003-53c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"G080-30-1","s":"A 28-year-old patient arrives at the emergency department with intense pain in his groin. The doctor lightly strokes the patient’s inner thigh and cremasteric reflex is absent. What condition is this patient suffering from?","o":["A. Testicular cancer","B. Testicular torsion","C. Cryptorchidism","D. Prostate cancer"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Testicular torsion","a":"B","why":"<b>Testicular torsion.</b> Sudden, intense pain with a missing cremasteric reflex on that side is the classic sign of a twisted spermatic cord. The twist cuts off the testis's blood supply, so it needs surgery at once.<br>A. Testicular cancer: a painless, hard mass; the reflex is kept.<br>C. Cryptorchidism: an undescended testis found in infancy, not a cause of sudden pain.<br>D. Prostate cancer: older men with urinary or bone symptoms; the reflex is kept.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 45 (Cremaster Muscle)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 53 (Clinical Correlates)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Montana interactive","n":"Montana (Great Falls) anterior abdominal wall, inguinal canal & scrotum interactive 2026 (D. Lawver)","sl":30,"f":"G080"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"115"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":34,"label":"Parietal lines wall, sharp pain; visceral covers organs","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G004-8.jpg","cap":"Sagittal diagram labelling parietal peritoneum, visceral peritoneum and peritoneal cavity","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 8","cu":"../sl/G004-8c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G004-10.jpg","cap":"Parietal peritoneum lines the wall; pain-sensitive and well localized","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 10"},{"src":"../sl/D041-140.jpg","cap":"Summary: parietal pain well localized, visceral pain poorly localized","where":"2025 TA review, slide 140","cu":"../sl/D041-140c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/M5_28.jpg","cap":"In panel A, parietal peritoneum lines the body wall; visceral peritoneum wraps the gut loops.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.28","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/G4_6.jpg","cap":"Parietal peritoneum lines the inside of the wall; visceral peritoneum covers the gut tube itself.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 4.6","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"G113-1-1","s":"A 24-year-old man presents to the emergency department with severe abdominal pain that initially began around his umbilicus but has now localized to the right lower quadrant. Physical examination reveals localized tenderness, guarding, and rebound tenderness. A CT scan demonstrates inflammation involving the parietal peritoneum overlying the right lower quadrant. Which of the following best explains why the patient's pain is now well localized?","o":["A. The visceral peritoneum is innervated by somatic sensory fibers that allow precise localization of pain.","B. The parietal peritoneum is innervated by somatic sensory fibers that allow precise localization of pain.","C. The visceral peritoneum is innervated by autonomic sensory fibers that allow precise localization of pain.","D. The parietal peritoneum is innervated by autonomic sensory fibers that produce poorly localized pain.","E. The peritoneal cavity contains somatic sensory nerves that directly transmit pain from abdominal organs."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"The parietal peritoneum is innervated by somatic sensory fibers that allow precise localization of pain","a":"B","why":"<b>B. The parietal peritoneum is innervated by somatic sensory fibers that allow precise localization of pain.</b> Once the inflamed appendix touches the parietal peritoneum of the right lower quadrant, somatic nerves of the body wall carry the pain, so it becomes sharp and well localized. The early pain around the umbilicus was visceral: vague and poorly localized.<br>A. The visceral peritoneum has no somatic sensory fibers.<br>C. Visceral (autonomic) pain fibers give vague, poorly localized pain.<br>D. The parietal peritoneum gets somatic nerves, and its pain is well localized.<br>E. The peritoneal cavity holds only a film of fluid; it contains no nerves.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice","n":"GI #2 practice questions Fall 2026 (Lecture #1 peritoneum, esophagus, stomach & celiac trunk; Lecture #2 small & large intestines)","sl":null,"f":"G113"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"116"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":34,"label":"Peritoneal cavity: potential space between the layers","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G004-9.jpg","cap":"Sagittal diagram: peritoneal cavity between parietal and visceral peritoneum","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 9","cu":"../sl/G004-9c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G074-40.jpg","cap":"Peritoneal cavity closed in males; open in females via tubes, uterus, vagina","where":"2026 Harlem clickers: Peritoneum, Esophagus & Stomach, slide 40"},{"src":"../tb/M5_24.jpg","cap":"In panel B, the peritoneal cavity (P) is the space between the parietal lining and the visceral covering.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.24","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-330-1","s":"Technically, the peritoneal cavity:","o":["A) contains all the abdominal organs.","B) is a closed cavity in both sexes.","C) is the potential space between the parietal and visceral peritoneum.","D) is typically open to the testis in the male.","E) contains lymph nodes."],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"is the potential space between the parietal and visceral peritoneum","a":"C","why":"<b>It is the potential space between parietal and visceral peritoneum.</b> It holds only a thin film of fluid, no organs.<br>A. Contains the organs: no; organs lie outside it, wrapped in visceral peritoneum.<br>B. Closed in both sexes: closed in males only. In females it opens to the outside through the uterine tubes, uterus and vagina.<br>D. Open to the testis: the processus vaginalis normally seals, so the male cavity is closed.<br>E. Lymph nodes: these sit in mesenteries and behind the peritoneum, not in the cavity.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 9 (Between parietal and visceral layers)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Harlem clicker","n":"Harlem peritoneum, esophagus, stomach & celiac trunk clickers 2026","sl":39,"f":"G074"},{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":330,"f":"F3903"},{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":100,"f":"F0008"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"117"},{"id":"G113-2-1","s":"A 52-year-old woman undergoes abdominal surgery for a perforated gastric ulcer. During the procedure, the surgeon identifies a space between the parietal and visceral layers of the peritoneum containing a small amount of lubricating serous fluid. The surgeon explains that this space allows abdominal organs to move against one another with minimal friction. Which of the following best describes the anatomical location of this space?","o":["A. Between the visceral peritoneum and the muscular wall of the gastrointestinal tract.","B. Between the parietal peritoneum and the transversalis fascia.","C. Between the parietal and visceral layers of the peritoneum.","D. Between the retroperitoneal organs and the posterior abdominal wall.","E. Within the abdominal organs, between their mucosal and muscular layers."],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Between the parietal and visceral layers of the peritoneum","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Between the parietal and visceral layers of the peritoneum.</b> This is the peritoneal cavity: a potential space between the layer lining the wall and the layer covering the organs. It holds only a thin film of serous fluid that lets the organs glide.<br>A. The visceral peritoneum lies directly on the organ wall; no space lies between them.<br>B. Between the parietal peritoneum and the transversalis fascia lies extraperitoneal fat, outside the cavity.<br>D. Behind the parietal peritoneum lies the retroperitoneum, also outside the cavity.<br>E. Mucosa and muscle are layers of the organ wall, not a peritoneal space.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice","n":"GI #2 practice questions Fall 2026 (Lecture #1 peritoneum, esophagus, stomach & celiac trunk; Lecture #2 small & large intestines)","sl":null,"f":"G113"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"118"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":35,"label":"Intraperitoneal vs retroperitoneal: meaning and examples","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G004-14.jpg","cap":"Sagittal section marking stomach, aorta, pancreas and uterus by peritoneal relation","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 14","cu":"../sl/G004-14c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G004-13.jpg","cap":"Fist-in-balloon model: organ invaginates the peritoneal sac","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 13","cu":"../sl/G004-13c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/D045-186.jpg","cap":"Intraperitoneal vs retroperitoneal definitions with example organs","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 186"}],"q":[{"id":"G081-20-1","s":"What does it mean that an organ is intraperitoneal?","o":["A. It lies free inside the peritoneal cavity, floating in the peritoneal fluid","B. It is almost completely covered by visceral peritoneum and is usually hung from a mesentery","C. It lies behind the parietal peritoneum, which covers only its anterior surface","D. It once had a mesentery but became fused to the posterior abdominal wall","E. It has parietal peritoneum only on its superior surface"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"It is almost completely covered by visceral peritoneum and is usually hung from a mesentery","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Almost completely covered by visceral peritoneum, usually hung from a mesentery.</b> The organ has pushed into the closed peritoneal sac like a fist into a balloon, so the sac wraps it.<br>A. Lying free in the fluid: the cavity holds only a thin film of fluid, never organs.<br>C. Covered only in front: that is a retroperitoneal organ.<br>D. Mesentery fused to the posterior wall: that is secondarily retroperitoneal.<br>E. Peritoneum only on the top surface: that is subperitoneal, like the urinary bladder.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 13 (Peritoneal organs)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive 11/11/26 (J. LaBarge)","sl":20,"f":"G081"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"119"},{"id":"G081-20-2","s":"How is a retroperitoneal organ, such as the kidney, related to the peritoneum?","o":["A. It is wrapped in visceral peritoneum and hangs from a mesentery","B. It keeps a short mesentery that lets it move freely","C. It is covered by peritoneum only on its superior surface, like the urinary bladder","D. It lies behind the parietal peritoneum, which covers only its anterior surface; its posterior surface rests on the posterior abdominal wall","E. It is covered by peritoneum only on its posterior surface, where it touches the abdominal wall"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"It lies behind the parietal peritoneum, which covers only its anterior surface; its posterior surface rests on the posterior abdominal wall","a":"D","why":"<b>D. It lies behind the parietal peritoneum, which covers only its front.</b> Its back rests on the posterior abdominal wall and it has no mesentery, so it is fixed. The kidney never had a mesentery (primarily retroperitoneal).<br>A. Wrapped and hung from a mesentery: that is an intraperitoneal organ.<br>B. A short mesentery that lets it move: retroperitoneal organs have none.<br>C. Peritoneum only on top: that is the subperitoneal bladder.<br>E. Peritoneum only behind: backwards; the back touches the wall and the front is covered.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 13 (Peritoneal organs)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive 11/11/26 (J. LaBarge)","sl":20,"f":"G081"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"120"},{"id":"F3112-40-1","s":"Which statement about retroperitoneal organs is correct?","o":["A. They are wrapped in visceral peritoneum almost all the way round","B. They are suspended from the posterior wall by a mesentery","C. They sit free inside the peritoneal cavity, bathed in fluid","D. Peritoneum covers only their front surface; they have no mesentery","E. They include the stomach, jejunum and transverse colon"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Peritoneum covers only their front surface; they have no mesentery","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Peritoneum covers only their front surface; they have no mesentery.</b> Retroperitoneal organs lie behind the parietal peritoneum, pressed against the posterior abdominal wall.<br>A. Wrapped almost all the way round: that defines intraperitoneal organs.<br>B. Suspended by a mesentery: also intraperitoneal; only intraperitoneal organs keep a mesentery.<br>C. Free inside the cavity: no organ lies in the cavity, which holds only a film of fluid.<br>E. Stomach, jejunum and transverse colon: all intraperitoneal, each on a mesentery or omentum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 13 (Peritoneal organs)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"Peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive (JL)","sl":40,"f":"F3112"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"121"},{"id":"G081-19-1","s":"Upon examination, a physician detects that an abdominal infection has spread to a retroperitoneal organ. Which of the following is retroperitoneal?","o":["A. Stomach","B. Transverse colon","C. Jejunum","D. Descending colon","E. Spleen"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Descending colon","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Descending colon.</b> It is secondarily retroperitoneal: its mesentery fused with the posterior wall, so peritoneum covers only its front and it cannot be lifted.<br>A. Stomach: intraperitoneal, hung from the greater and lesser omenta.<br>B. Transverse colon: intraperitoneal, on the transverse mesocolon.<br>C. Jejunum: intraperitoneal, on the mesentery proper.<br>E. Spleen: intraperitoneal, held by the gastrosplenic and splenorenal ligaments.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 13 (Peritoneal organs)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive 11/11/26 (J. LaBarge)","sl":19,"f":"G081"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"122"},{"id":"F3114-48-1","s":"Which of the following is retroperitoneal?","o":["A. First 2 cm of the duodenum","B. Tail of the pancreas","C. Transverse colon","D. Second (descending) part of the duodenum","E. Sigmoid colon"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Second (descending) part of the duodenum","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Second (descending) part of the duodenum.</b> SAD PUCKER lists the retroperitoneal organs: suprarenal glands, aorta and inferior vena cava, duodenum (all but its first 2 cm), pancreas (except the tail), ureters, ascending and descending colon, kidneys, abdominal esophagus, upper two-thirds of the rectum.<br>A. First 2 cm of the duodenum: intraperitoneal, with the lesser and greater omenta attached.<br>B. Tail of the pancreas: intraperitoneal, inside the splenorenal ligament.<br>C. Transverse colon: hangs on the transverse mesocolon.<br>E. Sigmoid colon: hangs on the sigmoid mesocolon.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 13 (Peritoneal organs)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 14 (Peritoneal organs)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"Small & large intestines interactive","sl":48,"f":"F3114"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"123"},{"id":"G113-5-1","s":"A 42-year-old man undergoes abdominal surgery following blunt abdominal trauma. During the procedure, the surgeon identifies an organ that is almost completely covered by visceral peritoneum, suspended from the posterior abdominal wall by a mesentery, and relatively mobile within the abdominal cavity. The organ is found to have sustained an injury resulting in intraperitoneal bleeding. Which of the following organs best fits this description?","o":["A. Ascending colon","B. Descending colon","C. Duodenum (second portion)","D. Jejunum","E. Pancreas"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Jejunum","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Jejunum.</b> An intraperitoneal organ is covered by visceral peritoneum almost all round and hangs from a mesentery, so it can move. The jejunum hangs from the mesentery proper, so an injury to it bleeds into the peritoneal cavity.<br>A. Ascending colon: secondarily retroperitoneal, fixed to the posterior wall.<br>B. Descending colon: secondarily retroperitoneal, fixed.<br>C. Second part of the duodenum: secondarily retroperitoneal; only the first 2 cm is intraperitoneal.<br>E. Pancreas: secondarily retroperitoneal, except its tail.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice","n":"GI #2 practice questions Fall 2026 (Lecture #1 peritoneum, esophagus, stomach & celiac trunk; Lecture #2 small & large intestines)","sl":null,"f":"G113"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"124"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":35,"label":"Secondarily retroperitoneal: pancreas, duodenum, ascending/descending colon","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G001-8.jpg","cap":"Retroperitoneal vs secondarily retroperitoneal organs, each with its organ list","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 8","cu":"../sl/G001-8c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/D041-148.jpg","cap":"SAD PUCKER list; secondary: duodenum 2-4, pancreas except tail, colon","where":"2025 TA review, slide 148","cu":"../sl/D041-148c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G005-63.jpg","cap":"Ascending colon on posterior wall: secondarily retroperitoneal","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 63","cu":"../sl/G005-63c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"F3112-39-1","s":"While exploring the open abdominal cavity in lab, you notice that some organs are easily movable while others are stationary. You remember this has something to do with organs either having mesenteries or being retroperitoneal. Which of the following organs would you not be able to easily move about?","o":["A. Transverse colon","B. Ascending colon","C. Appendix","D. Jejunum","E. Sigmoid colon"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Ascending colon","a":"B","why":"<b>Ascending colon.</b> It is secondarily retroperitoneal. Its mesentery fused to the posterior abdominal wall, so it is fixed in place and has peritoneum only on its front.<br>A. Transverse colon: hangs on the transverse mesocolon, so it moves.<br>C. Appendix: has its own mesoappendix.<br>D. Jejunum: hangs on the mesentery proper.<br>E. Sigmoid colon: hangs on the sigmoid mesocolon.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 13 (Peritoneal organs)","Small & Large Intestines slide 63 (Ascending colon)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"Peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive (JL)","sl":39,"f":"F3112"},{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":316,"f":"F3903"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"125"},{"id":"G082-9-1","s":"A 45-year-old patient complains of pain in the upper belly that radiates to their lower back, fever, and vomiting. They also have a history of high triglycerides and non-alcoholic fatty liver disease. The doctor diagnoses them with pancreatitis. How is the majority of the pancreas related to the peritoneum?","o":["A. Intraperitoneal","B. Primary retroperitoneal","C. Secondarily retroperitoneal","D. Extraperitoneal","E. Supraperitoneal"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Secondarily retroperitoneal","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Secondarily retroperitoneal.</b> The pancreas grew inside a mesentery, was then pressed against the posterior abdominal wall, and that mesentery fused away. Only its tail, inside the splenorenal ligament, stays intraperitoneal.<br>A. Intraperitoneal: true only of the tail.<br>B. Primary retroperitoneal: organs that never had a mesentery, such as the kidneys, ureters, aorta and inferior vena cava.<br>D. Extraperitoneal: a loose word for anything outside the peritoneum, not this organ's category.<br>E. Supraperitoneal: not a real category.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 13 (Peritoneal organs)","Pancreas & Spleen slide 11 (Pancreas)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Montana interactive","n":"Montana (Great Falls) peritoneum, esophagus, stomach & celiac trunk interactive 2026 (D. Lawver)","sl":9,"f":"G082"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"126"},{"id":"G113-6-1","s":"A 56-year-old woman undergoes abdominal CT imaging following a motor vehicle accident. The radiologist identifies a hematoma surrounding an abdominal organ located posterior to the parietal peritoneum. During surgery, the surgeon explains that this organ developed outside the peritoneal cavity and remained in that position throughout embryological development. Which of the following organs is most likely being described?","o":["A. Descending colon","B. Pancreas","C. Duodenum (third portion)","D. Kidney","E. Ascending colon"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Kidney","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Kidney.</b> The kidney is primarily retroperitoneal: it formed behind the peritoneum and never had a mesentery. A hematoma around it stays behind the parietal peritoneum.<br>A. Descending colon: secondarily retroperitoneal; it began on a mesentery that later fused to the posterior wall.<br>B. Pancreas: secondarily retroperitoneal, except its tail.<br>C. Third part of the duodenum: secondarily retroperitoneal.<br>E. Ascending colon: secondarily retroperitoneal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice","n":"GI #2 practice questions Fall 2026 (Lecture #1 peritoneum, esophagus, stomach & celiac trunk; Lecture #2 small & large intestines)","sl":null,"f":"G113"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"127"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":36,"label":"Ligament, omentum, mesentery: double-layer definitions","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G004-48.jpg","cap":"Table defining omenta, mesenteries and peritoneal ligaments as double layers","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 48"},{"src":"../sl/G004-23.jpg","cap":"Mesentery joins an intraperitoneal organ to the posterior wall","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 23"},{"src":"../tb/G4_52.jpg","cap":"In panel A, a mesentery is a double layer of peritoneum joining the visceral covering to the parietal lining.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 4.52","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/M5_27.jpg","cap":"Named double folds: lesser omentum (hepatogastric, hepatoduodenal) and greater omentum (gastrocolic, gastrosplenic, gastrophrenic).","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.27","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"G113-3-1","s":"A 35-year-old woman undergoes abdominal surgery for a small intestinal obstruction. During the procedure, the surgeon identifies a double layer of peritoneum that attaches the jejunum and ileum to the posterior abdominal wall. The structure contains blood vessels, lymphatics, and nerves supplying the small intestine. Which of the following best describes the anatomical structure identified by the surgeon?","o":["A. A peritoneal ligament connecting two abdominal organs.","B. A mesentery connecting an intraperitoneal organ to the posterior abdominal wall.","C. A lesser omentum connecting the stomach to the liver.","D. A greater omentum connecting the stomach to the transverse colon.","E. A peritoneal fold connecting the abdominal wall to the diaphragm."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"A mesentery connecting an intraperitoneal organ to the posterior abdominal wall","a":"B","why":"<b>B. A mesentery connecting an intraperitoneal organ to the posterior abdominal wall.</b> The mesentery proper is a double layer of peritoneum that hangs the jejunum and ileum from the posterior wall. Their vessels, lymphatics and nerves run between its two layers.<br>A. A peritoneal ligament joins one organ to another or to the wall, such as the falciform ligament; a fold from gut to posterior wall is a mesentery.<br>C. The lesser omentum joins the stomach to the liver.<br>D. The greater omentum joins the stomach to the transverse colon.<br>E. A fold between the wall and the diaphragm would not reach the small intestine.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice","n":"GI #2 practice questions Fall 2026 (Lecture #1 peritoneum, esophagus, stomach & celiac trunk; Lecture #2 small & large intestines)","sl":null,"f":"G113"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"128"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":36,"label":"Hepatorenal ligament: liver to right kidney","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G004-37.jpg","cap":"Peritoneum passes from the right liver lobe onto the right kidney here","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 37"}],"q":[{"id":"G081-31-4","s":"Which two structures does the hepatorenal ligament connect?","o":["A. Liver and the lesser curvature of the stomach","B. Liver and the first part of the duodenum","C. Liver and the anterior abdominal wall","D. Liver and right kidney","E. Spleen and left kidney"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Liver and right kidney","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Liver and right kidney.</b> The hepatorenal ligament is a peritoneal fold from the back of the right lobe of the liver to the right kidney. The hepatorenal recess (Morison pouch) lies between the same two organs.<br>A. Liver to lesser curvature: hepatogastric ligament.<br>B. Liver to first part of the duodenum: hepatoduodenal ligament.<br>C. Liver to anterior abdominal wall: falciform ligament.<br>E. Spleen to left kidney: splenorenal (lienorenal) ligament.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 37 (Subhepatic Recess)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive 11/11/26 (J. LaBarge)","sl":31,"f":"G081"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"129"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":36,"label":"Mesentery root: duodenojejunal junction to right sacroiliac joint","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G004-25.jpg","cap":"Root of mesentery (green) running obliquely down to the right","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 25","cu":"../sl/G004-25c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G004-26.jpg","cap":"Posterior wall: root of mesentery, duodenojejunal junction, transverse mesocolon root","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 26","cu":"../sl/G004-26c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/M5_28.jpg","cap":"In panel B, the root of the mesentery of the small intestine runs obliquely down and to the right.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.28","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/M5_44.jpg","cap":"In panel B, the mesentery fans out from its root starting at the duodenojejunal junction.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.44","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-373-1","s":"While assisting in a bowel resection the surgeon asked you to extend the small bowel from its root. Where do you find the origin of the root of the mesentery?","o":["A. Directed obliquely to the left from the approximately the left side of the L2 vertebra to the left sacroiliac joint","B. Directed obliquely to the right from the duodenojejunal junction to the right (ileocolic junction)","C. Directed obliquely to the right from approximately the right side of the L2 vertebra to the right side of the sacroiliac joint","D. Directed approximately vertical from the lateral aspect of the left paracolic gutter to the rectum.","E. Directed approximately horizontally from the right to the left paracoloic gutters at the L2 vertebra"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Directed obliquely to the right from the duodenojejunal junction to the right (ileocolic junction)","a":"B","why":"<b>It runs from the duodenojejunal junction, on the left of the second lumbar vertebra (L2), obliquely down and to the right to the ileocolic junction and right sacroiliac joint.</b> The root is about fifteen centimetres long.<br>A. Wrong direction; it runs to the right, not to the left sacroiliac joint.<br>C. It starts on the left side of L2, not the right.<br>D. No mesentery runs vertically from the left paracolic gutter to the rectum.<br>E. A roughly horizontal root is the transverse mesocolon, along the lower border of the pancreas.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 25 (Root of Mesentery)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Harlem clicker","n":"Harlem small & large intestines clickers 2026","sl":18,"f":"G109"},{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":373,"f":"F3903"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"130"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":37,"label":"Greater omentum: greater curvature to transverse colon","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G004-21.jpg","cap":"Greater omentum parts starred: gastrophrenic, gastrosplenic, gastrocolic ligaments","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 21","cu":"../sl/G004-21c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G004-20.jpg","cap":"Greater omentum from greater curvature and duodenum to transverse colon","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 20","cu":"../sl/G004-20c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"G081-31-3","s":"The gastrocolic ligament is part of which peritoneal fold, and what does it connect?","o":["A. Lesser omentum; the liver to the lesser curvature of the stomach","B. Greater omentum; the greater curvature of the stomach to the transverse colon","C. Transverse mesocolon; the transverse colon to the posterior abdominal wall","D. Greater omentum; the greater curvature of the stomach to the spleen","E. Lesser omentum; the first part of the duodenum to the transverse colon"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Greater omentum; the greater curvature of the stomach to the transverse colon","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Greater omentum; greater curvature of the stomach to the transverse colon.</b> The gastrocolic ligament is the apron part of the greater omentum and the front wall of the lower lesser sac.<br>A. Lesser omentum, liver to lesser curvature: that is the hepatogastric ligament.<br>C. Transverse mesocolon: hangs the transverse colon from the posterior wall; it does not touch the stomach.<br>D. Greater curvature to spleen: that is the gastrosplenic ligament.<br>E. Lesser omentum to the transverse colon: no part of the lesser omentum reaches the colon.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 20 (Greater omentum)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 21 (Parts of greater omentum)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive 11/11/26 (J. LaBarge)","sl":31,"f":"G081"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"131"},{"id":"G113-4-1","s":"A 58-year-old man undergoes surgery for a gastric tumor. During the procedure, the surgeon identifies a large, apron-like fold of peritoneum that extends from the greater curvature of the stomach, hangs anterior to the small intestine, and attaches to the transverse colon. The surgeon explains that this structure contains variable amounts of fat and can help limit the spread of intra-abdominal infection. Which of the following best identifies this structure and distinguishes it from a peritoneal ligament?","o":["A. Lesser omentum; a double layer of peritoneum connecting the stomach to the liver.","B. Mesentery proper; a double layer of peritoneum suspending the small intestine from the posterior abdominal wall.","C. Greater omentum; a peritoneal fold connecting the stomach to the transverse colon and extending over the small intestine.","D. Falciform ligament; a peritoneal fold connecting the liver to the anterior abdominal wall.","E. Gastrosplenic ligament; a peritoneal fold connecting the stomach to the spleen."],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Greater omentum; a peritoneal fold connecting the stomach to the transverse colon and extending over the small intestine","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Greater omentum; a peritoneal fold connecting the stomach to the transverse colon and extending over the small intestine.</b> This fatty, four-layered apron hangs from the greater curvature in front of the small intestine and folds back up to the transverse colon. An omentum is a fold that leaves the stomach; a peritoneal ligament is a double layer joining one organ to another or to the wall.<br>A. The lesser omentum runs from the lesser curvature to the liver.<br>B. The mesentery proper holds the jejunum and ileum to the posterior wall.<br>D. The falciform ligament joins the liver to the anterior wall.<br>E. The gastrosplenic ligament runs from the greater curvature to the spleen.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice","n":"GI #2 practice questions Fall 2026 (Lecture #1 peritoneum, esophagus, stomach & celiac trunk; Lecture #2 small & large intestines)","sl":null,"f":"G113"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"132"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":37,"label":"Greater omentum walls off infection","slides":[{"src":"../sl/D068-40.jpg","cap":"Greater omentum: fatty four-layer apron that helps limit spread of abdominal infection","where":"2026 Middletown TA weekly, slide 40","cu":"../sl/D068-40c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"G113-11-1","s":"A 62-year-old woman undergoes abdominal surgery for a suspected intra-abdominal infection. During the procedure, the surgeon observes that a large, fatty peritoneal fold extending from the greater curvature of the stomach is adherent to an area of localized inflammation near the transverse colon. The surgeon explains that this structure can help limit the spread of infection by adhering to inflamed abdominal organs. Which of the following best identifies this structure and describes its anatomical relationship?","o":["A. Falciform ligament; connects the liver to the anterior abdominal wall and diaphragm.","B. Lesser omentum; connects the liver to the lesser curvature of the stomach and proximal duodenum.","C. Greater omentum; extends from the greater curvature of the stomach, hangs anterior to the small intestine, and attaches to the transverse colon.","D. Transverse mesocolon; connects the greater curvature of the stomach to the transverse colon.","E. Hepatoduodenal ligament; connects the liver to the transverse colon and contains the portal triad."],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Greater omentum; extends from the greater curvature of the stomach, hangs anterior to the small intestine, and attaches to the transverse colon","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Greater omentum; extends from the greater curvature, hangs in front of the small intestine and attaches to the transverse colon.</b> This fatty apron is full of immune cells. It moves to an inflamed or perforated organ and sticks to it, walling off the infection so it cannot spread.<br>A. The falciform ligament joins the liver to the anterior wall and diaphragm; it does not move to inflamed bowel.<br>B. The lesser omentum runs from the lesser curvature to the liver.<br>D. The transverse mesocolon holds the transverse colon to the posterior wall, not to the stomach.<br>E. The hepatoduodenal ligament joins the liver to the first part of the duodenum, not to the colon.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice","n":"GI #2 practice questions Fall 2026 (Lecture #1 peritoneum, esophagus, stomach & celiac trunk; Lecture #2 small & large intestines)","sl":null,"f":"G113"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"133"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":37,"label":"Pancreatic tail lies in splenorenal ligament","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G004-43.jpg","cap":"Lesser sac opened: tail of pancreas lies in the splenorenal ligament","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 43"},{"src":"../sl/G007-11.jpg","cap":"Pancreatic tail passes between splenorenal ligament layers with the splenic vessels","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 11","cu":"../sl/G007-11c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/M5_24.jpg","cap":"In panel A, the splenorenal ligament runs from the left kidney to the spleen, carrying the splenic artery and pancreatic tail.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.24","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/N266.jpg","cap":"Find the tail of the pancreas reaching the spleen inside the splenorenal (lienorenal) ligament.","where":"Netter Atlas 6e, Plate 266","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-304-1","s":"Due to his fluctuating levels of insulin, a 62 year old male undergoes a series of tests. A tumor is detected in the tail of the pancreas. The tumor’s definite borders would make it easy to excise. However, before the surgeon can reach the tumor, which of the following ligaments needs to be dissected?","o":["A. Splenorenal","B. Gastrosplenic","C. Gastrocolic","D. Hepatorenal","E. Hepatogastric"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Splenorenal","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Splenorenal.</b> The pancreatic tail runs between the two layers of the splenorenal ligament, with the splenic vessels, to the splenic hilum. To reach a tail tumor, that ligament must be opened.<br>B. Gastrosplenic: joins the spleen to the stomach's greater curvature.<br>C. Gastrocolic: greater omentum from stomach to transverse colon.<br>D. Hepatorenal: joins the liver to the right kidney.<br>E. Hepatogastric: lesser omentum from liver to lesser curvature.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Pancreas & Spleen slide 11 (Pancreas)","Pancreas & Spleen slide 23 (Spleen)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":304,"f":"F3903"},{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":106,"f":"F0008"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"134"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":38,"label":"Lesser omentum: hepatogastric plus hepatoduodenal ligaments","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G004-22.jpg","cap":"Lesser omentum parts circled: hepatogastric and hepatoduodenal ligaments","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 22"},{"src":"../sl/G006-16.jpg","cap":"Lesser omentum: hepatogastric and hepatoduodenal parts, portal triad in free edge","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 16"},{"src":"../sl/G004-49.jpg","cap":"Table: hepatogastric to lesser curvature, hepatoduodenal to first duodenum","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 49"},{"src":"../tb/G4_57.jpg","cap":"The lesser omentum runs from liver to lesser curvature: hepatogastric part on the left, hepatoduodenal part on the right.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 4.57","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"G081-31-5","s":"The hepatogastric (gastrohepatic) ligament is part of which peritoneal fold, and what does it connect?","o":["A. Greater omentum; the liver to the greater curvature of the stomach","B. Lesser omentum; the liver to the first part of the duodenum","C. Lesser omentum; the liver to the lesser curvature of the stomach","D. Falciform ligament; the liver to the anterior abdominal wall","E. Greater omentum; the stomach to the diaphragm"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Lesser omentum; the liver to the lesser curvature of the stomach","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Lesser omentum; liver to the lesser curvature of the stomach.</b> The hepatogastric (gastrohepatic) ligament is the thin left part of the lesser omentum.<br>A. Greater omentum, liver to greater curvature: no fold joins the liver to the greater curvature.<br>B. Lesser omentum, liver to first part of the duodenum: that is the hepatoduodenal ligament, which holds the portal triad.<br>D. Falciform ligament: joins the liver to the anterior abdominal wall.<br>E. Stomach to diaphragm: that is the gastrophrenic ligament, part of the greater omentum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 22 (Parts of lesser omentum)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive 11/11/26 (J. LaBarge)","sl":31,"f":"G081"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"135"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":38,"label":"Portal triad runs in the hepatoduodenal ligament","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G004-22.jpg","cap":"Hepatoduodenal ligament circled, labelled as containing the portal triad","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 22"},{"src":"../sl/G006-16.jpg","cap":"Hepatoduodenal ligament (enclosing portal triad) vs hepatogastric ligament","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 16"}],"q":[{"id":"G081-36-2","s":"In which ligament does the portal triad run?","o":["A. Hepatogastric ligament","B. Falciform ligament","C. Gastrocolic ligament","D. Coronary ligament","E. Hepatoduodenal ligament (free edge of the lesser omentum)"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Hepatoduodenal ligament (free edge of the lesser omentum)","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Hepatoduodenal ligament.</b> It is the thick free right edge of the lesser omentum, running from the liver to the first part of the duodenum, and it carries the proper hepatic artery, portal vein and bile duct.<br>A. Hepatogastric ligament: the thin left part of the lesser omentum; it carries the gastric vessels, not the triad.<br>B. Falciform ligament: liver to anterior wall; carries the round ligament of the liver.<br>C. Gastrocolic ligament: part of the greater omentum.<br>D. Coronary ligament: liver to diaphragm.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 22 (Parts of lesser omentum)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 44 (Epiploic or Omental (Winslow’s) Foramen)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive 11/11/26 (J. LaBarge)","sl":36,"f":"G081"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"136"},{"id":"F3112-29-1","s":"In which ligament does the portal triad run?","o":["A. Hepatogastric ligament","B. Falciform ligament","C. Hepatoduodenal ligament","D. Splenorenal ligament","E. Coronary ligament"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Hepatoduodenal ligament","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Hepatoduodenal ligament.</b> It is the thick free right edge of the lesser omentum. That free edge forms the front wall of the epiploic (omental) foramen, the opening that joins the greater sac to the lesser sac (omental bursa).<br>A. Hepatogastric ligament: the thin left part of the lesser omentum, carrying the gastric vessels of the lesser curvature.<br>B. Falciform ligament: carries the round ligament of the liver and the paraumbilical veins.<br>D. Splenorenal ligament: carries the splenic vessels and the pancreatic tail.<br>E. Coronary ligament: outlines the bare area of the liver.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 22 (Parts of lesser omentum)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 44 (Epiploic or Omental (Winslow’s) Foramen)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"Peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive (JL)","sl":29,"f":"F3112"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"137"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":39,"label":"Portal triad: hepatic artery, portal vein, bile duct","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G006-16.jpg","cap":"Portal triad labelled: proper hepatic artery, portal vein, bile duct","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 16"},{"src":"../sl/G004-46.jpg","cap":"Axial section: portal triad in hepatoduodenal ligament, in front of omental foramen","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 46"},{"src":"../sl/G074-15.jpg","cap":"Hepatoduodenal ligament, part of lesser omentum, labelled as containing the portal triad","where":"2026 Harlem clickers: Peritoneum, Esophagus & Stomach, slide 15"},{"src":"../tb/M5_24.jpg","cap":"In panel A, the triad in the free edge: common bile duct, portal vein and hepatic artery.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.24","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/G4_114.jpg","cap":"Follow the hepatic artery proper, portal vein and bile duct running together up to the liver.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 4.114","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"G081-36-1","s":"Which three structures make up the portal triad?","o":["A. Common hepatic artery, common hepatic duct, hepatic veins","B. Proper hepatic artery, common bile duct, hepatic veins","C. Gastroduodenal artery, common bile duct, superior mesenteric vein","D. Proper hepatic artery, common bile duct, hepatic portal vein","E. Proper hepatic artery, cystic duct, inferior vena cava"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Proper hepatic artery, common bile duct, hepatic portal vein","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Proper hepatic artery, common bile duct, hepatic portal vein.</b> All three run in the free edge of the lesser omentum, the hepatoduodenal ligament: bile duct on the right, artery on the left, portal vein behind.<br>A. Common hepatic artery, common hepatic duct, hepatic veins: the common hepatic artery lies lower, along the pancreas; the hepatic veins drain into the inferior vena cava.<br>B. Hepatic veins: never in the ligament.<br>C. Gastroduodenal artery and superior mesenteric vein: lie behind the duodenum and pancreas.<br>E. Cystic duct and inferior vena cava: gallbladder duct above; vena cava behind the foramen.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 22 (Parts of lesser omentum)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive 11/11/26 (J. LaBarge)","sl":36,"f":"G081"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"138"},{"id":"F3903-326-1","s":"A 47-year-old Asian man is diagnosed with carcinoma of the stomach. During a gastric resection, the surgeon wants to remove the lesser omentum because of tumor extension into it. Which of the following structures lie in the free edge of the lesser omentum and consequently must be dissected out in order to be preserved?","o":["A. Common bile duct, cystic duct, and hepatic artery","B. Cystic duct, hepatic artery, and hepatic vein","C. Hepatic vein and cystic duct","D. Portal vein, common bile duct, and hepatic artery","E. Portal vein, hepatic artery, and hepatic vein"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Portal vein, common bile duct, and hepatic artery","a":"D","why":"<b>Portal vein, common bile duct and hepatic artery.</b> This is the portal triad in the free edge of the lesser omentum (the hepatoduodenal ligament).<br>A. Leaves out the portal vein, the largest structure there. The cystic duct belongs to the gallbladder and is not part of the triad.<br>B, C and E. The hepatic veins leave the back of the liver into the IVC (inferior vena cava); they are never in the lesser omentum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 44 (Epiploic or Omental (Winslow’s) Foramen)","Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 16 (Review of lesser omentum)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Harlem clicker","n":"Harlem peritoneum, esophagus, stomach & celiac trunk clickers 2026","sl":13,"f":"G074"},{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":326,"f":"F3903"},{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":96,"f":"F0008"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"139"},{"id":"G113-10-1","s":"A 54-year-old man undergoes surgery for a liver injury following blunt abdominal trauma. During the procedure, the surgeon identifies a double layer of peritoneum extending from the liver to the lesser curvature of the stomach and proximal duodenum. The surgeon carefully examines its free edge because it contains three important structures that must be preserved. Which of the following structures would the surgeon expect to find within this peritoneal fold?","o":["A. Splenic artery, splenic vein, and pancreatic duct.","B. Left gastric artery, splenic artery, and common hepatic artery","C. Superior mesenteric artery, superior mesenteric vein, and inferior vena cava.","D. Proper hepatic artery, hepatic portal vein, and common bile duct.","E. Cystic artery, cystic duct, and hepatic vein."],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Proper hepatic artery, hepatic portal vein, and common bile duct","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Proper hepatic artery, hepatic portal vein and common bile duct.</b> The fold is the lesser omentum. Its free right edge, the hepatoduodenal ligament, carries the portal triad: bile duct in front on the right, proper hepatic artery in front on the left, portal vein behind both.<br>A. The splenic vessels run along the pancreas to the spleen; the pancreatic duct lies inside the pancreas.<br>B. The left gastric, splenic and common hepatic arteries branch from the celiac trunk below and behind the lesser sac.<br>C. The superior mesenteric vessels lie behind the pancreatic neck; the inferior vena cava lies behind the epiploic foramen.<br>E. The cystic artery and duct serve the gallbladder; the hepatic veins drain the liver into the inferior vena cava.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice","n":"GI #2 practice questions Fall 2026 (Lecture #1 peritoneum, esophagus, stomach & celiac trunk; Lecture #2 small & large intestines)","sl":null,"f":"G113"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"140"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":39,"label":"Free-edge vessels: proper hepatic artery, portal vein","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G006-16.jpg","cap":"Proper hepatic artery and portal vein inside the hepatoduodenal ligament","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 16"},{"src":"../sl/G007-11.jpg","cap":"Portal triad in right free margin of lesser omentum, above the pancreas","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 11","cu":"../sl/G007-11c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/G4_54.jpg","cap":"In the lesser omentum's free edge: hepatic artery proper, portal vein and bile duct, in front of the omental foramen.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 4.54","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/G4_114.jpg","cap":"The hepatic artery proper continues from the common hepatic artery after the gastroduodenal branch, beside the portal vein.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 4.114","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-308-1","s":"A liver is finally available for a 47-year-old patient on the transplant list. During the transplant procedure, the resident compresses the hepatoduodenal ligament to control the bleeding in the surgical field. Which artery is the resident compressing?","o":["A. Gastroduodenal","B. Common hepatic","C. Right gastric","D. Proper hepatic","E. Celiac"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Proper hepatic","a":"D","why":"<b>Proper hepatic artery.</b> It is the artery in the hepatoduodenal ligament, beside the portal vein and bile duct. Squeezing the ligament (Pringle maneuver) stops its flow.<br>A. Gastroduodenal: leaves the common hepatic and runs behind the first part of the duodenum.<br>B. Common hepatic: runs along the top of the pancreas; it becomes the proper hepatic only after giving off the gastroduodenal.<br>C. Right gastric: runs along the lesser curvature.<br>E. Celiac trunk: leaves the aorta at the twelfth thoracic vertebra, behind the lesser sac.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 44 (Epiploic or Omental (Winslow’s) Foramen)","Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 16 (Review of lesser omentum)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":308,"f":"F3903"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"141"},{"id":"F3112-28-1","s":"A liver is finally available for a 47-year-old patient on the transplant list. During the transplant procedure, the resident compresses the hepatoduodenal ligament to control the bleeding in the surgical field. Which vein is the resident compressing?","o":["A. Gastroduodenal","B. Hepatic","C. Right gastric","D. Proper hepatic","E. Hepatic portal"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Hepatic portal","a":"E","why":"<b>Hepatic portal vein.</b> It is the only large vein in the hepatoduodenal ligament and lies behind the artery and bile duct. Squeezing this ligament (the Pringle maneuver) stops blood flowing into the liver.<br>A. Gastroduodenal: an artery behind the duodenum.<br>B. Hepatic veins: leave the back of the liver straight into the IVC (inferior vena cava), outside the ligament.<br>C. Right gastric: small vessels along the lesser curvature.<br>D. Proper hepatic: an artery, not a vein.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 22 (Parts of lesser omentum)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 44 (Epiploic or Omental (Winslow’s) Foramen)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive 11/11/26 (J. LaBarge)","sl":35,"f":"G081"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"Peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive (JL)","sl":28,"f":"F3112"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"142"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":40,"label":"Lesser sac: behind stomach, opens via epiploic foramen","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G004-46.jpg","cap":"Axial section: omental bursa behind stomach, entered through omental foramen","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 46"},{"src":"../sl/G004-47.jpg","cap":"Sagittal sections: omental bursa between stomach and pancreas","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 47","cu":"../sl/G004-47c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G004-40.jpg","cap":"Lesser sac closed except for its epiploic foramen opening","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 40","cu":"../sl/G004-40c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"G081-36-4","s":"The omental (epiploic) foramen connects which two spaces?","o":["A. Greater sac and lesser sac (omental bursa)","B. Right and left subphrenic recesses","C. Peritoneal cavity and the retroperitoneum","D. Supracolic and infracolic compartments","E. Lesser sac and the pericardial cavity"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Greater sac and lesser sac (omental bursa)","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Greater sac and lesser sac (omental bursa).</b> The foramen is the only natural opening of the lesser sac, on its right side, behind the hepatoduodenal ligament.<br>B. Right and left subphrenic recesses: both in the greater sac above the liver, split by the falciform ligament.<br>C. Peritoneal cavity and retroperitoneum: no opening joins them; parietal peritoneum separates them.<br>D. Supracolic and infracolic compartments: both parts of the greater sac, linked by the paracolic gutters.<br>E. Pericardial cavity: a separate serous cavity in the thorax.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 44 (Epiploic or Omental (Winslow’s) Foramen)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 33 (Lesser Sac (aka Omental Bursa))"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive 11/11/26 (J. LaBarge)","sl":36,"f":"G081"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"143"},{"id":"G082-4-1","s":"A 65-year-old patient who is undergoing dialysis complains of belly pain, bloating, loss of appetite and diarrhea. They are diagnosed with bacterial peritonitis that has spread from the greater sac to the lesser sac. What structure allowed for this infection to spread to the lesser sac?","o":["A. Greater omentum","B. Lesser omentum","C. Omental foramen","D. Pyloric sphincter","E. Transverse mesocolon"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Omental foramen","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Omental (epiploic) foramen.</b> It is the only opening between the greater sac and the lesser sac (omental bursa), just behind the free edge of the lesser omentum, so infection passes through it.<br>A. Greater omentum: an apron from the greater curvature to the transverse colon, not an opening.<br>B. Lesser omentum: forms the front wall of the lesser sac; it does not join the sacs.<br>D. Pyloric sphincter: a valve inside the gut, not in the peritoneal cavity.<br>E. Transverse mesocolon: divides the greater sac into upper and lower compartments.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 44 (Epiploic or Omental (Winslow’s) Foramen)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 33 (Lesser Sac (aka Omental Bursa))"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Montana interactive","n":"Montana (Great Falls) peritoneum, esophagus, stomach & celiac trunk interactive 2026 (D. Lawver)","sl":4,"f":"G082"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"144"},{"id":"G111-33-1","s":"A patient presents with acute abdominal pain and fever. Examination of her abdomen reveals fluid (ascites) within the lesser sac, which is now draining into the greater peritoneal sac. Which of the following pathways accounts for the seepage of fluid from the lesser to the greater sac?","o":["A. Epiploic foramen","B. Left paracolic gutter","C. Posterior fornix","D. Right paracolic gutter","E. Vesico-uterine pouch"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Epiploic foramen","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Epiploic foramen.</b> The lesser sac (omental bursa) is closed on every side except the epiploic (omental) foramen, just behind the free edge of the lesser omentum. Fluid in the lesser sac can reach the greater sac only through it.<br>B. Left paracolic gutter: a channel of the greater sac beside the descending colon.<br>C. Posterior fornix: the deep recess of the vagina behind the cervix, outside the peritoneal cavity.<br>D. Right paracolic gutter: a greater-sac channel from the hepatorenal recess to the pelvis.<br>E. Vesicouterine pouch: a shallow pelvic pouch of the greater sac.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 44 (Epiploic or Omental (Winslow’s) Foramen)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 33 (Lesser Sac (aka Omental Bursa))"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown liver, gallbladder, spleen & portal vein interactive 2026 (J. LaBarge)","sl":33,"f":"G111"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"145"},{"id":"G113-7-1","s":"A 45-year-old man undergoes abdominal surgery for a perforated gastric ulcer. During the procedure, the surgeon enters the lesser sac to access the posterior surface of the stomach and the anterior surface of the pancreas. The surgeon identifies the epiploic (omental) foramen as the natural communication between this space and the remainder of the peritoneal cavity. Which of the following correctly describes the relationship between the lesser sac and the greater sac?","o":["A. The lesser sac is located anterior to the stomach, and it communicates with the greater sac through the hepatoduodenal ligament.","B. The lesser sac is located inferior to the transverse colon, and it communicates with the greater sac through the transverse mesocolon.","C. The lesser sac is located posterior to the pancreas, and it communicates with the greater sac through the inferior vena cava.","D. The lesser sac is located between the liver and diaphragm, and it communicates with the greater sac through the falciform ligament.","E. The lesser sac is located posterior to the stomach, and it communicates with the greater sac through the epiploic foramen, which is bounded anteriorly by the hepatoduodenal ligament."],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"The lesser sac is located posterior to the stomach, and it communicates with the greater sac through the epiploic foramen, which is bounded anteriorly by the hepatoduodenal ligament","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Posterior to the stomach; it opens into the greater sac through the epiploic foramen, bounded in front by the hepatoduodenal ligament.</b> The lesser sac (omental bursa) lies behind the stomach and lesser omentum and in front of the pancreas. Its only opening is the epiploic foramen, just behind the free edge of the lesser omentum.<br>A. The sac is behind the stomach; the hepatoduodenal ligament is a border of the opening, not the opening.<br>B. Below the transverse colon lies the infracolic part of the greater sac.<br>C. The sac is in front of the pancreas; the inferior vena cava is the back border of the foramen, not a passage.<br>D. Between liver and diaphragm lies the subphrenic recess, which the falciform ligament divides.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice","n":"GI #2 practice questions Fall 2026 (Lecture #1 peritoneum, esophagus, stomach & celiac trunk; Lecture #2 small & large intestines)","sl":null,"f":"G113"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"146"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":40,"label":"Fluid in the lesser sac (omental bursa)","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G004-46.jpg","cap":"Cross-section: omental bursa (dark blue) behind stomach, entered through omental foramen","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 46"},{"src":"../sl/G004-64.jpg","cap":"Posterior gastric perforation or pancreatic fluid passes into the omental bursa","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 64"},{"src":"../sl/G004-47.jpg","cap":"Sagittal view: omental bursa behind stomach with superior and inferior recesses","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 47","cu":"../sl/G004-47c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G074-12.jpg","cap":"Lesser sac: closed except epiploic foramen; superior, inferior and splenic recesses","where":"2026 Harlem clickers: Peritoneum, Esophagus & Stomach, slide 12","cu":"../sl/G074-12c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-324-1","s":"The omental bursa:","o":["A) is a completely sealed-off recess of the peritoneal cavity.","B) is located anterior to the stomach.","C) can contain pancreatic fluid following rupture of the pancreas.","D) has medial and lateral recesses.","E) contains the portal vein."],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"can contain pancreatic fluid following rupture of the pancreas","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Can contain pancreatic fluid.</b> The omental bursa (lesser sac) lies behind the stomach and in front of the pancreas. An inflamed or injured pancreas can leak fluid into it, forming a pancreatic pseudocyst.<br>A. Not sealed: it opens into the greater sac through the omental (epiploic) foramen.<br>B. It lies behind the stomach, not in front.<br>D. Its recesses are superior, inferior and splenic, not medial and lateral.<br>E. The portal vein runs in the hepatoduodenal ligament, the front edge of the omental foramen, not inside the bursa.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 64 (Fluid in omental bursa)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 33 (Lesser Sac (aka Omental Bursa))"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Harlem clicker","n":"Harlem peritoneum, esophagus, stomach & celiac trunk clickers 2026","sl":7,"f":"G074"},{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":324,"f":"F3903"},{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":94,"f":"F0008"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"147"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":41,"label":"Epiploic foramen: caudate lobe above, duodenum below","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G004-44.jpg","cap":"Epiploic foramen borders listed; finger passed through the foramen","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 44","cu":"../sl/G004-44c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G004-45.jpg","cap":"Finger in omental foramen: liver above, lesser omentum in front","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 45"},{"src":"../sl/G074-6.jpg","cap":"Axial section: vena cava behind the omental foramen; aorta lies further left","where":"2026 Harlem clickers: Peritoneum, Esophagus & Stomach, slide 6"},{"src":"../tb/M5_39.jpg","cap":"In panel A, the arrow enters the omental foramen with the caudate lobe above and the portal triad in front.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.39","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/M5_24.jpg","cap":"Portal triad in front of the foramen, inferior vena cava behind it; the aorta is not a border.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.24","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"G081-36-3","s":"Which opening lies directly behind the free edge of the hepatoduodenal ligament?","o":["A. Hepatorenal recess (Morison's pouch)","B. Omental (epiploic) foramen","C. Esophageal hiatus","D. Superior recess of the omental bursa","E. Right paracolic gutter"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Omental (epiploic) foramen","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Omental (epiploic) foramen.</b> The free edge of the lesser omentum (hepatoduodenal ligament) is the front wall of the foramen, so the opening lies just behind it and leads into the lesser sac.<br>A. Hepatorenal recess: a pocket of the greater sac to the right, between liver and right kidney.<br>C. Esophageal hiatus: an opening in the diaphragm for the esophagus.<br>D. Superior recess of the omental bursa: deep inside the lesser sac, behind the caudate lobe.<br>E. Right paracolic gutter: a channel beside the ascending colon.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 44 (Epiploic or Omental (Winslow’s) Foramen)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 45 (Is bounded superiorly by peritoneum on the caudate lobe of the liver, inferiorly)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive 11/11/26 (J. LaBarge)","sl":36,"f":"G081"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"148"},{"id":"F3903-322-1","s":"Which is not a boundary of the epiploic (omental) foramen?","o":["A. Aorta","B. Caudate lobe of the liver","C. First part of the duodenum","D. Hepatoduodenal ligament"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Aorta","a":"A","why":"<b>Aorta.</b> The aorta lies to the left, behind the lesser sac; it does not border the foramen. The back wall is peritoneum on the IVC (inferior vena cava).<br>B. Caudate lobe of the liver: superior boundary.<br>C. First part of the duodenum: inferior boundary.<br>D. Hepatoduodenal ligament: anterior boundary (free edge of the lesser omentum).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 44 (Epiploic or Omental (Winslow’s) Foramen)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Harlem clicker","n":"Harlem peritoneum, esophagus, stomach & celiac trunk clickers 2026","sl":2,"f":"G074"},{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":322,"f":"F3903"},{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":92,"f":"F0008"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"149"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":42,"label":"Hepatorenal pouch: lowest point when supine","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G004-62.jpg","cap":"Hepatorenal recess is the lowest, most gravity-dependent space when supine","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 62","cu":"../sl/G004-62c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G004-38.jpg","cap":"Morison pouch links to lesser sac via epiploic foramen and right gutter","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 38","cu":"../sl/G004-38c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G004-63.jpg","cap":"Lying body: fluid pools in hepatorenal pouch and pelvic pouch","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 63"},{"src":"../sl/G074-19.jpg","cap":"Morison pouch: liver in front, kidney behind; opens to lesser sac and pelvis","where":"2026 Harlem clickers: Peritoneum, Esophagus & Stomach, slide 19","cu":"../sl/G074-19c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/G4_93.jpg","cap":"The hepatorenal recess (Morison pouch) lies between the liver and the right kidney, below the subphrenic recess.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 4.93","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-328-1","s":"The hepatorenal recess (Morison pouch):","o":["A) is part of the omental bursa.","B) is in the subphrenic recess.","C) receives infected fluids draining from the omental bursa in the supine position.","D) is a potential space in the hepatorenal ligament.","E) is extraperitoneal."],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"receives infected fluids draining from the omental bursa in the supine position","a":"C","why":"<b>It receives infected fluid draining out of the omental bursa when supine.</b> Morison pouch (the hepatorenal recess) is the lowest part of the peritoneal cavity when lying down. It connects with the omental bursa through the epiploic foramen, so fluid leaving the bursa lands here.<br>A. It is part of the greater sac, not the omental bursa.<br>B. It is subhepatic (below the liver), not subphrenic (above it).<br>D. It is a recess, not a space inside a ligament.<br>E. It lies inside the peritoneal cavity, so it is not extraperitoneal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 37 (Subhepatic Recess)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 38 (Subhepatic Recess)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 62 (Spread of Pathological Fluids)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Harlem clicker","n":"Harlem peritoneum, esophagus, stomach & celiac trunk clickers 2026","sl":16,"f":"G074"},{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":328,"f":"F3903"},{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":98,"f":"F0008"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"150"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":43,"label":"Supine fluid pools in hepatorenal recess","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G004-62.jpg","cap":"Hepatorenal recess: lowest, gravity-dependent part of the peritoneal cavity when supine","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 62","cu":"../sl/G004-62c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G004-63.jpg","cap":"Supine side view: hepatorenal pouch; rectovesical/rectouterine pouch down in the pelvis","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 63"},{"src":"../sl/F3112-20.jpg","cap":"Hepatorenal recess when supine; rectouterine and rectovesical pouches in female and male pelvis","where":"2025 Middletown clicker, slide 20","cu":"../sl/F3112-20c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"F3112-18-1","s":"In a patient lying supine, where does infected peritoneal fluid collect?","o":["A. Right paracolic gutter","B. Rectouterine pouch","C. Lesser sac (omental bursa)","D. Left subphrenic recess","E. Hepatorenal recess (Morison pouch)"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Hepatorenal recess (Morison pouch)","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Hepatorenal recess (Morison pouch).</b> Fluid settles at the lowest point. Lying on the back, that is the recess between the liver in front and the right kidney behind.<br>A. Right paracolic gutter: a channel that carries fluid between the upper abdomen and the pelvis, not where it settles.<br>B. Rectouterine pouch: the lowest point when a woman is upright.<br>C. Lesser sac: closed except at the epiploic foramen; fills after a posterior gastric perforation or pancreatitis.<br>D. Left subphrenic recess: under the diaphragm, not the lowest spot when supine.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 62 (Spread of Pathological Fluids)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 37 (Subhepatic Recess)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"Peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive (JL)","sl":18,"f":"F3112"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"151"},{"id":"F3112-17-1","s":"A 50-year-old female heavy drinker is rushed to the ER due to a slightly distended abdomen and right upper quadrant diffuse pain. A diagnosis of mild ascites is made. In order to run a diagnostic test, you need to obtain a sample of the gathering fluid. If the patient is laying supine, where would the majority of the fluid be localized?","o":["A. Rectouterine pouch","B. Lesser sac","C. Right paracolic gutter","D. Rectovesical pouch","E. Hepatorenal recess"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Hepatorenal recess","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Hepatorenal recess.</b> Lying supine, the hepatorenal recess (Morrison's pouch, between liver and right kidney) is the lowest part of the peritoneal cavity, so ascites (free fluid in the belly) collects there.<br>A. Rectouterine pouch: lowest point in a standing woman.<br>B. Lesser sac: a closed space behind the stomach, open only through the omental foramen.<br>C. Right paracolic gutter: a channel linking the hepatorenal recess to the pelvis, not the low point.<br>D. Rectovesical pouch: lowest point in a standing man.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 62 (Spread of Pathological Fluids)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 37 (Subhepatic Recess)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive 11/11/26 (J. LaBarge)","sl":22,"f":"G081"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"Peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive (JL)","sl":17,"f":"F3112"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"152"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":43,"label":"Upright fluid: rectovesical (male) or rectouterine (female) pouch","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G004-63.jpg","cap":"Fluid pools in the pelvic rectovesical/rectouterine pouch and hepatorenal pouch","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 63"},{"src":"../sl/G004-14.jpg","cap":"Female sagittal section: uterovesical and rectouterine (rectovaginal) pouches","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 14","cu":"../sl/G004-14c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/M5_28.jpg","cap":"In panel A, the rectovesical pouch between rectum and bladder is the lowest space upright; in females, the rectouterine pouch.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.28","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"G081-23-1","s":"In a woman who is upright and walking (ambulant), where does infected peritoneal fluid collect?","o":["A. Hepatorenal recess","B. Rectouterine pouch","C. Vesicouterine pouch","D. Right subphrenic recess","E. Left paracolic gutter"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Rectouterine pouch","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Rectouterine pouch (of Douglas).</b> Upright, the pelvis is the lowest part of the peritoneal cavity, and in a woman its deepest point lies between the rectum and the uterus.<br>A. Hepatorenal recess: the lowest point when lying supine.<br>C. Vesicouterine pouch: between bladder and uterus, and shallower.<br>D. Right subphrenic recess: the highest point when upright; lowest only upside down.<br>E. Left paracolic gutter: a channel fluid runs along, not where it settles.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 61 (Spread of Pathological Fluids)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 62 (Spread of Pathological Fluids)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive 11/11/26 (J. LaBarge)","sl":23,"f":"G081"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"153"},{"id":"G081-23-2","s":"In a man who is upright and walking (ambulant), where does infected peritoneal fluid collect?","o":["A. Rectouterine pouch","B. Hepatorenal recess","C. Rectovesical pouch","D. Inferior recess of the omental bursa","E. Right paracolic gutter"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Rectovesical pouch","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Rectovesical pouch.</b> Upright, fluid sinks into the pelvis. A man has no uterus, so the deepest pouch lies between the rectum and the bladder.<br>A. Rectouterine pouch: exists only in women.<br>B. Hepatorenal recess: the lowest point when lying supine.<br>D. Inferior recess of the omental bursa: inside the closed lesser sac, which fills only from the stomach or pancreas.<br>E. Right paracolic gutter: a channel from the hepatorenal recess to the pelvis, not where fluid settles.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 61 (Spread of Pathological Fluids)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 62 (Spread of Pathological Fluids)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive 11/11/26 (J. LaBarge)","sl":23,"f":"G081"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"154"},{"id":"F3903-296-1","s":"A 50-year-old male heavy drinker is rushed to the ER due to a slightly distended abdomen and right upper quadrant diffuse pain. A diagnosis of mild ascites is made. In order to run a diagnostic test, you need to obtain a sample of the gathering fluid. If the patient is sitting upright, where would the majority of the fluid be localized?","o":["A. Rectouterine pouch","B. Lesser sac","C. Right paracolic gutter","D. Rectovesical pouch","E. Hepatorenal recess"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Rectovesical pouch","a":"D","why":"<b>Rectovesical pouch.</b> When sitting or standing, fluid sinks to the lowest part of the peritoneal cavity, the pelvis. In a man that is the rectovesical pouch, between rectum and bladder.<br>A. Rectouterine pouch: the lowest point in a woman, but this patient is male.<br>B. Lesser sac: closed except for the epiploic foramen; not a low point.<br>C. Right paracolic gutter: a channel fluid flows along, not where it pools upright.<br>E. Hepatorenal recess: lowest only when lying supine.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 61 (Spread of Pathological Fluids)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 62 (Spread of Pathological Fluids)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":296,"f":"F3903"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"155"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":43,"label":"Fluid pools at the lowest point: gravity","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G004-63.jpg","cap":"Fluid runs downhill: hepatorenal pouch lying down, pelvic pouch when upright","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 63"},{"src":"../sl/G004-62.jpg","cap":"Lying supine, the hepatorenal recess is the lowest, gravity-dependent pocket","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 62","cu":"../sl/G004-62c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"G081-23-3","s":"Why does infected peritoneal fluid collect in the hepatorenal recess of a supine patient and in the rectouterine or rectovesical pouch of an upright patient?","o":["A. These spaces lie outside the peritoneal cavity, so fluid cannot drain out of them","B. Peristalsis of the nearby bowel pumps fluid into them","C. The peritoneum lining these spaces secretes extra fluid","D. These spaces have no lymphatic drainage","E. They are the lowest, gravity-dependent parts of the peritoneal cavity in that position"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"They are the lowest, gravity-dependent parts of the peritoneal cavity in that position","a":"E","why":"<b>E. They are the lowest, gravity-dependent parts of the peritoneal cavity in that position.</b> Free fluid runs downhill along the recesses and gutters and settles at the lowest point.<br>A. Outside the cavity: the recess and both pouches are parts of the greater sac, inside the cavity.<br>B. Peristalsis: moves contents inside the gut, not fluid around it.<br>C. Extra secretion: their lining makes the same thin serous fluid as the rest of the peritoneum.<br>D. No lymphatic drainage: the whole peritoneum absorbs fluid quickly, which is why peritoneal dialysis works.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 62 (Spread of Pathological Fluids)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive 11/11/26 (J. LaBarge)","sl":23,"f":"G081"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"156"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":44,"label":"Recurrent laryngeal nerve between esophagus and trachea","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G004-75.jpg","cap":"Cervical esophagus relations: recurrent laryngeal nerves ascend between it and trachea","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 75","cu":"../sl/G004-75c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/F1180-111.jpg","cap":"Recurrent laryngeal nerves loop under subclavian and aortic arch, ascend between trachea and esophagus","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 111"}],"q":[{"id":"G082-17-1","s":"A 55-year-old patient complains of hoarseness. They have a history of high blood pressure, high cholesterol, and gastric reflux. Computed tomography (CT) scan is negative for aortic arch and subclavian aneurisms; however, a small growth located in the cervical esophagus is present. What is the most likely cause for this patient's hoarseness?","o":["A. Damage to the anterior esophageal plexus","B. Damage to the posterior esophageal plexus","C. Compression of the recurrent laryngeal nerve in the tracheoesophageal groove","D. Damage to the sympathetic chain in the cervical region","E. Damage to the ansa cervicalis"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Compression of the recurrent laryngeal nerve in the tracheoesophageal groove","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Compression of the recurrent laryngeal nerve in the tracheoesophageal groove.</b> The nerve climbs between the esophagus and the trachea to the laryngeal muscles, so a cervical esophageal mass pressing on it causes hoarseness.<br>A. Anterior esophageal plexus: a vagal network on the lower thoracic esophagus, far below the neck.<br>B. Posterior esophageal plexus: same place, and it does not serve the larynx either.<br>D. Cervical sympathetic chain: damage gives Horner syndrome (drooping eyelid, small pupil), not hoarseness.<br>E. Ansa cervicalis: supplies the infrahyoid (strap) muscles, not the vocal cords.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 75 (Cervical esophagus)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Montana interactive","n":"Montana (Great Falls) peritoneum, esophagus, stomach & celiac trunk interactive 2026 (D. Lawver)","sl":17,"f":"G082"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"157"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":44,"label":"Esophagus arteries: inferior thyroid, aortic, left gastric","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G004-88.jpg","cap":"Esophagus by thirds: inferior thyroid, aortic branches, left gastric artery","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 88"},{"src":"../sl/G004-89.jpg","cap":"Esophageal arteries: inferior thyroid above, aortic in middle, left gastric below","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 89"},{"src":"../tb/M5_33.jpg","cap":"In panel A, aortic esophageal arteries supply the thoracic esophagus; left gastric branches supply the abdominal part.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.33","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-340-1","s":"Which of the following supplies blood to the lower third of the esophagus?","o":["A. Aortic branches","B. Left gastric artery","C. esophageal artery","D. Right gastric artery","E. Inferior thyroid artery"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Left gastric artery","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Left gastric artery.</b> Its esophageal branches climb through the esophageal hiatus to the lower third of the esophagus.<br>A. Aortic branches: middle third.<br>C. Esophageal arteries straight off the thoracic aorta also serve the middle third; the lower third's branches come from the left gastric.<br>D. Right gastric artery: right part of the lesser curvature.<br>E. Inferior thyroid artery: upper third.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 110 (Left gastric:)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Harlem clicker","n":"Harlem peritoneum, esophagus, stomach & celiac trunk clickers 2026","sl":36,"f":"G074"},{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":340,"f":"F3903"},{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":108,"f":"F0008"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"158"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":45,"label":"Lower esophageal sphincter: physiological, formed by right crus","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G004-87.jpg","cap":"Diaphragm at the esophageal hiatus acts as physiological inferior esophageal sphincter","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 87","cu":"../sl/G004-87c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/D042-116.jpg","cap":"Diaphragm from below: right crus encircles the esophageal hiatus","where":"2025 TA review, slide 116"},{"src":"../sl/G004-121.jpg","cap":"Cross-section at T11: esophagogastric junction lies left of the vertebral body","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 121"},{"src":"../tb/G4_133.jpg","cap":"Follow the right crus up from the lumbar vertebrae; its fibers loop around the esophagus at the hiatus.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 4.133","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/M5_34.jpg","cap":"In panel C, the diaphragm squeezes the esophagus at the hiatus, acting as the physiological lower esophageal sphincter.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.34","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"G113-8-1","s":"A 50-year-old man presents with progressive difficulty swallowing solid foods. An upper endoscopy reveals narrowing of the esophagus near the level where it passes through the diaphragm. The physician explains that the esophagus is a muscular tube that transports food from the pharynx to the stomach through coordinated muscular contractions. Which of the following best describes the anatomical location of the esophageal opening in the diaphragm and the structure that helps prevent gastric contents from refluxing into the esophagus?","o":["A. T8; the pyloric sphincter.","B. T10; the lower esophageal sphincter.","C. T12; the cardiac sphincter.","D. T10; the ileocecal valve.","E. T12; the gastroesophageal junction."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"T10; the lower esophageal sphincter","a":"B","why":"<b>B. T10; the lower esophageal sphincter.</b> The esophagus passes through the esophageal hiatus, in the right crus of the diaphragm, at the tenth thoracic vertebra (T10). Just below, the right crus clamps the esophagus as the physiological lower esophageal sphincter, which stops stomach contents from flowing back up.<br>A. T8 is the caval opening; the pyloric sphincter guards the stomach outlet.<br>C. T12 is the aortic hiatus; cardiac sphincter is an older name for the lower esophageal sphincter, but the level is wrong.<br>D. Right level, but the ileocecal valve lies between the ileum and the cecum.<br>E. T12 is wrong, and the junction (at T11) is not itself the barrier; the sphincter just above it is.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice","n":"GI #2 practice questions Fall 2026 (Lecture #1 peritoneum, esophagus, stomach & celiac trunk; Lecture #2 small & large intestines)","sl":null,"f":"G113"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"159"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":46,"label":"Celiac trunk branches and sub-branches","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G004-110.jpg","cap":"Celiac trunk: left gastric, splenic, common hepatic, with their stomach branches","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 110"},{"src":"../sl/F3112-12.jpg","cap":"Line diagram of every celiac branch, from left gastric to cystic","where":"2025 Middletown clicker, slide 12"}],"q":[{"id":"G081-44-1","s":"Which three arteries are the direct branches of the celiac trunk?","o":["A. Left gastric, right gastric, splenic","B. Common hepatic, gastroduodenal, splenic","C. Left gastric, splenic, common hepatic","D. Left gastric, splenic, proper hepatic","E. Splenic, common hepatic, superior mesenteric"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Left gastric, splenic, common hepatic","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Left gastric, splenic, common hepatic.</b> The celiac trunk leaves the aorta at the twelfth thoracic vertebra (T12) and splits almost at once into these three.<br>A. Right gastric: usually a branch of the proper hepatic artery, not of the trunk.<br>B. Gastroduodenal: a branch of the common hepatic artery.<br>D. Proper hepatic: the continuation of the common hepatic beyond the gastroduodenal branch.<br>E. Superior mesenteric: a separate aortic branch at the first lumbar vertebra (L1), for the midgut.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 109 (Blood supply to stomach)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive 11/11/26 (J. LaBarge)","sl":44,"f":"G081"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"160"},{"id":"G081-44-2","s":"The gastroduodenal artery is a branch of which artery?","o":["A. Proper hepatic artery","B. Common hepatic artery","C. Splenic artery","D. Superior mesenteric artery","E. Right gastric artery"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Common hepatic artery","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Common hepatic artery.</b> The common hepatic runs right from the celiac trunk and gives the gastroduodenal artery, which drops behind the first part of the duodenum. What is left continues as the proper hepatic artery.<br>A. Proper hepatic artery: the sister branch beyond the gastroduodenal origin, going to the liver.<br>C. Splenic artery: runs left to the spleen.<br>D. Superior mesenteric artery: gives the inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery, not the gastroduodenal.<br>E. Right gastric artery: a small lesser-curvature artery.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 110 (Left gastric:)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 112"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive 11/11/26 (J. LaBarge)","sl":44,"f":"G081"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"161"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":46,"label":"Celiac trunk drawing: name each labelled artery","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G081-13.jpg","cap":"Line diagram of the celiac trunk; @ marks the two curvature anastomoses","where":"2026 Middletown interactive, slide 13"}],"q":[{"id":"G081-27-4","s":"In this picture, what artery is labeled D?","o":["A. Superior mesenteric artery","B. Common hepatic artery","C. Celiac trunk","D. Splenic artery","E. Left gastric artery"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Celiac trunk","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Celiac trunk.</b> Label D marks the short, wide trunk leaving the front of the aorta at the twelfth thoracic vertebra (T12) and splitting almost at once into its three branches.<br>A. Superior mesenteric artery: arises just below, at the first lumbar vertebra (L1), behind the neck of the pancreas.<br>B. Common hepatic artery: runs right from the trunk toward the liver.<br>D. Splenic artery: runs left from the trunk to the spleen.<br>E. Left gastric artery: climbs from the trunk to the cardia.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G081-27.jpg","in":"Line drawing (anterior view) of the liver, gallbladder, stomach, spleen, duodenum, pancreas and aorta showing the celiac trunk and its branches; 16 blue boxes A-P hide the artery names (only 'Pancreas' and 'Aorta' are printed). Box D: leader line to the short, wide trunk arising from the front of the aorta.","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 109 (Blood supply to stomach)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 112"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive 11/11/26 (J. LaBarge)","sl":27,"f":"G081"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"162"},{"id":"G081-27-3","s":"In this picture, what artery is labeled C?","o":["A. Left gastric artery","B. Right gastric artery","C. Splenic artery","D. Common hepatic artery","E. Short gastric artery"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Left gastric artery","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Left gastric artery.</b> Label C marks the artery that rises from the celiac trunk to the cardia, gives esophageal twigs, then turns along the upper lesser curvature. It is the smallest of the three celiac branches.<br>B. Right gastric artery: runs on the lower lesser curvature near the pylorus, usually from the proper hepatic artery.<br>C. Splenic artery: runs left behind the stomach to the spleen.<br>D. Common hepatic artery: runs right toward the liver.<br>E. Short gastric arteries: small twigs to the fundus from the splenic artery.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G081-27.jpg","in":"Line drawing (anterior view) of the liver, gallbladder, stomach, spleen, duodenum, pancreas and aorta showing the celiac trunk and its branches; 16 blue boxes A-P hide the artery names (only 'Pancreas' and 'Aorta' are printed). Box C: leader line to the artery running up to the cardia and along the upper lesser curvature.","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 109 (Blood supply to stomach)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 110 (Left gastric:)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive 11/11/26 (J. LaBarge)","sl":27,"f":"G081"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"163"},{"id":"G081-27-6","s":"In this picture, what artery is labeled F?","o":["A. Common hepatic artery","B. Splenic artery","C. Left gastro-omental artery","D. Left gastric artery","E. Gastroduodenal artery"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Splenic artery","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Splenic artery.</b> Label F marks the long, wavy vessel running left behind the stomach, along the top of the pancreas, to the spleen. It is the largest celiac branch.<br>A. Common hepatic artery: runs right, toward the liver.<br>C. Left gastro-omental artery: a splenic branch running along the greater curvature.<br>D. Left gastric artery: climbs to the cardia and the lesser curvature.<br>E. Gastroduodenal artery: drops behind the first part of the duodenum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G081-27.jpg","in":"Line drawing (anterior view) of the liver, gallbladder, stomach, spleen, duodenum, pancreas and aorta showing the celiac trunk and its branches; 16 blue boxes A-P hide the artery names (only 'Pancreas' and 'Aorta' are printed). Box F: leader line to the tortuous artery running left behind the stomach toward the spleen.","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 109 (Blood supply to stomach)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 110 (Left gastric:)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive 11/11/26 (J. LaBarge)","sl":27,"f":"G081"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"164"},{"id":"G081-27-16","s":"In this picture, what artery is labeled P?","o":["A. Proper hepatic artery","B. Splenic artery","C. Celiac trunk","D. Gastroduodenal artery","E. Common hepatic artery"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Common hepatic artery","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Common hepatic artery.</b> Label P marks the artery running right from the celiac trunk along the upper border of the pancreas. It gives the gastroduodenal artery and continues as the proper hepatic artery.<br>A. Proper hepatic artery: the continuation beyond the gastroduodenal branch, climbing to the liver.<br>B. Splenic artery: runs left to the spleen.<br>C. Celiac trunk: the short parent trunk on the aorta.<br>D. Gastroduodenal artery: drops behind the first part of the duodenum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G081-27.jpg","in":"Line drawing (anterior view) of the liver, gallbladder, stomach, spleen, duodenum, pancreas and aorta showing the celiac trunk and its branches; 16 blue boxes A-P hide the artery names (only 'Pancreas' and 'Aorta' are printed). Box P (top centre): vertical leader line to the artery running right from the celiac trunk.","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 109 (Blood supply to stomach)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 112"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive 11/11/26 (J. LaBarge)","sl":27,"f":"G081"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"165"},{"id":"G081-27-10","s":"In this picture, what artery is labeled J?","o":["A. Proper hepatic artery","B. Gastroduodenal artery","C. Right gastric artery","D. Supraduodenal artery","E. Common hepatic artery"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastroduodenal artery","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Gastroduodenal artery.</b> Label J marks the artery that leaves the common hepatic artery and drops directly behind the first part of the duodenum. Its branches are the supraduodenal, superior pancreaticoduodenal and right gastro-omental arteries.<br>A. Proper hepatic artery: the continuation that climbs to the liver.<br>C. Right gastric artery: runs on the lesser curvature.<br>D. Supraduodenal artery: a small twig on top of the duodenum.<br>E. Common hepatic artery: the parent, running right from the celiac trunk.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G081-27.jpg","in":"Line drawing (anterior view) of the liver, gallbladder, stomach, spleen, duodenum, pancreas and aorta showing the celiac trunk and its branches; 16 blue boxes A-P hide the artery names (only 'Pancreas' and 'Aorta' are printed). Box J: leader line to the artery descending behind the first part of the duodenum.","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 110 (Left gastric:)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 112"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive 11/11/26 (J. LaBarge)","sl":27,"f":"G081"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"166"},{"id":"G081-27-12","s":"In this picture, what artery is labeled L?","o":["A. Common hepatic artery","B. Right hepatic artery","C. Gastroduodenal artery","D. Proper hepatic artery","E. Cystic artery"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Proper hepatic artery","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Proper hepatic artery.</b> Label L marks the artery climbing to the liver after the gastroduodenal artery has branched off. It runs in the hepatoduodenal ligament and splits into right and left hepatic arteries.<br>A. Common hepatic artery: the segment before the gastroduodenal branch, running right along the pancreas.<br>B. Right hepatic artery: one of its two end branches, into the right lobe.<br>C. Gastroduodenal artery: drops down behind the duodenum.<br>E. Cystic artery: a small branch to the gallbladder.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G081-27.jpg","in":"Line drawing (anterior view) of the liver, gallbladder, stomach, spleen, duodenum, pancreas and aorta showing the celiac trunk and its branches; 16 blue boxes A-P hide the artery names (only 'Pancreas' and 'Aorta' are printed). Box L: leader line to the artery climbing toward the liver after the gastroduodenal has branched off.","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 110 (Left gastric:)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 112"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive 11/11/26 (J. LaBarge)","sl":27,"f":"G081"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"167"},{"id":"G081-27-1","s":"In this picture, what artery is labeled A?","o":["A. Short gastric arteries","B. Esophageal branches of the left gastric artery","C. Left inferior phrenic artery","D. Posterior gastric artery","E. Left gastric artery"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Esophageal branches of the left gastric artery","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Esophageal branches of the left gastric artery.</b> Label A marks the small twigs climbing the abdominal esophagus. They leave the left gastric artery at the cardia and pass up through the esophageal hiatus.<br>A. Short gastric arteries: leave the end of the splenic artery and run to the fundus near the spleen.<br>C. Left inferior phrenic artery: supplies the underside of the diaphragm.<br>D. Posterior gastric artery: an inconstant splenic branch to the back wall of the stomach.<br>E. Left gastric artery: the parent vessel, rising to the cardia and then running along the lesser curvature.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G081-27.jpg","in":"Line drawing (anterior view) of the liver, gallbladder, stomach, spleen, duodenum, pancreas and aorta showing the celiac trunk and its branches; 16 blue boxes A-P hide the artery names (only 'Pancreas' and 'Aorta' are printed). Box A (top right): two leader lines to small branches climbing the abdominal esophagus.","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 110 (Left gastric:)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive 11/11/26 (J. LaBarge)","sl":27,"f":"G081"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"168"},{"id":"G081-27-2","s":"In this picture, what artery is labeled B?","o":["A. Left gastro-omental artery","B. Esophageal branches","C. Posterior gastric artery","D. Short gastric arteries","E. Left gastric artery"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Short gastric arteries","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Short gastric arteries.</b> Label B marks the small vessels on the fundus beside the spleen. Four or five leave the end of the splenic artery and run through the gastrosplenic ligament to the fundus.<br>A. Left gastro-omental artery: runs along the left part of the greater curvature.<br>B. Esophageal branches: climb the esophagus from the left gastric artery.<br>C. Posterior gastric artery: a single inconstant branch climbing behind the lesser sac to the back wall; the fundic twigs near the spleen are short gastrics.<br>E. Left gastric artery: runs to the cardia and the lesser curvature.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G081-27.jpg","in":"Line drawing (anterior view) of the liver, gallbladder, stomach, spleen, duodenum, pancreas and aorta showing the celiac trunk and its branches; 16 blue boxes A-P hide the artery names (only 'Pancreas' and 'Aorta' are printed). Box B: leader line to faint branches on the fundus near the spleen.","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 110 (Left gastric:)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive 11/11/26 (J. LaBarge)","sl":27,"f":"G081"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"169"},{"id":"G081-27-5","s":"In this picture, what artery is labeled E?","o":["A. Right gastro-omental artery","B. Short gastric artery","C. Splenic artery","D. Left gastric artery","E. Left gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Left gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Left gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery.</b> Label E marks the artery along the left part of the greater curvature. It leaves the splenic artery near the splenic hilum and meets the right gastro-omental artery.<br>A. Right gastro-omental artery: runs along the right part of the greater curvature, from the gastroduodenal artery.<br>B. Short gastric artery: a small twig to the fundus.<br>C. Splenic artery: the parent vessel, running behind the stomach to the spleen.<br>D. Left gastric artery: runs on the lesser curvature, not the greater.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G081-27.jpg","in":"Line drawing (anterior view) of the liver, gallbladder, stomach, spleen, duodenum, pancreas and aorta showing the celiac trunk and its branches; 16 blue boxes A-P hide the artery names (only 'Pancreas' and 'Aorta' are printed). Box E: leader line to the artery along the left part of the greater curvature.","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 110 (Left gastric:)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive 11/11/26 (J. LaBarge)","sl":27,"f":"G081"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"170"},{"id":"G081-27-7","s":"In this picture, what artery is labeled G?","o":["A. Right gastric artery","B. Left gastro-omental artery","C. Superior pancreaticoduodenal artery","D. Right gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery","E. Gastroduodenal artery"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Right gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Right gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery.</b> Label G marks the artery along the right part of the greater curvature. It comes from the gastroduodenal artery below the pylorus and meets the left gastro-omental artery.<br>A. Right gastric artery: runs on the lesser curvature near the pylorus.<br>B. Left gastro-omental artery: runs along the left part of the greater curvature, from the splenic artery.<br>C. Superior pancreaticoduodenal artery: runs between the duodenum and the head of the pancreas.<br>E. Gastroduodenal artery: the short parent vessel behind the first part of the duodenum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G081-27.jpg","in":"Line drawing (anterior view) of the liver, gallbladder, stomach, spleen, duodenum, pancreas and aorta showing the celiac trunk and its branches; 16 blue boxes A-P hide the artery names (only 'Pancreas' and 'Aorta' are printed). Box G: leader line to the artery along the lower (right) part of the greater curvature.","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 111 (Gasroduodenal:)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive 11/11/26 (J. LaBarge)","sl":27,"f":"G081"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"171"},{"id":"G081-27-8","s":"In this picture, what artery is labeled H?","o":["A. Supraduodenal artery","B. Gastroduodenal artery","C. Superior pancreaticoduodenal artery","D. Inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery","E. Right gastro-omental artery"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Superior pancreaticoduodenal artery","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Superior pancreaticoduodenal artery.</b> Label H marks the arcade between the C-loop of the duodenum and the head of the pancreas. It descends from the gastroduodenal artery and feeds the duodenum down to the major papilla.<br>A. Supraduodenal artery: a small twig to the top of the first part of the duodenum.<br>B. Gastroduodenal artery: the parent vessel behind the first part of the duodenum.<br>D. Inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery: comes up from the superior mesenteric artery and is not drawn here.<br>E. Right gastro-omental artery: runs along the greater curvature.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G081-27.jpg","in":"Line drawing (anterior view) of the liver, gallbladder, stomach, spleen, duodenum, pancreas and aorta showing the celiac trunk and its branches; 16 blue boxes A-P hide the artery names (only 'Pancreas' and 'Aorta' are printed). Box H (bottom left): leader line to the arcade between the duodenal C-loop and the head of the pancreas.","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 111 (Gasroduodenal:)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive 11/11/26 (J. LaBarge)","sl":27,"f":"G081"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"172"},{"id":"G081-27-9","s":"In this picture, what artery is labeled I?","o":["A. Supraduodenal artery","B. Right gastric artery","C. Superior pancreaticoduodenal artery","D. Right gastro-omental artery","E. Gastroduodenal artery"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Supraduodenal artery","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Supraduodenal artery.</b> Label I marks a small twig to the first part of the duodenum. It is one of the three branches of the gastroduodenal artery.<br>B. Right gastric artery: runs on the lesser curvature of the stomach.<br>C. Superior pancreaticoduodenal artery: runs down between the duodenal C-loop and the head of the pancreas.<br>D. Right gastro-omental artery: runs along the greater curvature.<br>E. Gastroduodenal artery: the parent vessel that drops behind the first part of the duodenum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G081-27.jpg","in":"Line drawing (anterior view) of the liver, gallbladder, stomach, spleen, duodenum, pancreas and aorta showing the celiac trunk and its branches; 16 blue boxes A-P hide the artery names (only 'Pancreas' and 'Aorta' are printed). Box I: leader line to a small branch on the first part of the duodenum.","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 111 (Gasroduodenal:)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive 11/11/26 (J. LaBarge)","sl":27,"f":"G081"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"173"},{"id":"G081-27-11","s":"In this picture, what artery is labeled K?","o":["A. Left gastric artery","B. Gastroduodenal artery","C. Right gastro-omental artery","D. Proper hepatic artery","E. Right gastric artery"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Right gastric artery","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Right gastric artery.</b> Label K marks the artery on the right part of the lesser curvature, near the pylorus. It comes from the hepatic artery (common or proper) and meets the left gastric artery along the curvature.<br>A. Left gastric artery: supplies the cardia and upper lesser curvature.<br>B. Gastroduodenal artery: drops behind the first part of the duodenum.<br>C. Right gastro-omental artery: runs on the greater curvature.<br>D. Proper hepatic artery: climbs to the liver in the hepatoduodenal ligament.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G081-27.jpg","in":"Line drawing (anterior view) of the liver, gallbladder, stomach, spleen, duodenum, pancreas and aorta showing the celiac trunk and its branches; 16 blue boxes A-P hide the artery names (only 'Pancreas' and 'Aorta' are printed). Box K: leader line to the artery along the right part of the lesser curvature, near the pylorus.","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 110 (Left gastric:)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 112"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive 11/11/26 (J. LaBarge)","sl":27,"f":"G081"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"174"},{"id":"G081-27-13","s":"In this picture, what artery is labeled M?","o":["A. Cystic artery","B. Right hepatic artery","C. Left hepatic artery","D. Proper hepatic artery","E. Supraduodenal artery"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Cystic artery","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Cystic artery.</b> Label M marks the small branch running to the gallbladder. It usually arises from the right hepatic artery.<br>B. Right hepatic artery: the larger parent vessel entering the right lobe of the liver.<br>C. Left hepatic artery: enters the left lobe.<br>D. Proper hepatic artery: the trunk below the split into right and left hepatic arteries.<br>E. Supraduodenal artery: a small gastroduodenal twig on the first part of the duodenum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G081-27.jpg","in":"Line drawing (anterior view) of the liver, gallbladder, stomach, spleen, duodenum, pancreas and aorta showing the celiac trunk and its branches; 16 blue boxes A-P hide the artery names (only 'Pancreas' and 'Aorta' are printed). Box M: leader line to the small branch running to the gallbladder.","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 110 (Left gastric:)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive 11/11/26 (J. LaBarge)","sl":27,"f":"G081"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"175"},{"id":"G081-27-14","s":"In this picture, what artery is labeled N?","o":["A. Left hepatic artery","B. Right hepatic artery","C. Cystic artery","D. Proper hepatic artery","E. Right gastric artery"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Right hepatic artery","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Right hepatic artery.</b> Label N marks the branch running into the right part of the liver. It is one of the two end branches of the proper hepatic artery, and it gives the cystic artery.<br>A. Left hepatic artery: runs into the left part of the liver.<br>C. Cystic artery: a small branch to the gallbladder.<br>D. Proper hepatic artery: the single trunk before it splits.<br>E. Right gastric artery: runs on the lesser curvature of the stomach.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G081-27.jpg","in":"Line drawing (anterior view) of the liver, gallbladder, stomach, spleen, duodenum, pancreas and aorta showing the celiac trunk and its branches; 16 blue boxes A-P hide the artery names (only 'Pancreas' and 'Aorta' are printed). Box N: leader line to the branch running into the right part of the liver.","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 110 (Left gastric:)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive 11/11/26 (J. LaBarge)","sl":27,"f":"G081"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"176"},{"id":"G081-27-15","s":"In this picture, what artery is labeled O?","o":["A. Right hepatic artery","B. Proper hepatic artery","C. Left gastric artery","D. Left hepatic artery","E. Common hepatic artery"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Left hepatic artery","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Left hepatic artery.</b> Label O marks the branch running into the left part of the liver, one of the two end branches of the proper hepatic artery.<br>A. Right hepatic artery: runs into the right part of the liver and gives the cystic artery.<br>B. Proper hepatic artery: the trunk before it splits.<br>C. Left gastric artery: rises from the celiac trunk to the cardia, not to the liver.<br>E. Common hepatic artery: runs right from the celiac trunk, below the liver.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G081-27.jpg","in":"Line drawing (anterior view) of the liver, gallbladder, stomach, spleen, duodenum, pancreas and aorta showing the celiac trunk and its branches; 16 blue boxes A-P hide the artery names (only 'Pancreas' and 'Aorta' are printed). Box O: leader line to the branch running into the left part of the liver.","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 110 (Left gastric:)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive 11/11/26 (J. LaBarge)","sl":27,"f":"G081"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"177"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":47,"label":"Curvature arcades: gastrics lesser, gastro-omentals greater","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G004-110.jpg","cap":"Gastric arteries on lesser curvature, gastro-omental arteries on greater curvature","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 110"},{"src":"../sl/F3112-12.jpg","cap":"Line diagram: @ symbols mark the lesser- and greater-curvature anastomoses","where":"2025 Middletown clicker, slide 12"},{"src":"../sl/G004-114.jpg","cap":"Table: right gastric joins left gastric; right gastro-omental from gastroduodenal","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 114"},{"src":"../tb/G4_60.jpg","cap":"Left and right gastric arteries meet along the lesser curvature; left and right gastro-omental arteries along the greater.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 4.60","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F3112-34-1","s":"Which two arteries anastomose along the lesser curvature of the stomach?","o":["A. Left and right gastro-omental arteries","B. Left gastric and short gastric arteries","C. Right gastric and right gastro-omental arteries","D. Left and right gastric arteries"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Left and right gastric arteries","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Left and right gastric arteries.</b> The left gastric (from the celiac trunk) meets the right gastric (from the hepatic artery) along the lesser curvature.<br>A. Left and right gastro-omental arteries: they form the greater-curvature arcade; the left comes from the splenic artery, the right from the gastroduodenal.<br>B. Left gastric and short gastric arteries: the short gastrics, from the splenic artery, supply the fundus.<br>C. Right gastric and right gastro-omental arteries: they lie on opposite curvatures and do not pair up.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 110 (Left gastric:)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 111 (Gasroduodenal:)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"Peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive (JL)","sl":34,"f":"F3112"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"178"},{"id":"G081-40-1","s":"Which pair of arteries anastomoses along the greater curvature of the stomach?","o":["A. Left and right gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) arteries","B. Left and right gastric arteries","C. Short gastric and left gastric arteries","D. Right gastric and right gastro-omental arteries","E. Gastroduodenal and left gastro-omental arteries"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Left and right gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) arteries","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Left and right gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) arteries.</b> They run in the greater omentum along the greater curvature and join end to end. The left comes from the splenic artery, the right from the gastroduodenal artery.<br>B. Left and right gastric arteries: the lesser-curvature arcade.<br>C. Short gastric and left gastric: short gastrics feed the fundus; the left gastric runs on the lesser curvature.<br>D. Right gastric and right gastro-omental: on opposite curvatures.<br>E. Gastroduodenal and left gastro-omental: the gastroduodenal is the parent of the right gastro-omental, not an arcade partner.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 110 (Left gastric:)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 111 (Gasroduodenal:)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive 11/11/26 (J. LaBarge)","sl":40,"f":"G081"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"179"},{"id":"F3112-33-1","s":"During dissection lab, a student accidentally cuts the artery located on the right aspect of the lesser curvature of the stomach. which of the following arteries would keep blood flowing to the lesser curvature if this injury happened in a live patient?","o":["A. Esophageal","B. Right gastroepiploic","C. Gastroduodenal","D. Left gastric","E. Right gastric"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Left gastric","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Left gastric.</b> The artery on the right side of the lesser curvature is the right gastric. It joins the left gastric artery along the lesser curvature, so the left gastric keeps that curvature supplied.<br>A. Esophageal arteries: supply the esophagus.<br>B. Right gastroepiploic (gastro-omental): runs on the greater curvature.<br>C. Gastroduodenal: runs behind the duodenum; its branch feeds the greater curvature.<br>E. Right gastric: the cut artery itself.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 110 (Left gastric:)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive 11/11/26 (J. LaBarge)","sl":39,"f":"G081"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"Peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive (JL)","sl":33,"f":"F3112"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"180"},{"id":"F3903-312-1","s":"During dissection lab, a student accidentally cuts the artery located on the left aspect of the smaller curvature of the stomach. which of the following arteries would keep blood flowing to the small curvature if this injury happened in a live patient?","o":["A. Esophageal","B. Right gastroepiploic","C. Gastroduodenal","D. Left gastroepiploic","E. Right gastric"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Right gastric","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Right gastric.</b> The artery on the left side of the lesser curvature is the left gastric. It joins the right gastric artery along the lesser curvature, so the right gastric keeps the lesser curvature supplied.<br>A. Esophageal arteries: supply the esophagus.<br>B. Right gastroepiploic: runs on the greater curvature.<br>C. Gastroduodenal: behind the duodenum; gives the right gastroepiploic.<br>D. Left gastroepiploic: on the greater curvature, from the splenic artery.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 110 (Left gastric:)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":312,"f":"F3903"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"181"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":48,"label":"Splenic artery branches: short gastrics, left gastro-omental","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G004-110.jpg","cap":"Splenic artery gives posterior gastric, short gastrics to fundus, left gastro-omental","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 110"},{"src":"../sl/G004-113.jpg","cap":"Table: short gastrics run in gastrosplenic ligament to the fundus","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 113"},{"src":"../sl/G004-96.jpg","cap":"Stomach regions: cardia, fundus, body and pyloric part","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 96","cu":"../sl/G004-96c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-332-1","s":"A 51 year-old woman is admitted to the hospital with an acutely painful abdomen Radiographic examination reveals penetration of the fundic region of the stomach by an ulcer, resulting in intraabdominal bleeding. Which of the following arteries is the most likely source of the bleeding?","o":["A. Common hepatic artery","B. Inferior phrenic artery","C. Left gastroepiploic artery","D. Short gastric artery","E. Right gastroepiploic artery"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Short gastric artery","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Short gastric artery.</b> Four to five short gastric arteries leave the splenic artery near the spleen and run in the gastrosplenic ligament to the fundus, so a fundic ulcer erodes them.<br>A. Common hepatic artery: runs right, toward the liver.<br>B. Inferior phrenic artery: supplies the underside of the diaphragm.<br>C. Left gastroepiploic artery: left part of the greater curvature, below the fundus.<br>E. Right gastroepiploic artery: right part of the greater curvature near the pylorus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 110 (Left gastric:)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Harlem clicker","n":"Harlem peritoneum, esophagus, stomach & celiac trunk clickers 2026","sl":21,"f":"G074"},{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":332,"f":"F3903"},{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":102,"f":"F0008"},{"t":"? tutor review","n":"old Exam 2: abdomen part only","sl":113,"f":"F1243"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"182"},{"id":"F3112-44-1","s":"A surgeon is trying to fix a tear in the fundus of the stomach of a patient caused by an ulcer. He accidentally cuts the splenic artery superior to the body of the pancreas. Blood flow to which of the following arteries will be affected?","o":["A. Left gastroepiploic","B. Proper hepatic artery","C. Cystic artery","D. Right hepatic","E. Common hepatic artery"],"g":["A"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Right hepatic","a":"D","why":"<b>A. Left gastroepiploic.</b> The left gastroepiploic (left gastro-omental) artery is a branch of the splenic artery, so cutting the splenic artery stops its flow. The splenic artery also gives the short gastric and posterior gastric arteries.<br>B. Proper hepatic: from the common hepatic artery.<br>C. Cystic: from the right hepatic artery.<br>D. Right hepatic: from the proper hepatic artery.<br>E. Common hepatic: a sister celiac branch, not downstream of the splenic.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 110 (Left gastric:)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive 11/11/26 (J. LaBarge)","sl":43,"f":"G081"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"Peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive (JL)","sl":44,"f":"F3112"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"183"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":48,"label":"Posterior gastric ulcer erodes splenic artery","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G004-134.jpg","cap":"Perforated posterior gastric ulcer can erode the large splenic artery","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 134"},{"src":"../sl/G004-104.jpg","cap":"Stomach bed: splenic artery and vein run behind stomach along pancreas","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 104"},{"src":"../tb/M5_39.jpg","cap":"In panel B, the splenic artery runs along the pancreas directly behind where the stomach sits.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.39","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/M5_24.jpg","cap":"In panel A, the splenic artery lies just behind the stomach, across the thin omental bursa.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.24","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F3112-23-1","s":"A 37-year-old female is rushed to the ER due to high fever and severe stomach pains. An endoscopy reveals a perforated ulcer in the posterior wall of the body of the stomach which has led to the damage of the artery running immediately posterior. Which of the following arteries has the gastric acid eroded?","o":["A. Gastroduodenal artery","B. Common hepatic artery","C. Splenic artery","D. Left gastric artery","E. Posterior pancreatic artery"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Splenic artery","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Splenic artery.</b> The splenic artery runs along the upper border of the pancreas, directly behind the posterior wall of the stomach body (the stomach bed). A perforating posterior gastric ulcer erodes it.<br>A. Gastroduodenal artery: behind the first part of the duodenum (posterior duodenal ulcers).<br>B. Common hepatic artery: runs right, toward the liver.<br>D. Left gastric artery: along the lesser curvature.<br>E. Posterior (dorsal) pancreatic artery: a small branch behind the pancreas, not against the stomach.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 134 (Gastric ulcer)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 103 (Posterior relations of stomach)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive 11/11/26 (J. LaBarge)","sl":26,"f":"G081"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"Peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive (JL)","sl":23,"f":"F3112"},{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":300,"f":"F3903"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"184"},{"id":"G082-13-1","s":"A 35-year-old patient complains of abdominal pain in the left upper quadrant and blood in their stool. Additionally, H. pylori is present in the stool. Endoscopy reveals a peptic ulcer in the body of the stomach. What blood vessel is a risk of bleeding as a result of this ulcer?","o":["A. Common hepatic artery","B. Right gastric artery","C. Gastroduodenal artery","D. Splenic artery","E. Anterior pancreaticoduodenal artery"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Splenic artery","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Splenic artery.</b> It runs left along the top of the pancreas, directly behind the body of the stomach. An ulcer that eats through the back wall of the body erodes it and bleeds heavily.<br>A. Common hepatic artery: runs right, toward the liver.<br>B. Right gastric artery: on the lesser curvature near the pylorus.<br>C. Gastroduodenal artery: behind the first part of the duodenum; eroded by posterior duodenal ulcers.<br>E. Anterior pancreaticoduodenal artery: on the front of the pancreatic head, far from the gastric body.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 134 (Gastric ulcer)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 109 (Blood supply to stomach)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Montana interactive","n":"Montana (Great Falls) peritoneum, esophagus, stomach & celiac trunk interactive 2026 (D. Lawver)","sl":13,"f":"G082"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"185"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":49,"label":"Major duodenal papilla in descending duodenum","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G005-16.jpg","cap":"Bile and main pancreatic ducts open at the major duodenal papilla, second part","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 16","cu":"../sl/G005-16c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G005-9.jpg","cap":"Parts of duodenum: only the start of the superior part is intraperitoneal","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 9"},{"src":"../tb/G4_63.jpg","cap":"The major duodenal papilla sits on the descending (second) part of the duodenum.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 4.63","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"G113-9-1","s":"A 38-year-old woman undergoes an upper gastrointestinal endoscopy because of recurrent epigastric discomfort. The physician identifies an ulcer in the first portion of the duodenum, immediately distal to the pylorus of the stomach. The physician explains that the duodenum receives partially digested food from the stomach and mixes it with bile and pancreatic secretions to facilitate digestion. Which of the following best describes the anatomical location and function of the duodenum?","o":["A. It is located primarily in the right upper quadrant and epigastric region and receives gastric contents, bile, and pancreatic secretions.","B. It is located primarily in the left upper quadrant and functions primarily in water absorption.","C. It is located primarily in the left lower quadrant and functions primarily in nutrient absorption from the colon.","D. It is located within the greater omentum and functions primarily in bile production.","E. It is located entirely within the pelvis and functions primarily in fecal storage."],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"It is located primarily in the right upper quadrant and epigastric region and receives gastric contents, bile, and pancreatic secretions","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Right upper quadrant and epigastric region; receives gastric contents, bile and pancreatic secretions.</b> The C-shaped duodenum lies mostly right of the midline, curving around the head of the pancreas. It receives acid chyme through the pylorus, and bile and pancreatic juice at the major duodenal papilla in its second part.<br>B. The left upper quadrant holds the stomach and spleen; water is absorbed mainly in the colon.<br>C. The left lower quadrant holds the descending and sigmoid colon; nutrients are absorbed in the small intestine.<br>D. The duodenum is not in the greater omentum; bile is made by the liver.<br>E. Feces are stored in the rectum, in the pelvis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice","n":"GI #2 practice questions Fall 2026 (Lecture #1 peritoneum, esophagus, stomach & celiac trunk; Lecture #2 small & large intestines)","sl":null,"f":"G113"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"186"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":49,"label":"Gastroduodenal artery branches","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G004-111.jpg","cap":"Gastroduodenal branches: supraduodenal, superior pancreaticoduodenal, right gastro-epiploic","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 111"},{"src":"../sl/G004-114.jpg","cap":"Table: right gastro-omental and superior pancreaticoduodenal arise from gastroduodenal","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 114"},{"src":"../sl/G007-15.jpg","cap":"Pancreas arteries: gastroduodenal with superior and inferior pancreaticoduodenal arcades","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 15"},{"src":"../tb/M5_40.jpg","cap":"The gastroduodenal artery gives the supraduodenal, superior pancreaticoduodenal and right gastro-omental arteries.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.40","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/G4_64.jpg","cap":"Follow the gastroduodenal artery down behind the duodenum into the superior pancreaticoduodenal arcades.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 4.64","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"G081-40-2","s":"The right gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery is a branch of which artery?","o":["A. Splenic artery","B. Proper hepatic artery","C. Superior mesenteric artery","D. Gastroduodenal artery","E. Right gastric artery"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastroduodenal artery","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Gastroduodenal artery.</b> Behind the first part of the duodenum it gives the superior pancreaticoduodenal and right gastro-omental arteries; the right gastro-omental then runs left along the greater curvature.<br>A. Splenic artery: gives the left gastro-omental artery.<br>B. Proper hepatic artery: goes up to the liver; it usually gives the right gastric artery.<br>C. Superior mesenteric artery: gives the inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery.<br>E. Right gastric artery: runs on the lesser curvature.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 111 (Gasroduodenal:)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive 11/11/26 (J. LaBarge)","sl":40,"f":"G081"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"187"},{"id":"F3903-320-1","s":"A surgeon is trying to fix a tear in the duodenum of a patient caused by an ulcerated gallstone. He accidentally cuts the gastroduodenal artery close to its origin. Blood flow to which of the following arteries will be affected?","o":["A. Proper hepatic artery","B. Cystic artery","C. Right hepatic","D. Right gastroepiploic","E. Common hepatic artery"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Right gastroepiploic","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Right gastroepiploic.</b> The gastroduodenal artery's branches are the supraduodenal, the superior pancreaticoduodenal and the right gastroepiploic (right gastro-omental). Cutting it near its origin stops flow to all three.<br>A. Proper hepatic artery: a sister branch of the common hepatic, upstream.<br>B. Cystic artery: from the right hepatic artery.<br>C. Right hepatic artery: from the proper hepatic artery.<br>E. Common hepatic artery: the parent, upstream of the cut.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 111 (Gasroduodenal:)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":320,"f":"F3903"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"188"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":49,"label":"Duodenum: pancreaticoduodenal arteries, veins to the portal vein","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G005-33.jpg","cap":"Superior (from gastroduodenal) and inferior (from superior mesenteric) pancreaticoduodenal arteries on the duodenum","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 33"},{"src":"../sl/G006-48.jpg","cap":"Splenic and superior mesenteric veins join behind the pancreas to form the portal vein","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 48","cu":"../sl/G006-48c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"G113-12-1","s":"A 48-year-old man presents with an upper gastrointestinal bleed. An upper endoscopy identifies an actively bleeding ulcer in the proximal duodenum. The physician explains that the duodenum receives arterial blood from branches of two major arteries and that venous blood from much of the upper gastrointestinal tract ultimately drains into the hepatic portal system before reaching the liver. Which of the following correctly identifies the arterial supply and venous drainage of the proximal duodenum?","o":["A. Arterial supply from the left gastric and splenic arteries; venous drainage directly into the inferior vena cava.","B. Arterial supply from the superior and inferior pancreaticoduodenal arteries; venous drainage into the hepatic portal system.","C. Arterial supply from the superior mesenteric and inferior mesenteric arteries; venous drainage directly into the hepatic veins.","D. Arterial supply from the common hepatic and left gastric arteries; venous drainage directly into the inferior vena cava.","E. Arterial supply from the splenic and superior mesenteric arteries; venous drainage into the azygos venous system."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Arterial supply from the superior and inferior pancreaticoduodenal arteries; venous drainage into the hepatic portal system","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Superior and inferior pancreaticoduodenal arteries; venous drainage into the hepatic portal system.</b> The superior pancreaticoduodenal artery (from the gastroduodenal, a celiac branch) feeds the duodenum down to the major papilla; the inferior one (from the superior mesenteric artery) feeds it below. They meet in an arcade. Duodenal veins drain into the portal vein, directly or through the superior mesenteric vein.<br>A. The left gastric and splenic arteries send no branches to the duodenum; its veins do not enter the inferior vena cava.<br>C. The inferior mesenteric artery serves the hindgut; hepatic veins drain only the liver.<br>D. The left gastric serves the lesser curvature, and the drainage is portal.<br>E. The splenic artery skips the duodenum; azygos veins drain the chest wall.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice","n":"GI #2 practice questions Fall 2026 (Lecture #1 peritoneum, esophagus, stomach & celiac trunk; Lecture #2 small & large intestines)","sl":null,"f":"G113"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"189"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":50,"label":"Stomach lymph drains to celiac nodes, cisterna chyli","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G007-18.jpg","cap":"Stomach and pancreas lymph: pyloric, pancreaticosplenic and celiac lymph nodes","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 18"},{"src":"../sl/G004-117.jpg","cap":"Stomach lymph follows arteries to gastric, gastroepiploic, pancreaticosplenic, pyloric nodes","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 117","cu":"../sl/G004-117c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/D041-190.jpg","cap":"Flowchart: stomach nodes drain via celiac nodes and intestinal trunks to cisterna chyli","where":"2025 TA review, slide 190"},{"src":"../tb/M5_42.jpg","cap":"In panel A, gastric, gastro-omental and pyloric nodes along the arteries all drain to the celiac nodes.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.42","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-338-1","s":"A 54-year-old male is admitted to the emergency department with severe upper abdominal pain. Gastroscopy reveals a tumor in the antrum of the stomach. A CT scan is ordered to evaluate lymphatic drainage of the stomach. Which of the following lymph nodes is most likely to be involved in a malignancy of the stomach?","o":["A. Celiac","B. Superior mesenteric","C. Inferior mesenteric","D. Lumbar","E. Hepatic"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Celiac","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Celiac.</b> All stomach lymph, the antrum's included, passes through nodes along the gastric arteries and ends in the celiac nodes around the celiac trunk.<br>B. Superior mesenteric nodes: midgut.<br>C. Inferior mesenteric nodes: hindgut.<br>D. Lumbar nodes: kidneys, gonads, back wall and legs.<br>E. Hepatic nodes: get some pyloric lymph on the way, but all of it then goes to the celiac nodes, so celiac is most likely.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 117 (Lymphatics)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 68 (Objective)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Harlem clicker","n":"Harlem peritoneum, esophagus, stomach & celiac trunk clickers 2026","sl":30,"f":"G074"},{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":338,"f":"F3903"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"190"},{"id":"G113-13-1","s":"A 56-year-old man is diagnosed with gastric adenocarcinoma involving the lesser curvature of the stomach. Imaging reveals enlargement of lymph nodes along the left gastric artery. The surgeon explains that lymph from the stomach follows the regional arterial supply before reaching larger lymphatic channels that ultimately return lymph to the systemic circulation. Which of the following best describes the expected pathway of lymphatic drainage from the stomach?","o":["A. Left gastric lymph nodes → superior mesenteric lymph nodes → cisterna chyli → thoracic duct.","B. Left gastric lymph nodes → celiac lymph nodes → intestinal lymphatic trunks → cisterna chyli → thoracic duct.","C. Inferior mesenteric lymph nodes → para-aortic lymph nodes → right lymphatic duct → right subclavian vein.","D. Pancreaticosplenic lymph nodes → inferior mesenteric lymph nodes → cisterna chyli → thoracic duct.","E. Hepatic lymph nodes → axillary lymph nodes → thoracic duct."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Left gastric lymph nodes → celiac lymph nodes → intestinal lymphatic trunks → cisterna chyli → thoracic duct","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Left gastric nodes → celiac nodes → intestinal lymphatic trunks → cisterna chyli → thoracic duct.</b> Stomach lymph runs beside the arteries. The lesser curvature drains first to gastric nodes along the left gastric artery, then to the celiac nodes, then through the intestinal trunk into the cisterna chyli and up the thoracic duct.<br>A. Superior mesenteric nodes drain the midgut, not the stomach.<br>C. Inferior mesenteric and para-aortic (lumbar) nodes drain the hindgut and posterior wall; the right lymphatic duct drains only the right upper body.<br>D. Pancreaticosplenic nodes drain on to the celiac nodes, not inferior mesenteric nodes.<br>E. Axillary nodes drain the upper limb and breast.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice","n":"GI #2 practice questions Fall 2026 (Lecture #1 peritoneum, esophagus, stomach & celiac trunk; Lecture #2 small & large intestines)","sl":null,"f":"G113"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"191"},{"id":"G113-14-1","s":"A 63-year-old woman is diagnosed with pancreatic adenocarcinoma involving the head of the pancreas. During surgical planning, the oncologist reviews the lymphatic drainage of foregut organs to determine which regional lymph nodes should be evaluated for metastatic disease. The physician explains that lymph from the foregut organs ultimately converges on lymphatic channels associated with the major abdominal arterial branches. Which of the following correctly describes the pathway through which lymph from most foregut organs ultimately reaches the systemic circulation?","o":["A. Regional lymph nodes → celiac lymph nodes → intestinal lymphatic trunks → cisterna chyli → thoracic duct → left venous angle.","B. Regional lymph nodes → inferior mesenteric lymph nodes → lumbar lymphatic trunks → right lymphatic duct → right venous angle.","C. Regional lymph nodes → superior mesenteric lymph nodes → right lymphatic duct → left subclavian vein.","D. Regional lymph nodes → superficial inguinal lymph nodes → cisterna chyli → thoracic duct.","E. Regional lymph nodes → hepatic veins → inferior vena cava → right atrium."],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Regional lymph nodes → celiac lymph nodes → intestinal lymphatic trunks → cisterna chyli → thoracic duct → left venous angle","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Regional nodes → celiac nodes → intestinal trunks → cisterna chyli → thoracic duct → left venous angle.</b> Lymph from most foregut organs gathers at the celiac nodes around the celiac trunk. It passes through the intestinal trunk into the cisterna chyli, climbs the thoracic duct and empties where the left internal jugular and left subclavian veins meet.<br>B. Inferior mesenteric nodes drain the hindgut; lumbar trunks carry lymph from the legs, pelvis and posterior wall; the right lymphatic duct drains only the right upper body.<br>C. The right lymphatic duct ends on the right side, not in the left subclavian vein.<br>D. Superficial inguinal nodes drain the lower limb and lower body wall.<br>E. Hepatic veins carry blood, not lymph.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice","n":"GI #2 practice questions Fall 2026 (Lecture #1 peritoneum, esophagus, stomach & celiac trunk; Lecture #2 small & large intestines)","sl":null,"f":"G113"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"192"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":51,"label":"Vagus: more peristalsis, relaxed pylorus","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G004-118.jpg","cap":"Vagal trunks increase peristalsis, relax pylorus; sympathetics do the opposite","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 118","cu":"../sl/G004-118c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G004-90.jpg","cap":"Anterior and posterior vagal trunks sending gastric branches over the stomach","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 90"},{"src":"../tb/M5_43.jpg","cap":"Anterior and posterior vagal trunks give gastric branches; the anterior trunk also sends a pyloric branch.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.43","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/M5_42.jpg","cap":"In panel B, the vagus nerves form the esophageal plexus, then anterior and posterior vagal trunks to the stomach.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.42","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"G113-16-1","s":"A 52-year-old woman undergoes surgery involving the upper abdomen. During the procedure, the surgeon identifies the anterior and posterior vagal trunks as they pass through the esophageal hiatus of the diaphragm. The surgeon explains that these nerves provide parasympathetic innervation to most foregut organs and play an important role in regulating digestive activity. Which of the following best describes the parasympathetic innervation of the foregut?","o":["A. Preganglionic fibers originate from T5–T9 and synapse in the celiac ganglia before reaching the stomach.","B. Preganglionic fibers originate from S2–S4 and travel through the pelvic splanchnic nerves to reach the liver and stomach.","C. Preganglionic fibers originate in the brainstem and travel through the vagus nerve, synapsing in ganglia located near or within the walls of target organs.","D. Preganglionic fibers originate from L1–L2 and travel through the lumbar splanchnic nerves to reach the pancreas.","E. Preganglionic fibers originate from T10–T12 and travel through the greater splanchnic nerves before synapsing in the sympathetic chain."],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Preganglionic fibers originate in the brainstem and travel through the vagus nerve, synapsing in ganglia located near or within the walls of target organs","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Preganglionic fibers originate in the brainstem, travel through the vagus nerve and synapse in ganglia near or within the walls of target organs.</b> The vagus carries parasympathetic fibers from the medulla through the esophageal hiatus as the anterior and posterior vagal trunks. They pass through the celiac plexus without synapsing and relay in small ganglia in the organ wall.<br>A. T5 to T9 fibers relaying in the celiac ganglion are sympathetic.<br>B. S2 to S4 (sacral) pelvic splanchnic nerves serve the hindgut and pelvic organs, not the foregut.<br>D. L1 to L2 (lumbar) splanchnic nerves are sympathetic, to the hindgut.<br>E. Greater splanchnic nerves come from T5 to T9, are sympathetic and synapse in the celiac ganglion, not the chain.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice","n":"GI #2 practice questions Fall 2026 (Lecture #1 peritoneum, esophagus, stomach & celiac trunk; Lecture #2 small & large intestines)","sl":null,"f":"G113"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"193"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":51,"label":"Foregut sympathetics and pain: greater splanchnic, celiac ganglion","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G004-119.jpg","cap":"Greater thoracic splanchnic nerve synapses in celiac ganglion; fibers reach stomach","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 119"},{"src":"../sl/G004-118.jpg","cap":"Stomach sympathetics: greater splanchnic nerve to celiac plexus","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 118","cu":"../sl/G004-118c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/D041-196.jpg","cap":"Foregut visceral pain returns via greater splanchnic nerves (T5–T9)","where":"2025 TA review, slide 196"},{"src":"../sl/G074-35.jpg","cap":"Stomach nerves: greater splanchnic to celiac plexus; vagal trunks","where":"2026 Harlem clickers: Peritoneum, Esophagus & Stomach, slide 35","cu":"../sl/G074-35c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/M5_42.jpg","cap":"In panel C, thoracic splanchnic nerves reach the celiac ganglion; postganglionic fibers ride the arteries to the stomach.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.42","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/N300.jpg","cap":"Greater splanchnic nerves from about T6 to T9 synapse in the celiac ganglia; pain fibers return the same way.","where":"Netter Atlas 6e, Plate 300","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"G082-24-1","s":"After eating lunch, a camper decides to take a short hike to a nearby lake. Suddenly they come across a cow moose and her calf. The moose starts to charge the camper who immediately runs for a tree and rapidly climbs up it to about 20 ft. Which nervous ganglion and plexus is involved with the inhibition of digestion in the stomach?","o":["A. Sympathetics in the superior mesenteric ganglion and plexus","B. Parasympathetics in the celiac ganglion and plexus","C. Sympathetics in the celiac ganglion and plexus","D. Parasympathetics in the superior mesenteric ganglion and plexus","E. Vegus nerve in the celiac ganglion and plexus"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Sympathetics in the celiac ganglion and plexus","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Sympathetics in the celiac ganglion and plexus.</b> Fright drives sympathetic output. Greater splanchnic fibers synapse in the celiac ganglion, and postganglionic fibers follow the arteries to the stomach, slowing peristalsis and tightening the pyloric sphincter.<br>A. Superior mesenteric ganglion: relays sympathetics to the midgut, not the stomach.<br>B. Parasympathetics in the celiac plexus: vagal fibers pass through without synapsing, and they speed digestion.<br>D. Parasympathetics in the superior mesenteric plexus: wrong division and wrong level.<br>E. Vagus nerve: parasympathetic; it stimulates digestion rather than inhibiting it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 118 (Nerve supply to stomach)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Montana interactive","n":"Montana (Great Falls) peritoneum, esophagus, stomach & celiac trunk interactive 2026 (D. Lawver)","sl":24,"f":"G082"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"194"},{"id":"F3903-336-1","s":"A 41-year-old woman is admitted to the hospital with upper abdominal pain. A gastroscopic examination reveals multiple small ulcerations in the body of the stomach. Which of the following nerves transmits the sensation of pain from this region?","o":["A. Spinal nerves T5 to T12","B. Greater thoracic splanchnic nerves","C. Lesser thoracic splanchnic nerves","D. Lumbar splanchnic nerves","E. Spinal nerves T12 to L2"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Greater thoracic splanchnic nerves","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Greater thoracic splanchnic nerves.</b> Pain from the stomach body (foregut) travels back with sympathetic fibers in the greater splanchnic nerves to cord segments T5 to T9 (fifth to ninth thoracic).<br>A. Spinal nerves T5 to T12 (fifth to twelfth thoracic) carry pain from the body wall, not the stomach.<br>C. Lesser thoracic splanchnic nerves: midgut pain.<br>D. Lumbar splanchnic nerves: hindgut pain.<br>E. Spinal nerves T12 to L2 (twelfth thoracic to second lumbar): lower body wall and groin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 118 (Nerve supply to stomach)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Harlem clicker","n":"Harlem peritoneum, esophagus, stomach & celiac trunk clickers 2026","sl":32,"f":"G074"},{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":336,"f":"F3903"},{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":271,"f":"F1180"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":214,"f":"F1178"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"195"},{"id":"G113-15-1","s":"A 45-year-old man undergoes surgery to remove a tumor involving the celiac plexus. Following surgery, the physician explains that the tumor affected autonomic nerve fibers traveling to several foregut organs, including the stomach, liver, and proximal duodenum. These sympathetic fibers normally originate from the thoracic spinal cord and travel through splanchnic nerves before reaching the abdominal autonomic plexuses. Which of the following best describes the sympathetic pathway supplying the foregut organs?","o":["A. Preganglionic fibers from T1–T4 travel through the vagus nerve and synapse in the wall of the stomach.","B. Preganglionic fibers from T5–T9 travel through the greater splanchnic nerves and synapse in prevertebral ganglia associated with the celiac plexus.","C. Preganglionic fibers from L1–L2 travel through the pelvic splanchnic nerves and synapse in the inferior mesenteric ganglion.","D. Preganglionic fibers from S2–S4 travel through the sacral splanchnic nerves and synapse in the celiac ganglion.","E. Preganglionic fibers from T10–T12 travel through the phrenic nerves and synapse in the hepatic plexus."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Preganglionic fibers from T5–T9 travel through the greater splanchnic nerves and synapse in prevertebral ganglia associated with the celiac plexus","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Preganglionic fibers from T5–T9 travel through the greater splanchnic nerves and synapse in prevertebral ganglia of the celiac plexus.</b> Foregut sympathetic fibers start in cord segments T5 to T9 (fifth to ninth thoracic). They pass through the sympathetic trunk without synapsing and relay in the celiac ganglion, in front of the aorta. Postganglionic fibers follow the arteries to the organs.<br>A. The vagus is a cranial parasympathetic nerve.<br>C. Pelvic splanchnic nerves are parasympathetic, from S2 to S4 (second to fourth sacral); L1 to L2 fibers run in lumbar splanchnic nerves to the hindgut.<br>D. S2 to S4 fibers are parasympathetic, to the hindgut and pelvis.<br>E. The phrenic nerve (C3 to C5) is the motor nerve of the diaphragm.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice","n":"GI #2 practice questions Fall 2026 (Lecture #1 peritoneum, esophagus, stomach & celiac trunk; Lecture #2 small & large intestines)","sl":null,"f":"G113"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"196"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":52,"label":"Anterior to first duodenal part → gallbladder","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G005-9.jpg","cap":"Gallbladder lying in front of the first duodenal part at L1","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 9"},{"src":"../sl/G005-10.jpg","cap":"Gallbladder and quadrate lobe resting on the first part of duodenum","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 10"},{"src":"../tb/G4_63.jpg","cap":"The gallbladder (green) sits in front of the superior (first) part of the duodenum at the L1 level.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 4.63","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/N325.jpg","cap":"In this cross section, the gallbladder lies directly in front of the superior (first) part of the duodenum.","where":"Netter Atlas 6e, Plate 325","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-371-1","s":"Your 64-year-old patient had an ulcer of the anterior wall of the 1st portion of the duodenum that resulted in an erosion into which of the following?","o":["A. Common hepatic artery","B. Portal vein","C. Gall bladder","D. Stomach","E. Common bile duct"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Gall bladder","a":"C","why":"<b>Gallbladder.</b> The gallbladder (with the quadrate lobe of the liver) lies directly <b>in front of the first part</b> of the duodenum, so an anterior-wall ulcer there can erode into it.<br>A. Common hepatic artery: runs <b>above</b> the first part, along the top of the pancreas.<br>B. Portal vein: lies <b>behind</b> the first part.<br>D. Stomach: joins the duodenum at the pylorus; it is not in front of it.<br>E. Common bile duct: lies <b>behind</b> the first part.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 8 (Superior (First part) of duodenum)","Small & Large Intestines slide 13 (Superior (First) part of duodenum)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Harlem clicker","n":"Harlem small & large intestines clickers 2026","sl":13,"f":"G109"},{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":371,"f":"F3903"},{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":119,"f":"F0008"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"197"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":53,"label":"Posterior duodenal ulcer → gastroduodenal artery","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G005-87.jpg","cap":"Duodenal ulcers are commonest on the posterior wall of the first part","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 87","cu":"../sl/G005-87c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G005-33.jpg","cap":"Duodenum reflected: gastroduodenal artery runs right behind the first part","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 33"},{"src":"../tb/G4_64.jpg","cap":"The gastroduodenal artery runs down right behind the first part of the duodenum and gives the posterior superior pancreaticoduodenal artery.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 4.64","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/N286.jpg","cap":"Top panel, duodenum flipped left: the gastroduodenal and posterior superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries lie on its back surface.","where":"Netter Atlas 6e, Plate 286","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"G110-5-1","s":"A 50-year-old man arrives at his doctor with abdominal pain and blood in his stool. His history includes regular headaches and NSAIDs taken in response. He is diagnosed with a duodenal ulcer. The artery that is at risk if the ulcer perforates the lining of the duodenum is the","o":["A. superior mesenteric a","B. inferior mesenteric a","C. celiac trunk","D. gastroduodenal a","E. marginal a"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"gastroduodenal a","a":"D","why":"<b>Gastroduodenal artery.</b> Duodenal ulcers form in the first part, and the gastroduodenal artery runs straight down directly behind it. An ulcer that eats through the posterior wall opens this artery and bleeds.<br>A. Superior mesenteric artery: lower, behind the pancreatic neck and in front of the third part.<br>B. Inferior mesenteric artery: arises at the third lumbar vertebra and goes to the hindgut.<br>C. Celiac trunk: the short parent trunk above the pancreas, not touching the duodenum.<br>E. Marginal artery: runs along the colon.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 87 (Duodenal ulcer)","Small & Large Intestines slide 88","Small & Large Intestines slide 11 (Superior  (First) part of duodenum)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Montana interactive","n":"Montana (Great Falls) small & large intestines and blood supply interactive 2026 (B. Chinnery)","sl":5,"f":"G110"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"198"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":53,"label":"Duodenal ulcer: posterior bleeds GDA, anterior perforates","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G005-87.jpg","cap":"Duodenal ulcers: common on posterior wall of the first part","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 87","cu":"../sl/G005-87c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G005-88.jpg","cap":"Gastroduodenal artery descending behind the first part of the duodenum","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 88"},{"src":"../sl/G005-11.jpg","cap":"First part of duodenum, behind: bile duct, gastroduodenal artery, portal vein, inferior vena cava","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 11"},{"src":"../tb/G4_64.jpg","cap":"The gastroduodenal artery lies just behind the first duodenal part, so a posterior ulcer bleeds from it.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 4.64","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F0008-121-1","s":"A 42-year-old woman is admitted to the hospital with an acutely painful abdomen and vomiting bright red blood. Radiographic examination reveals penetration of a posterior duodenal ulcer resulting in intraabdominal bleeding. Which of the following arteries is most commonly eroded by this type of ulcer?","o":["A. Gastroduodenal artery","B. Superior mesenteric","C. Posterior inferior pancreaticoduodenal","D. Right gastric","E. Right gastroepiploic"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastroduodenal artery","a":"A","why":"<b>Gastroduodenal artery (GDA).</b> Most duodenal ulcers are on the posterior wall of the first part of the duodenum. The GDA runs right behind that wall, so a deep ulcer erodes into it and bleeds.<br>B. Superior mesenteric: lower, behind the neck of the pancreas.<br>C. Posterior inferior pancreaticoduodenal: near the third part of the duodenum.<br>D. Right gastric: along the lesser curvature of the stomach.<br>E. Right gastroepiploic: along the greater curvature.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 87 (Duodenal ulcer)","Small & Large Intestines slide 11 (Superior  (First) part of duodenum)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":121,"f":"F0008"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"199"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":54,"label":"Third duodenal part lies on IVC and aorta","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G005-21.jpg","cap":"Third part relations: IVC and aorta behind, SMA and SMV in front","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 21","cu":"../sl/G005-21c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G005-23.jpg","cap":"Behind the third part: right psoas, IVC, aorta, right ureter","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 23","cu":"../sl/G005-23c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/G4_63.jpg","cap":"The inferior (third) part of the duodenum crosses in front of the inferior vena cava and aorta at L3.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 4.63","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-375-1","s":"A 62-year-old man complains of abdominal pain just superior to his umbilicus. On CT examination, a mass is found anterior to the IVC at the L3 or L4 level. Which of the following structures would most likely be compressed by this mass at this level?","o":["A. Third part of the duodenum","B. The portal vein","C. Right sympathetic trunk","D. Left sympathetic trunk","E. Left renal artery"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Third part of the duodenum","a":"A","why":"<b>Third part of the duodenum.</b> At the third lumbar vertebra (L3) the horizontal part crosses <b>in front of the IVC (inferior vena cava)</b> and aorta, so a mass right there presses on it.<br>B. Portal vein: forms higher, behind the neck of the pancreas.<br>C. Right sympathetic trunk: lies on the vertebral bodies <b>behind</b> the IVC.<br>D. Left sympathetic trunk: on the left side of the vertebrae, by the aorta.<br>E. Left renal artery: higher and on the left, behind the left renal vein.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 21 (Inferior (third) part)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Harlem clicker","n":"Harlem small & large intestines clickers 2026","sl":22,"f":"G109"},{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":375,"f":"F3903"},{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":111,"f":"F0008"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"200"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":54,"label":"SMA/SMV cross in front of third duodenal part","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G005-22.jpg","cap":"SMA and SMV crossing in front of the third part of duodenum","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 22"},{"src":"../sl/G005-99.jpg","cap":"SMA syndrome: no fat pad, SMA compresses the duodenum on the aorta","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 99"},{"src":"../sl/D042-147.jpg","cap":"Table: uncinate, third duodenum, left renal vein all behind the SMA","where":"2025 TA review, slide 147","cu":"../sl/D042-147c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/G4_63.jpg","cap":"The superior mesenteric artery and vein cross in front of the inferior (third) part of the duodenum.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 4.63","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/N321.jpg","cap":"Side view: the third duodenal part sits below and behind the superior mesenteric artery, in front of the aorta.","where":"Netter Atlas 6e, Plate 321","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F3114-18-1","s":"In SMA (superior mesenteric artery) syndrome, which part of the duodenum is compressed?","o":["A. First (superior) part","B. Second (descending) part","C. Third (horizontal) part","D. Fourth (ascending) part"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Third (horizontal) part","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Third (horizontal) part.</b> It crosses between the superior mesenteric artery in front and the aorta behind. Rapid weight loss shrinks the fat pad in this angle and the duodenum gets squeezed. The left renal vein crosses the same angle and can be squeezed too (nutcracker syndrome).<br>A. First part: starts at the pylorus, above this angle.<br>B. Second part: runs down to the right of the vertebrae, beside the pancreatic head.<br>D. Fourth part: rises left of the aorta to the duodenojejunal flexure.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 42 (SMA)","Small & Large Intestines slide 4 (Objectives)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"Small & large intestines interactive","sl":18,"f":"F3114"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"201"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":54,"label":"Pancreatic cancer → retroperitoneal third part of duodenum","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G005-24.jpg","cap":"Pancreatic head sits directly above the third part of duodenum","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 24"},{"src":"../sl/G007-7.jpg","cap":"Pancreatic head cradled by the C-shaped retroperitoneal duodenum","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 7","cu":"../sl/G007-7c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/G4_63.jpg","cap":"The pancreatic head sits inside the C-shaped retroperitoneal duodenum, with the inferior (third) part right below it.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 4.63","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F3114-47-1","s":"Pancreatic cancer is considered one of the deadliest cancers in humans due to the retroperitoneal location of the pancreas. This allows the cancer to spread rapidly without having to cross the peritoneal barrier. Which of the following organs would be at risk in the case of a metastatic pancreatic cancer patient?","o":["A. Stomach","B. 3rd part of the duodenum","C. Jejunum","D. Transverse colon","E. Cecum"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"3rd part of the duodenum","a":"B","why":"<b>Third part of the duodenum.</b> Like the pancreas it is <b>retroperitoneal</b>, and it lies just below the pancreatic head. A tumor can grow straight into it without crossing peritoneum.<br>A. Stomach: intraperitoneal; the lesser sac (omental bursa) separates it from the pancreas.<br>C. Jejunum: intraperitoneal, hanging on the mesentery.<br>D. Transverse colon: intraperitoneal, on the transverse mesocolon.<br>E. Cecum: in the right lower quadrant, far from the pancreas.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Pancreas & Spleen slide 7 (Pancreas)","Small & Large Intestines slide 21 (Inferior (third) part)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown SMA, IMA, small & large intestines interactive 11/12/26 (R. Connington)","sl":41,"f":"G083"},{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown SMA, IMA, small & large intestines interactive 2026, re-posted version (R. Connington)","sl":42,"f":"G108"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"Small & large intestines interactive","sl":47,"f":"F3114"},{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":359,"f":"F3903"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"202"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":54,"label":"Pancreatic head sits above the third duodenal part","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G110-12.jpg","cap":"Third part: pancreatic head and superior mesenteric vessels above; body above fourth part","where":"2026 Montana interactive, slide 12","cu":"../sl/G110-12c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G005-24.jpg","cap":"Pancreatic head resting on the third part, inside the duodenal C-loop","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 24"}],"q":[{"id":"G110-9-1","s":"The head of the pancreas sits superiorly to","o":["A. the first part of the duodenum","B. the second part of the duodenum","C. the third part of the duodenum","D. the fourth part of the duodenum"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"the third part of the duodenum","a":"C","why":"<b>The third part of the duodenum.</b> The head of the pancreas fills the C-loop and sits on top of the third (horizontal) part, which runs below it across the third lumbar vertebra. The superior mesenteric artery and vein also lie just above the third part.<br>A. First part: lies above the pancreas; only the pancreatic neck touches its lower side.<br>B. Second part: curves down the right side of the head, so the head is medial to it.<br>D. Fourth part: the body of the pancreas lies above it; the head lies to its right.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 21 (Inferior (third) part)","Small & Large Intestines slide 24 (Superiorly: SMA,SMV, head of pancreas)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Montana interactive","n":"Montana (Great Falls) small & large intestines and blood supply interactive 2026 (B. Chinnery)","sl":9,"f":"G110"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"203"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":55,"label":"Jejunum: LUQ, thick wall, long vasa recta","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G005-41.jpg","cap":"Table: jejunum vs ileum wall, vasa recta, arcades, fat, folds","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 41"},{"src":"../sl/G005-39.jpg","cap":"Jejunum: left upper quadrant, thick wall, circular folds, few arcades","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 39","cu":"../sl/G005-39c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/F3114-33.jpg","cap":"Jejunum: long vasa recta, few arcades; ileum: many arcades, fat","where":"2025 Middletown clicker, slide 33"},{"src":"../tb/M5_44.jpg","cap":"Panel A: the jejunum coils in the upper left of the abdomen; the ileum lies lower right.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.44","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/G4_66.jpg","cap":"Panel A (jejunum): one or two rows of arterial arcades and long vasa recta.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 4.66","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F3914-29-1","s":"What features of the jejunum differentiate it from the ileum?","o":["A. Deeper red color, thin-walled, long vasa recta, less prominent arterial arcades","B. Thin and light wall, long vasa recta, many short arterial arcades, more fat","C. More plicae circularis, long vasa recta, few large arterial arcades, less fat","D. Peyers patches, thick-walled, greater vascularity, more fat"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"More plicae circularis, long vasa recta, few large arterial arcades, less fat","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> The jejunum has <b>more circular folds</b> (plicae circulares), <b>long vasa recta</b>, a <b>few large arterial loops</b> and <b>less fat</b> in its mesentery.<br>A. Wrong because the jejunum is <b>thick</b>-walled, not thin.<br>B. Describes the ileum: thin wall, many short arcades, more fat.<br>D. Peyer patches and more fat are ileum features.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 39 (Jejunum)","Small & Large Intestines slide 40 (Ileum)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown SMA, IMA, small & large intestines interactive 11/12/26 (R. Connington)","sl":29,"f":"G083"},{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown SMA, IMA, small & large intestines interactive 2026, re-posted version (R. Connington)","sl":29,"f":"G108"},{"t":"2023 Middletown TA weekly","n":"09-28-23 TA session","sl":29,"f":"F3914"},{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"10-06-2022 TA session (foregut viscera)","sl":9,"f":"F3907"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"204"},{"id":"F3114-31-1","s":"Which feature marks the jejunum rather than the ileum?","o":["A. Many rows of small arterial arcades","B. Abundant fat in the mesentery","C. Peyer patches","D. Location mainly in the right lower quadrant","E. Long vasa recta"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Long vasa recta","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Long vasa recta.</b> The jejunum has a few large arcades that send long straight arteries (vasa recta) to a thick, folded, very vascular wall. It absorbs most nutrients.<br>A. Many rows of small arcades: ileum.<br>B. Abundant mesenteric fat: ileum; the jejunal mesentery is thin and clear.<br>C. Peyer patches: lymphoid nodules of the ileum.<br>D. Right lower quadrant: ileum; the jejunum lies mainly in the left upper quadrant.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 39 (Jejunum)","Small & Large Intestines slide 40 (Ileum)","Small & Large Intestines slide 44 (Superior mesenteric artery)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"Small & large intestines interactive","sl":31,"f":"F3114"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"205"},{"id":"G113-17-1","s":"A 24-year-old patient undergoes abdominal surgery following a traumatic injury. The surgeon identifies a portion of the small intestine that has numerous prominent circular folds, long vasa recta, and relatively few arterial arcades. The bowel is located primarily in the upper left quadrant of the abdominal cavity. Which of the following best describes the anatomical and functional characteristics of this portion of the small intestine?","o":["A. Jejunum; primarily responsible for nutrient absorption and characterized by prominent plicae circulares, few arterial arcades, and long vasa recta.","B. Ileum; primarily responsible for vitamin B12 absorption and characterized by numerous arterial arcades and short vasa recta.","C. Duodenum; primarily responsible for bile storage and characterized by extensive mesenteric attachments.","D. Ileum; primarily responsible for iron absorption and characterized by prominent plicae circulares and long vasa recta.","E. Jejunum; primarily responsible for vitamin B12 absorption and characterized by numerous Peyer's patches."],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Jejunum; primarily responsible for nutrient absorption and characterized by prominent plicae circulares, few arterial arcades, and long vasa recta","a":"A","why":"<b>Jejunum: nutrient absorption, prominent circular folds, few arterial arcades, long vasa recta.</b> Left upper quadrant, tall crowded circular folds (plicae circulares), a few large arcades and long straight vessels (vasa recta) mark the jejunum.<br>B. Ileum, vitamin B12, many arcades, short vasa recta: true of the ileum, which lies in the right lower quadrant with low folds.<br>C. Duodenum, bile storage: the gallbladder stores bile; the duodenum is fixed, with almost no mesentery.<br>D. Ileum, iron, prominent folds, long vasa recta: iron is taken up in the duodenum and proximal jejunum, and these are jejunal features.<br>E. Jejunum, vitamin B12, Peyer patches: both belong to the ileum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice","n":"GI #2 practice questions Fall 2026 (Lecture #1 peritoneum, esophagus, stomach & celiac trunk; Lecture #2 small & large intestines)","sl":null,"f":"G113"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"206"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":55,"label":"Ileum: RLQ, many arcades, fat, Peyer patches","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G005-41.jpg","cap":"Table: jejunum vs ileum wall, vasa recta, arcades, fat, folds","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 41"},{"src":"../sl/G005-40.jpg","cap":"Ileum: right lower quadrant, thin wall, Peyer patches, more arcades","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 40","cu":"../sl/G005-40c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/F3114-33.jpg","cap":"Ileum: short vasa recta, stacked arcades, encroaching fat","where":"2025 Middletown clicker, slide 33"}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-347-1","s":"A 62-year old patient schedules a visit with his physician due to increasing pain in his abdomen and a steady decrease in weight regardless of his diet. An MRI shows an inflamed small intestine. To determine which part of the small intestine is most likely affected, the physician must differentiate between the jejunum and ileum. Which of the following characteristics are typical of the ileum?","o":["(A) Less plicae circulares","(B) Less mesenteric arterial arcades","(C) More absorption nutrients","(D) Longer vasa recta","(E) Less fat in its mesentery"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Less plicae circulares","a":"A","why":"<b>Less plicae circulares (fewer circular folds).</b> The ileum's folds are low and sparse, and gone near its end.<br>B. Fewer arterial arcades: jejunum. The ileum has <b>more</b>.<br>C. More nutrient absorption: jejunum.<br>D. Longer vasa recta: jejunum. The ileum's are <b>short</b>.<br>E. Less fat in the mesentery: jejunum. The ileum has <b>more</b> fat.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 39 (Jejunum)","Small & Large Intestines slide 40 (Ileum)","Small & Large Intestines slide 44 (Superior mesenteric artery)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown SMA, IMA, small & large intestines interactive 11/12/26 (R. Connington)","sl":27,"f":"G083"},{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown SMA, IMA, small & large intestines interactive 2026, re-posted version (R. Connington)","sl":28,"f":"G108"},{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":347,"f":"F3903"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"Small & large intestines interactive","sl":30,"f":"F3114"},{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":320,"f":"F1180"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":259,"f":"F1178"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"207"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":56,"label":"SMA arises from aorta at L1","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G005-42.jpg","cap":"SMA arises from the front of the aorta at lower L1","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 42","cu":"../sl/G005-42c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G012-25.jpg","cap":"Sagittal CT: celiac trunk, then SMA just below, off the aorta","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen III, slide 25","cu":"../sl/G012-25c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/G4_167.jpg","cap":"The superior mesenteric artery leaves the aorta at the lower border of L1, just below the celiac trunk.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 4.167","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/N321.jpg","cap":"Side view: the superior mesenteric artery leaves the front of the aorta at L1, just below the celiac trunk.","where":"Netter Atlas 6e, Plate 321","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"G081-9-1","s":"On a line diagram of the abdominal aorta, which sequence lists its three unpaired anterior (gut) branches from superior to inferior?","o":["A. Celiac trunk, inferior mesenteric artery, superior mesenteric artery","B. Celiac trunk, superior mesenteric artery, inferior mesenteric artery","C. Superior mesenteric artery, celiac trunk, inferior mesenteric artery","D. Inferior phrenic artery, celiac trunk, superior mesenteric artery","E. Superior mesenteric artery, inferior mesenteric artery, celiac trunk"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Celiac trunk, superior mesenteric artery, inferior mesenteric artery","a":"B","why":"<b>Celiac trunk, superior mesenteric artery, inferior mesenteric artery.</b> The three unpaired gut arteries leave the front of the aorta one below the other: the celiac trunk at T12 (twelfth thoracic vertebra), the SMA (superior mesenteric artery) at L1 (first lumbar vertebra), the IMA (inferior mesenteric artery) at about L3.<br>A. Celiac, IMA, SMA: the IMA is the lowest, not the middle one.<br>C. SMA, celiac, IMA: the celiac trunk is the highest.<br>D. Inferior phrenic, celiac, SMA: the inferior phrenic arteries are paired and go to the diaphragm, and the IMA is missing.<br>E. SMA, IMA, celiac: upside down.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 42 (SMA)","Small & Large Intestines slide 79 (Inferior mesenteric artery)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 109 (Blood supply to stomach)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive 11/11/26 (J. LaBarge)","sl":9,"f":"G081"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"208"},{"id":"G113-23-1","s":"A patient undergoes mesenteric angiography following suspected acute intestinal ischemia. The radiologist identifies an artery arising from the anterior aspect of the abdominal aorta at approximately the level of L1. It gives rise to jejunal, ileal, middle colic, and right colic branches. Which of the following vessels is being visualized?","o":["A. Celiac trunk.","B. Inferior mesenteric artery.","C. Superior mesenteric artery.","D. Renal artery.","E. Common hepatic artery."],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Superior mesenteric artery","a":"C","why":"<b>Superior mesenteric artery (SMA).</b> It leaves the front of the aorta at the first lumbar vertebra (L1), about 1 cm below the celiac trunk, and gives the jejunal, ileal, ileocolic, right colic and middle colic arteries to the midgut.<br>A. Celiac trunk: twelfth thoracic vertebra; left gastric, splenic and common hepatic branches.<br>B. Inferior mesenteric artery: about the third lumbar vertebra; left colic, sigmoid and superior rectal branches.<br>D. Renal artery: leaves the side of the aorta for the kidney.<br>E. Common hepatic artery: a branch of the celiac trunk, not of the aorta.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice","n":"GI #2 practice questions Fall 2026 (Lecture #1 peritoneum, esophagus, stomach & celiac trunk; Lecture #2 small & large intestines)","sl":null,"f":"G113"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"209"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":56,"label":"Uncinate process lies behind the SMA","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G005-22.jpg","cap":"Uncinate process tucked behind the SMV and SMA","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 22"},{"src":"../sl/D041-222.jpg","cap":"Behind the SMA: left renal vein, uncinate process, third duodenum","where":"2025 TA review, slide 222","cu":"../sl/D041-222c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"G113-21-1","s":"A 55-year-old patient undergoes surgery to remove a tumor located near the uncinate process of the pancreas. During dissection, the surgeon identifies the superior mesenteric artery and vein passing anterior to the third part of the duodenum. The surgeon also identifies the left renal vein passing posterior to the superior mesenteric artery. Which of the following best describes these anatomical relationships?","o":["A. The superior mesenteric vessels pass posterior to the third part of the duodenum and anterior to the uncinate process.","B. The superior mesenteric artery passes anterior to the third part of the duodenum and posterior to the neck of the pancreas, while the uncinate process extends posterior to the superior mesenteric vessels.","C. The superior mesenteric artery passes anterior to the left renal vein and posterior to the third part of the duodenum.","D. The superior mesenteric artery passes anterior to the uncinate process and posterior to the left renal vein.","E. The superior mesenteric artery passes through the pancreatic parenchyma and anterior to the second part of the duodenum."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"The superior mesenteric artery passes anterior to the third part of the duodenum and posterior to the neck of the pancreas, while the uncinate process extends posterior to the superior mesenteric vessels","a":"B","why":"<b>The superior mesenteric artery (SMA) runs in front of the third part of the duodenum and behind the pancreatic neck; the uncinate process lies behind the superior mesenteric vessels.</b> Behind the SMA, top to bottom: left renal vein, uncinate process, third part of the duodenum.<br>A. Vessels behind the third part of the duodenum: reversed; they cross in front of it.<br>C. Behind the third part of the duodenum: reversed (in front of the left renal vein is right).<br>D. Behind the left renal vein: reversed; the vein passes behind the SMA.<br>E. Through the pancreas, in front of the second part: it passes behind the neck and crosses the third part.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice","n":"GI #2 practice questions Fall 2026 (Lecture #1 peritoneum, esophagus, stomach & celiac trunk; Lecture #2 small & large intestines)","sl":null,"f":"G113"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"210"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":56,"label":"SMV runs to the right of SMA","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G005-43.jpg","cap":"SMV (blue) running to the right of the SMA (red)","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 43"},{"src":"../sl/G005-22.jpg","cap":"SMV and SMA side by side over the uncinate and third duodenum","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 22"},{"src":"../tb/G4_115.jpg","cap":"The superior mesenteric vein (blue) runs on the right side of the superior mesenteric artery.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 4.115","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/N327.jpg","cap":"Cross section: the superior mesenteric vein sits to the right of the superior mesenteric artery.","where":"Netter Atlas 6e, Plate 327","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-377-1","s":"The superior mesenteric artery:","o":["A) arises from the aorta at the T10 level.","B) is accompanied by the superior mesenteric vein on its right.","C) gives rise to a series of arterial arcades called vasa recta.","D) passes posterior to the horizontal part of the duodenum.","E) is accompanied by sympathetic fibers derived from lumbar splanchnic nerves."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"is accompanied by the superior mesenteric vein on its right","a":"B","why":"<b>Accompanied by the SMV (superior mesenteric vein) on its right.</b> The vein runs beside the SMA (superior mesenteric artery) on its right side, up to join the splenic vein.<br>A. It arises at the first lumbar vertebra, not the tenth thoracic.<br>C. Its branches form arcades; vasa recta are the straight arteries that <b>leave</b> the arcades.<br>D. It passes <b>in front of</b> the horizontal part.<br>E. Its sympathetic fibers come from <b>thoracic</b> splanchnic nerves; lumbar splanchnics serve the inferior mesenteric plexus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 42 (SMA)","Radiology Abd III slide 26 (Superior Mesenteric Artery)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":377,"f":"F3903"},{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":113,"f":"F0008"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"211"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":57,"label":"SMA syndrome: third duodenum between SMA, aorta","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G005-99.jpg","cap":"Side view: fat lost, duodenum pinched between superior mesenteric artery and aorta","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 99"},{"src":"../sl/G005-97.jpg","cap":"Superior mesenteric vessels crossing in front of the third part of duodenum","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 97"},{"src":"../sl/D042-147.jpg","cap":"Table: uncinate process, third duodenum, left renal vein relations to SMA","where":"2025 TA review, slide 147","cu":"../sl/D042-147c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-302-1","s":"A patient with SMA syndrome experiences severe postprandial pain due to compression of which structure between the SMA and aorta?","o":["A. First part of duodenum","B. Appendix","C. Third part of duodenum","D. Ascending colon"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Third part of duodenum","a":"C","why":"<b>Third part of the duodenum.</b> The third (horizontal) part crosses in front of the aorta and behind the SMA (superior mesenteric artery), right in the angle between them.<br>A. First part of duodenum: at L1 (first lumbar vertebra), above the SMA origin.<br>B. Appendix: right lower quadrant.<br>D. Ascending colon: right posterior abdominal wall.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 96 (SMA syndrome)","Small & Large Intestines slide 97 (Anteriorly: SMA,SMV, small intestines)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown SMA, IMA, small & large intestines interactive 11/12/26 (R. Connington)","sl":16,"f":"G083"},{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown SMA, IMA, small & large intestines interactive 2026, re-posted version (R. Connington)","sl":17,"f":"G108"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":302,"f":"D045"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"212"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":57,"label":"Nutcracker: SMA compresses left renal vein","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G007-8.jpg","cap":"Left renal vein crossing the aorta just below the SMA origin","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 8"},{"src":"../sl/D042-147.jpg","cap":"Table: left renal vein squeezed between SMA and aorta (nutcracker)","where":"2025 TA review, slide 147","cu":"../sl/D042-147c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"F3902-132-1","s":"A 67 yr old female presents to your office with blood in her urine. She tells you that she has had lower quadrant abdominal pain, as well as nausea and vomiting. Upon further examination, you recognize that the Superior Mesenteric Artery is compressing a structure against the abdominal Aorta, causing these symptoms. What blood supply is being directly compressed in this syndrome?","o":["A. Superior Mesenteric Vein","B. Left Renal Vein","C. Hepatic Portal Vein","D. Left Renal Artery","E. Inferior Vena Cava"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Left Renal Vein","a":"B","why":"<b>Left renal vein.</b> In nutcracker syndrome the SMA (superior mesenteric artery) pins the <b>left renal vein against the aorta</b>. Back-pressure in the kidney causes blood in the urine and pain.<br>A. Superior mesenteric vein: runs to the right of the SMA, not between SMA and aorta.<br>C. Hepatic portal vein: forms behind the pancreatic neck and runs up to the liver.<br>D. Left renal artery: comes off the aorta itself, behind the vein.<br>E. Inferior vena cava: lies to the right of the aorta, outside the angle.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 42 (SMA)","Small & Large Intestines slide 4 (Objectives)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown SMA, IMA, small & large intestines interactive 11/12/26 (R. Connington)","sl":16,"f":"G083"},{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown SMA, IMA, small & large intestines interactive 2026, re-posted version (R. Connington)","sl":17,"f":"G108"},{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":132,"f":"F3902"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"213"},{"id":"G110-13-1","s":"A 21-year-old female presents with intermittent left flank pain and gross hematuria. A contrast-enhanced CT scan of the abdomen reveals narrowing of a specific vascular structure as it courses through an acute angle formed by the abdominal aorta and the superior mesenteric artery. Compression of which of the following vessels is responsible for this patient's presentation?","o":["A. L. renal artery","B. R. renal artery","C. R. renal vein","D. L. renal vein"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"L. renal vein","a":"D","why":"<b>Left renal vein.</b> Flank pain and blood in the urine, with a vessel narrowed in the angle between the superior mesenteric artery (SMA) and the aorta, is nutcracker syndrome. The left renal vein must cross in front of the aorta, under the SMA, to reach the inferior vena cava.<br>A. Left renal artery: leaves the aorta itself, behind the vein, so it is not in the angle.<br>B. Right renal artery: passes behind the inferior vena cava, far right of the angle.<br>C. Right renal vein: short; it enters the inferior vena cava directly and never crosses the aorta.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 42 (SMA)","Small & Large Intestines slide 43 (Crossed anteriorly by the splenic vein and the neck of the pancreas.)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Montana interactive","n":"Montana (Great Falls) small & large intestines and blood supply interactive 2026 (B. Chinnery)","sl":13,"f":"G110"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"214"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":58,"label":"Small-bowel veins → SMV → portal vein","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G005-49.jpg","cap":"Jejunal and ileal veins draining into the SMV and hepatic portal vein","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 49"},{"src":"../sl/G005-35.jpg","cap":"SMV joins the splenic vein to form the portal vein","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 35"},{"src":"../tb/M5_33.jpg","cap":"Panel D: jejunal and ileal veins join the superior mesenteric vein, which forms the hepatic portal vein.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.33","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"G113-20-1","s":"A 72-year-old patient develops acute mesenteric ischemia involving a segment of the small intestine. Angiography reveals an obstruction of the superior mesenteric artery proximal to the origin of several intestinal branches. The patient subsequently develops impaired venous drainage from the affected region. Which of the following correctly describes the arterial and venous circulation of the small intestine?","o":["A. The Jejunum and ileum receive arterial blood from the inferior mesenteric artery, and venous blood drains into the inferior mesenteric vein.","B. The jejunum and ileum receive arterial blood from the hepatic artery, and venous blood drains directly into the inferior vena cava.","C. The jejunum receives blood from the celiac trunk, while the ileum receives blood from the inferior mesenteric artery.","D. The jejunum and ileum receive arterial blood from jejunal and ileal branches of the superior mesenteric artery, and venous blood drains into the superior mesenteric vein, which contributes to the hepatic portal vein.","E. The jejunum and ileum receive blood from the inferior mesenteric artery, and venous blood drains directly into the hepatic veins."],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"The jejunum and ileum receive arterial blood from jejunal and ileal branches of the superior mesenteric artery, and venous blood drains into the superior mesenteric vein, which contributes to the hepatic portal vein","a":"D","why":"<b>Jejunal and ileal branches of the superior mesenteric artery; drainage to the superior mesenteric vein, which helps form the hepatic portal vein.</b> The small bowel has no other arterial source, and its vein joins the splenic vein behind the pancreatic neck to form the portal vein.<br>A. Inferior mesenteric artery and vein: they serve the hindgut.<br>B. Hepatic artery, straight to the inferior vena cava: the hepatic artery feeds the liver, and gut blood passes through the liver first.<br>C. Celiac trunk to jejunum, inferior mesenteric artery to ileum: both get superior mesenteric branches only.<br>E. Inferior mesenteric artery, hepatic veins: wrong artery; hepatic veins drain the liver itself.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice","n":"GI #2 practice questions Fall 2026 (Lecture #1 peritoneum, esophagus, stomach & celiac trunk; Lecture #2 small & large intestines)","sl":null,"f":"G113"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"215"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":58,"label":"SMA supplies midgut: jejunum to two-thirds transverse colon","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G001-22.jpg","cap":"Midgut: duodenum past bile duct to two-thirds transverse colon; SMA","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 22","cu":"../sl/G001-22c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G005-45.jpg","cap":"Table: SMA branches and the gut segment each one supplies","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 45"},{"src":"../sl/G005-80.jpg","cap":"SMA and IMA branches with the marginal artery along the colon","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 80"},{"src":"../tb/M5_33.jpg","cap":"Panel C: the superior mesenteric artery (orange branches) supplies small intestine through the proximal two-thirds of transverse colon.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.33","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F3114-37-1","s":"Where does the colon's arterial supply switch from the superior mesenteric artery to the inferior mesenteric artery?","o":["A. At the ileocecal junction","B. At the hepatic (right colic) flexure","C. Where the descending colon meets the sigmoid colon","D. Where the sigmoid colon meets the rectum","E. Two-thirds of the way along the transverse colon"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Two-thirds of the way along the transverse colon","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Two-thirds of the way along the transverse colon.</b> This is the midgut–hindgut border. The superior mesenteric artery supplies the colon up to here through the middle colic; the inferior mesenteric artery takes over through the left colic.<br>A. Ileocecal junction: both sides belong to the superior mesenteric (ileocolic artery).<br>B. Hepatic flexure: still midgut, fed by the right and middle colic arteries.<br>C. Descending–sigmoid junction: both sides are hindgut, fed by the inferior mesenteric.<br>D. Sigmoid–rectum junction: still the inferior mesenteric (superior rectal artery).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 44 (Superior mesenteric artery)","Small & Large Intestines slide 81 (Marginal Artery)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"Small & large intestines interactive","sl":37,"f":"F3114"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"216"},{"id":"F3114-36-1","s":"An arteriogram of a 34-year old female reveals a blood clot which is blocking the proximal part of her SMA. She is rushed to the OR. Her physician fears that which of the following portion of her intestines might have become ischemic?","o":["A. Duodenum","B. Jejunum","C. Transverse colon","D. Sigmoid colon","E. Rectum"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Jejunum","a":"B","why":"<b>Jejunum.</b> It gets <b>all</b> its blood from the SMA (superior mesenteric artery) and has no other source, so a proximal SMA clot makes it ischemic.<br>A. Duodenum: only the lower half is SMA-fed, and the celiac also fills it through the pancreaticoduodenal arcade.<br>C. Transverse colon: only the proximal two-thirds is SMA; the marginal artery brings IMA (inferior mesenteric artery) blood.<br>D. Sigmoid colon: IMA territory.<br>E. Rectum: IMA plus internal iliac branches.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 44 (Superior mesenteric artery)","Small & Large Intestines slide 22 (Anteriorly: SMA,SMV, small intestines)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown SMA, IMA, small & large intestines interactive 11/12/26 (R. Connington)","sl":32,"f":"G083"},{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown SMA, IMA, small & large intestines interactive 2026, re-posted version (R. Connington)","sl":33,"f":"G108"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"Small & large intestines interactive","sl":36,"f":"F3114"},{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":351,"f":"F3903"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"217"},{"id":"G083-34-3","s":"Where does the territory of the superior mesenteric artery end and that of the inferior mesenteric artery begin?","o":["A. Ileocecal junction","B. Right colic (hepatic) flexure","C. Junction of the proximal two-thirds and distal one-third of the transverse colon","D. Junction of the descending and sigmoid colon","E. Rectosigmoid junction"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Junction of the proximal two-thirds and distal one-third of the transverse colon","a":"C","why":"<b>Junction of the proximal two-thirds and distal one-third of the transverse colon.</b> This is the midgut–hindgut border. The middle colic artery of the SMA (superior mesenteric artery) feeds the colon up to here; the left colic artery of the IMA (inferior mesenteric artery) takes over beyond it, and the marginal artery links them.<br>A. Ileocecal junction: both sides are SMA (ileocolic artery).<br>B. Right colic (hepatic) flexure: still midgut, fed by the right and middle colic arteries.<br>D. Descending–sigmoid junction: both sides are IMA.<br>E. Rectosigmoid junction: still IMA (sigmoid and superior rectal arteries).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 67 (Transverse colon)","Small & Large Intestines slide 68 (Transverse colon)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown SMA, IMA, small & large intestines interactive 11/12/26 (R. Connington)","sl":34,"f":"G083"},{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown SMA, IMA, small & large intestines interactive 2026, re-posted version (R. Connington)","sl":34,"f":"G108"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"218"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":58,"label":"SMA branches, each with the organ it feeds","slides":[{"src":"../sl/D042-154.jpg","cap":"SMA line diagram: inferior pancreaticoduodenal first, ileocolic last, beside an angiogram","where":"2025 TA review, slide 154"},{"src":"../sl/G005-45.jpg","cap":"Table: each superior mesenteric branch and the gut segment it supplies","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 45"},{"src":"../sl/G005-31.jpg","cap":"Duodenum: superior pancreaticoduodenals from gastroduodenal, inferior ones from the SMA","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 31"}],"q":[{"id":"G083-34-1","s":"Which organ is correctly paired with the branch of the superior mesenteric artery that supplies it?","o":["A. Cecum – middle colic artery","B. Ascending colon – left colic artery","C. Distal duodenum and lower head of the pancreas – inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery","D. Jejunum – ileocolic artery","E. Appendix – right colic artery"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Distal duodenum and lower head of the pancreas – inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery","a":"C","why":"<b>Distal duodenum and lower head of the pancreas – inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery.</b> It is the first branch of the SMA (superior mesenteric artery).<br>A. Cecum – middle colic: the cecum gets the ileocolic; the middle colic feeds the transverse colon.<br>B. Ascending colon – left colic: the ascending colon gets the right colic; the left colic comes from the IMA (inferior mesenteric artery).<br>D. Jejunum – ileocolic: the jejunum gets the jejunal arteries; the ileocolic feeds the terminal ileum and cecum.<br>E. Appendix – right colic: the appendix gets the appendicular artery, from the ileocolic.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 31 (duodenum)","Small & Large Intestines slide 44 (Superior mesenteric artery)","Small & Large Intestines slide 61 (Cecum and Vermiform appendix)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown SMA, IMA, small & large intestines interactive 11/12/26 (R. Connington)","sl":34,"f":"G083"},{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown SMA, IMA, small & large intestines interactive 2026, re-posted version (R. Connington)","sl":34,"f":"G108"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"219"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":58,"label":"Postprandial pain + weight loss → SMA stenosis","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G012-25.jpg","cap":"Sagittal CT: SMA leaving front of aorta just below celiac trunk","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen III, slide 25","cu":"../sl/G012-25c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G005-42.jpg","cap":"SMA: front branch of aorta at L1 supplying the midgut","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 42","cu":"../sl/G005-42c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/N321.jpg","cap":"Side view: the superior mesenteric artery leaves the front of the aorta just below the celiac trunk.","where":"Netter Atlas 6e, Plate 321","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F0008-123-1","s":"Your 55-year-old patient complains of slowly progressive postprandial abdominal pain and weight loss. A sagittal image from a CT arteriogram is shown. The red arrows show stenosis of which artery?","o":["A. Superior mesenteric artery","B. Inferior mesenteric artery","C. Right renal artery","D. Inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery","E. Right external iliac artery"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Superior mesenteric artery","a":"A","why":"<b>Superior mesenteric artery (SMA).</b> Slowly worsening pain after meals plus weight loss is <b>chronic mesenteric ischemia</b> (intestinal angina), usually from SMA narrowing. On a sagittal CT (computed tomography) scan the SMA leaves the front of the aorta just below the celiac trunk.<br>B. IMA (inferior mesenteric artery): narrowing is usually silent, as the marginal artery fills in.<br>C. Right renal artery: causes high blood pressure, not meal pain.<br>D. Inferior pancreaticoduodenal: a small SMA branch.<br>E. Right external iliac: causes leg pain on walking.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"The CT arteriogram referred to is not present in the deck; answerable from the vignette (postprandial pain + weight loss = chronic mesenteric ischemia).","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 42 (SMA)","Radiology Abd III slide 25 (Superior Mesenteric Artery)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Harlem clicker","n":"Harlem small & large intestines clickers 2026","sl":7,"f":"G109"},{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":123,"f":"F0008"},{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":369,"f":"F3903"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"220"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":59,"label":"Ascending colon is secondarily retroperitoneal","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G005-63.jpg","cap":"Ascending colon on right posterior wall, secondarily retroperitoneal","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 63","cu":"../sl/G005-63c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/D041-210.jpg","cap":"Cross-section: retroperitoneal ascending colon versus jejunum on its mesentery","where":"2025 TA review, slide 210","cu":"../sl/D041-210c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/N328.jpg","cap":"The ascending colon sits flat on the right back wall with no mesentery, while jejunum and ileum hang on one.","where":"Netter Atlas 6e, Plate 328","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/M5_25.jpg","cap":"This mesocolon fusion, shown for the descending colon, also makes the ascending colon secondarily retroperitoneal.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.25","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"D041-208-1","s":"Which portion of the colon is classified as secondarily retroperitoneal?","o":["A. Transverse colon","B. Sigmoid colon","C. Cecum","D. Ascending colon"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Ascending colon","a":"D","why":"<b>Ascending colon.</b> It starts out with a mesentery in the embryo. That mesentery then fuses to the posterior wall, so the colon becomes secondarily retroperitoneal. The descending colon does the same.<br>A. Transverse colon: intraperitoneal, hanging on the transverse mesocolon.<br>B. Sigmoid colon: intraperitoneal, on the sigmoid mesocolon.<br>C. Cecum: intraperitoneal; it has no mesentery but is almost fully covered by peritoneum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 63 (Ascending colon)","Small & Large Intestines slide 69 (Descending colon)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 13 (Peritoneal organs)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown SMA, IMA, small & large intestines interactive 11/12/26 (R. Connington)","sl":43,"f":"G083"},{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown SMA, IMA, small & large intestines interactive 2026, re-posted version (R. Connington)","sl":43,"f":"G108"},{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":208,"f":"D041"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"221"},{"id":"G113-18-1","s":"A 68-year-old patient undergoes abdominal imaging that reveals inflammation involving the ascending colon. The surgeon explains that most of the ascending colon is fixed to the posterior abdominal wall, whereas other portions of the large intestine are suspended by mesenteries. Which of the following correctly identifies the peritoneal relationships of the large intestine?","o":["A. Cecum and transverse colon are retroperitoneal, while the ascending and descending colon are intraperitoneal.","B. Ascending and descending colon are secondary retroperitoneal, while the transverse and sigmoid colon are intraperitoneal.","C. Entire large intestine is retroperitoneal except for the rectum.","D. Ascending colon and sigmoid colon are intraperitoneal, while the transverse colon is retroperitoneal.","E. Cecum, ascending colon, and descending colon are all primarily retroperitoneal."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Ascending and descending colon are secondary retroperitoneal, while the transverse and sigmoid colon are intraperitoneal","a":"B","why":"<b>Ascending and descending colon are secondarily retroperitoneal; transverse and sigmoid colon are intraperitoneal.</b> The ascending and descending colon lost their mesenteries by fusion to the posterior wall; the transverse and sigmoid colon still hang on mesocolons.<br>A. Cecum and transverse colon retroperitoneal: reversed; both are intraperitoneal.<br>C. All retroperitoneal except the rectum: the transverse and sigmoid colon hang free, and the rectum is fixed.<br>D. Transverse colon retroperitoneal: it is the most mobile part, on the transverse mesocolon; the ascending colon is fixed.<br>E. Primarily retroperitoneal: they once had a mesentery, so they are secondarily retroperitoneal; the cecum is intraperitoneal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice","n":"GI #2 practice questions Fall 2026 (Lecture #1 peritoneum, esophagus, stomach & celiac trunk; Lecture #2 small & large intestines)","sl":null,"f":"G113"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"222"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":59,"label":"Behind sigmoid mesocolon apex: ureter, iliac split","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G005-73.jpg","cap":"Sigmoid mesocolon; left ureter and common iliac division lie behind it","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 73","cu":"../sl/G005-73c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/N265.jpg","cap":"Inset: lifting the sigmoid mesocolon shows the left ureter and dividing common iliac vessels behind its apex.","where":"Netter Atlas 6e, Plate 265","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F0008-147-1","s":"A 37-year-old male comes into the clinic with an abdominal lump and pain in the left hypogastric region. He mentions the lump seems to have been developing for the past four months. A CT scan shows a tumor of the sigmoid colon. Which of the following structures, deep to the apex of the sigmoid mesocolon, could be at risk during the excising of the tumor?","o":["A. Testicular artery","B. Inferior mesenteric artery","C. Obturator artery","D. Superior rectal artery","E. External iliac artery"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"External iliac artery","a":"E","why":"<b>External iliac artery.</b> Deep to the apex of the root of the sigmoid mesocolon lie the left ureter and the spot where the left common iliac artery splits into internal and external iliac arteries. Both are at risk when the sigmoid is removed.<br>A. Testicular artery: runs down on the psoas, lateral to this spot.<br>B. Inferior mesenteric artery: arises much higher, at L3 (third lumbar vertebra).<br>C. Obturator artery: deep in the pelvis, on the side wall.<br>D. Superior rectal artery: runs inside the mesocolon, not behind its apex.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 73 (Sigmoid colon)","Small & Large Intestines slide 72 (Sigmoid colon)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":147,"f":"F0008"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"223"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":59,"label":"Sigmoid colon: commonest adult volvulus","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G110-35.jpg","cap":"Volvulus: a sigmoid loop twisted on itself; megacolon on X-ray beside it","where":"2026 Montana interactive, slide 35"},{"src":"../sl/G005-73.jpg","cap":"Sigmoid colon on its long, mobile mesocolon, prone to volvulus","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 73","cu":"../sl/G005-73c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"G110-33-1","s":"A 60-year-old patient presents with severe pain in his abdominopelvic cavity, vomiting, fever, and a distended stomach. An abdominal radiograph confirms the presence of a volvulus. Which of the following is the most common place for a volvulus to occur in adults?","o":["A. stomach","B. small intestine","C. ascending colon","D. descending colon","E. sigmoid colon"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"sigmoid colon","a":"E","why":"<b>Sigmoid colon.</b> It hangs on the long, mobile sigmoid mesocolon, so a loop can twist on itself. It is the commonest site of volvulus in adults.<br>A. Stomach: rarely twists; its omenta and ligaments hold it.<br>B. Small intestine: twisting of the whole small bowel (midgut volvulus) is mainly a problem of infants with malrotation.<br>C. Ascending colon: secondarily retroperitoneal and fixed, so it cannot twist.<br>D. Descending colon: also secondarily retroperitoneal and fixed.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 100 (Volvulus)","Small & Large Intestines slide 73 (Sigmoid colon)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Montana interactive","n":"Montana (Great Falls) small & large intestines and blood supply interactive 2026 (B. Chinnery)","sl":33,"f":"G110"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"224"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":60,"label":"Appendix arises from the posteromedial cecum","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G083-40.jpg","cap":"Appendix stems from the cecum; the nearby ileocolic gives the appendicular artery","where":"2026 Middletown interactive, slide 40"},{"src":"../sl/G005-57.jpg","cap":"Vermiform appendix hanging from the cecum, just below the ileal opening","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 57","cu":"../sl/G005-57c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"G083-39-1","s":"The vermiform appendix arises from which site?","o":["A. Terminal ileum","B. Posteromedial wall of the cecum","C. Lateral wall of the ascending colon","D. Right colic (hepatic) flexure"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Posteromedial wall of the cecum","a":"B","why":"<b>Posteromedial wall of the cecum.</b> The appendix arises from the back and inner (medial) side of the cecum, below the ileocecal junction. Its base is fixed there in everyone, while its free tip can point in different directions.<br>A. Terminal ileum: enters the cecum from the left, just above the appendix base.<br>C. Lateral wall of the ascending colon: the appendix comes off the cecum, below the ascending colon and on its medial side.<br>D. Right colic (hepatic) flexure: lies high, under the liver, far from the appendix.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 59 (Vermiform appendix)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown SMA, IMA, small & large intestines interactive 11/12/26 (R. Connington)","sl":39,"f":"G083"},{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown SMA, IMA, small & large intestines interactive 2026, re-posted version (R. Connington)","sl":39,"f":"G108"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"225"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":60,"label":"McBurney's point: one-third from ASIS → appendicitis","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G005-91.jpg","cap":"McBurney's point: one-third of the way from right ASIS to umbilicus","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 91"},{"src":"../sl/G003-24.jpg","cap":"McBurney's point: two-thirds from umbilicus to ASIS; appendectomy incision site","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 24","cu":"../sl/G003-24c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/N275.jpg","cap":"McBurney's point lies one-third of the way from the right anterior superior iliac spine to the umbilicus.","where":"Netter Atlas 6e, Plate 275","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"G110-22-1","s":"A 19-year-old male comes to the emergency department with a 12-hour history of dull, periumbilical pain that has now shifted to the right lower quadrant, accompanied by low-grade fever, anorexia, and nausea. On physical examination, maximal tenderness to palpation is elicited at McBurney's point. This classic surface landmark corresponds to which of the following anatomical descriptions?","o":["A. 1/3 of the distance between the anterior superior iliac spine and the belly button","B. ½ of the distance between the ASIS and the belly button","C. 1/3 of the distance between the AIIS and the belly button","D. ½ of the distance between the AIIS and the belly button","E. the point of no return"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"1/3 of the distance between the anterior superior iliac spine and the belly button","a":"A","why":"<b>One-third of the distance from the anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS) to the umbilicus.</b> McBurney's point lies on the line from the right ASIS to the belly button, one-third of the way in from the ASIS, over the base of the appendix.<br>B. Halfway along the same line: too far medial.<br>C. One-third from the anterior inferior iliac spine (AIIS): wrong landmark; the AIIS lies below the ASIS.<br>D. Halfway from the AIIS: wrong landmark and wrong fraction.<br>E. The point of no return: not an anatomical landmark.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 91 (Mc Burney’s point)","Small & Large Intestines slide 90 (Appendicitis)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Montana interactive","n":"Montana (Great Falls) small & large intestines and blood supply interactive 2026 (B. Chinnery)","sl":22,"f":"G110"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"226"},{"id":"G113-19-1","s":"A 19-year-old patient presents with acute abdominal pain that initially develops around the umbilicus before migrating to the right lower quadrant. On physical examination, maximal tenderness is noted at McBurney's point. Which of the following anatomical explanations best accounts for the location of the tenderness?","o":["A. The appendix is a foregut derivative supplied by the celiac trunk and located near the left upper quadrant.","B. The appendix is a hindgut derivative supplied by the inferior mesenteric artery and located near the left anterior superior iliac spine.","C. The appendix is a midgut derivative supplied by the superior mesenteric artery, and its base is located near the junction of the lateral one-third and medial two-thirds of the line connecting the right anterior superior iliac spine and the umbilicus.","D. The appendix is supplied by the inferior mesenteric artery and is consistently located in the right hypochondriac region.","E. The appendix is supplied by the celiac trunk and is located at the midpoint between the xiphoid process and umbilicus."],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"The appendix is a midgut derivative supplied by the superior mesenteric artery, and its base is located near the junction of the lateral one-third and medial two-thirds of the line connecting the right anterior superior iliac spine and the umbilicus","a":"C","why":"<b>Midgut derivative supplied by the superior mesenteric artery; its base lies one-third of the way from the right anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS) to the umbilicus.</b> The appendicular artery is a branch of the ileocolic artery, and McBurney's point lies over the appendix base.<br>A. Foregut, celiac trunk, left upper quadrant: the appendix is midgut, in the right lower quadrant.<br>B. Hindgut, inferior mesenteric artery, left ASIS: wrong gut, artery and side.<br>D. Inferior mesenteric artery, right hypochondriac region: wrong artery; that region lies under the right ribs.<br>E. Celiac trunk, xiphoid–umbilicus midpoint: wrong artery, and that point is in the upper abdomen.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice","n":"GI #2 practice questions Fall 2026 (Lecture #1 peritoneum, esophagus, stomach & celiac trunk; Lecture #2 small & large intestines)","sl":null,"f":"G113"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"227"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":60,"label":"Crohn disease: patchy, anywhere from mouth to anus","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G110-28.jpg","cap":"Crohn disease: patchy lesions in small and large bowel; ulcerative colitis: continuous from rectum","where":"2026 Montana interactive, slide 28"}],"q":[{"id":"G110-26-1","s":"After years of having bowel pain and constipation, a patient is diagnosed with Crohn’s disease. Immunosuppressants are prescribed. This disease affects","o":["A. small intestines only","B. large intestines only","C. sigmoid colon and rectum only","D. anus only","E. from mouth to anus"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"from mouth to anus","a":"E","why":"<b>From mouth to anus.</b> Crohn disease is an autoimmune inflammatory bowel disease with patchy lesions that can appear anywhere along the gut, most often in the terminal ileum, so it typically causes right lower quadrant pain.<br>A. Small intestine only: Crohn disease often reaches the colon too.<br>B. Large intestine only: the pattern of ulcerative colitis.<br>C. Sigmoid colon and rectum only: where ulcerative colitis usually sits.<br>D. Anus only: Crohn disease can involve the anus, but never only the anus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 92 (IBD)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Montana interactive","n":"Montana (Great Falls) small & large intestines and blood supply interactive 2026 (B. Chinnery)","sl":26,"f":"G110"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"228"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":60,"label":"Diverticulitis: inflamed sigmoid outpouchings","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G005-94.jpg","cap":"Diverticulosis versus diverticulitis: colon outpouchings, inflamed in diverticulitis","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 94"},{"src":"../sl/G005-95.jpg","cap":"Diverticula in cross-section and on colonoscopy","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 95"}],"q":[{"id":"G110-30-1","s":"A 54-year-old patient presents with abdominal pain and bloating. Their history indicates a diet low in fiber and high in red meat, and accompanying constipation. A contrast X-ray indicates diverticulosis. Diverticulosis is a condition in which outpouching of the large intestines occurs. If these outpouchings become infected, the condition is called","o":["A. Irritable bowel syndrome","B. Diverticulitis","C. Volvulus","D. Megacolon","E. Crohn’s disease"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Diverticulitis","a":"B","why":"<b>Diverticulitis.</b> Diverticulosis means many outpouchings of the colon wall, most often in the sigmoid colon, favored by a low-fiber diet and constipation; it is painless while quiet. When the pouches become inflamed or infected, it is diverticulitis, with left lower quadrant pain and fever.<br>A. Irritable bowel syndrome: pain and changed bowel habits with no structural damage.<br>C. Volvulus: a loop twisted on its mesentery.<br>D. Megacolon: a hugely dilated colon with paralyzed peristalsis.<br>E. Crohn disease: patchy autoimmune inflammation, mouth to anus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 93 (Diverticulosis)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Montana interactive","n":"Montana (Great Falls) small & large intestines and blood supply interactive 2026 (B. Chinnery)","sl":30,"f":"G110"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"229"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":61,"label":"Cecum and appendix: ileocolic artery (from SMA)","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G005-61.jpg","cap":"Appendicular artery from the ileocolic artery, running in the mesoappendix","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 61","cu":"../sl/G005-61c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G005-80.jpg","cap":"Superior mesenteric branches: ileocolic, appendicular, right colic, middle colic","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 80"},{"src":"../sl/G005-62.jpg","cap":"Cecum and appendix nerves: vagus and sympathetics via superior mesenteric plexus","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 62","cu":"../sl/G005-62c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/N273.jpg","cap":"The appendicular artery comes off the ileocolic artery and runs in the mesoappendix to the appendix.","where":"Netter Atlas 6e, Plate 273","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F3114-43-1","s":"The appendix comes off the cecum. Its artery, the appendicular artery, is a branch of which artery?","o":["A. Right colic artery","B. Ileocolic artery","C. Middle colic artery","D. Left colic artery"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Ileocolic artery","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Ileocolic artery.</b> The appendix arises from the posteromedial wall of the cecum, below the ileocecal junction. The ileocolic artery, the terminal branch of the superior mesenteric artery, reaches this corner and gives the appendicular artery, which runs in the mesoappendix.<br>A. Right colic artery: supplies the ascending colon.<br>C. Middle colic artery: supplies the transverse colon.<br>D. Left colic artery: an inferior mesenteric branch to the descending colon.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 59 (Vermiform appendix)","Small & Large Intestines slide 61 (Cecum and Vermiform appendix)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"Small & large intestines interactive","sl":43,"f":"F3114"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"230"},{"id":"F3114-42-1","s":"An 13-year-old female is rushed to the emergency room complaining of severe pain located in her lower right hand quadrant. She is diagnosed with appendicitis and is sent to the OR. To prevent any extra bleeding during the procedure, the surgeon has to remember to ligate which of the following arteries?","o":["A. Right colic","B. Left colic","C. Ileocolic","D. Inferior mesenteric","E. Superior mesenteric"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Ileocolic","a":"C","why":"<b>Ileocolic artery.</b> The appendix is supplied by the appendicular artery, a branch of the ileocolic artery. The appendicular artery is not listed, so its parent, the ileocolic, is the answer.<br>A. Right colic: supplies the ascending colon.<br>B. Left colic: an IMA (inferior mesenteric artery) branch to the descending colon.<br>D. Inferior mesenteric: supplies the hindgut, far from the appendix.<br>E. Superior mesenteric: the main trunk to the whole midgut; tying it would kill the small bowel.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 61 (Cecum and Vermiform appendix)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown SMA, IMA, small & large intestines interactive 11/12/26 (R. Connington)","sl":37,"f":"G083"},{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown SMA, IMA, small & large intestines interactive 2026, re-posted version (R. Connington)","sl":38,"f":"G108"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"Small & large intestines interactive","sl":42,"f":"F3114"},{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":355,"f":"F3903"},{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":309,"f":"F1180"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":248,"f":"F1178"},{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":170,"f":"F3902"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"231"},{"id":"F3903-381-1","s":"A 19-year-old man with a ruptured appendix is sent to the emergency department for surgery. To cut off the blood supply to the appendix (if collateral circulation is discounted), a surgeon should ligate which of the following arteries?","o":["A. Ileocolic artery","B. Middle colic artery","C. Right colic artery","D. Inferior mesenteric artery","E. Common iliac artery"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Ileocolic artery","a":"A","why":"<b>Ileocolic artery.</b> The appendix's only artery, the appendicular artery, branches from the ileocolic artery. Tying the ileocolic cuts off the appendix.<br>B. Middle colic: supplies the transverse colon.<br>C. Right colic: supplies the ascending colon.<br>D. Inferior mesenteric: supplies the colon from the distal transverse colon to the upper rectum.<br>E. Common iliac: supplies the pelvis and leg, not the gut.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 61 (Cecum and Vermiform appendix)","Small & Large Intestines slide 90 (Appendicitis)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Harlem clicker","n":"Harlem small & large intestines clickers 2026","sl":35,"f":"G109"},{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":381,"f":"F3903"},{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":115,"f":"F0008"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"232"},{"id":"F3114-24-1","s":"A 58-year-old male is admitted to the hospital with complaints of abdominal pain in his lower right hand quadrant. Radiographic examination reveals diffuse ulcerations in his cecum. His physician determines that the involved area of the bowel needs to be removed. If the patient's anatomy follows the most typical patterns, which vessels and nerves will be cut during the operation?","o":["A. Superior mesenteric plexus and middle colic artery","B. Vagus nerve and the right colic artery","C. Inferior mesenteric plexus and sigmoid artery","D. Vagus nerve and the ileocolic artery","E. Pelvic splanchnic nerves and the left colic artery"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Vagus nerve and the ileocolic artery","a":"D","why":"<b>Vagus nerve and ileocolic artery.</b> The cecum is midgut. Its arteries are the anterior and posterior cecal branches of the ileocolic artery. Its parasympathetic nerves are vagal fibers in the superior mesenteric plexus.<br>A. The superior mesenteric plexus fits, but the middle colic supplies the transverse colon.<br>B. The vagus fits, but the right colic supplies the ascending colon.<br>C. Inferior mesenteric plexus and sigmoid artery: hindgut, on the left.<br>E. Pelvic splanchnic nerves and left colic artery: hindgut, on the left.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 61 (Cecum and Vermiform appendix)","Small & Large Intestines slide 62 (Cecum and Vermiform appendix)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown SMA, IMA, small & large intestines interactive 11/12/26 (R. Connington)","sl":22,"f":"G083"},{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown SMA, IMA, small & large intestines interactive 2026, re-posted version (R. Connington)","sl":23,"f":"G108"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"Small & large intestines interactive","sl":24,"f":"F3114"},{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":343,"f":"F3903"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"233"},{"id":"G083-39-2","s":"Which artery directly supplies the appendix, and which artery does it branch from?","o":["A. Appendicular artery, from the ileocolic artery","B. Appendicular artery, from the right colic artery","C. Appendicular artery, from the middle colic artery","D. Ileocolic artery, directly from the inferior mesenteric artery","E. Right colic artery, directly from the superior mesenteric artery"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Appendicular artery, from the ileocolic artery","a":"A","why":"<b>Appendicular artery, from the ileocolic artery.</b> The ileocolic is the last branch of the SMA (superior mesenteric artery). Near the cecum it gives the appendicular artery, which runs in the mesoappendix and is the only artery of the appendix.<br>B. From the right colic: the right colic feeds the ascending colon.<br>C. From the middle colic: the middle colic feeds the transverse colon.<br>D. Ileocolic straight from the IMA (inferior mesenteric artery): the ileocolic comes from the SMA.<br>E. Right colic straight from the SMA: true origin, but it feeds the ascending colon, not the appendix.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 61 (Cecum and Vermiform appendix)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown SMA, IMA, small & large intestines interactive 11/12/26 (R. Connington)","sl":39,"f":"G083"},{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown SMA, IMA, small & large intestines interactive 2026, re-posted version (R. Connington)","sl":39,"f":"G108"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"234"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":62,"label":"SMA vs IMA: split at distal transverse colon","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G005-80.jpg","cap":"Superior mesenteric branches on the right colon, inferior mesenteric on the left","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 80"},{"src":"../sl/G001-29.jpg","cap":"Hindgut: distal third of transverse colon to rectum, supplied by IMA","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 29","cu":"../sl/G001-29c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/D042-142.jpg","cap":"Table: blood supply of each gut segment, superior vs inferior mesenteric","where":"2025 TA review, slide 142"},{"src":"../tb/M5_33.jpg","cap":"Panel C: orange superior mesenteric branches end, and pink inferior mesenteric branches begin, at the distal transverse colon.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.33","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/N288.jpg","cap":"The middle colic artery (superior mesenteric) meets the left colic artery (inferior mesenteric) near the left colic flexure.","where":"Netter Atlas 6e, Plate 288","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-379-1","s":"A 21-year-old man receives a penetrating knife wound in the abdomen and is injured in both the superior mesenteric artery and the vagus nerve. Which portion of the colon would most likely be impaired by this injury?","o":["A. Ascending and transverse colons","B. Ascending and descending colons","C. Transverse and sigmoid colons","D. Descending and sigmoid colons","E. Transverse and descending colons"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Ascending and transverse colons","a":"A","why":"<b>Ascending and transverse colons.</b> The SMA (superior mesenteric artery) and the vagus nerve both serve the midgut, which ends at the proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon. So the ascending colon and most of the transverse colon are hit.<br>B. The descending colon is hindgut: IMA (inferior mesenteric artery) and pelvic splanchnic nerves.<br>C. The sigmoid colon is hindgut.<br>D. Descending and sigmoid: both hindgut, spared.<br>E. The descending colon is hindgut, spared.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 64 (Ascending colon)","Small & Large Intestines slide 67 (Transverse colon)","Small & Large Intestines slide 62 (Cecum and Vermiform appendix)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Harlem clicker","n":"Harlem small & large intestines clickers 2026","sl":33,"f":"G109"},{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":379,"f":"F3903"},{"t":"? tutor review","n":"old Exam 2: abdomen part only","sl":107,"f":"F1243"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"235"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":63,"label":"Descending colon: left colic artery (IMA)","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G005-79.jpg","cap":"Inferior mesenteric branches: left colic (ascending, descending branches), sigmoid, superior rectal","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 79","cu":"../sl/G005-79c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G005-85.jpg","cap":"Inferior mesenteric angiogram: left colic artery and its two branches labelled","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 85"},{"src":"../tb/G4_117.jpg","cap":"The left colic artery runs to the descending colon and splits into ascending and descending branches.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 4.117","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"D041-230-1","s":"On an abdominal angiogram, a branch of the inferior mesenteric artery is seen traveling upward and laterally toward the descending colon. Which artery is this?","o":["A. Left colic artery","B. Sigmoidal artery","C. Superior rectal artery","D. Middle colic artery","E. Ileocolic artery"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Left colic artery","a":"A","why":"<b>Left colic artery.</b> The first branch of the IMA (inferior mesenteric artery) runs up and to the left to the descending colon and joins the marginal artery.<br>B. Sigmoidal arteries: run down and left to the sigmoid colon.<br>C. Superior rectal artery: the IMA's continuation straight down to the rectum.<br>D. Middle colic: an SMA (superior mesenteric artery) branch to the transverse colon.<br>E. Ileocolic: an SMA branch to the cecum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Radiology Abd III slide 43 (Left Colic Artery)","Small & Large Intestines slide 79 (Inferior mesenteric artery)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown SMA, IMA, small & large intestines interactive 11/12/26 (R. Connington)","sl":24,"f":"G083"},{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown SMA, IMA, small & large intestines interactive 2026, re-posted version (R. Connington)","sl":24,"f":"G108"},{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":230,"f":"D041"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"236"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":63,"label":"IMA arises at L3: aneurysm at L3–L4","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G005-79.jpg","cap":"Inferior mesenteric artery arises from the aorta at about L3","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 79","cu":"../sl/G005-79c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/G4_167.jpg","cap":"The inferior mesenteric artery arises from the aorta at L3, so an L3 to L4 aneurysm involves its origin.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 4.167","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"G113-24-1","s":"A 70-year-old patient undergoes angiography for suspected ischemia of the descending and sigmoid colon. The radiologist identifies an artery arising from the abdominal aorta at approximately the level of L3. Its branches include the left colic, sigmoid, and superior rectal arteries. Which of the following vessels is being visualized?","o":["A. Superior mesenteric artery.","B. Inferior mesenteric artery.","C. Celiac trunk.","D. Internal iliac artery.","E. Common hepatic artery."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Inferior mesenteric artery","a":"B","why":"<b>Inferior mesenteric artery (IMA).</b> It leaves the front of the aorta at about the third lumbar vertebra (L3) and supplies the hindgut through the left colic, sigmoid and superior rectal arteries.<br>A. Superior mesenteric artery: arises at the first lumbar vertebra; its colic branches are the ileocolic, right colic and middle colic.<br>C. Celiac trunk: arises at the twelfth thoracic vertebra and supplies the foregut.<br>D. Internal iliac artery: a pelvic branch of the common iliac artery; it gives the middle rectal artery, not the superior rectal.<br>E. Common hepatic artery: a celiac branch to the liver, stomach and duodenum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice","n":"GI #2 practice questions Fall 2026 (Lecture #1 peritoneum, esophagus, stomach & celiac trunk; Lecture #2 small & large intestines)","sl":null,"f":"G113"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"237"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":63,"label":"Paraduodenal hernia: bowel slips into the paraduodenal fossa","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G110-18.jpg","cap":"Paraduodenal fossa beside the duodenojejunal junction, inferior mesenteric vessels in its fold","where":"2026 Montana interactive, slide 18"}],"q":[{"id":"G110-16-1","s":"Paraduodenal hernias are situations when the duodenum or other intestines herniate through","o":["A. the greater omentum","B. the lesser omentum","C. the paraduodenal fossa","D. the mesentery root"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"the paraduodenal fossa","a":"C","why":"<b>The paraduodenal fossa.</b> A peritoneal fold to the left of the fourth part of the duodenum can leave a pocket behind it. A loop of small bowel slips into this pocket and may strangulate. The inferior mesenteric vessels run in the fold.<br>A. Greater omentum: the fatty apron hanging from the greater curvature of the stomach.<br>B. Lesser omentum: joins the lesser curvature of the stomach to the liver.<br>D. Mesentery root: the attachment line of the mesentery on the posterior wall, not a pocket.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 89 (Paraduodenal hernias)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Montana interactive","n":"Montana (Great Falls) small & large intestines and blood supply interactive 2026 (B. Chinnery)","sl":16,"f":"G110"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"238"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":64,"label":"Marginal artery of Drummond: main SMA–IMA link","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G005-81.jpg","cap":"Marginal artery of Drummond along the colon, joining superior and inferior mesenteric","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 81","cu":"../sl/G005-81c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G005-80.jpg","cap":"Marginal artery labelled along the whole colon","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 80"},{"src":"../tb/N288.jpg","cap":"The marginal artery runs along the inner edge of the whole colon, linking superior and inferior mesenteric branches.","where":"Netter Atlas 6e, Plate 288","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"G083-48-1","s":"The marginal artery (of Drummond) is a continuous arcade formed by anastomosing branches of which two arteries?","o":["A. Celiac trunk and superior mesenteric artery","B. Superior and inferior mesenteric arteries","C. Inferior mesenteric artery and internal iliac artery","D. Superior mesenteric artery and internal iliac artery","E. Celiac trunk and inferior mesenteric artery"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Superior and inferior mesenteric arteries","a":"B","why":"<b>Superior and inferior mesenteric arteries.</b> The marginal artery is a chain of joined colic branches along the colon: ileocolic, right colic and middle colic from the SMA (superior mesenteric artery), left colic and sigmoid from the IMA (inferior mesenteric artery). It is the main link between the two.<br>A. Celiac trunk and SMA: joined by the pancreaticoduodenal arcade at the duodenum.<br>C. IMA and internal iliac: joined by the superior and middle rectal arteries on the rectum.<br>D. SMA and internal iliac: no direct link.<br>E. Celiac trunk and IMA: no direct link; the SMA lies between them.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 81 (Marginal Artery)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown SMA, IMA, small & large intestines interactive 11/12/26 (R. Connington)","sl":48,"f":"G083"},{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown SMA, IMA, small & large intestines interactive 2026, re-posted version (R. Connington)","sl":48,"f":"G108"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"239"},{"id":"G113-22-1","s":"A 63-year-old patient presents with chronic abdominal pain due to progressive narrowing of the arteries supplying the intestines. Imaging reveals that blood is being redirected through collateral vessels between branches of the superior and inferior mesenteric arteries. Which of the following arterial connections is most important for maintaining collateral circulation between the midgut and hindgut?","o":["A. Gastroduodenal artery and right gastric artery.","B. Marginal artery of Drummond connecting branches of the superior and inferior mesenteric arteries.","C. Splenic artery and left gastric artery.","D. Inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery and superior pancreaticoduodenal artery.","E. Middle colic artery and splenic artery."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Marginal artery of Drummond connecting branches of the superior and inferior mesenteric arteries","a":"B","why":"<b>Marginal artery of Drummond, linking branches of the superior and inferior mesenteric arteries.</b> This almost-always-present chain runs along the inner (mesenteric) border of the whole colon, from the ileocolic to the sigmoid arteries.<br>A. Gastroduodenal and right gastric arteries: celiac branches around the stomach and duodenum.<br>C. Splenic and left gastric arteries: both celiac branches; no mesenteric link.<br>D. Inferior and superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries: they link the celiac trunk with the superior mesenteric artery (foregut to midgut), not the hindgut.<br>E. Middle colic and splenic arteries: they do not join; the middle colic reaches the left colic through the marginal artery.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice","n":"GI #2 practice questions Fall 2026 (Lecture #1 peritoneum, esophagus, stomach & celiac trunk; Lecture #2 small & large intestines)","sl":null,"f":"G113"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"240"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":64,"label":"Marginal artery built from colic branches only","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G005-80.jpg","cap":"Marginal artery fed by ileocolic, right, middle, left colic and sigmoid arteries","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 80"},{"src":"../sl/F3114-17.jpg","cap":"Colon anastomoses: mesenteric colic branches versus internal iliac rectal branches","where":"2025 Middletown clicker, slide 17"},{"src":"../tb/N288.jpg","cap":"The marginal artery is built from the ileocolic, right, middle and left colic and sigmoid branches.","where":"Netter Atlas 6e, Plate 288","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F3114-54-1","s":"The marginal artery is built from branches of the superior and inferior mesenteric arteries. Which artery does NOT feed it?","o":["A. Middle colic artery","B. Middle rectal artery","C. Ileocolic artery","D. Left colic artery","E. Sigmoid arteries"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Middle rectal artery","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Middle rectal artery.</b> It comes from the internal iliac artery and joins the rectal network, not the marginal artery. The marginal artery runs along the colon's mesenteric edge from the ileocolic to the sigmoid arteries.<br>A. Middle colic artery: a superior mesenteric branch that feeds it at the transverse colon.<br>C. Ileocolic artery: its starting point, from the superior mesenteric.<br>D. Left colic artery: an inferior mesenteric branch that feeds it at the descending colon.<br>E. Sigmoid arteries: inferior mesenteric branches that form its last stretch.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 81 (Marginal Artery)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"Small & large intestines interactive","sl":54,"f":"F3114"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"241"},{"id":"F3114-53-1","s":"A 27-year-old male is rushed to the emergency room complaining of pain and discomfort located on the left side of his abdomen. An arteriogram shows a blockage of the IMA. He is rushed to the OR before the descending colon becomes necrotic. The marginal artery has been able to keep the descending artery supplied with minimum blood supply. Which of the following arteries is not a tributary to the marginal artery?","o":["A. Right colic","B. Left colic","C. Ileocolic","D. Middle colic","E. Middle rectal"],"g":["E"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Middle colic","a":"D","why":"<b>Middle rectal.</b> The marginal artery is built only from colic branches: the ileocolic, right colic and middle colic from the SMA (superior mesenteric artery), and the left colic and sigmoid arteries from the IMA (inferior mesenteric artery). The middle rectal comes from the internal iliac artery, supplies the middle rectum and never joins the chain.<br>A. Right colic: feeds it along the ascending colon.<br>B. Left colic: feeds it along the descending colon.<br>C. Ileocolic: its starting point.<br>D. Middle colic: a major feeder; it joins the left colic near the splenic flexure.","ex":"","fl":["The middle colic is a main feeder of the marginal artery; the middle rectal (from the internal iliac artery) is the one that does not feed it, so the answer is E."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 81 (Marginal Artery)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown SMA, IMA, small & large intestines interactive 11/12/26 (R. Connington)","sl":46,"f":"G083"},{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown SMA, IMA, small & large intestines interactive 2026, re-posted version (R. Connington)","sl":47,"f":"G108"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"Small & large intestines interactive","sl":53,"f":"F3114"},{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":363,"f":"F3903"},{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":318,"f":"F1180"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":257,"f":"F1178"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"242"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":64,"label":"Marginal artery course: inner colon margin, ileocolic to sigmoid","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G083-49.jpg","cap":"Marginal artery: arcade on the colon's inner margin, ileocolic around to sigmoid","where":"2026 Middletown interactive, slide 49"},{"src":"../sl/G005-81.jpg","cap":"Marginal artery in the mesentery close to the bowel, linking SMA and IMA","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 81","cu":"../sl/G005-81c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"G083-48-2","s":"What is the course of the marginal artery?","o":["A. Along the inner (mesenteric) margin of the colon, from the ileocolic artery around to the sigmoid arteries","B. A short loop near the root of the mesentery linking only the middle colic and left colic arteries","C. Along the mesenteric border of the small intestine, from the jejunal arteries to the ileocolic artery","D. Along the rectum, from the superior rectal artery to the inferior rectal artery","E. Around the head of the pancreas, from the superior to the inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Along the inner (mesenteric) margin of the colon, from the ileocolic artery around to the sigmoid arteries","a":"A","why":"<b>Along the inner (mesenteric) margin of the colon, from the ileocolic artery around to the sigmoid arteries.</b> It runs in the mesentery close to the bowel, joins every colic artery into one continuous chain, and is almost always present.<br>B. Short loop near the mesenteric root, middle colic to left colic only: the arc of Riolan, which is often absent.<br>C. Small-intestine border, jejunal to ileocolic: the jejunal and ileal arcades.<br>D. Rectum, superior to inferior rectal: the rectal anastomoses, which do not join it.<br>E. Head of the pancreas: the pancreaticoduodenal arcade.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 81 (Marginal Artery)","Small & Large Intestines slide 82 (Arc of Riolan)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown SMA, IMA, small & large intestines interactive 11/12/26 (R. Connington)","sl":48,"f":"G083"},{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown SMA, IMA, small & large intestines interactive 2026, re-posted version (R. Connington)","sl":48,"f":"G108"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"243"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":64,"label":"Blocked IMA or SMA: collateral from the other","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G005-83.jpg","cap":"Arc of Riolan: superior–inferior mesenteric bypass in occlusion or stenosis","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 83","cu":"../sl/G005-83c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G005-81.jpg","cap":"Marginal artery links superior and inferior mesenteric territories","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 81","cu":"../sl/G005-81c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/N288.jpg","cap":"Blocked inferior mesenteric artery: middle colic blood reaches the left colic artery through the marginal artery.","where":"Netter Atlas 6e, Plate 288","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-383-1","s":"A 70-year-old man is admitted to the emergency department with severe diarrhea. An arteriogram reveals 90% blockage at the origin of the inferior mesenteric artery from the aorta. Which of the following arteries would most likely provide collateral supply to the descending colon?","o":["A. Left gastroepiploic artery","B. Middle colic artery","C. Sigmoid artery","D. Splenic artery","E. Superior rectal artery"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Middle colic artery","a":"B","why":"<b>Middle colic artery.</b> With the IMA (inferior mesenteric artery) blocked, SMA (superior mesenteric artery) blood flows from the middle colic through the marginal artery and arc of Riolan into the left colic territory, reaching the descending colon.<br>A. Left gastroepiploic: supplies the greater curvature of the stomach.<br>C. Sigmoid artery: an IMA branch, cut off by the same block.<br>D. Splenic artery: supplies the spleen, pancreas and stomach.<br>E. Superior rectal artery: also an IMA branch.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 81 (Marginal Artery)","Small & Large Intestines slide 67 (Transverse colon)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Harlem clicker","n":"Harlem small & large intestines clickers 2026","sl":28,"f":"G109"},{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":383,"f":"F3903"},{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":117,"f":"F0008"},{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":311,"f":"F1180"},{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":174,"f":"F3902"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":250,"f":"F1178"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"244"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":65,"label":"Jejunal lymph → superior mesenteric nodes → cisterna chyli","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G005-52.jpg","cap":"Small-bowel lymph: mesenteric nodes, superior mesenteric nodes, intestinal trunk, cisterna chyli","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 52","cu":"../sl/G005-52c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G005-53.jpg","cap":"Superior mesenteric nodes, intestinal trunk, cisterna chyli and thoracic duct","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 53"},{"src":"../tb/N295.jpg","cap":"Jejunal lymph runs through superior mesenteric nodes into the intestinal trunk and the cisterna chyli.","where":"Netter Atlas 6e, Plate 295","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"G113-25-1","s":"A patient is diagnosed with metastatic adenocarcinoma of the ileum. During surgery, the surgeon identifies enlarged lymph nodes along the ileal arterial branches and near the superior mesenteric artery. The surgeon explains that lymph from these nodes ultimately enters the central lymphatic circulation. Which of the following correctly describes the pathway of lymphatic drainage?","o":["A. Ileal lymphatics → inferior mesenteric nodes → cisterna chyli → right lymphatic duct.","B. Ileal lymphatics → mesenteric nodes → superior mesenteric nodes → intestinal lymph trunks → cisterna chyli → thoracic duct.","C. Ileal lymphatics → celiac nodes → thoracic duct → cisterna chyli.","D. Ileal lymphatics → para-aortic nodes → right lymphatic duct → superior vena cava.","E. Ileal lymphatics → superficial inguinal nodes → cisterna chyli → thoracic duct."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Ileal lymphatics → mesenteric nodes → superior mesenteric nodes → intestinal lymph trunks → cisterna chyli → thoracic duct","a":"B","why":"<b>Ileal lymphatics → mesenteric nodes → superior mesenteric nodes → intestinal lymph trunks → cisterna chyli → thoracic duct.</b> Gut lymph follows the arteries back to the aorta, then enters the cisterna chyli at the start of the thoracic duct.<br>A. Inferior mesenteric nodes, right lymphatic duct: those nodes drain the hindgut, and the right lymphatic duct takes no gut lymph.<br>C. Celiac nodes, thoracic duct before cisterna chyli: wrong nodes, reversed order.<br>D. Para-aortic nodes, right lymphatic duct: those nodes drain the kidneys, gonads and body wall.<br>E. Superficial inguinal nodes: they drain the leg, perineum and lower anal canal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice","n":"GI #2 practice questions Fall 2026 (Lecture #1 peritoneum, esophagus, stomach & celiac trunk; Lecture #2 small & large intestines)","sl":null,"f":"G113"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"245"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":66,"label":"Hindgut parasympathetics: pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2–S4)","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G005-78.jpg","cap":"Pelvic splanchnic nerves from sacral cord versus thoracic and lumbar splanchnics","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 78"},{"src":"../sl/G008-13.jpg","cap":"Splanchnic nerve types: only pelvic splanchnics are parasympathetic","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 13","cu":"../sl/G008-13c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/G1_48.jpg","cap":"Sacral outflow from S2 to S4 via pelvic splanchnic nerves takes over from the vagus at the distal transverse colon.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 1.48","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/N302.jpg","cap":"Pelvic splanchnic nerves feed the inferior hypogastric plexus, which sends fibers up to the sigmoid and descending colon.","where":"Netter Atlas 6e, Plate 302","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"D041-236-1","s":"Parasympathetic innervation to the hindgut (distal 1/3 of transverse colon through rectum) is supplied by which nerves?","o":["A. Vagus nerves","B. Pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2–S4)","C. Lumbar splanchnic nerves","D. Glossopharyngeal nerve"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2–S4)","a":"B","why":"<b>Pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2–S4, sacral segments two to four).</b> They carry the parasympathetic supply of the hindgut, from the distal one-third of the transverse colon to the rectum.<br>A. Vagus nerves: parasympathetic only to the foregut and midgut.<br>C. Lumbar splanchnic nerves: sympathetic to the hindgut.<br>D. Glossopharyngeal nerve: head and neck only (for example, the parotid gland).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 77 (Descending colon and sigmoid colon)","ANS slide 13 (Splanchnic nerve: nerve that conveys innervation to and from the viscera of the )"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown SMA, IMA, small & large intestines interactive 11/12/26 (R. Connington)","sl":24,"f":"G083"},{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown SMA, IMA, small & large intestines interactive 2026, re-posted version (R. Connington)","sl":24,"f":"G108"},{"t":"2026 Middletown TA weekly","n":"Anatomy TA session GI week 2 part 2 11/12/26","sl":48,"f":"D069"},{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":236,"f":"D041"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"246"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":66,"label":"Colon nerves and arteries, segment by segment","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G005-80.jpg","cap":"Colon arteries: ileocolic, right, middle, left colic, sigmoid, marginal","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 80"},{"src":"../sl/G005-68.jpg","cap":"Transverse colon nerves: superior mesenteric vs inferior mesenteric plexus","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 68","cu":"../sl/G005-68c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G005-77.jpg","cap":"Descending and sigmoid colon: pelvic splanchnic parasympathetics, lumbar splanchnic sympathetics","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 77"},{"src":"../tb/N302.jpg","cap":"Each colic artery carries its nerve plexus: superior mesenteric ganglion for the right colon, inferior mesenteric for the left.","where":"Netter Atlas 6e, Plate 302","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/G1_48.jpg","cap":"The red mark shows where vagal supply ends and pelvic splanchnic (S2 to S4) supply begins on the transverse colon.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 1.48","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F3114-25-1","s":"Which nerves carry parasympathetic fibers to the descending and sigmoid colon?","o":["A. Vagus nerves","B. Lumbar splanchnic nerves","C. Pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2–S4)","D. Sacral splanchnic nerves","E. Greater splanchnic nerves"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2–S4)","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Pelvic splanchnic nerves (sacral segments 2–4).</b> They carry preganglionic parasympathetic fibers to the hindgut, including the descending and sigmoid colon and the rectum, through the inferior hypogastric plexus.<br>A. Vagus nerves: reach only the foregut and midgut, not the descending or sigmoid colon.<br>B. Lumbar splanchnic nerves: the sympathetic supply of the hindgut.<br>D. Sacral splanchnic nerves: sympathetic, from the sacral part of the sympathetic trunk.<br>E. Greater splanchnic nerves: sympathetic, from the fifth to ninth thoracic segments, to the foregut.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 54 (Innervation)","Small & Large Intestines slide 77 (Descending colon and sigmoid colon)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"Small & large intestines interactive","sl":25,"f":"F3114"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"247"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":66,"label":"Hindgut sympathetics synapse in the inferior mesenteric ganglion","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G083-25.jpg","cap":"Colon sympathetics: superior mesenteric ganglia, then inferior mesenteric ganglia past two-thirds","where":"2026 Middletown interactive, slide 25"},{"src":"../sl/G008-10.jpg","cap":"Postganglionic cell bodies: paravertebral trunk ganglia versus prevertebral (celiac, mesenteric) ganglia","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 10","cu":"../sl/G008-10c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"G083-24-1","s":"Postganglionic sympathetic neurons that supply the descending colon have their cell bodies in which ganglion?","o":["A. Celiac ganglion","B. Superior mesenteric ganglion","C. Inferior mesenteric ganglion","D. Aorticorenal ganglion","E. Lumbar paravertebral (sympathetic trunk) ganglia"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Inferior mesenteric ganglion","a":"C","why":"<b>Inferior mesenteric ganglion.</b> Sympathetic fibers for the hindgut leave the spinal cord at L1–L2 (first and second lumbar segments) in the lumbar splanchnic nerves. They pass through the sympathetic trunk without synapsing, synapse in the inferior mesenteric ganglion, and the postganglionic fibers follow the left colic artery to the descending colon.<br>A. Celiac ganglion: foregut, such as the stomach and liver.<br>B. Superior mesenteric ganglion: midgut, up to the proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon.<br>D. Aorticorenal ganglion: the kidney.<br>E. Lumbar paravertebral ganglia: splanchnic fibers pass through them without synapsing.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 68 (Transverse colon)","Small & Large Intestines slide 77 (Descending colon and sigmoid colon)","ANS slide 10 (Cell bodies of postganglionic fibers will be located in 2 locations)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown SMA, IMA, small & large intestines interactive 11/12/26 (R. Connington)","sl":24,"f":"G083"},{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown SMA, IMA, small & large intestines interactive 2026, re-posted version (R. Connington)","sl":24,"f":"G108"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"248"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":66,"label":"Cecum and appendix nerves: vagus + lower thoracic, superior mesenteric plexus","slides":[{"src":"../sl/D069-46.jpg","cap":"Bowel nerves by segment: cecum and appendix get vagus and lower thoracic sympathetics","where":"2026 Middletown TA weekly, slide 46","cu":"../sl/D069-46c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G005-62.jpg","cap":"Cecum and appendix: superior mesenteric plexus carries vagal and lower thoracic fibers","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 62","cu":"../sl/G005-62c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"G113-26-1","s":"A 45-year-old patient undergoes surgery involving the terminal ileum and proximal colon. During the procedure, the surgeon stimulates autonomic nerve fibers supplying this region. The stimulation produces increased intestinal motility and secretion. Which of the following correctly describes the autonomic innervation of this region?","o":["A. Parasympathetic innervation is supplied by the pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2–S4), while sympathetic innervation is supplied by lumbar splanchnic nerves.","B. Both sympathetic and parasympathetic innervation are supplied exclusively by the vagus nerve.","C. Parasympathetic innervation is supplied by the phrenic nerve, while sympathetic innervation is supplied by the vagus nerve.","D. Parasympathetic innervation is supplied by the greater splanchnic nerves, while sympathetic innervation is supplied by the pelvic splanchnic nerves.","E. Parasympathetic innervation is supplied by the vagus nerve, while sympathetic innervation is supplied by fibers traveling through the superior mesenteric plexus."],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Parasympathetic innervation is supplied by the vagus nerve, while sympathetic innervation is supplied by fibers traveling through the superior mesenteric plexus","a":"E","why":"<b>Parasympathetic from the vagus nerve; sympathetic fibers through the superior mesenteric plexus.</b> The terminal ileum and proximal colon are midgut, so their nerves ride the superior mesenteric artery; vagal (parasympathetic) activity raises motility and secretion.<br>A. Pelvic splanchnic and lumbar splanchnic nerves: the hindgut supply, from the distal transverse colon down.<br>B. Vagus for both: the vagus carries no sympathetic fibers.<br>C. Phrenic and vagus: the phrenic nerve supplies the diaphragm, and the vagus is parasympathetic.<br>D. Greater splanchnic parasympathetic, pelvic splanchnic sympathetic: reversed; greater splanchnics are sympathetic, pelvic splanchnics parasympathetic.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 faculty practice","n":"GI #2 practice questions Fall 2026 (Lecture #1 peritoneum, esophagus, stomach & celiac trunk; Lecture #2 small & large intestines)","sl":null,"f":"G113"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"249"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":67,"label":"Quadrate lobe: between gallbladder and round ligament","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G006-18.jpg","cap":"Quadrate lobe between gallbladder fossa and round ligament, below porta hepatis","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 18"},{"src":"../sl/G006-15.jpg","cap":"Visceral fissures: round ligament, ligamentum venosum, gallbladder fossa, IVC groove","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 15","cu":"../sl/G006-15c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"F0008-145-1","s":"A 65-year-old man, with a history of cirrhosis of the liver, presents with pain in his upper right hand quadrant. After physical examination, the doctor suspects a tumor in the liver and sends him to a radiologist for a CT scan. The scan confirms a tumor in the quadrate lobe of the liver. What structures border the diseased lobe?","o":["A. Fossa of gall bladder and ligamentum teres hepatis","B. Fossa of gall bladder and ligamentum venosum","C. Ligamentum teres hepatis and groove for inferior vena cava","D. Ligamentum venosum and groove for inferior vena cava","E. Ligamentum venosum and ligamentum teres hepatis"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Fossa of gall bladder and ligamentum teres hepatis","a":"A","why":"<b>Gallbladder fossa and ligamentum teres hepatis.</b> On the visceral surface, the quadrate lobe is the square block between the gallbladder fossa (on its right) and the fissure for the round ligament (on its left). The porta hepatis lies just behind it.<br>B. Ligamentum venosum: borders the caudate lobe, not the quadrate lobe.<br>C. Groove for the IVC (inferior vena cava): borders the caudate lobe.<br>D. Ligamentum venosum and IVC groove: these two frame the caudate lobe.<br>E. Ligamentum venosum and ligamentum teres: together they mark off the left lobe.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 15 (Visceral surface of liver)","Radiology Abd II slide 17 (Quadrate lobe)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown liver, gallbladder, spleen & portal vein interactive 2026 (J. LaBarge)","sl":30,"f":"G111"},{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":145,"f":"F0008"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"250"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":68,"label":"Gallbladder nerves arrive through the celiac plexus","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G111-23.jpg","cap":"Gallbladder: celiac plexus carries greater splanchnic and vagal fibers; cystic artery","where":"2026 Middletown interactive, slide 23","cu":"../sl/G111-23c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G006-45.jpg","cap":"Gallbladder nerves: celiac plexus, anterior vagal trunk, right phrenic (sensory)","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 45"}],"q":[{"id":"G111-22-1","s":"A 42-year-old obese woman comes to the clinic with episodes of severe right hypochondrial pain, usually associated with eating a fatty meal. A history of gallstones suggests that she is experiencing cholecystitis (gallbladder inflammation). Which of the following nerves innervate the gallbladder","o":["A. Greater splanchnic only","B. Intercostal","C. Phrenic","D. Pelvic splanchnic","E. Celiac plexus"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Celiac plexus","a":"E","why":"<b>Celiac plexus.</b> Both autonomic supplies reach the gallbladder through the celiac plexus: sympathetic fibers from the greater splanchnic nerves (fifth to ninth thoracic segments, T5–T9) and parasympathetic fibers from the vagus.<br>A. Greater splanchnic only: these sympathetic fibers do reach it, but 'only' leaves out the vagal fibers.<br>B. Intercostal: supply the chest and abdominal wall, not the viscera.<br>C. Phrenic: the right phrenic adds only sensory fibers, the cause of right shoulder referred pain.<br>D. Pelvic splanchnic: sacral (S2–S4) parasympathetic fibers for the hindgut and pelvic organs.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 25 (Nerve supply)","Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 37 (FIGURE 5.72. Nerves and veins of the liver and biliary system. Nerves are promin)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown liver, gallbladder, spleen & portal vein interactive 2026 (J. LaBarge)","sl":22,"f":"G111"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"251"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":69,"label":"Calot's triangle: borders and contents","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G006-46.jpg","cap":"Calot's triangle: cystic duct, common hepatic duct, liver; contains cystic artery","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 46","cu":"../sl/G006-46c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G006-42.jpg","cap":"Cystic artery origin and its variations around the cystohepatic triangle","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 42"},{"src":"../sl/D071-13.jpg","cap":"Calot's triangle: common hepatic duct medial, cystic duct below and lateral, liver above","where":"2026 Middletown TA weekly, slide 13"},{"src":"../tb/G4_114.jpg","cap":"Find the triangle of cystic duct, common hepatic duct and liver, with the cystic artery from the right hepatic artery.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 4.114","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/N280.jpg","cap":"Find the labeled cystohepatic (Calot's) triangle; the portal vein lies behind the ducts, outside the triangle.","where":"Netter Atlas 6e, Plate 280","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"G111-26-1","s":"In which triangle is the cystic artery found?","o":["A. Inguinal triangle (of Hesselbach)","B. Cystohepatic triangle (of Calot)","C. Lumbar triangle (of Petit)","D. Femoral triangle"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Cystohepatic triangle (of Calot)","a":"B","why":"<b>Cystohepatic triangle (of Calot).</b> The cystic artery, usually a branch of the right hepatic artery, runs inside this small triangle at the gallbladder neck, so surgeons find and clip it here during a cholecystectomy.<br>A. Inguinal triangle (of Hesselbach): on the inner surface of the anterior abdominal wall; site of direct inguinal hernias.<br>C. Lumbar triangle (of Petit): on the lower back, just above the iliac crest.<br>D. Femoral triangle: in the upper thigh; holds the femoral nerve, artery and vein.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 46 (Calot's triangle)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown liver, gallbladder, spleen & portal vein interactive 2026 (J. LaBarge)","sl":26,"f":"G111"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"252"},{"id":"G111-26-2","s":"What are the borders of the cystohepatic triangle (of Calot)?","o":["A. Cystic duct, common bile duct, visceral surface of the liver","B. Cystic duct, common hepatic duct, visceral surface of the right lobe of the liver","C. Cystic artery, common hepatic duct, visceral surface of the liver","D. Cystic duct, common hepatic duct, first part of the duodenum","E. Common hepatic duct, common bile duct, hepatic portal vein"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Cystic duct, common hepatic duct, visceral surface of the right lobe of the liver","a":"B","why":"<b>Cystic duct, common hepatic duct, visceral surface of the right lobe of the liver.</b> The cystic duct is the lower border, the common hepatic duct the medial border and the liver the upper border.<br>A. Common bile duct: begins below the cystic duct junction, outside the triangle.<br>C. Cystic artery: a content running inside, not a border.<br>D. First part of the duodenum: lies below the triangle.<br>E. Common bile duct and portal vein: neither is a border; the portal vein lies deeper.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 46 (Calot's triangle)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown liver, gallbladder, spleen & portal vein interactive 2026 (J. LaBarge)","sl":26,"f":"G111"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"253"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":69,"label":"Cholecystectomy: clip cystic artery from right hepatic","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G006-42.jpg","cap":"Cystic artery usually arises from right hepatic artery inside Calot's triangle","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 42"},{"src":"../sl/G006-46.jpg","cap":"Calot's triangle with cystic artery branching off the right hepatic artery","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 46","cu":"../sl/G006-46c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/G4_97.jpg","cap":"See the cystic artery branching from the right hepatic artery; the laparoscopic photo shows the cystic duct and cystic artery.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 4.97","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-334-1","s":"During a laparoscopic cholecystectomy on a 61-year-old male, which of the following arteries must be clamped to remove the gallbladder safely?","o":["A. Common hepatic","B. Proper hepatic","C. Right hepatic","D. Left hepatic","E. Gastroduodenal"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Right hepatic","a":"C","why":"<b>Right hepatic artery</b> is the best choice here. The vessel actually clipped is the <b>cystic artery</b>, which usually branches off the right hepatic artery inside Calot's (cystohepatic) triangle. The right hepatic artery itself must be found and protected, because it feeds the right liver.<br>A. Common hepatic: a celiac trunk branch, far from the gallbladder.<br>B. Proper hepatic: splits into right and left hepatic before the cystic artery arises.<br>D. Left hepatic: goes to the left liver, away from the gallbladder.<br>E. Gastroduodenal: runs behind the first part of the duodenum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 36 (Blood supply to the Bile duct)","Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 46 (Calot's triangle)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Harlem clicker","n":"Harlem peritoneum, esophagus, stomach & celiac trunk clickers 2026","sl":26,"f":"G074"},{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":334,"f":"F3903"},{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":104,"f":"F0008"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"254"},{"id":"F1180-338-1","s":"Which of the following is incorrect pertaining to the gall bladder?","o":["A) Some venous drainage is directly into the visceral surface of the liver.","B) Cholecystitis can result from an impacted gallstone.","C) A gallstone lodged in the cystic duct causes intense spasmodic pain.","D) During cholecystectomy surgeons typically ligate the left hepatic artery.","E) The infundibulum of the gall bladder is a pouch that appears between the neck and the cystic duct in diseased states."],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"During cholecystectomy surgeons typically ligate the left hepatic artery","a":"D","why":"<b>D is false.</b> In a cholecystectomy (gallbladder removal) surgeons tie off the <b>cystic artery</b>, which usually comes from the <b>right</b> hepatic artery. The left hepatic artery goes to the left liver and is left alone.<br>A. True: veins from the fundus and body pass straight into the liver.<br>B. True: a stuck stone blocks outflow and inflames the gallbladder.<br>C. True: a cystic duct stone causes biliary colic, an intense cramping pain.<br>E. True: this pouch (Hartmann's pouch) is a bulge at the neck in a diseased gallbladder.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 43 (The venous drainage from the neck of the gallbladder and cystic duct flows via t)","Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 36 (Blood supply to the Bile duct)","Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 46 (Calot's triangle)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":141,"f":"F0008"},{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":338,"f":"F1180"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":274,"f":"F1178"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"255"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":70,"label":"Portal vein: most posterior in hepatoduodenal ligament","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G006-16.jpg","cap":"Portal triad in hepatoduodenal ligament: portal vein behind artery and bile duct","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 16"},{"src":"../sl/G006-48.jpg","cap":"Portal vein formation behind the neck of the pancreas","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 48","cu":"../sl/G006-48c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/G4_54.jpg","cap":"In the hepatoduodenal ligament, the portal vein lies behind the bile duct and hepatic artery proper, by the omental foramen.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 4.54","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F0008-137-1","s":"Which of the following is incorrect pertaining to the portal vein?","o":["A) It is typically formed by the superior mesenteric and splenic veins posterior to the neck of the pancreas.","B) It carries more of the total blood volume reaching the liver than any other vessel.","C) It is typically the most anterior of the structures within the hepatoduodenal ligament.","D) It divides into right and left branches at the porta hepatis.","E) It may spread cancer to the liver."],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"It is typically the most anterior of the structures within the hepatoduodenal ligament (it is the most posterior)","a":"C","why":"<b>C is false.</b> The portal vein is the most <b>posterior</b> structure in the hepatoduodenal ligament. The bile duct is in front on the right, and the hepatic artery proper is in front on the left.<br>A. True: superior mesenteric plus splenic veins, behind the pancreatic neck.<br>B. True: it brings about seventy percent of the liver's blood; the hepatic artery brings about thirty.<br>D. True: it splits into right and left branches at the porta hepatis.<br>E. True: gut cancers spread to the liver through it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 48 (It is formed anterior to the IVC and posterior to the neck of the pancreas (clos)","Pancreas & Spleen slide 9 (Pancreas)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":137,"f":"F0008"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"256"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":71,"label":"Venogram: inferior mesenteric vein joins splenic vein","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G006-48.jpg","cap":"Inferior mesenteric vein rising to join the splenic vein left of the confluence","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 48","cu":"../sl/G006-48c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G006-56.jpg","cap":"MR angiogram: splenic vein, superior and inferior mesenteric veins labelled","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 56"},{"src":"../sl/D071-35.jpg","cap":"Inferior mesenteric vein ends in the splenic vein, superior mesenteric vein, or their junction","where":"2026 Middletown TA weekly, slide 35"},{"src":"../tb/G4_120.jpg","cap":"Find the inferior mesenteric vein rising to join the splenic vein, left of where the portal vein forms.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 4.120","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F0008-128-1","s":"In the portal venogram shown below, the structure labeled “6” is:","o":["A. Hepatic veins","B. Inferior mesenteric vein","C. Right gastric vein","D. Right gastroepiploic vein","E. Splenic vein"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Inferior mesenteric vein","a":"B","why":"<b>Inferior mesenteric vein (IMV).</b> In this venogram, 3 is the portal vein trunk and 4 is the splenic vein running to the patient's left. Label 5 is the SMV (superior mesenteric vein) coming up from below. Label 6 is a thinner vein rising from below into the splenic vein, as the IMV usually does.<br>A. Hepatic veins: inside the liver, draining up into the IVC (inferior vena cava).<br>C. Right gastric vein: a small vein entering the portal vein directly.<br>D. Right gastroepiploic vein: joins the SMV.<br>E. Splenic vein: that is label 4.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/F0008-128.jpg","in":"Portal venogram (catheter from the right) with numbered veins 1-6: 1-2 right/left portal branches, 3 portal vein trunk, 4 splenic vein running to the left, 5 superior mesenteric vein descending, 6 a thin vein joining from below near the splenic-SMV confluence. Asked: label 6.","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 51 (Tributaries of the splenic vein)","Radiology Abd III slide 54 (Portal Vein)","Radiology Abd III slide 57 (Splenic Vein)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":128,"f":"F0008"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"257"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":71,"label":"Left and right gastric veins both drain into the portal vein","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G006-49.jpg","cap":"Veins draining straight into the portal vein, including left and right gastric","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 49"},{"src":"../sl/G006-50.jpg","cap":"Superior mesenteric vein tributaries: right gastroepiploic, right colic, ileocolic and others","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 50"},{"src":"../sl/G006-51.jpg","cap":"Splenic vein tributaries: inferior mesenteric (with left colic), left gastroepiploic, short gastrics","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 51"}],"q":[{"id":"G081-8-1","s":"A 40 year old woman got shot in the mid abdomen. From the angiogram, it was noted that the source of the bleeding came from pairs of veins that typically drain into the same vein. Which of the following veins were damaged?","o":["A. Left and right ovarian veins","B. Left and right gastroepiploic veins","C. Left and right suprarenal veins","D. Left and right colic veins","E. Left and right gastric veins"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Left and right gastric veins","a":"E","why":"<b>Left and right gastric veins.</b> Both drain straight into the hepatic portal vein: the right gastric near the pylorus, the left gastric from the lesser curvature and lower esophagus. Every other pair splits between two veins.<br>A. Ovarian: right into the IVC (inferior vena cava), left into the left renal vein.<br>B. Gastroepiploic (gastro-omental): left into the splenic vein, right into the SMV (superior mesenteric vein).<br>C. Suprarenal: right into the IVC, left into the left renal vein.<br>D. Colic: right into the SMV, left into the IMV (inferior mesenteric vein).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 49 (Portal vein)","Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 50 (Tributaries of superior mesenteric vein)","Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 51 (Tributaries of the splenic vein)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive 11/11/26 (J. LaBarge)","sl":8,"f":"G081"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"258"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":72,"label":"Esophageal varices: left gastric with azygos veins","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G006-56.jpg","cap":"Portocaval sites: A esophageal, B rectal, C umbilical, D retroperitoneal","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 56"},{"src":"../sl/G006-54.jpg","cap":"Table: left gastric with azygos causes esophageal varices; rectal causes hemorrhoids","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 54","cu":"../sl/G006-54c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G085-32.jpg","cap":"Cirrhotic liver: portal blood backs up into swollen lower esophageal veins","where":"2026 Middletown supplemental, slide 32"},{"src":"../tb/N292.jpg","cap":"At site 1, esophageal branches of the left gastric vein (portal) meet esophageal veins draining to the azygos (caval).","where":"Netter Atlas 6e, Plate 292","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"G085-31-1","s":"In this picture of portal-systemic venous anastomoses, the circled site labeled (1) dilates in portal hypertension. What clinical sign does it produce?","o":["A. Caput medusae","B. Esophageal varices","C. Hemorrhoids","D. Retroperitoneal varices"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Esophageal varices","a":"B","why":"<b>Esophageal varices.</b> Site (1) is the lower esophagus, where esophageal branches of the left gastric vein (portal) meet esophageal branches of the azygos vein (caval). In portal hypertension they swell into varices, which can burst and cause vomiting of blood.<br>A. Caput medusae: paraumbilical with epigastric veins, at the umbilicus.<br>C. Hemorrhoids: superior rectal with middle and inferior rectal veins, at the anal canal.<br>D. Retroperitoneal varices: colic with retroperitoneal veins on the back wall; no visible sign.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G085-31.jpg","in":"BRS-style anterior-view drawing of portal (purple) and systemic (blue) veins; anastomosis sites numbered (1) lower esophagus, (2) anal canal, (3) umbilicus, (4) colon/retroperitoneal veins, with (1)-(3) circled in green; no legend naming the sites. Asks site (1).","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 54 (Porto systemic anastomosis)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown supplemental","n":"GI supplemental session #1 11/13/26 (GI week 2 review)","sl":31,"f":"G085"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"259"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":73,"label":"Caput medusae: paraumbilical with epigastric veins","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G006-56.jpg","cap":"Site C: paraumbilical veins meeting epigastric veins around the umbilicus","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 56"},{"src":"../sl/G006-55.jpg","cap":"Paraumbilical (portal) with epigastric (systemic) veins: caput medusae","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 55","cu":"../sl/G006-55c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G085-34.jpg","cap":"Caput medusae: swollen veins radiating from the navel in portal hypertension","where":"2026 Middletown supplemental, slide 34"}],"q":[{"id":"G085-31-3","s":"In this picture of portal-systemic venous anastomoses, the circled site labeled (3) dilates in portal hypertension. What clinical sign does it produce?","o":["A. Esophageal varices","B. Hemorrhoids","C. Retroperitoneal varices","D. Caput medusae"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Caput medusae","a":"D","why":"<b>Caput medusae.</b> Site (3) is the umbilicus, where the paraumbilical veins (portal, running along the round ligament) meet the epigastric veins of the abdominal wall (caval). In portal hypertension they swell and radiate from the navel.<br>A. Esophageal varices: left gastric with azygos veins, at the lower esophagus.<br>B. Hemorrhoids: superior rectal with middle and inferior rectal veins, at the anal canal.<br>C. Retroperitoneal varices: colic with retroperitoneal veins on the back wall; no visible sign.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G085-31.jpg","in":"BRS-style anterior-view drawing of portal (purple) and systemic (blue) veins; anastomosis sites numbered (1) lower esophagus, (2) anal canal, (3) umbilicus, (4) colon/retroperitoneal veins, with (1)-(3) circled in green; no legend naming the sites. Asks site (3).","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 55 (Porto systemic anastamosis)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown supplemental","n":"GI supplemental session #1 11/13/26 (GI week 2 review)","sl":31,"f":"G085"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"260"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":73,"label":"Hemorrhoids: superior rectal with middle and inferior rectal veins","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G085-36.jpg","cap":"Hemorrhoids: internal and external rectal venous plexuses around the dentate line","where":"2026 Middletown supplemental, slide 36","cu":"../sl/G085-36c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G006-54.jpg","cap":"Rectal site: superior rectal (portal) meets middle and inferior rectal (caval)","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 54","cu":"../sl/G006-54c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"G085-31-2","s":"In this picture of portal-systemic venous anastomoses, the circled site labeled (2) dilates in portal hypertension. What clinical sign does it produce?","o":["A. Retroperitoneal varices","B. Caput medusae","C. Hemorrhoids","D. Esophageal varices"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Hemorrhoids","a":"C","why":"<b>Hemorrhoids.</b> Site (2) is the anal canal, where the superior rectal vein (portal, draining to the inferior mesenteric vein) meets the middle and inferior rectal veins (caval, draining to the internal iliac vein). In portal hypertension this rectal venous plexus swells.<br>A. Retroperitoneal varices: colic with retroperitoneal veins on the back wall; no visible sign.<br>B. Caput medusae: paraumbilical with epigastric veins, at the umbilicus.<br>D. Esophageal varices: left gastric with azygos veins, at the lower esophagus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G085-31.jpg","in":"BRS-style anterior-view drawing of portal (purple) and systemic (blue) veins; anastomosis sites numbered (1) lower esophagus, (2) anal canal, (3) umbilicus, (4) colon/retroperitoneal veins, with (1)-(3) circled in green; no legend naming the sites. Asks site (2).","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 54 (Porto systemic anastomosis)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown supplemental","n":"GI supplemental session #1 11/13/26 (GI week 2 review)","sl":31,"f":"G085"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"261"},{"id":"G111-15-1","s":"A 51-year-old woman with a history of alcohol abuse is diagnosed with cirrhosis of the liver and portal hypertension. In addition to esophageal varices, she presents with rectal varices. Which of the following portosystemic anastomoses is most likely responsible for these rectal varices?","o":["A. Inferior mesenteric vein to inferior rectal veins","B. Left gastric veins to inferior rectal veins","C. Portal vein to the middle and inferior rectal veins","D. Superior mesenteric vein to superior rectal veins","E. Superior rectal vein to the middle and inferior rectal veins"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Superior rectal vein to the middle and inferior rectal veins","a":"E","why":"<b>Superior rectal vein to the middle and inferior rectal veins.</b> The superior rectal vein drains to the inferior mesenteric vein (portal); the middle and inferior rectal veins drain to the internal iliac vein (caval). They meet in the rectal venous plexus, which swells in portal hypertension.<br>A. Inferior mesenteric to inferior rectal: the inferior mesenteric vein reaches the rectum only through the superior rectal vein.<br>B. Left gastric: drains the lower esophagus (esophageal varices).<br>C. Portal vein: the trunk never touches the rectal veins.<br>D. Superior mesenteric to superior rectal: both are portal veins.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 54 (Porto systemic anastomosis)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown liver, gallbladder, spleen & portal vein interactive 2026 (J. LaBarge)","sl":15,"f":"G111"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"262"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":73,"label":"Portal hypertension: varices, caput medusae, hemorrhoids","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G006-56.jpg","cap":"Four portocaval sites that swell in portal hypertension","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 56"},{"src":"../sl/G006-55.jpg","cap":"Paraumbilical with epigastric veins gives caput medusae; colonic with retroperitoneal","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 55","cu":"../sl/G006-55c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/N292.jpg","cap":"Find the four numbered portocaval sites: esophageal (varices), para-umbilical (caput medusae), rectal (hemorrhoids), retroperitoneal.","where":"Netter Atlas 6e, Plate 292","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"G111-28-1","s":"A 50-year-old male presents to the ER with complaints of bright red blood in his stool and frequent bouts of vomiting with trace amounts of blood in the emesis and mild distension of his abdomen. He has a 15 year history of alcoholism and cigarette smoking. The attending physician is suspects stenosis of a venous structure; which structure is most likely causing these problems?","o":["A. Superior Vena Cava","B. Azygous vein","C. Superior rectal","D. Hepatic portal vein","E. Right Renal"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Hepatic portal vein","a":"D","why":"<b>Hepatic portal vein.</b> A narrowed portal vein raises pressure in the whole portal system. Blood then escapes through the portocaval anastomoses: rectal varices (bright red blood in the stool) and esophageal varices (blood in the vomit), with ascites swelling the abdomen.<br>A. Superior vena cava: caval; its block does not back up portal blood.<br>B. Azygos vein: the caval escape route, not the cause.<br>C. Superior rectal vein: its block would explain only the rectal bleeding.<br>E. Right renal vein: caval; its block affects only the kidney.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 54 (Porto systemic anastomosis)","Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 55 (Porto systemic anastamosis)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown liver, gallbladder, spleen & portal vein interactive 2026 (J. LaBarge)","sl":28,"f":"G111"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"263"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":73,"label":"Portocaval shunt joins a portal and caval vein","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G006-56.jpg","cap":"MR angiogram: splenic vein running near the left renal vein","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 56"},{"src":"../sl/G006-53.jpg","cap":"Splenic vein behind the pancreas, receiving the inferior mesenteric vein","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 53"},{"src":"../tb/N323.jpg","cap":"In the top scan, the splenic vein (portal) lies just in front of the left renal vein (caval), so surgeons join them.","where":"Netter Atlas 6e, Plate 323","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F0008-130-1","s":"A 62-year-old female patient has chronic liver disease and portal hypertension. To relieve the pressure in the portal system, a portocaval shunt is performed. Which of the following pairs of veins may be anastomoses to perform this shunt?","o":["A. Left ovarian-left renal","B. Right renal-right suprarenal","C. Splenic- left renal","D. Superior mesenteric-inferior mesenteric","E. Superior mesenteric-splenic"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Splenic- left renal","a":"C","why":"<b>Splenic vein to left renal vein.</b> A portocaval shunt must join a <b>portal</b> vein to a <b>caval</b> (systemic) vein, so portal blood can bypass the liver. The splenic vein (portal) lies close to the left renal vein (caval) behind the pancreas.<br>A. Left ovarian and left renal: both caval.<br>B. Right renal and right suprarenal: both caval.<br>D. Superior and inferior mesenteric: both portal.<br>E. Superior mesenteric and splenic: both portal; they already join to form the portal vein.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 54 (Porto systemic anastomosis)","Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 55 (Porto systemic anastamosis)","Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 51 (Tributaries of the splenic vein)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown liver, gallbladder, spleen & portal vein interactive 2026 (J. LaBarge)","sl":29,"f":"G111"},{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":130,"f":"F0008"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"264"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":74,"label":"Pancreatic tail is the intraperitoneal part","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G007-11.jpg","cap":"Retroperitoneal head and body; tail enters the splenorenal ligament","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 11","cu":"../sl/G007-11c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G007-27.jpg","cap":"Axial diagram: pancreatic tail wrapped in the lienorenal ligament","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 27"},{"src":"../tb/M5_24.jpg","cap":"The pancreatic body lies on the posterior wall, but the tail runs into the splenorenal ligament toward the spleen.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.24","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/N282.jpg","cap":"In the spleen in situ, find the tail of the pancreas reaching the spleen inside the splenorenal (lienorenal) ligament.","where":"Netter Atlas 6e, Plate 282","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F1252-35-1","s":"Which part of the pancreas is NOT retroperitoneal?","o":["A. Head","B. Body","C. Tail"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Tail","a":"C","why":"<b>Tail.</b> The tail runs into the splenorenal ligament toward the splenic hilum, so it is wrapped in peritoneum (intraperitoneal). The rest of the pancreas stays behind the peritoneum.<br>A. Head: retroperitoneal, inside the C-loop of the duodenum.<br>B. Body: retroperitoneal; it lies across the aorta in the floor of the lesser sac (omental bursa).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Pancreas & Spleen slide 11 (Pancreas)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive 11/11/26 (J. LaBarge)","sl":31,"f":"G081"},{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":35,"f":"F1252"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"265"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":74,"label":"Pancreatic head overlies IVC and renal vessels","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G007-7.jpg","cap":"Head of pancreas lying on the inferior vena cava, bile duct embedded","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 7","cu":"../sl/G007-7c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G007-8.jpg","cap":"Head outline over inferior vena cava, both renal veins, right renal artery","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 8"},{"src":"../tb/G4_115.jpg","cap":"See the pancreatic head resting on the inferior vena cava; the superior mesenteric vein passes behind the neck instead.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 4.115","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/N327.jpg","cap":"In this section, the uncinate process lies on the inferior vena cava and renal veins; the superior mesenteric vein is in front.","where":"Netter Atlas 6e, Plate 327","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"G111-14-1","s":"A tumor in the head of the pancreas will least likely impact which following structure?","o":["A. The splenorenal ligament","B. Common bile duct","C. IVC","D. Right renal vein","E. Right renal artery"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"The splenorenal ligament","a":"A","why":"<b>The splenorenal ligament.</b> It holds the tail of the pancreas and the splenic vessels at the far left, at the splenic hilum, well out of reach of a head tumor.<br>B. Common bile duct: embedded in the back of the head; squeezed, it causes jaundice.<br>C. Inferior vena cava: lies directly behind the head.<br>D. Right renal vein: lies behind the head.<br>E. Right renal artery: passes behind the inferior vena cava and the head.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Pancreas & Spleen slide 7 (Pancreas)","Pancreas & Spleen slide 8 (Overlies IVC, left and right renal veins and right renal artery.)","Pancreas & Spleen slide 23 (Spleen)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown liver, gallbladder, spleen & portal vein interactive 2026 (J. LaBarge)","sl":14,"f":"G111"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"266"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":74,"label":"SMA and SMV lie behind the pancreatic neck","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G007-9.jpg","cap":"Neck overlies superior mesenteric vessels; portal vein forms behind it","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 9","cu":"../sl/G007-9c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G007-10.jpg","cap":"Body touches aorta and superior mesenteric artery posteriorly","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 10","cu":"../sl/G007-10c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/G4_115.jpg","cap":"Find the superior mesenteric artery and vein passing behind the pancreatic neck, where the portal vein forms.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 4.115","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F0008-132-1","s":"Which of the following is incorrect pertaining to the pancreas?","o":["A) The superior mesenteric artery lies anterior to its neck.","B) Its head is embraced mainly by the descending part of the duodenum.","C) It is retroperitoneal.","D) Its main duct joins with the common bile duct before entering the duodenum.","E) It is partially supplied by branches from the splenic artery."],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"The superior mesenteric artery lies anterior to its neck (it lies posterior)","a":"A","why":"<b>A is false.</b> The SMA (superior mesenteric artery) lies <b>behind</b> the neck of the pancreas, not in front. It leaves the aorta behind the neck, then passes forward over the uncinate process.<br>B. True: the C-shaped duodenum hugs the head, mostly by its descending part.<br>C. True: the pancreas is retroperitoneal, except the tail.<br>D. True: the main duct joins the bile duct at the hepatopancreatic ampulla.<br>E. True: the splenic artery sends pancreatic branches to the body and tail.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Pancreas & Spleen slide 9 (Pancreas)","Small & Large Intestines slide 42 (SMA)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":132,"f":"F0008"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"267"},{"id":"G111-19-1","s":"A 59-year-old man presents with deep epigastric pain. A CT scan of the abdomen reveals a pancreatic tumor that partially envelops a large artery. Which of the following arteries is most likely involved?","o":["A. Common hepatic","B. Gastroduodenal","C. Left gastric","D. Middle colic","E. Superior mesenteric"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Superior mesenteric","a":"E","why":"<b>Superior mesenteric artery.</b> It leaves the aorta behind the neck of the pancreas and passes forward over the uncinate process, so the gland grips it from front and back, and a tumor can partly wrap around it.<br>A. Common hepatic: runs to the right along the upper border, not through the gland.<br>B. Gastroduodenal: a small branch passing down behind the first part of the duodenum, on the front of the head.<br>C. Left gastric: runs up to the lesser curvature of the stomach.<br>D. Middle colic: leaves the superior mesenteric artery below the pancreas, into the transverse mesocolon.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Pancreas & Spleen slide 9 (Pancreas)","Small & Large Intestines slide 42 (SMA)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown liver, gallbladder, spleen & portal vein interactive 2026 (J. LaBarge)","sl":19,"f":"G111"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"268"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":75,"label":"Retroperitoneal epigastrium: pancreas and its splenic branches","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G111-12.jpg","cap":"Pancreas lying retroperitoneally across the posterior abdominal wall","where":"2026 Middletown interactive, slide 12","cu":"../sl/G111-12c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G007-15.jpg","cap":"Splenic artery along the upper border, branching into the pancreas","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 15"}],"q":[{"id":"G111-11-1","s":"An elderly patient was brought to your office with a retroperitoneal infection in the epigastric region. Which structure might be at most at risk?","o":["A. Proper hepatic artery","B. Inferior mesenteric vein","C. Appendicular artery","D. Right gastro-omental vein","E. Pancreatic branches of splenic artery"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Pancreatic branches of splenic artery","a":"E","why":"<b>Pancreatic branches of the splenic artery.</b> The pancreas lies retroperitoneally across the epigastrium, and the splenic artery runs along its upper border, giving branches to its neck, body and tail. An infection behind the peritoneum there reaches them.<br>A. Proper hepatic artery: inside the lesser omentum (hepatoduodenal ligament).<br>B. Inferior mesenteric vein: retroperitoneal, but it runs up the left side and reaches the epigastrium only at its end, behind the pancreatic body.<br>C. Appendicular artery: in the mesoappendix, right lower quadrant.<br>D. Right gastro-omental vein: in the greater omentum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Pancreas & Spleen slide 5 (Pancreas)","Pancreas & Spleen slide 14 (Pancreas)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown liver, gallbladder, spleen & portal vein interactive 2026 (J. LaBarge)","sl":11,"f":"G111"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"269"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":76,"label":"Spleen lies deep to left ribs 9–11","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G007-22.jpg","cap":"Spleen in left upper quadrant against ribs 9–11, lateral view numbered","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 22"},{"src":"../sl/G007-25.jpg","cap":"Spleen in situ under the diaphragm beside the 9th rib","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 25"},{"src":"../sl/F1180-136.jpg","cap":"Rib relations: spleen left ribs 9–11, liver right 7–11, kidneys 11–12","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 136"},{"src":"../sl/D071-19.jpg","cap":"Spleen: left upper quadrant, intraperitoneal, white and red pulp, filters the blood","where":"2026 Middletown TA weekly, slide 19","cu":"../sl/D071-19c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/G4_103.jpg","cap":"In the left side view, find the spleen behind the lower left ribs, labeled at rib IX.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 4.103","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/M5_31.jpg","cap":"In the back view, find the spleen against the lower left ribs, high on the left side of the back.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.31","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F3900-125-1","s":"A teenage girl is rushed to the hospital due to left sided abdominal pain, shoulder pain, confusion, lightheadedness and distended abdomen. Upon arrival it turns out she was in a serious accident is suspected to have many broken ribs. You suspect a splenic rupture given her symptoms and past history of a serious viral infection. Which ribs does the spleen normally have a relationship with and should be evaluated for fracture?","o":["A. Ribs 7-8 on left","B. Ribs 9-11 on left","C. Ribs 11-12 on the right","D. Rib 12 on the left"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Ribs 9-11 on left","a":"B","why":"<b>Ribs 9–11 on the left.</b> The spleen lies in the left upper quadrant behind the stomach. Behind it, the diaphragm separates it from ribs 9 to 11, so broken ribs there can tear it. Left shoulder pain (Kehr's sign) comes from blood irritating the diaphragm.<br>A. Ribs 7–8, left: too high and forward; over the stomach.<br>C. Ribs 11–12, right: wrong side and too low; the right kidney sits lower, against rib 12.<br>D. Rib 12, left: too low; that is the level of the left kidney (ribs 11–12).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Pancreas & Spleen slide 22 (Largest lymphatic organ, very vascular Located in left upper quadrant (LUQ))","Pancreas & Spleen slide 28 (Ribs 9-11)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown liver, gallbladder, spleen & portal vein interactive 2026 (J. LaBarge)","sl":5,"f":"G111"},{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":125,"f":"F3900"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"270"},{"id":"G111-4-1","s":"A 17 year old male was transported to the ED after the vehicle he was in was struck to the side by a large truck. The patient complained of pain along the left mid axillary line and the physician noticed a large prominent bulge protruding along the left hypochondriac region. Diagnostic imaging revealed fracture of ribs 9 and 10. Vital sign reveal that the patient is hypotensive.  What organ is most likely injured?","o":["A. Stomach","B. Descending colon","C. Spleen","D. Pancreas","E. 3rd part of the duodenum"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Spleen","a":"C","why":"<b>Spleen.</b> Left ribs 9 and 10 at the midaxillary line lie over the spleen, which sits against ribs 9 to 11. Low blood pressure means heavy internal bleeding, typical of a torn spleen.<br>A. Stomach: in front; rarely bleeds like this after a blow.<br>B. Descending colon: lower, in the left flank.<br>D. Pancreas: deep near the midline, not against the ribs.<br>E. Third part of the duodenum: crosses the midline low down, at the third lumbar vertebra (L3).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Pancreas & Spleen slide 22 (Largest lymphatic organ, very vascular Located in left upper quadrant (LUQ))","Pancreas & Spleen slide 28 (Ribs 9-11)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown liver, gallbladder, spleen & portal vein interactive 2026 (J. LaBarge)","sl":4,"f":"G111"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"271"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":76,"label":"Kidney levels: liver keeps the right kidney lower, at rib 12","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G111-6.jpg","cap":"Back view: spleen at ribs 9–11; left kidney higher than right","where":"2026 Middletown interactive, slide 6","cu":"../sl/G111-6c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"G111-5-2","s":"Which ribs is the right kidney related to posteriorly?","o":["A. 9th-11th ribs","B. 10th and 11th ribs","C. 11th and 12th ribs","D. 12th rib only","E. 7th-11th ribs"],"g":["D"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"11th and 12th ribs","a":"C","why":"<b>12th rib only.</b> The liver holds the right kidney about 2.5 cm lower than the left, so only rib 12 lies behind its upper pole. The higher left kidney lies against ribs 11 and 12.<br>A. 9th–11th ribs: the spleen's ribs, on the left.<br>B. 10th and 11th ribs: too high for either kidney.<br>C. 11th and 12th ribs: the left kidney's ribs; the right kidney sits lower.<br>E. 7th–11th ribs: the liver's ribs, on the right.","ex":"","fl":["The answer is the 12th rib only (D): the liver holds the right kidney lower, so only rib 12 lies behind its upper pole, while ribs 11 and 12 are the left kidney's relation."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown liver, gallbladder, spleen & portal vein interactive 2026 (J. LaBarge)","sl":5,"f":"G111"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"272"},{"id":"G111-5-3","s":"The left kidney sits higher than the right kidney on the posterior abdominal wall. What is the reason for this difference?","o":["A. The spleen lies below the left kidney and pushes it up","B. The large liver lies above the right kidney and keeps it lower","C. The head of the pancreas sits on top of the right kidney and pushes it down","D. The inferior vena cava lies on the right side and pulls the right kidney down"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"The large liver lies above the right kidney and keeps it lower","a":"B","why":"<b>The large liver lies above the right kidney and keeps it lower.</b> The big right lobe of the liver fills the space above the right kidney, so the right kidney sits about 2.5 cm lower than the left.<br>A. Spleen: lies above and lateral to the left kidney, not below it.<br>C. Head of the pancreas: lies in front of the inferior vena cava and renal vessels, not on top of the right kidney.<br>D. Inferior vena cava: runs up beside the right kidney and does not pull it down.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 14 (Visceral surface of Liver)","Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 5 (The liver lies mainly in the right upper quadrant of the abdomen, where it is pr)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown liver, gallbladder, spleen & portal vein interactive 2026 (J. LaBarge)","sl":5,"f":"G111"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"273"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":77,"label":"Gastrosplenic ligament: spleen to greater curvature","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G007-26.jpg","cap":"Axial section: gastrosplenic ligament to stomach, splenorenal ligament to left kidney","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 26","cu":"../sl/G007-26c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G007-27.jpg","cap":"Gastrosplenic and lienorenal ligaments with the vessels each one carries","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 27"},{"src":"../tb/M5_24.jpg","cap":"Find the gastrosplenic ligament from spleen to stomach and the splenorenal ligament from spleen to left kidney.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.24","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/M5_27.jpg","cap":"From the front, see the gastrosplenic ligament joining the greater curvature of the stomach to the spleen.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.27","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F3900-38-1","s":"The gastrosplenic ligament has attachments from the ______ to the __________.","o":["A. Jejunum and the greater curvature of the stomach","B. Spleen and the greater curvature of the stomach","C. Spleen and the lesser curvature of the stomach","D. Ileum and the lesser curvature of the stomach"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Spleen and the greater curvature of the stomach","a":"B","why":"<b>Spleen and greater curvature of the stomach.</b> The gastrosplenic ligament runs from the splenic hilum to the greater curvature. It carries the short gastric and left gastro-omental vessels.<br>A. Jejunum: hangs on the mesentery, far from the spleen.<br>C. Lesser curvature: tied to the liver by the hepatogastric ligament.<br>D. Ileum and lesser curvature: neither is tied to the spleen.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Pancreas & Spleen slide 23 (Spleen)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive 11/11/26 (J. LaBarge)","sl":31,"f":"G081"},{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":38,"f":"F3900"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"274"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":77,"label":"Short gastric arteries run in gastrosplenic ligament","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G007-27.jpg","cap":"Gastrosplenic ligament carries short gastric and left gastro-epiploic arteries","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 27"},{"src":"../sl/G007-25.jpg","cap":"Short gastric vessels running in the gastrosplenic ligament to the stomach","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 25"},{"src":"../tb/N282.jpg","cap":"In the spleen in situ, find the short gastric vessels running in the gastrosplenic ligament to the stomach.","where":"Netter Atlas 6e, Plate 282","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/M5_40.jpg","cap":"See the short gastric arteries leaving the splenic artery near the spleen to reach the gastric fundus.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.40","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"G111-8-1","s":"In the previous case, it became necessary to surgically remove the spleen as the bleeding became more profuse. Which vessels should the physician need to be careful to ligate?","o":["A. Short gastric","B. Splenic","C. Right gastric","D. Pancreaticoduodenal"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Short gastric","a":"A","why":"<b>Short gastric.</b> These several short vessels run from the splenic hilum to the fundus of the stomach inside the gastrosplenic ligament. In a splenectomy they must be tied with care: a torn short gastric vessel bleeds, and a misplaced tie can cut off the fundus.<br>B. Splenic: also tied, at the hilum, but it is the one obvious main trunk; the short gastrics are the ones that need care.<br>C. Right gastric: lesser curvature near the pylorus, far from the spleen.<br>D. Pancreaticoduodenal: around the pancreatic head, on the right.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Pancreas & Spleen slide 23 (Spleen)","Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 51 (Tributaries of the splenic vein)","Pancreas & Spleen slide 29 (Spleen)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown liver, gallbladder, spleen & portal vein interactive 2026 (J. LaBarge)","sl":8,"f":"G111"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"275"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":77,"label":"Splenorenal (lienorenal) ligament: spleen to left kidney","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G007-26.jpg","cap":"Axial section: splenorenal ligament runs from the splenic hilum to the left kidney","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 26","cu":"../sl/G007-26c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G007-23.jpg","cap":"Splenic attachments: gastrosplenic to greater curvature, splenorenal to left kidney","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 23"}],"q":[{"id":"G081-31-1","s":"Which two organs does the splenorenal ligament connect?","o":["A. Spleen and the greater curvature of the stomach","B. Spleen and the left colic flexure","C. Spleen and left kidney","D. Left colic flexure and the diaphragm","E. Liver and right kidney"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Spleen and left kidney","a":"C","why":"<b>Spleen and left kidney.</b> The splenorenal ligament runs from the splenic hilum back to the front of the left kidney. It carries the splenic artery and vein and the tail of the pancreas.<br>A. Spleen and greater curvature: the gastrosplenic ligament.<br>B. Spleen and left colic flexure: the small splenocolic ligament.<br>D. Left colic flexure and diaphragm: the phrenicocolic ligament, on which the spleen rests.<br>E. Liver and right kidney: the hepatorenal ligament, on the far right.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Pancreas & Spleen slide 23 (Spleen)","Pancreas & Spleen slide 11 (Pancreas)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive 11/11/26 (J. LaBarge)","sl":31,"f":"G081"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"276"},{"id":"G081-31-6","s":"What is another name for the splenorenal ligament?","o":["A. Gastrolienal ligament","B. Phrenicocolic ligament","C. Lienorenal ligament","D. Splenocolic ligament","E. Hepatorenal ligament"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Lienorenal ligament","a":"C","why":"<b>Lienorenal ligament.</b> Lien means spleen, so lienorenal and splenorenal name the same fold, from the splenic hilum to the left kidney, carrying the splenic vessels and the pancreatic tail.<br>A. Gastrolienal ligament: another name for the gastrosplenic ligament, spleen to greater curvature.<br>B. Phrenicocolic ligament: left colic flexure to the diaphragm; the spleen rests on it.<br>D. Splenocolic ligament: spleen to the left colic flexure.<br>E. Hepatorenal ligament: liver to right kidney.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Pancreas & Spleen slide 23 (Spleen)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive 11/11/26 (J. LaBarge)","sl":31,"f":"G081"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"277"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":77,"label":"Splenorenal ligament: pancreatic tail and splenic vessels","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G007-27.jpg","cap":"Lienorenal ligament holds splenic artery, vein, pancreatic tail; gastrosplenic holds short gastrics","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 27"},{"src":"../sl/G007-25.jpg","cap":"Spleen in situ: splenorenal ligament with splenic vessels and tail of pancreas","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 25"},{"src":"../sl/G007-11.jpg","cap":"Pancreatic tail passes between the layers of the splenorenal ligament","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 11","cu":"../sl/G007-11c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/D071-19.jpg","cap":"Pancreas and spleen side by side: the pancreatic tail reaches the splenic hilum","where":"2026 Middletown TA weekly, slide 19","cu":"../sl/D071-19c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"F3112-50-1","s":"Which ligament contains the tail of the pancreas and the splenic vessels?","o":["A. Gastrosplenic ligament","B. Gastrocolic ligament","C. Hepatoduodenal ligament","D. Phrenicocolic ligament","E. Splenorenal (lienorenal) ligament"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Splenorenal (lienorenal) ligament","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Splenorenal (lienorenal) ligament.</b> It joins the spleen to the left kidney ('lieno-' means spleen). The pancreatic tail and splenic vessels run inside it, so the tail is the only intraperitoneal part of the pancreas.<br>A. Gastrosplenic ligament: spleen to greater curvature; carries the short gastric and left gastro-omental vessels.<br>B. Gastrocolic ligament: greater curvature to transverse colon, part of the greater omentum.<br>C. Hepatoduodenal ligament: carries the portal triad.<br>D. Phrenicocolic ligament: ties the left colic flexure to the diaphragm.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Pancreas & Spleen slide 11 (Pancreas)","Pancreas & Spleen slide 23 (Spleen)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"Peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive (JL)","sl":50,"f":"F3112"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"278"},{"id":"F3112-49-1","s":"Due to his fluctuating levels of insulin, a 62 year old male undergoes a series of tests. A tumor is detected in the tail of the pancreas. The tumor's definite borders would make it easy to excise. However, before the surgeon can reach the tumor, which of the following ligaments needs to be dissected?","o":["A. Splenorenal","B. Gastrosplenic","C. Gastrocolic","D. Hepatorenal","E. Hepatogastric"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Splenorenal","a":"A","why":"<b>Splenorenal ligament.</b> The tail of the pancreas runs inside this ligament, with the splenic artery and vein, toward the hilum of the spleen. To reach a tail tumor, the surgeon opens this ligament.<br>B. Gastrosplenic: spleen to greater curvature; holds the short gastric and left gastro-omental vessels.<br>C. Gastrocolic: greater curvature to transverse colon (part of the greater omentum).<br>D. Hepatorenal: liver to right kidney, on the far right.<br>E. Hepatogastric: liver to lesser curvature (part of the lesser omentum).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Pancreas & Spleen slide 11 (Pancreas)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive 11/11/26 (J. LaBarge)","sl":30,"f":"G081"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"Peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive (JL)","sl":49,"f":"F3112"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"279"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G6","u":78,"label":"Withdrawal reflex is somatic, not autonomic","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G008-6.jpg","cap":"Somatic vs autonomic table: voluntary skeletal muscle vs smooth muscle, heart, glands","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 6","cu":"../sl/G008-6c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G008-3.jpg","cap":"Divisions of nervous system: somatic voluntary, autonomic involuntary","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 3"}],"q":[{"id":"F2936-420-1","s":"The following reflexes are all autonomic Except:","o":["A. Salivary secretion.","B. Micturition.","C. Flexion of the arm following a painful stimulus.","D. Acceleration of the heart"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Flexion of the arm following a painful stimulus.","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Flexion of the arm after a painful stimulus.</b> This withdrawal reflex runs through somatic nerves to skeletal muscle, so it is not autonomic.<br>A. Salivary secretion: a gland, run by autonomic nerves.<br>B. Micturition (emptying the bladder): a parasympathetic reflex contracts the bladder's smooth muscle.<br>D. Speeding up the heart: sympathetic nerves act on heart muscle.<br>Rule: autonomic nerves supply smooth muscle, heart muscle and glands; somatic nerves supply skeletal muscle.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 3 (Divisions of the nervous system)","ANS slide 6 (Difference between the Somatic and Autonomic Nervous systems)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown autonomic nervous system interactive 2026","sl":20,"f":"G112"},{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"combined clickers for the final: abdomen/GI-embryo clickers only","sl":420,"f":"F2936"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"280"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G6","u":78,"label":"Preganglionic body in brain/cord; postganglionic in ganglion","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G008-7.jpg","cap":"Two-neuron chain: preganglionic body in spinal cord, postganglionic body in ganglion","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 7","cu":"../sl/G008-7c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G008-10.jpg","cap":"Paravertebral chain and prevertebral ganglia hold postganglionic sympathetic cell bodies","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 10","cu":"../sl/G008-10c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/G1_40.jpg","cap":"The preganglionic cell body sits in the spinal cord; postganglionic cell bodies cluster outside it as a ganglion.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 1.40","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/G4_158.jpg","cap":"Preganglionic sympathetic fibers leave the cord and synapse in the trunk ganglion or the celiac (prevertebral) ganglion.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 4.158","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F2936-2-1","s":"Which of the following will be seen in a ‘ganglion’?","o":["A. Cell bodies of somatic motor neurons","B. Cell bodies of spinal efferent neurons","C. Postganglionic sympathetic neuronal cell body","D. Preganglionic parasympathetic neuronal cell body","E. Preganglionic sympathetic neuronal cell body"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Postganglionic sympathetic neuronal cell body","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Postganglionic sympathetic cell body.</b> A ganglion is a cluster of nerve cell bodies outside the brain and spinal cord. Autonomic ganglia hold the cell bodies of the second (postganglionic) neuron.<br>A. Somatic motor neurons: cell bodies in the anterior horn of the spinal cord.<br>B. Spinal efferent neurons: also inside the spinal cord.<br>D. Preganglionic parasympathetic: brainstem and sacral 2 to 4 cord segments.<br>E. Preganglionic sympathetic: lateral horn of thoracic 1 to lumbar 2.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 7 (Pre/Postganglionic system)","ANS slide 10 (Cell bodies of postganglionic fibers will be located in 2 locations)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"combined clickers for the final: abdomen/GI-embryo clickers only","sl":2,"f":"F2936"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"281"},{"id":"F2936-445-1","s":"Prevertebral and paravertebral ganglia","o":["A. Cell bodies of the presynaptic neurons of the sympathetic nervous system","B. Cell bodies of the presynaptic neurons of the parasympathetic neurons","C. Cell bodies of the postynaptic neurons of the sympathetic nervous system","D. Cell bodies of the potsynaptic neurons of the parasympathetic nervous system"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Cell bodies of the postsynaptic neurons of the sympathetic nervous system","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Cell bodies of postsynaptic (postganglionic) sympathetic neurons.</b> Paravertebral ganglia (the chain beside the vertebrae) and prevertebral ganglia (celiac and mesenteric, in front of the aorta) are both sympathetic, and both hold the second neuron.<br>A. Presynaptic sympathetic: lateral horn of thoracic 1 to lumbar 2.<br>B. Presynaptic parasympathetic: brainstem and sacral 2 to 4 cord.<br>D. Postsynaptic parasympathetic: the 4 head ganglia or small ganglia in the organ walls.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 10 (Cell bodies of postganglionic fibers will be located in 2 locations)","ANS slide 8 (Pre/Postganglionic system)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"combined clickers for the final: abdomen/GI-embryo clickers only","sl":445,"f":"F2936"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"282"},{"id":"G112-19-1","s":"Match the structure to the appropriate location: 5. Post-Ganglionic Sympathetic Cell Body","o":["A. Brainstem","B. Enteric Ganglion","C. Gray Ramus","D. Prevertebral Ganglion","E. White Ramus"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Prevertebral Ganglion","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Prevertebral ganglion.</b> Postganglionic sympathetic cell bodies lie in sympathetic ganglia: the paravertebral chain, or the prevertebral ganglia (celiac, superior and inferior mesenteric) in front of the aorta.<br>A. Brainstem: preganglionic parasympathetic cell bodies (cranial nerves III, VII, IX, X).<br>B. Enteric ganglion: postganglionic parasympathetic cell bodies in the gut wall.<br>C. Gray ramus: a bundle of postganglionic sympathetic fibers, with no cell bodies.<br>E. White ramus: a bundle of preganglionic sympathetic fibers, with no cell bodies.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 10 (Cell bodies of postganglionic fibers will be located in 2 locations)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown autonomic nervous system interactive 2026","sl":19,"f":"G112"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"283"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G6","u":78,"label":"Sympathetic short pre/long post; parasympathetic reverse","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G008-8.jpg","cap":"Ganglia locations and fiber lengths: sympathetic short-long, parasympathetic long-short","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 8","cu":"../sl/G008-8c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G008-26.jpg","cap":"Summary table: origin, fiber length, ganglia, transmitters for both divisions","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 26"},{"src":"../tb/G1_46.jpg","cap":"Sympathetic: short solid preganglionic fiber to the nearby trunk ganglion, then a long dotted postganglionic fiber to the heart.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 1.46","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/G1_48.jpg","cap":"Parasympathetic: long preganglionic fibers run to ganglia beside or inside the organ; the postganglionic fibers are short.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 1.48","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"G112-11-2","s":"Which pair of fiber lengths characterizes the parasympathetic division?","o":["A. Short preganglionic fibers and long postganglionic fibers","B. Long preganglionic fibers and short postganglionic fibers","C. Short preganglionic fibers and short postganglionic fibers","D. Long preganglionic fibers and long postganglionic fibers","E. Preganglionic fibers only, ending directly on the effector cells with no ganglion"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Long preganglionic fibers and short postganglionic fibers","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Long preganglionic fibers and short postganglionic fibers.</b> Parasympathetic ganglia sit next to or inside the target organ, so the first fiber travels almost the whole way and the second is tiny.<br>A. Short preganglionic, long postganglionic: the sympathetic pattern, because its ganglia lie near the spinal cord.<br>C and D. Both short or both long: true of neither division; fiber length follows where the ganglion sits.<br>E. No ganglion: every autonomic path uses two neurons, so a ganglion always lies between them.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 8 (Pre/Postganglionic system)","ANS slide 26 (Summary)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown autonomic nervous system interactive 2026","sl":11,"f":"G112"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"284"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G6","u":78,"label":"Head ganglia are parasympathetic, not sympathetic","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G008-8.jpg","cap":"Sympathetic ganglia beside vertebrae or aorta; four head ganglia are parasympathetic","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 8","cu":"../sl/G008-8c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G008-26.jpg","cap":"Summary table: sympathetic ganglia lateral to vertebrae or in front of aorta","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 26"},{"src":"../tb/G1_48.jpg","cap":"The four head ganglia (ciliary, pterygopalatine, otic, submandibular) all sit on parasympathetic cranial nerve pathways.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 1.48","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/G4_124.jpg","cap":"Sympathetic ganglia lie in a chain beside the vertebral column, from neck to coccyx, not in the head.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 4.124","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F2936-423-1","s":"Which of the below does NOT describe the Sympathetic division?","o":["A. Ganglion in the head","B. Short preganglionic neurons","C. Thoracolumbar output","D. Synapse with blood vessels","E. Stimulate sweat glands"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Ganglion in the head","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Ganglion in the head.</b> The four autonomic ganglia in the head (ciliary, pterygopalatine, otic, submandibular) are parasympathetic. Sympathetic ganglia lie beside the vertebrae or in front of the aorta; the highest one, the superior cervical ganglion, is in the neck.<br>B. Short preganglionic neurons: true of sympathetic.<br>C. Thoracolumbar output (thoracic 1 to lumbar 2): true.<br>D. Synapse with blood vessels: true; sympathetic fibers supply all vessels.<br>E. Stimulate sweat glands: true; only sympathetic fibers reach them.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 8 (Pre/Postganglionic system)","ANS slide 16 (Sympathetic Division)","ANS slide 26 (Summary)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown autonomic nervous system interactive 2026","sl":21,"f":"G112"},{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"combined clickers for the final: abdomen/GI-embryo clickers only","sl":423,"f":"F2936"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"285"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G6","u":79,"label":"Sympathetic preganglionic cell bodies: lateral horn T1–L2","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G008-9.jpg","cap":"Lateral horn of T1–L2 holds sympathetic preganglionic cell bodies, by body region","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 9","cu":"../sl/G008-9c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G008-16.jpg","cap":"Spinal segments: head, neck and upper limb T1–T4; sweat glands sympathetic","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 16"},{"src":"../tb/G1_45.jpg","cap":"Sympathetic preganglionic cell bodies sit in the lateral horn only at T1 to L2; upper ones supply head and arm.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 1.45","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/N304.jpg","cap":"Find the intermediolateral cell column (lateral horn) of the thoracic cord, where the red preganglionic sympathetic fiber starts.","where":"Netter Atlas 6e, Plate 304","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F2936-16-1","s":"Almost all the cell bodies of sympathetic fibers are located in which part of central nervous system?","o":["A. Cervical segment of spinal cord","B. Sacral segment of spinal cord","C. Thoracic segment of spinal cord","D. Coccygeal segment of spinal cord","E. Grey matter of brain"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Thoracic segment of spinal cord","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Thoracic segments of the spinal cord.</b> Sympathetic preganglionic cell bodies lie in the lateral horn from thoracic 1 to lumbar 2. Twelve of those fourteen segments are thoracic.<br>A. Cervical cord: no sympathetic outflow; the neck gets fibers that climb the chain from the upper thoracic cord.<br>B. Sacral cord: sacral 2 to 4 holds parasympathetic cell bodies.<br>D. Coccygeal cord: no autonomic outflow.<br>E. Brain gray matter: brainstem autonomic nuclei are parasympathetic.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 9 (Sympathetic Division)","ANS slide 5 (Divisions of the ANS)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown autonomic nervous system interactive 2026","sl":29,"f":"G112"},{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"combined clickers for the final: abdomen/GI-embryo clickers only","sl":16,"f":"F2936"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"286"},{"id":"F2936-6-1","s":"A lesion in which of the following will abolish sweating from the upper limb?","o":["A. Dorsal rami of the first four thoracic spinal nerves","B. Lateral grey horn in the first four thoracic segments of the spinal cord","C. Posterior grey horn in the first four thoracic segments of the spinal cord","D. Posterior roots of the first four thoracic spinal nerves","E. The first four thoracic spinal ganglia"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Lateral grey horn in the first four thoracic segments of the spinal cord","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Lateral gray horn of the first four thoracic segments.</b> Sweat glands get only sympathetic fibers. For the upper limb, the preganglionic cell bodies sit in the lateral horn of thoracic 1 to 4; destroy them and the arm stops sweating.<br>A. Dorsal rami of thoracic 1 to 4: supply the skin of the back, not the arm.<br>C. Posterior gray horn: sensory neurons.<br>D. Posterior roots: sensory fibers only.<br>E. First four thoracic spinal ganglia: dorsal root ganglia, which hold sensory cell bodies.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 16 (Sympathetic Division)","ANS slide 9 (Sympathetic Division)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown autonomic nervous system interactive 2026","sl":35,"f":"G112"},{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"combined clickers for the final: abdomen/GI-embryo clickers only","sl":6,"f":"F2936"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"287"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G6","u":79,"label":"White ramus in, gray ramus out","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G008-11.jpg","cap":"Sympathetic course: lateral horn, ventral root, white ramus, ganglion, gray ramus","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 11","cu":"../sl/G008-11c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G112-6.jpg","cap":"White ramus carries myelinated preganglionic fibers in; gray ramus carries unmyelinated postganglionic out","where":"2026 Middletown interactive, slide 6","cu":"../sl/G112-6c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/G4_158.jpg","cap":"Trace the path: anterior root, white ramus into the trunk ganglion, then postganglionic fibers back out through the gray ramus.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 4.158","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/G1_45.jpg","cap":"White rami exist only at T1 to L2, but every spinal nerve gets a gray ramus carrying postganglionic fibers.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 1.45","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F2936-4-1","s":"Which of the following are found in grey rami communicans?","o":["A. Postganglionic parasympathetic axons","B. Postganglionic sympathetic axons","C. Preganglionic parasympathetic axons","D. Preganglionic sympathetic axons","E. Somatic motor axons"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Postganglionic sympathetic axons","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Postganglionic sympathetic axons.</b> After synapsing in a chain ganglion, unmyelinated postganglionic fibers return to the spinal nerve through the gray ramus. Gray = unmyelinated = postganglionic.<br>A. Postganglionic parasympathetic: start in ganglia near or in the organ, never in the chain.<br>C. Preganglionic parasympathetic: travel in cranial nerves and pelvic splanchnic nerves.<br>D. Preganglionic sympathetic: enter the chain through the white ramus (myelinated).<br>E. Somatic motor axons: stay in the spinal nerve.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 11 (Course of Sympathetic fibers)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"combined clickers for the final: abdomen/GI-embryo clickers only","sl":4,"f":"F2936"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"288"},{"id":"G112-17-1","s":"Match the structure to the appropriate location: 3. Pre-Ganglionic Sympathetic Fiber","o":["A. Brainstem","B. Enteric Ganglion","C. Gray Ramus","D. Prevertebral Ganglion","E. White Ramus"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"White Ramus","a":"E","why":"<b>E. White ramus.</b> Preganglionic sympathetic fibers leave the cord in the ventral root and enter the chain through the white ramus communicans. They are myelinated, so the ramus looks white, and it exists only at T1–L2 (thoracic 1 to lumbar 2).<br>A. Brainstem: holds preganglionic parasympathetic cell bodies.<br>B. Enteric ganglion: holds postganglionic parasympathetic cell bodies in the gut wall.<br>C. Gray ramus: carries unmyelinated postganglionic sympathetic fibers back to the spinal nerve.<br>D. Prevertebral ganglion: a cluster of postganglionic sympathetic cell bodies, where splanchnic preganglionic fibers end by synapsing.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 11 (Course of Sympathetic fibers)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown autonomic nervous system interactive 2026","sl":17,"f":"G112"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"289"},{"id":"G112-15-1","s":"Match the structure to the appropriate location: 1. Post-Ganglionic Sympathetic Fiber","o":["A. Brainstem","B. Enteric Ganglion","C. Gray Ramus","D. Prevertebral Ganglion","E. White Ramus"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Gray Ramus","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Gray ramus.</b> Postganglionic sympathetic fibers leave the chain ganglion through the gray ramus communicans and rejoin the spinal nerve. They are unmyelinated, so the ramus looks gray, and every spinal nerve has one.<br>A. Brainstem: holds preganglionic parasympathetic cell bodies.<br>B. Enteric ganglion: holds postganglionic parasympathetic cell bodies in the gut wall.<br>D. Prevertebral ganglion: holds postganglionic sympathetic cell bodies, where such fibers start, not the path they run in.<br>E. White ramus: carries the myelinated preganglionic sympathetic fibers into the chain.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 11 (Course of Sympathetic fibers)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown autonomic nervous system interactive 2026","sl":15,"f":"G112"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"290"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G6","u":80,"label":"Horner syndrome: broken sympathetic path to head","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G008-17.jpg","cap":"T1–T2 fibers climb to superior cervical ganglion; carotid plexus to eye, eyelid","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 17","cu":"../sl/G008-17c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G008-18.jpg","cap":"Horner syndrome: ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis from cervical chain lesion or Pancoast tumor","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 18","cu":"../sl/G008-18c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/G1_45.jpg","cap":"T1 fibers exit by the anterior root and white ramus, then climb the trunk to the head; a break causes Horner syndrome.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 1.45","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/N206.jpg","cap":"Find the cervicothoracic (stellate) ganglion at the top of the chain beside the first rib, near the lung apex.","where":"Netter Atlas 6e, Plate 206","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F2936-429-1","s":"A 45-year-old woman is admitted to the hospital with severe dyspnea. Radiographic examination confirms the presence of a Pancoast tumor. Physical examination reveals that the patient has miosis of the pupil, partial ptosis of the eyelid, and anhydrosis of the face. Which of the following structures has most likely been injured?","o":["A. Sympathetic chain","B. Vagus nerve","C. Phrenic nerve","D. Arch of aorta","E. Cardiopulmonary plexus"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Sympathetic chain","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Sympathetic chain.</b> Miosis (small pupil), partial ptosis (droopy upper lid) and anhidrosis (no sweating) of the face are Horner syndrome: loss of sympathetic supply to the head. A Pancoast tumor at the lung apex presses on the cervicothoracic sympathetic chain.<br>B. Vagus nerve: parasympathetic; injury causes hoarseness, not Horner.<br>C. Phrenic nerve: paralyzes the diaphragm.<br>D. Arch of aorta: an artery, not a nerve.<br>E. Cardiopulmonary plexus: goes to heart and lungs, not the face.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 18 (Horner’s Syndrome)","ANS slide 17 (Sympathetic Innervation of the Head and Neck)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown autonomic nervous system interactive 2026","sl":23,"f":"G112"},{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"combined clickers for the final: abdomen/GI-embryo clickers only","sl":429,"f":"F2936"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"291"},{"id":"F2936-12-1","s":"A 45-year-old man presents with loss of sympathetic innervations to the left side of his head, characteristic of Horner syndrome. This could occur due to damage of which of the following structure?","o":["A. Cervical anterior roots","B. Thoracic posterior rami","C. Thoracic posterior roots","D. Thoracic anterior roots","E. Thoracic posterior rootlets"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Thoracic anterior roots","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Thoracic anterior (ventral) roots.</b> Sympathetic fibers to the head start in the lateral horn of thoracic 1 to 2, leave through the anterior roots, then climb the chain to the superior cervical ganglion. Cutting those roots gives Horner syndrome.<br>A. Cervical anterior roots: the cervical cord has no sympathetic outflow.<br>B. Thoracic posterior rami: carry postganglionic fibers to the back, not the head.<br>C. Thoracic posterior roots and E. posterior rootlets: sensory fibers only.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 11 (Course of Sympathetic fibers)","ANS slide 17 (Sympathetic Innervation of the Head and Neck)","ANS slide 18 (Horner’s Syndrome)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"combined clickers for the final: abdomen/GI-embryo clickers only","sl":12,"f":"F2936"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"292"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G6","u":81,"label":"Thoracic splanchnics carry preganglionic sympathetic fibers","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G008-27.jpg","cap":"Table: greater T5–T9, lesser T10–T11, least T12, all presynaptic sympathetic","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 27"},{"src":"../sl/G008-14.jpg","cap":"Splanchnic diagram: abdominopelvic splanchnics preganglionic, synapse in prevertebral ganglia","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 14","cu":"../sl/G008-14c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/G4_125.jpg","cap":"Greater, lesser and least splanchnic nerves pass through the thoracic trunk without synapsing and reach the prevertebral plexus.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 4.125","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/G4_158.jpg","cap":"The greater splanchnic nerve carries preganglionic sympathetic fibers that synapse in the celiac ganglion.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 4.158","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F2936-426-1","s":"Lesser splanchnic","o":["A. Carry preganglionic sympathetic fibers","B. Carry post ganglionic sympathetic fibers","C. Carry preganglionic parasympathetic fibers","D. Carry postganglionic parasympathetic fibers"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Carry preganglionic sympathetic fibers","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Preganglionic sympathetic fibers.</b> The greater, lesser and least splanchnic nerves are preganglionic sympathetic fibers that pass through the chain without synapsing. The lesser splanchnic (thoracic 10 to 11) synapses in prevertebral ganglia (aorticorenal and superior mesenteric).<br>B. Postganglionic sympathetic: only the cardiopulmonary splanchnics are.<br>C. Preganglionic parasympathetic: that is the pelvic splanchnics (sacral 2 to 4).<br>D. Postganglionic parasympathetic: short fibers inside organ walls, not named nerves.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 13 (Splanchnic nerve: nerve that conveys innervation to and from the viscera of the )","ANS slide 14 (Splanchnic nerve: nerve that conveys innervation to and from the viscera of the )","ANS slide 27 (| Splanchnic Nerves | Autonomic Fiber Type | System | Origin | Destination |)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"combined clickers for the final: abdomen/GI-embryo clickers only","sl":426,"f":"F2936"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"293"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G6","u":81,"label":"Superior hypogastric plexus: in front of L5","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G008-23.jpg","cap":"Pelvic plexus diagram: superior hypogastric plexus at aortic bifurcation, hypogastric nerves","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 23","cu":"../sl/G008-23c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/G4_157.jpg","cap":"The superior hypogastric plexus lies below the aortic bifurcation, in front of the L5 vertebra and sacral promontory.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 4.157","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F2936-440-1","s":"This plexus is located anterior to the fifth lumbar vertebra and supplies pelvic viscera.","o":["A. Superior hypogastric plexus","B. Superior mesenteric plexus","C. Inferior mesenteric plexus","D. Renal plexus","E. Celiac plexus"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Superior hypogastric plexus","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Superior hypogastric plexus.</b> It lies at the aortic bifurcation, in front of the fifth lumbar vertebra and sacral promontory, and splits into right and left hypogastric nerves that run to the pelvis.<br>B. Superior mesenteric plexus: around the superior mesenteric artery at the first lumbar vertebra.<br>C. Inferior mesenteric plexus: around the inferior mesenteric artery at the third lumbar vertebra.<br>D. Renal plexus: around the renal arteries, first to second lumbar.<br>E. Celiac plexus: around the celiac trunk at the twelfth thoracic vertebra.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 23 (Parasympathetic Division)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown autonomic nervous system interactive 2026","sl":31,"f":"G112"},{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"combined clickers for the final: abdomen/GI-embryo clickers only","sl":440,"f":"F2936"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"294"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G6","u":82,"label":"Parasympathetic preganglionic cell bodies: brainstem and S2–S4","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G112-34.jpg","cap":"Parasympathetic preganglionic cell bodies: brainstem nuclei and lateral horn of S2–S4","where":"2026 Middletown interactive, slide 34","cu":"../sl/G112-34c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G008-5.jpg","cap":"Parasympathetic is craniosacral (cranial nerves III, VII, IX, X; S2–S4); sympathetic is T1–L2","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 5"},{"src":"../tb/G1_48.jpg","cap":"Parasympathetic preganglionic cell bodies are in the brainstem (cranial nerves III, VII, IX, X) and sacral cord S2 to S4.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 1.48","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F2936-447-1","s":"Gray matter of S2-S4","o":["A. Cell bodies of the presynaptic neurons of the sympathetic nervous system","B. Cell bodies of the presynaptic neurons of the parasympathetic neurons","C. Cell bodies of the postynaptic neurons of the sympathetic nervous system","D. Cell bodies of the potsynaptic neurons of the parasympathetic nervous system"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Cell bodies of the presynaptic neurons of the parasympathetic neurons","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Cell bodies of presynaptic (preganglionic) parasympathetic neurons.</b> Parasympathetic outflow is craniosacral: brainstem nuclei plus the lateral horn of sacral 2 to 4.<br>A. Presynaptic sympathetic: lateral horn of thoracic 1 to lumbar 2.<br>C. Postsynaptic sympathetic: paravertebral and prevertebral ganglia.<br>D. Postsynaptic parasympathetic: small ganglia in or next to the organs, and the 4 head ganglia.<br>Rule: a cell body inside the spinal cord is always presynaptic.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 20 (Parasympathetic Division)","ANS slide 26 (Summary)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown autonomic nervous system interactive 2026","sl":33,"f":"G112"},{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"combined clickers for the final: abdomen/GI-embryo clickers only","sl":447,"f":"F2936"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"295"},{"id":"F2936-8-1","s":"Which of the following best describes the parasympathetic nervous system?","o":["A. It is part of the autonomic nervous system that is mainly active during stress related activity","B. Its preganglionic axons are located in the white rami communicans","C. Parts of its preganglionic neurons are located in the sacral segments of the spinal cord","D. Some of its postganglionic neurons are located in paravertebral ganglia","E. The ganglia that are located in front of the vertebral column are parasympathetic in function"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Parts of its preganglionic neurons are located in the sacral segments of the spinal cord","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Part of its preganglionic neurons lie in the sacral spinal cord.</b> Parasympathetic outflow is craniosacral: cranial nerves III, VII, IX, X (oculomotor, facial, glossopharyngeal, vagus) and sacral 2 to 4.<br>A. Active in stress: sympathetic ('fight or flight').<br>B. Axons in the white rami: those are sympathetic preganglionic fibers.<br>D. Neurons in paravertebral ganglia: the sympathetic chain.<br>E. Ganglia in front of the vertebral column (prevertebral: celiac, mesenteric): sympathetic.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 20 (Parasympathetic Division)","ANS slide 5 (Divisions of the ANS)","ANS slide 26 (Summary)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"combined clickers for the final: abdomen/GI-embryo clickers only","sl":8,"f":"F2936"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"296"},{"id":"G112-11-3","s":"Where do preganglionic parasympathetic neurons originate (the craniosacral outflow)?","o":["A. Brainstem nuclei of CN III, VII, IX and X, and the lateral horn of S2-S4","B. Brainstem nuclei of CN III, V, VII and X, and the lateral horn of S1-S3","C. The lateral horn of T1-L2","D. Brainstem nuclei of CN III, VII, IX and X, and the lateral horn of L1-L2","E. Brainstem nuclei of CN V, VII, IX and XI, and the dorsal horn of S2-S4"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Brainstem nuclei of CN III, VII, IX and X, and the lateral horn of S2-S4","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Brainstem nuclei of cranial nerves III, VII, IX and X, and the lateral horn of S2–S4 (sacral 2 to 4).</b> That is why the parasympathetic division is called craniosacral.<br>B. Cranial nerve V and S1–S3: the trigeminal nerve has no parasympathetic nucleus, and the sacral levels are 2 to 4.<br>C. Lateral horn of T1–L2 (thoracic 1 to lumbar 2): the sympathetic, thoracolumbar outflow.<br>D. Lateral horn of L1–L2: the lumbar cord is sympathetic; the sacral outflow is S2–S4.<br>E. Cranial nerves V and XI and the dorsal horn: neither nerve is parasympathetic, and the dorsal horn is sensory.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 5 (Divisions of the ANS)","ANS slide 20 (Parasympathetic Division)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown autonomic nervous system interactive 2026","sl":11,"f":"G112"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"297"},{"id":"G112-18-1","s":"Match the structure to the appropriate location: 4. Pre-Ganglionic Parasympathetic Cell Body","o":["A. Brainstem","B. Enteric Ganglion","C. Gray Ramus","D. Prevertebral Ganglion","E. White Ramus"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Brainstem","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Brainstem.</b> Preganglionic parasympathetic cell bodies lie in the brainstem nuclei of cranial nerves III, VII, IX and X, and in the lateral horn of sacral 2 to 4. Of the choices, only the brainstem fits.<br>B. Enteric ganglion: postganglionic parasympathetic cell bodies in the gut wall.<br>C. Gray ramus: postganglionic sympathetic fibers, no cell bodies.<br>D. Prevertebral ganglion: postganglionic sympathetic cell bodies.<br>E. White ramus: preganglionic sympathetic fibers, no cell bodies.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 20 (Parasympathetic Division)","ANS slide 26 (Summary)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown autonomic nervous system interactive 2026","sl":18,"f":"G112"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"298"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G6","u":82,"label":"Vagus supplies the gut to the left colic flexure","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G112-10.jpg","cap":"Vagus supplies the gut to the end of the midgut; S2–S4 supply the hindgut","where":"2026 Middletown interactive, slide 10"}],"q":[{"id":"G112-11-6","s":"How far along the GI tract does the vagus nerve supply preganglionic parasympathetic fibers?","o":["A. To the pylorus","B. To the duodenojejunal flexure","C. To the ileocecal junction","D. To the left colic (splenic) flexure","E. To the rectosigmoid junction"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"To the left colic (splenic) flexure","a":"D","why":"<b>D. To the left colic (splenic) flexure.</b> The vagus supplies the gut to the end of the midgut, at the left colic flexure. Beyond it, the descending and sigmoid colon and the rectum get the pelvic splanchnic nerves (sacral 2 to 4).<br>A. Pylorus: too short; the vagus also supplies the small intestine.<br>B. Duodenojejunal flexure: too short; the jejunum and ileum get vagal fibers.<br>C. Ileocecal junction: too short; the cecum and the ascending and transverse colon are midgut, still vagal.<br>E. Rectosigmoid junction: too far; the sigmoid colon is hindgut, supplied by the pelvic splanchnics.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 20 (Parasympathetic Division)","ANS slide 21 (CN III – innervate the eye, dilate the pupil)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown autonomic nervous system interactive 2026","sl":11,"f":"G112"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"299"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G6","u":82,"label":"Vagal preganglionics reach gut; postganglionics in wall","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G008-20.jpg","cap":"Vagus sends preganglionic fibers to gut up to left colic flexure; synapse in walls","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 20","cu":"../sl/G008-20c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G005-54.jpg","cap":"Small bowel nerves: parasympathetic to jejunum and ileum from posterior vagal trunk","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 54","cu":"../sl/G005-54c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/G4_158.jpg","cap":"The vagus carries preganglionic parasympathetic fibers straight to enteric neurons in the gut wall, where they synapse.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 4.158","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"G112-11-5","s":"Vagal preganglionic fibers to the stomach pass through the celiac plexus. Where do they synapse?","o":["A. Celiac ganglion","B. Sympathetic chain ganglia at T5-T9","C. Ganglia in the wall of the stomach (myenteric and submucosal plexuses)","D. Otic ganglion","E. Inferior (nodose) ganglion of the vagus"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Ganglia in the wall of the stomach (myenteric and submucosal plexuses)","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Ganglia in the wall of the stomach (myenteric and submucosal plexuses).</b> Vagal fibers are long preganglionic fibers. They pass through the celiac plexus without synapsing and synapse on tiny ganglia inside the organ wall.<br>A. Celiac ganglion: a sympathetic prevertebral ganglion, where greater splanchnic fibers synapse.<br>B. Chain ganglia at T5–T9 (thoracic 5 to 9): sympathetic; vagal fibers never enter the chain.<br>D. Otic ganglion: the parasympathetic ganglion of the glossopharyngeal nerve (cranial nerve IX), for the parotid gland.<br>E. Inferior (nodose) ganglion of the vagus: holds sensory cell bodies; no synapse occurs there.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 20 (Parasympathetic Division)","ANS slide 8 (Pre/Postganglionic system)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown autonomic nervous system interactive 2026","sl":11,"f":"G112"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"300"},{"id":"G112-16-1","s":"Match the structure to the appropriate location: 2. Post-Ganglionic Parasympathetic Cell Body","o":["A. Brainstem","B. Enteric Ganglion","C. Gray Ramus","D. Prevertebral Ganglion","E. White Ramus"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Enteric Ganglion","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Enteric ganglion.</b> Vagal and pelvic splanchnic preganglionic fibers synapse on postganglionic parasympathetic neurons whose cell bodies sit in tiny ganglia in the gut wall (the myenteric and submucosal plexuses). Their fibers are very short.<br>A. Brainstem: holds preganglionic parasympathetic cell bodies.<br>C. Gray ramus: a bundle of postganglionic sympathetic fibers; parasympathetic fibers never use the rami.<br>D. Prevertebral ganglion: holds postganglionic sympathetic cell bodies.<br>E. White ramus: a bundle of preganglionic sympathetic fibers.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 20 (Parasympathetic Division)","ANS slide 8 (Pre/Postganglionic system)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown autonomic nervous system interactive 2026","sl":16,"f":"G112"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"301"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G6","u":82,"label":"Vagus constricts bronchial smooth muscle","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G008-22.jpg","cap":"Vagus joins sympathetic fibers to form cardiac, pulmonary and esophageal plexuses","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 22"},{"src":"../sl/G008-28.jpg","cap":"Effects table, lungs row: parasympathetic constricts bronchi, sympathetic dilates them","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 28"},{"src":"../tb/N206.jpg","cap":"Follow the vagus nerve branches into the pulmonary plexuses around the main bronchi; these constrict bronchial smooth muscle.","where":"Netter Atlas 6e, Plate 206","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F2936-433-1","s":"A 17-year-old girl is admitted to the hospital with severe dyspnea. Physical examination reveals that the patient is suffering from an asthma attack, with associated bronchospasm. Which of the following nerves is responsible for the innervation of the bronchial smooth muscle cells?","o":["A. Greater thoracic splanchnic","B. Phrenic","C. Vagus","D. Intercostal","E. Lesser thoracic splanchnic"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Vagus","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Vagus.</b> Bronchial smooth muscle gets parasympathetic fibers from the vagus through the pulmonary plexus. Vagal action constricts the bronchi, as in the bronchospasm of asthma; sympathetic action dilates them.<br>A. Greater thoracic splanchnic and E. lesser thoracic splanchnic: go to the abdomen (celiac and superior mesenteric ganglia).<br>B. Phrenic: motor nerve to the diaphragm.<br>D. Intercostal: chest wall muscles and skin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 24 (Parasympathetic Division)","ANS slide 28 (| Organ, Tract, or System |  | Effect of Sympathetic Stimulation | Effect of Par)","ANS slide 22 (Will provide preganglionic parasympathetic fibers to plexi around the viscera up)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown autonomic nervous system interactive 2026","sl":25,"f":"G112"},{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"combined clickers for the final: abdomen/GI-embryo clickers only","sl":433,"f":"F2936"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"302"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G6","u":83,"label":"Pelvic splanchnics: S2–S4 preganglionic parasympathetic","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G008-23.jpg","cap":"S2–S4 pelvic splanchnics join inferior hypogastric plexus; gut beyond left colic flexure","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 23","cu":"../sl/G008-23c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G008-27.jpg","cap":"Splanchnic table: pelvic splanchnics presynaptic parasympathetic from S2–S4 anterior rami","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 27"}],"q":[{"id":"F2936-443-1","s":"Pelvic splanchnics arising from levels ______ bring ______ to the inferior hypogastric plexus.","o":["A. S2-S4, preganglionic parasympathetic fibers","B. S1-S2, postganglionic sympathetic fibers","C. S3-S4, preganglionic sympatehtic fibers","D. T1-L2/L3, postganglionic sympathetic fibers","E. T1-L2/L3, preganglionic parasympathetic fibers"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"S2-S4, preganglionic parasympathetic fibers","a":"A","why":"<b>A. S2–S4 (sacral 2 to 4), preganglionic parasympathetic fibers.</b> Pelvic splanchnic nerves leave the anterior rami of sacral 2 to 4 and join the inferior hypogastric plexus, where they mix with sympathetic fibers.<br>B. Sacral 1 to 2, postganglionic sympathetic: wrong level and wrong type.<br>C. Sacral 3 to 4, preganglionic sympathetic: pelvic splanchnics are never sympathetic (the similar-named sacral splanchnics are).<br>D and E. Thoracic 1 to lumbar 2 or 3: sympathetic outflow, which reaches the plexus through the hypogastric nerves.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 23 (Parasympathetic Division)","ANS slide 27 (| Splanchnic Nerves | Autonomic Fiber Type | System | Origin | Destination |)","ANS slide 13 (Splanchnic nerve: nerve that conveys innervation to and from the viscera of the )"],"src":[{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"combined clickers for the final: abdomen/GI-embryo clickers only","sl":443,"f":"F2936"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"303"},{"id":"G112-11-4","s":"Preganglionic parasympathetic fibers from S2-S4 run in the pelvic splanchnic nerves and join the hypogastric nerves to form which mixed plexus?","o":["A. Superior hypogastric plexus","B. Inferior hypogastric plexus","C. Inferior mesenteric plexus","D. Celiac plexus","E. Sacral plexus"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Inferior hypogastric plexus","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Inferior hypogastric plexus.</b> Pelvic splanchnic nerves (preganglionic parasympathetic, sacral 2 to 4) meet the hypogastric nerves (postganglionic sympathetic) beside the rectum and bladder and form this mixed pelvic plexus.<br>A. Superior hypogastric plexus: the sympathetic plexus in front of the fifth lumbar vertebra (L5); it sends the hypogastric nerves down.<br>C. Inferior mesenteric plexus: sympathetic, around the inferior mesenteric artery at the third lumbar vertebra.<br>D. Celiac plexus: around the celiac trunk at the twelfth thoracic vertebra; its parasympathetic fibers are vagal.<br>E. Sacral plexus: the somatic plexus that forms the sciatic and pudendal nerves.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 23 (Parasympathetic Division)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown autonomic nervous system interactive 2026","sl":11,"f":"G112"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"304"},{"id":"D042-188-1","s":"A pelvic fracture injures pelvic splanchnic nerves. Which of the following GI functions would most likely be impaired?","o":["A. Peristalsis of the jejunum","B. Peristalsis of the descending colon","C. Contraction of the internal anal sphincter","D. Secretion of digestive enzymes in the stomach","E. Vasoconstriction of colonic blood vessels"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Peristalsis of the descending colon","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Peristalsis of the descending colon.</b> Pelvic splanchnic nerves give parasympathetic supply to the gut beyond the left colic (splenic) flexure, so losing them weakens descending colon motility.<br>A. Jejunum: parasympathetic supply from the vagus.<br>C. Contraction of the internal anal sphincter: done by sympathetic fibers; parasympathetic fibers relax it.<br>D. Stomach enzyme secretion: vagus.<br>E. Vasoconstriction of colon vessels: sympathetic.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 23 (Parasympathetic Division)","ANS slide 28 (| Organ, Tract, or System |  | Effect of Sympathetic Stimulation | Effect of Par)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown autonomic nervous system interactive 2026","sl":11,"f":"G112"},{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":188,"f":"D042"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"305"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G6","u":84,"label":"Parasympathetic = rest and digest; sympathetic = fight or flight","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G112-3.jpg","cap":"Autonomic nerves drive smooth muscle, heart and glands: fight-or-flight or rest-and-digest","where":"2026 Middletown interactive, slide 3"},{"src":"../sl/G008-5.jpg","cap":"Parasympathetic: rest and digest, craniosacral. Sympathetic: fight or flight, T1–L2","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 5"}],"q":[{"id":"G112-11-1","s":"Which phrase best describes the function of the parasympathetic division?","o":["A. Fight or flight: it prepares the body for stress and exertion","B. Rest and digest: it conserves energy and promotes digestion","C. Voluntary control of skeletal muscle, skin and joints","D. A body-wide response through release of adrenaline from the suprarenal medulla","E. Constriction of all blood vessels and stimulation of sweat glands"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Rest and digest: it conserves energy and promotes digestion","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Rest and digest: it conserves energy and promotes digestion.</b> The parasympathetic division slows the heart, constricts the pupil and speeds up peristalsis and digestive secretion.<br>A. Fight or flight: the sympathetic division.<br>C. Voluntary control of skeletal muscle, skin and joints: the somatic nervous system.<br>D. Body-wide adrenaline from the suprarenal (adrenal) medulla: sympathetic; parasympathetic fibers never reach the adrenal medulla.<br>E. Vessel constriction and sweating: sympathetic jobs; only sympathetic fibers reach vessel walls and sweat glands.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 5 (Divisions of the ANS)","ANS slide 24 (Parasympathetic Division)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown autonomic nervous system interactive 2026","sl":11,"f":"G112"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"306"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G6","u":84,"label":"Sympathetic vs parasympathetic effects on organs","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G008-28.jpg","cap":"Effects table: sympathetic vs parasympathetic on eye, heart, lungs, gut, bladder","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 28"},{"src":"../sl/G008-16.jpg","cap":"Sympathetic actions: dilates pupils, bronchodilation, contracts sphincters, sweating","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 16"},{"src":"../sl/G008-24.jpg","cap":"Parasympathetic actions: constricts pupil, slows heart, constricts bronchi, increases peristalsis","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 24","cu":"../sl/G008-24c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"F2936-14-1","s":"Stimulation of parasympathetic nervous system produces which of the following action","o":["A. Dilatation of pupil","B. Constriction of anal sphinctor","C. Decrease gastric enzymes secretions","D. Decrease heart rate","E. Decrease gastrointestinal motility"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Decrease heart rate","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Decrease heart rate.</b> The parasympathetic system ('rest and digest') slows the heart through the vagus nerve.<br>A. Dilation of the pupil: sympathetic; parasympathetic constricts the pupil.<br>B. Constriction of the anal sphincter: sympathetic fibers contract the internal sphincter; parasympathetic fibers relax it for defecation.<br>C. Less gastric enzyme secretion: sympathetic; parasympathetic increases it.<br>E. Less gut motility: sympathetic; parasympathetic increases peristalsis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 24 (Parasympathetic Division)","ANS slide 28 (| Organ, Tract, or System |  | Effect of Sympathetic Stimulation | Effect of Par)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown autonomic nervous system interactive 2026","sl":11,"f":"G112"},{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"combined clickers for the final: abdomen/GI-embryo clickers only","sl":14,"f":"F2936"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"307"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G6","u":84,"label":"Norepinephrine at postganglionic sympathetic endings","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G008-26.jpg","cap":"Summary: sympathetic postganglionic ending releases norepinephrine, except sweat glands","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 26"},{"src":"../sl/G008-16.jpg","cap":"Transmitters: acetylcholine at ganglia, norepinephrine at sympathetic targets","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 16"}],"q":[{"id":"F2936-10-1","s":"A research scientist discovers a drug that selectively blocks the release of norepinephrine (NE) from nerve endings. At which of the following sites would this drug have the greatest effect on normal synaptic transmission?","o":["A. Preganglionic parasympathetic nerve terminals","B. Preganglionic sympathetic nerve terminals","C. Postganglionic parasympathetic nerve terminals","D. Postganglionic sympathetic nerve terminals","E. None"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Postganglionic sympathetic nerve terminals","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Postganglionic sympathetic nerve terminals.</b> These endings release NE (norepinephrine) onto the target organ, so blocking NE release silences them.<br>A. Preganglionic parasympathetic and B. preganglionic sympathetic terminals: both release ACh (acetylcholine) onto the ganglion cell.<br>C. Postganglionic parasympathetic terminals: release ACh.<br>E. None: wrong, because sympathetic postganglionic endings depend on NE. (Exception: sympathetic fibers to sweat glands release ACh.)","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 16 (Sympathetic Division)","ANS slide 26 (Summary)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"combined clickers for the final: abdomen/GI-embryo clickers only","sl":10,"f":"F2936"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"308"},{"id":"F2936-435-1","s":"A research scientist at a pharmaceutical company discovers a new drug that selectively blocks the release of norepinephrine from nerve endings. At which of the following sites would this drug have the greatest effect on normal synaptic transmission?","o":["A. Somatic neuron motor end plates","B. Postsynaptic parasympathetic nerve terminals","C. Postsynaptic sympathetic nerve terminals","D. Synapses between presynaptic and postsynaptic parasympathetic neurons","E. Synapses between presynaptic and postsynaptic sympathetic neurons"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Postsynaptic sympathetic nerve terminals","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Postsynaptic (postganglionic) sympathetic nerve terminals.</b> They release NE (norepinephrine), so a drug that blocks NE release hits them.<br>A. Somatic motor end plates: release ACh (acetylcholine) onto skeletal muscle.<br>B. Postsynaptic parasympathetic terminals: release ACh.<br>D and E. Synapses between pre- and postsynaptic neurons, in both divisions: the preganglionic fiber releases ACh in every autonomic ganglion.<br>Exception: sympathetic fibers to sweat glands release ACh, not NE.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 16 (Sympathetic Division)","ANS slide 26 (Summary)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown autonomic nervous system interactive 2026","sl":27,"f":"G112"},{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"combined clickers for the final: abdomen/GI-embryo clickers only","sl":435,"f":"F2936"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"309"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":85,"label":"Right ventricle is the most anterior chamber","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G009-13.jpg","cap":"Lateral chest film outline: right ventricle forms anterior border behind sternum","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Thorax, slide 13","cu":"../sl/G009-13c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G009-66.jpg","cap":"Axial CT: right ventricle, the most anterior chamber behind the sternum","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Thorax, slide 66","cu":"../sl/G009-66c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/G3_60.jpg","cap":"On the lateral film, the right ventricle forms the front border behind the sternum; the left atrium is behind.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 3.60","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/G3_66.jpg","cap":"On axial computed tomography, the right ventricle is the chamber just behind the sternum, in front of everything else.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 3.66","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F0008-150-1","s":"The most anterior aspect of the heart on a lateral chest X-Ray is:","o":["A. Right Atrium","B. Right Ventricle","C. Left Atrium","D. Left Ventricle","E. If you had a heart, you wouldn’t ask…"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Right Ventricle","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Right ventricle.</b> On a lateral chest film, the front (anterior) heart border, right behind the sternum, is the right ventricle.<br>A. Right atrium: forms the right heart border on the frontal film.<br>C. Left atrium: forms the upper back border on the lateral film.<br>D. Left ventricle: forms the lower back border on the lateral film and the left border on the frontal film.<br>E. A joke option.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Lateral chest radiograph shown beside the question; not needed to answer.","tg":["Radiology Thorax slide 13 (Chest Radiography)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":150,"f":"F0008"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"310"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":85,"label":"Counting posterior ribs on the chest film","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G084-5.jpg","cap":"PA film: posterior ribs numbered beside the spine, anterior ribs out laterally","where":"2026 Middletown interactive, slide 5"}],"q":[{"id":"G084-4-1","s":"Which Structure are the red arrows pointing at?","o":["A. 4th Rib","B. 5th Rib","C. 6th Rib","D. The heart","E. Right Lower Lobe"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"6th Rib","a":"C","why":"<b>C. 6th rib.</b> The arrows mark the back (posterior) part of a rib, the part that leaves the spine and runs almost flat outward. Posterior ribs are the clearest to count: start with the first rib beside the spine and count down; this is the sixth.<br>A. 4th rib: two ribs higher.<br>B. 5th rib: the rib just above.<br>D. The heart: the broad soft-tissue shadow in the middle, not a thin white bar of bone.<br>E. Right lower lobe: lung, which looks black with air; the arrows point at bone.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G084-4.jpg","in":"PA chest X-ray; three red arrows point at one posterior rib in the right mid-lung field (the posterior 6th rib).","tg":["Radiology Thorax slide 10 (Chest Radiography)","Radiology Thorax slide 11 (Chest Radiography)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown GI radiology interactive 2026","sl":4,"f":"G084"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"311"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":85,"label":"Mitral (bicuspid) valve is heard at the apex","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G084-10.jpg","cap":"Listening points: aortic and pulmonary at upper sternal borders, mitral at apex","where":"2026 Middletown interactive, slide 10"},{"src":"../sl/G084-9.jpg","cap":"Where murmurs are loudest: mitral stenosis at the apex, aortic stenosis right upper sternum","where":"2026 Middletown interactive, slide 9"}],"q":[{"id":"G084-8-1","s":"A 57-year-old male complains of chest pains when he goes jogging. During his physical exam, you detect a murmur and suspect Bicuspid valve stenosis. Where did you place your stethoscope to detect this murmur?","o":["A. Red","B. Green","C. Purple","D. Blue","E. Yellow"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Yellow (cardiac apex, left 5th intercostal space at the midclavicular line)","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Yellow (the apex).</b> The bicuspid valve is the mitral valve, the two-cusp valve between the left atrium and left ventricle. It is heard best at the apex, the left fifth intercostal space at the midclavicular line, so the murmur of mitral stenosis is loudest there.<br>A. Red: right upper sternal border, the aortic area (a congenitally bicuspid aortic valve would be heard here).<br>B. Green: left upper sternal border, the pulmonary area.<br>C. Purple: upper lateral left chest, away from the valve areas.<br>D. Blue: lower sternal border, the tricuspid area.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G084-8.jpg","in":"PA chest X-ray with five coloured dots: A red at the right hilum/right parasternal area, B green at the left upper sternal border, C purple in the left upper lateral chest, D blue over the lower sternum, E yellow over the cardiac apex (left midclavicular line).","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown GI radiology interactive 2026","sl":8,"f":"G084"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"312"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":86,"label":"Thoracic inlet structures on axial CT","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G009-25.jpg","cap":"Axial CT at thoracic inlet: trachea, black air-filled tube in the midline","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Thorax, slide 25","cu":"../sl/G009-25c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G009-30.jpg","cap":"Axial CT: brachiocephalic trunk, in front and right of the trachea","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Thorax, slide 30","cu":"../sl/G009-30c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G009-32.jpg","cap":"Axial CT: left subclavian artery, left of and behind the trachea","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Thorax, slide 32","cu":"../sl/G009-32c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/G3_85.jpg","cap":"The trachea is the black air-filled circle in the midline, with the esophagus just behind it.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 3.85","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"G084-18-1","s":"35yo Male farmer presents to the ER with bouts of uncontrollable vomiting and coughing. The attending believes he may have aspirated some food while eating. The attending physician requests that you perform a diagnostic bronchoscope procedure. Which structure would you insert the camera?","o":["A. A","B. B","C. C","D. D","E. E","F. None of the above"],"g":["F"],"k":["F"],"rv":false,"at":"None of the above (the trachea is unlabelled)","a":"F","why":"<b>F. None of the above.</b> A bronchoscope passes through the larynx into the trachea, the black air-filled ring in the midline, and the trachea carries no letter here.<br>A. Brachiocephalic trunk, in front of the trachea.<br>B. Left common carotid artery, left of the trachea.<br>C. Left subclavian artery, furthest left and back.<br>D. Right brachiocephalic vein, at the front on the patient's right.<br>E. Esophagus, the small tube behind the trachea; an endoscope for the gut goes here, not a bronchoscope.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G084-18.jpg","in":"Axial CT of the upper mediastinum (above the aortic arch): A brachiocephalic trunk, B left common carotid artery, C left subclavian artery, D right brachiocephalic vein, E esophagus; the air-filled trachea is not lettered.","tg":["Radiology Thorax slide 25 (CT of the chest: Axial)","Radiology Thorax slide 30 (CT of the chest: Axial)","Radiology Thorax slide 65 (CT of the chest: Axial)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown GI radiology interactive 2026","sl":18,"f":"G084"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"313"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":86,"label":"Inhaled object → right main bronchus","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G009-20.jpg","cap":"Coronal CT: right mainstem bronchus, short, wide and steep","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Thorax, slide 20"},{"src":"../sl/G009-19.jpg","cap":"Coronal CT: left mainstem bronchus, longer and more horizontal","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Thorax, slide 19"}],"q":[{"id":"F0008-152-1","s":"A 7 year old boy comes into the ER choking. Mom says her son was seen playing with army men just prior to the choking fit. Where is the most likely place the toy is stuck?","o":["A. Oropharynx","B. Trachea","C. Right main stem bronchus","D. Left main stem bronchus","E. Esophagus"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Right main stem bronchus","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Right main bronchus.</b> It is wider, shorter and more vertical than the left, so inhaled objects usually drop into it.<br>A. Oropharynx: small objects pass through it into the larynx and trachea.<br>B. Trachea: an object small enough to pass the larynx usually continues into a bronchus.<br>D. Left main bronchus: longer, narrower and more horizontal, pushed sideways by the aortic arch and heart.<br>E. Esophagus: swallowed, not inhaled, objects go there; they cause trouble swallowing, not choking.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Radiology Thorax slide 20 (CT of the chest: Coronal)","Radiology Thorax slide 19 (CT of the chest: Coronal)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":152,"f":"F0008"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"314"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":86,"label":"Right middle lobe pneumonia blurs the right heart border","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G084-14.jpg","cap":"Lung lobes on PA and lateral films: right middle lobe touches the heart","where":"2026 Middletown interactive, slide 14"},{"src":"../sl/G009-76.jpg","cap":"Consolidation: a dense white patch that hides the vessels passing through it","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Thorax, slide 76"}],"q":[{"id":"G084-12-1","s":"A 4-year-old female is brought to the pediatrician after having trouble breathing for the last 2 weeks. Upon examining the child and performing radiographic analysis, the pediatrician arrives at a diagnosis of?","o":["A. Pneumothorax","B. Right Middle Lobe Pneumonia","C. Tuberculosis","D. Fractures of left T3-4 ribs","E. Ascending Aorta Aneurysm"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Right Middle Lobe Pneumonia","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Right middle lobe pneumonia.</b> A dense, patchy white consolidation fills the lower right lung beside the heart and blurs the right heart border. The middle lobe touches the right atrium, so its consolidation erases that border (the silhouette sign).<br>A. Pneumothorax: a black space with no lung markings, not a white patch.<br>C. Tuberculosis: usually upper-lobe disease, often with cavities.<br>D. Fractures of left ribs 3 and 4: the ribs look intact, and a fracture does not whiten the lung.<br>E. Ascending aorta aneurysm: widens the upper right mediastinum, not the lower lung.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G084-12.jpg","in":"Pediatric PA chest X-ray: patchy opacity in the right lower zone against the right heart border (right middle lobe consolidation, right heart border silhouetted).","tg":["Radiology Thorax slide 72 (Types of Opacities)","Radiology Thorax slide 76 (Consolidative opacity)","Radiology Thorax slide 60 (CT of the chest: Axial)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown GI radiology interactive 2026","sl":12,"f":"G084"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"315"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":87,"label":"Superior vena cava sits right of ascending aorta","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G009-45.jpg","cap":"Axial CT: ascending aorta; round vessel just to its right is superior vena cava","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Thorax, slide 45","cu":"../sl/G009-45c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G009-11.jpg","cap":"Frontal chest film outline labels the superior vena cava on the upper right","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Thorax, slide 11","cu":"../sl/G009-11c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/N237.jpg","cap":"In the top scan, the superior vena cava is the round vessel just right of and behind the ascending aorta.","where":"Netter Atlas 6e, Plate 237","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"G084-21-1","s":"71yo female comes into her PCP office with a chief complaint of edema in her upper extremities and overall fatigue. She denies any trauma or illnesses but notes both her arms are filled with “water”. Her PCP sends her out for a CT scan a find a blockage here?","o":["A. Yellow","B. Purple","C. Blue","D. Green","E. Red"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Purple (superior vena cava)","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Purple: superior vena cava.</b> Swelling of both arms points to a block in the vein that drains both of them: the superior vena cava, which receives both brachiocephalic veins (a block in one brachiocephalic vein swells only one arm). It is the smaller round vessel on the patient's right of the ascending aorta.<br>A. Yellow: ascending aorta, the large round artery in front.<br>C. Blue: trachea, black with air.<br>D. Green: descending aorta, left of the spine.<br>E. Red: vertebral body.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G084-21.jpg","in":"Axial contrast CT at the level of the ascending aorta: A yellow ascending aorta, B purple superior vena cava, C blue trachea, D green descending aorta, E red vertebral body.","tg":["Radiology Thorax slide 45 (CT of the chest: Axial)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown GI radiology interactive 2026","sl":21,"f":"G084"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"316"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":88,"label":"Left pulmonary vein enters left atrium; emboli lodge in arteries","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G084-25.jpg","cap":"Axial CT: left pulmonary vein entering the left atrium, beside the descending aorta","where":"2026 Middletown interactive, slide 25"},{"src":"../sl/G009-52.jpg","cap":"Massive pulmonary embolus: arrows on dark clots inside the pulmonary arteries","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Thorax, slide 52"}],"q":[{"id":"G084-24-1","s":"On this axial CT at the level of the heart, which colored dot marks the left pulmonary vein entering the left atrium?","o":["A. Blue","B. Yellow","C. Green","D. Purple","E. Red"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Green","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Green: left pulmonary vein.</b> It is the small vessel entering the back of the left atrium on the patient's left, just in front of the descending aorta, carrying oxygenated blood from the left lung. A pulmonary embolus (a leg clot after a long flight) passes through the right heart and lodges in the pulmonary arteries, never in a pulmonary vein.<br>A. Blue: left ventricle, the thick-walled chamber running to the apex.<br>B. Yellow: descending thoracic aorta, round, beside the spine.<br>D. Purple: right atrium, the right heart edge.<br>E. Red: right lung, black with air, not a vessel.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G084-24.jpg","in":"Axial CT through the heart and left atrium level: A blue left ventricle, B yellow descending thoracic aorta, C green left pulmonary vein (just left-anterior of the aorta), D purple right atrium, E red right lung. On repeat slide 26 the blue dot is on the sternum and the red dot on the right ventricle.","tg":["Radiology Thorax slide 50 (CT: PE Protocol)","Radiology Thorax slide 51 (Massive Pulmonary Embolus)","Radiology Thorax slide 48 (CT of the chest: Axial)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown GI radiology interactive 2026","sl":24,"f":"G084"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"317"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":89,"label":"Spleen: posterolateral left upper quadrant","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G010-62.jpg","cap":"Axial CT: star on the spleen, far left behind the stomach","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen I, slide 62","cu":"../sl/G010-62c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G007-22.jpg","cap":"Spleen in the left upper quadrant, against ribs 9 to 11","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 22"},{"src":"../tb/G4_99.jpg","cap":"The spleen is the solid organ at the patient's far left and back, against the ribs (image right).","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 4.99","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F0008-134-1","s":"In the following enhanced CT scan of the abdomen, the arrow points to the:","o":["A) spleen.","B) liver.","C) stomach.","D) left kidney.","E) inferior lobe of the left lung."],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"spleen","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Spleen.</b> The arrow points to the solid organ at the far left, posterolateral, behind the stomach and under ribs 9 to 11. In the left upper quadrant (image right), that is the spleen.<br>B. Liver: the large organ on the patient's right (image left).<br>C. Stomach: the contrast- and air-filled organ in the middle-left, in front of the spleen.<br>D. Left kidney: lower and closer to the spine.<br>E. Lower lobe of the left lung: black (air), above the diaphragm.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/F0008-134.jpg","in":"Axial contrast CT of the upper abdomen (liver on the right, contrast-filled stomach in the middle); blue arrow points to the solid organ at the far left posterolateral side behind the stomach. Slide 136 shows the same CT labeled (Spleen).","tg":["Pancreas & Spleen slide 28 (Ribs 9-11)","Pancreas & Spleen slide 22 (Largest lymphatic organ, very vascular Located in left upper quadrant (LUQ))"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":134,"f":"F0008"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"318"},{"id":"G084-27-1","s":"A brand-new medical student was asked to jump into surgery to help remove the spleen this afternoon. She double checked to make sure she didn’t cut out the liver. Which structure did she remove?","o":["A. Blue","B. Purple","C. Yellow","D. Green","E. Red"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Red (spleen)","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Red: spleen.</b> The spleen is the solid organ at the far left and back of the upper abdomen, behind the stomach, against the lower ribs.<br>A. Blue: right lobe of the liver, the largest organ, on the patient's right.<br>B. Purple: left lobe of the liver, in front, across the midline.<br>C. Yellow: inferior vena cava, the round vein right of the aorta, in front of the right crus.<br>D. Green: stomach, marked by its black air bubble.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G084-27.jpg","in":"Axial contrast CT of the upper abdomen: A blue right lobe of the liver, B purple left lobe of the liver, C yellow near the IVC/caudate region, D green gas-filled stomach (fundus), E red spleen (posterior left upper quadrant).","tg":["Radiology Abd I slide 5 (RUQ: gallbladder, liver)","Radiology Abd II slide 9 (Right Lobe of the Liver)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown GI radiology interactive 2026","sl":27,"f":"G084"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"319"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":90,"label":"Sphincter of Oddi at the major papilla","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G011-69.jpg","cap":"Magnetic resonance cholangiopancreatography: arrow at major papilla, sphincter of Oddi","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen II, slide 69","cu":"../sl/G011-69c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G011-66.jpg","cap":"Same image: arrow on the common bile duct","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen II, slide 66","cu":"../sl/G011-66c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/G4_102.jpg","cap":"Follow the bile duct down to where it enters the descending duodenum; the sphincter of Oddi sits at that opening.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 4.102","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"F0008-158-1","s":"An MRCP 3D image is shown. What structure is indicated by the arrow?","o":["A. Sphincter of Oddi","B. Minor Papilla","C. CBD","D. Cystic Duct","E. Pancreatic Duct"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Sphincter of Oddi","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Sphincter of Oddi (at the major duodenal papilla).</b> On this MRCP (magnetic resonance cholangiopancreatography), the arrow is at the very bottom of the common bile duct, where it enters the duodenal wall with the pancreatic duct.<br>B. Minor papilla: higher up, where the accessory pancreatic duct drains.<br>C. CBD (common bile duct): the long tube above the arrow.<br>D. Cystic duct: joins the gallbladder neck to the bile duct, high up.<br>E. Pancreatic duct: runs across from the patient's left to meet the CBD.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/F0008-158.jpg","in":"3D MRCP: gallbladder top left, bile duct descending to the contrast-filled duodenum; blue arrow points to the lowest end of the common bile duct where it enters the duodenal wall.","tg":["Radiology Abd II slide 69 (MRCP: Major Papilla/Sphincter of Oddi)","Radiology Abd II slide 66 (MRCP: Common Bile Duct)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":158,"f":"F0008"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"320"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":90,"label":"Portal vein on venogram","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G012-56.jpg","cap":"Portal venogram: arrows on the portal vein trunk and its split","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen III, slide 56","cu":"../sl/G012-56c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G012-54.jpg","cap":"Diagram: superior mesenteric and splenic veins join to form the portal vein","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen III, slide 54","cu":"../sl/G012-54c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"F0008-156-1","s":"Name the structure indicated by the arrow:","o":["A. Superior mesenteric vein","B. Inferior vena cava","C. Hepatic vein","D. Portal vein","E. Splenic Vein"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Portal vein","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Portal vein.</b> The arrows point to the big trunk that enters the liver and splits into right and left branches. It forms behind the pancreatic neck, where the SMV (superior mesenteric vein) joins the splenic vein.<br>A. SMV: the vertical vein rising from below to join it.<br>B. IVC (inferior vena cava): behind the liver; it does not branch inside the liver.<br>C. Hepatic veins: drain out of the top of the liver into the IVC.<br>E. Splenic vein: runs horizontally from the patient's left.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/F0008-156.jpg","in":"Coronal contrast MR/CT venogram; two blue arrows point to the large trunk entering the liver and branching into right and left intrahepatic branches, fed from below by the confluence of the SMV and splenic vein.","tg":["Radiology Abd II slide 14 (Portal Vein)","Radiology Abd III slide 54 (Portal Vein)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":156,"f":"F0008"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"321"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":91,"label":"Celiac trunk first, superior mesenteric artery below","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G012-9.jpg","cap":"Sagittal CT: celiac trunk, the first front branch of the aorta","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen III, slide 9","cu":"../sl/G012-9c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G012-25.jpg","cap":"Sagittal CT: superior mesenteric artery just below the celiac trunk","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen III, slide 25","cu":"../sl/G012-25c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/N285.jpg","cap":"In the lower scan the celiac trunk is the aorta's first front branch; the superior mesenteric artery arises just below.","where":"Netter Atlas 6e, Plate 285","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"G084-41-1","s":"A 17 year old female is found to be malnourished even though she eats enough. The doctors at a famous clinic think it may be a Greater Splanchnic nerve pathology. So while doing invasive surgery they look for lesions along/within this structure.","o":["A. Red 1","B. Green 2","C. Blue 3","D. Purple 4"],"g":["C"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Celiac Trunk (per the notes)","a":"B","why":"<b>C. Blue 3: celiac trunk.</b> The greater splanchnic nerves (thoracic segments T5 to T9) end in the celiac ganglia, which surround the celiac trunk. On this sagittal view the celiac trunk is the first short branch pointing forward off the front of the aorta, just below the diaphragm.<br>A. Red 1: liver.<br>B. Green 2: the abdominal aorta itself.<br>D. Purple 4: superior mesenteric artery, the next branch below, running down.","ex":"","fl":["The answer is Blue 3 (C), the celiac trunk, because the greater splanchnic nerves end in the celiac ganglia around it; Green 2 sits on the abdominal aorta."],"ch":true,"img":"../img/G084-41.jpg","in":"Sagittal MRI of the abdomen with four numbered dots: 1 red on the liver, 2 green on the abdominal aorta, 3 blue on the celiac trunk (first anterior branch), 4 purple on the superior mesenteric artery.","tg":["Radiology Abd III slide 9 (Celiac Trunk)","Radiology Abd I slide 87 (Coronal View: Celiac Trunk)","ANS slide 27 (| Splanchnic Nerves | Autonomic Fiber Type | System | Origin | Destination |)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown GI radiology interactive 2026","sl":41,"f":"G084"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"322"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":91,"label":"Left renal vein: between superior mesenteric artery and aorta","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G005-42.jpg","cap":"Superior mesenteric artery: splenic vein in front; left renal vein and duodenum behind","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 42","cu":"../sl/G005-42c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G012-60.jpg","cap":"Coronal CT: right and left renal veins running into the inferior vena cava","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen III, slide 60","cu":"../sl/G012-60c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"G084-40-1","s":"On a midline sagittal MRI of the abdomen, a red arrow points to a vessel cut in cross-section in the angle between the superior mesenteric artery (in front) and the abdominal aorta (behind), just below the pancreas. Which vessel is it?","o":["A. Splenic vein","B. Left renal vein","C. Inferior mesenteric vein","D. Portal vein","E. Left gonadal vein"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Left renal vein","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Left renal vein.</b> It runs from the left kidney across the front of the aorta to the inferior vena cava, just below the superior mesenteric artery, so on a sagittal view it is the round vessel caught in the angle between them.<br>A. Splenic vein: passes in front of the superior mesenteric artery, behind the pancreas.<br>C. Inferior mesenteric vein: runs up on the left into the splenic vein.<br>D. Portal vein: higher, behind the pancreatic neck, heading to the liver.<br>E. Left gonadal vein: drains into the left renal vein, left of the aorta.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Labelled sagittal MRI (heart, liver, stomach, celiac artery, SMA, pancreas, abdominal aorta, transverse colon, small-bowel coils, L5); the red arrow sits between the SMA and the aorta. Only the answered version exists, so the stem describes the view.","tg":["Radiology Abd III slide 60 (Renal veins)","Small & Large Intestines slide 42 (SMA)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown GI radiology interactive 2026","sl":40,"f":"G084"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"323"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":92,"label":"Celiac trunk branches on angiogram","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G012-11.jpg","cap":"Normal celiac trunk: left gastric, common hepatic and its branches, splenic","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen III, slide 11","cu":"../sl/G012-11c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/F1180-367.jpg","cap":"Labeled celiac angiogram: common hepatic, splenic, left gastric, hepatic, right gastroepiploic","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 367","cu":"../sl/F1180-367c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G012-20.jpg","cap":"Celiac CT angiogram: left and right hepatic arteries marked","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen III, slide 20","cu":"../sl/G012-20c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/G4_112.jpg","cap":"The celiac trunk splits into left gastric, common hepatic (toward the liver) and the long splenic artery.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 4.112","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/N285.jpg","cap":"A second celiac angiogram: common hepatic, gastroduodenal, hepatic artery proper, left gastric and splenic arteries labeled.","where":"Netter Atlas 6e, Plate 285","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"G084-30-1","s":"47yo female comes into her PC office with complaints of some mid-abdominal pain. She denies any trauma or change in activities of daily living (ADL’s). Her PCP did not find any overt pathologies on physical exam, so she ordered a CT angiogram. The results show an occlusion, as indicated by the red arrow*. Which structure is it?","o":["A. Celiac Trunk","B. Gastroduodenal Artery","C. Left Hepatic Artery","D. Cystic Artery","E. Superior Mesenteric Artery"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastroduodenal Artery","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Gastroduodenal artery.</b> The arrow is on the vessel running straight down from the common hepatic artery on the patient's right, behind the first part of the duodenum; it goes on to the superior pancreaticoduodenal and right gastro-omental arteries.<br>A. Celiac trunk: the short trunk at the catheter tip, on the aorta.<br>C. Left hepatic artery: runs up into the left lobe of the liver.<br>D. Cystic artery: a small branch of the right hepatic artery to the gallbladder.<br>E. Superior mesenteric artery: a separate aortic branch, not filled by a celiac injection.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G084-30.jpg","in":"Celiac arteriogram; the red arrow points at the vessel descending from the common hepatic artery behind the duodenum (gastroduodenal artery).","tg":["Radiology Abd III slide 21 (Celiac Trunk: MIP)","Radiology Abd III slide 11 (“Normal” Celiac Trunk)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown GI radiology interactive 2026","sl":30,"f":"G084"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"324"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":93,"label":"Ileocolic artery: lowest right superior mesenteric branch","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G012-39.jpg","cap":"Superior mesenteric angiogram: ileocolic artery, lowest right-sided branch","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen III, slide 39","cu":"../sl/G012-39c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/D042-154.jpg","cap":"Line diagram plus labeled superior mesenteric angiogram: ileocolic, right colic, middle colic","where":"2025 TA review, slide 154"}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-385-1","s":"The image following is an arteriogram of the superior mesenteric artery and its branches. The artery indicated by the arrow is the:","o":["A) Inferior mesenteric.","B) Middle colic.","C) Inferior pancreaticoduodenal.","D) First jejunal branch.","E) Ileocolic."],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Ileocolic","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Ileocolic.</b> On an SMA (superior mesenteric artery) arteriogram, the arrow marks the lowest branch from the right side of the SMA, running toward the cecum in the right lower quadrant: the ileocolic artery.<br>A. Inferior mesenteric: a separate aortic branch, not filled by an SMA injection.<br>B. Middle colic: the first right-sided branch, high up, to the transverse colon.<br>C. Inferior pancreaticoduodenal: the first small branch, near the SMA origin.<br>D. First jejunal branch: leaves the left side, high up.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/F3903-385-1.jpg","in":"Slide 3 (the image 'following' the question): Gray's drawing of the SMA branches beside a plain SMA arteriogram; the white arrow points to the lowest right-sided branch curving toward the right lower quadrant (ileocecal region) = ileocolic artery. Slide 4 repeats the drawing with a second arteriogram ('Figure F3') whose orange arrow marks the same artery. No labels on the arteriograms.","tg":["Radiology Abd III slide 39 (Ileocolic Artery)","Radiology Abd III slide 29 (Superior Mesenteric Artery DSA)","Radiology Abd III slide 37 (Ileocolic trunk)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Harlem clicker","n":"Harlem small & large intestines clickers 2026","sl":3,"f":"G109"},{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":385,"f":"F3903"},{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":125,"f":"F0008"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"325"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":93,"label":"Right colic artery runs to the ascending colon","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G084-34.jpg","cap":"Superior mesenteric angiogram labeled: middle colic, right colic, ileocolic, jejunal and ileal branches","where":"2026 Middletown interactive, slide 34"},{"src":"../sl/G012-38.jpg","cap":"Superior mesenteric angiogram: arrow on the right colic artery to the ascending colon","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen III, slide 38"}],"q":[{"id":"G084-33-1","s":"17yo female comes in with severe right lower abdominal pain that started 2 hours ago. The surgeon on call decides that her first 1/3rd of her ascending colon must be surgically removed. You explain to the patient and their family that several arteries need to be ligated during surgery. The most important one being the right colic artery, which is indicated by an arrow.","o":["A. Green","B. Purple","C. Yellow","D. Blue","E. Red"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Blue (right colic artery)","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Blue: right colic artery.</b> It leaves the right side of the superior mesenteric artery and runs to the patient's right (image left) to the ascending colon.<br>A. Green: the superior mesenteric artery trunk itself.<br>B. Purple: a jejunal branch, from the left side of the trunk.<br>C. Yellow: middle colic artery, running up to the transverse colon.<br>E. Red: ileocolic artery, heading down to the cecum, appendix and terminal ileum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G084-33.jpg","in":"Superior mesenteric arteriogram with five coloured arrows: C yellow on the middle colic artery, E red on the ileocolic artery, D blue on the right colic artery (right side), B purple on a jejunal branch (left), A green on the SMA trunk/ileal branches.","tg":["Radiology Abd III slide 38 (Right Colic Artery)","Radiology Abd III slide 39 (Ileocolic Artery)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown GI radiology interactive 2026","sl":33,"f":"G084"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"326"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":93,"label":"Sigmoid arteries: inferior mesenteric branches to sigmoid colon","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G012-44.jpg","cap":"Inferior mesenteric angiogram: arrow on a sigmoid artery heading into the left pelvis","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen III, slide 44","cu":"../sl/G012-44c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G012-40.jpg","cap":"Inferior mesenteric artery branches: left colic, sigmoid arteries, then superior rectal","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen III, slide 40"}],"q":[{"id":"G084-36-1","s":"A 27-year-old female comes in with problems in GI tract CT. scan shows and embolism in the sigmoidal artery. The surgeon is going to remove the sigmoid colon, where it has died, and this associated artery.","o":["A. Green","B. Purple","C. Yellow","D. Blue","E. Red"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Purple (sigmoid artery)","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Purple: sigmoid artery.</b> The sigmoid arteries leave the inferior mesenteric artery below the left colic and run down and to the patient's left (image right) into the left lower quadrant, to the sigmoid colon.<br>A. Green: left colic artery, running up to the descending colon.<br>C. Yellow: the inferior mesenteric artery trunk.<br>D. Blue: the catheter, a smooth straight tube coming up from the groin.<br>E. Red: superior rectal artery, the continuation of the inferior mesenteric artery down into the pelvis to the rectum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/G084-36.jpg","in":"Inferior mesenteric arteriogram with five coloured arrows: A green on the left colic artery, C yellow on the IMA trunk, E red on the superior rectal artery, D blue on the catheter, B purple on a sigmoid artery running to the sigmoid colon.","tg":["Radiology Abd III slide 44 (Sigmoid Artery)","Radiology Abd III slide 41 (Inferior Mesenteric Artery DSA)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown interactive","n":"Middletown GI radiology interactive 2026","sl":36,"f":"G084"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"327"}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":93,"label":"Marginal artery of Drummond along colon edge","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G012-46.jpg","cap":"Inferior mesenteric angiogram: arrow on marginal artery of Drummond along colon","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen III, slide 46","cu":"../sl/G012-46c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G012-45.jpg","cap":"Collaterals diagram: marginal artery along colon edge, arc of Riolan inside","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen III, slide 45","cu":"../sl/G012-45c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"F0008-154-1","s":"Identify the structure indicated by the arrow","o":["A. Left colic artery","B. Left colic vein","C. Marginal artery of Drummond","D. Arc of Riolan","E. Arc of Buehler"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Marginal artery of Drummond","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Marginal artery of Drummond.</b> On this IMA (inferior mesenteric artery) angiogram, the arrow is on a vessel running close along the edge of the colon near the splenic flexure. The marginal artery runs along the mesenteric border of the colon and links SMA (superior mesenteric artery) and IMA branches.<br>A. Left colic artery: runs from the IMA toward the colon, not along it.<br>B. Left colic vein: veins do not fill on an arterial injection.<br>D. Arc of Riolan: a central loop, nearer the mesentery root.<br>E. Arc of Buehler: links the celiac trunk and SMA.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/F0008-154.jpg","in":"IMA digital-subtraction angiogram; blue arrow points to a vessel running along the edge of the colon near the left colic (splenic) flexure, parallel to the bowel wall.","tg":["Radiology Abd III slide 46 (Marginal Artery of Drummond)","Small & Large Intestines slide 81 (Marginal Artery)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":154,"f":"F0008"}],"tier":1,"y26":0,"n":"328"}]},{"l":"ANAT-Kahoot","u":94,"label":"Each gut division's artery: celiac, superior and inferior mesenteric","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G076-6.jpg","cap":"Foregut celiac trunk, midgut superior mesenteric, hindgut inferior mesenteric artery","where":"2026 GI supplemental session (Nov 6), slide 6","cu":"../sl/G076-6c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G001-7.jpg","cap":"Below the diaphragm each gut division gets its own aortic branch","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 7","cu":"../sl/G001-7c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"D070-1-1","s":"Which of the following blood vessels supplies derivatives of the embryonic hindgut?","o":["A. Superior mesenteric artery","B. Inferior mesenteric artery","C. Celiac trunk","D. Right gastric artery"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Inferior mesenteric artery","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Inferior mesenteric artery.</b> Below the diaphragm each gut division has its own branch of the aorta. The hindgut (distal third of the transverse colon to the upper anal canal) gets the inferior mesenteric artery, which leaves the aorta at the third lumbar vertebra.<br>A. Superior mesenteric artery: midgut.<br>C. Celiac trunk: foregut.<br>D. Right gastric artery: a celiac branch (from the hepatic artery) to the lesser curvature of the stomach, which is foregut.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 7 (Blood supply)","GI Development slide 29 (Hindgut)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Harlem clicker","n":"GI development Kahoot 2026 (Harlem faculty review, 21 questions)","sl":1,"f":"D070"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"K1"}]},{"l":"ANAT-Kahoot","u":94,"label":"Esophagus plugs, then reopens from week 8","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G001-10.jpg","cap":"Lining fills the tube by week 5; apoptosis reopens it from week 8","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 10","cu":"../sl/G001-10c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"D070-2-1","s":"At what week does recanalization of the esophagus start?","o":["A. 5th","B. 10th","C. 8th","D. 7th"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"8th","a":"C","why":"<b>C. 8th.</b> The esophageal lining multiplies until the tube is an almost solid rod. Then cells die by apoptosis, small vacuoles merge, and the lumen starts to reopen (recanalize) by week 8.<br>A. 5th: about when the plug has formed, not when it reopens.<br>B. 10th: when the midgut loop returns to the abdomen.<br>D. 7th: the lumen is still plugged; reopening begins by week 8.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 10 (Esophagus)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Harlem clicker","n":"GI development Kahoot 2026 (Harlem faculty review, 21 questions)","sl":2,"f":"D070"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"K2"}]},{"l":"ANAT-Kahoot","u":94,"label":"Secondary retroperitoneal: duodenum, pancreas, ascending/descending colon","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G001-8.jpg","cap":"Secondary retroperitoneal: duodenum parts 2-4, pancreas except tail, ascending/descending colon","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 8","cu":"../sl/G001-8c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G001-26.jpg","cap":"Ascending and descending colon mesenteries fuse to the posterior wall","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 26","cu":"../sl/G001-26c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/D042-51.jpg","cap":"Table sorting organs into intra-, primary and secondary retroperitoneal","where":"2025 TA review, slide 51"},{"src":"../tb/M5_25.jpg","cap":"Descending colon starts on a mesentery, which then fuses to the back wall: secondarily retroperitoneal.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.25","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/G4_7.jpg","cap":"Panels A to C: the mesentery swings over and fuses with the back wall, turning gut secondarily retroperitoneal.","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students 3e, Fig. 4.7","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"D070-3-1","s":"Which organ is considered to develop retroperitoneal?","o":["A. ascending colon","B. stomach","C. spleen","D. jejunum"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"ascending colon","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Ascending colon.</b> It starts on a mesentery, but after the midgut returns that mesentery fuses to the posterior wall, so it ends up retroperitoneal (secondary retroperitoneal). It is the only option that is retroperitoneal at all.<br>B. Stomach: intraperitoneal, hangs on its omenta.<br>C. Spleen: intraperitoneal, inside the dorsal mesogastrium.<br>D. Jejunum: intraperitoneal, on the mesentery proper.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 8 (Retroperitoneal Organs)","GI Development slide 26 (Mesenteries)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Harlem clicker","n":"GI development Kahoot 2026 (Harlem faculty review, 21 questions)","sl":3,"f":"D070"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"K3"}]},{"l":"ANAT-Kahoot","u":94,"label":"Cranial (upper) limb of loop becomes small intestine","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G001-24.jpg","cap":"Upper arm becomes small intestine; lower arm becomes cecum and large intestine","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 24","cu":"../sl/G001-24c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/E10_16.jpg","cap":"The cranial limb (light) becomes jejunum and upper ileum; the caudal limb (dark) becomes lower ileum, cecum, colon.","where":"BRS Embryology 5e, Figure 10-16","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"D070-4-1","s":"The adult derivative of the upper arm from midgut herniation and rotation is","o":["A. Large intestine","B. stomach","C. Small intestine","D. cecum"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Small intestine","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Small intestine.</b> The cranial (upper, proximal) arm of the midgut loop becomes the distal duodenum, the jejunum and the upper ileum; it grows fast and coils into loops.<br>A. Large intestine: from the caudal (lower) arm.<br>B. Stomach: foregut, not part of the loop.<br>D. Cecum: forms as a swelling on the caudal arm.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 24 (Herniation and Rotation)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Harlem clicker","n":"GI development Kahoot 2026 (Harlem faculty review, 21 questions)","sl":4,"f":"D070"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"K4"}]},{"l":"ANAT-Kahoot","u":94,"label":"Midgut loop rotates around the superior mesenteric artery","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G001-24.jpg","cap":"Midgut loop wrapped around the superior mesenteric artery, its rotation axis","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 24","cu":"../sl/G001-24c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G001-23.jpg","cap":"Herniation at week six; superior mesenteric artery is the rotation axis","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 23"},{"src":"../tb/E10_16.jpg","cap":"The loop rotates around the superior mesenteric artery, its axis; extra twisting can choke this artery.","where":"BRS Embryology 5e, Figure 10-16","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"D070-5-1","s":"What structure forms the axis of rotation for the midgut loop?","o":["A. Celiac trunk","B. ventral mesentery","C. dorsal mesentery","D. Superior mesenteric artery"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Superior mesenteric artery","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Superior mesenteric artery.</b> It runs down the middle of the primary intestinal loop, in the loop's mesentery, so the loop turns around it: 90° counterclockwise in the cord and 180° more on return.<br>A. Celiac trunk: the foregut artery, outside the loop.<br>B. Ventral mesentery: exists only along the foregut.<br>C. Dorsal mesentery: holds the loop and carries the artery, but the artery is the axis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 23 (Herniation and Rotation)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Harlem clicker","n":"GI development Kahoot 2026 (Harlem faculty review, 21 questions)","sl":5,"f":"D070"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"K5"}]},{"l":"ANAT-Kahoot","u":94,"label":"Each gut division's artery: celiac, superior and inferior mesenteric","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G076-6.jpg","cap":"Foregut celiac trunk, midgut superior mesenteric, hindgut inferior mesenteric artery","where":"2026 GI supplemental session (Nov 6), slide 6","cu":"../sl/G076-6c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G001-7.jpg","cap":"Below the diaphragm each gut division gets its own aortic branch","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 7","cu":"../sl/G001-7c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"D070-6-1","s":"Derivatives of the embryonic foregut are supplied by which of the following arteries?","o":["A. Inferior mesenteric","B. Superior mesenteric","C. Celiac trunk","D. Pulmonary trunk"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Celiac trunk","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Celiac trunk.</b> Below the diaphragm the foregut organs, such as the abdominal esophagus, stomach, upper duodenum, liver and gallbladder, are supplied by the celiac trunk, which leaves the aorta at the twelfth thoracic vertebra.<br>A. Inferior mesenteric: hindgut.<br>B. Superior mesenteric: midgut.<br>D. Pulmonary trunk: carries blood from the right ventricle to the lungs; it supplies no gut organ.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 7 (Blood supply)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Harlem clicker","n":"GI development Kahoot 2026 (Harlem faculty review, 21 questions)","sl":6,"f":"D070"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"K6"}]},{"l":"ANAT-Kahoot","u":94,"label":"Spleen: mesenchyme in dorsal mesogastrium behind stomach","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G001-15.jpg","cap":"Spleen forms from mesenchyme between dorsal mesogastrium layers, week five","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 15","cu":"../sl/G001-15c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/D042-60.jpg","cap":"Spleen developing inside the dorsal mesogastrium behind the stomach","where":"2025 TA review, slide 60"},{"src":"../sl/D045-153.jpg","cap":"Spleen from dorsal mesogastrium mesenchyme in week five; fetal blood-forming organ","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 153"},{"src":"../tb/E10_7.jpg","cap":"The splenic rudiment forms inside the dorsal mesentery (dorsal mesogastrium) behind the stomach.","where":"BRS Embryology 5e, Figure 10-7","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"D070-7-1","s":"The spleen is derived solely from which of the following germ layers?","o":["A. Mesenchyme","B. Endoderm","C. Ectoderm","D. Splanchnopleure"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Mesenchyme","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Mesenchyme.</b> The spleen forms in week 5 from a cluster of mesenchymal cells (mesoderm) between the two layers of the dorsal mesogastrium, behind the stomach, so it has no endoderm.<br>B. Endoderm: makes the gut lining and gland epithelium, such as liver and pancreas.<br>C. Ectoderm: makes skin and the nervous system.<br>D. Splanchnopleure: visceral mesoderm together with the endoderm it wraps, a two-layer sheet, so it is not the spleen's single source.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 15 (Spleen)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Harlem clicker","n":"GI development Kahoot 2026 (Harlem faculty review, 21 questions)","sl":7,"f":"D070"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"K7"}]},{"l":"ANAT-Kahoot","u":94,"label":"Meckel's diverticulum: leftover vitelline duct on the ileum","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G001-28.jpg","cap":"Ileal diverticulum: free pouch, fibrous cord, or open umbilical fistula","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 28","cu":"../sl/G001-28c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G001-24.jpg","cap":"Panel A: omphaloenteric (vitelline) duct joins midgut loop to yolk sac","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 24","cu":"../sl/G001-24c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/E10_19.jpg","cap":"Arrow: Meckel's diverticulum, a blind pouch on the ileum (IL) left over from the vitelline duct.","where":"BRS Embryology 5e, Figure 10-19","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/E10_16.jpg","cap":"The vitelline duct joins the tip of the midgut loop to the yolk sac; if it persists you get Meckel's.","where":"BRS Embryology 5e, Figure 10-16","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"D070-8-1","s":"An infant presents with rectal bleeding and a scan reveals an outgrowth of the ileum containing gastric and pancreatic mucosa cells, in addition to ileal tissue. The diagnosis is most likely:","o":["A. pyloric stenosis","B. urorectal fistula","C. imperforate anus","D. Meckel's diverticulum"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Meckel's diverticulum","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Meckel's diverticulum.</b> The gut end of the vitelline duct persists as a pouch on the distal ileum. Ectopic gastric or pancreatic tissue in its lining ulcerates the nearby ileum and causes painless rectal bleeding in a child.<br>A. Pyloric stenosis: projectile nonbilious vomiting at 2–6 weeks.<br>B. Urorectal fistula: meconium in the urine, from failed division of the cloaca.<br>C. Imperforate anus: no anal opening at birth.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 28 (Meckel’s Diverticulum)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Harlem clicker","n":"GI development Kahoot 2026 (Harlem faculty review, 21 questions)","sl":8,"f":"D070"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"K8"}]},{"l":"ANAT-Kahoot","u":94,"label":"Projectile non-bilious vomiting + olive mass: pyloric stenosis","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G001-14.jpg","cap":"Pyloric stenosis: thickened pylorus and projectile vomiting","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 14","cu":"../sl/G001-14c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/E10_8.jpg","cap":"Barium study: curved arrows mark the long, thin pyloric channel squeezed by thickened pyloric muscle.","where":"BRS Embryology 5e, Figure 10-8","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"D070-9-1","s":"Pyloric stenosis is characterized by vomiting, without bile, starting in the 2nd-3rd week after birth due to narrowing of the pyloric lumen primarily from hypertrophy of the:","o":["A. epithelial layer","B. muscular layer","C. endothelial layer","D. gut tube"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"muscular layer","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Muscular layer.</b> In hypertrophic pyloric stenosis the circular smooth muscle of the pylorus overgrows and narrows the outlet. It is felt as an olive-shaped mass, and surgery splits the muscle (pyloromyotomy).<br>A. Epithelial layer: the lining is normal; a lining plug that fails to reopen gives atresia, not this.<br>C. Endothelial layer: lines blood vessels, not the gut lumen.<br>D. Gut tube: the whole tube is not thickened; only the pyloric muscle is.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 14 (Pyloric Stenosis)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Harlem clicker","n":"GI development Kahoot 2026 (Harlem faculty review, 21 questions)","sl":9,"f":"D070"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"K9"}]},{"l":"ANAT-Kahoot","u":94,"label":"Urorectal septum divides cloaca; defect causes fistula","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G001-30.jpg","cap":"Urorectal septum splits cloaca into bladder in front, rectum behind","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 30","cu":"../sl/G001-30c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G001-32.jpg","cap":"Urorectal fistula diagram from abnormal urorectal septum","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 32","cu":"../sl/G001-32c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G002-14.jpg","cap":"Normal cloacal partition by urorectal septum versus rectovaginal fistula","where":"2026 Middletown clicker, slide 14","cu":"../sl/G002-14c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/E10_26.jpg","cap":"Top panels: the urorectal septum grows down and splits the cloaca into urogenital sinus in front, rectum behind.","where":"BRS Embryology 5e, Figure 10-26","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/E10_30.jpg","cap":"Rectovaginal fistula: a faulty urorectal septum leaves the rectum opening into the vagina.","where":"BRS Embryology 5e, Figure 10-30","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"D070-10-1","s":"Which of the following conditions results from failure of proper division of the cloaca?","o":["A. urorectal/rectovaginal fistula","B. imperforate anus","C. umbilical fistula","D. esophageal fistula"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"urorectal/rectovaginal fistula","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Urorectal/rectovaginal fistula.</b> If the urorectal septum does not fully divide the cloaca, the rectum stays joined to the urogenital part: a urorectal fistula in boys, a rectovaginal fistula in girls.<br>B. Imperforate anus: the anal membrane fails to rupture; the septum is normal.<br>C. Umbilical fistula: an open vitelline duct (stool) or urachus (urine) at the umbilicus.<br>D. Esophageal fistula: a faulty tracheoesophageal septum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 32 (Clinical Correlates)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Harlem clicker","n":"GI development Kahoot 2026 (Harlem faculty review, 21 questions)","sl":10,"f":"D070"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"K10"}]},{"l":"ANAT-Kahoot","u":94,"label":"External genitalia: tubercle, urethral folds, labioscrotal swellings","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G077-65.jpg","cap":"Septum splits the cloacal membrane; genitalia form around the front urogenital membrane","where":"2026 TA session: Embryology 7 GI, slide 65"},{"src":"../sl/G003-32.jpg","cap":"Gubernaculum ties the testis to the primordial scrotum, built from labioscrotal swellings","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 32","cu":"../sl/G003-32c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"D070-11-1","s":"Which derivative-structure pair is correct?","o":["A. genital tubercle -> Fallopian tubes","B. labioscrotal folds -> labia minora","C. genital tubercle -> glans penis","D. urogenital folds -> labia majora"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"genital tubercle -> glans penis","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Genital tubercle → glans penis.</b> The genital tubercle elongates into the phallus; its tip becomes the glans of the penis (the clitoris in a female).<br>A. Genital tubercle → fallopian tubes: the uterine tubes come from the paramesonephric ducts inside the body.<br>B. Labioscrotal folds → labia minora: they become the labia majora (the scrotum in a male).<br>D. Urogenital folds → labia majora: they become the labia minora (in a male they close over the penile urethra).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 Harlem clicker","n":"GI development Kahoot 2026 (Harlem faculty review, 21 questions)","sl":11,"f":"D070"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"K11"}]},{"l":"ANAT-Kahoot","u":94,"label":"Faster dorsal wall becomes greater curvature","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G001-12.jpg","cap":"Dorsal stomach wall grows faster, forms greater curvature, then rotates left","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 12","cu":"../sl/G001-12c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/D045-137.jpg","cap":"Stomach rotation: greater curvature labelled as former dorsal wall","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 137"},{"src":"../tb/E10_7.jpg","cap":"The dorsal stomach wall grows faster and becomes the greater curvature as the stomach turns 90 degrees.","where":"BRS Embryology 5e, Figure 10-7","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"D070-12-1","s":"Which of the following is incorrect?","o":["A. Gut tube dilates in a dorsal-ventral manner","B. Dorsal aspect dilates faster than ventral","C. Formation of the stomach involves two rotations","D. First rotation of the stomach is 90 degrees clockwise"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Gut tube dilates in a dorsal-ventral manner","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Gut tube dilates in a dorsal-ventral manner.</b> This is the false statement: the stomach widens ventral to dorsal, with its dorsal (back) wall bulging out fastest to make the greater curvature.<br>B. True: the dorsal wall grows faster than the ventral wall.<br>C. True: the stomach turns around its long axis and also around a front-to-back axis.<br>D. True: the first turn is 90° clockwise seen from above, which brings the dorsal wall to the left.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 12 (Stomach and Stomach Rotation)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Harlem clicker","n":"GI development Kahoot 2026 (Harlem faculty review, 21 questions)","sl":12,"f":"D070"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"K12"}]},{"l":"ANAT-Kahoot","u":94,"label":"Duodenal atresia/stenosis = failed recanalization","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G001-17.jpg","cap":"Duodenal plug: partial recanalization gives stenosis, none gives atresia","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 17","cu":"../sl/G001-17c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/D042-64.jpg","cap":"Gut tube occlusion and recanalization, weeks five to nine; stenosis, atresia","where":"2025 TA review, slide 64"}],"q":[{"id":"D070-13-1","s":"Which developmental event leads to duodenal stenosis?","o":["A. Failure of epithelial proliferation","B. Incomplete recanalization","C. Lack of blood supply by the celiac trunk and Sup Mes Artery","D. Failure of midgut loop to herniate"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Incomplete recanalization","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Incomplete recanalization.</b> The duodenal lining first multiplies and fills the lumen. If the plug reopens only partly, the lumen stays narrow (stenosis); if it never reopens, the result is atresia.<br>A. Failure of epithelial proliferation: proliferation always happens; the error is in the reopening.<br>C. Lack of blood supply by the celiac trunk and superior mesenteric artery: a vascular accident causes atresias lower down, in the jejunum and ileum.<br>D. Failure of midgut loop to herniate: has nothing to do with the duodenal lumen.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 16 (Duodenum and Recanalization)","GI Development slide 17 (Duodenal Stenosis and Atresia)","GI Development slide 10 (Esophagus)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Harlem clicker","n":"GI development Kahoot 2026 (Harlem faculty review, 21 questions)","sl":13,"f":"D070"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"K13"}]},{"l":"ANAT-Kahoot","u":94,"label":"Kupffer cells: septum transversum mesoderm","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G001-18.jpg","cap":"Septum transversum mesoderm forms liver fibrous tissue and Kupffer cells","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 18","cu":"../sl/G001-18c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G002-18.jpg","cap":"Liver bud in septum transversum; Kupffer cells lining liver sinusoids","where":"2026 Middletown clicker, slide 18"}],"q":[{"id":"D070-14-1","s":"Which structure is not of endodermal origin?","o":["A. Hepatic duct","B. Kupffer cells","C. Bile duct","D. Pancreas"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Kupffer cells","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Kupffer cells.</b> These liver macrophages come from mesoderm of the septum transversum, not from the endodermal liver bud. They line the sinusoids.<br>A. Hepatic duct: endoderm of the hepatic diverticulum.<br>C. Bile duct: the stalk of the hepatic diverticulum, endoderm.<br>D. Pancreas: two buds of foregut endoderm; its ducts, acini and islets are all endoderm.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 18 (Liver and Gallbladder)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Harlem clicker","n":"GI development Kahoot 2026 (Harlem faculty review, 21 questions)","sl":14,"f":"D070"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"K14"}]},{"l":"ANAT-Kahoot","u":94,"label":"Septum transversum: diaphragm tendon, ventral mesentery, liver covering","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G077-46.jpg","cap":"Endoderm makes liver cells and ducts; septum transversum mesoderm makes fibrous tissue, Kupffer cells","where":"2026 TA session: Embryology 7 GI, slide 46","cu":"../sl/G077-46c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G001-19.jpg","cap":"Liver grows into septum transversum; its mesoderm becomes ventral mesentery and liver covering","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 19","cu":"../sl/G001-19c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"D070-15-1","s":"The septum transversum will contribute to all of the following except?","o":["A. Central tendon of the diaphragm","B. Hepatic cords","C. Ventral mesentery","D. Visceral peritoneum of the liver"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Hepatic cords","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Hepatic cords.</b> The cords (plates) of liver cells grow from the endoderm of the hepatic diverticulum into the septum transversum; the septum does not make them.<br>A. Central tendon of the diaphragm: most of the septum transversum becomes it.<br>C. Ventral mesentery: the thin septum mesoderm left between the liver, the stomach and the front wall.<br>D. Visceral peritoneum of the liver: septum mesoderm on the liver surface becomes its capsule and peritoneal covering.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 18 (Liver and Gallbladder)","GI Development slide 19 (Ventral Mesentery)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Harlem clicker","n":"GI development Kahoot 2026 (Harlem faculty review, 21 questions)","sl":15,"f":"D070"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"K15"}]},{"l":"ANAT-Kahoot","u":94,"label":"Foregut organs: esophagus, stomach, liver, gallbladder, pancreas","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G001-9.jpg","cap":"Foregut list: esophagus, stomach, liver, gallbladder, pancreas, first duodenum","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 9"},{"src":"../sl/D045-126.jpg","cap":"Foregut, midgut and hindgut derivatives beside a labelled embryo","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 126"},{"src":"../sl/G001-22.jpg","cap":"Midgut list: distal duodenum to two-thirds of transverse colon","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 22","cu":"../sl/G001-22c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/E10_9.jpg","cap":"Liver, gallbladder and both pancreatic buds all sprout from the foregut right beside the stomach.","where":"BRS Embryology 5e, Figure 10-9","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/M5_33.jpg","cap":"Panel C: celiac trunk (to foregut) supplies the foregut organs: liver, gallbladder, stomach and upper duodenum.","where":"Moore 10e, Figure 5.33","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"D070-16-1","s":"The pancreas is derived from which segment of the gut?","o":["A. Foregut","B. Midgut","C. Hindgut"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Foregut","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Foregut.</b> Both pancreatic buds sprout from foregut endoderm at the duodenum, from the last stretch of foregut before the bile duct opening, so the pancreas is a foregut organ, like the liver and gallbladder.<br>B. Midgut: duodenum past the bile duct opening to the proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon.<br>C. Hindgut: distal third of the transverse colon to the upper anal canal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 9 (Foregut)","GI Development slide 20 (Pancreas)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Harlem clicker","n":"GI development Kahoot 2026 (Harlem faculty review, 21 questions)","sl":16,"f":"D070"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"K16"}]},{"l":"ANAT-Kahoot","u":94,"label":"Midgut derivatives: lower duodenum to two-thirds transverse colon","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G001-22.jpg","cap":"Midgut list: lower duodenum to two-thirds transverse colon; superior mesenteric artery","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 22","cu":"../sl/G001-22c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/D041-135.jpg","cap":"Foregut, midgut and hindgut derivative lists with celiac, superior and inferior mesenterics","where":"2025 TA review, slide 135","cu":"../sl/D041-135c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"D070-17-1","s":"Which of the following structures is derived from the midgut?","o":["A. Appendix","B. Stomach","C. Liver","D. Pancreas"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Appendix","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Appendix.</b> The appendix grows as a small pouch off the cecum, on the caudal arm of the midgut loop, so it is midgut and supplied by the superior mesenteric artery.<br>B. Stomach: foregut.<br>C. Liver: foregut bud (hepatic diverticulum).<br>D. Pancreas: two foregut buds at the duodenum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 22 (Midgut)","GI Development slide 25 (Cecum and Appendix)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Harlem clicker","n":"GI development Kahoot 2026 (Harlem faculty review, 21 questions)","sl":17,"f":"D070"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"K17"}]},{"l":"ANAT-Kahoot","u":94,"label":"Anal canal: upper two-thirds endoderm, lower third ectoderm","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G001-31.jpg","cap":"Anal canal: hindgut above pectinate line, proctodeum (ectoderm) below","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 31","cu":"../sl/G001-31c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/F3903-277.jpg","cap":"Upper two-thirds endoderm with autonomic nerves; lower third ectoderm, somatic","where":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker, slide 277","cu":"../sl/F3903-277c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/E10_26.jpg","cap":"Photo: upper anal canal from hindgut (endoderm), lower anal canal from proctodeum (ectoderm), split at pectinate line.","where":"BRS Embryology 5e, Figure 10-26","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"D070-18-1","s":"Which of the following is correct about the pectinate line?","o":["A. Superior ⅔ ectodermal in origin","B. Inferior ⅓ ectodermal in origin","C. Superior ⅔ mesodermal in origin","D. Inferior ⅓ endodermal in origin"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Inferior ⅓ ectodermal in origin","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Inferior one-third ectodermal in origin.</b> Below the pectinate line the anal canal comes from ectoderm of the proctodeum (anal pit); above it, the superior two-thirds is hindgut endoderm.<br>A. Superior two-thirds ectodermal: it is endoderm.<br>C. Superior two-thirds mesodermal: the lining is endoderm; mesoderm makes only the wall's muscle and connective tissue.<br>D. Inferior one-third endodermal: it is ectoderm.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 31 (Anal Canal)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Harlem clicker","n":"GI development Kahoot 2026 (Harlem faculty review, 21 questions)","sl":18,"f":"D070"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"K18"}]},{"l":"ANAT-Kahoot","u":94,"label":"Urorectal septum divides cloaca; defect causes fistula","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G001-30.jpg","cap":"Urorectal septum splits cloaca into bladder in front, rectum behind","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 30","cu":"../sl/G001-30c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G001-32.jpg","cap":"Urorectal fistula diagram from abnormal urorectal septum","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 32","cu":"../sl/G001-32c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G002-14.jpg","cap":"Normal cloacal partition by urorectal septum versus rectovaginal fistula","where":"2026 Middletown clicker, slide 14","cu":"../sl/G002-14c.jpg"},{"src":"../tb/E10_26.jpg","cap":"Top panels: the urorectal septum grows down and splits the cloaca into urogenital sinus in front, rectum behind.","where":"BRS Embryology 5e, Figure 10-26","tb":1},{"src":"../tb/E10_30.jpg","cap":"Rectovaginal fistula: a faulty urorectal septum leaves the rectum opening into the vagina.","where":"BRS Embryology 5e, Figure 10-30","tb":1}],"q":[{"id":"D070-19-1","s":"Which structure is responsible for partitioning of the cloaca into developing urinary bladder and the rectum?","o":["A. Allantois","B. Urogenital sinus","C. Urorectal septum","D. Mesonephric blastema"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Urorectal septum","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Urorectal septum.</b> This wedge of mesoderm grows down between the allantois and the hindgut and divides the cloaca into the urogenital sinus in front (bladder) and the anorectal canal behind (rectum).<br>A. Allantois: a pouch off the cloaca into the cord; it becomes the urachus.<br>B. Urogenital sinus: the front product of the division, not the divider.<br>D. Mesonephric blastema: tissue of the embryonic kidney; it plays no part in dividing the cloaca.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 30 (Cloaca and Cloacal Division)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Harlem clicker","n":"GI development Kahoot 2026 (Harlem faculty review, 21 questions)","sl":19,"f":"D070"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"K19"}]},{"l":"ANAT-Kahoot","u":94,"label":"Ventral mesentery becomes falciform ligament + lesser omentum","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G001-19.jpg","cap":"Ventral mesentery becomes falciform ligament and lesser omentum","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 19","cu":"../sl/G001-19c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/F3900-224.jpg","cap":"Liver splits the ventral mesentery into falciform ligament and lesser omentum","where":"2023 TA review, slide 224"}],"q":[{"id":"D070-20-1","s":"Which of the following structures give rise to the ventral mesentery?","o":["A. falciform ligament + lesser omentum","B. falciform ligament + greater omentum","C. hepatogastric ligament + lesser omentum","D. hepatogastric ligament + greater omentum"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"falciform ligament + lesser omentum","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Falciform ligament + lesser omentum.</b> The stem asks it backwards: the ventral mesentery gives rise to these two. The liver splits it; the part in front of the liver becomes the falciform ligament, and the part between the liver and the stomach becomes the lesser omentum.<br>B. Falciform ligament + greater omentum: the greater omentum is dorsal mesogastrium.<br>C. Hepatogastric ligament + lesser omentum: the hepatogastric ligament is part of the lesser omentum, so the falciform ligament is missing.<br>D. Hepatogastric ligament + greater omentum: a piece of the lesser omentum plus a dorsal derivative.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 19 (Ventral Mesentery)","GI Development slide 6 (Mesenteries)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Harlem clicker","n":"GI development Kahoot 2026 (Harlem faculty review, 21 questions)","sl":20,"f":"D070"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"K20"}]},{"l":"ANAT-Kahoot","u":94,"label":"Hirschsprung: neural crest fails, ganglion cells absent","slides":[{"src":"../sl/G001-32.jpg","cap":"Megacolon: no postganglionic parasympathetic cell bodies; poor neural crest migration","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 32","cu":"../sl/G001-32c.jpg"},{"src":"../sl/G002-11.jpg","cap":"Hirschsprung disease: narrow distal segment with dilated colon above","where":"2026 Middletown clicker, slide 11"},{"src":"../sl/G002-12.jpg","cap":"Gut wall layers showing submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses","where":"2026 Middletown clicker, slide 12","cu":"../sl/G002-12c.jpg"}],"q":[{"id":"D070-21-1","s":"Which of the following results from improper migration of neural crest cells?","o":["A. Imperforate anus","B. Omphalocele","C. Hirschsprung’s Disease","D. Anorectal agenesis"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Hirschsprung’s Disease","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Hirschsprung's disease.</b> Neural crest cells migrate poorly down the gut and stop short, so the rectum and sigmoid colon lack ganglion cells, cannot relax, and the colon above dilates (megacolon).<br>A. Imperforate anus: the anal membrane fails to rupture.<br>B. Omphalocele: the midgut fails to return from the cord.<br>D. Anorectal agenesis: the rectum ends high with no anus; a defect of the hindgut's end, not of nerve cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 32 (Clinical Correlates)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Harlem clicker","n":"GI development Kahoot 2026 (Harlem faculty review, 21 questions)","sl":21,"f":"D070"}],"tier":1,"y26":1,"n":"K21"}]}],"sum":{},"units":[{"l":"ANAT-G1","h":"Intraperitoneal or retroperitoneal","p":"<p>Whether an organ is intraperitoneal or retroperitoneal depends on its history. Two facts settle it: whether the organ ever hung on a mesentery, and whether it kept that mesentery.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G077-5.jpg\" alt=\"Cross section: intraperitoneal organ on a mesentery; retroperitoneal organs behind\"></div><figcaption>Cross section: intraperitoneal organ on a mesentery; retroperitoneal organs behind <small>· 2026 TA session: Embryology 7 GI, slide 5</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Intraperitoneal: keeps its mesentery</h4>\n<p>An <b>intraperitoneal</b> organ hangs into the peritoneal cavity on a mesentery and keeps it for life. Visceral peritoneum wraps it almost all the way around, and it is usually mobile. Examples are the stomach, the start of the duodenum (first part), the jejunum, the ileum, the cecum, the appendix (on its mesoappendix), the transverse and sigmoid colon, the liver and the spleen. The <b>tail of the pancreas</b> is also intraperitoneal, because it stays inside a peritoneal fold, the splenorenal ligament.</p>\n<h4>Primary retroperitoneal: never had one</h4>\n<p>The peritoneum is a <b>serous membrane</b>. A <b>primary retroperitoneal</b> organ develops outside it, behind the peritoneal cavity, from the start and never has a mesentery. The examples to know are the kidneys, the ureters, the suprarenal (adrenal) glands and the uterine cervix. Plain ‘retroperitoneal’ means this primary group: an organ that developed outside the serous membrane. It is not wrapped in one layer of peritoneum, as an intraperitoneal organ is, and it never hung between the two layers of a mesentery.</p>\n<h4>Secondary retroperitoneal: had one, then lost it</h4>\n<p>A <b>secondary retroperitoneal</b> organ starts inside a mesentery, like an intraperitoneal organ. Then rotation of the gut pushes it against the posterior wall. The layer of mesentery touching the wall fuses with the parietal peritoneum there and disappears. The list to know is the <b>duodenum (parts 2–4), the pancreas except its tail, the ascending colon and the descending colon</b>. It happens in three steps.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Stomach rotation swings the duodenum to the right, and the duodenum and the pancreas inside its mesentery press against the back wall.</li>\n<li>The mesoduodenum fuses with the posterior wall, so duodenal parts 2–4 and the head, neck and body of the pancreas lose the peritoneum behind them.</li>\n<li>After the midgut returns in week 10 and settles, the mesenteries of the ascending and descending colon fuse to the back wall in the same way.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>In the adult, a primary and a secondary retroperitoneal organ look the same. Each has peritoneum only on its front surface, and its back lies flat on the posterior wall. Only the history tells them apart. A mnemonic for all retroperitoneal organs is SAD PUCKER: suprarenal glands, aorta and inferior vena cava, duodenum (parts 2–4), pancreas (except the tail), ureters, colon (ascending and descending), kidneys, esophagus (thoracic part) and rectum (partly). Its D, P and C are the secondary ones. The pancreas is a foregut organ, not a midgut one.</p>\n<h4>Two broader words</h4>\n<p><b>Extraperitoneal</b> describes any structure outside the peritoneal sac. It names a place, not a history. <b>Subperitoneal</b> (infraperitoneal) structures sit below the peritoneum in the pelvis and are covered only on their upper surface, like the urinary bladder.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Intraperitoneal: keeps mesentery; peritoneum nearly all around (stomach, jejunum, ileum, liver, spleen)</li>\n<li>Tail of pancreas: intraperitoneal, in splenorenal ligament; appendix keeps mesoappendix</li>\n<li>Primary retroperitoneal: never a mesentery → kidneys, ureters, suprarenals, cervix</li>\n<li>Plain ‘retroperitoneal’ = primary: developed outside the serous membrane (peritoneum)</li>\n<li>Secondary: mesentery, then fused to back wall → duodenum 2–4, pancreas (not tail)</li>\n<li>Secondary also: ascending + descending colon (after midgut returns)</li>\n<li>Both retroperitoneal types: peritoneum on front only; history differs</li>\n<li>SAD PUCKER: D, P, C = the secondary ones; pancreas = foregut</li>\n<li>Extraperitoneal = outside the sac (place); subperitoneal = below, top covered (bladder)</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Building the gut tube","sn":1,"pn":"1.3"},{"l":"ANAT-G1","h":"Three divisions: organs, arteries, nerves","p":"<p>The gut tube is divided into the foregut, the midgut and the hindgut. The dorsal aorta runs behind the whole tube. Below the diaphragm, each division gets one unpaired branch of the abdominal aorta, which reaches it through the dorsal mesentery. These arteries begin as vitelline arteries of the embryo. The gut later rotates and moves, but each region keeps its original artery. The rule therefore works both ways: an organ's division tells you its artery, and its artery tells you its division.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G001-22.jpg\" alt=\"Celiac trunk, superior and inferior mesenteric arteries running in the dorsal mesentery\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G001-22c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Celiac trunk, superior and inferior mesenteric arteries running in the dorsal mesentery\"></div></div><figcaption>Celiac trunk, superior and inferior mesenteric arteries running in the dorsal mesentery <small>· 2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 22</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Foregut: celiac trunk</h4>\n<p>The foregut makes the pharynx and the respiratory tract above the diaphragm. Below them it makes the esophagus, the stomach (including the pylorus, its caudal end, which controls emptying into the duodenum), the <b>first (upper) part of the duodenum</b>, the liver, the <b>gallbladder</b> and bile duct, and the pancreas. It makes organs in two ways. The esophagus, stomach and duodenum form as the tube changes shape. The respiratory tract, liver, gallbladder and pancreas grow out as buds. In the abdomen the foregut is supplied by the <b>celiac trunk</b>, which leaves the aorta at the twelfth thoracic vertebra (T12). Its branches, such as the left and right gastric arteries to the stomach, stay in foregut territory. The upper esophagus and the airway are foregut too: the respiratory diverticulum buds from the foregut and becomes the trachea, the bronchi and the lungs. These lie above the diaphragm, outside celiac territory, and get their blood from arteries of the neck and chest. The abdominal esophagus, below the diaphragm, is celiac territory (left gastric artery), as are the stomach, the first part of the duodenum and the gallbladder. The spleen gets celiac blood but is not a gut-tube organ; it forms from mesoderm in the dorsal mesogastrium.</p>\n<h4>Midgut and hindgut</h4>\n<p>The midgut makes the duodenum past the bile duct opening, the jejunum, the ileum, the cecum, the appendix, the ascending colon and the proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon. It is supplied by the <b>superior mesenteric artery (SMA)</b>, which leaves the aorta at the first lumbar vertebra (L1). The hindgut makes the distal third of the transverse colon, the descending colon, the sigmoid colon, the rectum, the upper anal canal and the urogenital sinus. It is supplied by the <b>inferior mesenteric artery (IMA)</b>, which leaves the aorta at the third lumbar vertebra (L3).</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G077-14.jpg\" alt=\"Aorta: celiac trunk at T12, SMA at L1, IMA at L3\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G077-14c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Aorta: celiac trunk at T12, SMA at L1, IMA at L3\"></div></div><figcaption>Aorta: celiac trunk at T12, SMA at L1, IMA at L3 <small>· 2026 TA session: Embryology 7 GI, slide 14</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Where one division meets the next</h4>\n<p>The foregut ends inside the duodenum, at the opening of the bile duct: the <b>major duodenal papilla</b> in the second part. The papilla is the small bump on the inner duodenal wall where the duct opens; the ampulla of Vater is the dilated end of the joined bile and pancreatic ducts just behind it, so both names mark the same boundary. Duodenum above the papilla is foregut and gets celiac blood. Duodenum past it is midgut and gets SMA blood. The duodenum is thus the transition point both between foregut and midgut and between the celiac trunk and the SMA. The pylorus lies wholly in the foregut, and the duodenojejunal flexure, where the fourth part turns into the jejunum, lies wholly in the midgut; neither marks a border. The liver bud and both pancreatic buds sprout from this last stretch of foregut. The midgut ends two-thirds of the way along the transverse colon, near the left colic (splenic) flexure. The duodenum therefore has foregut and midgut parts, and the transverse colon has midgut and hindgut parts.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G077-59.jpg\" alt=\"Adult foregut, midgut and hindgut organs with their three arteries\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G077-59c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Adult foregut, midgut and hindgut organs with their three arteries\"></div></div><figcaption>Adult foregut, midgut and hindgut organs with their three arteries <small>· 2026 TA session: Embryology 7 GI, slide 59</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Nerves and pain</h4>\n<p>Each division also has its own autonomic nerves, which travel in plexuses along the arteries. The foregut gets sympathetic fibers from the greater splanchnic nerves, thoracic cord segments 5 to 9 (T5–T9), to the celiac ganglion. The midgut gets the lesser splanchnic nerve, T10–T11, to the superior mesenteric ganglion. The jejunum and ileum also get sympathetic fibers through the greater and least splanchnic nerves, so the greater and the lesser splanchnic nerve both reach the jejunum. The hindgut gets the lumbar splanchnic nerves, lumbar segments 1 and 2 (L1–L2), to the inferior mesenteric ganglion. Parasympathetic fibers come from the vagus nerve for the foregut and midgut, and from the pelvic splanchnic nerves, sacral segments 2 to 4 (S2–S4), for the hindgut. Visceral pain is felt in the epigastric region for the foregut, around the umbilicus for the midgut, and in the hypogastric (suprapubic) region for the hindgut.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Foregut → celiac trunk (T12; gastric branches too); midgut → SMA (L1); hindgut → IMA (L3)</li>\n<li>Foregut: esophagus, stomach + pylorus (caudal foregut), duodenum part 1, liver, gallbladder, pancreas</li>\n<li>Upper esophagus + airway (trachea, bronchi, lungs): foregut but above diaphragm, not celiac</li>\n<li>Spleen: celiac blood, but dorsal mesogastrium mesoderm, not gut tube</li>\n<li>Midgut: duodenum past papilla → proximal 2/3 transverse colon</li>\n<li>Hindgut: distal 1/3 transverse colon → upper anal canal + urogenital sinus</li>\n<li>Major duodenal papilla (= ampulla of Vater site): foregut/midgut + celiac/SMA border</li>\n<li>Pylorus: all foregut; duodenojejunal flexure: all midgut</li>\n<li>Sympathetic: greater T5–T9 / lesser T10–T11 (jejunum: greater + lesser) / lumbar L1–L2</li>\n<li>Parasympathetic: vagus (fore + mid); pelvic splanchnic S2–S4 (hind)</li>\n<li>Pain: epigastric / periumbilical / hypogastric</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Building the gut tube","sn":1,"pn":"1.4"},{"l":"ANAT-G1","h":"Plug, reopen, and amniotic fluid","p":"<h4>Plug and reopen</h4>\n<p>The front part of the foregut makes both the airway and the esophagus. A partition, the <b>tracheoesophageal septum</b>, splits it into the trachea in front and the esophagus behind. As the embryo grows, the lining (epithelial) cells of the esophagus multiply so fast that they nearly fill the tube. By about week 5 the esophagus is an almost solid rod; this is <b>occlusion</b>. Then small fluid-filled spaces (vacuoles) open inside the plug as cells die by apoptosis, and the spaces merge. The lumen opens again starting by week 8; this is <b>recanalization</b>. The duodenum goes through the same plug-and-reopen cycle and finishes by week 8.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G001-10.jpg\" alt=\"Occlusion then recanalization of the gut tube, weeks 5 to 9\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G001-10c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Occlusion then recanalization of the gut tube, weeks 5 to 9\"></div></div><figcaption>Occlusion then recanalization of the gut tube, weeks 5 to 9 <small>· 2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 10</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>If the tube never reopens, the result is <b>atresia</b>: no lumen, only a solid epithelial plug and a full block. If it reopens only partly, the result is <b>stenosis</b>: a narrowed lumen. A narrowed duodenum stays narrow and may need surgery. A fistula is a different kind of defect, an abnormal channel between two organs such as the trachea and esophagus. Esophageal atresia is one result of failed recanalization; when it comes with a fistula, a faulty tracheoesophageal septum is also to blame.</p>\n<h4>Where amniotic fluid comes from and goes</h4>\n<p>Two flows keep amniotic fluid in balance. Fluid comes in mainly as <b>fetal urine</b>, especially in later pregnancy. Fluid goes out because the fetus <b>swallows</b> it throughout gestation. The swallowed fluid runs down the esophagus, stomach and duodenum, is absorbed in the intestine, and returns to the mother through the placenta. Think of it as a sink: urine is the running faucet, and the fetal gut is the drain.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G077-53.jpg\" alt=\"Fetus swallows fluid, intestine absorbs it; an upper block causes polyhydramnios\"></div><figcaption>Fetus swallows fluid, intestine absorbs it; an upper block causes polyhydramnios <small>· 2026 TA session: Embryology 7 GI, slide 53</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Polyhydramnios: too much fluid</h4>\n<p><b>Polyhydramnios</b> means too much amniotic fluid. It results from less swallowing and absorbing, or from more fetal urine. The main developmental gut cause is a blocked or narrowed upper gut that failed to recanalize: <b>esophageal atresia</b>, or duodenal atresia or stenosis. Swallowed fluid cannot reach the intestine to be absorbed, so it piles up. On exam the fundal height is larger than the dates, and ultrasound shows a high amniotic fluid index (AFI). With esophageal atresia the fetal stomach bubble is small or absent. More fetal urine, as with maternal diabetes, also raises the volume, and a fetus with anencephaly does not swallow. Many mild cases have no cause found.</p>\n<p>Blocks low in the gut, such as imperforate anus or Hirschsprung disease, do not raise the volume, because the upper intestine still absorbs the swallowed fluid. Ruptured membranes leak fluid and lower the volume. An umbilical hernia is only a weak spot in the body wall. A diaphragmatic hernia comes from failed pleuroperitoneal folds, and its main harm is small lungs. The amnion overproducing fluid, a placental problem or maternal lymphatics are not the mechanism.</p>\n<h4>Oligohydramnios: too little fluid</h4>\n<p><b>Oligohydramnios</b> means too little amniotic fluid. Its key cause is <b>bilateral renal agenesis</b>: with no kidneys there is no fetal urine, so in later pregnancy the fluid is very low. The cramped fetus shows the Potter sequence: a flat face, limb defects and small lungs. Renal hypoplasia, pelvic kidneys and polycystic kidneys all need kidneys to exist. Surfactant deficiency, the cause of respiratory distress syndrome, has nothing to do with fluid volume.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Tracheoesophageal septum: trachea in front, esophagus behind</li>\n<li>Week 5 epithelium plugs lumen → vacuoles (apoptosis) → recanalized by week 8</li>\n<li>Esophagus and duodenum both plug and reopen</li>\n<li>No recanalization → atresia (plug); partial → stenosis (narrow lumen)</li>\n<li>Fluid in = fetal urine; fluid out = swallowing → intestinal absorption → placenta</li>\n<li>Polyhydramnios: upper block (esophageal or duodenal atresia/stenosis) or more urine (diabetes)</li>\n<li>Signs: fundal height &gt; dates, high AFI, small stomach bubble</li>\n<li>Low blocks (imperforate anus, Hirschsprung): normal volume</li>\n<li>Oligohydramnios: bilateral renal agenesis → Potter (flat face, limb defects, small lungs)</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Foregut: esophagus, stomach, duodenum","sn":2,"pn":"2.1"},{"l":"ANAT-G1","h":"Esophageal atresia and fistula types","p":"<h4>What it is and why it happens</h4>\n<p>In <b>esophageal atresia</b> the upper esophagus ends in a blind pouch. It usually comes with a <b>tracheoesophageal fistula (TEF)</b>, an abnormal channel between the trachea and the esophagus. The fault lies in how the front of the foregut is divided. The <b>tracheoesophageal septum</b> forms between the lung bud in front and the esophagus behind and normally splits the two cleanly. When the septum forms abnormally, deviating <b>posteriorly</b> (not anteriorly), the tubes are not fully separated, leaving a blind pouch and a fistula. Atresia with a fistula is therefore a partitioning error. Failed recanalization of the esophagus, which normally reopens by about week 8, not week 11, also contributes to the atresia. Body-wall closure (gastroschisis), midgut return (omphalocele) and the vitelline duct (Meckel diverticulum) play no part.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G076-20.jpg\" alt=\"End of week 5: lung bud grows in front of the esophagus\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G076-20c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: End of week 5: lung bud grows in front of the esophagus\"></div></div><figcaption>End of week 5: lung bud grows in front of the esophagus <small>· 2026 GI supplemental session (Nov 6), slide 20</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The newborn</h4>\n<p>Before birth, ultrasound shows polyhydramnios and a persistently small fetal stomach, because the fetus cannot swallow fluid down. After birth the baby drools excessive saliva despite suctioning, since saliva cannot pass the blind pouch. A nasogastric (NG) tube cannot be passed into the stomach; on a chest X-ray it coils in the blind upper pouch. Choking, coughing and vomiting start with the first feed. The baby turns blue (cyanosis) when milk or stomach acid reaches the airway. Esophageal atresia often comes with other defects, such as anal or duodenal atresia and heart, spine and kidney defects. This cluster is the VACTERL association: vertebral, anal, cardiac, tracheoesophageal, renal and limb defects.</p>\n<h4>Sorting the five types</h4>\n<p>One rule sorts them: <b>air reaches the stomach only if the lower esophagus connects to the trachea</b>. Each breath then pushes air through the fistula into the stomach, and the belly swells. A second rule: feeds pour into the lungs only if the upper pouch opens into the trachea.</p>\n<p>The most common type, about 85%, is <b>atresia with a distal fistula</b>. The upper pouch is blind, so saliva and feeds come back up. The lower esophagus opens off the trachea, so the stomach fills with air, the belly distends, and stomach acid refluxes up into the lungs. In <b>pure atresia without a fistula</b>, both ends are blind. The baby vomits and has froth at the mouth at the first feed. The stomach has no air and the belly is flat, and with no airway link there is usually no cyanosis. In the <b>H-type fistula</b> there is no atresia: the esophagus is open, with a side channel to the trachea. Feeds go down, air reaches the stomach, and the baby coughs and chokes with feeds. In <b>atresia with a proximal fistula only</b>, the upper pouch opens into the trachea and the lower esophagus is a blind stump. Feeds pour into the airway, causing choking and cyanosis, and the stomach has no air. In <b>atresia with fistulas at both ends</b>, both pieces open into the trachea, so there is air in the stomach plus aspiration of feeds.</p>\n<h4>Breathing problems that are not gut defects</h4>\n<p>Respiratory distress syndrome (RDS) comes from premature lungs that lack surfactant; the baby struggles to breathe but does not drool. A congenital diaphragmatic hernia comes from pleuroperitoneal folds that fail to close. Bowel enters the chest, the lungs are small, and the belly is sunken (scaphoid). Polyhydramnios belongs to the pregnancy; it is a clue to atresia, not the baby's diagnosis. Incomplete canalization of the duodenum gives bilious vomiting rather than choking with feeds.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Esophageal atresia: blind upper pouch; usually + TEF</li>\n<li>Faulty tracheoesophageal septum (posterior deviation) + failed recanalization (by week 8)</li>\n<li>Polyhydramnios, small fetal stomach; drooling, choking/cyanosis; NG tube coils in pouch</li>\n<li>Often other defects: VACTERL (anal, duodenal atresia, heart, spine, kidney)</li>\n<li>Air in stomach only if lower esophagus joins trachea</li>\n<li>Distal TEF (~85%): blind pouch, air-filled stomach, acid into lungs</li>\n<li>Pure atresia: froth, no stomach air, flat belly, no cyanosis</li>\n<li>H-type: open esophagus, air in stomach, coughing with feeds</li>\n<li>Proximal TEF only: feeds into airway, no stomach air; both ends: air + aspiration</li>\n<li>RDS: no drooling; diaphragmatic hernia: scaphoid belly, small lungs</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Foregut: esophagus, stomach, duodenum","sn":2,"pn":"2.2"},{"l":"ANAT-G1","h":"Pylorus and duodenum: infant blocks","p":"<h4>How the duodenum forms</h4>\n<p>The duodenum is built from the last part of the foregut and the first part of the midgut. The border lies just past the <b>opening of the bile duct</b>, the major duodenal papilla in the second part. There the celiac trunk's territory ends and the superior mesenteric artery's (SMA's) begins. Stomach rotation swings the duodenum into a C-shaped loop on the right; its mesentery then fuses to the posterior wall, so parts 2–4 become secondary retroperitoneal. Like the esophagus, the duodenum plugs and reopens. In weeks 5–6 its endodermal lining multiplies and fills the lumen as a solid cord. In weeks 6–8 vacuoles appear and merge. <b>Recanalization is finished by week 8</b>, the end of the embryonic period.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G001-17.jpg\" alt=\"Duodenal stenosis (A) and atresia (B); partial and failed recanalization\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G001-17c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Duodenal stenosis (A) and atresia (B); partial and failed recanalization\"></div></div><figcaption>Duodenal stenosis (A) and atresia (B); partial and failed recanalization <small>· 2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 17</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Duodenal atresia and stenosis</h4>\n<p>If the duodenum never reopens, the result is <b>duodenal atresia</b>: an epithelial plug and a full block. If it reopens only partly, the result is <b>duodenal stenosis</b>: a narrowed lumen. Both come from failed recanalization after epithelial proliferation. Proliferation always happens first; the error lies in the reopening. Rotation, retroperitoneal fusion, the vitelline duct, neural crest and the pancreatic ducts play no part. Atresias lower down, in the jejunum and ileum, are usually caused by a vascular accident instead. The fetus cannot pass swallowed fluid on to be absorbed, so the mother has <b>polyhydramnios</b>.</p>\n<p>Most duodenal atresias lie <b>distal to the bile duct opening</b>, so the vomit contains bile. Vomiting starts on the first day. An X-ray shows the <b>double bubble</b>: air in the stomach and in the first part of the duodenum. In complete atresia no gas reaches the bowel beyond. In stenosis a little gas gets through, and on an upper gastrointestinal contrast study a thin stream of contrast passes the narrowed segment into the jejunum. The baby passes little meconium and has a swollen upper belly. Duodenal atresia and stenosis are linked to <b>Down syndrome</b> (trisomy 21). Clues are an older mother, a thick nuchal translucency on the 12-week ultrasound, and the baby's flat face, upslanting eyes and single palmar crease.</p>\n<h4>Bile in the vomit gives the level</h4>\n<p>Bile enters the duodenum at the major duodenal papilla. A block above the papilla gives vomit without bile (<b>nonbilious</b>). A block below it gives green, <b>bilious</b> vomit. Esophageal atresia, a tracheoesophageal fistula and pyloric stenosis all lie above the papilla, so their vomit has no bile.</p>\n<h4>Hypertrophic pyloric stenosis</h4>\n<p>The pylorus is the outlet of the stomach, so it is foregut. Pyloric stenosis is one of the most common gut obstructions in infancy. The cause is overgrowth (hypertrophy) of the circular smooth muscle of the pylorus, which becomes thick and narrows the outlet.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G001-14.jpg\" alt=\"Pyloric stenosis: thickened pylorus and projectile vomiting\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G001-14c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Pyloric stenosis: thickened pylorus and projectile vomiting\"></div></div><figcaption>Pyloric stenosis: thickened pylorus and projectile vomiting <small>· 2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 14</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The baby feeds normally at first, then starts vomiting at about 2–6 weeks; most are boys, often first-born. The vomit has no bile. It grows more forceful until it is <b>projectile</b>, because the stomach behind the block is distended. A firm, <b>olive-shaped mass</b> is felt at the right costal margin. The baby gains little weight and becomes dehydrated. Surgery that splits the thick muscle (pyloromyotomy) repairs it.</p>\n<h4>Other infant blocks to tell apart</h4>\n<p><b>Annular pancreas</b> is a ring of pancreas around the second part of the duodenum. Its signs start at birth, and there is no olive mass. Esophageal stenosis causes trouble swallowing and spitting up, with no abdominal mass. <b>Extrahepatic biliary atresia</b> is not a vomiting disorder: bile cannot reach the gut, so the baby has jaundice, pale stools and dark urine. Meckel diverticulum causes painless rectal bleeding, not vomiting at birth. Placental abruption, breech position and eclampsia have nothing to do with the baby's duodenum.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G077-55.jpg\" alt=\"Polyhydramnios causes: duodenal atresia, annular pancreas; pyloric stenosis is not one\"></div><figcaption>Polyhydramnios causes: duodenal atresia, annular pancreas; pyloric stenosis is not one <small>· 2026 TA session: Embryology 7 GI, slide 55</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Duodenum = foregut end + midgut start; border at major papilla (celiac → SMA)</li>\n<li>Plugs weeks 5–6, vacuoles weeks 6–8, recanalized by week 8</li>\n<li>No recanalization → atresia; partial → stenosis</li>\n<li>Duodenal atresia: day-1 bilious vomiting, double bubble, polyhydramnios, little meconium</li>\n<li>Atresia/stenosis ↔ Down syndrome (older mother, thick nuchal translucency)</li>\n<li>Atresia: no distal gas; stenosis: some distal gas</li>\n<li>Block above papilla → nonbilious; below → bilious</li>\n<li>Pyloric stenosis: thick circular muscle; 2–6 wk; nonbilious → projectile; olive mass</li>\n<li>Pyloric stenosis: dehydration, poor weight gain; pyloromyotomy</li>\n<li>Annular pancreas: from birth, no olive; biliary atresia: jaundice, pale stool, dark urine</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Foregut: esophagus, stomach, duodenum","sn":2,"pn":"2.4"},{"l":"ANAT-G1","h":"Liver bud and gallbladder","p":"<h4>The hepatic diverticulum</h4>\n<p>The liver, the gallbladder and the bile ducts all grow from one bud of foregut endoderm, the <b>hepatic diverticulum</b> (liver bud). It sprouts from the <b>ventral</b> wall of the lowest (inferior) part of the foregut, the future duodenum. It grows forward into the <b>septum transversum</b>, a thick plate of mesoderm between the heart and the gut. Most of the septum later becomes the central tendon of the diaphragm. Put another way, the liver grows within the ventral mesogastrium: the thin part of the septum transversum that the liver grows into, between the stomach and the front body wall, is the ventral mesentery.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G076-19.jpg\" alt=\"At 4½ weeks the liver grows into the septum transversum\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G076-19c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: At 4½ weeks the liver grows into the septum transversum\"></div></div><figcaption>At 4½ weeks the liver grows into the septum transversum <small>· 2026 GI supplemental session (Nov 6), slide 19</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>How the bud branches</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>The large cranial part, the hepatic duct part, grows into the septum and forms the liver cells (parenchyma) and the bile capillaries.</li>\n<li>A small caudal branch, the cystic diverticulum, forms the <b>gallbladder</b> and the <b>cystic duct</b>.</li>\n<li>The stalk that joins the bud to the gut narrows into the <b>bile duct</b>.</li>\n<li>Rotation of the stomach moves the diverticulum from a ventral to a lateral position.</li>\n<li>By week 9 the liver makes up about 10% of the weight of the fetus.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The outgrowth into the septum transversum is the key step. If the diverticulum fails to grow out and branch there, its cystic branch never forms, and the gallbladder is missing. Stomach rotation only changes the bud's position. The dorsal pancreatic bud, not the liver bud, makes the pancreatic tail and the accessory duct. Duodenal submucosal glands come from the duodenal lining.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G001-18.jpg\" alt=\"Hepatic diverticulum into septum transversum; cystic diverticulum; both pancreatic buds\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G001-18c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Hepatic diverticulum into septum transversum; cystic diverticulum; both pancreatic buds\"></div></div><figcaption>Hepatic diverticulum into septum transversum; cystic diverticulum; both pancreatic buds <small>· 2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 18</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Two sources, one liver</h4>\n<p>The liver has two germ-layer sources. <b>Endoderm</b> of the hepatic diverticulum makes the hepatocytes (liver cells), which grow into the septum as the <b>hepatic cords</b>, as well as the bile capillaries, the epithelial lining of the bile ducts, the gallbladder and the cystic duct. <b>Mesoderm</b> of the septum transversum makes the fibrous tissue (capsule and framework), the <b>visceral peritoneum</b> that covers the liver, and the <b>Kupffer cells</b>. The rest of the septum makes the central tendon of the diaphragm and the ventral mesentery, so the septum gives everything around the liver cells but never the hepatic cords themselves. Kupffer cells are the liver's macrophages, part of the mononuclear phagocyte system. They line the sinusoids and clear old red blood cells and debris arriving in the portal blood. They are mesoderm, not endoderm, surface ectoderm, neuroectoderm or neural crest. The blood-forming cells also come from septum transversum mesoderm, and the sinusoids form from the vitelline and umbilical veins. In the fetus the liver is itself a blood-forming organ.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G002-18.jpg\" alt=\"Liver sinusoid: hepatocytes and Kupffer cells lining the sinusoidal lumen\"></div><figcaption>Liver sinusoid: hepatocytes and Kupffer cells lining the sinusoidal lumen <small>· 2026 Middletown clicker, slide 18</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Neighbors</h4>\n<p>The ventral pancreatic bud sprouts at the same spot as the hepatic diverticulum, next to the bile duct. The dorsal pancreatic bud sprouts from the opposite (dorsal) wall. The spleen is not a bud at all; it forms in the dorsal mesogastrium. The ventral mesentery that the liver grows into becomes the lesser omentum and falciform ligament.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Hepatic diverticulum: endoderm bud from ventral wall of inferior foregut</li>\n<li>Grows into septum transversum (= ventral mesogastrium; same tissue)</li>\n<li>Hepatic duct part → hepatocytes + bile capillaries</li>\n<li>Cystic diverticulum → gallbladder + cystic duct; stalk → bile duct</li>\n<li>No outgrowth into septum → no gallbladder</li>\n<li>Stomach rotation: bud ventral → lateral; liver ≈ 10% of fetal weight by week 9</li>\n<li>Endoderm → hepatocytes (hepatic cords) + bile duct epithelium</li>\n<li>Septum transversum mesoderm → fibrous tissue + Kupffer cells (sinusoid macrophages)</li>\n<li>Septum also → central tendon, ventral mesentery, liver's visceral peritoneum; never hepatic cords</li>\n<li>Ventral pancreatic bud: same spot; spleen: dorsal mesogastrium, not a bud</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Foregut glands: liver and pancreas","sn":3,"pn":"3.1"},{"l":"ANAT-G1","h":"Two pancreatic buds","p":"<h4>Two buds</h4>\n<p>The pancreas grows from <b>two buds of foregut endoderm</b> at the duodenum. The <b>dorsal bud</b> appears first. It grows from the dorsal wall of the duodenum into the dorsal mesentery. The <b>ventral bud</b> grows from the ventral wall at the same spot as the hepatic diverticulum, next to the bile duct.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G001-20.jpg\" alt=\"Ventral bud swings behind and below the dorsal bud; buds and ducts fuse\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G001-20c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Ventral bud swings behind and below the dorsal bud; buds and ducts fuse\"></div></div><figcaption>Ventral bud swings behind and below the dorsal bud; buds and ducts fuse <small>· 2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 20</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>How the buds meet</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>The stomach rotates 90° to the left.</li>\n<li>The stomach's rotation swings the duodenum to the right into a C-shaped loop.</li>\n<li>The turning duodenum carries the ventral bud around its back, so the ventral bud ends up <b>posterior and inferior</b> to the dorsal bud.</li>\n<li>The ventral bud pulls the bile duct along with it, so the bile duct ends up behind the head of the pancreas.</li>\n<li>The tissue (parenchyma) and the ducts of the two buds fuse.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Two rotations therefore build the pancreas: the stomach's, which drives the turn, and the duodenum's, which carries the bud. The liver does not turn the pancreas; it simply grows into the septum transversum. After fusion, the ventral bud makes the <b>uncinate process</b> and the lower (inferior) part of the head. The dorsal bud makes the rest of the head, the neck, the body and the <b>tail</b>. The main pancreatic duct is made from the ventral bud's duct plus the far part of the dorsal bud's duct. The near part of the dorsal duct may stay as the accessory pancreatic duct. The head and the uncinate process end up nested in the C-loop of the duodenum, against its second (descending) part, with the bile duct running behind the head to the major papilla. Only the tail leaves the loop: it runs in the splenorenal ligament toward the hilum of the spleen. The hepatoduodenal ligament carries the bile duct down to the duodenum but holds no pancreas.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G077-39.jpg\" alt=\"Bud formation, rotation of ventral bud with bile duct, fusion of ducts\"></div><figcaption>Bud formation, rotation of ventral bud with bile duct, fusion of ducts <small>· 2026 TA session: Embryology 7 GI, slide 39</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>What the pancreas is made of</h4>\n<p>The pancreas starts as an intraperitoneal organ in the dorsal mesentery. Later all of it except the tail fuses to the posterior wall and becomes secondary retroperitoneal. All of its epithelial cells come from endoderm: the ducts, the enzyme-making acini and the hormone-making <b>islet cells</b>. Its connective tissue and the smooth muscle around it come from visceral (splanchnic) mesoderm. Ectoderm makes the skin and nervous system, neural crest makes gut nerve cells and the adrenal medulla, and somatic mesoderm makes the body wall. None of them makes islet cells.</p>\n<h4>Bud defects</h4>\n<p>In <b>annular pancreas</b>, the ventral bud is <b>bilobed</b> (two-lobed, bifid). During gut rotation one lobe swings behind the duodenum and the other in front. The two meet and make a ring of pancreas around the second part of the duodenum. The ring squeezes the duodenum and, when severe, blocks it completely. Like duodenal atresia, it can cause polyhydramnios before birth and a double bubble on X-ray. The vomiting can be bilious or nonbilious, depending on the level.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G001-21.jpg\" alt=\"Annular pancreas: a bilobed ventral bud wraps both sides of the duodenum\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G001-21c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Annular pancreas: a bilobed ventral bud wraps both sides of the duodenum\"></div></div><figcaption>Annular pancreas: a bilobed ventral bud wraps both sides of the duodenum <small>· 2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 21</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Other bud errors leave no ring. If the ducts of the two buds fail to fuse, the result is <b>pancreas divisum</b>, with two separate duct systems. If the dorsal bud never forms, the pancreas lacks its neck, body and tail. If the ventral bud is missing, there is no uncinate process. Over-rotation of the stomach and an overgrown septum transversum are not causes. Midgut volvulus is a different cause of bilious vomiting: a twisted, poorly fixed midgut.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Dorsal bud: dorsal duodenal wall, into dorsal mesentery, appears first</li>\n<li>Ventral bud: ventral wall, same spot as hepatic diverticulum, beside bile duct</li>\n<li>Stomach rotation → duodenal C-loop → ventral bud posterior + inferior to dorsal bud</li>\n<li>Ventral bud drags bile duct; parenchyma and ducts fuse</li>\n<li>Ventral → uncinate + lower head; dorsal → rest of head, neck, body, tail</li>\n<li>Main duct = ventral duct + distal dorsal duct; accessory = proximal dorsal duct</li>\n<li>Head + uncinate: in duodenal C-loop (part 2); tail: splenorenal ligament</li>\n<li>Intraperitoneal first → secondary retroperitoneal except tail</li>\n<li>Ducts, acini, islets: endoderm; connective tissue: visceral mesoderm</li>\n<li>Annular: bilobed ventral bud rings duodenum part 2 (double bubble); divisum: ducts unfused</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Foregut glands: liver and pancreas","sn":3,"pn":"3.3"},{"l":"ANAT-G1","h":"Herniation and rotation of the loop","p":"<h4>What the midgut makes</h4>\n<p>The midgut makes the duodenum distal to the bile duct opening, the jejunum, the ileum, the cecum, the appendix, the ascending colon and the proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon. All of it is supplied by the <b>superior mesenteric artery (SMA)</b>. A block in the duodenum below the bile duct opening, with bilious vomiting, is therefore a midgut block. The first part of the duodenum, above that opening, is foregut and gets celiac blood. The distal third of the transverse colon, the descending colon, the rectum and the urinary bladder (from the urogenital sinus) belong to the hindgut, supplied by the inferior mesenteric artery (IMA). The liver and pancreas are foregut buds.</p>\n<h4>The primary intestinal loop</h4>\n<p>The midgut lengthens fast into a U-shaped loop, the <b>primary intestinal loop</b>. The SMA runs down the middle of the loop, in its mesentery, so the SMA is both the loop's blood supply and its axis of rotation. The apex of the loop stays joined to the yolk sac by the vitelline duct. The <b>cranial (upper, proximal) arm</b> becomes the distal duodenum, the jejunum and the upper ileum; it grows very fast and coils into intestinal loops. The <b>caudal (lower, distal) arm</b> becomes the lower ileum, the cecum, the appendix, the ascending colon and the proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon. It forms a small cecal swelling, the marker of the future cecum. The appendix grows as a small pouch (diverticulum) off the cecum, not directly off one of the arms.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G076-22.jpg\" alt=\"Primary intestinal loop: cephalic and caudal limbs around the SMA, vitelline duct\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G076-22c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Primary intestinal loop: cephalic and caudal limbs around the SMA, vitelline duct\"></div></div><figcaption>Primary intestinal loop: cephalic and caudal limbs around the SMA, vitelline duct <small>· 2026 GI supplemental session (Nov 6), slide 22</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Herniation, rotation and return, in order</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>At the <b>start of week 6</b>, the abdomen is too small for the fast-growing gut, the big liver and the kidneys, so the loop pushes out through the umbilical ring into the extraembryonic coelom of the cord. This normal event is the physiologic (congenital) umbilical herniation.</li>\n<li>While herniated, the loop turns <b>90° counterclockwise (CCW)</b>, seen from the front, around the SMA. The cranial arm moves right and the caudal arm moves left.</li>\n<li>The small intestine keeps lengthening in the cord, so loops in the cord at week 8 are normal; the return has not happened yet.</li>\n<li>In <b>week 10</b> the loops slide back into the abdomen (retraction) and turn another <b>180° CCW</b>, for a total of <b>270°</b>. The small intestine returns first.</li>\n<li>The <b>cecum is the last part to return</b>. It lands in the right upper quadrant (RUQ) under the liver, then descends to the right lower quadrant (RLQ).</li>\n</ol>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G001-24.jpg\" alt=\"Midgut loop herniates into the cord, rotates around the SMA, returns (A to E)\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G001-24c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Midgut loop herniates into the cord, rotates around the SMA, returns (A to E)\"></div></div><figcaption>Midgut loop herniates into the cord, rotates around the SMA, returns (A to E) <small>· 2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 24</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Clockwise turning is the wrong direction; it is reversed rotation. At the end of normal rotation the transverse colon passes in front of the SMA. Because the SMA is the axis, an extra or wrong turn wraps the mesentery around the SMA and kinks it, so blood flow to the midgut drops, as in a volvulus. The celiac trunk, the IMA, the renal arteries and the common iliac arteries are not affected, because their territories are not in the herniated loop. If the cranial arm loses its blood supply during herniation, the jejunum suffers; the cecum and appendix belong to the caudal arm, and the stomach and gallbladder are foregut.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G001-25.jpg\" alt=\"Cecal swelling on the caudal limb; appendix grows off the cecum\"></div><figcaption>Cecal swelling on the caudal limb; appendix grows off the cecum <small>· 2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 25</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Midgut: duodenum past bile duct opening → proximal 2/3 transverse colon; all SMA</li>\n<li>Loop: SMA = blood supply + rotation axis; apex → vitelline duct</li>\n<li>Cranial arm → distal duodenum, jejunum, upper ileum (coils fast)</li>\n<li>Caudal arm → lower ileum, cecum, appendix, ascending + 2/3 transverse colon</li>\n<li>Start of week 6: herniates into cord; 90° CCW around SMA</li>\n<li>Week 8: loops in cord normal, still elongating, not yet returned</li>\n<li>Week 10: return + 180° CCW = 270° total; small bowel first</li>\n<li>Cecum last (RUQ → RLQ); appendix = diverticulum of cecum</li>\n<li>Extra or wrong twist → mesentery kinks SMA → midgut ischemia</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Midgut: loop, rotation, return","sn":4,"pn":"4.1"},{"l":"ANAT-G1","h":"Settling in, and rotation errors","p":"<h4>Fixing the bowel in place</h4>\n<p>Once the midgut has returned and reached its adult position, some of its mesenteries fuse to the posterior wall and others stay free. The mesenteries of the <b>ascending and descending colon fuse</b> with the posterior abdominal wall. These parts are therefore <b>secondary retroperitoneal</b>: they started with a mesentery and now have visceral peritoneum only on the front. The appendix, the cecum and the sigmoid colon keep their mesenteries and stay intraperitoneal. The transverse colon's mesentery fuses with the greater omentum, and the transverse colon stays intraperitoneal. The mesentery proper of the jejunum and ileum attaches to the posterior wall along a diagonal line, from the left upper quadrant (LUQ) to the right lower quadrant (RLQ).</p>\n<p>Compare organs outside the midgut. The kidney is primary retroperitoneal and never had a mesentery. The stomach keeps its mesenteries as the omenta, the spleen stays in the dorsal mesogastrium, and the liver is intraperitoneal apart from its bare area.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G001-26.jpg\" alt=\"Adult mesenteries: transverse mesocolon, mesentery proper, sigmoid mesocolon\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G001-26c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Adult mesenteries: transverse mesocolon, mesentery proper, sigmoid mesocolon\"></div></div><figcaption>Adult mesenteries: transverse mesocolon, mesentery proper, sigmoid mesocolon <small>· 2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 26</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The normal end point</h4>\n<p>Normal rotation is 270° counterclockwise (CCW) around the superior mesenteric artery (SMA): 90° in the cord and 180° on return. It leaves the cecum in the RLQ, the transverse colon passing in front of the SMA, and the duodenojejunal junction left of the midline.</p>\n<h4>Rotation errors</h4>\n<p>In <b>nonrotation</b>, the loop makes only its first 90° turn and skips the last 180° on return. The small intestine therefore stays on the <b>right</b>, the large intestine on the <b>left</b>, and the cecum and appendix lie low in the midline, in the suprapubic region. It can go unnoticed until surgery for something else, even in a teenager. In <b>reversed rotation</b>, the loop first turns 90° <b>clockwise</b> instead of CCW. The transverse colon then ends up <b>behind the SMA</b> (and behind the duodenum). Too much counterclockwise turning does something else: it twists the mesentery around the SMA (volvulus). In <b>mixed rotation</b>, the turning stops partway. The cecum sits high, just below the pylorus in the right upper quadrant (RUQ), and fibrous bands tie it to the posterior wall across the second part of the duodenum. The duodenojejunal junction lies right of the midline, with the small bowel mostly on the right. A newborn with malrotation and bilious vomiting is a surgical emergency.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G076-27.jpg\" alt=\"Normal end point: transverse colon in front, cecum and appendix low on right\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G076-27c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Normal end point: transverse colon in front, cecum and appendix low on right\"></div></div><figcaption>Normal end point: transverse colon in front, cecum and appendix low on right <small>· 2026 GI supplemental session (Nov 6), slide 27</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>A <b>volvulus</b> is a poorly fixed loop that twists on its mesentery. The twist cuts off SMA blood flow and causes bilious vomiting and dying bowel; it is a twist, not a fixed layout. <b>Duplication</b> of the intestine is an extra cyst or tube of bowel beside normal bowel, and the layout is normal. Neither puts all the small bowel on the right and all the colon on the left. A mobile hepatic flexure cannot move the whole bowel. Failed fusion of the lateral body folds gives gastroschisis, and failed duodenal recanalization gives duodenal atresia or stenosis.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>After return: ascending + descending mesocolons fuse → secondary retroperitoneal</li>\n<li>Cecum, appendix, sigmoid keep mesenteries (intraperitoneal)</li>\n<li>Transverse mesocolon fuses with greater omentum; mesentery proper diagonal LUQ → RLQ</li>\n<li>Normal: 270° CCW → cecum RLQ, transverse colon in front of SMA</li>\n<li>Nonrotation: only 90°; small bowel right, colon left, cecum suprapubic midline</li>\n<li>Reversed: 90° clockwise first → transverse colon behind SMA</li>\n<li>Mixed (malrotation): cecum high in RUQ, bands across duodenum, duodenojejunal junction right</li>\n<li>Volvulus: twist → SMA ischemia, bilious vomiting; duplication: extra bowel, normal layout</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Midgut: loop, rotation, return","sn":4,"pn":"4.2"},{"l":"ANAT-G1","h":"Omphalocele and gastroschisis","p":"<p>Both defects leave bowel outside the abdomen at birth, but each comes from a different step going wrong. Omphalocele is a failure of the <b>midgut to return</b>. Gastroschisis is a failure of the <b>body wall to close</b>. The normal herniation of the midgut also makes the front body wall more prone to such defects.</p>\n<h4>Omphalocele: the loop never comes back</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>At the start of week 6, the midgut loop herniates through the primitive umbilical ring into the umbilical cord. This is normal.</li>\n<li>In week 10 the loops should return to the abdomen; loops still out by week 11 have failed to return.</li>\n<li>In omphalocele the midgut fails to return, so the hernia persists at the umbilicus.</li>\n<li>The bowel, often with part of the liver, stays inside a sac of <b>amnion</b> lined by <b>peritoneum</b>. There is no skin and no muscle over it, and the cord comes off the sac.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>On sight, an omphalocele is a large midline sac at the umbilicus with a shiny, see-through covering, and no bowel is exposed. It often comes with a <b>trisomy</b> (such as 13 or 18) and other anomalies, and its mortality varies with those anomalies.</p>\n<h4>Gastroschisis: the wall never closes</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>During lateral folding (week 4), the <b>lateral body folds</b> bring the somatic lateral plate mesoderm forward from both sides.</li>\n<li>Normally the two sides meet and fuse in the midline, closing the anterior abdominal wall.</li>\n<li>In gastroschisis the bilateral somatic lateral plate mesoderm <b>fails to fuse</b>, leaving a full-thickness gap beside the umbilicus.</li>\n<li>Bowel slips out through the gap and floats free in the amniotic fluid, with no peritoneum, no amnion and no skin over it.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The gap lies <b>lateral to the umbilicus</b>, usually on the right, and the cord itself is normal. Only bowel comes out; the liver stays inside. On prenatal ultrasound, bowel loops float freely in the amniotic cavity with no covering membrane. A trisomy is not typical. Its mortality is 5–10%. The one feature that tells the two defects apart is the <b>amniotic membrane</b>: present over an omphalocele, absent over gastroschisis.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G001-27.jpg\" alt=\"Omphalocele in a shiny amnion sac (top); gastroschisis with bare bowel (middle)\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G001-27c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Omphalocele in a shiny amnion sac (top); gastroschisis with bare bowel (middle)\"></div></div><figcaption>Omphalocele in a shiny amnion sac (top); gastroschisis with bare bowel (middle) <small>· 2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 27</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The folds and the ring</h4>\n<p>Each embryonic fold closes a different part of the body. The head fold makes the foregut and closes the upper chest wall; its failure can leave the heart outside the chest (ectopia cordis). The tail fold makes the hindgut and cloaca and closes the lower wall; its failure is linked to bladder exstrophy. The lateral body folds close the gut tube and the anterior abdominal wall. The neural folds roll up into the neural tube. The primitive umbilical ring is the opening in the body wall that the midgut loop passes through; a loop that fails to come back through it is an omphalocele.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Omphalocele: midgut fails to return (normally back in week 10)</li>\n<li>Omphalocele: midline, through umbilicus; sac of amnion + peritoneum; no skin or muscle</li>\n<li>Omphalocele: bowel ± liver; cord on sac; often trisomy 13/18</li>\n<li>Gastroschisis: bilateral somatic lateral plate mesoderm (lateral body folds) fails to fuse</li>\n<li>Gastroschisis: right of umbilicus, normal cord, bare bowel, no liver, no trisomy</li>\n<li>Gastroschisis mortality 5–10%; omphalocele: depends on associated anomalies</li>\n<li>Key difference: amniotic membrane</li>\n<li>Head fold failure → ectopia cordis; tail fold → bladder exstrophy</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Umbilical defects and Meckel diverticulum","sn":5,"pn":"5.1"},{"l":"ANAT-G1","h":"Naming the defect; umbilical hernia","p":"<h4>Umbilical hernia: back in, then out again</h4>\n<p>An umbilical hernia starts with a normal return. The midgut loop comes back into the abdomen in week 10, but the umbilical ring does not close firmly and stays weak. Later, raised pressure inside the abdomen pushes greater omentum or small intestine out through the weak ring. The hernia passes under the skin, so it is covered by <b>skin and subcutaneous tissue</b>. In an infant it is a soft bulge at the umbilicus that grows when the baby cries, strains or coughs, and it may show up weeks or months after birth. A baby born with a front-wall defect is more prone to it, and adults get umbilical hernias too, most often women and obese patients. The weak spot is the umbilical ring, not the lateral abdominal muscles. Omphalocele and gastroschisis, by contrast, are only congenital and are present at birth.</p>\n<h4>Naming a defect at birth</h4>\n<p>Look first at the covering, then at the site. Loops of bowel with <b>no covering at all</b>, coming out <b>beside</b> a normal cord, usually to its right, are <b>gastroschisis</b>. This is a full-thickness wall gap from failed fusion of the lateral folds. In gastroschisis the intestines do not herniate into the cord, are not covered by a membrane, rarely come with a trisomy, and leave the liver inside. A shiny, see-through sac in the <b>midline</b>, with the cord coming off it and bowel and often liver inside, is an <b>omphalocele</b>. A <b>skin-covered</b> bulge at the umbilicus that grows with straining is an <b>umbilical hernia</b>. Skin is the decisive difference from omphalocele, whose sac is amnion with no skin or muscle.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G077-72.jpg\" alt=\"Omphalocele in an amnion sac versus gastroschisis with bare bowel; reversed rotation\"></div><figcaption>Omphalocele in an amnion sac versus gastroschisis with bare bowel; reversed rotation <small>· 2026 TA session: Embryology 7 GI, slide 72</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Other hernias and other problems</h4>\n<p>An <b>epigastric hernia</b> pushes through the linea alba above the umbilicus and occurs mostly in adults. An <b>indirect inguinal hernia</b> passes through the deep inguinal ring into the inguinal canal and can reach the scrotum. A Meckel diverticulum is a pouch of the ileum that stays inside the abdomen. A midgut volvulus is bowel twisted inside the abdomen, causing bilious vomiting. A tracheoesophageal fistula (TEF) is an abnormal channel between the trachea and esophagus. Polyhydramnios is too much amniotic fluid during the pregnancy, not a wall defect. Pyloric stenosis is a thick pyloric muscle with projectile vomiting.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Umbilical hernia: normal return, weak umbilical ring, bulge appears later</li>\n<li>Covered by skin + subcutaneous tissue; grows with crying, straining, coughing</li>\n<li>Contents: greater omentum, small intestine; adults too (women, obesity)</li>\n<li>Bare bowel beside a normal cord (right side) → gastroschisis</li>\n<li>Gastroschisis: no sac, no liver, no trisomy</li>\n<li>Midline amnion sac, cord on sac, liver inside → omphalocele</li>\n<li>Skin-covered umbilical bulge → umbilical hernia</li>\n<li>Epigastric: linea alba above umbilicus, adults; indirect inguinal: deep ring</li>\n<li>Meckel diverticulum, volvulus: inside the abdomen</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Umbilical defects and Meckel diverticulum","sn":5,"pn":"5.2"},{"l":"ANAT-G1","h":"Meckel diverticulum","p":"<h4>The vitelline duct normally disappears</h4>\n<p>The vitelline duct, also called the yolk stalk or omphaloenteric duct, joins the apex of the midgut loop to the yolk sac. Normally it narrows, closes and disappears. If its gut end persists, it leaves an outpouching of the <b>ileum</b>: a <b>Meckel (ileal) diverticulum</b>. Meckel diverticulum is therefore a remnant of the vitelline duct, the result of a yolk stalk that failed to regress.</p>\n<h4>Key facts</h4>\n<p>It is an outgrowth of the distal ileum, on the side opposite the mesentery (antimesenteric). It is about <b>2 inches</b> long and lies about <b>2 feet</b> from the ileocecal junction. It occurs in about 2–3% of people. As the small intestine returns to the body cavity, the diverticulum can stay anchored to the anterior abdominal wall at the umbilicus.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G001-28.jpg\" alt=\"Meckel diverticulum: free pouch with ulcer (A), fibrous cord (B), fistula (C)\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G001-28c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Meckel diverticulum: free pouch with ulcer (A), fibrous cord (B), fistula (C)\"></div></div><figcaption>Meckel diverticulum: free pouch with ulcer (A), fibrous cord (B), fistula (C) <small>· 2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 28</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Three forms</h4>\n<p>In the <b>free pouch</b>, only the ileal end of the duct persists. Its lining often holds ectopic <b>stomach (or pancreatic) tissue</b>. The acid ulcerates the nearby ileum and causes <b>painless rectal bleeding</b>, bright to dark red, in a child, which can lower the hemoglobin. A technetium-99m pertechnetate scan finds the ectopic gastric mucosa in the distal ileum. In the <b>fibrous cord</b> form, a cord runs from the pouch to the umbilicus, and bowel can twist around it and block. In the <b>vitelline (omphaloenteric) fistula</b>, the whole duct stays open from the ileum to the umbilicus. The infant's umbilicus is swollen and will not heal. It drains secretions or <b>stool</b>, often foul-smelling, and a lump may be felt in the right lower quadrant. The fistula is thus a route that carries fecal matter to the umbilicus.</p>\n<h4>Names not to mix up</h4>\n<p>Several other remnants and defects sound similar. Rathke pouch is a pocket in the roof of the primitive mouth that makes the front part of the pituitary gland. The round ligament of the liver (ligamentum teres) is the closed umbilical vein, while the round ligament of the uterus is the leftover gubernaculum. Omphalocele and gastroschisis put bowel outside the body at birth. Failed recanalization gives atresia or stenosis, and an upper block gives polyhydramnios. Duplication of the intestine is a separate cyst or tube of bowel, not a stalk remnant. Malrotation and nonrotation change where the bowel lies, not its shape. Megacolon (Hirschsprung disease) gives constipation and a swollen belly, and colon atresia blocks stool completely. In anal agenesis stool may leave through a fistula to the perineum or urinary tract, not the umbilicus. A perforated descending colon spills stool into the belly, not out of the umbilicus.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Meckel = persistent gut end of vitelline duct (yolk stalk, omphaloenteric duct)</li>\n<li>Distal ileum, antimesenteric side</li>\n<li>~2 inches long, ~2 feet from ileocecal junction, 2–3% of people</li>\n<li>Free pouch: gastric/pancreatic tissue → ulcer → painless rectal bleeding in a child</li>\n<li>Technetium-99m pertechnetate scan shows the ectopic gastric mucosa</li>\n<li>Fibrous cord to umbilicus: bowel twists around it → obstruction</li>\n<li>Vitelline fistula: whole duct open → stool or secretions from unhealed umbilicus</li>\n<li>Rathke pouch → anterior pituitary; ligamentum teres = closed umbilical vein</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Umbilical defects and Meckel diverticulum","sn":5,"pn":"5.3"},{"l":"ANAT-G1","h":"Dividing the cloaca, opening the anus","p":"<h4>The cloaca</h4>\n<p>In week 4, tail (caudal) folding bends the end of the hindgut on itself. The widened end is the <b>cloaca</b>, a chamber lined by endoderm and shared by the gut and the future urinary tract. It is closed below by the <b>cloacal membrane</b>, where ectoderm lies on endoderm with no mesoderm between. The <b>allantois</b> runs from the top of the cloaca, the future bladder, toward the umbilical cord. It later becomes the urachus, which closes into the median umbilical ligament.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G001-30.jpg\" alt=\"Urorectal septum divides the cloaca; anal membrane closes the anal canal\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G001-30c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Urorectal septum divides the cloaca; anal membrane closes the anal canal\"></div></div><figcaption>Urorectal septum divides the cloaca; anal membrane closes the anal canal <small>· 2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 30</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Dividing it and opening the anus</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>From week 4 to week 7, the <b>urorectal septum</b>, a wedge of mesoderm between the allantois and the hindgut, grows down toward the cloacal membrane.</li>\n<li>It divides the cloaca into the <b>urogenital sinus</b> in front (the developing bladder and urethra) and the <b>anorectal canal</b> behind (the rectum and upper anal canal); until division is complete, a small channel, the cloacal duct, still joins them.</li>\n<li>In week 7 the septum fuses with the cloacal membrane and splits it into a front urogenital membrane and a back <b>anal membrane</b>; the fusion point becomes the perineal body.</li>\n<li>At the end of week 7 to the start of week 8, the anal membrane ruptures, and ectoderm of the anal pit (proctodeum) grows in to meet the endoderm of the rectum. The anus is now open (patent).</li>\n</ol>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G077-76.jpg\" alt=\"Anal membrane breaks down around week 8 to form the patent anus\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G077-76c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Anal membrane breaks down around week 8 to form the patent anus\"></div></div><figcaption>Anal membrane breaks down around week 8 to form the patent anus <small>· 2026 TA session: Embryology 7 GI, slide 76</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The part that ruptures is the anal membrane, the back part of the cloacal membrane, so ‘cloacal membrane rupture’ and ‘anal membrane rupture’ describe the same event. The urorectal septum divides the cloaca; it never ruptures. The proctodeum is the pit that meets the membrane; it does not rupture either. The anus opens by rupture of this membrane, not by recanalization. The mesonephric ridge makes the embryonic kidney and the male genital ducts and plays no part here.</p>\n<p>The outside of the region forms around the cloacal membrane at the same time. Three pairs of swellings build the <b>external genitalia</b>. The <b>genital tubercle</b>, in front, lengthens into the phallus; its tip becomes the <b>glans of the penis</b> (the clitoris in a female). The <b>urogenital (urethral) folds</b>, on each side of the urogenital membrane, close over the penile urethra in a male and stay apart as the <b>labia minora</b> in a female. The <b>labioscrotal swellings</b>, outside the folds, become the <b>scrotum</b> or the <b>labia majora</b>. The uterine (fallopian) tubes are internal; they come from the paramesonephric ducts, not from these swellings.</p>\n<h4>When it goes wrong</h4>\n<p>If the urorectal septum does not fully divide the cloaca, the rectum stays joined to the front, urogenital part. In boys this is a <b>urorectal (rectourethral) fistula</b>: meconium comes out through the urethra or shows up in the urine. In girls it is a <b>rectovaginal</b> or rectovestibular fistula: meconium comes out of the vagina or the vaginal vestibule. In both there is no normal anal opening at the perineum.</p>\n<p>If the anal membrane does not rupture, ectoderm and endoderm cannot meet, and the result is <b>imperforate anus</b>. The septum is normal, so there is no fistula. The newborn passes no meconium at all, feeds normally without vomiting at first, and slowly develops a swollen belly. A finger exam finds no anus, or only a membrane. In <b>anal stenosis</b> the opening is narrow, so some meconium passes. In anal agenesis the canal ends as a blind sac, and in anorectal agenesis the rectum ends high as well. In rectal atresia the anus is present, but a finger meets a blind end. Defects elsewhere look different: allantois defects give a urachal fistula or cyst, failed neural crest migration gives Hirschsprung disease with an open anus, and the hindgut does not rotate.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G077-66.jpg\" alt=\"Partitioning errors: urorectal and rectovaginal fistulas, imperforate anus, Hirschsprung contrast\"></div><figcaption>Partitioning errors: urorectal and rectovaginal fistulas, imperforate anus, Hirschsprung contrast <small>· 2026 TA session: Embryology 7 GI, slide 66</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Week 4 tail fold → cloaca (endoderm), closed by cloacal membrane</li>\n<li>Allantois → urachus → median umbilical ligament</li>\n<li>Urorectal septum (mesoderm), weeks 4–7: urogenital sinus in front, anorectal canal behind</li>\n<li>Week 7: septum meets membrane → urogenital + anal membranes; perineal body</li>\n<li>End week 7–start week 8: anal membrane ruptures → proctodeum meets rectum → patent anus</li>\n<li>‘Cloacal membrane’ rupture = rupture of its back part, the anal membrane</li>\n<li>Septum defect → urorectal fistula (boys) or rectovaginal/rectovestibular fistula (girls)</li>\n<li>Membrane intact → imperforate anus: no meconium, normal feeding, distension, no fistula</li>\n<li>Anal stenosis: some meconium; rectal atresia: anus present, blind end above</li>\n<li>Genital tubercle → glans; urethral folds → labia minora; labioscrotal → scrotum/labia majora</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Hindgut and anal canal","sn":6,"pn":"6.2"},{"l":"ANAT-G1","h":"Anal canal: two origins","p":"<h4>Two pieces meet at the pectinate line</h4>\n<p>The anal canal is built from two pieces. The <b>superior two-thirds</b> comes from <b>endoderm</b> of the hindgut (the cloaca). The <b>inferior third</b> comes from <b>ectoderm</b> of the proctodeum (anal pit), which grows inward after the membrane ruptures at the end of week 7 to the start of week 8. The two meet at the <b>pectinate (dentate) line</b>, which marks where the anal membrane used to be. The muscle and connective tissue of the wall are mesoderm on both sides; only the lining differs. Because the upper lining is gut endoderm, it is columnar like the rest of the gut: simple columnar, becoming stratified columnar near the line. The lower lining is stratified squamous, like skin.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G076-36.jpg\" alt=\"Anal canal: from hindgut above the pectinate line, from proctodeum below\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G076-36c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Anal canal: from hindgut above the pectinate line, from proctodeum below\"></div></div><figcaption>Anal canal: from hindgut above the pectinate line, from proctodeum below <small>· 2026 GI supplemental session (Nov 6), slide 36</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Everything changes at the line</h4>\n<p>The origin decides the supply on each side. Above the line the sensory nerves are <b>visceral (autonomic)</b>, from the hypogastric plexus with sympathetic and parasympathetic fibers. They sense stretch and distension but not sharp pain. These visceral fibers reach the canal through the inferior hypogastric (pelvic) plexus and the superior rectal plexus: parasympathetic fibers from the pelvic splanchnic nerves and sympathetic fibers from the hypogastric nerves. The vagus nerve does not reach this far. Below the line the nerves are <b>somatic</b>: the inferior rectal nerve, a branch of the pudendal nerve. They sense sharp, well-localized pain, temperature, touch and pressure. Above the line the artery is the superior rectal artery, from the inferior mesenteric artery (IMA), and the superior rectal vein drains to the portal system. Below the line the artery is the inferior rectal artery, from the internal pudendal artery, and the inferior rectal vein drains to the caval (systemic) system. Lymph from above the line goes to the internal iliac nodes; lymph from below goes to the superficial inguinal nodes.</p>\n<h4>Clinical links</h4>\n<p><b>Internal hemorrhoids</b> are swollen venous plexuses above the pectinate line. They sit in endoderm, are covered by mucosa, and have only visceral nerves. They cause <b>painless</b>, bright red bleeding after straining, such as blood on the toilet paper. They stay painless unless they prolapse and get trapped. <b>External hemorrhoids</b> lie below the line, in ectoderm, under skin, with somatic nerves. They are <b>painful</b>, worst when they clot. Skin nerves also carry itch, so a painful, itchy hemorrhoid is external; internal hemorrhoids, with only visceral nerves, neither hurt nor itch. Their blood supply differs too (superior versus inferior rectal vessels), but the nerve supply is what decides pain and itch. Straining, pregnancy and childbirth raise the pressure in these veins and can set off either type. An anal fissure below the line is sharply painful for the same reason. Cancer spread follows the lymph: above the line to the internal iliac nodes, below it to the inguinal nodes. The anal canal is also a portocaval anastomosis, where portal veins (superior rectal) meet caval veins (middle and inferior rectal).</p>\n<p>The anal canal is hindgut plus proctodeum; none of it is midgut. Neural crest makes the nerve cells of its wall, and splanchnic mesoderm makes its smooth muscle and connective tissue, but neither makes the lining.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Superior 2/3: hindgut endoderm; inferior 1/3: proctodeum ectoderm</li>\n<li>Pectinate line = old anal membrane site</li>\n<li>Lining: columnar above; stratified squamous below</li>\n<li>Above: visceral (pelvic splanchnic, hypogastric) fibers, stretch only; no vagus</li>\n<li>Below: inferior rectal branch of pudendal → sharp pain (fissure, external hemorrhoid)</li>\n<li>Arteries: superior rectal (IMA) above; inferior rectal (internal pudendal) below</li>\n<li>Veins: portal above, caval below → portocaval anastomosis</li>\n<li>Lymph: internal iliac above; superficial inguinal below</li>\n<li>Internal hemorrhoids: painless bright red bleeding; endoderm, mucosa, visceral nerves</li>\n<li>External hemorrhoids: painful + itchy; ectoderm, skin, somatic nerves</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Hindgut and anal canal","sn":6,"pn":"6.3"},{"l":"ANAT-G1","h":"Hirschsprung disease","p":"<h4>Neural crest builds the gut's own nervous system</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>Neural crest cells enter the gut wall and migrate down it from head to tail (craniocaudally).</li>\n<li>They settle in two networks, the <b>submucosal (Meissner) plexus</b> and the <b>myenteric (Auerbach) plexus</b>, which together form the enteric nervous system.</li>\n<li>They become the ganglion cells of these plexuses, which are the <b>postganglionic parasympathetic cell bodies</b>.</li>\n<li>Preganglionic parasympathetic fibers arrive from outside, in the vagus and pelvic splanchnic nerves, and synapse on them.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Sympathetic postganglionic cell bodies sit outside the gut wall, in the celiac and mesenteric ganglia, and the preganglionic sympathetic fibers come from the spinal cord. Endoderm makes only the lining and glands. Mesoderm makes the smooth muscle and connective tissue, including the internal anal sphincter. The dorsal mesentery only holds the gut and carries its vessels.</p>\n<h4>Hirschsprung disease (congenital aganglionic megacolon)</h4>\n<p>In Hirschsprung disease, neural crest cells migrate poorly and stop short of the end of the gut. The last segment, usually the <b>rectum and sigmoid colon</b>, has <b>no ganglion cells</b> in either plexus. What is missing is the <b>postganglionic parasympathetic</b> cell bodies; preganglionic parasympathetic fibers and all sympathetic neurons are still present. Without the ganglion cells the aganglionic segment cannot relax, and neither can the internal anal sphincter. The sphincter's smooth muscle is normal, but its plexus lacks neurons. The segment stays narrow and blocks the passage of stool, and the normal colon above it fills and <b>dilates</b>: megacolon.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G001-32.jpg\" alt=\"Clinical correlates; bottom photo: megacolon, dilated colon above a narrow segment\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G001-32c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Clinical correlates; bottom photo: megacolon, dilated colon above a narrow segment\"></div></div><figcaption>Clinical correlates; bottom photo: megacolon, dilated colon above a narrow segment <small>· 2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 32</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The newborn</h4>\n<p>The baby passes no meconium in the first days. Feeding is often normal at first, but the belly swells, and as the block persists the baby may vomit, even bile. A contrast enema shows a narrow distal segment with a dilated colon above it. In an older infant, constipation becomes chronic, with a large fecal mass in the colon. On rectal exam the anus, anal canal and rectum are normal in shape, but the canal feels tight. When the examining finger is withdrawn, gas and liquid stool gush out forcefully: the ‘squirt sign’. A biopsy of the narrow segment shows no ganglion cells, and surgery removes the segment.</p>\n<h4>Three causes of no meconium</h4>\n<p>The anus tells them apart. In Hirschsprung disease the anus is normal and open, because the defect lies in the nerve cells. In <b>imperforate anus</b> there is no opening, because the anal membrane failed to rupture. In <b>rectal atresia</b> an anus is present, but a finger meets a blind end above it. In anal agenesis there is no anal canal, and in anorectal agenesis the rectum also ends high with no anus. A cloaca that the urorectal septum fails to divide gives a rectourinary or rectovaginal fistula, not an aganglionic segment. The urachus (leftover allantois), the ventral body-wall folds and the linea alba (the midline tendon of the abdominal wall) play no part.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Neural crest migrates craniocaudally → submucosal (Meissner) + myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses</li>\n<li>Ganglion cells = postganglionic parasympathetic cell bodies</li>\n<li>Still present in Hirschsprung: preganglionic parasympathetic fibers, all sympathetic neurons</li>\n<li>Crest stops short → aganglionic rectum + sigmoid</li>\n<li>Segment + internal anal sphincter can't relax → colon above dilates (megacolon)</li>\n<li>No meconium, distension (later even bilious vomiting), tight canal, squirt sign</li>\n<li>Contrast enema: narrow distal segment, dilated colon above</li>\n<li>Biopsy: no ganglion cells; resect the segment</li>\n<li>Anus: normal (Hirschsprung), absent (imperforate), present + blind end (rectal atresia)</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Hindgut and anal canal","sn":6,"pn":"6.4"},{"l":"ANAT-G2","h":"Nine regions, key planes and referred pain","p":"<h4>The cavity and the nine regions</h4>\n<p>The abdominal cavity lies below the diaphragm and opens downward into the pelvic cavity at the pelvic inlet, so the two are often treated as one abdominopelvic cavity. The diaphragm is domed and rises as high as the 4th intercostal space. The lower ribs therefore wrap around and protect the upper organs: the liver, stomach, spleen and kidneys.</p>\n<p>To say where a finding lies, the front of the abdomen is divided into nine regions by two vertical lines and two horizontal planes, like a tic-tac-toe grid. The vertical lines are the right and left <b>midclavicular lines</b>. Each runs from the tip of the 9th costal cartilage down to the mid-inguinal point on the inguinal ligament. The upper horizontal plane is the <b>subcostal plane</b>, just below the 10th costal cartilage. The lower one is the <b>transtubercular plane</b>, which passes through both iliac tubercles and the body of the fifth lumbar vertebra (L5). Some physicians use the transpyloric plane in place of the subcostal plane, and the interspinous plane in place of the transtubercular plane.</p>\n<p>The top row, from the patient's right to left, is the right hypochondrium, the epigastric region and the left hypochondrium. The middle row is the right flank, the umbilical region and the left flank; flank, lateral and lumbar are three names for the same region. The bottom row is the right inguinal (groin) region, the pubic (hypogastric) region and the left inguinal region.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G003-7.jpg\" alt=\"Nine regions, with the areas where foregut, midgut and hindgut pain is felt\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G003-7c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Nine regions, with the areas where foregut, midgut and hindgut pain is felt\"></div></div><figcaption>Nine regions, with the areas where foregut, midgut and hindgut pain is felt <small>· 2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 7</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The transpyloric plane</h4>\n<p>The transpyloric plane lies halfway between the top of the manubrium (the jugular notch) and the pubic symphysis. It marks the level of the first lumbar vertebra (L1), and many structures sit on it: the pylorus of the stomach (when the patient lies down), the fundus of the gallbladder, the neck of the pancreas, the origin of the superior mesenteric artery (SMA), the origin of the hepatic portal vein, the root of the transverse mesocolon, the duodenojejunal junction, the hila of both kidneys, the hilum of the spleen and the 9th costal cartilages. The lower end of the spinal cord lies near this level too. The <b>celiac trunk</b> is not on the plane: it leaves the aorta one level higher, at the twelfth thoracic vertebra (T12), just above the SMA.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G003-9.jpg\" alt=\"Transpyloric plane at L1 and the organs it crosses\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G003-9c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Transpyloric plane at L1 and the organs it crosses\"></div></div><figcaption>Transpyloric plane at L1 and the organs it crosses <small>· 2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 9</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Where gut pain is felt</h4>\n<p>Pain from a gut organ is vague and is felt in the region that matches the organ's embryonic origin. Foregut organs (the stomach, first part of the duodenum, liver, gallbladder and pancreas) refer pain to the <b>epigastric</b> region. Midgut organs refer pain to the <b>umbilical</b> region, and hindgut organs to the <b>pubic</b> region.</p>\n<p>Three diseases are tied to the region where they are tender on palpation. A <b>peptic ulcer</b> is tender in the epigastrium, and stomach pain can also be felt behind, in the middle of the upper back between the shoulder blades. <b>Cholecystitis</b> (an inflamed gallbladder) and gallstones are tender in the right hypochondrium, just beneath the right costal margin, not in the right flank below it, and the pain often spreads to the right shoulder and shoulder blade. <b>Appendicitis</b> starts as vague pain around the umbilicus, because the appendix is a midgut organ. Hours later, once the inflammation reaches the parietal peritoneum, somatic nerves take over: the pain turns sharp and settles in the right inguinal (iliac) region, in the right lower quadrant (RLQ), where tenderness is greatest. The appendix itself does not move; only the kind of pain changes. An enlarged spleen gives pain in the left hypochondrium, and a kidney stone gives flank pain that spreads down to the groin.</p>\n<h4>The gallbladder fundus</h4>\n<p>The fundus of the gallbladder touches the front wall where the right <b>linea semilunaris</b> (the lateral edge of rectus abdominis) meets the right 9th costal cartilage. The examiner presses there and asks the patient to breathe in deeply. An inflamed gallbladder descends onto the fingers, and sharp pain stops the breath (Murphy sign).</p>\n<h4>Choosing an incision</h4>\n<p>A surgeon cuts over the region that gives the best access to the target organ, and along the muscle fibers where possible; this avoids irreversible necrosis of the muscle. The stomach lies mainly in the epigastric region and left hypochondrium, so even an ulcer on its posterior wall is reached through an epigastric incision. For an appendectomy the incision is centered on <b>McBurney's point</b>. Draw a line from the umbilicus to the anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS); the point lies two-thirds of the way along it, toward the ASIS.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>9 regions: midclavicular lines + subcostal (below 10th costal cartilage) + transtubercular (L5) planes</li>\n<li>Alternatives: transpyloric for subcostal, interspinous for transtubercular</li>\n<li>Transpyloric = L1, halfway jugular notch → pubic symphysis</li>\n<li>On it: pylorus, gallbladder fundus, pancreatic neck, SMA + portal vein origins, renal + splenic hila</li>\n<li>Also: transverse mesocolon root, duodenojejunal junction, 9th costal cartilages; celiac trunk NOT (T12)</li>\n<li>Referred pain: foregut → epigastric; midgut → umbilical; hindgut → pubic</li>\n<li>Peptic ulcer → epigastrium (+ mid back); cholecystitis → right hypochondrium, right shoulder</li>\n<li>Appendicitis: periumbilical (visceral, midgut) → RLQ/right inguinal (parietal); appendix doesn't move</li>\n<li>Gallbladder fundus: right linea semilunaris × right 9th costal cartilage; Murphy sign</li>\n<li>Incision over target organ, along fibers; McBurney = ⅔ of way umbilicus → ASIS</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Mapping the abdomen","sn":1,"pn":"1.1"},{"l":"ANAT-G2","h":"Four quadrants and their organs","p":"<h4>Two planes make four quadrants</h4>\n<p>For quick clinical description, the abdomen is split into four quadrants by just two planes. The vertical <b>median (midsagittal) plane</b> runs down the midline and separates right from left. The horizontal <b>transumbilical plane</b> runs through the umbilicus and separates upper from lower. The right lower quadrant (RLQ), for example, is the area to the right of the median plane and below the transumbilical plane, and every other quadrant is defined by the same two planes. The midclavicular lines and the subcostal, transpyloric, transtubercular and interspinous planes all belong to the nine-region system, not to the quadrants. The umbilicus sits a little higher or lower with body shape, size and weight, so the transumbilical plane varies slightly between people.</p>\n<p>The exact line matters most for organs that lie close to it. If an examiner draws the horizontal line too high, the lower quadrants grow, and an organ that normally sits just above the true line can be wrongly called ‘lower’. The gallbladder fundus, which comes down to the right costal margin, is the upper-quadrant organ most at risk. The spleen stays hidden behind the left ribs and the bladder stays deep in the pelvis, so their labels do not change.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G003-11.jpg\" alt=\"Organs in each quadrant: table and figure\"></div><figcaption>Organs in each quadrant: table and figure <small>· 2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 11</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>What lies in each quadrant</h4>\n<p>Learn where each organ mostly lies, since large organs cross the lines. The <b>right upper quadrant (RUQ)</b> holds the right lobe of the liver, the gallbladder, the pylorus, the first three parts of the duodenum, the head of the pancreas, the right kidney and suprarenal gland, the right colic (hepatic) flexure, the upper ascending colon and the right half of the transverse colon.</p>\n<p>The <b>left upper quadrant (LUQ)</b> holds the left lobe of the liver, the <b>spleen</b>, the <b>stomach</b>, the jejunum and proximal ileum, the body and tail of the pancreas, the left kidney and suprarenal gland, the left colic (splenic) flexure, the left half of the transverse colon and the upper descending colon. A lesion in the tail of the pancreas therefore shares its quadrant with the spleen.</p>\n<p>The <b>RLQ</b> holds the cecum, the appendix, most of the ileum, the lower ascending colon, the right ovary and uterine tube, and the abdominal parts of the right ureter and right spermatic cord. The <b>left lower quadrant (LLQ)</b> holds the sigmoid colon, the lower descending colon, the left ovary and uterine tube, and the abdominal parts of the left ureter and left spermatic cord, which runs there toward the deep inguinal ring. The urinary bladder sits low in the midline and reaches the lower quadrants only when it is very full; an enlarged uterus does the same.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G078-3.jpg\" alt=\"The main organs in each quadrant\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G078-3c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: The main organs in each quadrant\"></div></div><figcaption>The main organs in each quadrant <small>· 2026 TA session: GI week 1 part 2, slide 3</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Clinical clues</h4>\n<p>Pain from an organ under the diaphragm is often felt at the tip of the shoulder. An inflamed gallbladder irritates the diaphragm, which is supplied by the phrenic nerve (third to fifth cervical spinal nerves, C3–C5). The skin of the shoulder is supplied by the same spinal levels, so the brain reads the pain as shoulder pain. RUQ pain that spreads to the right shoulder therefore points to the gallbladder.</p>\n<p>The <b>spleen</b> lies in the LUQ, deep to left ribs 9–11. It is the abdominal organ most often torn in blunt trauma, such as a car crash, and it bleeds heavily, so the patient becomes pale and hypotensive. Stomach pain is epigastric and often worse after meals, and pancreatic pain goes through to the back; both organs lie mostly in the LUQ. In the LLQ, pain that spreads into the pelvis with bloating in an older adult points to the sigmoid colon (for example diverticulitis), and cyclic pain with an enlarged structure in a young woman points to the left ovary.</p>\n<h4>How high the organs sit</h4>\n<p>The spleen and most of the liver hide under the ribs, well above the umbilicus. The gallbladder fundus lies lower, at the right costal margin. The appendix, sigmoid colon and bladder lie below the umbilicus.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Quadrants = median (midsagittal) plane + transumbilical plane</li>\n<li>Midclavicular, subcostal, transpyloric, transtubercular, interspinous → nine regions only</li>\n<li>RUQ: right liver lobe, gallbladder, pylorus, duodenum 1–3, pancreatic head, right kidney, hepatic flexure</li>\n<li>LUQ: spleen, stomach, left liver lobe, jejunum, pancreatic body + tail, left kidney, splenic flexure</li>\n<li>RLQ: cecum, appendix, most of ileum, right ovary/tube, right ureter + spermatic cord</li>\n<li>LLQ: sigmoid + lower descending colon, left ovary/tube, left ureter + spermatic cord</li>\n<li>Bladder, uterus: midline; reach lower quadrants only when enlarged</li>\n<li>Horizontal line drawn too high → gallbladder fundus mislabeled ‘lower’</li>\n<li>Shoulder-tip pain: diaphragm irritation, phrenic C3–C5</li>\n<li>Spleen: ribs 9–11; most torn in blunt trauma → pale, hypotensive</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Mapping the abdomen","sn":1,"pn":"1.2"},{"l":"ANAT-G2","h":"Layers and the three flat muscles","p":"<h4>Layers from skin to peritoneum</h4>\n<p>The anterolateral abdominal wall is built in layers, and the order is the same wherever you cut. A knife entering the wall passes through them in this order:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The skin.</li>\n<li><b>Camper fascia</b>, the superficial fatty layer of the subcutaneous tissue.</li>\n<li><b>Scarpa fascia</b>, the deep membranous layer of the subcutaneous tissue.</li>\n<li>The three flat muscles, external oblique, internal oblique and transversus abdominis, each wrapped in its own thin investing fascia (superficial, intermediate and deep).</li>\n<li>The <b>transversalis fascia</b>, which lines the inner surface of the muscles.</li>\n<li>Extraperitoneal fat.</li>\n<li>The parietal peritoneum.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Camper and Scarpa fasciae together form the superficial fascia (subcutaneous tissue) between the skin and the muscles. Scarpa fascia is distinct mainly in the lower wall, below the umbilicus. A surgeon who cuts through the skin and the fatty layer and then meets a thin, membranous sheet has reached Scarpa fascia. Below, it continues into the perineum as Colles fascia and into the scrotum as the dartos layer. Neither fascia lies deep to the peritoneum, belongs to the posterior wall, or forms part of the rectus sheath.</p>\n<p>Next to the midline, the three flat muscles turn into flat tendons (aponeuroses) that wrap rectus abdominis as the rectus sheath. A stab wound just beside the linea alba therefore cuts aponeuroses instead of muscle bellies, but in the same order: external oblique, internal oblique, transversus abdominis, then transversalis fascia and peritoneum.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G003-13.jpg\" alt=\"Layers of the anterolateral abdominal wall, front view and longitudinal section\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G003-13c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Layers of the anterolateral abdominal wall, front view and longitudinal section\"></div></div><figcaption>Layers of the anterolateral abdominal wall, front view and longitudinal section <small>· 2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 13</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The three flat muscles</h4>\n<p>The three flat muscles continue the three intercostal muscle layers of the chest, so their fiber directions match. The <b>external oblique</b> is the outermost and largest, the continuation of the external intercostals. Its fibers run <b>inferomedially</b>, down and toward the midline, like hands slid into front pockets. It arises from the outer surfaces of ribs 5–12 and inserts into the linea alba, the pubic tubercle and the front of the iliac crest. Its aponeurosis joins the linea alba, and its free lower edge folds back on itself to form the <b>inguinal ligament</b>. Its nerves are the seventh to twelfth thoracic spinal nerves (T7–T12).</p>\n<p>The <b>internal oblique</b> lies in the middle and continues the internal intercostals. Its fibers run at right angles to those of the external oblique: they fan up and forward from the hip, so traced from their upper attachment they run inferolaterally. It arises from the thoracolumbar fascia and the front two-thirds of the iliac crest, and inserts into the lower borders of ribs 10–12, the linea alba and the pecten pubis. Its nerves are T6–T12 and the first lumbar nerve (L1). Its lowest fibers follow the spermatic cord to form the cremaster muscle.</p>\n<p>The <b>transversus abdominis</b> is the innermost, continuing the transversus thoracis, and its fibers run horizontally. It arises from the inner surfaces of costal cartilages 7–12, the thoracolumbar fascia and the iliac crest, and inserts into the linea alba, pubic crest and pecten pubis; its nerves are T6–T12. Low down, its aponeurosis fuses with the internal oblique aponeurosis to form the <b>conjoint tendon</b>, which inserts on the pubic crest. The external oblique takes no part in the conjoint tendon. The nerves and vessels of the wall run in the plane between the internal oblique and transversus abdominis, just as intercostal nerves run between the inner layers of chest muscle.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G073-6.jpg\" alt=\"External oblique, fibers running down and medially (full picture: all three muscles)\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G073-6c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: External oblique, fibers running down and medially (full picture: all three muscles)\"></div></div><figcaption>External oblique, fibers running down and medially (full picture: all three muscles) <small>· 2026 Harlem clickers: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 6</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>What the wall muscles do</h4>\n<p>The external and internal obliques <b>flex and rotate the trunk</b> and bend it to the side; they also raise intra-abdominal pressure and compress and support the viscera. The transversus abdominis mainly compresses the viscera. Rectus abdominis flexes the trunk, compresses the viscera and controls the tilt of the pelvis. When all four (rectus abdominis, external oblique, internal oblique and transversus abdominis) contract together they raise <b>intra-abdominal pressure</b>, which drives coughing, forced expiration, defecation, childbirth and the Valsalva maneuver. When a person braces before lifting, the pressurized abdomen acts as a stiff cylinder that steadies the spine and supports the trunk against the load. Other jobs belong to other muscles: iliopsoas flexes the hip, and limb and chest muscles move the arms and legs. The back muscles (erector spinae, multifidus and quadratus lumborum) extend and steady the spine from behind, but they do not compress the abdomen.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Skin → Camper (fatty) → Scarpa (membranous) → ext. oblique → int. oblique → transversus → transversalis fascia → fat → peritoneum</li>\n<li>Scarpa: distinct below umbilicus; → Colles fascia (perineum), dartos (scrotum)</li>\n<li>Beside the midline: same order, as aponeuroses (rectus sheath)</li>\n<li>Flat muscles = chest layers: external intercostal, internal intercostal, transversus thoracis</li>\n<li>Ext. oblique: largest, outermost; fibers inferomedial (hands in pockets); T7–T12</li>\n<li>Ext. oblique free lower edge → inguinal ligament</li>\n<li>Int. oblique: fibers at right angles; T6–L1; lowest fibers → cremaster</li>\n<li>Transversus: horizontal fibers; mainly compresses viscera; T6–T12</li>\n<li>Conjoint tendon = int. oblique + transversus aponeuroses → pubic crest (NOT ext. oblique)</li>\n<li>Obliques flex + rotate trunk; rectus + obliques + transversus raise intra-abdominal pressure (cough, Valsalva, bracing)</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Building the abdominal wall","sn":2,"pn":"2.1"},{"l":"ANAT-G2","h":"Rectus abdominis and the rectus sheath","p":"<h4>Rectus abdominis and its lines</h4>\n<p>Rectus abdominis is a pair of long, strap-like muscles on either side of the midline, with fibers running parallel to it. Each runs from the pubic symphysis and pubic crest up to the xiphoid process and costal cartilages 5–7, and it is about three times broader above than below. Several <b>tendinous intersections</b> cross each muscle and give the six-pack look. The muscle flexes the trunk, compresses the viscera and controls the tilt of the pelvis.</p>\n<p>Two vertical lines frame the muscles. The <b>linea alba</b> is the midline seam between the right and left recti, where the aponeuroses of both sides interweave. The <b>linea semilunaris</b> is the curved line along the lateral edge of each rectus, where the flat muscles of the side wall turn into aponeurosis and meet the sheath. So the linea alba separates the right rectus from the left, and the linea semilunaris separates each rectus from the lateral muscles. The small <b>pyramidalis</b> sits in front of the lower rectus; it is absent in about 20% of people and, when present, tenses the linea alba.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G078-8.jpg\" alt=\"Rectus abdominis, linea alba, linea semilunaris and pyramidalis\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G078-8c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Rectus abdominis, linea alba, linea semilunaris and pyramidalis\"></div></div><figcaption>Rectus abdominis, linea alba, linea semilunaris and pyramidalis <small>· 2026 TA session: GI week 1 part 2, slide 8</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>How the sheath is built</h4>\n<p>The <b>rectus sheath</b> is the tough envelope around rectus abdominis, in front and behind. It is made of the aponeuroses of the three flat muscles, with the transversalis fascia lining it from behind. Camper and Scarpa fasciae, the thoracolumbar fascia and the muscle's own epimysium are not part of it. The key step is that the <b>internal oblique aponeurosis splits</b> at the lateral edge of the rectus into an anterior and a posterior leaflet, which pass in front of and behind the muscle.</p>\n<p><b>Above the arcuate line</b>, the external oblique aponeurosis joins the anterior leaflet of the internal oblique to form the anterior layer of the sheath. The posterior leaflet of the internal oblique joins the transversus abdominis aponeurosis to form the posterior layer. The muscle itself touches only the two internal oblique leaflets, because the external oblique aponeurosis lies outside the front leaflet and the transversus aponeurosis lies outside the back leaflet. A stab wound from the front that stops at the muscle has cut, as its deepest layer, the anterior leaflet of the internal oblique.</p>\n<p>A little below the umbilicus, about one-third of the way from the umbilicus down to the pubic symphysis, the posterior layer ends in a curved lower border, the <b>arcuate line</b>. <b>Below the arcuate line</b>, all three aponeuroses pass in front of the muscle, with the transversus aponeurosis deepest. There is <b>no posterior sheath</b>, so the back of rectus abdominis lies directly on the <b>transversalis fascia</b>, with the inferior epigastric vessels between them. The arcuate line is the landmark a surgeon uses to find the lower end of the posterior sheath, for example when raising a rectus flap.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G003-19.jpg\" alt=\"Rectus sheath in cross-section above and below the arcuate line\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G003-19c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Rectus sheath in cross-section above and below the arcuate line\"></div></div><figcaption>Rectus sheath in cross-section above and below the arcuate line <small>· 2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 19</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Cutting through the wall beside the linea alba</h4>\n<p>Above the arcuate line, a cut from the front passes through the skin, Camper and Scarpa fasciae, the external oblique aponeurosis, the anterior leaflet of the internal oblique, the rectus (with the superior epigastric vessels behind it), the posterior leaflet of the internal oblique, the transversus aponeurosis, the transversalis fascia, extraperitoneal fat and peritoneum.</p>\n<p>Below the arcuate line, including a transverse suprapubic incision for a Caesarean section, the cut passes through the skin and subcutaneous tissue, the anterior sheath made of all three aponeuroses, the rectus, the inferior epigastric vessels, the transversalis fascia, extraperitoneal fat and peritoneum. No posterior rectus sheath is met there. A wound below the line that stops just short of the inferior epigastric artery has cut, as its deepest sheath layer, the transversus aponeurosis.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G073-12.jpg\" alt=\"Front view, left rectus removed: posterior sheath ends at the arcuate line\"></div><figcaption>Front view, left rectus removed: posterior sheath ends at the arcuate line <small>· 2026 Harlem clickers: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 12</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Other lines nearby are easy to confuse. The intercristal line joins the tops of the iliac crests at the fourth lumbar vertebra (L4). The pectineal line is a ridge on the pubic bone. The iliopubic tract is a thickening of transversalis fascia along the inguinal ligament, and the inguinal ligament itself lies in the groin, not behind the rectus.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Rectus: pubis → xiphoid + costal cartilages 5–7; tendinous intersections = six-pack</li>\n<li>Linea alba: midline seam; linea semilunaris: lateral rectus edge, separates lateral muscles</li>\n<li>Pyramidalis: absent ~20%; tenses linea alba</li>\n<li>Sheath = 3 flat-muscle aponeuroses (+ transversalis fascia behind)</li>\n<li>Int. oblique aponeurosis splits around rectus</li>\n<li>Above arcuate, anterior layer: ext. oblique + int. oblique front leaflet</li>\n<li>Above arcuate, posterior layer: int. oblique back leaflet + transversus aponeurosis</li>\n<li>Above arcuate: rectus touches only int. oblique leaflets</li>\n<li>Arcuate line: ~⅓ umbilicus → pubis; lower end of posterior sheath</li>\n<li>Below arcuate: all 3 aponeuroses in front (transversus deepest); rectus on transversalis fascia</li>\n<li>Suprapubic (Caesarean) cut: no posterior sheath</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Building the abdominal wall","sn":2,"pn":"2.2"},{"l":"ANAT-G2","h":"The two epigastric arteries meet","p":"<h4>The inferior epigastric artery climbs up</h4>\n<p>The inferior epigastric artery runs a fixed course from the groin up into the rectus sheath:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>It arises from the external iliac artery just above the inguinal ligament.</li>\n<li>It climbs up and medially on the transversalis fascia, just <b>medial to the deep inguinal ring</b>, under a ridge of peritoneum called the lateral umbilical fold. Here it gives off the cremasteric artery to the spermatic cord.</li>\n<li>Below the arcuate line it runs vertically right behind rectus abdominis. It lies <b>in front of the transversalis fascia</b> and behind the muscle and the transversus abdominis aponeurosis, which at this level passes in front of the muscle.</li>\n<li>It enters the rectus sheath <b>at the arcuate line</b>, passing between the muscle and the posterior layer of the sheath.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The key relation below the arcuate line is that the artery lies anterior to the transversalis fascia. It also lies posterior to the transversus abdominis aponeurosis there, because that aponeurosis has moved to the front of the muscle.</p>\n<p>Because the artery runs vertically behind the lower rectus, any cut across the lower rectus meets it. A transverse incision through the rectus and both sheath layers at the arcuate line cuts it, and so does harvesting a suprapubic piece of rectus as a graft, so the surgeon must find and tie it first. Nearby structures lie off this line: the thoracoepigastric vein runs in the fat of the lateral wall, the first lumbar artery on the posterior wall, the ilioinguinal nerve lower down in the inguinal canal, and the twelfth thoracic (T12, subcostal) nerve runs roughly sideways, parallel to a transverse cut.</p>\n<h4>The superior epigastric artery comes down</h4>\n<p>The superior epigastric artery, the end branch of the internal thoracic artery, enters the sheath from above and runs down behind the upper rectus. It lies in front of the posterior layer of the sheath (the posterior internal oblique leaflet and the transversus aponeurosis) and the transversalis fascia.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G003-20.jpg\" alt=\"Superior and inferior epigastric arteries inside the rectus sheath\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G003-20c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Superior and inferior epigastric arteries inside the rectus sheath\"></div></div><figcaption>Superior and inferior epigastric arteries inside the rectus sheath <small>· 2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 20</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The anastomosis</h4>\n<p>The change in the posterior sheath at the arcuate line is what lets the two arteries meet. The inferior epigastric artery slips into the sheath there and joins the superior epigastric artery behind and within rectus abdominis. This link connects the subclavian artery (through the internal thoracic) with the external iliac artery, so blood can flow either way around a blockage. If the external iliac artery is blocked, blood from above can run down this channel to reach the leg.</p>\n<p>Only the two epigastric arteries meet inside the sheath. The superficial epigastric artery stays in the subcutaneous fat. The superficial and deep circumflex iliac arteries head for the iliac crest outside the rectus, and the musculophrenic artery meets the deep circumflex iliac only through small branches in the lateral wall; the main bypass is the epigastric link. The superior and inferior mesenteric, sigmoid, superior rectal, left gastric and common hepatic arteries are gut arteries inside the abdomen, not wall arteries.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G078-16.jpg\" alt=\"Internal thoracic and external iliac branches meeting behind rectus abdominis\"></div><figcaption>Internal thoracic and external iliac branches meeting behind rectus abdominis <small>· 2026 TA session: GI week 1 part 2, slide 16</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>If one source is lost</h4>\n<p>The internal thoracic artery is often cut near the lower end of the sternum and used as a coronary bypass graft. The superior epigastric artery then loses its source. The rectus still receives blood from below: flow increases up the inferior epigastric artery and crosses the anastomosis into the superior epigastric territory. The umbilical artery cannot help, because its far end is obliterated.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Inferior epigastric: from external iliac, just above inguinal ligament</li>\n<li>Climbs medial to deep ring in lateral umbilical fold; gives cremasteric artery</li>\n<li>Below arcuate: behind rectus, in front of transversalis fascia (behind transversus aponeurosis)</li>\n<li>Enters rectus sheath at arcuate line</li>\n<li>Transverse cut through rectus at arcuate line or suprapubic graft → cuts inferior epigastric</li>\n<li>Superior epigastric (internal thoracic) + inferior epigastric meet within rectus sheath</li>\n<li>= subclavian ↔ external iliac bypass (e.g. blocked external iliac)</li>\n<li>Internal thoracic used for coronary bypass → rectus fed via inferior epigastric</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Vessels, nerves and lymph of the wall","sn":3,"pn":"3.2"},{"l":"ANAT-G2","h":"Veins and lymph of the wall","p":"<h4>Superficial veins split at the umbilicus</h4>\n<p>The superficial veins form a network in the subcutaneous tissue, and the umbilicus is its watershed. Above the umbilicus, blood runs up through the lateral thoracic vein to the <b>axillary vein</b>. Below the umbilicus, blood runs down through the superficial epigastric and superficial circumflex iliac veins into the great saphenous or <b>femoral vein</b>, and on to the external iliac vein.</p>\n<p>The <b>thoracoepigastric vein</b> runs up the side of the trunk and joins the superficial epigastric vein to the lateral thoracic vein. In this way it links the femoral vein below with the axillary vein above, connecting the territories of the two venae cavae. When the inferior vena cava (IVC) is blocked, blood from the lower body detours up this vein, and it swells into a visible cord along the lateral chest and abdominal wall.</p>\n<h4>Paraumbilical veins and caput medusae</h4>\n<p>The skin around the umbilicus is also drained by small <b>paraumbilical veins</b>, which run along the round ligament of the liver into the hepatic portal vein. They join the portal system to the systemic veins of the wall. In portal hypertension, for example from cirrhosis, blood cannot pass easily through the liver. It backs up through the paraumbilical veins into the superficial wall veins, which swell and radiate from the umbilicus as <b>caput medusae</b>. A heavy drinker with a ring of swollen veins around the umbilicus has caput medusae, not an umbilical hernia, which is a single soft bulge through the navel.</p>\n<p>The prefix <b>para-</b> means next to. The paraumbilical veins run next to the umbilicus, and the para-aortic nodes lie beside the aorta. Do not confuse it with peri- (around), sub- or infra- (below), trans- (through or across) or supra- (above).</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G078-17.jpg\" alt=\"Caput medusae: dilated veins radiating from the umbilicus\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G078-17c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Caput medusae: dilated veins radiating from the umbilicus\"></div></div><figcaption>Caput medusae: dilated veins radiating from the umbilicus <small>· 2026 TA session: GI week 1 part 2, slide 17</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Deep veins</h4>\n<p>The deep veins follow the deep arteries. The superior epigastric veins drain into the internal thoracic veins. The inferior epigastric and deep circumflex iliac veins drain into the external iliac veins. The musculophrenic and intercostal veins also drain the wall. The superior and inferior epigastric veins connect inside the rectus sheath, but that deep link is not the visible superficial bypass of IVC obstruction.</p>\n<h4>Lymph follows the blood supply</h4>\n<p>Superficial lymph vessels follow the superficial vessels, so they divide at the same plane, the level of the umbilicus (the tenth thoracic dermatome, T10). Skin and superficial tissue <b>above the umbilicus</b> drain upward to the <b>axillary nodes</b>, following a blood supply that comes from above: the superior epigastric and musculophrenic arteries from the internal thoracic artery, and the lateral thoracic vessels. Skin <b>below the umbilicus</b> drains down to the <b>superficial inguinal nodes</b>, following the superficial epigastric and superficial circumflex iliac vessels from the femoral artery. A few upper-wall vessels also reach the parasternal nodes along the internal thoracic vessels, but none come from below the umbilicus. A skin cancer or infection a few centimeters above the umbilicus therefore spreads first to the axillary nodes.</p>\n<p>Skin lymph passes through these regional nodes first; it does not run straight into the thoracic duct or the cisterna chyli. Other node groups drain other territories. Internal iliac nodes take the pelvic organs, cervical nodes the head and neck, and popliteal nodes the leg and foot. Para-aortic (lumbar) nodes take the testis, kidneys and posterior wall, and deep inguinal nodes take the glans penis and deep lower limb.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G003-23.jpg\" alt=\"Wall lymph: above umbilicus to axillary, below to superficial inguinal nodes\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G003-23c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Wall lymph: above umbilicus to axillary, below to superficial inguinal nodes\"></div></div><figcaption>Wall lymph: above umbilicus to axillary, below to superficial inguinal nodes <small>· 2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 23</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Watershed = umbilicus (T10) for superficial veins and lymph</li>\n<li>Above: lateral thoracic vein → axillary vein; lymph → axillary nodes</li>\n<li>Below: superficial epigastric vein → great saphenous/femoral vein; lymph → superficial inguinal nodes</li>\n<li>Thoracoepigastric vein links femoral ↔ axillary; dilates when IVC blocked</li>\n<li>Paraumbilical veins → portal vein; portal hypertension → caput medusae</li>\n<li>Para- = next to (paraumbilical, para-aortic); peri- = around</li>\n<li>Deep: superior epigastric → internal thoracic; inferior epigastric + deep circumflex iliac → external iliac</li>\n<li>Skin lymph → regional nodes first, not thoracic duct or cisterna chyli</li>\n<li>Internal iliac = pelvis; para-aortic = testis, kidney; deep inguinal = glans</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Vessels, nerves and lymph of the wall","sn":3,"pn":"3.3"},{"l":"ANAT-G2","h":"Nerves and dermatomes of the wall","p":"<h4>Which nerves supply the wall</h4>\n<p>The wall is supplied by the anterior (ventral) rami of the lower thoracic spinal nerves and the first lumbar nerve (T = thoracic, L = lumbar). These are ordinary spinal nerves that carry both <b>motor and sensory</b> fibers. They are not branches of the sympathetic trunk, they do not come from sacral rami, and they do not supply the thigh muscles. Taken together, the nerves to the wall muscles span T5–L1, and the named nerves to the skin span T7–L1.</p>\n<p>The <b>thoracoabdominal nerves</b> are the 7th to 11th intercostal nerves (T7–T11). They leave their intercostal spaces at the costal margin and run on into the abdominal wall, so they serve both the chest and the abdomen, hence the name. They supply the upper wall muscles and the skin from the xiphoid down to just below the umbilicus. The anterior ramus of T12 runs below the 12th rib and is called the <b>subcostal nerve</b>; it supplies the band of the wall below the umbilicus and above the groin. The first lumbar nerve (L1) gives the <b>iliohypogastric nerve</b>, which supplies the suprapubic skin just above the pubis (and the internal oblique and transversus), and the <b>ilioinguinal nerve</b>, which supplies the groin, the upper medial thigh, the root of the penis and the front of the scrotum, or the mons pubis and labium majus.</p>\n<h4>Their course</h4>\n<p>The nerves run forward with the arteries in the <b>neurovascular plane between the internal oblique and transversus abdominis</b>. This copies the chest, where the intercostal nerves run between the internal and innermost intercostal layers. They supply the flat muscles and rectus abdominis, then the skin, and also the parietal peritoneum lining the wall. They stay outside the peritoneum; they never pierce it to reach the muscles. The two L1 nerves run forward between the flat muscles and then pierce the internal oblique to reach the groin, so irritation along that path, for example after straining the obliques, is felt in the inguinal region.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G073-9.jpg\" alt=\"Thoracoabdominal, subcostal and L1 nerves running between the muscle layers\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G073-9c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Thoracoabdominal, subcostal and L1 nerves running between the muscle layers\"></div></div><figcaption>Thoracoabdominal, subcostal and L1 nerves running between the muscle layers <small>· 2026 Harlem clickers: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 9</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Dermatome landmarks</h4>\n<p>A dermatome is the strip of skin supplied by one spinal nerve. A few landmarks anchor the trunk dermatomes. <b>T4 is the nipple</b>, on the chest, and <b>T7 is the xiphoid process</b>. <b>T10 is the umbilicus</b>, so numbness or local anesthesia around the umbilicus involves the tenth intercostal (thoracoabdominal) nerve. <b>L1 is the inguinal region and the suprapubic skin</b>, down to the pubic symphysis. T8 and T9 lie between the xiphoid and the umbilicus, and T11 and T12 just below the umbilicus. Memory hooks: ‘seven at the sternum's end’, ‘T-ten at the belly button’ and ‘L1 for the inguinal ligament’.</p>\n<p>On the trunk the dermatomes are fairly uniform horizontal bands, each wrapping from the back around to the front and stopping at the midline. In the upper limb they are irregular, because each band was stretched out along the arm and digits as the limb grew.</p>\n<p>Two clinical patterns follow. Midgut pain, including the early pain of appendicitis, is referred to the umbilicus because the midgut's visceral sensory fibers enter the spinal cord at T10. Shingles (herpes zoster) reactivates in one spinal nerve, so its rash runs as a one-sided band that stops at the midline; a band wrapping from the back around to the umbilicus is T10.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G003-22.jpg\" alt=\"Dermatomes and cutaneous nerves of the abdominal wall\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G003-22c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Dermatomes and cutaneous nerves of the abdominal wall\"></div></div><figcaption>Dermatomes and cutaneous nerves of the abdominal wall <small>· 2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 22</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Nerves outside the wall supply nearby skin and muscle. The lateral femoral cutaneous nerve supplies the lateral thigh. The femoral nerve (second to fourth lumbar, L2–L4) supplies quadriceps. Sacral nerves supply the perineum and the back of the leg and foot.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Anterior rami: skin T7–L1, muscles T5–L1; motor + sensory; not sympathetic</li>\n<li>Thoracoabdominal = intercostal nerves T7–T11</li>\n<li>Subcostal = anterior ramus of T12</li>\n<li>L1 → iliohypogastric (suprapubic) + ilioinguinal (groin, upper medial thigh, anterior scrotum/labium)</li>\n<li>Run between int. oblique and transversus; stay outside peritoneum</li>\n<li>T4 nipple; T7 xiphoid; T10 umbilicus; L1 groin + suprapubic</li>\n<li>Trunk dermatomes: even horizontal bands; upper limb: irregular (stretched along arm, digits)</li>\n<li>Midgut and early appendicitis pain → T10 umbilicus</li>\n<li>Shingles: one-sided band, stops at midline</li>\n<li>Femoral nerve L2–L4 → quadriceps; lateral femoral cutaneous → lateral thigh</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Vessels, nerves and lymph of the wall","sn":3,"pn":"3.4"},{"l":"ANAT-G2","h":"Inner surface and Hesselbach triangle","p":"<h4>Five folds on the inner wall</h4>\n<p>Seen from inside, for example at laparoscopy, the lower front wall is lined by parietal peritoneum. Five ridges, the umbilical folds, run up toward the umbilicus. Each fold is raised by a structure lying just outside the peritoneum.</p>\n<p>The single <b>median umbilical fold</b> runs up the midline. It covers the <b>urachus</b>, the closed-off remnant of the allantois, which ties the top of the bladder to the umbilicus; once fibrous it is called the median umbilical ligament. On each side of it is a <b>medial umbilical fold</b>, raised by an <b>obliterated umbilical artery</b>; these run up toward the umbilicus and the falciform ligament. Further out on each side is a <b>lateral umbilical fold</b>, raised by the <b>inferior epigastric vessels</b>. Unlike the other two, these vessels stay open for life. The lateral folds fade out at the arcuate line, where the vessels pass into the rectus sheath.</p>\n<p>The other fetal remnants lie on the liver, not on the anterior wall. The umbilical vein becomes the round ligament of the liver, in the free edge of the falciform ligament, and the ductus venosus becomes the ligamentum venosum.</p>\n<h4>Three fossae</h4>\n<p>The shallow pits between the folds are the fossae. The supravesical fossa lies between the median and medial folds, above the bladder. The <b>medial inguinal fossa</b> lies between the medial fold (obliterated umbilical artery) and the lateral fold (inferior epigastric artery); it overlies Hesselbach triangle. The <b>lateral inguinal fossa</b> lies lateral to the lateral fold, and the deep inguinal ring opens there.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G003-25.jpg\" alt=\"Inner surface of the lower anterior wall: folds, fossae, Hesselbach triangle and deep ring\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G003-25c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Inner surface of the lower anterior wall: folds, fossae, Hesselbach triangle and deep ring\"></div></div><figcaption>Inner surface of the lower anterior wall: folds, fossae, Hesselbach triangle and deep ring <small>· 2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 25</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Hesselbach (inguinal) triangle</h4>\n<p>Hesselbach triangle is a patch of the lower anterior wall with three borders. Its medial border is the lateral edge of <b>rectus abdominis</b>. Its lateral border is the <b>inferior epigastric vessels</b>. Its inferior border is the <b>inguinal ligament</b>. In this area the wall behind the inguinal canal is mostly transversalis fascia, so it is the weakest area of the anterior wall and the site of <b>direct</b> inguinal hernias. There is no artery called the inferior iliac artery, and the femoral vessels, lacunar ligament, pectineal line, superior epigastric vessels and the two inguinal rings do not form its borders.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G073-24.jpg\" alt=\"Hesselbach triangle between inferior epigastric vessels, rectus edge and inguinal ligament\"></div><figcaption>Hesselbach triangle between inferior epigastric vessels, rectus edge and inguinal ligament <small>· 2026 Harlem clickers: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 24</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The deep inguinal ring lies just lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels, and <b>indirect</b> hernias begin there. The inferior epigastric vessels, seen from inside as the lateral umbilical fold, are therefore the landmark that separates the two kinds of inguinal hernia. A hernia medial to them, through the triangle, is direct and comes from a weak posterior wall; one lateral to them, through the deep ring, is indirect. Thigh structures such as the femoral vessels, lacunar ligament and pectineal line lie below the inguinal ligament.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Median fold (1): urachus (allantois), bladder → umbilicus</li>\n<li>Medial folds (2): obliterated umbilical arteries</li>\n<li>Lateral folds (2): inferior epigastric vessels (patent); end at arcuate line</li>\n<li>Umbilical vein → round ligament of liver; ductus venosus → ligamentum venosum</li>\n<li>Supravesical fossa: median–medial folds; medial inguinal fossa: medial–lateral folds (over Hesselbach)</li>\n<li>Lateral inguinal fossa: lateral to lateral fold; deep ring</li>\n<li>Hesselbach: rectus edge (medial), inferior epigastric vessels (lateral), inguinal ligament (inferior)</li>\n<li>Lateral umbilical fold (inferior epigastric) divides: medial = direct (weakest spot), lateral = indirect</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The inguinal region","sn":4,"pn":"4.1"},{"l":"ANAT-G2","h":"The inguinal ligament","p":"<h4>What the ligament is</h4>\n<p>The <b>inguinal ligament</b> is the free lower edge of the external oblique aponeurosis, folded back on itself into a rolled band. It runs obliquely from the <b>anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS)</b> to the <b>pubic tubercle</b>. Both bony points can be felt through the skin, so they are used to find the ligament at the bedside, for example in a hernia exam. The anterior inferior iliac spine (AIIS) lies lower on the hip bone and gives origin to rectus femoris. The pubic symphysis is the midline joint, medial to the tubercle. The greater trochanter, ischial tuberosity, iliac crest, sacral promontory and coccyx are other landmarks, not ends of the ligament.</p>\n<p>At its medial end, some fibers fan backward to attach to the pectineal line of the pubis. This curved shelf is the <b>lacunar ligament</b>, which forms the medial edge of the femoral ring. The deep side of the ligament is reinforced by a thickening of transversalis fascia, the <b>iliopubic tract</b>. The ligament forms most of the floor of the inguinal canal, which runs just above its medial half.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G003-30.jpg\" alt=\"Inguinal ligament, lacunar ligament and the femoral vessels passing under it\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G003-30c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Inguinal ligament, lacunar ligament and the femoral vessels passing under it\"></div></div><figcaption>Inguinal ligament, lacunar ligament and the femoral vessels passing under it <small>· 2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 30</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>What passes under it</h4>\n<p>The gap between the ligament and the hip bone is the doorway from the abdomen into the thigh. The external iliac artery and vein pass under the ligament and become the <b>femoral artery and vein</b>. From lateral to medial, the structures under it are the femoral nerve (with iliopsoas), the femoral artery, the femoral vein and the femoral canal. The canal's opening, the femoral ring, lies just lateral to the lacunar ligament.</p>\n<p>Several nearby structures do not pass under it. The testicular artery travels through the inguinal canal, above the ligament. The obturator nerve leaves the pelvis through the obturator canal. The inferior epigastric vessels climb up the inside of the anterior wall.</p>\n<h4>Finding the groin openings on the surface</h4>\n<p>The <b>deep inguinal ring</b> lies about 1.25 cm (half an inch) above the middle of the inguinal ligament, where the midclavicular line crosses it. Pressing a thumb there blocks an indirect hernia, which starts at the deep ring, so when the patient coughs the bulge stays in. The <b>superficial inguinal ring</b> lies just above and lateral to the pubic tubercle. The <b>femoral ring</b> lies below the ligament, medial to the femoral vein and just lateral to the lacunar ligament.</p>\n<p>This crossing point is the mid-inguinal point of the bedside rule: the deep ring lies half an inch above it. The femoral pulse is felt just below the ligament close to the same spot.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Inguinal ligament = rolled lower edge of ext. oblique aponeurosis</li>\n<li>ASIS → pubic tubercle (not AIIS, not pubic symphysis)</li>\n<li>Lacunar ligament: medial fibers → pectineal line; medial edge of femoral ring</li>\n<li>Iliopubic tract: transversalis fascia thickening on its deep side</li>\n<li>Forms most of inguinal canal floor</li>\n<li>Under it, lateral → medial: femoral nerve, artery, vein, canal (+ iliopsoas)</li>\n<li>Not under it: testicular artery (canal), obturator nerve, inferior epigastric vessels</li>\n<li>Deep ring: ½ inch above mid-inguinal point; superficial ring: above-lateral to pubic tubercle</li>\n<li>Femoral ring: below ligament, medial to femoral vein</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The inguinal region","sn":4,"pn":"4.2"},{"l":"ANAT-G2","h":"How the canal forms, and its two rings","p":"<h4>How the canal forms</h4>\n<p>The inguinal canal is the track left by the descending gonad, so its shape follows from development:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>In the embryo, the gonads of both sexes develop high on the posterior abdominal wall.</li>\n<li>A cord, the <b>gubernaculum</b>, ties each gonad to the future scrotum or labium majus.</li>\n<li>A pouch of peritoneum, the <b>processus vaginalis</b>, pushes out through the wall ahead of the testis.</li>\n<li>In males the testis descends and pushes through every layer of the abdominal wall to reach the scrotum, taking a sleeve of each layer with it. The tunnel it leaves is the <b>inguinal canal</b>. The gubernaculum guides it down and remains in the adult as the small <b>scrotal ligament</b>, which anchors the lower pole of the testis to the scrotum.</li>\n<li>In females the ovary attaches to the uterus and stays inside. The gubernaculum remains as the ovarian ligament and the round ligament of the uterus, which runs through the canal.</li>\n</ol>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G003-33.jpg\" alt=\"The descending testis pushes through the wall layers, led by the processus vaginalis\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G003-33c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: The descending testis pushes through the wall layers, led by the processus vaginalis\"></div></div><figcaption>The descending testis pushes through the wall layers, led by the processus vaginalis <small>· 2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 33</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The canal is an oblique tunnel about 4 cm (1½ inches) long, just above the medial half of the inguinal ligament; the whole ligament is only about three times that length. It runs downward, forward and medially from the deep ring to the superficial ring. It is larger in males because it carries the spermatic cord.</p>\n<h4>The deep ring: the entrance</h4>\n<p>The <b>deep (internal) inguinal ring</b> is the entrance to the canal from the abdomen. It is a round or oval opening in the <b>transversalis fascia</b>, the deepest layer of the wall. It is not a hole in the peritoneum: the peritoneum covers it from inside, which is why a hernia that enters it carries a peritoneal sac. The ring lies above the middle of the inguinal ligament, <b>lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels</b>. These vessels curve up along its medial edge and stay outside it, so a surgeon controlling bleeding there clamps them just medial to the ring. Because the same vessels form the lateral border of Hesselbach triangle, the deep ring lies immediately lateral to the triangle. The ring transmits the spermatic cord in males and the round ligament of the uterus in females.</p>\n<h4>The superficial ring: the exit</h4>\n<p>The <b>superficial (external) inguinal ring</b> is the exit, toward the scrotum or labium majus. It is a triangular gap in the <b>external oblique aponeurosis</b>, the outermost layer, just above and lateral to the pubic tubercle. Its edges are a medial crus and a lateral crus of the aponeurosis, joined across the top by intercrural fibers. The deep ring has no crura.</p>\n<p>So the canal starts in the innermost layer and ends in the outermost one. The internal oblique and transversus abdominis arch over the canal as its roof; they form neither ring. The internal oblique gives the cremaster, and the ilioinguinal nerve enters the canal from the side by piercing it. The falx inguinalis (conjoint tendon) backs the medial part of the canal, and Scarpa fascia lies under the skin; neither forms a ring. A hernia that comes through the back wall of the canal without using the deep ring lies medial to the inferior epigastric vessels, which then form its lateral border, with rectus abdominis medial to it.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G073-16.jpg\" alt=\"Inguinal canal opened from the front: both rings, conjoint tendon, cremaster\"></div><figcaption>Inguinal canal opened from the front: both rings, conjoint tendon, cremaster <small>· 2026 Harlem clickers: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 16</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Gonads form on posterior wall; gubernaculum → scrotum/labium majus</li>\n<li>Processus vaginalis leads testis through all wall layers → inguinal canal</li>\n<li>Gubernaculum remnant: ♂ scrotal ligament; ♀ ovarian ligament + round ligament of uterus</li>\n<li>Canal ~4 cm (1½ in), above medial half of inguinal ligament; larger in ♂</li>\n<li>Deep ring: oval hole in transversalis fascia (NOT peritoneum); entrance</li>\n<li>Deep ring lateral to inferior epigastric vessels (they hug its medial edge) = immediately lateral to Hesselbach triangle</li>\n<li>Superficial ring: triangular gap in ext. oblique aponeurosis; medial + lateral crura, intercrural fibers</li>\n<li>Superficial ring: above and lateral to pubic tubercle; exit</li>\n<li>Int. oblique + transversus: roof arches; form neither ring</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The inguinal region","sn":4,"pn":"4.3"},{"l":"ANAT-G2","h":"Walls and contents of the canal","p":"<h4>Why the walls change along the canal</h4>\n<p>The canal runs obliquely through a layered wall. It starts at the deep ring in the transversalis fascia and runs medially and downward, above the inguinal ligament, to the superficial ring in the external oblique aponeurosis. On the way it passes under the arching lower fibers of the internal oblique and transversus abdominis. Different layers therefore form its walls in different stretches, so it is described in thirds: the lateral third at the deep ring, the middle third over the pubic ramus, and the medial third near the superficial ring.</p>\n<h4>The four boundaries</h4>\n<p>The mnemonic MALT gives the main layer of each boundary. The roof is <b>M</b>uscle, the anterior wall is <b>A</b>poneurosis, the floor is <b>L</b>igament, and the posterior wall is <b>T</b>ransversalis fascia with the conjoint <b>T</b>endon.</p>\n<p>The <b>anterior wall</b> is the external oblique aponeurosis along the whole canal, reinforced in the lateral third by the internal oblique, whose lowest fibers arise there. The <b>roof</b> is formed by transversalis fascia in the lateral third, by the musculoaponeurotic arches of the internal oblique and transversus abdominis in the middle third, and by the medial crus of the external oblique aponeurosis in the medial third. The <b>floor</b> is the iliopubic tract laterally, the <b>inguinal ligament</b> in the middle, and the lacunar ligament medially.</p>\n<p>The <b>posterior wall</b> is transversalis fascia along its whole length. In the medial third, behind the superficial ring, it is reinforced by the <b>conjoint tendon</b> (falx inguinalis) and the reflected inguinal ligament. The conjoint tendon is the fused aponeuroses of the <b>internal oblique and transversus abdominis</b>, inserting on the pubic crest; the external oblique does not contribute. In short, the posterior wall is transversalis fascia plus the transversus abdominis aponeurosis. Where this wall is weak, in Hesselbach triangle, a direct hernia pushes straight through, so a direct hernia comes from a defective transversalis fascia and transversus aponeurosis behind the canal. A defect of the fascia or peritoneum around the deep ring gives an indirect hernia instead. The pectineal (Cooper) ligament lies on the pecten pubis, below and behind the canal, and is not one of its walls.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G003-35.jpg\" alt=\"Contents of the inguinal canal in the male and the female\"></div><figcaption>Contents of the inguinal canal in the male and the female <small>· 2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 35</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>What runs through the canal</h4>\n<p>In males the main content is the <b>spermatic cord</b>. It enters at the deep ring, runs through the canal, leaves at the superficial ring and continues into the scrotum. The cord contains the ductus deferens, the testicular artery and the pampiniform plexus of veins, among other things, so any one of these is only part of the content. In females the main content is the <b>round ligament of the uterus</b>. It runs from the uterus through the deep ring and along the canal, and ends in the labium majus, the female counterpart of the scrotum (not the labium minus); in a woman with an inguinal hernia it is the structure found stretched over the sac.</p>\n<p>The <b>ilioinguinal nerve</b> runs through the canal in both sexes, but it enters from the side by piercing the internal oblique, not through the deep ring. It runs along the canal and leaves through the superficial ring. In males it lies beside the cord, outside its coverings, so it passes through neither the deep ring nor the cord. The genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve runs inside the cord in males and alongside the round ligament in females.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G078-33.jpg\" alt=\"Inguinal canal layers and contents in the female and the male\"></div><figcaption>Inguinal canal layers and contents in the female and the male <small>· 2026 TA session: GI week 1 part 2, slide 33</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Several structures stay out of the canal. The iliohypogastric nerve runs above it. The inferior epigastric vessels run in the wall, medial to the deep ring, and the superior epigastric vessels are far above. The urachus and the umbilical ligaments lie on the inner surface of the wall. The ovarian ligament, the suspensory ligament of the ovary, the broad ligament with its parts (mesovarium, mesosalpinx and mesometrium) and the ureter stay in the pelvis. The femoral nerve passes under the inguinal ligament, and the obturator nerve leaves through the obturator canal.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Oblique course → walls change by thirds; MALT</li>\n<li>Roof (lateral → medial): transversalis fascia → int. oblique + transversus arches → medial crus</li>\n<li>Anterior wall: ext. oblique aponeurosis (+ int. oblique laterally)</li>\n<li>Floor: iliopubic tract → inguinal ligament → lacunar ligament</li>\n<li>Posterior wall: transversalis fascia; + conjoint tendon medially</li>\n<li>Conjoint tendon = int. oblique + transversus aponeuroses → pubic crest</li>\n<li>Weak posterior wall (transversalis fascia, transversus aponeurosis) → direct hernia</li>\n<li>Canal's job: ♂ spermatic cord → scrotum; ♀ round ligament of uterus → labium majus</li>\n<li>Ilioinguinal: both sexes; enters from side, exits superficial ring; not deep ring, not inside cord</li>\n<li>Not in canal: iliohypogastric, epigastric vessels, ovarian and broad ligaments, ureter, obturator nerve</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The inguinal region","sn":4,"pn":"4.4"},{"l":"ANAT-G2","h":"Direct versus indirect, and the direct hernia","p":"<h4>One landmark sorts inguinal hernias</h4>\n<p>A hernia is a sac of peritoneum, often holding bowel, pushed out through a weak spot in the wall. Inguinal hernias appear above the inguinal ligament, and the <b>inferior epigastric vessels</b> decide which kind they are. A hernia that comes out <b>medial</b> to these vessels, through Hesselbach triangle, is <b>direct</b>. A hernia that comes out <b>lateral</b> to them, through the deep inguinal ring, is <b>indirect</b>. An analogy: the indirect hernia walks down an existing hallway (the canal), while the direct hernia busts straight through the wall.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G003-37.jpg\" alt=\"Direct hernia medial, indirect hernia lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G003-37c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Direct hernia medial, indirect hernia lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels\"></div></div><figcaption>Direct hernia medial, indirect hernia lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels <small>· 2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 37</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The direct hernia</h4>\n<p>A <b>direct</b> inguinal hernia pushes straight forward through the posterior wall of the canal in Hesselbach triangle (the medial inguinal fossa). It is <b>acquired</b>: the transversalis fascia and transversus abdominis aponeurosis behind the canal weaken with age, and raised pressure from heavy lifting, straining or a chronic cough pushes bowel through. Older age and lifting are risk factors; the anatomical cause is the weakened posterior wall itself. The typical patient is an older man, or any adult man who lifts heavily. The bulge grows slowly over months, sits medially close to the pubic tubercle just above the inguinal ligament, and swells on coughing, straining or standing. Direct hernias can occur on one side or both; they are not always bilateral.</p>\n<p>Because it does not use the deep ring, a direct sac does not enter the spermatic cord. It lies beside the cord, outside the internal spermatic fascia, and it <b>rarely descends into the scrotum</b>. It is covered by peritoneum, extraperitoneal tissue and the transversalis fascia that it pushes ahead of it (also called the weak fascia of transversus abdominis lateral to the falx, the conjoint tendon). If it bulges out through the superficial ring, it picks up external spermatic fascia there. It is never covered by the internal spermatic fascia, which wraps only the cord and an indirect sac.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G073-25.jpg\" alt=\"Normal wall, indirect hernia through the deep ring, direct hernia through the wall\"></div><figcaption>Normal wall, indirect hernia through the deep ring, direct hernia through the wall <small>· 2026 Harlem clickers: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 25</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The indirect hernia in brief</h4>\n<p>An <b>indirect</b> hernia enters the deep ring, runs inside the spermatic cord and so is wrapped in all three of the cord's coverings. It is usually <b>congenital</b>, from a processus vaginalis that never closed, and it is typical of infants, children and young men, though it occurs at any age. It can follow the cord all the way into the scrotum or labium majus. The next part covers it in detail. Appearing after birth does not settle the type, since hernias of both kinds can appear after birth.</p>\n<h4>At the bedside</h4>\n<p>The examiner invaginates the loose scrotal skin with a finger, pushes it up into the superficial ring, and asks the patient to cough. An impulse against the <b>fingertip</b> comes down the canal from the deep ring, so the hernia is indirect. An impulse against the <b>side (pulp) of the finger</b>, from medial and behind, pushes straight through the posterior wall in Hesselbach triangle, so the hernia is direct. With a large direct defect, the finger can seem to pass straight back into the abdomen through the weak area.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G073-26.jpg\" alt=\"Finger in the superficial ring: where direct and indirect hernias push\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G073-26c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Finger in the superficial ring: where direct and indirect hernias push\"></div></div><figcaption>Finger in the superficial ring: where direct and indirect hernias push <small>· 2026 Harlem clickers: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 26</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Groin lumps that are not inguinal hernias</h4>\n<p>A femoral hernia bulges below the inguinal ligament, not at the superficial ring, and is relatively more common in women. Enlarged lymph nodes are firm and tender and have no cough impulse. A hydrocele is fluid around the testis inside the scrotum, and the tunica vaginalis is never the site of a hernia sac. Testicular torsion causes sudden, severe testis pain, and epididymitis a tender swelling behind the testis. A testicular cancer is a hard scrotal mass without a cough impulse. A hernia through the linea alba is epigastric or umbilical, not inguinal.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Inferior epigastric vessels decide: medial = direct, lateral = indirect</li>\n<li>Direct: through Hesselbach triangle (medial inguinal fossa), posterior wall of canal</li>\n<li>Direct: acquired weak transversalis fascia/transversus aponeurosis; older ♂, lifting, cough</li>\n<li>Direct: slow, medial near pubic tubercle; rarely in scrotum; one or both sides</li>\n<li>Direct coverings: peritoneum + transversalis fascia (+ ext. spermatic at superficial ring); never internal spermatic</li>\n<li>Indirect: deep ring, inside cord, 3 coverings, congenital, young, can reach scrotum</li>\n<li>Finger in superficial ring + cough: tip = indirect; side/medial = direct</li>\n<li>Femoral: below inguinal ligament; nodes: no cough impulse; torsion: sudden pain</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Groin hernias","sn":5,"pn":"5.1"},{"l":"ANAT-G2","h":"The indirect hernia","p":"<h4>The path of an indirect hernia</h4>\n<p>An indirect hernia follows the route the testis took, from inside out:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The sac leaves the abdomen through the <b>deep inguinal ring</b>, in the lateral inguinal fossa. That is lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels and to the lateral umbilical fold that covers them. The deep ring is the neck (proximal end) of the sac.</li>\n<li>It runs down the inguinal canal <b>inside the spermatic cord</b>, beside the ductus deferens.</li>\n<li>It leaves the canal at the superficial ring, which is its exit, not its start.</li>\n<li>If large enough, it follows the cord into the scrotum, or the round ligament into the labium majus.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Any hernia that enters the deep ring is indirect, and any hernia found lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels, just above the inguinal ligament, is entering the deep ring. Both statements describe the same position, because the deep ring is the opening lateral to these vessels. A direct hernia is never lateral to them and never uses the deep ring.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G003-41.jpg\" alt=\"Descent of the testis, and the deep ring seen from inside\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G003-41c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Descent of the testis, and the deep ring seen from inside\"></div></div><figcaption>Descent of the testis, and the deep ring seen from inside <small>· 2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 41</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Why it is wrapped like the cord</h4>\n<p>The sac slips through the hole already present in the transversalis fascia; it does not push that fascia ahead of it. Pushing peritoneum and transversalis fascia out in front of the bowel is what a direct hernia does. Once inside the cord, the indirect sac lies within the cord's three coverings: the internal spermatic fascia, the cremaster muscle and fascia, and the external spermatic fascia.</p>\n<h4>Why it is usually congenital</h4>\n<p>The processus vaginalis normally closes once the testis reaches the scrotum. If it stays open, the deep ring leads into a ready-made peritoneal tube, and bowel can slide down it. A child's groin hernia that follows the cord is therefore indirect. Adults get indirect hernias too. A man lifting heavy furniture can feel sudden pain as a knuckle of bowel enters the deep ring, and an older man can have a slowly enlarging indirect sac that reaches the scrotum.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G078-41.jpg\" alt=\"Indirect versus direct hernias around Hesselbach triangle, with comparison table\"></div><figcaption>Indirect versus direct hernias around Hesselbach triangle, with comparison table <small>· 2026 TA session: GI week 1 part 2, slide 41</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Nerves near the sac</h4>\n<p>The <b>genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve</b> (first and second lumbar spinal nerves, L1–L2) runs inside the cord and supplies the cremaster, the muscle of the cremasteric reflex. Separating an indirect sac from the cord during repair puts this nerve at risk. The ilioinguinal nerve runs in the canal, outside the cord. Other nerves lie away from this field: the iliohypogastric nerve runs above the canal, the femoral and lateral femoral cutaneous nerves pass under the inguinal ligament, and the obturator nerve runs through the pelvis and the obturator canal.</p>\n<h4>Other hernias to rule out</h4>\n<p>A femoral hernia lies below the inguinal ligament, beside the femoral vein. An umbilical hernia comes through the navel. Ventral and incisional hernias come through the front wall away from the groin, often at the midline or a surgical scar. A lumbar hernia comes through the back of the abdominal wall.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Indirect: neck at deep ring, lateral to inferior epigastric vessels / lateral umbilical fold</li>\n<li>Lateral inguinal fossa → canal inside cord → superficial ring (exit) → scrotum/labium</li>\n<li>Slips through existing transversalis hole; pushing fascia ahead = direct</li>\n<li>Coverings = cord's 3: internal spermatic, cremaster, external spermatic</li>\n<li>Usually congenital: patent processus vaginalis; children, young ♂ (any age)</li>\n<li>Genital branch of genitofemoral (L1–L2) in cord → at risk in repair</li>\n<li>Ilioinguinal in canal outside cord; iliohypogastric above canal</li>\n<li>Femoral below ligament; umbilical at navel; incisional at scar; lumbar posterior</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Groin hernias","sn":5,"pn":"5.2"},{"l":"ANAT-G2","h":"Hernias in infants and women; femoral and obturator","p":"<h4>Infants and children</h4>\n<p>Groin hernias in infants and children are almost always <b>indirect and congenital</b>. The processus vaginalis has stayed open, so the deep ring opens into an intact peritoneal pouch, and bowel slides through it into the canal or scrotum. The swelling is soft, non-tender and reducible, and it becomes less prominent when the baby is calm. If the processus closes above but fluid stays trapped below, the result is a <b>hydrocele</b> instead: fluid around the testis or cord that lets light through and does not push back into the abdomen. A congenital hydrocele is a sister condition of the hernia, not its cause.</p>\n<p>Some conditions near the groin do not cause hernias. An ectopic testis, one that has left the normal path of descent, does not cause a hernia, and epispadias (a urethra opening on the top of the penis) is unrelated. A torn or weak transversalis fascia is the adult, direct mechanism, not the cause in a child. Testicular torsion gives a sudden, very painful, swollen testis.</p>\n<h4>Umbilical hernia</h4>\n<p>An umbilical hernia comes through the umbilicus. In infants it follows a defect of the anterior wall at the umbilicus. In adults it follows a weak wall plus raised intra-abdominal pressure, and it is seen most often in women and obese patients.</p>\n<h4>Femoral hernia</h4>\n<p>In a femoral hernia, bowel passes <b>below the inguinal ligament</b>, through the femoral ring into the femoral canal, medial to the femoral vein. The lump therefore lies <b>inferior and lateral to the pubic tubercle</b>, while inguinal hernias lie above the ligament, superior and medial to the tubercle. The femoral ring is narrow, and its medial edge is the sharp lacunar ligament. Bowel is easily trapped there and loses its blood supply (strangulation). The patient, often a woman after heavy straining, has a painful, globular lump in the groin with severe abdominal pain, nausea and vomiting. Femoral hernias are rare in infants.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G073-27.jpg\" alt=\"Indirect, direct and femoral hernias relative to epigastric and femoral vessels\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G073-27c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Indirect, direct and femoral hernias relative to epigastric and femoral vessels\"></div></div><figcaption>Indirect, direct and femoral hernias relative to epigastric and femoral vessels <small>· 2026 Harlem clickers: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 27</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Who gets which hernia</h4>\n<p>About 85% of inguinal hernias occur in men, because the spermatic cord passes through the canal. Women get femoral hernias more often than men do, helped by their wider pelvis. Even in women, though, femoral hernias are less common than inguinal hernias. The <b>indirect inguinal hernia is the most common hernia in both sexes</b>, women included. Direct hernias are mostly a problem of older men, and epigastric hernias are small and uncommon.</p>\n<h4>Obturator hernia</h4>\n<p>In an obturator hernia, bowel is trapped in the obturator canal, deep in the upper thigh, so there is <b>no visible or palpable groin lump</b>. The typical patient is a thin, elderly woman, often after major weight loss, who presents with small bowel obstruction: cramps, vomiting and a distended abdomen. The sac presses on the <b>obturator nerve</b>, giving sharp, cutting pain down the inner thigh that is worse when the hip is extended and rotated medially (the Howship–Romberg sign).</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Child's groin hernia: indirect, congenital; deep ring opens into patent processus vaginalis</li>\n<li>Processus closed above, fluid below → hydrocele (transilluminates, not reducible)</li>\n<li>Ectopic testis, epispadias don't cause hernia; weak transversalis = adult direct</li>\n<li>Umbilical: infant wall defect; adults: women, obese, raised pressure</li>\n<li>Femoral: below inguinal ligament, medial to femoral vein; inferolateral to pubic tubercle</li>\n<li>Femoral: narrow ring + sharp lacunar ligament → strangulation, vomiting</li>\n<li>85% of inguinal hernias in ♂; women &gt; men for femoral</li>\n<li>Most common hernia in both sexes = indirect inguinal</li>\n<li>Obturator: thin elderly woman, no lump, obstruction; inner-thigh pain on extension + medial rotation</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Groin hernias","sn":5,"pn":"5.3"},{"l":"ANAT-G2","h":"The cord: contents and three coats","p":"<h4>How the cord is assembled</h4>\n<p>The testis develops on the posterior abdominal wall near the kidneys. When it descends, it drags its artery, veins, lymph vessels and nerves down with it through the anterior wall. At the deep ring the ductus deferens joins these vessels, and from there on the bundle is called the <b>spermatic cord</b>. It runs through the inguinal canal and down to the testis in the scrotum.</p>\n<h4>What is inside</h4>\n<p>The <b>ductus (vas) deferens</b> carries sperm. Three arteries travel with it: the <b>testicular artery</b> from the aorta, the <b>cremasteric artery</b> from the inferior epigastric artery, and the artery of the ductus deferens, usually from the inferior vesical artery (a branch of the internal iliac). The <b>pampiniform plexus</b> of veins wraps the testicular artery. Lymph vessels, the <b>genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve</b>, a plexus of sympathetic nerve fibers and the remains of the processus vaginalis complete the list. A rule of threes helps: three arteries, three fascial coats, and three nerve-and-vein items (the pampiniform plexus, the genital branch and the sympathetic fibers).</p>\n<p>Several structures nearby are not in the cord. The <b>femoral branch</b> of the genitofemoral nerve passes under the inguinal ligament to the skin of the upper front thigh; only the genital branch enters the cord. The ilioinguinal nerve runs in the canal but outside the cord. The epididymis sits on the testis, and the tunica albuginea is the testis's own capsule. The dartos and tunica vaginalis are layers of the scrotum, not coats of the cord.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G078-45.jpg\" alt=\"Cross-section of the spermatic cord: its three coats and contents\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G078-45c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Cross-section of the spermatic cord: its three coats and contents\"></div></div><figcaption>Cross-section of the spermatic cord: its three coats and contents <small>· 2026 TA session: GI week 1 part 2, slide 45</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Three coats from three wall layers</h4>\n<p>As the testis crossed the wall, it took a sleeve from each layer it met, so each coat matches the wall layer at the same depth. The <b>external spermatic fascia</b>, the outer coat, continues the <b>external oblique aponeurosis</b> and is picked up at the superficial ring. The <b>cremaster muscle and cremasteric fascia</b>, the middle coat, come from the lowest muscle fibers of the <b>internal oblique</b>, carried down around the cord in the canal. The <b>internal spermatic fascia</b>, the inner coat, continues the <b>transversalis fascia</b> and is picked up at the deep ring, which is a hole in that fascia.</p>\n<p>The transversus abdominis gives no coat, because its lower fibers arch above the deep ring and the cord passes beneath them. Extraperitoneal fat becomes loose tissue inside the cord. The peritoneum gives the processus vaginalis, whose lower end becomes the tunica vaginalis of the testis. Camper and Scarpa fasciae give the cord no coat; Scarpa fascia continues into the scrotum as the dartos layer (next part). Rectus abdominis lies medial to the canal and takes no part.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G003-44.jpg\" alt=\"Abdominal wall layers continuing as the coverings of the cord and testis\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G003-44c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Abdominal wall layers continuing as the coverings of the cord and testis\"></div></div><figcaption>Abdominal wall layers continuing as the coverings of the cord and testis <small>· 2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 44</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>From outside in, the cord therefore has the external spermatic fascia, then the cremaster muscle and fascia with the genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve, then the internal spermatic fascia, and finally the vessels, duct and nerves. An indirect hernia in a newborn, with bowel down in the scrotum, runs inside the internal spermatic fascia. The deepest wall layer it has passed through is the transversalis fascia, with only peritoneum deeper.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Cord forms at deep ring when ductus deferens joins testicular vessels</li>\n<li>Contents: ductus deferens, testicular + cremasteric + ductus arteries, pampiniform plexus</li>\n<li>Also: lymphatics, genital branch of genitofemoral, sympathetic plexus, processus vaginalis remnant</li>\n<li>Not in cord: femoral branch of genitofemoral, ilioinguinal nerve, epididymis</li>\n<li>Ext. oblique aponeurosis → external spermatic fascia (superficial ring)</li>\n<li>Int. oblique → cremaster muscle + fascia</li>\n<li>Transversalis fascia → internal spermatic fascia (deep ring)</li>\n<li>Transversus abdominis: no coat; peritoneum → processus vaginalis → tunica vaginalis</li>\n<li>Outside → in: external spermatic → cremaster → internal spermatic → contents</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The spermatic cord and scrotum","sn":6,"pn":"6.1"},{"l":"ANAT-G2","h":"The cremaster and the cremasteric reflex","p":"<h4>The cremaster muscle</h4>\n<p>The cremaster is made of loops of <b>skeletal</b> muscle derived from the lowest fibers of the <b>internal oblique</b>. It wraps the spermatic cord and testis, lying over the internal spermatic fascia. Its motor nerve is the <b>genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve</b>, from the first and second lumbar spinal nerves (L1–L2), which runs inside the cord. When it contracts it <b>raises the testis</b> toward the inguinal canal, in the cold or for protection. In the cold the testes rise and the scrotal skin wrinkles to save heat; in the warmth the testes descend and the skin relaxes to lose heat. With the dartos it helps control testicular temperature.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G003-45.jpg\" alt=\"Cremaster muscle around the spermatic cord, with the genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G003-45c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Cremaster muscle around the spermatic cord, with the genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve\"></div></div><figcaption>Cremaster muscle around the spermatic cord, with the genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve <small>· 2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 45</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The cremasteric reflex</h4>\n<p>The cremasteric reflex shows whether the cremaster and its nerves work. It is a standard test in newborns and in the neurologic exam after spinal injury.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The examiner lightly strokes the skin of the upper inner (superomedial) thigh.</li>\n<li>The sensory (afferent) limb carries the stroke to the spinal cord through the <b>ilioinguinal nerve</b> (L1). The femoral branch of the genitofemoral nerve adds a second sensory path.</li>\n<li>The motor (efferent) limb is the <b>genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve</b> (L1–L2), which contracts the cremaster.</li>\n<li>The testis on the same side rises (testicular retraction).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The reflex therefore tests spinal cord segments <b>L1–L2</b>. Other reflexes test other levels: the patellar reflex (knee jerk) tests the second to fourth lumbar segments (L2–L4), the plantar reflex the fifth lumbar and first sacral segments (L5–S1), the calcaneal reflex (ankle jerk) the first and second sacral segments (S1–S2), and the anocutaneous reflex (anal wink) the second to fourth sacral segments (S2–S4).</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G073-36.jpg\" alt=\"Cremasteric reflex: ilioinguinal sensory limb, genital branch motor limb\"></div><figcaption>Cremasteric reflex: ilioinguinal sensory limb, genital branch motor limb <small>· 2026 Harlem clickers: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 36</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Several other responses are easy to confuse with it. Stroking the abdominal skin twitches the abdominal wall (the abdominal reflex). The dartos wrinkles the scrotal skin slowly under sympathetic control. Erection is a parasympathetic response. The bulbospongiosus, a perineal muscle supplied by the pudendal nerve, helps ejaculation. None of these lifts the testis, and the iliohypogastric, femoral and obturator nerves play no part in the reflex.</p>\n<h4>Nerves of the scrotal skin</h4>\n<p>The front of the scrotum is supplied by the ilioinguinal nerve (through its anterior scrotal branches) and the genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve. The back of the scrotum is supplied by the posterior scrotal nerves, branches of the pudendal nerve (S2–S4), and by the perineal branch of the posterior femoral cutaneous nerve. The pudendal nerve's branches (posterior scrotal, perineal, deep perineal and the dorsal nerve of the penis) serve the perineum and penis, not the cremaster. The periprostatic plexus is autonomic, to the prostate and erectile tissue.</p>\n<h4>If the genital branch is injured</h4>\n<p>Damage to the genital branch takes away the cremaster's motor supply, so the cremasteric reflex is lost and the testis on that side does not rise when the thigh is stroked. Part of the skin over the front of the scrotum also goes numb. The scrotal skin still wrinkles normally, because the dartos runs on sympathetic fibers and does not depend on the cremaster.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Cremaster: skeletal, from int. oblique; over internal spermatic fascia</li>\n<li>Motor: genital branch of genitofemoral (L1–L2), runs in cord</li>\n<li>Action: raises testis (cold, protection); temperature control with dartos</li>\n<li>Reflex: stroke superomedial thigh → same-side testis rises</li>\n<li>Afferent: ilioinguinal (L1) (+ femoral branch of genitofemoral); efferent: genital branch</li>\n<li>Tests L1–L2; patellar L2–L4, plantar L5–S1, ankle S1–S2, anal wink S2–S4</li>\n<li>Anterior scrotum: ilioinguinal + genital branch; posterior: pudendal (S2–S4) + posterior femoral cutaneous</li>\n<li>Genital branch lesion: no reflex + numb anterior scrotum; dartos wrinkling intact</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The spermatic cord and scrotum","sn":6,"pn":"6.3"},{"l":"ANAT-G2","h":"Epididymis and ductus deferens","p":"<h4>The epididymis</h4>\n<p>The <b>epididymis</b> is a long, tightly coiled tube that lies on the <b>posterior</b> side of the testis, outside the tunica albuginea. It is the site where sperm are stored and <b>mature</b>. It has three parts. The <b>head</b> sits on top of the testis and receives the efferent ductules from the rete testis. The <b>body</b> runs down the back of the testis. The <b>tail</b> lies at the back and bottom of the testis (posteroinferiorly) and is continuous with the ductus deferens. The epididymis adds little fluid; most of the seminal fluid comes later from the seminal glands and the prostate.</p>\n<p>The rete testis lies inside the testis, in the mediastinum testis. It sends sperm to the head of the epididymis through the efferent ductules. It is not where the ductus deferens begins.</p>\n<h4>The ductus (vas) deferens</h4>\n<p>The ductus deferens carries sperm from the epididymis toward the urethra. Its path runs upward and back into the body:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>It begins at the <b>tail of the epididymis</b>, not at the head or body.</li>\n<li>It runs up the whole length of the spermatic cord, behind the testis and then through the inguinal canal.</li>\n<li>It enters the abdominal cavity at the deep inguinal ring.</li>\n<li>It runs back into the pelvis toward the prostate. Behind the bladder it joins the duct of the seminal gland to form the ejaculatory duct, which opens into the urethra inside the prostate.</li>\n</ol>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G078-55.jpg\" alt=\"Male pelvis and testis: epididymis, ductus deferens and testicular vessels\"></div><figcaption>Male pelvis and testis: epididymis, ductus deferens and testicular vessels <small>· 2026 TA session: GI week 1 part 2, slide 55</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Clinical</h4>\n<p>A urinary tract infection can travel up the ductus deferens and reach the tail of the epididymis first. The result is epididymitis: a painful, elongated, inflamed swelling along the back of the testis. In a <b>vasectomy</b>, the ductus is cut or tied in the scrotum, where it lies in the cord before it enters the abdomen. The cut must be made on both sides for the operation to work.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Epididymis: posterior to testis, outside tunica albuginea; sperm storage + maturation</li>\n<li>Head on top (efferent ductules in); body behind; tail posteroinferior</li>\n<li>Rete testis: inside testis → efferent ductules → epididymis head (not ductus start)</li>\n<li>Seminal fluid: seminal glands + prostate, not epididymis</li>\n<li>Ductus deferens: tail of epididymis → cord → canal → deep ring → pelvis</li>\n<li>+ seminal gland duct → ejaculatory duct → urethra in prostate</li>\n<li>Urinary infection → ductus → tail of epididymis first (epididymitis)</li>\n<li>Vasectomy: in scrotum, before abdomen, both sides</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Testis, epididymis and drainage","sn":7,"pn":"7.2"},{"l":"ANAT-G2","h":"Lymph of the testis and the scrotum","p":"<h4>Lymph follows the blood vessels</h4>\n<p>Lymph vessels run with the blood vessels, so an organ drains to the nodes beside its arteries. The testis and the scrotum lie side by side but have different blood supplies, so their lymph goes to completely different nodes. This lets a clinician trace a testicular cancer and a cancer of the scrotal skin to different nodes.</p>\n<p>The <b>testis</b> formed high on the posterior abdominal wall, beside the kidneys, and its artery still comes from the aorta. As it descended, it dragged its lymph vessels down with it. Its lymph therefore runs back up the spermatic cord with the testicular vessels, through the inguinal canal, to the <b>para-aortic nodes</b> beside the aorta and inferior vena cava, at about the level of the first lumbar vertebra (L1). These nodes are also called the lumbar nodes or the (lateral) aortic nodes; all three names describe the same group. A cancer inside the testis spreads to them first, not to the groin. From there it can climb the lumbar trunks and thoracic duct to nodes in the chest. Cells that enter the pampiniform plexus take a venous route instead and reach the lungs through the blood.</p>\n<p>The <b>scrotum</b>, its skin and wall, develops locally in the groin and perineum. It is supplied by the internal and external pudendal arteries and the cremasteric artery. Its lymph runs with the superficial vessels of the lower abdominal wall and perineum to the <b>superficial inguinal nodes</b>. A tumor of the scrotal skin, outside the testis, spreads to these groin nodes.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G003-52.jpg\" alt=\"Lymph: testis to para-aortic nodes, scrotum to superficial inguinal nodes\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G003-52c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Lymph: testis to para-aortic nodes, scrotum to superficial inguinal nodes\"></div></div><figcaption>Lymph: testis to para-aortic nodes, scrotum to superficial inguinal nodes <small>· 2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 52</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The first nodes for the testis are the para-aortic (lumbar) nodes; the pre-aortic nodes in front of the aorta mainly drain the gut. The cisterna chyli is not a node group; it is the sac that later collects lymph from the lumbar and intestinal trunks.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G078-56.jpg\" alt=\"Testis versus scrotum lymph drainage, and the path of testicular descent\"></div><figcaption>Testis versus scrotum lymph drainage, and the path of testicular descent <small>· 2026 TA session: GI week 1 part 2, slide 56</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Node groups to keep apart</h4>\n<p>Each node group has its own main territory. The superficial inguinal nodes drain skin below the umbilicus, the scrotum, the perineum and the superficial lower limb. The deep inguinal nodes drain the glans penis and the deep lower limb. The external iliac nodes receive lymph from the lower limb after the inguinal nodes, and from part of the bladder. The internal iliac nodes drain the pelvic organs and deep perineum. The sacral (presacral) nodes drain the rectum and the back of the pelvis. The para-aortic (lumbar) nodes drain the testis or ovary, the kidneys and the posterior abdominal wall. The pre-aortic nodes, in front of the aorta at the origins of the celiac trunk and the superior and inferior mesenteric arteries, mainly drain the gut. The axillary nodes drain skin above the umbilicus.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Lymph follows blood vessels → testis and scrotum drain differently</li>\n<li>Testis (aortic artery, formed by kidneys) → para-aortic = lumbar = aortic nodes (~L1)</li>\n<li>Testicular cancer → para-aortic first, not groin; then thoracic duct → chest nodes</li>\n<li>Scrotum (pudendal arteries) → superficial inguinal nodes</li>\n<li>Pre-aortic: mainly gut; cisterna chyli = collecting sac, not first node</li>\n<li>Deep inguinal: glans, deep lower limb; internal iliac: pelvic organs</li>\n<li>Sacral: rectum; external iliac: lower limb (after inguinal), part of bladder</li>\n<li>Axillary: skin above umbilicus</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Testis, epididymis and drainage","sn":7,"pn":"7.3"},{"l":"ANAT-G2","h":"Descent problems and scrotal swellings","p":"<h4>Undescended testis (cryptorchidism)</h4>\n<p>In <b>cryptorchidism</b> (cryptorchism), the testis has not fully descended into the scrotum. It has stopped somewhere along its <b>normal path</b>: in the abdomen, at the pelvic brim, at the deep ring, in the inguinal canal, or just outside the superficial ring. A testis that is missing from the scrotum but can be felt in the inguinal canal is an undescended testis. A testis that leaves the normal path, for example into the perineum or the thigh, is an <b>ectopic testis</b>, a different condition.</p>\n<p>An undescended testis sits at core body temperature, so it makes sperm poorly, and it carries a much higher risk of <b>testicular cancer</b>. It is brought down into the scrotum surgically or removed.</p>\n<h4>Androgen insensitivity syndrome</h4>\n<p>In androgen insensitivity syndrome, the person has male chromosomes (XY) and testes, but the body's cells cannot respond to testosterone. The body develops as female, with a short, blind vagina and no uterus or ovaries, so the condition may first come to light as trouble conceiving. The testes stay in the abdomen or groin. Because undescended testes carry a high cancer risk, cancer is the main concern, and the testes are usually removed. Testosterone has no effect in these patients, there is no special breast cancer risk, and torsion or hernia are far less worrying.</p>\n<p>Other terms are easy to confuse with these. True hermaphroditism means both ovarian and testicular tissue are present. Pseudohermaphroditism means gonads of one sex with external genitalia that resemble the other sex. Congenital adrenal hyperplasia is an adrenal enzyme defect that can masculinize a female. Chordee is a downward bend of the penis.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G003-53.jpg\" alt=\"Hydrocele, hematocele and torsion of the spermatic cord\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G003-53c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Hydrocele, hematocele and torsion of the spermatic cord\"></div></div><figcaption>Hydrocele, hematocele and torsion of the spermatic cord <small>· 2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 53</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Swellings of the scrotum</h4>\n<p>A <b>hydrocele</b> is fluid in the cavity of the tunica vaginalis, between its two layers. When a light is shone through the scrotum, it passes through the fluid (transillumination). A <b>hematocele</b> is blood in the tunica vaginalis or scrotum, usually after trauma; the light is dim and almost fully blocked. A <b>varicocele</b> is dilated pampiniform veins, a bag of worms, mostly on the left. <b>Torsion of the spermatic cord</b> twists the cord and cuts off the testis's blood supply. It causes sudden, severe pain and needs treatment at once, or the testis is lost. On the twisted side the <b>cremasteric reflex is absent</b> in almost every case, so stroking the inner thigh does not raise that testis. A testicular cancer, by contrast, is a painless hard mass, and an undescended testis does not cause sudden pain.</p>\n<p>Some look-alike terms belong elsewhere. A rectocele is the rectum bulging into the vagina, and a cystocele is the bladder bulging into the vagina. Hypospadias is a urethra that opens on the underside of the penis.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Cryptorchidism: testis stuck on normal path (abdomen, pelvic brim, deep ring, canal, outside superficial ring)</li>\n<li>Off the path (perineum, thigh) = ectopic testis</li>\n<li>Undescended → poor sperm production, ↑ testicular cancer → brought down or removed</li>\n<li>Androgen insensitivity: XY, female body, blind vagina, no uterus; testes removed (cancer)</li>\n<li>Hermaphroditism: both gonad tissues; pseudo-: gonads of one sex, genitalia of other</li>\n<li>Hydrocele: fluid in tunica vaginalis; transilluminates</li>\n<li>Hematocele: blood after trauma; light blocked</li>\n<li>Varicocele: dilated pampiniform veins, left</li>\n<li>Torsion: sudden severe pain, absent cremasteric reflex; emergency</li>\n<li>Rectocele, cystocele: into vagina; hypospadias: urethra on underside</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Testis, epididymis and drainage","sn":7,"pn":"7.4"},{"l":"ANAT-G3","h":"Two layers and the space between","p":"<h4>One sheet, two layers</h4>\n<p>The <b>peritoneum</b> is a thin, slippery serous membrane that lines the abdomen and pelvis. It lies just deep to the extraperitoneal fascia and fat of the abdominal wall. It is one continuous sheet, but it has two layers. Like other serous membranes, it makes only a thin, watery (serous) fluid. It makes no bile and no mucus.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G004-12.jpg\" alt=\"Abdominal wall layers down to parietal peritoneum; visceral peritoneum on the gut\"></div><figcaption>Abdominal wall layers down to parietal peritoneum; visceral peritoneum on the gut <small>· 2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach &amp; Celiac, slide 12</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The <b>parietal peritoneum</b> lines the internal surface of the abdominal and pelvic walls. It shares the blood vessels, lymphatics and <b>somatic</b> nerves of the part of the wall it lines. Those somatic nerves make it sensitive to pain, heat, cold and cutting. Pain from the parietal layer is therefore sharp and <b>well localized</b>: the patient can point to the spot.</p>\n<p>The <b>visceral peritoneum</b> covers the organs (viscera). It shares the blood vessels, lymphatics and nerves of the organ it covers, and it lacks somatic sensory nerves. Pain from it is therefore vague and <b>poorly localized</b>. An easy way to keep them apart: parietal goes with the wall and feels like skin; visceral goes with the organ and feels like gut.</p>\n<p>Appendicitis shows the difference. At first only the visceral peritoneum of the appendix is inflamed, so the pain is vague and felt around the umbilicus. Once the inflamed appendix touches the parietal peritoneum of the right lower quadrant, its somatic nerves take over. The pain then becomes sharp and well localized, with tenderness, guarding and rebound tenderness over that spot.</p>\n<h4>Where the two layers come from</h4>\n<p>In the embryo, the lateral plate mesoderm splits into two sheets. The <b>somatic</b> layer lines the body wall and becomes the parietal peritoneum. The <b>splanchnic</b> layer wraps the gut tube and becomes the visceral peritoneum. The gap between the two sheets becomes the body cavity. Endoderm forms only the inner lining of the gut, and ectoderm forms skin and nerves, so neither forms peritoneum.</p>\n<h4>The peritoneal cavity</h4>\n<p>The <b>peritoneal cavity</b> is the <b>potential space</b> between the parietal and the visceral layer. Normally the two layers touch, separated only by a thin film of peritoneal fluid that lets the organs glide. The cavity holds no organs and no lymph nodes. Lymph nodes sit in the mesenteries and behind the peritoneum. The cavity also runs on into the pelvis with no divider.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/D041-140.jpg\" alt=\"Sketch: parietal and visceral peritoneum with the peritoneal cavity between\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/D041-140c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Sketch: parietal and visceral peritoneum with the peritoneal cavity between\"></div></div><figcaption>Sketch: parietal and visceral peritoneum with the peritoneal cavity between <small>· 2025 TA review, slide 140</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Three nearby spaces lie outside the cavity. Superficial to the parietal layer lie the extraperitoneal fat, then the transversalis fascia and the rest of the body wall. Deep to the visceral layer lies the organ itself. Behind the parietal layer at the back of the abdomen lies the retroperitoneum, which holds the kidneys and great vessels (1.2).</p>\n<p>In males the cavity is <b>completely closed</b>. The processus vaginalis, the pouch of peritoneum that followed the testis into the scrotum, normally seals off, so the cavity does not stay open to the testis. In females the cavity opens to the outside through the uterine tubes, the uterine cavity and the vagina. This opening is a possible path for infection from outside.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Parietal: lines abdominal and pelvic wall; somatic nerves → sharp, well-localized pain</li>\n<li>Visceral: covers organs; no somatic sensory nerves → vague, poorly localized pain</li>\n<li>Parietal ← somatic lateral plate mesoderm; visceral ← splanchnic lateral plate mesoderm</li>\n<li>Endoderm and ectoderm form no peritoneum</li>\n<li>Peritoneal cavity = potential space between the layers; thin serous fluid only</li>\n<li>No organs, no lymph nodes inside the cavity</li>\n<li>Outside it: extraperitoneal fat + transversalis fascia (superficial), organ (deep), retroperitoneum (behind)</li>\n<li>Appendicitis: vague pain around the umbilicus (visceral) → sharp right lower quadrant pain (parietal)</li>\n<li>Closed in males (processus vaginalis sealed); open in females via tubes, uterus, vagina</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The peritoneum and its cavity","sn":1,"pn":"1.1"},{"l":"ANAT-G3","h":"Intraperitoneal and retroperitoneal organs","p":"<h4>What the words mean</h4>\n<p>Organs are sorted by how much of their surface the peritoneum covers. An <b>intraperitoneal</b> organ is covered by visceral peritoneum on almost every side. It usually hangs from a mesentery or an omentum, so it can be lifted and moved. The stomach, the first 2 cm of the duodenum, the jejunum, ileum, appendix, transverse colon, sigmoid colon, spleen, liver and the <b>tail of the pancreas</b> are intraperitoneal.</p>\n<p>The name is misleading. An intraperitoneal organ is not inside the peritoneal cavity, which holds only fluid. The organ has pushed into the closed sac, like a fist pressed into an inflated balloon. The balloon wall wraps the fist, but the fist never enters the balloon.</p>\n<p>A <b>retroperitoneal</b> organ lies behind the peritoneum, between the parietal peritoneum and the posterior abdominal wall. Peritoneum covers only its front surface. It has no mesentery, so it is fixed in place.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G074-4.jpg\" alt=\"Sagittal section: greater sac pink, lesser sac blue; pancreas, duodenum, aorta behind\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G074-4c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Sagittal section: greater sac pink, lesser sac blue; pancreas, duodenum, aorta behind\"></div></div><figcaption>Sagittal section: greater sac pink, lesser sac blue; pancreas, duodenum, aorta behind <small>· 2026 Harlem clickers: Peritoneum, Esophagus &amp; Stomach, slide 4</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Primary versus secondary</h4>\n<p><b>Primarily retroperitoneal</b> organs formed behind the peritoneum and never had a mesentery. They are the kidneys, ureters, suprarenal (adrenal) glands, aorta and inferior vena cava (IVC). So is the uterine cervix. The gonads also form in the posterior wall without a gut mesentery.</p>\n<p><b>Secondarily retroperitoneal</b> organs began inside a mesentery like the rest of the gut. Later they were pressed against the posterior abdominal wall. Their mesentery fused with the peritoneum of the wall and disappeared. Now they are fixed, with peritoneum only on their front. There are four: the <b>duodenum</b> (all but its first 2 cm), the <b>pancreas</b> (all but its tail), the <b>ascending colon</b> and the <b>descending colon</b>. The pancreatic tail stays intraperitoneal because it runs inside the splenorenal ligament to the spleen (2.2).</p>\n<p>The urinary bladder is <b>subperitoneal</b>. It lies below the peritoneum, which covers only its upper surface.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G004-26.jpg\" alt=\"Posterior abdominal wall: retroperitoneal organs, colon beds and mesentery roots\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G004-26c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Posterior abdominal wall: retroperitoneal organs, colon beds and mesentery roots\"></div></div><figcaption>Posterior abdominal wall: retroperitoneal organs, colon beds and mesentery roots <small>· 2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach &amp; Celiac, slide 26</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Movable or fixed</h4>\n<p>This explains what you feel in the lab. Organs on a mesentery move freely. The transverse colon hangs on the transverse mesocolon, the sigmoid colon on the sigmoid mesocolon, the jejunum and ileum on the mesentery proper, and the appendix on its mesoappendix. The ascending and descending colon have lost their mesentery, so they stay stuck to the posterior wall and cannot be lifted.</p>\n<p>It also tells you where blood goes after an injury. A torn intraperitoneal organ, such as the jejunum, bleeds into the peritoneal cavity. A hematoma around a retroperitoneal organ, such as the kidney, stays behind the parietal peritoneum.</p>\n<h4>Memory word: SAD PUCKER</h4>\n<p>The retroperitoneal organs spell SAD PUCKER. <b>S</b> is the suprarenal glands, <b>A</b> the aorta and IVC, <b>D</b> the duodenum (parts 2–4) and <b>P</b> the pancreas (except the tail). <b>U</b> is the ureters, <b>C</b> the ascending and descending colon, <b>K</b> the kidneys, <b>E</b> the abdominal esophagus, and <b>R</b> the upper two-thirds of the rectum (its lower third lies below the peritoneum). Only D, P and C are secondarily retroperitoneal. The rectum is retroperitoneal, but it is not counted as secondarily retroperitoneal.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Intraperitoneal: covered almost all round, on mesentery or omentum, movable (stomach)</li>\n<li>Intraperitoneal ≠ inside the cavity; fist pressed into a balloon</li>\n<li>Retroperitoneal: behind parietal peritoneum; front covered only; no mesentery; fixed</li>\n<li>Primary (never had mesentery): kidneys, ureters, suprarenals, aorta, IVC (+ cervix)</li>\n<li>Secondary (mesentery fused): duodenum 2–4, pancreas except tail, ascending + descending colon</li>\n<li>Intraperitoneal exceptions: first 2 cm of duodenum; pancreatic tail (splenorenal lig.)</li>\n<li>Bladder: subperitoneal, top surface only</li>\n<li>SAD PUCKER: Suprarenal, Aorta/IVC, Duodenum, Pancreas, Ureters, Colon, Kidneys, Esophagus, Rectum</li>\n<li>Movable: transverse + sigmoid colon, jejunum, ileum, appendix; fixed: ascending colon</li>\n<li>Injury: jejunum bleeds into the cavity; kidney hematoma stays behind the peritoneum</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The peritoneum and its cavity","sn":1,"pn":"1.2"},{"l":"ANAT-G3","h":"Peritoneal formations and the liver ligaments","p":"<h4>Folds that carry vessels</h4>\n<p>Wherever the peritoneum leaves the wall to reach an organ, or passes from one organ to another, it forms a fold of two layers pressed together. Vessels, lymphatics and nerves run between the two layers to reach the organs. There are five kinds of fold.</p>\n<p>A <b>peritoneal ligament</b> is a <b>double layer</b> of peritoneum that connects an organ to another organ or to the abdominal wall. The falciform ligament is an example. An <b>omentum</b> is a double-layered fold that passes from the stomach and the proximal duodenum to nearby organs. There are two, the greater omentum (2.2) and the lesser omentum (2.3). A <b>mesentery</b> is a double layer that connects an intraperitoneal organ to the body wall, usually the posterior wall. Examples are the mesentery proper, the transverse mesocolon, the sigmoid mesocolon and the mesoappendix. The mesentery proper hangs the jejunum and ileum from the posterior wall, and their vessels, lymphatics and nerves run between its two layers.</p>\n<p>Two smaller formations complete the list. A <b>peritoneal fold</b> is peritoneum lifted off the wall by vessels, ducts or closed-off fetal vessels beneath it, such as the umbilical folds on the inner anterior abdominal wall. A <b>peritoneal recess</b> (fossa) is a pouch formed by a fold, such as the inferior recess of the omental bursa.</p>\n<p>Ligaments, omenta and mesenteries are always double layers, never single. Omenta and mesenteries are both made of <b>visceral peritoneum</b>, carried out by the gut tube as it grew. At their roots they become continuous with the parietal peritoneum. Retroperitoneal organs have no mesentery at all. The one four-layered fold is the greater omentum, whose double layer hangs down and folds back up.</p>\n<p>The embryo explains the pattern. The ventral mesentery becomes the lesser omentum and the falciform, coronary and triangular ligaments of the liver. The dorsal mesentery becomes the greater omentum, the mesentery proper, the mesocolons and the mesoappendix.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G004-24.jpg\" alt=\"Falciform ligament, greater and lesser omentum, transverse mesocolon and mesentery\"></div><figcaption>Falciform ligament, greater and lesser omentum, transverse mesocolon and mesentery <small>· 2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach &amp; Celiac, slide 24</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Ligaments of the liver</h4>\n<p>The <b>falciform ligament</b> joins the front of the liver to the anterior abdominal wall and the diaphragm. It is the only fold that ties the liver to the anterior wall. It is a remnant of the ventral mesentery and is separate from the greater omentum. It also divides the subphrenic space above the liver into right and left halves (3.3), so a right subphrenic abscess tends to stop at the midline. Its free lower edge carries the <b>round ligament of the liver</b> (ligamentum teres hepatis), the closed-off left umbilical vein. The gastroduodenal artery is not in it; that artery runs behind the duodenum.</p>\n<p>The <b>coronary</b> and <b>triangular ligaments</b> join the top of the liver to the diaphragm. The <b>ligamentum venosum</b> is the fibrous remnant of the fetal ductus venosus. Other fetal vessels leave other remnants. The right umbilical vein disappears early in the embryo and leaves no ligament. The umbilical arteries become the medial umbilical ligaments, and the vitelline veins become the portal vein and its tributaries. The <b>hepatorenal ligament</b> is a peritoneal fold from the back of the right lobe of the liver to the right kidney, continuing the lower layer of the coronary ligament. The hepatorenal recess (Morison pouch) lies between the same two organs (3.3). The fold from the liver to the lesser curvature of the stomach is the hepatogastric ligament, part of the lesser omentum.</p>\n<h4>Root of the mesentery</h4>\n<p>The <b>root of the mesentery</b> is the line where the mesentery proper attaches to the posterior wall. It is about 15 cm long. It begins at the duodenojejunal junction, on the left side of the second lumbar vertebra (L2). From there it runs <b>obliquely downward and to the right</b> to the ileocolic junction and the <b>right sacroiliac joint</b>. The distance from the root to the intestine averages about 20 cm. The root of the transverse mesocolon is different: it runs roughly horizontally along the inferior border of the pancreas.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Ligament = double layer, organ → organ or → wall (falciform)</li>\n<li>Omentum = double fold, stomach/proximal duodenum → nearby organs (greater, lesser)</li>\n<li>Mesentery = double layer, intraperitoneal organ → (posterior) wall; mesentery proper: jejunum + ileum</li>\n<li>Fold: raised by vessels (umbilical folds); recess: pouch (inferior recess of bursa)</li>\n<li>Omenta + mesenteries = visceral peritoneum; retroperitoneal organs: no mesentery</li>\n<li>Ventral mesentery → lesser omentum, falciform, coronary, triangular; dorsal → greater omentum, mesenteries</li>\n<li>Falciform: liver → anterior wall + diaphragm; splits subphrenic space R/L</li>\n<li>Round lig. (falciform free edge) = left umbilical vein; lig. venosum = ductus venosus; umbilical aa. → medial umbilical ligs.</li>\n<li>Coronary/triangular: liver → diaphragm; hepatorenal: liver → right kidney</li>\n<li>Mesentery root ~15 cm: duodenojejunal junction (left of L2) → right sacroiliac joint</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Ligaments, omenta and mesenteries","sn":2,"pn":"2.1"},{"l":"ANAT-G3","h":"Greater omentum and the spleen's ligaments","p":"<h4>The apron</h4>\n<p>The <b>greater omentum</b> is the large, fatty apron of peritoneum that hangs from the stomach. It runs from the <b>greater curvature</b> of the stomach and the first part of the duodenum to the front of the <b>transverse colon</b>. On the way it hangs down in front of the small intestine, then folds back up. Because a double layer hangs down and turns back, the apron has four layers of peritoneum. It does not hold the intestines to the posterior wall; that is the job of the mesentery. The transverse mesocolon, for example, holds the transverse colon to the posterior wall and never reaches the stomach.</p>\n<p>The greater omentum is also a defender. It stores fat and is packed with immune cells. It moves toward an inflamed or perforated organ, such as a burst ulcer or appendix, and sticks to it. This walls off the infection and limits its spread through the peritoneal cavity.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G004-21.jpg\" alt=\"Parts of the greater omentum (**) and of the lesser omentum (*)\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G004-21c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Parts of the greater omentum (**) and of the lesser omentum (*)\"></div></div><figcaption>Parts of the greater omentum (**) and of the lesser omentum (*) <small>· 2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach &amp; Celiac, slide 21</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Three parts, all from the greater curvature</h4>\n<p>The greater omentum is divided into three ligaments. Each name gives its two ends, and all three start on the greater curvature.</p>\n<p>The <b>gastrophrenic ligament</b> is the upper part. It runs from the fundus of the stomach to the diaphragm. The <b>gastrosplenic ligament</b> is the left part. It runs from the greater curvature to the hilum of the spleen, and it carries the short gastric and left gastro-omental vessels. The <b>gastrocolic ligament</b> runs from the greater curvature to the transverse colon. It is the apron itself, and it forms the front wall of the lower lesser sac (3.1).</p>\n<p>Ligaments that start on the liver belong elsewhere. The hepatogastric and hepatoduodenal ligaments form the lesser omentum (2.3). The hepatorenal ligament joins the liver to the right kidney, and the falciform ligament joins the liver to the anterior wall. None of these is part of the greater omentum. No ligament called gastrointestinal exists.</p>\n<h4>Ligaments of the spleen and the left colon</h4>\n<p>The <b>splenorenal (lienorenal) ligament</b> runs from the spleen to the left kidney. The splenic artery and vein run between its layers to the splenic hilum, and so does the <b>tail of the pancreas</b>. That is why the tail is the one intraperitoneal part of the pancreas (1.2). Surgery on the pancreatic tail therefore means opening the splenorenal ligament first. The gastrosplenic ligament ties the spleen to the stomach, and the gastrocolic ligament ties the stomach to the colon; neither holds the pancreas.</p>\n<p>The <b>phrenicocolic ligament</b> runs from the left colic (splenic) flexure to the diaphragm. It carries no major vessels.</p>\n<h4>Three pelvic ligaments</h4>\n<p>Three pelvic ligaments are also worth knowing. The suspensory ligament of the ovary runs from the lateral pelvic wall to the ovary. The ovarian ligament runs from the ovary to the uterus. The round ligament of the uterus runs from the uterus to the deep inguinal ring. The last two are remnants of the gubernaculum.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Greater omentum: greater curvature + 1st part duodenum → front of transverse colon; 4-layer apron</li>\n<li>Greater omentum: fat + immune cells; sticks to a perforation, walls off infection</li>\n<li>Gastrophrenic: fundus → diaphragm (upper part)</li>\n<li>Gastrosplenic: greater curvature → splenic hilum; short gastric + left gastro-omental vessels</li>\n<li>Gastrocolic: greater curvature → transverse colon = apron; front of lower lesser sac</li>\n<li>Liver ligaments (hepatogastric, hepatoduodenal, hepatorenal, falciform): not greater omentum</li>\n<li>No gastrointestinal ligament; mesentery, not omentum, holds intestines to wall (transverse mesocolon: colon → posterior wall)</li>\n<li>Splenorenal: spleen → left kidney; splenic vessels + pancreatic tail (open to reach tail)</li>\n<li>Phrenicocolic: splenic flexure → diaphragm</li>\n<li>Ovarian lig. + round lig. of uterus = gubernaculum; suspensory lig.: pelvic wall → ovary</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Ligaments, omenta and mesenteries","sn":2,"pn":"2.2"},{"l":"ANAT-G3","h":"The lesser omentum and its two ligaments","p":"<h4>One sheet, two parts</h4>\n<p>The <b>lesser omentum</b> is a double layer of peritoneum that runs from the <b>lesser curvature</b> of the stomach and the first part of the duodenum up to the underside of the liver. It comes from the ventral mesentery of the embryo. It is one continuous sheet with two named parts. Each part is named for the two organs it joins.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G004-123.jpg\" alt=\"Lesser omentum: hepatogastric and hepatoduodenal ligaments, with the omental foramen behind the free edge\"></div><figcaption>Lesser omentum: hepatogastric and hepatoduodenal ligaments, with the omental foramen behind the free edge <small>· 2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach &amp; Celiac, slide 123</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Hepatogastric ligament</h4>\n<p>The <b>hepatogastric ligament</b>, also called the gastrohepatic ligament, joins the liver to the lesser curvature of the stomach. It is the larger, left part of the lesser omentum, and it is thin and membranous. Near the lesser curvature it carries the left and right gastric vessels (6.2). It carries no portal triad, and it is not the free edge.</p>\n<h4>Hepatoduodenal ligament</h4>\n<p>The <b>hepatoduodenal ligament</b> joins the liver to the <b>first part of the duodenum</b>. It is the right part of the lesser omentum. It is thick because it holds the <b>portal triad</b>: the proper hepatic artery, the hepatic portal vein and the common bile duct (2.4). Its right border is the <b>free edge</b> of the lesser omentum. The edge is free because nothing attaches to its right side. Directly behind it lies the epiploic (omental) foramen, the doorway into the lesser sac (3.2).</p>\n<p>So the duodenal part is the thick edge with the triad, and the gastric part is the thin sheet beside it. The lesser omentum is the whole sheet; the hepatoduodenal ligament is the specific part that conducts the triad between the liver and the duodenum.</p>\n<h4>Ligaments that are not lesser omentum</h4>\n<p>Ligaments that leave the greater curvature (gastrophrenic, gastrosplenic and gastrocolic) form the greater omentum (2.2). Other liver ligaments go elsewhere. The falciform ligament runs to the anterior abdominal wall, and the coronary and triangular ligaments run to the diaphragm. The round ligament of the liver lies in the free edge of the falciform ligament, not of the lesser omentum. Far to the left, the splenorenal (lienorenal) ligament joins the spleen to the left kidney. No ligament called gastroduodenal forms the free edge. None of these lies in the lesser omentum, and none carries the portal triad.</p>\n<h4>In the operating room</h4>\n<p>Gallbladder and stomach operations work right beside the lesser omentum. A surgeon who opens it to see the gallbladder is working next to the triad. When blood flow into the liver must be stopped, the surgeon pinches the free edge, the hepatoduodenal ligament, between two fingers. This is the <b>Pringle maneuver</b> (2.4).</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Lesser omentum: lesser curvature + 1st part duodenum → liver; ventral mesentery</li>\n<li>= hepatogastric + hepatoduodenal ligaments, continuous</li>\n<li>Hepatogastric (gastrohepatic): liver → lesser curvature; left, thin; gastric vessels, no triad</li>\n<li>Hepatoduodenal: liver → 1st part duodenum; right, thick; free edge; portal triad</li>\n<li>Portal triad = proper hepatic a., hepatic portal v., common bile duct</li>\n<li>Epiploic foramen directly behind the free edge</li>\n<li>Not lesser omentum: gastrophrenic, gastrosplenic, gastrocolic, falciform, coronary, splenorenal</li>\n<li>Pinch the free edge (Pringle) → stops inflow to the liver</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Ligaments, omenta and mesenteries","sn":2,"pn":"2.3"},{"l":"ANAT-G3","h":"The portal triad in the free edge","p":"<h4>Three tubes in one ligament</h4>\n<p>The <b>portal triad</b> is three tubes that run together in the <b>hepatoduodenal ligament</b>, the free right edge of the lesser omentum. They travel to and from the porta hepatis, the doorway of the liver. The lesser omentum as a whole is made of the hepatoduodenal and hepatogastric ligaments; only the hepatoduodenal part carries the triad.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G004-46.jpg\" alt=\"Cross-section: portal triad in the free edge, omental foramen and vena cava behind\"></div><figcaption>Cross-section: portal triad in the free edge, omental foramen and vena cava behind <small>· 2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach &amp; Celiac, slide 46</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The <b>common bile duct</b> lies on the right, in front. It carries bile from the liver and gallbladder down to the duodenum. The <b>proper hepatic artery</b> lies on the left, in front. It brings arterial blood to the liver and gallbladder, and it is the only artery in the ligament. The <b>hepatic portal vein</b> lies behind both. It is the largest structure in the ligament and the only large vein there. It carries venous blood from the gut and spleen to the liver.</p>\n<p>Directly behind the ligament is the <b>epiploic (omental) foramen</b>. It joins the greater sac to the lesser sac (omental bursa) (3.2). Behind the foramen lies the inferior vena cava (IVC).</p>\n<h4>Where the hepatic artery comes from</h4>\n<p>The celiac trunk gives off the <b>common hepatic artery</b>, which runs to the right along the top of the pancreas. The common hepatic gives off the gastroduodenal artery, which turns down behind the first part of the duodenum. Only after that branch does the artery continue as the <b>proper hepatic artery</b>, which climbs in the hepatoduodenal ligament. It splits into right and left hepatic arteries. The right hepatic gives the <b>cystic artery</b> to the gallbladder. So the celiac trunk, the common hepatic artery and the gastroduodenal artery all lie below and behind the ligament, never inside it.</p>\n<h4>Look-alikes outside the triad</h4>\n<p>The <b>hepatic veins</b> are not part of the triad. They leave the back of the liver straight into the IVC and never run in the lesser omentum. The IVC itself lies behind the epiploic foramen, outside the ligament. The cystic duct and cystic artery serve the gallbladder, above the ligament. The right gastric artery usually branches from the proper hepatic artery but runs off to the lesser curvature. The left gastric artery runs along the lesser curvature, and the right gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery along the greater curvature. The splenic artery and vein run left along the pancreas to the spleen, and the pancreatic duct runs inside the pancreas. The superior mesenteric artery (SMA) and vein serve the midgut, lower down, behind the neck of the pancreas. The inferior mesenteric vein drains the hindgut and usually joins the splenic vein behind the pancreas, far below the ligament. The vagus is a nerve, not a vessel.</p>\n<h4>Pringle maneuver</h4>\n<p>To stop blood flowing into the liver, the surgeon slides a finger into the epiploic foramen, behind the free edge. The hepatoduodenal ligament is then squeezed against the thumb or with a soft clamp. This compresses the <b>proper hepatic artery</b> and the <b>hepatic portal vein</b> together. It controls bleeding in liver surgery and liver transplants.</p>\n<p>It also helps in gallbladder surgery. The cystic artery usually comes from the right hepatic artery, a branch of the proper hepatic. If the cystic artery is torn before it is clamped, squeezing the ligament slows the bleeding until the artery can be tied.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Triad runs in hepatoduodenal lig. (free edge of lesser omentum) → porta hepatis</li>\n<li>Bile duct: right, front; proper hepatic a.: left, front; portal vein: behind both</li>\n<li>Only artery = proper hepatic; only large vein = portal vein</li>\n<li>Celiac → common hepatic → gastroduodenal (behind duodenum) + proper hepatic → R/L hepatic</li>\n<li>Cystic a. ← right hepatic a.</li>\n<li>Not in triad: hepatic veins (→ IVC), IVC, cystic duct, common hepatic, splenic, SMA, inferior mesenteric v.</li>\n<li>Pringle: squeeze the ligament → proper hepatic a. + portal v. compressed</li>\n<li>Epiploic foramen behind the free edge: greater sac ↔ lesser sac</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Ligaments, omenta and mesenteries","sn":2,"pn":"2.4"},{"l":"ANAT-G3","h":"The greater sac and the lesser sac","p":"<h4>Two sacs, one opening</h4>\n<p>The peritoneal cavity has two parts. The <b>greater sac</b> is most of the cavity. It stretches across the whole width of the abdomen and from the diaphragm down to the pelvic floor. The transverse mesocolon divides it into a supracolic compartment above and an infracolic compartment below. It also has paracolic gutters and recesses where pus collects (3.3).</p>\n<p>The <b>lesser sac</b>, or <b>omental bursa</b>, is a smaller, irregular space. It lies <b>behind</b> the liver, the lesser omentum, the stomach and the upper front part of the greater omentum. It is closed on every side except one. Its only opening is the <b>epiploic (omental) foramen</b>, through which it communicates with the greater sac. The front border of that opening is the hepatoduodenal ligament, and its back border is peritoneum over the inferior vena cava (3.2). So it is not completely sealed off. It contains no portal vein; that vein runs in the hepatoduodenal ligament, in front of the foramen.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/D041-152.jpg\" alt=\"Lesser sac behind the stomach; supracolic and infracolic compartments\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/D041-152c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Lesser sac behind the stomach; supracolic and infracolic compartments\"></div></div><figcaption>Lesser sac behind the stomach; supracolic and infracolic compartments <small>· 2025 TA review, slide 152</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Walls of the lesser sac</h4>\n<p>Picture the sac as a flat pocket behind the stomach. Its front wall is the lesser omentum, the back of the stomach and, below the stomach, the <b>gastrocolic ligament</b>, the broadest part of the front wall. Its back wall is the stomach bed (5.1): the <b>pancreas</b>, the left kidney and suprarenal gland, the splenic artery and the diaphragm. On the left it ends at the spleen, with the gastrosplenic and splenorenal ligaments. On the right it opens through the epiploic foramen.</p>\n<h4>Recesses of the lesser sac</h4>\n<p>The sac has three recesses: superior, inferior and splenic. It has no medial or lateral recesses. The <b>superior recess</b> lies behind the stomach, the lesser omentum and the left lobe of the liver. The <b>inferior recess</b> lies behind the stomach and reaches down between the layers of the greater omentum. In the adult those layers fuse, so the inferior recess reaches only as far as the transverse colon. The <b>splenic recess</b> extends to the left, to the hilum of the spleen.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G074-10.jpg\" alt=\"Sagittal sections: walls and recesses of the omental bursa, infant and adult\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G074-10c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Sagittal sections: walls and recesses of the omental bursa, infant and adult\"></div></div><figcaption>Sagittal sections: walls and recesses of the omental bursa, infant and adult <small>· 2026 Harlem clickers: Peritoneum, Esophagus &amp; Stomach, slide 10</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Fluid in the omental bursa</h4>\n<p>The bursa sits directly behind the stomach and in front of the pancreas, so it is the first place to receive fluid from either organ. A perforated ulcer of the <b>posterior wall of the stomach</b> spills gastric contents straight into the bursa. An inflamed or injured pancreas, for example after blunt trauma, leaks pancreatic fluid forward into it. Walled off, this fluid forms a <b>pancreatic pseudocyst</b>.</p>\n<p>Fluid can leave the bursa only through the epiploic foramen, into the hepatorenal recess of the greater sac (3.3). So fluid in the lesser sac reaches the greater sac only through this foramen. The paracolic gutters and the pelvic pouches, such as the vesicouterine pouch, are channels and pockets of the greater sac itself. The posterior fornix of the vagina lies outside the peritoneal cavity, behind the cervix. The supracolic and infracolic compartments, the subhepatic space and the subphrenic space are all parts of the greater sac. They fill only later, if at all.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G004-43.jpg\" alt=\"Omental bursa opened from the front, with the stomach turned up\"></div><figcaption>Omental bursa opened from the front, with the stomach turned up <small>· 2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach &amp; Celiac, slide 43</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Getting into the lesser sac</h4>\n<p>A surgeon who must reach a bleeding vessel in the lesser sac usually cuts the <b>gastrocolic ligament</b>. It forms the broad front of the sac, so dividing it opens the sac widely and safely. The gastrosplenic ligament also borders the sac, but it is narrow and carries the short gastric vessels. The splenorenal ligament carries the splenic vessels and the pancreatic tail. The coronary ligament ties the liver to the diaphragm and is not a wall of the sac. The hepatoduodenal ligament holds the portal triad and is never cut.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Greater sac: whole abdomen, diaphragm → pelvic floor; supracolic, infracolic, gutters, recesses</li>\n<li>Lesser sac (omental bursa): behind liver, lesser omentum, stomach, upper greater omentum</li>\n<li>Only opening = epiploic foramen (right; hepatoduodenal lig. in front, IVC behind); not sealed; no portal vein inside</li>\n<li>Front: lesser omentum, back of stomach, gastrocolic lig.; back: pancreas, left kidney, splenic a.</li>\n<li>Left wall: spleen with gastrosplenic + splenorenal ligaments</li>\n<li>Recesses: superior, inferior (to transverse colon in adult), splenic; no medial/lateral</li>\n<li>Posterior gastric ulcer or pancreatic leak → bursa first; pancreatic pseudocyst</li>\n<li>Bursa drains only via the foramen → hepatorenal recess (not gutters, pelvic pouches, vaginal fornix)</li>\n<li>Surgical entry: cut the gastrocolic ligament</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The two sacs and their recesses","sn":3,"pn":"3.1"},{"l":"ANAT-G3","h":"The epiploic foramen","p":"<h4>The doorway into the lesser sac</h4>\n<p>The <b>epiploic foramen</b>, also called the omental foramen or the foramen of Winslow, is the natural opening between the greater sac and the lesser sac. It is about one finger wide. It lies on the right side of the lesser sac, just behind the free edge of the lesser omentum.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G004-45.jpg\" alt=\"Finger passed through the omental foramen into the omental bursa\"></div><figcaption>Finger passed through the omental foramen into the omental bursa <small>· 2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach &amp; Celiac, slide 45</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Its four borders</h4>\n<p>Imagine sliding your index finger into the foramen from the right, just behind the hepatoduodenal ligament. Each surface the finger touches is one border.</p>\n<p>Above the finger is the roof, the <b>superior</b> border: peritoneum on the <b>caudate lobe</b> of the liver. Below the finger is the floor, the <b>inferior</b> border: peritoneum on the <b>first (superior) part of the duodenum</b>. In front of the finger is the <b>anterior</b> border: the free edge of the lesser omentum, the <b>hepatoduodenal ligament</b>, holding the bile duct, proper hepatic artery and portal vein (2.4). Behind the finger is the <b>posterior</b> border: peritoneum covering the <b>inferior vena cava (IVC)</b>.</p>\n<p>These four structures bound the foramen, the doorway into the lesser sac. They are sometimes loosely called borders of the lesser sac; the walls of the whole sac are larger (3.1).</p>\n<h4>Neighbours that are not borders</h4>\n<p>The <b>aorta</b> lies to the left of the IVC, behind the lesser sac, so it does not border the foramen. The <b>quadrate lobe</b> of the liver lies in front of the porta hepatis, beside the gallbladder; the roof is the caudate lobe. The portal vein and bile duct sit inside the anterior border, in front of the finger. The gastroduodenal artery runs behind the first part of the duodenum, below the foramen.</p>\n<p>The common hepatic artery and the celiac trunk lie lower, along the top of the pancreas and behind the lesser sac. The head of the pancreas lies below, inside the C-shaped curve of the duodenum. The second and third parts of the duodenum and the transverse colon lie lower down. So do the superior mesenteric vein and artery (SMV and SMA), behind the neck of the pancreas, and the renal arteries at about the first lumbar vertebra. The right renal artery passes behind the IVC.</p>\n<h4>A blade behind the ligament</h4>\n<p>This anatomy matters in gallbladder and bile duct surgery, where the surgeon works right beside the hepatoduodenal ligament. A probe, scissors or knife passed just behind the ligament, through the foramen, meets the posterior wall. That wall is thin peritoneum over the IVC. A tear there floods the field with venous blood at once.</p>\n<h4>Intestine trapped in the foramen</h4>\n<p>Rarely, a loop of small intestine slips through the foramen into the omental bursa and is strangulated by the edges of the opening. The surgeon cannot widen the foramen by cutting, because every border carries or covers large blood vessels: the triad in front, the IVC behind, the duodenum below and the liver above. Instead the swollen loop is emptied with a needle and pushed back into the greater sac through the foramen.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Epiploic (omental, Winslow) foramen: greater sac ↔ lesser sac; one finger wide</li>\n<li>Superior: peritoneum on caudate lobe (not quadrate)</li>\n<li>Inferior: peritoneum on first part of duodenum (not 2nd/3rd, not colon)</li>\n<li>Anterior: hepatoduodenal lig. (free edge, portal triad)</li>\n<li>Posterior: peritoneum over IVC → blade behind the foramen tears the IVC</li>\n<li>Not borders: aorta (left), common hepatic a., celiac, pancreatic head, SMV/SMA, renal a.</li>\n<li>Strangulated loop: borders can't be cut → needle decompression, push back</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The two sacs and their recesses","sn":3,"pn":"3.2"},{"l":"ANAT-G3","h":"Recesses above and below the liver","p":"<p>The greater sac has pockets, called recesses, where fluid and pus collect. Three matter most: the subphrenic recess above the liver, the hepatorenal recess below it, and the paracolic gutters beside the colon.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G004-36.jpg\" alt=\"Subphrenic and subhepatic spaces in a sagittal section\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G004-36c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Subphrenic and subhepatic spaces in a sagittal section\"></div></div><figcaption>Subphrenic and subhepatic spaces in a sagittal section <small>· 2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach &amp; Celiac, slide 36</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Subphrenic recess</h4>\n<p>The <b>subphrenic</b> (suprahepatic) recess is a peritoneal pocket between the <b>diaphragm</b> and the front and top of the <b>liver</b>. The <b>falciform ligament</b> runs through it and divides it into right and left subphrenic recesses. The coronary ligaments form its back edge, where the bare area of the liver meets the diaphragm, but they do not divide it. Pelvic ligaments, such as the round ligament of the uterus and the suspensory ligament of the ovary, lie far away.</p>\n<p>When the body is upright, the subphrenic recess is the highest pocket of the greater sac. When the body is upside down, it becomes the lowest, so blood or fluid in the greater sac runs there. A <b>subphrenic abscess</b> is pus between the diaphragm and the liver or spleen. It follows abdominal operations such as removal of the spleen, or a bowel perforation. About half are on the right, a quarter on the left and a quarter on both sides.</p>\n<h4>Hepatorenal recess (Morison pouch)</h4>\n<p>The <b>hepatorenal recess</b>, also called the <b>Morison pouch</b> or the right subhepatic recess, is a deep peritoneal pocket <b>below</b> the liver. The liver lies in front of it. The right kidney and suprarenal gland lie behind it. It is part of the greater sac, inside the peritoneal cavity. So it is not extraperitoneal, not a space inside the hepatorenal ligament, and not part of the omental bursa.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G074-19.jpg\" alt=\"Perihepatic recesses: subphrenic recess above the liver, hepatorenal recess against the kidney\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G074-19c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Perihepatic recesses: subphrenic recess above the liver, hepatorenal recess against the kidney\"></div></div><figcaption>Perihepatic recesses: subphrenic recess above the liver, hepatorenal recess against the kidney <small>· 2026 Harlem clickers: Peritoneum, Esophagus &amp; Stomach, slide 19</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>It has two key connections. It communicates with the lesser sac through the epiploic foramen, and with the pelvis through the right paracolic gutter. In a patient lying <b>supine</b> (flat on the back) it is the <b>lowest part of the peritoneal cavity</b>, so fluid collects there (3.4).</p>\n<h4>Above versus below the liver</h4>\n<p>Subphrenic means above the liver; subhepatic means below it. The right subhepatic space is the hepatorenal recess. The left subhepatic space is the lesser sac. The superior recess of the lesser sac lies behind the stomach and the lesser omentum, not between the liver and the diaphragm. The costodiaphragmatic recess, between the lung base and the diaphragm, belongs to the pleura. The <b>paracolic gutters</b> lie lateral to the ascending colon (right gutter) and the descending colon (left gutter). They are channels along which fluid flows.</p>\n<h4>Path of a posterior gastric perforation</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>An ulcer in the back wall of the stomach perforates. The back wall faces the lesser sac, so its contents enter the <b>omental bursa</b> first (3.1).</li>\n<li>The bursa's only exit is the epiploic foramen, on its right side.</li>\n<li>Just beyond the foramen lies the <b>hepatorenal recess</b>. In a supine or bedridden patient it is the lowest point, so the fluid collects and peritonitis spreads there.</li>\n<li>From there the fluid can run down the right paracolic gutter to the pelvis.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The fluid does not settle in the right subphrenic space, which lies higher than the Morison pouch in a supine patient. It does not head left, because the bursa empties to the right through the foramen.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Subphrenic (suprahepatic): diaphragm ↔ front and top of liver; falciform → R and L</li>\n<li>Coronary ligaments = back edge of subphrenic recess; they don't divide it</li>\n<li>Upside down → subphrenic recess lowest; abscess: ½ right, ¼ left, ¼ both</li>\n<li>Hepatorenal (Morison, right subhepatic): liver in front; right kidney + suprarenal behind</li>\n<li>Greater sac, intraperitoneal; not bursa, not in a ligament, not subphrenic</li>\n<li>Links: lesser sac (epiploic foramen) + pelvis (right paracolic gutter)</li>\n<li>Lowest point when supine</li>\n<li>Posterior gastric ulcer → bursa → foramen → hepatorenal recess → right gutter → pelvis</li>\n<li>Left subhepatic = lesser sac; costodiaphragmatic recess = pleura</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The two sacs and their recesses","sn":3,"pn":"3.3"},{"l":"ANAT-G3","h":"Where fluid collects, by body position","p":"<p>Fluid that escapes into the peritoneal cavity, whether blood, pus, leaked gut contents or ascites, does not stay where it started. It spreads along the recesses and gutters and pools in the <b>lowest, gravity-dependent</b> point for the body's position. To predict where it lies, first ask how the patient is positioned. The recesses and pouches are ordinary parts of the cavity. Their lining makes no extra fluid and absorbs like the rest, so gravity alone decides where fluid settles.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G004-63.jpg\" alt=\"Fluid pools in the hepatorenal pouch lying down, the pelvic pouch upright\"></div><figcaption>Fluid pools in the hepatorenal pouch lying down, the pelvic pouch upright <small>· 2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach &amp; Celiac, slide 63</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Lying supine</h4>\n<p>When the patient lies flat on the back (supine), the lowest point of the peritoneal cavity is the <b>hepatorenal recess</b> (Morison pouch), between the right lobe of the liver and the right kidney. This recess dips deep against the posterior wall. A patient on a stretcher, on the operating table or in bed is supine. Blood from trauma, ascites from cirrhosis, and contents leaking from a perforated duodenal ulcer therefore collect here. The leaking duodenum sits right beside it. Ultrasound of a supine patient finds free fluid here first.</p>\n<h4>Sitting or standing</h4>\n<p>When the patient sits or stands, fluid sinks into the pelvis. In a woman, the uterus splits the pelvic peritoneum into two pouches. The <b>vesicouterine pouch</b>, between the bladder and the uterus, is shallow. The <b>rectouterine pouch</b> (pouch of Douglas), between the rectum and the uterus, is the deepest point, so fluid gathers there. In a man there is no uterus, so there is one pouch, the <b>rectovesical pouch</b>, between the rectum and the bladder. The rectouterine pouch exists only in women.</p>\n<h4>Upside down</h4>\n<p>If the body is inverted, the diaphragm becomes the floor. Fluid in the greater sac then runs to the <b>subphrenic recess</b>, between the diaphragm and the liver (3.3).</p>\n<h4>Places fluid passes but does not settle</h4>\n<p>The <b>paracolic gutters</b> are channels. The right gutter links the hepatorenal recess to the pelvis, so fluid moves along it when the patient changes position. It is not the lowest point in any position. The subphrenic recess lies higher than the hepatorenal recess in a supine patient. The <b>lesser sac</b> and its splenic recess are closed except at the epiploic foramen. They fill only from organs that face them, such as the back of the stomach or the pancreas (3.1).</p>\n<h4>Clinical conditions</h4>\n<p><b>Peritonitis</b> is bacterial contamination of the peritoneum, from trauma, infection, appendicitis or a perforated ulcer. Generalized peritonitis is dangerous. <b>Ascites</b> is excess fluid in the peritoneal cavity. Its causes include portal hypertension (for example from alcoholic cirrhosis), spread of cancer and starvation. The fluid hinders movement of the organs. <b>Paracentesis</b> is drawing fluid from the peritoneal cavity with a needle, to drain it or to test it.</p>\n<p>When peritoneal surfaces are inflamed, they can heal stuck together as <b>adhesions</b>, which cause chronic pain. Cutting them is adhesiotomy. Finally, the peritoneum is a semipermeable membrane over blood and lymph capillary beds, so fluid put into the cavity is absorbed fast. This is used for intraperitoneal chemotherapy, especially for ovarian cancer, and for peritoneal dialysis.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Fluid pools at the lowest gravity-dependent point for the position</li>\n<li>Supine → hepatorenal recess (Morison): right liver lobe ↔ right kidney</li>\n<li>Upright ♀ → rectouterine pouch (Douglas); vesicouterine pouch is shallow</li>\n<li>Upright ♂ → rectovesical pouch</li>\n<li>Upside down → subphrenic recess</li>\n<li>Gutters = channels only; lesser sac fills only from stomach or pancreas</li>\n<li>Peritonitis: bacterial; trauma, appendicitis, perforated ulcer</li>\n<li>Ascites: portal hypertension (cirrhosis), cancer, starvation; paracentesis = needle drainage</li>\n<li>Adhesions → chronic pain (adhesiotomy); fast absorption → chemo (ovarian), dialysis</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The two sacs and their recesses","sn":3,"pn":"3.4"},{"l":"ANAT-G3","h":"Course, relations and blood supply","p":"<p>The <b>esophagus</b> is a muscular tube that carries food from the pharynx to the stomach. It begins at the lower border of the <b>cricoid cartilage</b>, opposite the sixth cervical vertebra (C6). It descends in front of the vertebral column, through the superior and posterior mediastina, then passes through the diaphragm into the abdomen.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G004-71.jpg\" alt=\"Esophagus, left lateral view: cervical, broncho-aortic and diaphragmatic constrictions\"></div><figcaption>Esophagus, left lateral view: cervical, broncho-aortic and diaphragmatic constrictions <small>· 2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach &amp; Celiac, slide 71</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>In the neck</h4>\n<p>The cervical esophagus has the <b>trachea</b> in front and the vertebral column and longus colli muscles behind. At its sides lie the common carotid arteries and the lobes of the thyroid gland. The thoracic duct lies on its left. The <b>recurrent laryngeal nerves</b>, branches of the vagus, climb in the groove <b>between the esophagus and the trachea</b>. Each nerve therefore lies in front of the esophagus and behind the trachea.</p>\n<p>That position puts the nerves at risk. A sharp swallowed object that pierces the cervical esophagus can injure a recurrent laryngeal nerve. The nerve supplies all the intrinsic muscles of the larynx except the cricothyroid, so the patient becomes <b>hoarse</b>, with a weak voice. It runs right behind the thyroid lobes, so dissection in this groove during thyroid surgery can cut it too. Other nerves nearby do other jobs. The superior laryngeal nerve is higher: its external branch supplies only the cricothyroid, and its internal branch carries sensation from the larynx above the vocal folds. The <b>hypoglossal nerve</b> moves the tongue. The <b>phrenic nerve</b> runs far lateral, down the anterior scalene muscle to the diaphragm; its injury affects breathing. The <b>glossopharyngeal nerve</b> serves the pharynx and taste on the back of the tongue. The <b>long thoracic nerve</b> supplies serratus anterior; its injury gives a winged scapula.</p>\n<p>A mass growing in the cervical esophagus can press on the recurrent laryngeal nerve in the same groove and make the patient hoarse. Look-alike causes do not fit. The esophageal nerve plexus lies lower, on the thoracic esophagus, and does not serve the larynx. Damage to the cervical sympathetic chain gives Horner syndrome (drooping eyelid, small pupil). The ansa cervicalis supplies the infrahyoid (strap) muscles.</p>\n<h4>In the thorax</h4>\n<p>The thoracic esophagus starts in the superior mediastinum, between the trachea and the vertebral column, a little left of the midline. It passes behind and to the right of the aortic arch. It then descends in the posterior mediastinum along the <b>right side of the descending aorta</b>. Near the diaphragm it moves in front of and slightly left of the aorta. In front of it lie the trachea, aortic arch, right pulmonary artery, left main bronchus, pericardium and diaphragm. Behind it lie the vertebral column, longus colli muscles, right intercostal arteries, thoracic duct and hemiazygos veins.</p>\n<h4>Three narrow points</h4>\n<p>The esophagus is narrowed in three places. The <b>cervical constriction</b> is at its start, made by the <b>cricopharyngeus</b> muscle, the upper esophageal sphincter. The <b>thoracic (broncho-aortic) constriction</b> is where the aortic arch crosses it, and then the left main bronchus. The <b>diaphragmatic constriction</b> is where it passes through the esophageal hiatus (4.2).</p>\n<h4>Blood supply by thirds</h4>\n<p>Each third of the esophagus takes its arteries from whatever lies next to it. The <b>upper (cervical) third</b> gets the <b>inferior thyroid artery</b>, a branch of the thyrocervical trunk. Its veins drain to the inferior thyroid veins and its lymph to the deep cervical nodes. The <b>middle (thoracic) third</b> gets esophageal branches straight off the <b>descending thoracic aorta</b>. Its veins drain to the azygos veins and its lymph to the mediastinal nodes.</p>\n<p>The <b>lower third</b>, including the short abdominal part, gets esophageal branches of the <b>left gastric artery</b>, which climb up through the esophageal hiatus. The left inferior phrenic artery also contributes. Its veins drain to the left gastric vein, and its lymph runs along the left gastric vessels to the celiac nodes. The splenic, right gastric, right gastro-omental, gastroduodenal and superior mesenteric arteries send no branches to the esophagus. The superior thyroid artery supplies the thyroid gland and the larynx, not the esophagus.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G074-38.jpg\" alt=\"Arteries of the esophagus: inferior thyroid, aortic esophageal and left gastric branches\"></div><figcaption>Arteries of the esophagus: inferior thyroid, aortic esophageal and left gastric branches <small>· 2026 Harlem clickers: Peritoneum, Esophagus &amp; Stomach, slide 38</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Nerves</h4>\n<p>Parasympathetic fibers reach the esophagus through the vagus nerves, and sympathetic fibers through the sympathetic trunks. Low in the thorax, the two vagi form an esophageal nerve plexus around it (7.2).</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Esophagus: cricoid (C6) → stomach; superior + posterior mediastina → diaphragm</li>\n<li>Neck: trachea in front, spine + longus colli behind, thoracic duct on left</li>\n<li>Recurrent laryngeal n.: groove between esophagus and trachea; all intrinsic laryngeal muscles but cricothyroid → injury (thyroid surgery) = hoarseness</li>\n<li>Cervical esophageal mass presses the nerve → hoarseness (sympathetic chain → Horner; ansa → strap muscles)</li>\n<li>Not it: phrenic (diaphragm), long thoracic (winged scapula), superior laryngeal (cricothyroid; sensation), hypoglossal (tongue)</li>\n<li>Thorax: behind/right of arch, right of descending aorta, then in front of it</li>\n<li>Constrictions: cricopharyngeus, aortic arch + left main bronchus, hiatus</li>\n<li>Upper ⅓: inferior thyroid a.; middle ⅓: thoracic aorta branches</li>\n<li>Lower ⅓ + abdominal part: left gastric a. (+ left inferior phrenic)</li>\n<li>Veins: inferior thyroid → azygos → left gastric</li>\n<li>No esophageal branches: splenic, right gastric, right gastro-omental, gastroduodenal, superior mesenteric, superior thyroid</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The esophagus","sn":4,"pn":"4.1"},{"l":"ANAT-G3","h":"The esophagogastric junction and reflux","p":"<h4>Through the diaphragm</h4>\n<p>The esophagus leaves the thorax through the <b>esophageal hiatus</b>, an opening in the muscular <b>right crus</b> of the diaphragm. The hiatus lies at the level of the tenth thoracic vertebra (T10). Two other large openings bracket it: the caval opening for the inferior vena cava at T8, and the aortic hiatus at T12. The fibers of the right crus cross each other just below the hiatus, so they form a sling around the esophagus. The hiatus also passes the two vagal trunks and the esophageal branches of the left gastric vessels.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/D042-116.jpg\" alt=\"Diaphragm from below; the right crus forms the esophageal hiatus\"></div><figcaption>Diaphragm from below; the right crus forms the esophageal hiatus <small>· 2025 TA review, slide 116</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The abdominal esophagus</h4>\n<p>Below the diaphragm, the abdominal esophagus is only about 1.25 cm long. It lies in a groove on the back of the left lobe of the liver. Peritoneum covers only its front and left sides. It is cone-shaped, and its base sits on the upper opening of the stomach (the cardiac orifice). This lowest, conical end of the esophagus is the <b>antrum cardiacum</b>.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G004-74.jpg\" alt=\"Abdominal esophagus emerging through the right crus of the diaphragm\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G004-74c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Abdominal esophagus emerging through the right crus of the diaphragm\"></div></div><figcaption>Abdominal esophagus emerging through the right crus of the diaphragm <small>· 2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach &amp; Celiac, slide 74</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The junction and the Z-line</h4>\n<p>The <b>esophagogastric junction</b> lies to the left of the T11 vertebra, on the horizontal plane through the tip of the xiphoid process. It sits below the hiatus, not at it. At the junction the lining changes abruptly from esophageal <b>stratified squamous</b> epithelium to gastric <b>columnar</b> epithelium. The jagged line where they meet is the <b>Z-line</b>. It marks esophagus versus stomach, not upper versus lower esophagus. It is a feature of the lining: diaphragm fibers wrap the hiatus and do not attach to it, and taste receptors are on the tongue.</p>\n<p>The squamous lining just above the Z-line has little defence against acid. When acid washes up, this is the mucosa it burns. Reflux esophagitis, ulcers and Barrett change therefore form here, and this is the region of the esophagus most prone to ulcers.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G004-121.jpg\" alt=\"Cross-section at T11: esophagogastric junction left of the vertebra, beside the aorta\"></div><figcaption>Cross-section at T11: esophagogastric junction left of the vertebra, beside the aorta <small>· 2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach &amp; Celiac, slide 121</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The lower esophageal sphincter</h4>\n<p>The <b>lower esophageal sphincter (LES)</b>, in older books the cardiac sphincter, is a <b>physiological</b> sphincter, not an anatomical one. Unlike the pyloric sphincter, it has no thick ring of smooth muscle. The pyloric, ileocecal and internal urethral sphincters are all true anatomical rings of smooth muscle. The pyloric one guards the outlet of the stomach, and the ileocecal one the junction of ileum and cecum. Instead, just above the junction, the diaphragm muscle that forms the hiatus, the <b>right crus</b>, clamps the esophagus near T11. It contracts and relaxes, and its main job is to stop stomach contents from flowing back up. It works involuntarily.</p>\n<p>Several structures are not part of it. The left crus does not form it and does not start peristalsis. The median arcuate ligament arches over the aorta and has nothing to do with it. The opening for the inferior vena cava, at T8, is much higher. The <b>upper esophageal sphincter (UES)</b> is a different structure: the cricopharyngeus muscle in the neck, which guards against aspiration and lies far from stomach acid.</p>\n<h4>Reflux and heartburn</h4>\n<p><b>Pyrosis</b> (heartburn) is the most common esophageal discomfort. It comes from small amounts of food or gastric fluid flowing back into the <b>lower esophagus</b>. Causes include certain foods, gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD), hiatal hernia and certain drugs. The acid injures the lining (mucosa) first. The first segment it reaches is the <b>antrum cardiacum</b>, the conical lowest end of the esophagus. The wall here is smooth muscle; the nearby skeletal muscle is the right crus. The pyloric sphincter lies downstream of the acid, and the cricopharyngeus lies far above it.</p>\n<h4>Hiatal hernia</h4>\n<p>A <b>hiatal hernia</b> is part of the stomach pushing up through the esophageal hiatus into the mediastinum. It is more common in middle age and results from weak muscles. In a <b>sliding</b> hiatal hernia, the clamping action of the right crus is weak. The abdominal esophagus, the cardia and part of the fundus slide up into the thorax, especially when the person lies down or bends over. In a <b>paraesophageal</b> hiatal hernia, the cardia stays in its normal place, and part of the fundus rises beside the esophagus.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Openings: caval T8; esophageal hiatus (right crus) T10; aortic T12</li>\n<li>Abdominal esophagus ~1.25 cm, behind left liver lobe; front + left covered</li>\n<li>Antrum cardiacum = conical lowest end on cardiac orifice → first hit by GERD</li>\n<li>Esophagogastric junction: left of T11, xiphoid plane, below the hiatus</li>\n<li>Z-line: squamous → columnar; esophagus vs stomach; most ulcer-prone</li>\n<li>LES (old name cardiac sphincter): physiological, involuntary; right crus clamps; stops reflux</li>\n<li>Not LES: left crus, median arcuate lig.; T8 = caval opening</li>\n<li>UES = cricopharyngeus (neck); pyloric, ileocecal, internal urethral = true muscle rings</li>\n<li>Sliding hernia: weak crus, cardia slides up; paraesophageal: cardia stays, fundus rises</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The esophagus","sn":4,"pn":"4.2"},{"l":"ANAT-G3","h":"Level and branch map","p":"<p>The <b>celiac trunk</b> is the artery of the foregut. It supplies the abdominal esophagus, stomach, proximal duodenum, liver and biliary tract, spleen and pancreas.</p>\n<h4>Where it arises</h4>\n<p>The celiac trunk leaves the front of the abdominal aorta at the twelfth thoracic vertebra (<b>T12</b>), just after the aorta passes through the aortic hiatus of the diaphragm. It is very short and splits almost at once into <b>three branches</b>: the left gastric, splenic and common hepatic arteries.</p>\n<p>Several levels in this region are easy to mix up. The esophageal hiatus lies at T10. The esophagogastric junction lies at T11. The celiac trunk arises at T12. The <b>superior mesenteric artery (SMA)</b>, the artery of the midgut, arises just below it at the first lumbar vertebra (L1). The kidneys get their own renal arteries from the aorta.</p>\n<h4>The three branches and what they give</h4>\n<p>The <b>left gastric artery</b> is the smallest branch. It rises to the cardia, sends esophageal branches up through the hiatus, then runs along the lesser curvature. It is a direct branch of the celiac trunk, not of the SMA.</p>\n<p>The <b>splenic artery</b> is the largest branch. It runs to the left in a wavy course along the upper border of the pancreas to the spleen. On the way it gives pancreatic branches to the body and tail of the pancreas, a posterior gastric artery (not always present), the short gastric arteries and the left gastro-omental artery (6.3).</p>\n<p>The <b>common hepatic artery</b> runs to the right along the upper border of the pancreas. It gives the <b>gastroduodenal artery</b>, whose branches are the supraduodenal, the superior pancreaticoduodenal and the right gastro-omental arteries (6.4). What remains continues as the <b>proper hepatic artery</b>. It usually gives off the right gastric artery, then splits into right and left hepatic arteries. The right hepatic gives the <b>cystic artery</b> to the gallbladder. Every one of these is a celiac branch or a sub-branch of one.</p>\n<p>Gastro-omental and gastroepiploic are two names for the same arteries.</p>\n<h4>Arteries that are not from the celiac trunk</h4>\n<p>The <b>suprarenal arteries</b> come from the inferior phrenic arteries, the aorta and the renal arteries, not from the celiac trunk. The renal arteries come straight from the aorta. The <b>inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery</b> comes from the SMA.</p>\n<h4>Reading an angiogram</h4>\n<p>On a celiac angiogram the catheter climbs the aorta from below, and its tip sits in the short, wide <b>celiac trunk</b>, where the branches fan out. The image is viewed as if facing the patient, so the patient's right is on the image's left. The <b>splenic artery</b> is the long, wavy vessel running to the patient's left, toward the spleen. Small <b>pancreatic branches</b> drop from it into the body and tail of the pancreas. The <b>common hepatic artery</b> runs to the patient's right. It gives the <b>gastroduodenal artery</b>, which drops straight down behind the duodenum, and continues as the <b>proper hepatic artery</b>, which climbs to the liver. In the liver this splits into the <b>left hepatic artery</b>, the upper branch climbing toward the midline, and the <b>right hepatic artery</b>, which runs out laterally into the right lobe. The <b>left gastric artery</b> is the thin branch climbing straight up toward the cardia.</p>\n<p>Lower down, the gastroduodenal artery gives the <b>right gastro-omental artery</b>, which curves along the greater curvature on the patient's right. It also gives the <b>superior pancreaticoduodenal artery</b>, which runs down along the duodenal loop and the head of the pancreas. The superior mesenteric artery and its inferior pancreaticoduodenal branch stay dark, because the dye goes only into the celiac trunk.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G004-112.jpg\" alt=\"Celiac angiogram: hepatic, gastroduodenal, left gastric and the wavy splenic artery\"></div><figcaption>Celiac angiogram: hepatic, gastroduodenal, left gastric and the wavy splenic artery <small>· 2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach &amp; Celiac, slide 112</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Celiac trunk = foregut artery: abdominal esophagus to proximal duodenum, liver, spleen, pancreas</li>\n<li>Leaves aorta at T12 (aortic hiatus); SMA at L1; junction T11; hiatus T10</li>\n<li>Left gastric (smallest, direct celiac): esophageal branches, lesser curvature</li>\n<li>Splenic (largest, wavy, left): pancreatic, posterior gastric, short gastrics, left gastro-omental</li>\n<li>Common hepatic (right): gastroduodenal, proper hepatic → R/L hepatic → cystic; right gastric usually off proper hepatic</li>\n<li>Gastroduodenal → supraduodenal, superior pancreaticoduodenal, right gastro-omental</li>\n<li>Not celiac: suprarenal (inf. phrenic, aorta, renal), renal, inferior pancreaticoduodenal (SMA)</li>\n<li>Angiogram: catheter tip in trunk; long wavy vessel to patient's left = splenic a. (pancreatic branches drop down)</li>\n<li>Angiogram, patient's right: common hepatic → gastroduodenal (down) + proper hepatic → left hepatic (upper), right hepatic (lateral); left gastric climbs straight up</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The celiac trunk and its branches","sn":6,"pn":"6.1"},{"l":"ANAT-G3","h":"Two arcades along the curvatures","p":"<p>Two arterial arcades ring the stomach, one along each curvature. In each arcade an artery coming from the left meets an artery coming from the right. An end-to-end joining of two arteries like this is an <b>anastomosis</b>. Its value is backup: if one partner is cut or blocked, blood still reaches the territory from the other side.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G004-110.jpg\" alt=\"Celiac trunk and the arteries of the stomach, anterior view\"></div><figcaption>Celiac trunk and the arteries of the stomach, anterior view <small>· 2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach &amp; Celiac, slide 110</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The lesser curvature arcade</h4>\n<p>Along the lesser curvature run the two <b>gastric</b> arteries, in the lesser omentum close to the stomach. The <b>left gastric artery</b> is a direct branch of the celiac trunk. Besides the lesser curvature, it feeds the cardia and the lower esophagus. The <b>right gastric artery</b> usually arises from the proper hepatic artery, the continuation of the common hepatic, which is itself a celiac branch. It feeds the right part of the lesser curvature and the pylorus.</p>\n<p>The two meet along the curvature, so each can rescue the other. If the right gastric artery, on the right part of the lesser curvature, is cut, the left gastric keeps the curvature supplied. If the left gastric is cut, the right gastric does the same. Other arteries cannot take over this job. The esophageal arteries serve the esophagus. The gastroduodenal artery runs behind the duodenum, and its right gastro-omental branch serves the greater curvature.</p>\n<h4>The greater curvature arcade</h4>\n<p>Along the greater curvature run the two <b>gastro-omental</b> (gastroepiploic) arteries, in the greater omentum close to the stomach. The <b>left gastro-omental artery</b> comes from the splenic artery near the splenic hilum and feeds the left part of the greater curvature. The <b>right gastro-omental artery</b> comes from the gastroduodenal artery and feeds the right part. They meet along the curvature. An ulcer that burns through the greater curvature therefore hits a gastro-omental artery.</p>\n<h4>Keeping the names straight</h4>\n<p>The rule is short. <b>Gastric</b> arteries run only on the lesser curvature, and <b>gastro-omental</b> arteries run only on the greater curvature. The other stomach arteries are separate. The short gastric arteries supply the fundus, and the posterior gastric artery supplies the back wall; both come from the splenic artery (6.3). The splenic artery itself runs behind the stomach, the gastroduodenal artery behind the duodenum, and the proper hepatic artery goes up to the liver.</p>\n<h4>Veins of the lesser curvature</h4>\n<p>The left and right gastric veins run with their arteries along the lesser curvature. They drain directly into the <b>hepatic portal vein</b> (7.1). The splenic vein receives the short gastric and left gastro-omental veins instead, and the right gastro-omental vein goes to the superior mesenteric vein. A bleeding stomach ulcer makes the patient vomit blood (hematemesis); coughing up blood (hemoptysis) comes from the airways.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Anastomosis: left + right arteries meet → either side keeps flow</li>\n<li>Lesser curvature: left gastric (celiac) + right gastric (usually proper hepatic a.), in lesser omentum</li>\n<li>Left gastric also feeds cardia + lower esophagus; right gastric feeds pylorus</li>\n<li>Cut right gastric → left gastric supplies; cut left gastric → right gastric supplies</li>\n<li>Greater curvature: left gastro-omental (splenic) + right gastro-omental (gastroduodenal), in greater omentum</li>\n<li>Gastric = lesser; gastro-omental (= gastroepiploic) = greater; greater-curvature ulcer → gastro-omental</li>\n<li>Short gastrics → fundus; posterior gastric → back wall (both splenic)</li>\n<li>Gastric veins → hepatic portal vein directly</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The celiac trunk and its branches","sn":6,"pn":"6.2"},{"l":"ANAT-G3","h":"The splenic artery","p":"<h4>Course</h4>\n<p>The <b>splenic artery</b> is the largest branch of the celiac trunk. It runs to the left in a wavy (tortuous) course along the <b>upper border of the pancreas</b>. Here it lies behind the stomach, in the stomach bed (5.1). Near the spleen it passes through the splenorenal ligament to the hilum of the spleen. Because it runs along the pancreas, pancreatic surgery puts it and its branches at risk.</p>\n<h4>Branches</h4>\n<p>On its way the splenic artery gives five kinds of branch. <b>Pancreatic branches</b> supply the body and tail of the pancreas. The <b>posterior gastric artery</b>, which is not always present, climbs the back wall of the lesser sac to the back wall and fundus of the stomach. Near the spleen, four or five <b>short gastric arteries</b> leave the end of the splenic artery and run through the gastrosplenic ligament to the <b>fundus</b>. The <b>left gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery</b> leaves the splenic artery near the hilum, passes through the gastrosplenic ligament and then runs along the greater curvature, supplying its left part (6.2). Finally, splenic branches enter the spleen at the hilum.</p>\n<p>The short gastrics are direct branches of the splenic artery; they do not come from the left gastro-omental. No artery called the long gastric exists. If the splenic artery is cut, flow stops in all of its branches, including the left gastro-omental. The common hepatic artery is unaffected. It is a sister branch of the splenic artery, straight from the celiac trunk, and the proper hepatic, right hepatic and cystic arteries all descend from it (6.1).</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/D042-124.jpg\" alt=\"Splenic artery giving short gastric and left gastroepiploic arteries; gastric veins\"></div><figcaption>Splenic artery giving short gastric and left gastroepiploic arteries; gastric veins <small>· 2025 TA review, slide 124</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Each stomach region and its arteries</h4>\n<p>Each region of the stomach has its own job and its own main artery. The <b>fundus</b>, the dome above the cardia, holds the swallowed-air bubble and relaxes to receive food. This reflex is called <b>receptive relaxation</b>. The short gastric arteries feed the fundus.</p>\n<p>The <b>cardia</b> receives food from the esophagus and is fed by the left gastric artery. The <b>body</b> does most of the mechanical and chemical digestion and is fed by the two curvature arcades. The <b>pyloric antrum</b> makes gastrin and is fed by the right gastric and right gastro-omental arteries. The <b>pylorus</b> controls emptying and is fed by right gastric and gastroduodenal branches. Outside the stomach, the lower esophagus belongs to the left gastric artery. The first part of the duodenum belongs to the gastroduodenal artery, and the head of the pancreas to the pancreaticoduodenal arteries.</p>\n<h4>Ulcers that erode arteries</h4>\n<p>Where an ulcer sits tells you which artery it erodes. An ulcer that penetrates the <b>fundus</b> erodes the short gastric arteries. An ulcer on the greater curvature erodes a gastro-omental artery, and one on the lesser curvature a gastric artery. The most dangerous is a <b>posterior gastric ulcer</b>:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>An ulcer in the <b>posterior wall of the body</b> of the stomach perforates.</li>\n<li>Acid leaks backward, into the lesser sac and onto the stomach bed.</li>\n<li>The large splenic artery runs there, along the top of the pancreas, directly behind the posterior wall.</li>\n<li>The acid erodes the splenic artery and causes severe bleeding.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Other arteries lie farther away. The gastroduodenal artery lies behind the first part of the duodenum and is eroded by posterior duodenal ulcers (6.4). The common hepatic artery runs right, toward the liver. The superior mesenteric artery leaves the aorta lower, behind the neck of the pancreas, and runs down to the midgut. The inferior phrenic artery supplies the underside of the diaphragm. The supraduodenal artery is a small branch on top of the duodenum, and the posterior (dorsal) pancreatic artery is a small branch behind the pancreas.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Splenic a.: largest celiac branch; wavy, left along upper pancreas, behind stomach</li>\n<li>Through splenorenal lig. to splenic hilum; exposed in pancreatic surgery</li>\n<li>Branches: pancreatic (body, tail), posterior gastric, short gastrics (4–5), left gastro-omental, splenic</li>\n<li>Short gastrics: via gastrosplenic lig. → fundus; fundic ulcer erodes them</li>\n<li>Fundus: air bubble + receptive relaxation; antrum: gastrin; pylorus: emptying; body: digestion</li>\n<li>Cut splenic a. → left gastro-omental lost; common hepatic line spared</li>\n<li>No long gastric artery</li>\n<li>Posterior wall of body ulcer → lesser sac → erodes splenic a.</li>\n<li>Posterior duodenal ulcer → gastroduodenal a. (6.4)</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The celiac trunk and its branches","sn":6,"pn":"6.3"},{"l":"ANAT-G3","h":"The duodenum and the gastroduodenal artery","p":"<p>The <b>duodenum</b> is the first part of the small intestine. It is a C-shaped tube that curves around the head of the pancreas, mostly to the right of the midline, in the right upper quadrant and epigastric region. Only its first 2 cm is intraperitoneal; the rest is retroperitoneal (1.2). Like the rest of the small intestine it has villi. Its jobs are to neutralize the acid chyme from the stomach, to continue chemical digestion and to absorb nutrients. It receives acid chyme through the pylorus, and bile and pancreatic juice at the major duodenal papilla (below). Mechanical mixing of chyme is the stomach's work. Bile is made in the liver, water is absorbed mainly in the colon, and feces are stored in the rectum.</p>\n<h4>Four parts and what lies behind them</h4>\n<p>The <b>first (superior) part</b>, D1, receives acid chyme through the pylorus. The liver and gallbladder lie in front of it. Behind it lie the <b>gastroduodenal artery (GDA)</b>, the bile duct, the portal vein and the inferior vena cava (IVC).</p>\n<p>The <b>second (descending) part</b>, D2, runs down beside the head of the pancreas. On its back-inner wall is the <b>major duodenal papilla</b>. There the bile duct and the main pancreatic duct, joined as the hepatopancreatic ampulla, open together, so bile and pancreatic enzymes enter here. The transverse colon crosses in front of this part. Behind it lie the right kidney and renal vessels, with the IVC along its medial side.</p>\n<p>The <b>third (inferior or horizontal) part</b>, D3, crosses from right to left. The superior mesenteric artery and vein (SMA and SMV) cross in front of it, and the IVC and aorta lie behind it. The <b>fourth (ascending) part</b> rises along the left side of the aorta to the duodenojejunal junction.</p>\n<p>So the IVC lies behind the first three parts, while the GDA lies behind the first part only. The head of the pancreas sits inside the C, medial to the second part and above the third.</p>\n<h4>The gastroduodenal artery</h4>\n<p>The GDA is a branch of the common hepatic artery (6.1). It descends <b>directly behind the first part of the duodenum</b> and gives three branches. The <b>supraduodenal artery</b> supplies the top of the first part. The <b>superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries</b>, anterior and posterior, run down between the duodenum and the head of the pancreas. The <b>right gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery</b> runs along the greater curvature of the stomach. The GDA and the superior pancreaticoduodenal artery are two different arteries; the second branches from the first.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G005-32.jpg\" alt=\"Arterial supply of the duodenum: gastroduodenal, supraduodenal and pancreaticoduodenal arteries\"></div><figcaption>Arterial supply of the duodenum: gastroduodenal, supraduodenal and pancreaticoduodenal arteries <small>· 2026 lecture: Small &amp; Large Intestines, slide 32</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The family tree rules out look-alikes. The parent of the GDA is the common hepatic artery, upstream of it. Its sister branch, the proper hepatic artery, gives the right hepatic and cystic arteries. None of these depends on the GDA. The right gastric artery usually comes from the proper hepatic artery, not from the GDA.</p>\n<h4>Blood supply of the duodenum</h4>\n<p>The duodenum straddles the border between foregut and midgut, and its blood supply shows it. Down to the major papilla (the foregut part), it is fed by the supraduodenal and superior pancreaticoduodenal branches of the GDA. Below the papilla (the midgut part), it is fed by the <b>inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery</b>, a branch of the SMA. The splenic and left gastric arteries send it no branches, and the inferior mesenteric artery, the artery of the hindgut, does not reach it.</p>\n<p>The duodenal veins follow the arteries. They drain into the hepatic portal vein, some directly and others through the superior mesenteric vein, which joins the splenic vein behind the neck of the pancreas to form the portal vein. So duodenal blood passes through the liver first. None of it drains straight into the inferior vena cava, the hepatic veins or the azygos veins.</p>\n<p>The superior and inferior pancreaticoduodenal arteries meet on the head of the pancreas, linking the celiac trunk to the SMA. If the GDA is blocked, its three branches lose their main flow. Collaterals partly make up for it. The inferior pancreaticoduodenal arteries reach the pancreatic head and duodenum, the left gastro-omental artery reaches the greater curvature, and pancreatic branches of the splenic artery help too.</p>\n<h4>Posterior duodenal ulcer</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>An ulcer forms in the posterior wall of the first part of the duodenum, the part that receives and neutralizes acid chyme.</li>\n<li>It eats through the wall.</li>\n<li>The GDA lies directly behind it, so the artery is eroded and major bleeding follows.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Duodenum: C around pancreatic head; right upper quadrant + epigastrium; first 2 cm intraperitoneal; villi</li>\n<li>Receives chyme (pylorus), bile + pancreatic juice (papilla); neutralizes acid, digests, absorbs</li>\n<li>D1: GDA, bile duct, portal v., IVC behind; liver + gallbladder in front</li>\n<li>D2: major papilla (bile + pancreatic ducts); transverse colon in front</li>\n<li>D3: SMA/SMV in front; IVC + aorta behind; IVC behind D1–D3</li>\n<li>GDA (← common hepatic) runs directly behind D1</li>\n<li>GDA → supraduodenal, superior pancreaticoduodenal, right gastro-omental</li>\n<li>Above papilla: GDA branches; below: inferior pancreaticoduodenal (SMA)</li>\n<li>Duodenal veins → portal vein (directly or via SMV); not IVC, hepatic or azygos veins</li>\n<li>GDA block: collaterals = inferior pancreaticoduodenal, left gastro-omental, splenic</li>\n<li>Posterior D1 ulcer → erodes GDA</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The celiac trunk and its branches","sn":6,"pn":"6.4"},{"l":"ANAT-G3","h":"Veins and lymph of the stomach","p":"<h4>Veins</h4>\n<p>The veins of the stomach run with its arteries and carry the same names. All of them drain into the <b>hepatic portal system</b>, so blood from the stomach passes through the liver before it reaches the heart. None drains straight into the inferior vena cava (IVC).</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G004-115.jpg\" alt=\"Veins of the stomach draining to the splenic, superior mesenteric and portal veins\"></div><figcaption>Veins of the stomach draining to the splenic, superior mesenteric and portal veins <small>· 2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach &amp; Celiac, slide 115</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The <b>left and right gastric veins</b> run along the lesser curvature and drain <b>directly</b> into the hepatic portal vein. The <b>short gastric veins</b> and the <b>left gastro-omental vein</b> drain into the <b>splenic vein</b>. The <b>right gastro-omental vein</b> drains into the <b>superior mesenteric vein (SMV)</b>. A small <b>prepyloric vein</b> rises over the pylorus to the right gastric vein. The splenic vein then joins the SMV to form the hepatic portal vein. The <b>hepatic veins</b> are different: they drain the liver itself into the IVC, and no stomach vein enters them.</p>\n<h4>Lymph follows the arteries</h4>\n<p>The stomach's lymph vessels run beside its arteries, so each region drains first to small nodes along its own arteries. The lesser curvature drains to <b>gastric nodes</b> along the left and right gastric vessels. The fundus and the left part of the greater curvature drain along the short gastric and left gastro-omental vessels. They reach the <b>pancreaticosplenic nodes</b> (splenic nodes), at the splenic hilum and along the pancreas. The right part of the greater curvature drains to the <b>gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) nodes</b>. The pylorus drains to <b>pyloric nodes</b>, some of it through hepatic nodes.</p>\n<p>Every one of these first stations drains on to the same place: the <b>celiac nodes</b>, around the celiac trunk. From there lymph passes through the intestinal trunk into the <b>cisterna chyli</b>, then up the <b>thoracic duct</b>, which empties into the <b>left venous angle</b>, where the left internal jugular and left subclavian veins meet. Lymph from most other foregut organs takes the same route: its own regional nodes, then the celiac nodes, intestinal trunk, cisterna chyli and thoracic duct. Because all stomach lymph meets at the celiac nodes, a stomach cancer from any region, the antrum included, is most likely to reach them. The local first-station nodes vary with the tumor's site. A lesser-curvature cancer spreads through the left gastric nodes. A fundus cancer spreads to nodes at the splenic hilum and along the pancreas, even while the left gastric nodes stay normal.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/D041-190.jpg\" alt=\"Lymph drainage of abdominal organs: stomach nodes to celiac nodes and cisterna chyli\"></div><figcaption>Lymph drainage of abdominal organs: stomach nodes to celiac nodes and cisterna chyli <small>· 2025 TA review, slide 190</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The <b>cisterna chyli</b> is a lymph sac, not a node. It is the lower, widened end of the thoracic duct. It lies just right of and behind the aorta, between the crura of the diaphragm, behind the right crus.</p>\n<p>Other node groups drain other territories. Superior mesenteric nodes drain the midgut, and inferior mesenteric nodes the hindgut. Lumbar (para-aortic) nodes drain the kidneys, gonads, posterior wall and legs, and their lumbar trunks join the cisterna chyli. Superficial inguinal nodes drain the lower limb and lower body wall, and axillary nodes the upper limb and breast. Pancreaticoduodenal nodes drain the pancreatic head and duodenum. Lymph does not drain into veins such as the azygos vein. The right lymphatic duct drains only the right upper body, into the right venous angle.</p>\n<h4>A block at the cisterna chyli</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>A tumor compresses the cisterna chyli.</li>\n<li>Foregut lymph cannot move on. It backs up into the celiac nodes, which enlarge, and forward flow slows.</li>\n<li>Above the block, the thoracic duct receives less lymph, so it does not swell.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Left + right gastric veins → hepatic portal vein directly</li>\n<li>Short gastric + left gastro-omental → splenic v.; right gastro-omental → SMV</li>\n<li>Prepyloric v. → right gastric v.; splenic v. + SMV → portal v.</li>\n<li>Hepatic veins: liver → IVC; no stomach veins</li>\n<li>Lymph follows arteries: gastric, pancreaticosplenic, gastro-omental, pyloric nodes</li>\n<li>All → celiac nodes → intestinal trunk → cisterna chyli → thoracic duct → left venous angle (most foregut organs too)</li>\n<li>Lesser curvature: left gastric nodes; fundus: splenic-hilum/pancreatic nodes</li>\n<li>Cisterna chyli = sac, not node; right of aorta, behind right crus</li>\n<li>Not stomach: superior/inferior mesenteric, lumbar (para-aortic), inguinal, axillary nodes; not azygos or right lymphatic duct (right venous angle)</li>\n<li>Cisterna block → celiac nodes swell, flow slows; thoracic duct not dilated</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Veins, lymph and nerves","sn":7,"pn":"7.1"},{"l":"ANAT-G3","h":"Nerves of the stomach and foregut","p":"<p>The stomach and the other foregut organs receive both divisions of the autonomic nervous system. The two divisions arrive by different routes and have opposite effects.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G004-90.jpg\" alt=\"Vagal trunks, greater splanchnic nerves and the celiac plexus around the stomach\"></div><figcaption>Vagal trunks, greater splanchnic nerves and the celiac plexus around the stomach <small>· 2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach &amp; Celiac, slide 90</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Parasympathetic: the vagus</h4>\n<p>The parasympathetic supply comes from the <b>vagus nerve</b>, whose preganglionic nerve cells lie in the brainstem (the dorsal vagal nucleus of the medulla). Low in the thorax the two vagi form a plexus on the esophagus, then gather into two trunks that pass through the esophageal hiatus. The left vagus becomes the <b>anterior vagal trunk</b>, and the right vagus becomes the <b>posterior vagal trunk</b>. The anterior trunk mainly serves the stomach and the liver.</p>\n<p>Vagal fibers are preganglionic. They pass through the celiac plexus <b>without synapsing</b> and synapse on small ganglia in or on the wall of the organ. On the stomach, the vagus <b>increases peristalsis</b> and <b>relaxes the pyloric sphincter</b>. Together these push chyme on into the duodenum. The other parasympathetic outflow, the pelvic splanchnic nerves from cord segments S2–S4 (second to fourth sacral), serves the hindgut and pelvic organs, not the foregut.</p>\n<p>The pancreas gets its parasympathetic supply mainly from the <b>posterior vagal trunk</b>, which sends a celiac branch to the celiac plexus. These preganglionic fibers pass through without synapsing and follow the arteries to the pancreas. They synapse in small ganglia in and around it, the pancreatic plexus. They stimulate secretion, including insulin.</p>\n<h4>Sympathetic: the greater splanchnic nerve</h4>\n<p>The sympathetic supply starts in spinal cord segments <b>T5–T9</b> (fifth to ninth thoracic). Preganglionic fibers pass through the sympathetic trunk without synapsing, leave in the <b>greater (thoracic) splanchnic nerve</b> and synapse in the <b>celiac ganglion</b> of the celiac plexus. This is a prevertebral ganglion, in front of the aorta, not a ganglion of the sympathetic chain. Postganglionic fibers then follow the arteries to the stomach. Sympathetic action is the opposite of vagal action: it <b>inhibits peristalsis</b> and <b>contracts the pyloric sphincter</b>. On the pancreas it reduces secretion. So in a fright (fight or flight), sympathetic fibers relaying in the celiac ganglion and plexus shut digestion in the stomach down. Midgut sympathetics relay in the superior mesenteric ganglion instead.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/D041-197.jpg\" alt=\"Greater splanchnic nerve from T5–T9 to the celiac ganglion and foregut\"></div><figcaption>Greater splanchnic nerve from T5–T9 to the celiac ganglion and foregut <small>· 2025 TA review, slide 197</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The celiac ganglion is an autonomic relay station. The dorsal root, geniculate and trigeminal ganglia hold only sensory nerve cells. The celiac plexus is a mostly sympathetic network; the drive for peristalsis comes from the vagus. The phrenic nerve, from C3–C5 (third to fifth cervical), is the motor nerve of the diaphragm. There is no right gastric nerve (the right gastric is an artery) and no musculoskeletal nerve. The lesser splanchnic nerves carry sympathetic fibers to the midgut and kidneys, and the lumbar splanchnic nerves, from L1–L2 (first and second lumbar), to the hindgut and pelvis, relaying in the inferior mesenteric ganglion.</p>\n<h4>Pain from the foregut</h4>\n<p>Pain fibers from the foregut organs (stomach, duodenum, liver, gallbladder, pancreas) run back with the sympathetic fibers in the <b>greater splanchnic nerves</b> to cord segments T5–T9. The pain is felt in the epigastrium. Pain from the pancreas can also reach the back. Vagal sensory fibers carry reflex and fullness signals to the nucleus solitarius, not pain.</p>\n<p>Why is organ pain felt in the body wall? The visceral pain fibers enter cord segments T5–T9. Sensory fibers from the skin and muscles of the epigastrium and back enter the same segments. Both sets <b>converge</b> on the same spinal neurons. The brain is used to signals from the body wall, so it places the pain there; this is <b>referred pain</b>. So a posterior gastric ulcer that penetrates the pancreas gives epigastric pain that radiates to the mid-back. No somatic nerve of the back is irritated, no motor neuron is hurt, and the pudendal nerve serves only the perineum.</p>\n<p>The level of the splanchnic nerve matches the level of the gut. The lesser splanchnic nerves (T10–T11) carry midgut pain, and the lumbar splanchnic nerves (L1–L2) carry hindgut pain. Spinal nerves, such as T5–T12 or T12–L2, carry pain from the body wall, not from the organs.</p>\n<h4>Vagotomy</h4>\n<p>Cutting vagal fibers lowers acid secretion. Truncal vagotomy, cutting both trunks, was rarely done, because it also denervates other abdominal organs. Proximal gastric vagotomy denervated the stomach but spared the vagal branches to the pylorus, liver and biliary ducts, intestines and celiac plexus. Selective proximal vagotomy aimed only at the area that holds the acid-making parietal cells, sparing vagal drive for motility.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Parasympathetic: brainstem → vagus → anterior (left) + posterior (right) vagal trunks; pelvic splanchnics (S2–S4) serve hindgut, not foregut</li>\n<li>Vagus: ↑ peristalsis, relaxes pyloric sphincter; no synapse in celiac plexus</li>\n<li>Pancreas: posterior vagal trunk → synapse in pancreatic plexus → ↑ secretion, insulin</li>\n<li>Sympathetic: T5–T9 → through the chain without synapsing → greater splanchnic → synapse in celiac (prevertebral) ganglion</li>\n<li>Sympathetic (fight or flight, via celiac ganglion): ↓ peristalsis, contracts pyloric sphincter, ↓ pancreatic secretion</li>\n<li>Foregut pain: greater splanchnic → T5–T9 → epigastric (pancreas → back)</li>\n<li>Referred pain: visceral + somatic afferents converge on the same T5–T9 cord neurons → felt in the body wall</li>\n<li>Lesser splanchnic T10–T11 = midgut; lumbar splanchnic L1–L2 = hindgut; phrenic C3–C5 = diaphragm</li>\n<li>Vagal afferents: fullness, reflexes, not pain; dorsal root, geniculate, trigeminal = sensory</li>\n<li>Vagotomy ↓ acid; truncal rarely done; proximal/selective spare pylorus and motility</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Veins, lymph and nerves","sn":7,"pn":"7.2"},{"l":"ANAT-G4","h":"Four parts, and the first part's neighbors","p":"<h4>The small intestine and the duodenal C-loop</h4>\n<p>The small intestine runs from the pylorus of the stomach to the ileocecal junction, where the large intestine begins. It has three parts in a row: the <b>duodenum</b>, the jejunum and the ileum. The duodenum is the short first part, about 25 cm long. It bends into a <b>C-shaped loop around the head of the pancreas</b>, so the pancreatic head sits inside the curve of the C.</p>\n<p>The loop has four parts. The <b>superior (first) part</b> starts at the pylorus, in front of and to the right of the body of the first lumbar vertebra (L1), and runs back and to the right. Its first 2 cm, the <b>duodenal cap</b>, has a small mesentery, so it is intraperitoneal and mobile. The rest of the duodenum is retroperitoneal and fixed. The <b>descending (second) part</b> runs down the right side of L1 to the third lumbar vertebra (L3), beside the pancreatic head, and receives the bile and pancreatic ducts. The <b>inferior (third, horizontal) part</b> crosses the midline at L3, in front of the inferior vena cava (IVC) and the aorta. The <b>ascending (fourth) part</b> rises on the left of the aorta to the second lumbar vertebra (L2) and turns forward into the jejunum.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G005-9.jpg\" alt=\"Four parts of the duodenum curving around the head of the pancreas\"></div><figcaption>Four parts of the duodenum curving around the head of the pancreas <small>· 2026 lecture: Small &amp; Large Intestines, slide 9</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>What touches the first part</h4>\n<p>The first part has neighbors on four sides. Picture a box drawn around the duodenal cap.</p>\n<p><b>In front</b> lie the peritoneum, with the peritoneal cavity beyond it, the <b>gallbladder</b> and the <b>quadrate lobe of the liver</b>. The gallbladder hangs from the underside of the liver and rests on the front of the cap. A deep ulcer of the anterior wall can therefore erode into the gallbladder.</p>\n<p><b>Behind</b> lie four structures: the <b>bile duct</b>, the <b>gastroduodenal artery (GDA)</b>, the <b>portal vein</b> and the IVC. The bile duct and the GDA run down side by side directly behind the wall, with the bile duct on the right. The portal vein and then the IVC lie deeper. A wound that passes through the first part from the front meets these structures in turn.</p>\n<p><b>Above</b> lie the <b>neck of the gallbladder</b> and the <b>hepatoduodenal ligament</b>, the free edge of the lesser omentum. The cystic duct leaves the neck of the gallbladder up here, so it lies above the cap, not behind it. <b>Below</b> lie the <b>neck of the pancreas</b> and the greater omentum.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G005-10.jpg\" alt=\"Front view: gallbladder and quadrate lobe (circled) overlie the first part\"></div><figcaption>Front view: gallbladder and quadrate lobe (circled) overlie the first part <small>· 2026 lecture: Small &amp; Large Intestines, slide 10</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Close, but not touching</h4>\n<p>Several nearby structures are easy to misplace. The common hepatic artery runs along the upper border of the pancreas, above the first part. It gives off the GDA, which then drops behind the cap. The left lobe of the liver lies over the stomach; only the quadrate lobe lies over the duodenal cap. The right kidney lies behind the second part, not the first. The head of the pancreas fills the inside of the C, and only its neck lies just below the first part. The stomach is not in front of the duodenum at all; it joins it end to end at the pylorus.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Small intestine: duodenum → jejunum → ileum (pylorus to ileocecal junction)</li>\n<li>Duodenum ≈25 cm; C-loop around pancreatic head; 4 parts</li>\n<li>1st part L1; 2nd right of L1–L3; 3rd crosses midline at L3; 4th rises to L2</li>\n<li>First 2 cm (duodenal cap) intraperitoneal, mobile; rest retroperitoneal</li>\n<li>1st part in front: peritoneum, gallbladder, quadrate lobe → anterior ulcer may erode gallbladder</li>\n<li>1st part behind: bile duct, GDA, portal vein, IVC</li>\n<li>1st part above: gallbladder neck, hepatoduodenal lig.; cystic duct up here</li>\n<li>1st part below: pancreatic neck, greater omentum</li>\n<li>Right kidney behind 2nd part; left liver lobe over stomach; common hepatic a. above</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The duodenum","sn":1,"pn":"1.1"},{"l":"ANAT-G4","h":"Duodenal ulcers: bleeding or peritonitis","p":"<h4>Where duodenal ulcers form</h4>\n<p>An <b>ulcer</b> is an open sore in a lining that breaks down and fails to heal. In the duodenum, ulcers are most common in the first part, just past the pylorus, where acid from the stomach first hits the wall. This region is the duodenal cap, and most of its ulcers sit on the <b>posterior wall</b>. When an ulcer eats all the way through, the result depends on what lies on the other side of the wall.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G005-12.jpg\" alt=\"Behind the first part: bile duct, gastroduodenal artery, portal vein, inferior vena cava\"></div><figcaption>Behind the first part: bile duct, gastroduodenal artery, portal vein, inferior vena cava <small>· 2026 lecture: Small &amp; Large Intestines, slide 12</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The gastroduodenal artery</h4>\n<p>The <b>gastroduodenal artery (GDA)</b> is a branch of the common hepatic artery, which comes from the celiac trunk. The common hepatic artery runs to the right along the upper border of the pancreas, above the duodenum. There it gives off the GDA, which turns straight down and runs <b>directly behind the first part of the duodenum</b>, between it and the pancreas. The bile duct runs down just to its right. The portal vein and the inferior vena cava (IVC) lie deeper, farther from the wall.</p>\n<p>The GDA gives off several branches. The tiny supraduodenal artery goes to the upper surface of the first part. The right gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery runs to the left along the greater curvature of the stomach. The anterior and posterior <b>superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries</b> run down on the front and back of the pancreatic head, beside the second part. All of these are smaller than the GDA trunk, which is the large vessel pressed against the back of the cap.</p>\n<h4>Two ways an ulcer breaks through</h4>\n<p>A <b>posterior ulcer</b> erodes back through the wall and opens the GDA. Blood pours into the gut, and the patient vomits bright-red blood (hematemesis). Pain from a posterior ulcer can spread to the back. The portal vein and the bile duct also lie behind the first part, but the GDA is the vessel in direct contact with the wall, so it is the one that bleeds.</p>\n<p>An <b>anterior ulcer</b> has no large artery in front of it. The front of the first part is covered by peritoneum and faces the peritoneal cavity. When the ulcer perforates, gas and gut contents spill into that cavity and cause <b>peritonitis</b>: sudden severe pain and a rigid, board-like abdomen. Where the gallbladder rests on the cap, an anterior ulcer can erode into it instead (1.1).</p>\n<h4>Arteries at other ulcer sites</h4>\n<p>Each part of the upper gut has its own artery beside it, so the site of an ulcer predicts the vessel at risk. Ulcers on the lesser curvature of the stomach bleed from the <b>left and right gastric arteries</b>. Ulcers on the posterior wall of the stomach erode the <b>splenic artery</b>, which runs along the top of the pancreas. The greater curvature carries the right and left gastro-omental arteries, and the short gastric arteries run from the splenic artery to the fundus. The proper hepatic artery runs above the duodenum, inside the hepatoduodenal ligament. The superior mesenteric artery (SMA) lies much lower. It starts behind the neck of the pancreas and crosses the third part, and its inferior pancreaticoduodenal branches lie near the third part. None of these touches the back of the first part.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G005-88.jpg\" alt=\"Celiac branches: gastroduodenal, gastric, gastro-omental, short gastric and splenic arteries\"></div><figcaption>Celiac branches: gastroduodenal, gastric, gastro-omental, short gastric and splenic arteries <small>· 2026 lecture: Small &amp; Large Intestines, slide 88</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Ulcer: open sore that fails to heal; duodenal → mostly posterior wall of 1st part (cap)</li>\n<li>GDA: celiac → common hepatic → GDA, straight down behind 1st part</li>\n<li>Bile duct just right of GDA; portal vein and IVC deeper</li>\n<li>Posterior ulcer → erodes GDA → hematemesis; pain may spread to back</li>\n<li>Anterior ulcer → perforates into peritoneal cavity → peritonitis (rigid abdomen)</li>\n<li>GDA branches: supraduodenal, right gastro-omental, ant./post. superior pancreaticoduodenal</li>\n<li>Lesser-curvature gastric ulcer → left/right gastric a.; posterior gastric ulcer → splenic a.</li>\n<li>Greater curvature: gastro-omentals; fundus: short gastrics; proper hepatic above duodenum</li>\n<li>SMA much lower: crosses 3rd part; inferior pancreaticoduodenal near 3rd part</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The duodenum","sn":1,"pn":"1.2"},{"l":"ANAT-G4","h":"Second, third and fourth parts","p":"<h4>Second (descending) part</h4>\n<p>The second part is 7–10 cm long. It runs straight down along the right side of the first to third lumbar vertebrae (L1–L3), curving around the head of the pancreas, and it is entirely retroperitoneal. It lies to the right of the inferior vena cava (IVC) and parallel to it, resting on the right kidney rather than on the great vessels.</p>\n<p>Its medial wall faces the pancreatic head and receives the ducts. The bile duct and the main pancreatic duct enter its posteromedial wall and join to form the <b>hepatopancreatic ampulla</b>, which opens on a small mound, the <b>major duodenal papilla</b>. The accessory pancreatic duct opens on the minor duodenal papilla, about 2 cm higher. In front of the second part lie the liver, the transverse colon and coils of jejunum. Behind it lie the right kidney, the psoas major and the IVC.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G005-17.jpg\" alt=\"Bile and pancreatic ducts opening on the major and minor duodenal papillae\"></div><figcaption>Bile and pancreatic ducts opening on the major and minor duodenal papillae <small>· 2026 lecture: Small &amp; Large Intestines, slide 17</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Third (inferior, horizontal) part</h4>\n<p>The third part runs from right to left across the midline at the third lumbar vertebra (L3). On its way it lies <b>directly on the IVC and the abdominal aorta</b>. No other part of the duodenum lies in front of both great vessels. The first part lies in front of the IVC but not the aorta, the second part lies to the right of the IVC, and the fourth part lies to the left of the aorta. A mass in front of the IVC at L3, or an abdominal aortic aneurysm pushing forward, therefore presses on the third part.</p>\n<p>It has neighbors on three sides. <b>Behind</b> it lie the right psoas major, the IVC, the aorta and the right ureter. <b>Above</b> it lie the head of the pancreas, with its uncinate process, and the superior mesenteric artery and vein (SMA, SMV). <b>In front</b> lie the SMA, the SMV and coils of jejunum. The SMA and SMV come down from behind the neck of the pancreas, sit just above the third part, and then cross in front of it into the root of the mesentery. They are the vessels both above and in front of it. An injury to the SMA just below the pancreatic head is likely to reach the third part right behind it.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G005-22.jpg\" alt=\"Superior mesenteric artery and vein crossing in front of the third part\"></div><figcaption>Superior mesenteric artery and vein crossing in front of the third part <small>· 2026 lecture: Small &amp; Large Intestines, slide 22</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G005-23.jpg\" alt=\"Behind the third part: right psoas, inferior vena cava, aorta, right ureter\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G005-23c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Behind the third part: right psoas, inferior vena cava, aorta, right ureter\"></div></div><figcaption>Behind the third part: right psoas, inferior vena cava, aorta, right ureter <small>· 2026 lecture: Small &amp; Large Intestines, slide 23</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Other vessels lie elsewhere. The inferior mesenteric artery (IMA) arises below the duodenum and runs to the left, and the inferior mesenteric vein runs up on the left. The portal vein forms higher, behind the neck of the pancreas. The renal arteries leave the aorta higher, at L1–L2, and the left renal artery lies behind the left renal vein. The sympathetic trunks run on the sides of the vertebral bodies, deep to the IVC and aorta.</p>\n<p>The third part also lies in the narrow angle between the SMA in front and the aorta behind. The <b>left renal vein</b> crosses the same angle just above it. When the angle closes, both can be squeezed: the duodenum in SMA syndrome and the vein in nutcracker syndrome (3.2).</p>\n<h4>Retroperitoneal neighbors and pancreatic cancer</h4>\n<p>The pancreas and the second to fourth parts of the duodenum lie together behind the peritoneum, with no peritoneum between them. A pancreatic cancer can therefore grow straight into the third part, just below the pancreatic head, without crossing a peritoneal layer. Organs covered by peritoneum (intraperitoneal) are walled off. The stomach lies in front, separated from the pancreas by the lesser sac (omental bursa). The jejunum and ileum hang on the mesentery, the transverse colon hangs on the transverse mesocolon, and the cecum lies far away in the right lower quadrant.</p>\n<h4>Fourth (ascending) part</h4>\n<p>The fourth part rises to the left of the aorta, below the body of the pancreas, to the second lumbar vertebra (L2). It then turns sharply forward and joins the jejunum at the <b>duodenojejunal flexure</b>. The <b>suspensory muscle of the duodenum (ligament of Treitz)</b> holds the flexure up. It is skeletal muscle that runs from the diaphragm to the third and fourth parts, behind the pancreas and splenic vein and in front of the left kidney. Its contraction widens the angle of the flexure, which helps contents move on. In front of the fourth part lie the start of the root of the mesentery and coils of jejunum. Behind it lies the left psoas major, with the aorta on its right.</p>\n<h4>The pancreatic head and the four parts</h4>\n<p>The head of the pancreas fills the C-loop, so each part meets it from a different side. The first part lies above the pancreas, with only the pancreatic neck below it. The second part curves down the right side of the head. The third part runs below the head, so the head sits <b>superior to the third part</b>, with the SMA and SMV. The fourth part lies to the left of the head, under the <b>body of the pancreas</b>.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>2nd part: 7–10 cm, right of L1–L3, retroperitoneal, right of IVC, on right kidney</li>\n<li>Bile + main pancreatic ducts → hepatopancreatic ampulla → major papilla; accessory → minor papilla</li>\n<li>3rd part: crosses midline at L3, directly on IVC + aorta (only part on both)</li>\n<li>3rd behind: R psoas, IVC, aorta, R ureter; above: pancreatic head/uncinate, SMA/SMV</li>\n<li>SMA/SMV above, then in front of 3rd part → L3 mass, aneurysm, SMA stab reach it</li>\n<li>SMA–aorta angle: 3rd part (SMA syndrome); left renal vein just above (nutcracker)</li>\n<li>Pancreas + duodenum 2–4 retroperitoneal together → cancer invades 3rd part directly</li>\n<li>4th part: left of aorta → L2 → duodenojejunal flexure (ligament of Treitz)</li>\n<li>Treitz: skeletal muscle from diaphragm to 3rd + 4th parts; behind pancreas, in front of left kidney</li>\n<li>Head of pancreas: superior to 3rd part, medial to 2nd, right of 4th; body above 4th; neck below 1st</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The duodenum","sn":1,"pn":"1.3"},{"l":"ANAT-G4","h":"Jejunum versus ileum on the mesentery","p":"<h4>The mesentery</h4>\n<p>After the duodenojejunal flexure, the small intestine becomes the <b>jejunum</b> and then the <b>ileum</b>. Together they run about 6 m and end at the <b>ileocecal junction</b>, where the large intestine begins. Unlike the fixed duodenum, they lie in free, mobile coils.</p>\n<p>The coils hang from the posterior abdominal wall on the <b>mesentery</b>, a double-layered fold of peritoneum. Its root runs down and to the right across the back wall, from the duodenojejunal flexure to the ileocecal junction. Arteries, veins, nerves and lymph vessels reach the gut by running between its two layers. Other folds have their own names. The <b>mesoappendix</b> holds only the appendix, the <b>transverse and sigmoid mesocolons</b> hold those parts of the colon, and the <b>greater and lesser omenta</b> hang from the stomach.</p>\n<p>Because a loop on a mesentery can move, it can also twist around its own mesentery. This is a <b>volvulus</b>. The twist blocks the bowel and pinches the vessels in the mesentery, so the loop becomes obstructed and then ischemic. The three named types are midgut (small-bowel) volvulus, cecal volvulus and sigmoid volvulus. A twisted loop of small intestine is jejunum or ileum; the cecum, appendix and transverse colon belong to the large intestine.</p>\n<h4>Telling jejunum from ileum</h4>\n<p>The two blend into each other with no sharp border, but their two ends look clearly different. The <b>jejunum</b> is the proximal two-fifths. It lies mainly in the <b>left upper quadrant (LUQ)</b>, up and to the left of the umbilicus. Its wall is <b>thick</b>, heavy and very vascular, so it looks deeper red, and it is wider (2–4 cm). Inside, its circular folds (plicae circulares) are tall and closely packed. It absorbs more of the nutrients.</p>\n<p>The <b>ileum</b> is the distal three-fifths. It lies mainly in the <b>right lower quadrant (RLQ)</b>, partly in the pelvis. Its wall is <b>thin</b> and light, it is paler pink and narrower (2–3 cm), and its circular folds are low and sparse, disappearing near its end. Its wall holds many <b>Peyer patches</b>; the jejunum has none.</p>\n<p>The vessels in the mesentery give the most reliable sign. Jejunal arteries join into <b>a few large loops (arcades)</b>, usually one row, and from them come <b>long vasa recta</b>, the straight arteries that run into the gut wall. Ileal arteries form <b>many short loops</b> in two or three rows, like a honeycomb, and send out <b>short vasa recta</b>. The jejunal mesentery holds less fat, so clear windows show between its vessels; the ileal mesentery holds more fat. The usual mix-up is this: the ileum has more arcades but shorter vasa recta. Few arcades always go with long vasa recta, and many arcades with short ones. Both segments get their arteries from the superior mesenteric artery (SMA): jejunal branches for the jejunum and ileal branches for the ileum (3.3). Neither the celiac trunk nor the inferior mesenteric artery (IMA) reaches them.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G005-80.jpg\" alt=\"Inset B: jejunal arteries (few arcades, long vasa recta) versus ileal (many arcades, short)\"></div><figcaption>Inset B: jejunal arteries (few arcades, long vasa recta) versus ileal (many arcades, short) <small>· 2026 lecture: Small &amp; Large Intestines, slide 80</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G005-41.jpg\" alt=\"Table comparing jejunum and ileum in the living body\"></div><figcaption>Table comparing jejunum and ileum in the living body <small>· 2026 lecture: Small &amp; Large Intestines, slide 41</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>What is absorbed where</h4>\n<p>Most nutrients are absorbed in the duodenum and jejunum. <b>Iron</b> is taken up in the duodenum and proximal jejunum, so losing a long stretch of jejunum can lead to iron deficiency. The <b>terminal ileum</b> absorbs <b>vitamin B12</b> and reabsorbs <b>bile salts</b>. Vitamin B12 travels there bound to intrinsic factor, which the parietal cells of the stomach make. The ileum absorbs the pair; it does not make intrinsic factor.</p>\n<h4>Peyer patches and Brunner glands</h4>\n<p><b>Peyer patches</b> are clusters of lymphoid nodules in the wall of the ileum. They sample antigens that enter the gut and drive the making of immunoglobulin A (IgA), the antibody that guards mucosal surfaces. Chronic ileal disease that damages them can lower IgA. <b>Brunner glands</b> are different. They are mucous glands in the duodenum, and their mucus protects the wall from stomach acid.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Jejunum + ileum ≈6 m: duodenojejunal flexure → ileocecal junction</li>\n<li>Mesentery = double peritoneal fold to posterior wall; carries vessels, nerves, lymph</li>\n<li>Mesoappendix: appendix; mesocolons: transverse, sigmoid; omenta: stomach</li>\n<li>Volvulus = loop twists on its mesentery → obstruction + ischemia</li>\n<li>Jejunum: proximal 2/5, LUQ, thick, red, tall folds, few arcades, long vasa recta</li>\n<li>Ileum: distal 3/5, RLQ, thin, pale, low folds, many arcades, short vasa recta</li>\n<li>Arteries: jejunal and ileal branches of the SMA (not celiac, not IMA)</li>\n<li>Fat in mesentery: jejunum less, ileum more; absorption: jejunum more</li>\n<li>Peyer patches: ileum (none in jejunum) → IgA</li>\n<li>Iron: duodenum + proximal jejunum; B12 + bile salts: terminal ileum</li>\n<li>Intrinsic factor: gastric parietal cells; Brunner glands: duodenum (acid protection)</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Jejunum and ileum","sn":2,"pn":"2.1"},{"l":"ANAT-G4","h":"Origin, level and neighbors","p":"<p>The <b>superior mesenteric artery (SMA)</b> is the anterior branch of the abdominal aorta that supplies the <b>midgut</b>. Its territory runs from the duodenum below the bile-duct opening through the jejunum, ileum, cecum, appendix and ascending colon to most of the transverse colon.</p>\n<h4>Levels of the gut arteries on the aorta</h4>\n<p>The three gut arteries leave the front of the aorta one below the other. The <b>celiac trunk</b> comes first, at the twelfth thoracic vertebra (T12); through its left gastric, splenic and common hepatic branches it supplies the foregut, including the stomach, liver, pancreas and spleen. The <b>SMA</b> follows about 1 cm lower, in front of the <b>lower part of the first lumbar vertebra (L1)</b>. The renal arteries leave the sides of the aorta at L1–L2, and the gonadal arteries leave the front at the second lumbar vertebra (L2). The <b>inferior mesenteric artery (IMA)</b> arises at about the third lumbar vertebra (L3) and supplies the hindgut. At the fourth lumbar vertebra (L4) the aorta divides into the two common iliac arteries. No major gut artery arises at the eleventh thoracic vertebra (T11). Just above the celiac trunk, the paired inferior phrenic arteries leave the aorta for the diaphragm; they are not gut arteries. So, from top to bottom, the three unpaired gut arteries are always the celiac trunk, the SMA and the IMA. On an angiogram, an artery that leaves the front of the aorta at L1 and gives jejunal, ileal, right colic and middle colic branches is the SMA.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G005-43.jpg\" alt=\"Superior mesenteric artery leaving the aorta below the celiac trunk, behind the pancreatic neck\"></div><figcaption>Superior mesenteric artery leaving the aorta below the celiac trunk, behind the pancreatic neck <small>· 2026 lecture: Small &amp; Large Intestines, slide 43</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>What crosses in front and what lies behind</h4>\n<p>Right after it leaves the aorta, the SMA is <b>crossed in front</b> by the splenic vein and the <b>neck of the pancreas</b>. Put the other way round, the SMA lies behind the splenic vein and the pancreatic neck. Then it runs down in front of three structures, which lie <b>behind</b> it from top to bottom: the <b>left renal vein</b>, the <b>uncinate process of the pancreas</b> and the <b>third part of the duodenum</b>.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/D041-222.jpg\" alt=\"Structures in front of and behind the superior mesenteric artery\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/D041-222c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Structures in front of and behind the superior mesenteric artery\"></div></div><figcaption>Structures in front of and behind the superior mesenteric artery <small>· 2025 TA review, slide 222</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The pancreas around the superior mesenteric vessels</h4>\n<p>The pancreas drapes over the superior mesenteric vessels, and each of its parts has a fixed relation to them. The <b>head</b> lies to the right of the superior mesenteric vein (SMV), inside the C-loop of the duodenum. The <b>uncinate process</b> is a hook from the lower head that slips to the left <b>behind the SMV and SMA</b>; it is the only part of the pancreas behind them. The <b>neck</b> lies in front of the SMV and SMA, and the portal vein forms behind it. The <b>body</b> crosses in front of the aorta and the SMA, and the <b>tail</b> reaches the hilum of the spleen on the far left. The stomach lies in front of all of this. The first part of the duodenum lies higher and to the right, away from the SMA.</p>\n<h4>Course and companions</h4>\n<p>The SMA runs <b>down and to the right</b> in the root of the mesentery, toward the ileocecal junction. On the way it crosses in front of the uncinate process and the third part of the duodenum. Its jejunal and ileal branches join into loops called arcades, and the arcades give off the straight vasa recta; the arcades and the vasa recta are different vessels.</p>\n<p>The <b>SMV</b> runs beside the artery, on its <b>right and slightly in front</b>. It drains the midgut and passes up behind the neck of the pancreas, where it joins the splenic vein to form the portal vein.</p>\n<p>A nerve network, the superior mesenteric plexus, wraps the artery. Its sympathetic fibers come from the <b>thoracic splanchnic nerves</b>. The lumbar splanchnic nerves feed the inferior mesenteric plexus instead (7.2).</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>SMA: anterior aortic branch to midgut; lower L1, ~1 cm below celiac</li>\n<li>Levels: celiac T12 (left gastric, splenic, common hepatic); SMA L1 (jejunal, ileal, right + middle colic); renal L1–L2; gonadal L2; IMA ≈L3; bifurcation L4</li>\n<li>Unpaired gut arteries, top → bottom: celiac, SMA, IMA; inferior phrenics are paired (diaphragm)</li>\n<li>In front of SMA: splenic vein, neck of pancreas</li>\n<li>Behind SMA (top → bottom): left renal vein, uncinate process, 3rd part duodenum</li>\n<li>Pancreas: head right of SMV; uncinate behind SMV/SMA; neck in front; body over aorta</li>\n<li>SMA runs down and to the right in root of mesentery → ileocecal junction</li>\n<li>SMV: right of SMA, slightly in front; + splenic v. behind neck → portal v.</li>\n<li>Branches → arcades → vasa recta; plexus sympathetics from thoracic (not lumbar) splanchnics</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The superior mesenteric artery","sn":3,"pn":"3.1"},{"l":"ANAT-G4","h":"SMA syndrome and nutcracker syndrome","p":"<h4>The angle between the SMA and the aorta</h4>\n<p>The superior mesenteric artery (SMA) leaves the front of the aorta at the first lumbar vertebra (L1) and then runs down in front of it. The two arteries therefore form a narrow angle that opens downward, like a pair of jaws. Normally a pad of <b>mesenteric fat</b> fills the angle and holds the SMA away from the aorta, leaving room for what passes between them.</p>\n<p>Two structures cross through this angle. Higher up is the <b>left renal vein</b>, which must cross in front of the aorta to reach the inferior vena cava (IVC) on the right. Lower down is the <b>third part of the duodenum</b>, crossing the midline at the third lumbar vertebra (L3).</p>\n<p>Nothing else is trapped there. The left renal artery comes off the aorta itself, behind the vein. The right renal vein is short and enters the IVC directly, and the right renal artery passes behind the IVC, so neither crosses the aorta. The superior mesenteric vein (SMV) runs on the right of the SMA, and the IVC lies to the right of the aorta, both outside the angle. The portal vein forms higher, behind the neck of the pancreas, and runs up to the liver. No other gut artery makes this angle with the aorta. The celiac trunk arises higher, at the twelfth thoracic vertebra (T12), above the renal veins, and the inferior mesenteric artery (IMA) arises lower, at about L3, below the duodenum. The uncinate process of the pancreas lies behind the SMA too, but it does not grow to block the duodenum.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G005-98.jpg\" alt=\"Side view: mesenteric fat holds the superior mesenteric artery off the aorta\"></div><figcaption>Side view: mesenteric fat holds the superior mesenteric artery off the aorta <small>· 2026 lecture: Small &amp; Large Intestines, slide 98</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G005-99.jpg\" alt=\"Side view: without the fat, the artery pinches the duodenum against the aorta\"></div><figcaption>Side view: without the fat, the artery pinches the duodenum against the aorta <small>· 2026 lecture: Small &amp; Large Intestines, slide 99</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>SMA syndrome: the duodenum is pinched</h4>\n<p>In <b>SMA syndrome</b>, the third part of the duodenum is compressed between the SMA in front and the aorta behind. It is a blockage of the gut, so its signs come from food that cannot pass. It develops in steps.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Rapid weight loss, as in a very thin patient, shrinks the mesenteric fat pad.</li>\n<li>The angle between the SMA and the aorta narrows.</li>\n<li>The third part of the duodenum is pinched between them, so food cannot pass easily.</li>\n<li>After meals the patient has severe stabbing pain, from the blockage and from reversed peristalsis that tries to clear it. Early fullness (satiety), nausea and vomiting follow.</li>\n<li>The patient becomes afraid to eat (food fear), loses more weight and wastes, and the angle closes further.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The stomach upstream fills up, but it is not the part that is squeezed. Only the third part sits in the jaws. The first part lies at L1, above the SMA origin. The second part lies on the right, beside the IVC. The fourth part lies to the left of the aorta, past the angle.</p>\n<h4>Nutcracker syndrome: the left renal vein is pinched</h4>\n<p>In <b>nutcracker syndrome</b>, the SMA and the aorta act like the two arms of a nutcracker and squeeze the <b>left renal vein</b> from front to back. Blood backs up in the left kidney. This causes <b>blood in the urine (hematuria)</b> and flank or abdominal pain. The left gonadal vein drains into the left renal vein, so the back-pressure can also swell it, giving a left varicocele in a man. Weight loss raises the risk here too, because the same fat pad shrinks. Only the left renal vein is at risk, because the right renal vein never crosses the aorta.</p>\n<p>The two syndromes share one angle and one cause, so tell them apart by the structure squeezed. SMA syndrome blocks the duodenum and causes vomiting and fear of eating. Nutcracker syndrome blocks the left renal vein and causes blood in the urine.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>SMA leaves aorta at L1, runs down in front → narrow SMA–aorta angle</li>\n<li>Mesenteric fat pad holds SMA off aorta</li>\n<li>In angle: left renal vein (higher), 3rd part duodenum (lower, L3)</li>\n<li>Outside angle: left renal a., right renal v./a., SMV, IVC, portal v.</li>\n<li>Celiac too high (T12); IMA too low (≈L3)</li>\n<li>SMA syndrome: weight loss → fat lost → 3rd part crushed between SMA + aorta</li>\n<li>Signs: early satiety, nausea, vomiting, stabbing postprandial pain, food fear, wasting</li>\n<li>Nutcracker: SMA squeezes left renal vein against aorta → hematuria, flank pain</li>\n<li>Left gonadal v. → left renal v., so nutcracker → left varicocele</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The superior mesenteric artery","sn":3,"pn":"3.2"},{"l":"ANAT-G4","h":"Branches and the midgut territory","p":"<h4>Branches of the SMA, from top to bottom</h4>\n<p>The superior mesenteric artery (SMA) gives off its branches in a set order as it runs down and to the right in the root of the mesentery.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The <b>inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery</b> is the first branch. It runs to the right and splits into anterior and posterior branches for the lower head of the pancreas and the duodenum below the major papilla.</li>\n<li>The <b>middle colic artery</b> runs up into the transverse mesocolon to the transverse colon (5.1).</li>\n<li>The <b>jejunal and ileal (intestinal) arteries</b>, 15–18 of them, leave the left side of the SMA and run between the two layers of the mesentery.</li>\n<li>The <b>right colic artery</b> runs to the right, to the ascending colon.</li>\n<li>The <b>ileocolic artery</b> is the terminal branch. It runs down and to the right to the terminal ileum, the cecum, the appendix (through the appendicular artery) and the start of the ascending colon.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The intestinal arteries do not run straight to the gut. Neighboring branches join into loops called <b>arterial arcades</b>, and the arcades give off <b>vasa recta</b>, straight arteries that run into the gut wall. On an angiogram these straight vessels fan out from the arcades along the small bowel. The ileum has two or more rows of arcades; the jejunum usually has one (2.1).</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/D042-154.jpg\" alt=\"Superior mesenteric branches: line diagram beside a labeled angiogram\"></div><figcaption>Superior mesenteric branches: line diagram beside a labeled angiogram <small>· 2025 TA review, slide 154</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The midgut territory and its two borders</h4>\n<p>The SMA is the artery of the <b>midgut</b>. From proximal to distal it supplies the duodenum past the major duodenal papilla and the lower head of the pancreas, then the jejunum, ileum, cecum, appendix, ascending colon and the <b>proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon</b>. Each organ has a named branch. The inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery feeds the lower duodenum and pancreatic head, and the jejunal and ileal arteries feed the small bowel. The ileocolic feeds the terminal ileum and cecum, the appendicular feeds the appendix, the right colic and ileocolic feed the ascending colon, and the middle colic feeds the transverse colon.</p>\n<p>The territory has two borders, and both lie inside an organ rather than between organs. The <b>celiac–SMA border</b> is in the second (descending) part of the duodenum, just past the major duodenal papilla, where the bile duct opens; it is not between the first and second parts. Across it, the pancreaticoduodenal arcade links the two arteries (6.1). The <b>SMA–inferior mesenteric artery (IMA) border</b> is on the transverse colon, where its proximal two-thirds meets its distal one-third. There the marginal artery, which runs along the whole colon, links the two (6.2).</p>\n<p>These borders decide which organs suffer when the SMA is blocked near its origin. The <b>jejunum and ileum</b> lie far from both borders and have <b>no other source</b>, so they become ischemic first. The lower duodenum also gets celiac blood through the pancreaticoduodenal arcade, and the transverse colon gets IMA blood through the marginal artery. The descending and sigmoid colon are IMA territory, and the rectum gets IMA and internal iliac blood.</p>\n<h4>Veins</h4>\n<p>Jejunal, ileal and colic veins follow the arteries and drain into the <b>superior mesenteric vein (SMV)</b>. The SMV joins the splenic vein behind the neck of the pancreas to form the <b>portal vein</b>. Blood from the gut therefore goes to the liver first, and it reaches the inferior vena cava (IVC) only after passing through the liver. The renal vein drains only the kidney, and the azygos vein drains the back wall of the chest. The inferior mesenteric vein drains the hindgut, not the small bowel, and the hepatic veins carry blood out of the liver into the IVC; neither receives jejunal or ileal blood directly.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G005-49.jpg\" alt=\"Jejunal, ileal and colic veins draining to the superior mesenteric and portal veins\"></div><figcaption>Jejunal, ileal and colic veins draining to the superior mesenteric and portal veins <small>· 2026 lecture: Small &amp; Large Intestines, slide 49</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Chronic mesenteric ischemia (intestinal angina)</h4>\n<p>Atherosclerosis can slowly narrow the SMA, usually in an older patient. After a meal the gut needs more blood, and the narrowed artery cannot deliver it. Pain starts within about an hour of eating, so the patient comes to fear eating and loses weight. This is <b>chronic mesenteric ischemia</b>, also called intestinal angina. On a side-view (sagittal) computed tomography (CT) angiogram, the SMA is the vessel that leaves the front of the aorta just below the celiac trunk. SMA syndrome (3.2) also brings pain after meals and food fear, but there the artery is normal and it squeezes the duodenum, so vomiting and early fullness lead.</p>\n<p>Narrowing of the IMA alone is usually silent, because the marginal artery fills its territory from the SMA. A narrowed renal artery raises blood pressure instead, and a narrowed external iliac artery causes leg pain on walking. The inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery is only a small SMA branch.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>SMA branches: inferior pancreaticoduodenal (1st), middle colic, jejunal/ileal, right colic, ileocolic (terminal)</li>\n<li>15–18 jejunal/ileal arteries → arcades → vasa recta into wall</li>\n<li>Midgut: duodenum past major papilla → proximal 2/3 transverse colon</li>\n<li>Celiac–SMA border: 2nd part duodenum just past major papilla (pancreaticoduodenal arcade)</li>\n<li>SMA–IMA border: transverse colon at 2/3 : 1/3 junction (marginal artery)</li>\n<li>Jejunum/ileum: SMA only → first to go ischemic when SMA blocked</li>\n<li>Small-bowel veins → SMV + splenic v. behind pancreatic neck → portal v. → liver → IVC (not IMV, not straight to hepatic vv.)</li>\n<li>Intestinal angina: SMA atherosclerosis → pain after meals, food fear, weight loss</li>\n<li>IMA narrowing alone usually silent (marginal artery fills)</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The superior mesenteric artery","sn":3,"pn":"3.3"},{"l":"ANAT-G4","h":"Parts, peritoneum and markers","p":"<h4>The parts in order</h4>\n<p>The large intestine runs from the end of the ileum to the anus. Its <b>abdominal parts</b> are the cecum, the appendix, the ascending colon, the transverse colon, the descending colon and the upper sigmoid colon. Its <b>pelvic parts</b> are the <b>lower sigmoid colon, the rectum and the anal canal</b>. The sigmoid colon is split between the two: its upper part lies in the left iliac fossa, and only its lower part dips into the pelvis. The appendix counts as abdominal, even in the people whose tip hangs over the pelvic brim.</p>\n<p>Follow the colon as a frame around the small intestine. The <b>cecum</b> is a blind pouch below the ileocecal junction, in the right iliac fossa. The <b>ascending colon</b> climbs the right side of the posterior abdominal wall from the cecum to the liver, where it turns at the right colic (<b>hepatic</b>) flexure. The <b>transverse colon</b> runs across the abdomen from the hepatic flexure to the left colic (<b>splenic</b>) flexure. The <b>descending colon</b> runs down the left posterior wall from the splenic flexure to the sigmoid colon, passing in front of the lateral border of the left kidney. The <b>sigmoid colon</b> is an S-shaped loop of variable length. It runs from the left iliac fossa to the third sacral vertebra (S3), where it becomes the rectum.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G005-57.jpg\" alt=\"Large intestine with teniae coli, haustra and fatty appendices\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G005-57c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Large intestine with teniae coli, haustra and fatty appendices\"></div></div><figcaption>Large intestine with teniae coli, haustra and fatty appendices <small>· 2026 lecture: Small &amp; Large Intestines, slide 57</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Three markers of the colon</h4>\n<p>Three features tell the colon from the small intestine. The <b>teniae coli</b> are three narrow bands of the outer longitudinal muscle, which in the colon is gathered into strips instead of forming a full layer. Because the teniae are shorter than the colon, they pucker the wall into pouches called <b>haustra</b>. Small fatty tags called <b>appendices epiploicae</b> (epiploic appendages) hang along the surface.</p>\n<p>Two parts lack some of these. The <b>appendix has none of the three</b>, because its longitudinal muscle forms a complete layer. The <b>rectum has no teniae</b>, because the teniae spread out into a complete layer where the sigmoid colon becomes the rectum. The transverse, descending and sigmoid colon have all three.</p>\n<h4>Which parts hang free and which are fixed</h4>\n<p>An <b>intraperitoneal</b> segment is wrapped in peritoneum, usually hangs on a mesentery, and can move. A <b>secondarily retroperitoneal</b> segment had a mesentery in the embryo, but as the gut rotated into place that mesentery fused to the posterior abdominal wall. The segment is then fixed behind the peritoneum. A <b>primarily retroperitoneal</b> organ, such as the kidney, formed behind the peritoneum and never had a mesentery; the ascending and descending colon are not of this kind.</p>\n<p>The colon alternates between the two. The <b>cecum</b> is intraperitoneal, almost fully covered by peritoneum, but it has no mesentery. The <b>ascending colon</b> is <b>secondarily retroperitoneal</b> and fixed on the right posterior wall. The <b>transverse colon</b> is intraperitoneal on the <b>transverse mesocolon</b>, and it is the largest and <b>most mobile</b> part of the colon. The <b>descending colon</b> is secondarily retroperitoneal and fixed on the left, though about one-third of people keep a short mesentery. The <b>sigmoid colon</b> is intraperitoneal on the long <b>sigmoid mesocolon</b>, so it is very mobile and prone to twisting (volvulus). It is the <b>commonest site of volvulus in adults</b>. The ascending and descending colon are fixed and cannot twist, the stomach rarely does, and twisting of the whole small bowel (midgut volvulus) is mainly a problem of infants with malrotation. The <b>rectum</b> has no mesentery and is fixed in the pelvis. A segment that is secondarily retroperitoneal and lies on the right posterior wall is the ascending colon; its left-side partner is the descending colon.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/D041-210.jpg\" alt=\"Intraperitoneal jejunum on its mesentery beside the retroperitoneal ascending colon\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/D041-210c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Intraperitoneal jejunum on its mesentery beside the retroperitoneal ascending colon\"></div></div><figcaption>Intraperitoneal jejunum on its mesentery beside the retroperitoneal ascending colon <small>· 2025 TA review, slide 210</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G005-100.jpg\" alt=\"Volvulus: a loop of colon twisted on its own mesentery\"></div><figcaption>Volvulus: a loop of colon twisted on its own mesentery <small>· 2026 lecture: Small &amp; Large Intestines, slide 100</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Behind the sigmoid mesocolon</h4>\n<p>The root of the sigmoid mesocolon is shaped like an upside-down V. Deep to its apex, behind the peritoneum, lie the <b>left ureter</b> and the point where the <b>left common iliac artery divides</b> into the internal and external iliac arteries. A surgeon removing a sigmoid tumor works right over them, so the left ureter and the external iliac artery are at risk. The superior rectal artery runs inside the mesocolon, not behind its apex. The inferior mesenteric artery arises much higher, at about the third lumbar vertebra. The testicular artery runs down on the psoas, lateral to this spot, and the obturator artery lies deep in the pelvis on its side wall.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Abdominal: cecum, appendix, ascending, transverse, descending, upper sigmoid</li>\n<li>Pelvic: lower sigmoid, rectum, anal canal</li>\n<li>Teniae coli (3 longitudinal bands) → haustra; epiploic appendages = fat tags</li>\n<li>Appendix: none of the three; rectum: no teniae</li>\n<li>Ascending: cecum → hepatic flexure; transverse: hepatic → splenic; descending: splenic → sigmoid</li>\n<li>Sigmoid: left iliac fossa → S3 → rectum</li>\n<li>Cecum: intraperitoneal, no mesentery; ascending + descending: secondarily (not primarily) retroperitoneal, fixed</li>\n<li>Transverse: transverse mesocolon, largest + most mobile; rectum: no mesentery</li>\n<li>Sigmoid: long mesocolon, very mobile → commonest adult volvulus (ascending, descending fixed)</li>\n<li>Behind sigmoid mesocolon apex: left ureter + left common iliac split (external iliac)</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The large intestine","sn":4,"pn":"4.1"},{"l":"ANAT-G4","h":"Cecum, appendix and lower abdominal pain","p":"<h4>Cecum and ileocecal valve</h4>\n<p>The <b>cecum</b> is the blind pouch at the start of the large intestine, about 7.5 cm wide, in the right iliac fossa. It has no mesentery, although folds of peritoneum (cecal folds) may bind it to the wall. The ileum enters it from the left and pokes into it, forming the <b>ileocecal valve</b>. This valve does not prevent reflux.</p>\n<p>The <b>terminal ileum is the narrowest part</b> of the small intestine, so the ileocecal valve is the commonest site of intestinal blockage. In a child, the ileum can telescope into the cecum (ileocolic intussusception). In an adult, a gallstone that has eroded into the gut can lodge here. Other sites block less often. The jejunum has a wide lumen. Duodenal blockage is mainly a problem of newborns (atresia). A blocked appendix produces appendicitis, not bowel obstruction. The splenic flexure is known for poor blood flow, not for blockage (6.2).</p>\n<h4>The appendix and its positions</h4>\n<p>The <b>vermiform appendix</b> arises from the <b>posteromedial side of the cecum</b>, below the ileocecal junction. Its base is fixed there in everyone, but its free tip can point in different directions. It has a short mesentery from the cecum, the <b>mesoappendix</b>, which carries the appendicular artery. The appendix is midgut, so this artery is a branch of the ileocolic artery from the superior mesenteric artery, not of the celiac trunk or the inferior mesenteric artery (5.1).</p>\n<p>The usual position is <b>retrocecal</b>, behind the cecum: about 64% of people, roughly two-thirds. Next comes <b>pelvic</b>, hanging over the pelvic brim, in about 32%. The rest are rare: subcecal (below the cecum) about 2%, pre-ileal (in front of the terminal ileum) about 1%, and post-ileal (behind the terminal ileum) about 0.5%, the rarest. A subhepatic appendix, up under the liver, occurs only when the cecum failed to descend. Medial to the cecum is where the base attaches, not a position of the appendix.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G005-60.jpg\" alt=\"Ileocecal valve, appendix opening, and appendix positions with their frequencies\"></div><figcaption>Ileocecal valve, appendix opening, and appendix positions with their frequencies <small>· 2026 lecture: Small &amp; Large Intestines, slide 60</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Appendicitis and McBurney's point</h4>\n<p>Appendicitis is acute inflammation of the appendix. Its pain moves in a typical order.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Pain starts as a vague ache around the umbilicus. The appendix is midgut, and its visceral pain fibers reach the tenth thoracic cord segment (T10), whose skin band circles the umbilicus.</li>\n<li>As the inflamed appendix irritates the parietal peritoneum next to it, the pain moves to the right lower quadrant (RLQ) and becomes sharp and well localized.</li>\n<li>Tenderness and rebound are greatest at <b>McBurney's point</b>, one-third of the way along the line from the right anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS) to the umbilicus. Measured from the other end, it is two-thirds of the way from the umbilicus to the ASIS, and it lies over the base of the appendix. It is measured from the ASIS, not from the anterior inferior iliac spine (AIIS) just below it, and it is one-third of the way, not halfway.</li>\n<li>A low fever and a raised white cell count often follow. If the appendix ruptures, the result is an acute abdomen and peritonitis.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Other surface points mark other places. The midpoint between the costal margin and the umbilicus, and the midclavicular line at the subcostal plane, are in the upper abdomen. The point midway between the two ASISs is in the midline, above the bladder. The terminal ileum and cecum lie near McBurney's point, but the point marks the appendix.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G005-91.jpg\" alt=\"McBurney&#x27;s point (1), one-third from anterior superior iliac spine (3) toward umbilicus (2)\"></div><figcaption>McBurney&#x27;s point (1), one-third from anterior superior iliac spine (3) toward umbilicus (2) <small>· 2026 lecture: Small &amp; Large Intestines, slide 91</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Other causes of lower abdominal pain</h4>\n<p>Several bowel diseases differ from appendicitis, mainly in where the pain is and how it behaves. <b>Crohn disease</b> is an autoimmune inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) with patchy lesions that can affect <b>any part of the gut, from mouth to anus</b>, not only the small or only the large intestine. It most often hits the terminal ileum, so it typically causes RLQ pain, but it is chronic and relapsing, with diarrhea, not a single acute attack. <b>Ulcerative colitis</b>, the other IBD, stays in the large intestine. It usually affects the rectum and sigmoid colon in one continuous stretch and causes left lower quadrant (LLQ) pain. It does not form outpouchings. <b>Irritable bowel syndrome (IBS)</b> is not an IBD: it causes pain, bloating and changed bowel habits, but the bowel shows no inflammation, outpouchings or other damage.</p>\n<p><b>Diverticulosis</b> means many small outpouchings (diverticula) of the colon wall, pushed out by high pressure inside the colon, as with chronic constipation and a low-fiber diet. They are most common in the <b>sigmoid colon</b> and are painless while quiet. When they become inflamed and infected, the patient has <b>diverticulitis</b>, with LLQ pain and fever. A twist of a loop on its mesentery is a volvulus instead (4.1). Two other conditions point elsewhere. Superior mesenteric artery (SMA) syndrome causes upper abdominal pain after meals with weight loss (3.2), and megacolon is a hugely dilated colon with constipation (7.2).</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G005-94.jpg\" alt=\"Healthy colon wall, diverticulosis, and inflamed diverticulitis\"></div><figcaption>Healthy colon wall, diverticulosis, and inflamed diverticulitis <small>· 2026 lecture: Small &amp; Large Intestines, slide 94</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Cecum: blind pouch, ≈7.5 cm wide, right iliac fossa, no mesentery</li>\n<li>Ileocecal valve: ileum pokes into cecum; does not prevent reflux</li>\n<li>Terminal ileum narrowest → ileocecal valve commonest block (intussusception, gallstone)</li>\n<li>Appendix base: posteromedial cecum, below ileocecal junction; mesoappendix carries appendicular a. (ileocolic, from SMA: midgut)</li>\n<li>Retrocecal ≈64% &gt; pelvic ≈32% &gt; subcecal 2% &gt; pre-ileal 1% &gt; post-ileal 0.5%</li>\n<li>Appendicitis: periumbilical (T10) → RLQ once parietal peritoneum irritated; rupture → peritonitis</li>\n<li>McBurney's point: 1/3 from right ASIS (not AIIS, not halfway) to umbilicus, over appendix base</li>\n<li>Crohn: patchy, mouth to anus, mostly terminal ileum, RLQ, chronic; ulcerative colitis: colon only, rectum + sigmoid, LLQ</li>\n<li>IBS: pain + changed bowel habits, no structural damage (not an IBD)</li>\n<li>Diverticulosis: sigmoid outpouchings (low fiber, constipation), painless; diverticulitis: inflamed/infected → LLQ pain + fever</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The large intestine","sn":4,"pn":"4.2"},{"l":"ANAT-G4","h":"SMA branches to the right colon","p":"<p>Three branches of the superior mesenteric artery (SMA) supply the right half of the colon. From bottom to top they are the <b>ileocolic</b>, <b>right colic</b> and <b>middle colic arteries</b>. All three join the marginal artery that runs along the colon (6.2). On an SMA angiogram the jejunal and ileal arteries fan out to the left, while the three colic branches leave on the right. The middle colic goes up, the right colic goes straight to the right, and the ileocolic goes down and to the right.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G005-84.jpg\" alt=\"Superior mesenteric angiogram: middle colic up, right colic right, ileocolic down-right\"></div><figcaption>Superior mesenteric angiogram: middle colic up, right colic right, ileocolic down-right <small>· 2026 lecture: Small &amp; Large Intestines, slide 84</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Ileocolic artery: the last branch</h4>\n<p>The <b>ileocolic artery</b> is the terminal branch of the SMA. It runs down and to the right, behind the peritoneum, along the root of the mesentery to the right iliac fossa. There it gives an ileal branch to the terminal ileum, a colic branch to the start of the ascending colon (which joins the right colic), <b>anterior and posterior cecal arteries</b> to the cecum, and the <b>appendicular artery</b>.</p>\n<p>The appendicular artery runs in the mesoappendix and is the <b>only artery of the appendix</b>. The appendix therefore hangs at the end of a chain: SMA → ileocolic artery → appendicular artery. The appendicular artery is the vessel tied in an appendectomy. Tying its parent, the ileocolic, also cuts off the appendix. Tying the SMA itself would starve the whole small bowel. The epigastric arteries run in the abdominal wall, not to the gut, and the celiac trunk stops at the second part of the duodenum.</p>\n<p>The cecum and appendix are midgut in every way. Their vein is the ileocolic vein, which drains to the superior mesenteric vein (SMV). Their lymph goes to the ileocolic nodes. Their nerves come from the superior mesenteric plexus: parasympathetic fibers from the vagus and sympathetic fibers from the lower thoracic spinal cord segments. Removing the cecum therefore cuts the ileocolic artery and vagal fibers. The middle colic artery serves the transverse colon, and the left colic and sigmoid arteries and the pelvic splanchnic nerves serve the left colon.</p>\n<h4>Right colic artery</h4>\n<p>The <b>right colic artery</b> runs straight to the right, behind the peritoneum, to the ascending colon. It may arise from the ileocolic artery instead of the SMA. Together with the ileocolic, it supplies the <b>ascending colon</b>, and it reaches the right colic (<b>hepatic</b>) flexure. The hepatic flexure sits under the liver, <b>deep to the 9th and 10th ribs</b>. It is midgut, so its blood comes from the SMA, not from the celiac trunk or the inferior mesenteric artery (IMA).</p>\n<h4>Middle colic artery</h4>\n<p>The <b>middle colic artery</b> leaves the SMA just below the pancreas and runs <b>up into the transverse mesocolon</b>. There it splits into a right branch and a left branch. The right branch runs toward the hepatic flexure and joins the right colic. The left branch runs toward the splenic flexure and joins the left colic of the IMA. Between them they supply the proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon. On an angiogram it is the SMA branch that climbs upward and then divides to both sides. An artery feeding the transverse colon near the hepatic flexure is its right branch. The left colic belongs to the IMA and serves the splenic flexure and descending colon.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Right colon: ileocolic, right colic, middle colic (all SMA) → join marginal artery</li>\n<li>Angiogram: jejunal/ileal fan left; middle colic up, right colic right, ileocolic down-right</li>\n<li>Ileocolic = terminal SMA branch: ileal, colic, ant./post. cecal, appendicular</li>\n<li>Appendix: SMA → ileocolic → appendicular (mesoappendix; only artery; tied in appendectomy)</li>\n<li>Cecum/appendix: ileocolic v. → SMV; ileocolic nodes; vagus + lower thoracic sympathetics</li>\n<li>Right colic → ascending colon + hepatic flexure (may arise from ileocolic)</li>\n<li>Hepatic flexure: under liver, deep to ribs 9–10, SMA</li>\n<li>Middle colic: up into transverse mesocolon → R + L branches → proximal 2/3 transverse</li>\n<li>Middle colic R branch → hepatic flexure; L branch meets left colic (IMA)</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Arteries of the colon","sn":5,"pn":"5.1"},{"l":"ANAT-G4","h":"Midgut versus hindgut supply","p":"<h4>Three gut regions, three arteries</h4>\n<p>The gut tube develops as three regions: foregut, midgut and hindgut. Each region keeps its own artery and its own parasympathetic nerve, so one border line explains blood and nerves together.</p>\n<p>The <b>foregut</b> runs from the lower esophagus to the second part of the duodenum, as far as the major papilla, and also includes the liver, pancreas and spleen. Its artery is the <b>celiac trunk</b> and its parasympathetic nerve is the vagus. The <b>midgut</b> runs from the rest of the duodenum to the <b>proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon</b>. Its artery is the <b>superior mesenteric artery (SMA)</b>, and its parasympathetic nerve is again the vagus. The <b>hindgut</b> runs from the <b>distal one-third of the transverse colon</b> to the upper rectum. Its artery is the <b>inferior mesenteric artery (IMA)</b>, and its parasympathetic nerves are the pelvic splanchnic nerves, from sacral segments 2–4 (S2–S4).</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G005-46.jpg\" alt=\"Colon arteries: superior mesenteric branches on the right, inferior mesenteric on the left\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G005-46c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Colon arteries: superior mesenteric branches on the right, inferior mesenteric on the left\"></div></div><figcaption>Colon arteries: superior mesenteric branches on the right, inferior mesenteric on the left <small>· 2026 lecture: Small &amp; Large Intestines, slide 46</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The colon, segment by segment</h4>\n<p>Walk along the gut and name the artery at each step. The jejunum and ileum get the jejunal and ileal (intestinal) arteries. The terminal ileum, cecum and appendix get the ileocolic artery, with its cecal and appendicular branches. The ascending colon gets the ileocolic and right colic arteries, and the hepatic flexure gets the right colic. The inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery supplies the head of the pancreas and the lower duodenum. All of these are SMA branches.</p>\n<p>The <b>transverse colon is where the switch happens</b>. Its proximal two-thirds gets the <b>middle colic artery</b> from the SMA, and its distal one-third gets the <b>left colic artery</b> from the IMA. Three arteries reach the transverse colon: right colic, middle colic and left colic, because the right colic also reaches its hepatic end. Past the switch, everything is IMA. The descending colon gets the left colic and superior sigmoid arteries, the sigmoid colon gets the sigmoid arteries, and the upper rectum gets the superior rectal artery. The ileocolic artery never reaches the sigmoid colon; it stays in the right iliac fossa.</p>\n<h4>Reading an injury from the border</h4>\n<p>A lesion of one artery or nerve damages only its own side of the border. When the IMA is cut or blocked, the ascending colon keeps full flow, since only SMA branches feed it. The distal transverse, descending and sigmoid colon lose their main supply, although the marginal artery can partly refill them (6.2). Ischemia of the sigmoid colon, with left lower quadrant pain, points to the IMA and its sigmoid branches. An injury to both the SMA and the vagus affects the ascending colon and most of the transverse colon. The descending and sigmoid colon, with their IMA and pelvic splanchnic supply, are spared. The renal and gonadal arteries never supply gut; they go only to the kidney and to the testis or ovary.</p>\n<p>The nerves follow the same split. The vagus serves the SMA territory, and the pelvic splanchnic nerves serve the IMA territory (7.2).</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Foregut: lower esophagus → 2nd part duodenum (papilla) + liver, pancreas, spleen; celiac; vagus</li>\n<li>Midgut: rest of duodenum → proximal 2/3 transverse colon; SMA; vagus</li>\n<li>Hindgut: distal 1/3 transverse colon → upper rectum; IMA; pelvic splanchnics (S2–S4)</li>\n<li>SMA: intestinal (jejunum, ileum), ileocolic (cecum, appendix), right colic (ascending, hepatic flexure)</li>\n<li>Transverse colon: proximal 2/3 middle colic (SMA); distal 1/3 left colic (IMA)</li>\n<li>IMA: left colic + superior sigmoid (descending), sigmoid aa. (sigmoid), superior rectal (upper rectum)</li>\n<li>Inferior pancreaticoduodenal (SMA) → pancreatic head; ileocolic never reaches sigmoid</li>\n<li>IMA lost → ascending colon spared; SMA + vagus lost → ascending + transverse hit</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Arteries of the colon","sn":5,"pn":"5.2"},{"l":"ANAT-G4","h":"The inferior mesenteric artery","p":"<p>The <b>inferior mesenteric artery (IMA)</b> is the artery of the hindgut. It leaves the front of the aorta at about the third lumbar vertebra (L3). It is the lowest of the three gut arteries, and it runs down and to the left behind the peritoneum. An aortic aneurysm at L3–L4 therefore involves its origin. The other aortic branches lie higher: the celiac trunk at the twelfth thoracic vertebra (T12), the superior mesenteric artery (SMA) at L1, the renal arteries at L1–L2 and the gonadal arteries at L2. The aorta divides into the common iliac arteries at L4.</p>\n<h4>Three branches, from top to bottom</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>The <b>left colic artery</b> is the first branch. It runs to the left, and on an angiogram it climbs up and outward toward the descending colon. It divides into an ascending branch, for the distal transverse colon and the splenic flexure, and a descending branch, for the descending colon.</li>\n<li>The <b>sigmoid arteries</b>, two or three of them, run down and to the left in the sigmoid mesocolon to the sigmoid colon. The highest one, the superior sigmoid artery, also helps supply the descending colon.</li>\n<li>The <b>superior rectal artery</b> is the continuation of the IMA. It runs straight down into the pelvis and divides into two branches along the sides of the rectum. These anastomose with the <b>middle rectal arteries</b>, which come from the internal iliac artery.</li>\n</ol>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G005-85.jpg\" alt=\"Inferior mesenteric arteriogram: left colic branches, sigmoid arteries, superior rectal artery\"></div><figcaption>Inferior mesenteric arteriogram: left colic branches, sigmoid arteries, superior rectal artery <small>· 2026 lecture: Small &amp; Large Intestines, slide 85</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/D042-158.jpg\" alt=\"Inferior mesenteric branches: line diagram beside a labeled angiogram\"></div><figcaption>Inferior mesenteric branches: line diagram beside a labeled angiogram <small>· 2025 TA review, slide 158</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The inferior rectal artery is not an IMA branch. It comes from the internal pudendal artery, the artery of the perineum, and supplies the anal canal below the pectinate line. The superior rectal artery anastomoses with it as well. The vasa recta are the tiny straight end-vessels that enter the bowel wall, not a named IMA branch. There are no pelvic splanchnic arteries; the pelvic splanchnics are nerves.</p>\n<h4>Descending and sigmoid colon</h4>\n<p>The <b>left colic and superior sigmoid arteries</b> and their veins supply the descending colon. The sigmoid colon is fed by the sigmoid arteries. Reduced flow to the descending colon, with left lower quadrant (LLQ) pain, therefore involves the left colic and superior sigmoid arteries. The middle colic and right colic belong to the SMA on the right, and the superior rectal artery lies lower, at the rectum.</p>\n<p>The veins follow the arteries into the <b>inferior mesenteric vein (IMV)</b>. The IMV runs up on the left and joins the splenic vein (or the superior mesenteric vein, SMV) on the way to the portal vein.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G005-71.jpg\" alt=\"Colon veins: superior and inferior mesenteric veins draining to the portal vein\"></div><figcaption>Colon veins: superior and inferior mesenteric veins draining to the portal vein <small>· 2026 lecture: Small &amp; Large Intestines, slide 71</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Paraduodenal hernia</h4>\n<p>To the left of the fourth part of the duodenum there can be a large peritoneal fold, the paraduodenal fold, with a pocket behind it, the <b>paraduodenal fossa</b>. A loop of small bowel can slip into this fossa and strangulate; this is a <b>paraduodenal hernia</b>. The bowel enters the fossa. It does not pass through the greater omentum (the apron hanging from the stomach), the lesser omentum (stomach to liver) or the root of the mesentery. The IMV, the IMA and the left colic artery run close to the fold, so a surgeon must avoid them during the repair.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>IMA: hindgut artery; front of aorta at about L3</li>\n<li>Levels: celiac T12, SMA L1, renal L1–L2, gonadal L2, IMA ≈L3, iliac split L4</li>\n<li>Left colic = 1st branch; ascending (splenic flexure) + descending branches; up-left on angiogram</li>\n<li>Sigmoid arteries: 2–3, down-left in sigmoid mesocolon → sigmoid colon</li>\n<li>Superior rectal = IMA continuation → anastomoses with middle rectal (internal iliac)</li>\n<li>Inferior rectal: internal pudendal → anal canal below pectinate line</li>\n<li>Descending colon: left colic + superior sigmoid; sigmoid colon: sigmoid arteries</li>\n<li>Veins → IMV → splenic v. (or SMV) → portal v.</li>\n<li>Paraduodenal hernia: bowel into paraduodenal fossa, left of 4th part (not omenta, not mesentery root); IMV, IMA, left colic at risk</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Arteries of the colon","sn":5,"pn":"5.3"},{"l":"ANAT-G4","h":"Marginal artery and arc of Riolan","p":"<p>Two vessels link the superior mesenteric artery (SMA) with the inferior mesenteric artery (IMA), so that blood can cross the midgut–hindgut border on the transverse colon.</p>\n<h4>Marginal artery (of Drummond)</h4>\n<p>The <b>marginal artery</b> runs in the mesentery close to the bowel, along the inner (mesenteric) edge of the whole colon. It is built from the colic arteries themselves. Each colic artery divides near the bowel, and its branches join those of its neighbors into one continuous chain. On the SMA side the feeders are the <b>ileocolic, right colic and middle colic</b> arteries. On the IMA side they are the <b>left colic and sigmoid</b> arteries. The chain runs from the ileocolic artery all the way around the colon to the sigmoid branches, and short straight arteries (arteriae rectae) leave it to enter the colon wall.</p>\n<p>The marginal artery is <b>almost always present</b>; its absence should be treated as a variant. That makes it the <b>main collateral</b> between the SMA and the IMA. The rectal arteries form a separate system. The middle rectal arteries (from the internal iliac) and the inferior rectal arteries (from the internal pudendal) join the superior rectal on the rectum, but they do not feed the marginal artery.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G005-47.jpg\" alt=\"Marginal artery along the whole colon, joining superior and inferior mesenteric branches\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G005-47c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Marginal artery along the whole colon, joining superior and inferior mesenteric branches\"></div></div><figcaption>Marginal artery along the whole colon, joining superior and inferior mesenteric branches <small>· 2026 lecture: Small &amp; Large Intestines, slide 47</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Arc of Riolan</h4>\n<p>The <b>arc of Riolan</b> is also called the meandering mesenteric artery or the central anastomotic mesenteric artery. It is a short loop that runs <b>near the root of the mesentery</b>, away from the bowel wall. It is classically described as joining the <b>middle colic</b> branch of the SMA to the <b>left colic</b> branch of the IMA. It is <b>not always present</b>. When it is present, it is an important link between the SMA and the IMA if either is blocked or badly narrowed.</p>\n<p>The two links therefore differ in three ways. The marginal artery hugs the bowel, while the arc of Riolan lies near the mesenteric root. The marginal artery joins every colic artery, while the arc joins only the middle colic and left colic. The marginal artery is almost always there, while the arc often is not.</p>\n<h4>Flow when one source fails</h4>\n<p>Arterial patterns vary from body to body, and a colic artery may be tied in surgery or simply be absent. When one colic artery is missing, its neighbors fill its segment through the marginal artery. A missing right colic artery, for example, leaves the ascending colon perfused from the ileocolic and middle colic arteries by way of the marginal chain. A single blocked vessel therefore rarely starves the transverse colon. This protection comes from the arterial chain, not from the transverse colon's position (it is intraperitoneal) or from its veins, which only carry blood away. The appendicular artery, by contrast, is an end artery to the appendix alone.</p>\n<p>When the <b>IMA is blocked at its origin</b>, all its own branches (left colic, sigmoid and superior rectal) lie beyond the block and have no flow of their own. Blood comes instead from the SMA: middle colic → marginal artery and arc of Riolan → left colic territory → descending colon.</p>\n<p>When the <b>SMA is blocked near its origin</b>, its own branches, the middle colic included, lie beyond the block. The middle colic then receives blood rather than giving it. Blood comes from the IMA: left colic → marginal artery and arc of Riolan → middle colic branches, filled backward → transverse colon. The celiac trunk does not help here, because its only link to the SMA is the pancreaticoduodenal arcade at the duodenum (6.1).</p>\n<h4>The splenic flexure</h4>\n<p>The SMA and IMA territories meet near the splenic (left colic) flexure, at the far ends of both arterial trees. Collateral flow is thinnest there, so this region is prone to ischemia. The arc of Riolan matters most there.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Marginal artery (Drummond): mesenteric edge of whole colon, close to bowel</li>\n<li>Feeders: ileocolic, right colic, middle colic (SMA) + left colic, sigmoid (IMA)</li>\n<li>Runs ileocolic → sigmoid branches; almost always present → main SMA–IMA collateral</li>\n<li>Not part: middle rectal (internal iliac), inferior rectal (internal pudendal)</li>\n<li>Arc of Riolan: near mesenteric root; middle colic ↔ left colic; often absent</li>\n<li>One colic artery missing → neighbors fill its segment via marginal artery</li>\n<li>IMA blocked → middle colic → marginal/Riolan → left colic → descending colon</li>\n<li>SMA blocked → IMA → left colic → marginal/Riolan → middle colic (backward) → transverse</li>\n<li>Splenic flexure: SMA–IMA meeting point, thinnest collaterals → ischemia; Riolan key</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Collateral circulation","sn":6,"pn":"6.2"},{"l":"ANAT-G4","h":"Lymph drainage","p":"<p>Lymph from the gut follows its arteries back toward the aorta. Each group of nodes sits along an artery, so the artery that feeds a segment tells you where its lymph goes.</p>\n<h4>Small intestine: the path of absorbed fat</h4>\n<p>Fat absorbed by the small intestine does not enter the blood capillaries. It enters the lymph instead, which turns milky and is then called chyle. The path runs in steps.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Absorbed fat enters <b>lacteals</b>, blind lymph vessels in the center of each villus.</li>\n<li>The lacteals drain to lymph plexuses in the wall of the jejunum and ileum.</li>\n<li>Lymph vessels pass between the two layers of the mesentery to the mesenteric nodes, some close to the gut and some farther in.</li>\n<li>From there lymph reaches the <b>superior mesenteric nodes</b> at the root of the superior mesenteric artery (SMA); lymph from the terminal ileum goes to the ileocolic nodes on the way.</li>\n<li>The intestinal lymph trunk carries it to the <b>cisterna chyli</b>, the lymph sac at the start of the thoracic duct.</li>\n<li>The thoracic duct runs up through the chest and empties where the left subclavian and left internal jugular veins meet, not into the superior vena cava.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Absorbed sugars and amino acids take a different road. They travel in the blood of the portal vein to the liver. The azygos vein drains the back of the chest wall and plays no part in gut lymph. The right lymphatic duct drains only the right side of the head, neck and chest and the right arm, so gut lymph never enters it.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G005-53.jpg\" alt=\"Small-bowel lymph: mesenteric nodes, superior mesenteric nodes, cisterna chyli, thoracic duct\"></div><figcaption>Small-bowel lymph: mesenteric nodes, superior mesenteric nodes, cisterna chyli, thoracic duct <small>· 2026 lecture: Small &amp; Large Intestines, slide 53</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/D041-204.jpg\" alt=\"Villus structure with central lacteal, plicae circulares and small-intestine regions\"></div><figcaption>Villus structure with central lacteal, plicae circulares and small-intestine regions <small>· 2025 TA review, slide 204</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Colon: four steps of nodes</h4>\n<p>Lymph from the colon passes through four groups of nodes in a fixed order, from the bowel wall toward the aorta. Cancer cells spread through them in the same order, so the first group is the first place a colon cancer reaches. The first step is the <b>epicolic nodes</b>, on the gut wall itself. The second is the <b>paracolic nodes</b>, along the mesenteric border beside the marginal artery. The third is the <b>intermediate colic nodes</b>, along the named colic arteries, such as the middle colic nodes. The fourth is the <b>principal nodes</b>, the superior or inferior mesenteric nodes at the origin of each artery. From there lymph goes on to the intestinal trunks and the para-aortic nodes.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G005-51.jpg\" alt=\"Colon lymph nodes: epicolic, paracolic, middle colic and inferior mesenteric groups\"></div><figcaption>Colon lymph nodes: epicolic, paracolic, middle colic and inferior mesenteric groups <small>· 2026 lecture: Small &amp; Large Intestines, slide 51</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Segment by segment</h4>\n<p>The <b>duodenum</b> sits on the border between two arteries, so its lymph goes two ways. It drains first to pancreaticoduodenal nodes, along the pancreaticoduodenal arteries, and to pyloric nodes, along the gastroduodenal artery. From these it reaches both the <b>celiac nodes</b> and the <b>superior mesenteric nodes</b>. The <b>jejunum and ileum</b> drain through the mesenteric nodes to the superior mesenteric nodes. The <b>cecum and appendix</b>, fed by the ileocolic artery, drain to the <b>ileocolic nodes</b>.</p>\n<p>Every part of the colon uses its epicolic and paracolic nodes as the first stop. The <b>ascending colon</b> drains first to these. The <b>transverse colon</b> drains to the <b>middle colic nodes</b>, along its own artery. The <b>descending and sigmoid colon</b> share one chain that follows the inferior mesenteric artery (IMA): epicolic and paracolic nodes, then intermediate colic nodes along the left colic and sigmoid arteries, then the <b>inferior mesenteric nodes</b>. Ileocolic and superior mesenteric nodes belong to the right colon, and celiac nodes to the foregut.</p>\n<p>Other node groups drain other regions. Inguinal nodes drain the leg, the perineum and the lower anal canal. Internal iliac nodes drain the pelvic organs. Para-aortic (lumbar) nodes drain the kidneys, gonads and body wall. Hepatic nodes drain the liver region, and apical axillary nodes drain the arm and breast.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Gut lymph follows the arteries back to the aorta</li>\n<li>Fat → lacteals (villus) → wall plexus → mesenteric nodes → superior mesenteric nodes</li>\n<li>→ intestinal trunk → cisterna chyli → thoracic duct → left subclavian–internal jugular junction (never the right lymphatic duct)</li>\n<li>Duodenum: pancreaticoduodenal + pyloric → celiac + superior mesenteric nodes</li>\n<li>Terminal ileum, cecum, appendix → ileocolic nodes</li>\n<li>Colon: epicolic (wall) → paracolic (marginal a.) → intermediate colic → principal (SMA/IMA) nodes</li>\n<li>Ascending: epicolic/paracolic first; transverse: middle colic nodes</li>\n<li>Descending + sigmoid: epicolic/paracolic → intermediate colic → inferior mesenteric nodes</li>\n<li>Inguinal: leg, perineum, lower anal canal; internal iliac: pelvis; para-aortic: kidney, gonad, wall</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Lymph and nerves","sn":7,"pn":"7.1"},{"l":"ANAT-G4","h":"Autonomic nerves and Hirschsprung disease","p":"<p>Autonomic nerves reach the gut as networks (plexuses) wrapped around its arteries. They therefore follow the same split as the arteries: celiac, superior mesenteric and inferior mesenteric territories.</p>\n<h4>Splanchnic nerves: which are sympathetic</h4>\n<p>Splanchnic simply means visceral. Most splanchnic nerves are <b>sympathetic</b>. The greater splanchnic nerve comes from thoracic segments 5–9 (T5–T9), the lesser from T10–T11, the least from T12, and the lumbar splanchnic nerves from the first and second lumbar segments (L1–L2). Sympathetic outflow from the cord stops at L2, so no sympathetic fibers leave the lower lumbar segments. The only splanchnic nerves that carry <b>parasympathetic</b> fibers are the <b>pelvic splanchnic nerves</b>, from sacral segments 2–4 (S2–S4).</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G005-78.jpg\" alt=\"Splanchnic nerves (left); vagal trunks and pelvic splanchnic nerves (right)\"></div><figcaption>Splanchnic nerves (left); vagal trunks and pelvic splanchnic nerves (right) <small>· 2026 lecture: Small &amp; Large Intestines, slide 78</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Small intestine</h4>\n<p>The jejunum and ileum are supplied by the <b>superior mesenteric plexus</b>, which carries both kinds of fibers. Its parasympathetic fibers come from the <b>posterior vagal trunk</b>; the anterior vagal trunk goes mainly to the stomach and liver. Its sympathetic fibers start in the <b>T8–T10 spinal cord segments</b> (segments of the cord, not spinal nerves) and travel in the greater, lesser and least splanchnic nerves. Visceral pain from the small bowel runs back along these sympathetic paths to T8–T10, so it is felt around the umbilicus, the skin band of T10. The duodenum gets vagal and sympathetic fibers through the celiac and superior mesenteric plexuses, along the pancreaticoduodenal arteries.</p>\n<h4>Colon: the switch at the transverse colon</h4>\n<p>The nerves of the colon switch at the same place as its arteries, where the proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon meets the distal one-third. Before that point lies the midgut: the cecum, appendix, ascending colon and proximal transverse colon. There the <b>vagus</b> gives the parasympathetic supply and lower thoracic segments give the sympathetic supply. Both arrive through the superior mesenteric plexus and its ganglion, along the ileocolic, right colic and middle colic arteries.</p>\n<p>After that point lies the hindgut: the distal transverse colon, the descending and sigmoid colon and the upper rectum. The parasympathetic supply comes from the <b>pelvic splanchnic nerves</b> through the inferior hypogastric (pelvic) plexus. The sympathetic supply comes from the <b>lumbar splanchnic nerves</b> of the lumbar sympathetic trunk, through the inferior mesenteric plexus and its ganglion, along the left colic artery. The vagus never reaches the descending or sigmoid colon.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/D041-238.jpg\" alt=\"Foregut, midgut and hindgut: artery, parasympathetic, sympathetic and referred pain\"></div><figcaption>Foregut, midgut and hindgut: artery, parasympathetic, sympathetic and referred pain <small>· 2025 TA review, slide 238</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>For the midgut as a whole, the main sympathetic route is the lesser splanchnic nerve (T10–T11).</p>\n<p>Parasympathetic activity speeds up peristalsis and emptying and increases secretion, and sympathetic activity slows the gut and reduces secretion. The vagus carries only parasympathetic fibers; sympathetic fibers reach the midgut through the splanchnic nerves and the superior mesenteric plexus. The drive to empty the sigmoid colon and rectum therefore comes from S2–S4 through the pelvic splanchnic nerves, and these are the nerves a surgeon protects in a sigmoid resection. The superior hypogastric and inferior mesenteric plexuses carry mostly sympathetic fibers. The glossopharyngeal nerve serves only the head and neck, and the gastrocolic reflex is a reflex, not a nerve.</p>\n<h4>Where the fibers synapse</h4>\n<p>Sympathetic fibers synapse in the prevertebral ganglia: celiac, superior mesenteric and inferior mesenteric. The splanchnic nerves carry preganglionic fibers, so they pass through the paravertebral ganglia of the sympathetic trunk without synapsing. Fibers for the descending and sigmoid colon leave the cord at L1–L2 in the lumbar splanchnic nerves and synapse in the <b>inferior mesenteric ganglion</b>; the postganglionic fibers then follow the left colic and sigmoid arteries. The superior mesenteric ganglion serves the midgut, the celiac ganglion the foregut, and the aorticorenal ganglion the kidney. Parasympathetic fibers, both vagal and pelvic splanchnic, are still preganglionic when they reach the gut. They pass through those ganglia without synapsing and synapse inside the gut wall, on neurons of the <b>myenteric (Auerbach)</b> and <b>submucosal (Meissner) plexuses</b>. Destroying these two enteric plexuses removes the parasympathetic control of the small intestine. Cutting the celiac and superior mesenteric ganglia hits sympathetic synapses instead, and the phrenic nerve supplies the diaphragm, not the gut.</p>\n<h4>Megacolon and Hirschsprung disease</h4>\n<p><b>Megacolon</b> is an abnormal dilation of the colon with paralyzed peristalsis, and hard masses of stool (fecalomas) are common. It may be acute or chronic. Its causes are congenital (aganglionic), medications, or acquired: idiopathic, toxic, after infection such as Clostridioides difficile, or from other neurologic, systemic and metabolic diseases.</p>\n<p><b>Hirschsprung disease</b> is congenital megacolon. During development, neural crest cells fail to migrate all the way down the colon, so <b>ganglion cells are missing</b> from the myenteric (Auerbach) plexus of the lower colon. The smooth muscle forms normally. The problem is the missing enteric neurons, not too much sympathetic or parasympathetic drive. The aganglionic segment cannot relax or push its contents on, so stool backs up and the normal colon above it dilates. <b>Constipation</b> is the most prominent symptom, with abdominal distention, vomiting and pain. It can occur alone or with other genetic disorders such as Down syndrome.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Autonomic plexuses ride on the arteries → same split as the blood supply</li>\n<li>Greater T5–T9, lesser T10–T11, least T12, lumbar L1–L2: all sympathetic</li>\n<li>Pelvic splanchnics (S2–S4): the only parasympathetic splanchnics</li>\n<li>Jejunum/ileum: posterior vagal trunk; sympathetic T8–T10 → periumbilical (T10) pain</li>\n<li>Midgut colon (cecum, appendix → proximal 2/3 transverse): vagus + lower thoracic sympathetics, via superior mesenteric plexus</li>\n<li>Hindgut: pelvic splanchnics (inferior hypogastric plexus) + lumbar splanchnics (inferior mesenteric plexus)</li>\n<li>Parasympathetic ↑ peristalsis, emptying, secretion (vagus: no sympathetic fibers); superior hypogastric + inferior mesenteric plexuses mostly sympathetic</li>\n<li>Sympathetic synapse: prevertebral ganglia; parasympathetic: myenteric + submucosal plexuses</li>\n<li>Descending colon: lumbar splanchnics (L1–L2) pass the trunk unsynapsed → inferior mesenteric ganglion</li>\n<li>Hirschsprung: neural crest fails → no myenteric ganglion cells in lower colon</li>\n<li>Aganglionic segment can't relax → constipation, distention, vomiting; Down syndrome</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Lymph and nerves","sn":7,"pn":"7.2"},{"l":"ANAT-G5","h":"Underside, lobes, porta hepatis and lymph","p":"<p>The liver has two surfaces. The smooth <b>diaphragmatic surface</b> faces up, forward and back, under the dome of the diaphragm. On this convex surface the <b>falciform ligament</b> separates the large right lobe from the small left lobe. The <b>visceral surface</b> faces down and back, and the organs below press into it. It touches the right side of the stomach, the first part of the duodenum, the gallbladder, the right colic flexure and transverse colon, and the right kidney and suprarenal gland.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G006-14.jpg\" alt=\"Visceral surface: quadrate and caudate lobes, porta hepatis, gallbladder, IVC, fissures\"></div><figcaption>Visceral surface: quadrate and caudate lobes, porta hepatis, gallbladder, IVC, fissures <small>· 2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary &amp; Portal Vein, slide 14</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The H of fissures and the four lobes</h4>\n<p>The visceral surface carries fissures shaped like the letter <b>H</b>. The left limb of the H holds the two fetal cords: the fissure for the round ligament in front and the fissure for the ligamentum venosum behind. The right limb is the gallbladder fossa in front and the groove for the inferior vena cava (IVC) behind. The crossbar is the <b>porta hepatis</b>.</p>\n<p>The H marks out four anatomical lobes. The <b>right lobe</b>, the largest, lies to the right of the gallbladder fossa and the IVC groove. The <b>left lobe</b> lies to the left of the falciform ligament on the front, and of the round ligament and ligamentum venosum fissures underneath; together those two cords mark off the left lobe. Between the limbs of the H sit two small lobes, one in front of the crossbar and one behind it.</p>\n<p>The <b>quadrate lobe</b> is the square block in front. The gallbladder fossa lies on its right, and the round ligament (ligamentum teres) fissure lies on its left. Behind it is the porta hepatis, and just past that is the caudate lobe. The <b>caudate lobe</b> is the block behind. The IVC groove lies on its right and the ligamentum venosum fissure on its left, with the porta hepatis in front of it. An easy way to keep them apart: the quadrate lobe is framed by the two front structures (gallbladder and round ligament), and the caudate lobe by the two back structures (IVC and ligamentum venosum).</p>\n<h4>Functional parts</h4>\n<p>Surgeons divide the liver by its blood supply, not by its surface marks. A line from the gallbladder fossa to the IVC (Cantlie line) splits it into <b>right and left livers</b>. These are closer in size than the anatomical lobes, though the right is still larger. Each receives its own primary branch of the hepatic artery and portal vein and is drained by its own hepatic duct. The quadrate lobe therefore belongs to the left liver. The two livers are further divided into eight segments (I–VIII). The caudate lobe is segment I, a separate posterior part.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G006-19.jpg\" alt=\"Functional right and left livers and segments I–VIII, divided along Cantlie line\"></div><figcaption>Functional right and left livers and segments I–VIII, divided along Cantlie line <small>· 2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary &amp; Portal Vein, slide 19</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The porta hepatis: the doorway</h4>\n<p>The <b>porta hepatis</b> is the crosswise fissure between the quadrate and caudate lobes. Through it pass the <b>portal vein</b>, the <b>hepatic artery proper</b> (as its right and left branches) and the <b>hepatic ducts</b>, together with nerves and lymph vessels. In other words, the portal triad enters here and bile leaves here.</p>\n<p>Blood leaves the liver another way. Three <b>hepatic veins</b>, formed from the central veins of the lobules, empty straight into the IVC at the back of the liver, just below the diaphragm. They never use the porta hepatis.</p>\n<h4>Lymph and nerves</h4>\n<p>Superficial lymph vessels run in the fibrous capsule of the liver (Glisson capsule). Deep vessels follow the branches of the portal triad and the hepatic veins. Both sets drain mainly to the <b>hepatic nodes</b>, which lie along the hepatic vessels and ducts in the <b>lesser omentum</b>, near the porta hepatis, not in the greater omentum. From there lymph goes to the <b>celiac nodes</b> and on to the cisterna chyli. So cancer spreading through liver lymph eventually reaches the celiac nodes.</p>\n<p>Some lymph takes side routes. From the back of the liver it passes to phrenic nodes and then posterior mediastinal nodes. From the back of the left lobe it goes to left gastric nodes. Along the falciform ligament it runs to parasternal nodes, and along the round ligament to nodes of the anterior abdominal wall.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G006-24.jpg\" alt=\"Liver lymph: hepatic nodes to celiac nodes, plus phrenic and left gastric routes\"></div><figcaption>Liver lymph: hepatic nodes to celiac nodes, plus phrenic and left gastric routes <small>· 2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary &amp; Portal Vein, slide 24</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The liver follows the rule for every digestive organ: lymph runs back along the organ's artery to nodes in front of the aorta. <b>Celiac nodes</b> drain the foregut, <b>superior mesenteric nodes</b> the midgut and <b>inferior mesenteric nodes</b> the hindgut. All reach the cisterna chyli through the intestinal trunk. Retroperitoneal organs outside the gut follow another rule. The <b>kidneys</b>, suprarenal glands and gonads drain to the <b>lumbar (para-aortic) nodes</b> beside the aorta and IVC, then through the lumbar trunks to the cisterna chyli. External iliac nodes, in the pelvis, drain the lower limb and lower abdominal wall.</p>\n<p>The nerves come from the hepatic plexus, which follows the branches of the hepatic artery and portal vein. Its sympathetic fibers come from the celiac plexus, and its parasympathetic fibers come from the anterior and posterior vagal trunks.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Visceral surface: stomach, duodenum (1st part), gallbladder, right colic flexure, right kidney</li>\n<li>Quadrate lobe: gallbladder fossa (R) + round lig. fissure (L); porta hepatis, caudate behind</li>\n<li>Caudate lobe: IVC groove (R) + lig. venosum fissure (L)</li>\n<li>Left lobe: left of falciform (diaphragmatic surface), round lig. and lig. venosum (underneath)</li>\n<li>Cantlie line (gallbladder → IVC) = right/left livers; quadrate in left liver; caudate = I</li>\n<li>Porta hepatis: portal vein, hepatic artery proper, hepatic ducts, nerves, lymph</li>\n<li>3 hepatic veins → IVC just below diaphragm; never through porta hepatis</li>\n<li>Lymph: hepatic nodes (lesser omentum) → celiac nodes → cisterna chyli</li>\n<li>Side routes: phrenic → posterior mediastinal; left gastric; parasternal; anterior wall</li>\n<li>Gut lymph follows the artery: celiac (foregut), superior mesenteric (midgut), inferior mesenteric (hindgut) nodes</li>\n<li>Kidneys, suprarenals, gonads → lumbar (para-aortic) nodes</li>\n<li>Nerves: hepatic plexus; sympathetic from celiac, parasympathetic from vagal trunks</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The liver","sn":1,"pn":"1.3"},{"l":"ANAT-G5","h":"The gallbladder, its nerves and gallstones","p":"<p>The gallbladder is a pear-shaped sac that stores and concentrates bile. It lies in a fossa on the visceral surface of the liver, at the junction of the right and left livers, between the right lobe and the quadrate lobe.</p>\n<h4>Parts and the surface landmark</h4>\n<p>The gallbladder has three parts. The <b>fundus</b> is the rounded blind end. It sticks out past the lower edge of the liver and touches the anterior abdominal wall at the tip of the <b>right 9th costal cartilage</b>. On the skin, this is where the outer edge of the right rectus sheath (the linea semilunaris) meets the costal margin. The <b>body</b> is the wide middle part; it rests on the transverse colon and the first part of the duodenum. The <b>neck</b> is the narrow, tapering end. It makes an <b>S-shaped bend</b> and continues as the cystic duct. In a diseased gallbladder, a pouch can bulge from the neck toward the cystic duct; this is the Hartmann pouch, also called the infundibulum.</p>\n<p>The fundus point is where an inflamed gallbladder is examined. The examiner presses there and asks the patient to breathe in. As the diaphragm pushes the liver and gallbladder down onto the fingers, the pain makes the patient stop the breath. This is <b>Murphy sign</b>. Nearby organs hurt elsewhere. The duodenum lies deeper, curved around the pancreatic head near the midline. The kidney lies behind the peritoneum and hurts in the back and flank. The transverse colon hangs lower. The liver lies under ribs 7–11 rather than at this exact point.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G006-45.jpg\" alt=\"Gallbladder nerves: right phrenic (sensory), vagal and celiac fibers; cystic veins\"></div><figcaption>Gallbladder nerves: right phrenic (sensory), vagal and celiac fibers; cystic veins <small>· 2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary &amp; Portal Vein, slide 45</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Vessels</h4>\n<p>The <b>cystic artery</b> supplies the gallbladder, and it usually arises from the right hepatic artery (2.3). Veins from the neck and cystic duct, the cystic veins, enter the liver directly or join the portal vein. Veins from the <b>fundus and body</b> pass straight into the visceral surface of the liver and drain into its sinusoids. Lymph goes to the cystic node near the neck, then to the hepatic nodes, and on to the celiac nodes.</p>\n<h4>Nerves and referred pain</h4>\n<p>The gallbladder has three nerve supplies, and each does a different job. Its two autonomic supplies both arrive through the <b>celiac plexus</b>, which follows the arteries to it. The <b>sympathetic</b> fibers come from the <b>greater splanchnic nerves</b>, from spinal segments T5–T9 (fifth to ninth thoracic), and mainly control the blood vessels. The <b>parasympathetic</b> fibers come from the vagus nerve (vagal trunks) and make the gallbladder contract. So the celiac plexus is the one route that carries both, while the greater splanchnic nerves alone carry only the sympathetic part. <b>Sensory</b> fibers come from the <b>right phrenic nerve</b>, which arises from spinal segments C3–C5 (third to fifth cervical).</p>\n<p>The same cervical segments supply the skin over the shoulder. So when an inflamed gallbladder irritates the right phrenic fibers, the brain reads the pain as coming from the <b>right shoulder</b>. This is referred pain. The left phrenic nerve supplies only the left half of the diaphragm and plays no part. Nerves such as the left recurrent laryngeal (to the larynx) and the inferior mesenteric plexus (to the hindgut) lie far away. The pelvic splanchnic nerves, from S2–S4 (second to fourth sacral), serve the hindgut and pelvic organs, and the intercostal nerves supply the body wall; neither reaches the gallbladder. Dull visceral pain also travels back with the sympathetic fibers in the greater splanchnic nerve. That pain is felt first in the epigastrium, then moves to the right upper quadrant, and often spreads to the back toward the right scapula.</p>\n<h4>Where a stone sits decides the picture</h4>\n<p>A gallstone can lodge anywhere along the duct tree, and its site decides what the patient shows. A stone in the <b>cystic duct</b> or the gallbladder neck stops the gallbladder from emptying. The gallbladder squeezes against the block, causing intense cramping pain (biliary colic), and then becomes inflamed (<b>cholecystitis</b>). Bile from the liver still flows down the common hepatic duct and bile duct into the duodenum, so there is <b>no jaundice</b>.</p>\n<p>A stone in the <b>bile duct</b> blocks all bile on its way to the gut, so <b>jaundice</b> appears. A stone at the <b>hepatopancreatic ampulla</b> blocks both bile and pancreatic juice, giving jaundice plus pancreatitis. A stone in the <b>right or left hepatic duct</b> blocks bile from only half of the liver; it sits above the cystic duct, so the gallbladder is not involved. A block of the <b>main pancreatic duct</b> alone gives pancreatitis, not cholecystitis.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/D042-165.jpg\" alt=\"Bile pathway from hepatic ducts to the second part of duodenum\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/D042-165c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Bile pathway from hepatic ducts to the second part of duodenum\"></div></div><figcaption>Bile pathway from hepatic ducts to the second part of duodenum <small>· 2025 TA review, slide 165</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Gallbladder: fossa on visceral surface, between right and quadrate lobes</li>\n<li>Fundus: right 9th costal cartilage + linea semilunaris → Murphy sign on inspiration</li>\n<li>Body on transverse colon + duodenum part 1; neck = narrow S-shaped bend → cystic duct; Hartmann pouch</li>\n<li>Cystic artery (usually from right hepatic); fundus + body veins → liver sinusoids</li>\n<li>Lymph: cystic node → hepatic nodes → celiac nodes</li>\n<li>Celiac plexus carries both: greater splanchnic T5–T9 (sympathetic, vessels) + vagus (parasympathetic, contraction)</li>\n<li>Not pelvic splanchnic (S2–S4, hindgut + pelvis) or intercostal (body wall)</li>\n<li>Sensory: right phrenic (C3–C5) → right shoulder pain; left phrenic = left diaphragm only</li>\n<li>Cystic duct stone: biliary colic → cholecystitis, no jaundice</li>\n<li>Bile duct stone: jaundice; ampulla: jaundice + pancreatitis; pancreatic duct: pancreatitis</li>\n<li>R or L hepatic duct stone: above cystic duct, gallbladder spared</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Bile ducts and gallbladder","sn":2,"pn":"2.2"},{"l":"ANAT-G5","h":"Calot's triangle and cholecystectomy","p":"<p>Removal of the gallbladder (<b>cholecystectomy</b>) is one of the most common abdominal operations. It is usually done through small ports (laparoscopic). To free the gallbladder, the surgeon clips and cuts two structures: the <b>cystic duct</b> and the <b>cystic artery</b>. Both are found in a small triangle at the gallbladder neck, and surgeons like to quiz students on it.</p>\n<h4>The cystohepatic triangle (of Calot)</h4>\n<p>The triangle has three borders. The <b>cystic duct</b> forms its lower border, on the outer (lateral) side. The <b>common hepatic duct</b> forms its medial border. The <b>visceral surface of the right lobe of the liver</b> forms its upper border. The common bile duct is not a border; it begins below the point where the cystic duct joins, outside the triangle. The first part of the duodenum lies lower still and forms no border either.</p>\n<p>Inside the triangle runs the <b>cystic artery</b>, usually together with the <b>right hepatic artery</b> from which it arises. The <b>cystic lymph node</b> also sits here, near the gallbladder neck. Surgeons know it by several names: Calot node, Mascagni node or Lund node. The ducts that form the borders are not contents. The <b>portal vein</b> is not in the triangle either; it lies deeper, at the back of the hepatoduodenal ligament.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G006-46.jpg\" alt=\"Calot&#x27;s triangle: cystic duct, common hepatic duct, liver; cystic and right hepatic arteries\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G006-46c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Calot&#x27;s triangle: cystic duct, common hepatic duct, liver; cystic and right hepatic arteries\"></div></div><figcaption>Calot&#x27;s triangle: cystic duct, common hepatic duct, liver; cystic and right hepatic arteries <small>· 2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary &amp; Portal Vein, slide 46</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The triangle lies at the gallbladder neck, under the right liver, between the right and quadrate lobes and beside the porta hepatis. The caudate lobe lies far behind it and is no landmark here.</p>\n<p>Do not confuse it with other named triangles, which hold no gallbladder vessels. The inguinal triangle (of Hesselbach) lies on the inner surface of the anterior abdominal wall, where direct inguinal hernias push through. The lumbar triangle (of Petit) lies on the lower back, just above the iliac crest. The femoral triangle lies in the upper thigh and holds the femoral nerve, artery and vein.</p>\n<h4>Where the cystic artery comes from</h4>\n<p>The arteries reach the gallbladder in a chain. The celiac trunk gives the <b>common hepatic artery</b>. This gives off the <b>gastroduodenal artery</b>, which runs down behind the first part of the duodenum, and then continues as the <b>hepatic artery proper</b>. The hepatic artery proper splits into the right and left hepatic arteries. In about 75% of people the cystic artery arises from the <b>right hepatic artery</b>, inside the triangle. In the other quarter its origin or course differs. The <b>left hepatic artery</b> heads to the left liver, away from the gallbladder.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G006-42.jpg\" alt=\"Cystic artery from right hepatic artery in Calot&#x27;s triangle (75.5%) and its variants\"></div><figcaption>Cystic artery from right hepatic artery in Calot&#x27;s triangle (75.5%) and its variants <small>· 2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary &amp; Portal Vein, slide 42</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Safety rules in the operation</h4>\n<p>The artery that is clipped and cut is the <b>cystic artery</b>, found by tracing it from its usual parent, the right hepatic artery. The right hepatic artery itself must be identified and preserved, because it feeds the right liver. The common hepatic artery, the hepatic artery proper, the left hepatic artery and the gastroduodenal artery are all left alone. The triangle may also hold an <b>aberrant or accessory right hepatic artery</b>, or unusual (anomalous) sectoral bile ducts. A rogue right hepatic artery looping into the triangle is the vessel most easily cut by mistake.</p>\n<p>The cystic duct varies too, in its length and in where it joins the common hepatic duct. So the rule is to clear the outer (lateral) structures first and to identify the cystic duct for certain before dissecting in the triangle. Careless dissection in Calot's triangle is the <b>number one cause of bile duct injury</b>.</p>\n<h4>Why the gallbladder comes out</h4>\n<p>The usual reason is gallstone disease. A stone impacted in the cystic duct causes intense, spasmodic pain (biliary colic) and then inflammation (cholecystitis). A diseased gallbladder may show a Hartmann pouch (infundibulum) bulging between its neck and the cystic duct. Its fundus and body sit in the liver bed, and small veins pass from them directly into the liver.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Cholecystectomy: clip + cut cystic duct and cystic artery</li>\n<li>Calot borders: cystic duct (inferior, lateral side), common hepatic duct (medial), right lobe of liver (superior); not bile duct or duodenum</li>\n<li>Cystic artery lies in Calot's triangle; not Hesselbach (inguinal), Petit (lumbar) or femoral triangle</li>\n<li>Contents: cystic artery, often right hepatic artery, cystic node (Calot/Mascagni/Lund)</li>\n<li>Not inside: portal vein (deeper); bile duct starts below; ducts are borders</li>\n<li>Location: gallbladder neck, between right and quadrate lobes; not caudate</li>\n<li>Celiac → common hepatic → (gastroduodenal) → proper → right + left hepatic</li>\n<li>Cystic artery from right hepatic ~75%; variants ~25%</li>\n<li>Preserve right hepatic; left hepatic untouched; watch aberrant/accessory right hepatic</li>\n<li>Identify cystic duct first; Calot dissection = #1 cause of bile duct injury</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Bile ducts and gallbladder","sn":2,"pn":"2.3"},{"l":"ANAT-G5","h":"How the portal vein forms and runs","p":"<p>A <b>portal system</b> carries blood from one capillary bed to a second capillary bed before it returns to the heart. The hepatic portal system collects blood from the gut, from the stomach down to the rectum, and from the spleen, pancreas and gallbladder. It takes that blood to the liver sinusoids first. Only after the liver has processed it does the blood reach the inferior vena cava (IVC), through the hepatic veins. The two circulations are kept apart so that the liver can filter everything absorbed before it reaches the heart.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G006-48.jpg\" alt=\"Portal vein forming behind the pancreatic neck from superior mesenteric and splenic veins\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G006-48c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Portal vein forming behind the pancreatic neck from superior mesenteric and splenic veins\"></div></div><figcaption>Portal vein forming behind the pancreatic neck from superior mesenteric and splenic veins <small>· 2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary &amp; Portal Vein, slide 48</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Formation</h4>\n<p>The portal vein forms where two large veins unite: the <b>superior mesenteric vein (SMV)</b> and the <b>splenic vein</b>. They join <b>behind the neck of the pancreas</b>, in front of the IVC, near the level of the first lumbar vertebra (L1) and the transpyloric plane. The SMV brings blood up from the small intestine and the right colon. The splenic vein brings blood across from the spleen, running behind the body and tail of the pancreas.</p>\n<p>The <b>inferior mesenteric vein (IMV)</b> usually takes no part in the junction. It normally ends in the splenic vein before the junction. In some people the IMV ends in the SMV or at the junction itself. But the two veins that form the portal vein are always the SMV and the splenic vein.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/D041-251.jpg\" alt=\"SMV and splenic vein forming the portal vein; anastomosis list\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/D041-251c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: SMV and splenic vein forming the portal vein; anastomosis list\"></div></div><figcaption>SMV and splenic vein forming the portal vein; anastomosis list <small>· 2025 TA review, slide 251</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Many other named veins only feed into this system; they do not form it. The left and right gastric veins, the cystic vein and the superior pancreaticoduodenal vein drain straight into the portal vein. The inferior pancreaticoduodenal and middle colic veins drain into the SMV. The IVC, the renal veins and the <b>hepatic veins</b> are systemic (caval) veins and never form the portal vein. The right and left hepatic veins carry blood out of the liver, while the portal vein's own right and left branches carry blood in.</p>\n<h4>Course</h4>\n<p>From behind the pancreatic neck, the portal vein runs up behind the first part of the duodenum. It then enters the hepatoduodenal ligament, the free right edge of the lesser omentum. Here it is the <b>most posterior</b> of the three triad structures: the bile duct lies in front of it on the right, and the hepatic artery proper lies in front of it on the left. At the porta hepatis it divides into <b>right and left branches</b>, one for each functional half of the liver.</p>\n<h4>What makes it special</h4>\n<p>The portal vein brings about <b>70%</b> of the blood that reaches the liver, more than any other vessel. This blood is rich in nutrients but low in oxygen. The hepatic artery brings the other 30% or so, rich in oxygen. Pressure in the portal vein is normally higher than in the IVC. The portal vein and its tributaries have <b>no valves</b>, so blood can flow backward when the pressure rises (3.3). Cancer cells shed from the gut also travel in portal blood, so gut cancers often spread to the liver first.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Portal system: gut, spleen, pancreas, gallbladder → liver sinusoids → hepatic veins → IVC</li>\n<li>Portal vein = SMV + splenic vein, behind pancreatic neck, in front of IVC, L1</li>\n<li>IMV usually → splenic vein (variants: SMV or junction)</li>\n<li>Direct tributaries: L + R gastric, cystic, superior pancreaticoduodenal veins</li>\n<li>Never form it: IVC, renal veins, hepatic veins (liver → IVC)</li>\n<li>Course: behind duodenum part 1 → hepatoduodenal lig., most posterior → R + L branches</li>\n<li>~70% of liver blood (nutrient-rich, low oxygen); hepatic artery ~30%</li>\n<li>Portal pressure &gt; IVC; no valves; carries gut cancer to liver</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The portal vein and portal hypertension","sn":3,"pn":"3.1"},{"l":"ANAT-G5","h":"Tributaries, and portal versus caval veins","p":"<h4>Two venous systems</h4>\n<p>Every vein of the abdomen belongs to one of two systems, and many questions ask you to sort them. The <b>portal system</b> is the hepatic portal vein and every vein that feeds it. Its blood goes to the liver sinusoids first. Its members include the splenic vein, the superior mesenteric vein (SMV), the inferior mesenteric vein (IMV), the left and right gastric veins, the paraumbilical veins, the superior rectal vein and the colic veins.</p>\n<p>The <b>caval (systemic) system</b> is the superior vena cava (SVC), the inferior vena cava (IVC) and every vein that empties into them. Its blood goes straight back to the heart. Its members include the azygos and hemiazygos veins, the renal, suprarenal, gonadal and lumbar veins, the epigastric veins, the middle and inferior rectal veins, and the hepatic veins. The hepatic veins sit inside the liver, but they are caval: they carry blood out of the liver into the IVC.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G006-52.jpg\" alt=\"Portal vein tributaries: splenic, superior and inferior mesenteric, gastric and gastro-omental veins\"></div><figcaption>Portal vein tributaries: splenic, superior and inferior mesenteric, gastric and gastro-omental veins <small>· 2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary &amp; Portal Vein, slide 52</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Which vein drains where</h4>\n<p>A few veins empty <b>directly into the portal vein</b>: the left and right gastric veins along the lesser curvature, the cystic vein, and the superior pancreaticoduodenal vein. The paraumbilical veins reach its left branch, running along the round ligament.</p>\n<p>The <b>SMV</b> drains the small intestine and the right side of the colon. Its tributaries are the jejunal and ileal veins, the ileocolic, right colic and middle colic veins, the <b>right</b> gastroepiploic (gastro-omental) vein from the right part of the greater curvature, and the <b>inferior</b> pancreaticoduodenal vein from the lower head of the pancreas.</p>\n<p>The <b>splenic vein</b> drains the spleen and picks up veins on its way across. Its tributaries are the <b>IMV</b> (which brings the left colic, sigmoid and superior rectal veins), the <b>left</b> gastroepiploic (gastro-omental) vein from the left part of the greater curvature, the short gastric veins from the fundus, and the pancreatic veins. The IMV usually ends in the splenic vein; in some people it ends in the SMV or at the junction of the two instead. The IMV never enters the left renal vein or the hepatic veins, which are caval.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G006-53.jpg\" alt=\"Pancreatic veins: superior pancreaticoduodenal to portal vein, inferior to SMV, IMV to splenic\"></div><figcaption>Pancreatic veins: superior pancreaticoduodenal to portal vein, inferior to SMV, IMV to splenic <small>· 2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary &amp; Portal Vein, slide 53</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Two look-alike pairs are worth fixing in memory. The right gastroepiploic vein goes to the SMV, but the left gastroepiploic vein goes to the splenic vein. The superior pancreaticoduodenal vein goes to the portal vein, but the inferior pancreaticoduodenal vein goes to the SMV.</p>\n<p>The colic veins split too: the right colic vein goes to the SMV, and the left colic vein goes to the IMV. Paired caval veins split the same way. The right suprarenal and right gonadal (ovarian or testicular) veins end in the IVC, while the left ones end in the left renal vein. The left and right gastric veins are the pair that shares one drain, the portal vein itself.</p>\n<h4>Reading a portal venogram</h4>\n<p>A portal venogram is an X-ray taken while contrast fills the portal veins. Start with the widest vessel, the portal trunk, which runs up and to the right into the liver and branches there. From the junction, trace the splenic vein across to the patient's left, toward the spleen. The SMV rises from below, straight into the junction. A thinner vein that rises from below on the left and ends in the splenic vein is the IMV. The hepatic veins do not fill on this study, because they lie in the liver and drain up into the IVC. Small veins such as the right gastric (into the portal vein) and the right gastroepiploic (into the SMV) are too fine to form the main trunks seen on the film.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Portal (→ liver first): portal vein, splenic, SMV, IMV, gastric, paraumbilical, superior rectal, colic</li>\n<li>Caval (→ heart): SVC, IVC, azygos, renal, gonadal, lumbar, epigastric, middle/inferior rectal, hepatic</li>\n<li>Direct to portal vein: L + R gastric, cystic, superior pancreaticoduodenal; paraumbilical → left branch</li>\n<li>SMV: jejunal, ileal, ileocolic, right + middle colic, right gastroepiploic, inferior pancreaticoduodenal</li>\n<li>Splenic vein: IMV, left gastroepiploic, short gastrics, pancreatic veins</li>\n<li>Right gastroepiploic → SMV; left gastroepiploic → splenic</li>\n<li>Superior pancreaticoduodenal → portal vein; inferior → SMV</li>\n<li>Same drain for both: L + R gastric → portal v.; R suprarenal/gonadal → IVC, L → left renal v.</li>\n<li>Venogram: trunk up-right into liver; splenic to left; SMV from below; thin IMV into splenic</li>\n<li>Hepatic veins do not fill on a portal venogram</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The portal vein and portal hypertension","sn":3,"pn":"3.2"},{"l":"ANAT-G5","h":"Portocaval anastomoses and esophageal varices","p":"<h4>Why portal pressure rises, and where the blood goes</h4>\n<p>In a few places, small portal veins meet small caval veins directly. These meeting points are the <b>portocaval (portosystemic) anastomoses</b>. Normally very little blood crosses them. They matter when blood cannot get through the liver.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The liver becomes scarred (<b>cirrhosis</b>), for example from chronic alcohol use or chronic hepatitis C.</li>\n<li>Resistance to flow through the liver rises, so pressure in the portal vein rises; this is <b>portal hypertension</b>.</li>\n<li>Portal veins have no valves, so blood is pushed backward through the portal tributaries.</li>\n<li>The blood escapes through the anastomoses into caval veins, and the thin-walled veins at each meeting point swell into <b>varices</b>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>The four anastomoses</h4>\n<p>Each anastomosis pairs one small portal vein with one small caval vein, and each has its own sign. Learn them as pairs.</p>\n<p>At the <b>lower esophagus</b>, esophageal branches of the <b>left gastric vein</b> (portal) meet esophageal branches of the <b>azygos vein</b> (caval). When they swell, they become <b>esophageal varices</b>. At the <b>rectum and anal canal</b>, the <b>superior rectal vein</b> (portal) meets the <b>middle and inferior rectal veins</b> (caval); swelling here gives <b>hemorrhoids</b> (anorectal varices). At the <b>umbilicus</b>, the <b>paraumbilical veins</b> (portal) meet the <b>epigastric veins</b> of the abdominal wall (caval); swelling gives <b>caput medusae</b> (3.4). On the <b>posterior abdominal wall</b>, the <b>colic veins</b> (portal) meet <b>retroperitoneal veins</b> (caval, such as the lumbar veins); this link is hidden and gives no visible sign.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G006-56.jpg\" alt=\"Portocaval anastomoses A–D, and a magnetic resonance angiogram of the portal vein\"></div><figcaption>Portocaval anastomoses A–D, and a magnetic resonance angiogram of the portal vein <small>· 2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary &amp; Portal Vein, slide 56</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The anastomoses join small veins only, and the big trunks sit one step away. The superior mesenteric vein (SMV) and the splenic vein are portal trunks that collect from portal partners such as the colic veins. The caval partners drain onward into large systemic veins. The azygos empties into the superior vena cava (SVC). The rectal and retroperitoneal veins reach the inferior vena cava (IVC) through the iliac, lumbar or renal veins. The epigastric veins drain to both venae cavae. So the IVC itself never touches a portal vein; it only collects the caval partners' blood later. All of these links are between veins, never arteries.</p>\n<h4>Esophageal varices up close</h4>\n<p>Normally the lower esophagus drains through esophageal branches of the <b>left gastric vein</b>, which empties into the <b>hepatic portal vein</b>. The middle esophagus drains to the azygos vein instead. In portal hypertension, portal blood is pushed backward up the left gastric vein into the small veins under the lining (submucosa) of the lower esophagus. They swell into varices, and the blood escapes up into the azygos. So the left gastric vein is the vessel that feeds the varices, and the azygos is only the escape route.</p>\n<p>Varices can burst. The patient suddenly vomits blood (hematemesis), often a large amount and without pain. The blood comes up from the gut; coughing up blood from the airway (hemoptysis) is a different sign. This is the <b>most dangerous</b> complication of portal hypertension and can be fatal. The other links rarely threaten life. Hemorrhoids bleed far less dangerously, caput medusae rarely bleeds badly, and the colic–retroperitoneal link stays silent. A patient with cirrhosis often shows several signs at once, such as jaundice, a swollen abdomen with shifting dullness (ascites) and veins around the navel; the bleeding varices are the threat to life.</p>\n<h4>Veins with no esophageal role</h4>\n<p>Several nearby veins are easy to confuse with the left gastric vein. The <b>right gastric vein</b> drains the lesser curvature near the pylorus, far from the esophagus. The gastroduodenal artery runs behind the first part of the duodenum, and the veins beside it drain the duodenum and the head of the pancreas. The <b>hemiazygos and accessory hemiazygos veins</b> drain the left chest wall; the esophageal partner is the azygos itself. The <b>splenic</b> and <b>left gastro-omental veins</b> drain the spleen, the fundus and the greater curvature; the splenic vein helps form the portal vein but has no esophageal branches. The SMV drains the small intestine and right colon. The <b>left hepatic vein</b> drains liver blood into the IVC and is not portal at all. The SVC has no esophageal branches of its own; the azygos empties into it. The <b>pharyngeal veins</b> drain the throat into the internal jugular vein; they are caval, so pairing them with the azygos joins two caval veins. Arteries, such as the hepatic or splenic artery, do not form varices, which are swollen veins.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Cirrhosis → resistance ↑ → portal hypertension → valveless backflow → anastomoses swell</li>\n<li>Esophagus: left gastric (portal) ↔ azygos (caval) → esophageal varices</li>\n<li>Rectum: superior rectal ↔ middle + inferior rectal → hemorrhoids</li>\n<li>Umbilicus: paraumbilical ↔ epigastric → caput medusae</li>\n<li>Posterior wall: colic ↔ retroperitoneal (lumbar) → no visible sign</li>\n<li>Small veins only; IVC just collects caval partners' blood later</li>\n<li>Azygos → SVC; rectal, retroperitoneal → IVC; epigastric → both</li>\n<li>Lower esophagus normally: left gastric → portal vein; middle → azygos</li>\n<li>Burst varices → hematemesis (vomited, not coughed up as hemoptysis) = most dangerous sign</li>\n<li>No esophageal role: right gastric, veins beside the gastroduodenal artery, hemiazygos, splenic, left gastro-omental, hepatic veins; pharyngeal (caval)</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The portal vein and portal hypertension","sn":3,"pn":"3.3"},{"l":"ANAT-G5","h":"Caput medusae, hemorrhoids and surgical shunts","p":"<h4>The umbilical anastomosis and caput medusae</h4>\n<p>The <b>paraumbilical veins</b> are small portal veins. They run in the falciform ligament beside the round ligament, from the left branch of the portal vein down to the umbilicus. Normally they are almost closed. At the umbilicus they meet the <b>epigastric veins</b> of the anterior abdominal wall: the superficial, inferior and superior epigastric veins. These are systemic (caval) veins.</p>\n<p>In portal hypertension the paraumbilical veins open up and send portal blood out into the wall. Swollen, winding veins then radiate from the navel. The pattern is called <b>caput medusae</b>, after the snake-haired head of Medusa. Only portal hypertension causes it. Ordinary (systemic) high blood pressure, the kind treated with a drug such as lisinopril, does not.</p>\n<p>The other portal veins meet their caval partners elsewhere, not at the abdominal wall. The superior rectal branch of the inferior mesenteric vein (IMV) meets the rectal veins. The colic branches of the superior mesenteric vein (SMV) meet retroperitoneal veins on the back wall. The portal trunk itself never reaches the abdominal wall.</p>\n<h4>The rectal anastomosis and hemorrhoids</h4>\n<p>On the portal side, the <b>superior rectal vein</b> drains up into the IMV, then the splenic vein, then the portal vein. On the caval side, the <b>middle rectal vein</b> drains to the internal iliac vein, and the <b>inferior rectal vein</b> drains through the internal pudendal vein to the internal iliac vein. From there blood goes on to the inferior vena cava (IVC). Where the two sides meet, the rectal venous plexus swells in portal hypertension, giving <b>hemorrhoids</b> (anorectal varices).</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/D042-173.jpg\" alt=\"Esophageal, umbilical and rectal portocaval anastomoses and their signs\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/D042-173c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Esophageal, umbilical and rectal portocaval anastomoses and their signs\"></div></div><figcaption>Esophageal, umbilical and rectal portocaval anastomoses and their signs <small>· 2025 TA review, slide 173</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Whether a hemorrhoid hurts depends on the <b>pectinate (dentate) line</b> of the anal canal. Above it the lining is columnar mucosa with visceral (autonomic) nerves, which sense stretch but not sharp pain, so <b>internal hemorrhoids</b>, drained by the superior rectal vein, are painless. Below it the lining is stratified squamous epithelium with somatic nerves, the <b>inferior rectal nerves</b>, so <b>external hemorrhoids</b>, drained by the inferior rectal vein, are painful. <b>Hilton's white line</b> (the anocutaneous line) lies lower still, where the lining becomes true skin. It is somatic on both sides, so swollen veins between the two lines hurt.</p>\n<p>Each sign has its own pair, so the names should not be swapped. Caput medusae comes from the paraumbilical with the epigastric veins. Hemorrhoids come from the superior rectal with the middle and inferior rectal veins. Esophageal varices come from the left gastric with the azygos veins. A varicocele is a different problem: swelling of the pampiniform plexus in the scrotum, not a portocaval link. Superior mesenteric artery (SMA) syndrome is the artery pinching the third part of the duodenum, which is not a venous problem at all.</p>\n<h4>Other signs of portal hypertension</h4>\n<p>Portal hypertension also causes <b>ascites</b>, fluid in the peritoneal cavity. The abdomen swells, and on examination the dull note shifts as the patient turns (shifting dullness). Blood backed up in the splenic vein enlarges the spleen (splenomegaly). Three features of the portal system set all this up. Portal pressure is normally higher than IVC pressure. The portal veins have no valves, which lets blood run backward. And the portal vein carries about 70% of the liver's blood, more than the hepatic artery. Portal hypertension is the main cause of varices.</p>\n<p>The block can also sit in the portal vein itself. A narrowing (stenosis) or a clot of the hepatic portal vein raises pressure in the whole portal system, just as cirrhosis does. One portal vein block can therefore give several signs at once: bright red blood in the stool from rectal varices, blood in the vomit from esophageal varices, and a swollen abdomen from ascites. A block of one small tributary, such as the superior rectal vein, would affect only its own site. A block of a caval vein, such as the superior vena cava, the azygos or a renal vein, does not back up portal blood, so it cannot explain rectal varices with ascites.</p>\n<h4>Surgical portocaval shunts</h4>\n<p>When portal pressure must be lowered, a surgeon can make a new connection, a <b>portocaval shunt</b>, so that portal blood bypasses the liver into the caval system. The rule is simple: a shunt must join <b>one portal-side vein to one caval-side vein</b>. Joining two portal veins, or two caval veins, bypasses nothing.</p>\n<p>The classic shunt is the <b>splenorenal shunt</b>, which joins the <b>splenic vein</b> (portal) to the <b>left renal vein</b> (caval). These two veins lie close together behind the pancreas. A surgeon makes this link; it is not one of the four natural anastomoses. Another option joins the portal vein directly to the IVC, just below the liver.</p>\n<p>To judge any pair, sort each vein first. Portal-side veins include the portal vein, the splenic vein, the SMV and IMV, the left and right gastric veins, the right and left gastro-omental veins, the superior pancreaticoduodenal vein, small duodenal veins such as the supraduodenal veins, and the colic veins. Caval-side veins include the IVC, the renal, suprarenal, gonadal (ovarian or testicular) and lumbar veins, the azygos vein, the internal thoracic vein and its tributaries (the superior epigastric and musculophrenic veins), and the inferior epigastric vein. Both gonadal veins are caval: the right one ends in the IVC and the left one in the left renal vein. So any gastric or gastro-omental vein paired with a renal or gonadal vein mixes the two systems, while two mesenteric veins, or a gonadal vein with a renal vein, stay inside one system.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Paraumbilical (portal, along round lig.) ↔ epigastric (caval) at navel → caput medusae</li>\n<li>Caput medusae = portal hypertension only, not systemic hypertension</li>\n<li>Superior rectal → IMV ↔ middle/inferior rectal → internal iliac → hemorrhoids</li>\n<li>Pectinate (dentate) line: above = visceral nerves, internal hemorrhoids painless; below = inferior rectal nerves (somatic), external hemorrhoids painful; Hilton's white line lies lower, somatic on both sides</li>\n<li>Varicocele = pampiniform plexus; SMA syndrome = duodenum part 3; neither portocaval</li>\n<li>Portal hypertension also: ascites (shifting dullness), splenomegaly</li>\n<li>Portal vein stenosis → varices at several sites + ascites; one tributary block → one site</li>\n<li>Portal pressure &gt; IVC; no valves; ~70% of liver blood</li>\n<li>Shunt = one portal + one caval vein; same-system pairs bypass nothing</li>\n<li>Classic: splenic → left renal vein (surgical, not natural)</li>\n<li>Caval: renal, suprarenal, gonadal, lumbar, azygos, internal thoracic, superior/inferior epigastric</li>\n<li>Portal: gastric, gastro-omental, pancreaticoduodenal, supraduodenal, colic, SMV, IMV, splenic</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The portal vein and portal hypertension","sn":3,"pn":"3.4"},{"l":"ANAT-G5","h":"Parts and relations of the pancreas","p":"<p>The pancreas is a long, soft gland that lies across the posterior abdominal wall. It is <b>retroperitoneal</b>: peritoneum covers only its front. It crosses the bodies of the first and second lumbar vertebrae (L1–L2), behind the stomach, with the duodenum on its right and the spleen on its left. The transverse mesocolon attaches along its front edge. It has a head, a neck, a body and a tail, and each part has its own neighbors.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G007-12.jpg\" alt=\"Pancreas in place: head in the duodenal C-loop, neck over superior mesenteric vessels\"></div><figcaption>Pancreas in place: head in the duodenal C-loop, neck over superior mesenteric vessels <small>· 2026 lecture: Pancreas &amp; Spleen, slide 12</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Head and uncinate process</h4>\n<p>The <b>head</b> sits inside the C-shaped curve of the duodenum. The <b>second (descending) part</b> of the duodenum hugs its right side most closely. Behind, the head lies on the inferior vena cava (IVC), the <b>right and left renal veins</b> and the <b>right renal artery</b>. The left renal vein crosses in front of the aorta to reach the IVC, so its end lies behind the head too. The <b>bile duct</b> is embedded in the back of the head on its way to the duodenum, where it meets the main pancreatic duct (2.1).</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G007-8.jpg\" alt=\"Pancreas outline over the IVC, both renal veins and right renal artery\"></div><figcaption>Pancreas outline over the IVC, both renal veins and right renal artery <small>· 2026 lecture: Pancreas &amp; Spleen, slide 8</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>From the lower part of the head, a hook of tissue, the <b>uncinate process</b>, reaches to the left. It passes <b>behind</b> the superior mesenteric vein (SMV) and the superior mesenteric artery (SMA).</p>\n<h4>Neck, body and tail</h4>\n<p>The <b>neck</b> is the short, narrow part next to the pylorus of the stomach. It lies <b>in front of</b> the superior mesenteric vessels, and the portal vein forms behind it from the SMV and splenic vein (3.1). So the SMV and SMA are sandwiched, with the neck in front and the uncinate process behind. The head proper does not cover them. The SMA leaves the aorta behind the neck, then passes forward over the uncinate process and the third part of the duodenum; it is never in front of the neck. Because the gland grips the SMA between the neck in front and the uncinate process behind, a pancreatic tumor can partly wrap around it. The other arteries nearby do not pass through the gland this way: the common hepatic artery runs to the right along its upper border, the small gastroduodenal artery runs down on the front of the head, the left gastric artery runs up to the lesser curvature, and the middle colic artery leaves the SMA below the pancreas.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G007-9.jpg\" alt=\"Posterior view: splenic vein joining the SMV behind the pancreatic neck\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G007-9c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Posterior view: splenic vein joining the SMV behind the pancreatic neck\"></div></div><figcaption>Posterior view: splenic vein joining the SMV behind the pancreatic neck <small>· 2026 lecture: Pancreas &amp; Spleen, slide 9</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The <b>body</b> crosses the body of L2 and the <b>aorta</b>. It lies in the floor of the omental bursa (lesser sac), with the stomach in front. Behind it lie the aorta, the SMA, the left suprarenal gland, the left kidney and their vessels. The splenic artery runs along its upper border, and the splenic vein runs along its back.</p>\n<p>The <b>tail</b> lies in front of the left kidney, close to the left colic flexure. It passes between the layers of the <b>splenorenal ligament</b> together with the splenic vessels and reaches the hilum of the spleen. Because it lies inside a peritoneal fold, the tail is the only <b>intraperitoneal</b> part. The head, neck and body are retroperitoneal.</p>\n<h4>Tumors and operations, part by part</h4>\n<p>A tumor of the <b>head</b> presses on the bile duct embedded in its back. Bile backs up, and the patient turns yellow (jaundice). The same tumor can squeeze the <b>second part of the duodenum</b> wrapped around it, blocking it and causing nausea and vomiting. Other ducts and vessels lie out of its reach. The common hepatic duct, the cystic duct and the hepatic artery proper all sit above the pancreas, near the liver. The accessory pancreatic duct carries only pancreatic juice, so blocking it would not cause jaundice. The spleen lies by the tail, the right kidney lies behind and to the side, and the esophagus and azygos vein lie far away, in the chest and upper abdomen. The splenorenal ligament, which holds the tail, lies at the far left end of the gland, well out of a head tumor's reach.</p>\n<p>A <b>distal pancreatectomy</b> removes the body and tail. Along that segment lie the <b>aorta</b> (behind the body) and the <b>splenic artery and vein</b> (along the body and tail, and with the tail in the splenorenal ligament). These are the structures to protect. The IVC lies behind the head, which stays in place.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Pancreas: retroperitoneal except tail; L1–L2 behind stomach; duodenum right, spleen left</li>\n<li>Head in duodenal C-loop; descending (2nd) part hugs it</li>\n<li>Head overlies IVC, R + L renal veins, R renal artery; bile duct in its back</li>\n<li>Uncinate process: behind SMV + SMA</li>\n<li>Neck: by pylorus, in front of SMV + SMA; portal vein forms behind it</li>\n<li>Body: crosses L2 + aorta; floor of lesser sac; splenic artery on top, vein behind</li>\n<li>Tail: in splenorenal lig. with splenic vessels → splenic hilum; intraperitoneal</li>\n<li>Head tumor → bile duct (jaundice) + duodenum part 2 (vomiting); splenorenal lig. (tail) out of reach</li>\n<li>Tumor wrapping a large artery → SMA (gripped between neck and uncinate)</li>\n<li>Distal pancreatectomy: watch aorta + splenic vessels; IVC is behind head</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The pancreas","sn":4,"pn":"4.1"},{"l":"ANAT-G5","h":"Blood supply of the pancreas","p":"<p>The pancreas sits on the border between foregut and midgut, so it gets blood from both the celiac trunk (the foregut artery) and the superior mesenteric artery (SMA, the midgut artery). The neck, body and tail are fed from one source, and the head from two.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G007-15.jpg\" alt=\"Pancreatic arteries: splenic branches to body and tail, pancreaticoduodenal arcades around head\"></div><figcaption>Pancreatic arteries: splenic branches to body and tail, pancreaticoduodenal arcades around head <small>· 2026 lecture: Pancreas &amp; Spleen, slide 15</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Neck, body and tail: the splenic artery</h4>\n<p>The <b>splenic artery</b> is the largest branch of the celiac trunk. It runs a twisting course to the left along the <b>upper border</b> of the pancreas. On the way it gives many pancreatic branches to the neck, body and tail: the dorsal pancreatic artery, the great pancreatic artery and the artery to the tail. Because it feeds most of the gland, the splenic artery is the <b>main</b> blood supply of the pancreas.</p>\n<h4>The head: two arcades from two trunks</h4>\n<p>The head is fed by two pairs of pancreaticoduodenal arteries, one pair from above and one from below. The <b>superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries</b> (anterior and posterior) come from the <b>gastroduodenal artery</b>, the branch of the common hepatic artery that runs behind the first part of the duodenum. Their route is celiac trunk, common hepatic artery, gastroduodenal artery. The <b>inferior pancreaticoduodenal arteries</b> (anterior and posterior) come from the <b>SMA</b>. The superior and inferior vessels run toward each other on each side of the head and join in loops (arcades), which also supply the duodenum around the head.</p>\n<p>The arcades link the celiac territory with the SMA territory, so if one source fails, the other still feeds the head. If the celiac trunk is torn, for example, the SMA keeps blood flowing to the head through the inferior pancreaticoduodenal arteries. The SMA helps only by this route; it does not feed the body and tail. No pancreatic arteries come straight off the aorta. The inferior mesenteric artery (IMA) supplies the hindgut and never reaches the pancreas. Arteries such as the right gastric (lesser curvature) and the colic arteries (colon) feed other organs.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G007-31.jpg\" alt=\"Celiac and SMA branches to pancreas, duodenum, gallbladder and spleen\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G007-31c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Celiac and SMA branches to pancreas, duodenum, gallbladder and spleen\"></div></div><figcaption>Celiac and SMA branches to pancreas, duodenum, gallbladder and spleen <small>· 2026 lecture: Pancreas &amp; Spleen, slide 31</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The tail, the splenic artery and the spleen</h4>\n<p>The splenic artery enters the splenorenal ligament with the tail of the pancreas and reaches the splenic hilum, where the tail ends. So a tumor in the tail sits on the splenic artery and vein, and it can cut off the blood supply of the <b>spleen</b>. The other neighbors have their own arteries, away from the tail. The duodenum gets the gastroduodenal and pancreaticoduodenal arteries, near the head. The gallbladder gets the cystic artery. The kidneys get renal arteries straight from the aorta. The liver gets the hepatic artery and the portal vein, on the side of the head.</p>\n<h4>A retroperitoneal gland in the epigastrium</h4>\n<p>The pancreas lies behind the peritoneum across the epigastrium, and the pancreatic branches of the splenic artery lie with it. So an infection behind the peritoneum in the epigastric region threatens the <b>pancreatic branches of the splenic artery</b>. Vessels that run inside peritoneal folds lie in front of that space: the hepatic artery proper runs in the lesser omentum (hepatoduodenal ligament), and the gastro-omental vessels run in the greater omentum along the greater curvature. The appendicular artery runs in the mesoappendix, down in the right lower quadrant. The inferior mesenteric vein is retroperitoneal, but it climbs the left side of the posterior wall and reaches the epigastrium only at its end, behind the body of the pancreas.</p>\n<h4>Veins, lymph and nerves</h4>\n<p>Pancreatic veins drain mainly into the splenic vein, and the head also drains into the superior mesenteric vein (SMV). Both lead to the portal vein. Lymph goes mostly to the pancreaticosplenic nodes, then to the hepatic nodes, then to the celiac or superior mesenteric nodes. Nerves come from the celiac and superior mesenteric plexuses and travel along the arteries. Parasympathetic fibers come from the vagus, and sympathetic fibers from the greater splanchnic nerves.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Main supply: splenic artery (celiac) → neck, body, tail (dorsal, great pancreatic, tail arteries)</li>\n<li>Upper head: superior pancreaticoduodenal ← gastroduodenal ← common hepatic ← celiac</li>\n<li>Lower head: inferior pancreaticoduodenal ← SMA</li>\n<li>Arcades link celiac + SMA → head survives if one fails</li>\n<li>No pancreatic branches from aorta; IMA (hindgut) never reaches pancreas</li>\n<li>Splenic artery: upper border → splenorenal lig. → hilum; tail tumor → spleen ischemia</li>\n<li>Retroperitoneal epigastric infection → pancreatic branches of splenic artery; omental vessels spared</li>\n<li>Veins: splenic vein (mainly) + SMV → portal vein</li>\n<li>Lymph: pancreaticosplenic → hepatic → celiac or superior mesenteric nodes</li>\n<li>Nerves: celiac + superior mesenteric plexuses; vagus (para), greater splanchnic (sympathetic)</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The pancreas","sn":4,"pn":"4.2"},{"l":"ANAT-G5","h":"Position, blood supply and injury of the spleen","p":"<p>The spleen is the <b>largest lymphatic organ</b>. It is very vascular and filters the blood. Its <b>red pulp</b> removes worn-out red blood cells, and its <b>white pulp</b> is lymphoid tissue that mounts immune responses. It lies in the <b>left upper quadrant</b> (LUQ, the left hypochondrium), behind the stomach. Peritoneum covers it everywhere except at the hilum, where its vessels enter and leave. A thin capsule of fibroelastic tissue surrounds it.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G007-22.jpg\" alt=\"Spleen in the left upper quadrant behind ribs 9–11 (lateral view, numbered ribs)\"></div><figcaption>Spleen in the left upper quadrant behind ribs 9–11 (lateral view, numbered ribs) <small>· 2026 lecture: Pancreas &amp; Spleen, slide 22</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Relations and rib level</h4>\n<p>In front of the spleen lies the stomach. Behind it lies the left half of the diaphragm, which separates it from the pleura, the left lung and <b>left ribs 9–11</b>. Below it lies the left colic flexure, and medial to it lies the left kidney, with the left suprarenal (adrenal) gland on the kidney's upper pole. The spleen hugs the back of the left lower rib cage, around the posterior axillary line.</p>\n<p>Rib levels sort out which organ lies under a blow. The liver lies deep to right ribs 7–11. The spleen lies deep to left ribs 9–11. The left kidney lies lower and more medial, against ribs 11–12; the spleen sits above and lateral to it, not below. The right kidney sits about 2.5 cm lower than the left, because the large right lobe of the liver lies above it and keeps it down, so only rib 12 lies behind its upper pole. Left ribs 7–8 lie higher and further forward, over the stomach. The pancreas lies deep near the midline, not against the ribs, and the left lobe of the liver lies at the front near the midline. The descending colon lies lower, in the left flank, and the third part of the duodenum crosses the midline low down, at the third lumbar vertebra (L3).</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G007-25.jpg\" alt=\"Spleen in situ by the 9th rib, with stomach, left kidney, pancreatic tail\"></div><figcaption>Spleen in situ by the 9th rib, with stomach, left kidney, pancreatic tail <small>· 2026 lecture: Pancreas &amp; Spleen, slide 25</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Blood supply</h4>\n<p>The <b>splenic artery</b> is the largest branch of the <b>celiac trunk</b>. It follows a twisting course along the upper border of the pancreas to the hilum, where it splits into five or more branches. These branches do not join one another. As a result, the spleen has two or three vascular segments, with bloodless (avascular) planes between them. Other arteries do not feed the spleen: the superior mesenteric artery (SMA) supplies the midgut, the inferior mesenteric artery (IMA) the hindgut, and the right gastric artery the lesser curvature of the stomach.</p>\n<p>The <b>splenic vein</b> forms from several tributaries at the hilum. It runs to the right behind the body and tail of the pancreas and joins the superior mesenteric vein (SMV) to form the portal vein (3.1). Lymph from the spleen drains to the pancreaticosplenic nodes.</p>\n<h4>Splenic injury</h4>\n<p>The spleen is the abdominal organ most often injured by blunt trauma. A blow to the left lower chest wall or the left lower back, or fractures of left ribs 9–11, can tear it. Any of these ribs can be the culprit, such as ribs 9 and 10 or ribs 10 and 11 on the left, at or behind the axillary line. A spleen enlarged by infection, such as infectious mononucleosis, tears more easily.</p>\n<p>A torn spleen bleeds heavily into the abdomen, and the patient goes into shock. The signs are a fast pulse, falling blood pressure, cold and clammy skin, fast or labored breathing, confusion or lightheadedness, and a swelling abdomen. Blood under the left dome of the diaphragm irritates fibers of the phrenic nerve, from spinal segments C3–C5 (third to fifth cervical), so pain is referred to the <b>left shoulder</b> (Kehr sign). A torn spleen is hard to repair, so it is often removed (splenectomy). Neighboring organs rarely give this picture. The stomach rarely bleeds like this after a fall. The left kidney lies lower and bleeds less dangerously. A lung injury mainly causes breathing trouble or a collapsed lung rather than shock.</p>\n<h4>Finding the spleen on an axial CT image</h4>\n<p>An axial computed tomography (CT) image is viewed from the feet, so the patient's left is on the screen's right. First find the vertebra at the back in the middle. The round bright vessel just in front of it is the aorta, and the muscle masses beside the spine are the erector spinae muscles. The large solid organ on the patient's right (screen left) is the liver. In front on the patient's left is the stomach, often marked by black air. The <b>spleen</b> is the solid organ at the back of the left upper quadrant, tucked against the ribs behind the stomach.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Spleen: largest lymphatic organ; red pulp clears old red cells, white pulp = lymphoid; LUQ behind stomach; peritoneum except hilum; thin capsule</li>\n<li>Stomach in front; diaphragm + left ribs 9–11 behind; colic flexure below; kidney medial</li>\n<li>Ribs: liver right 7–11; spleen left 9–11; left kidney 11–12; right kidney 12 only</li>\n<li>Right kidney ~2.5 cm lower than left: large liver above it</li>\n<li>Splenic artery: largest celiac branch, tortuous along pancreas → 5+ hilar branches</li>\n<li>Branches don't anastomose → 2–3 segments with avascular planes</li>\n<li>Splenic vein: behind pancreas → joins SMV → portal vein; lymph → pancreaticosplenic nodes</li>\n<li>Most injured organ in blunt trauma; mononucleosis → enlarged, tears easily</li>\n<li>Rupture → shock (fast pulse, falling pressure, cold clammy skin) → often splenectomy</li>\n<li>Kehr sign: left shoulder pain via phrenic nerve (C3–C5)</li>\n<li>CT: patient's left on screen right; spleen at back of LUQ, behind stomach</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The spleen","sn":5,"pn":"5.1"},{"l":"ANAT-G5","h":"Ligaments of the spleen","p":"<p>Two folds of peritoneum hold the spleen in place. Both attach at its <b>hilum</b>, and both come from the <b>dorsal mesentery</b> of the stomach (the dorsal mesogastrium), in which the spleen develops. The ventral mesentery, by contrast, gave the liver its folds, the falciform ligament and the lesser omentum (1.2). Each splenic fold is named for the two organs it joins, and each carries its own vessels.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G007-27.jpg\" alt=\"Gastrosplenic and lienorenal ligaments and what each contains\"></div><figcaption>Gastrosplenic and lienorenal ligaments and what each contains <small>· 2026 lecture: Pancreas &amp; Spleen, slide 27</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The gastrosplenic ligament</h4>\n<p>The <b>gastrosplenic</b> (gastrolienal) ligament runs from the splenic hilum forward to the <b>greater curvature</b> of the stomach. The stomach lies in front of and medial to the spleen, so this ligament holds the spleen from the front and limits its forward movement. Inside it run the <b>short gastric</b> vessels and the <b>left gastro-omental</b> (gastroepiploic) vessels. The short gastric arteries leave the splenic artery near the hilum and pass through this ligament to the fundus of the stomach. The left gastro-omental artery also comes from the splenic artery and runs through the same ligament to the greater curvature. A tear of the gastrosplenic ligament, during a splenectomy for example, therefore tears the short gastric arteries. In a splenectomy both sets of vessels are tied: the splenic artery and vein in the splenorenal ligament, and the short gastric vessels in this one. The <b>short gastric vessels</b> are the ones to tie with special care. They are several, short and hidden in the ligament, so a torn one bleeds and a misplaced tie can cut off the blood supply of the fundus. The right gastric and pancreaticoduodenal vessels lie far to the right, at the lesser curvature and the pancreatic head.</p>\n<p>The <b>right</b> gastro-omental artery is not in this ligament. It comes from the gastroduodenal artery and runs along the right part of the greater curvature. The lesser curvature has no tie to the spleen at all; the liver holds it through the hepatogastric ligament.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/D045-215.jpg\" alt=\"Splenic artery giving short gastric and left gastroepiploic arteries\"></div><figcaption>Splenic artery giving short gastric and left gastroepiploic arteries <small>· 2025 Montana TA review, slide 215</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The splenorenal ligament</h4>\n<p>The <b>splenorenal</b> ligament runs from the splenic hilum back to the <b>left kidney</b> on the posterior abdominal wall. Lieno- means spleen, so the lienorenal ligament is the same ligament. It carries the <b>splenic artery and vein</b> and the <b>tail of the pancreas</b>. A stab wound here injures the splenic vessels. In a splenectomy, this ligament must be handled with care and its vessels tied, or the abdomen fills with blood. The tail of the pancreas touches the hilum inside this ligament, so it can be clamped or cut by mistake when the vessels are tied. The tail holds many of the islets that make insulin, so the patient may then have high blood sugar and need insulin. The head of the pancreas, the bile duct and the descending duodenum lie far to the right, and the left suprarenal gland lies behind the peritoneum on the left kidney, so none of them is at the hilum. A surgeon who wants to reach a tumor in the pancreatic tail must open this ligament first.</p>\n<p>The tail lying inside this ligament also explains how the pancreas sits in the peritoneum. The head, neck and body are retroperitoneal, and the tail, wrapped in the splenorenal ligament, is intraperitoneal (4.1).</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G007-26.jpg\" alt=\"Axial section: gastrosplenic ligament to stomach, splenorenal ligament to left kidney\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G007-26c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Axial section: gastrosplenic ligament to stomach, splenorenal ligament to left kidney\"></div></div><figcaption>Axial section: gastrosplenic ligament to stomach, splenorenal ligament to left kidney <small>· 2026 lecture: Pancreas &amp; Spleen, slide 26</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>What the spleen rests on, and what does not exist</h4>\n<p>From below, the spleen rests on the <b>phrenicocolic ligament</b> (sustentaculum lienis), a shelf of peritoneum from the left colic flexure to the diaphragm. It carries no large vessels. A small splenocolic ligament ties the lower pole of the spleen to the left colic flexure. The diaphragm lies above and behind the spleen, and the left kidney lies behind and medial to it. There is <b>no hepatosplenic ligament</b>; the liver and spleen are not tied together.</p>\n<h4>Other named folds, to keep apart</h4>\n<p>Several folds elsewhere have similar names. The <b>hepatoduodenal ligament</b> joins the liver to the first part of the duodenum and carries the portal triad. The <b>hepatogastric ligament</b> joins the liver to the lesser curvature, as part of the lesser omentum, and carries the left and right gastric vessels. The <b>gastrocolic ligament</b> joins the greater curvature to the transverse colon, as part of the greater omentum, and carries the gastro-omental arcade. The <b>hepatorenal ligament</b> joins the liver to the right kidney, on the far right. The <b>coronary ligament</b> joins the liver to the diaphragm around the bare area, and the <b>falciform ligament</b> joins the liver to the anterior abdominal wall. The suspensory ligament of the duodenum (ligament of Treitz) holds the duodenojejunal flexure, not the spleen. The jejunum and ileum hang on their own mesentery, far from the spleen.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Both splenic ligaments attach at hilum; from dorsal mesentery (dorsal mesogastrium)</li>\n<li>Gastrosplenic: hilum → greater curvature; short gastric + left gastro-omental vessels</li>\n<li>Gastrosplenic holds spleen anteriorly; tear → short gastric arteries</li>\n<li>Splenectomy: short gastric vessels need special care (bleeding, fundus supply)</li>\n<li>Right gastro-omental artery ← gastroduodenal; not in gastrosplenic lig.</li>\n<li>Splenorenal (= lienorenal): hilum → left kidney; splenic artery + vein + pancreatic tail</li>\n<li>Splenectomy: tie splenic vessels in splenorenal lig.; pancreatic tail at the hilum can be injured (→ high blood sugar); tail tumor: open it first</li>\n<li>Pancreas: head, neck, body retroperitoneal; tail intraperitoneal</li>\n<li>Spleen rests on phrenicocolic lig. (no large vessels); no hepatosplenic lig.</li>\n<li>Hepatoduodenal = portal triad; hepatogastric = gastric vessels; gastrocolic = greater omentum</li>\n<li>Hepatorenal = liver–right kidney; coronary = liver–diaphragm; Treitz = duodenojejunal flexure</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The spleen","sn":5,"pn":"5.2"},{"l":"ANAT-G6","h":"Two neurons, two divisions, and where the ganglia sit","p":"<h4>Somatic and autonomic</h4>\n<p>The nervous system can be split two ways. By structure, the <b>central nervous system (CNS)</b> is the brain and spinal cord, and the <b>peripheral nervous system (PNS)</b> is the cranial and spinal nerves that leave them. By function, nerves are <b>somatic</b> or <b>autonomic</b>. Fibers are also named by direction: afferent fibers carry signals toward a processing center, and efferent fibers carry them away.</p>\n<p>The somatic system is voluntary. It supplies the skin, the joints and <b>skeletal muscle</b>. A single motor neuron reaches the target: its cell body sits in the anterior horn of the spinal cord, and its axon runs all the way to the muscle. A withdrawal reflex, such as pulling the arm away from something painful, is somatic, because it moves skeletal muscle.</p>\n<p>The autonomic (visceral) system is involuntary; nobody can switch it on at will. It supplies <b>smooth muscle</b> (in vessel walls and the viscera), <b>heart muscle</b> and its conducting system, and <b>glands</b>. Salivating, emptying the bladder (micturition) and speeding up the heart are all autonomic.</p>\n<h4>The two-neuron chain</h4>\n<p>An autonomic signal needs two neurons in a row. The first is the <b>preganglionic (presynaptic) neuron</b>. Its cell body lies in the gray matter of the CNS, in the brainstem or spinal cord, and it develops from the neural tube. Its myelinated axon leaves the CNS and ends in an autonomic ganglion. The second is the <b>postganglionic (postsynaptic) neuron</b>. Its cell body lies in that ganglion, and it develops from neural crest cells. Its unmyelinated axon runs on to the smooth muscle, heart muscle or gland.</p>\n<p>A <b>ganglion</b> is a cluster of nerve cell bodies outside the CNS. This gives a simple rule: an autonomic cell body inside the brain or cord is preganglionic, and one in a ganglion is postganglionic. The white and gray rami that link the spinal nerves to the sympathetic chain are bundles of fibers only, so no cell bodies sit in them (2.1). Two other kinds of cell body are not autonomic at all. Somatic motor neurons sit in the anterior horn of the cord, and sensory neurons sit in the dorsal root (spinal) ganglia.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G008-8.jpg\" alt=\"One-neuron somatic path beside the two-neuron autonomic path, with ganglion locations\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G008-8c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: One-neuron somatic path beside the two-neuron autonomic path, with ganglion locations\"></div></div><figcaption>One-neuron somatic path beside the two-neuron autonomic path, with ganglion locations <small>· 2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 8</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Two divisions, two ganglion positions</h4>\n<p>The <b>sympathetic</b> division prepares the body for fight or flight. Its preganglionic cell bodies lie in the lateral horn of thoracic 1 to lumbar 2 (T1–L2), so it is called thoracolumbar. Its ganglia are large and sit close to the cord. <b>Paravertebral ganglia</b> form the sympathetic chain beside the vertebral bodies, and <b>prevertebral ganglia</b> (celiac, superior and inferior mesenteric) sit in front of the aorta. Both kinds hold postganglionic sympathetic cell bodies. The highest sympathetic ganglion is the superior cervical ganglion, in the neck just below the skull, so no sympathetic ganglion lies in the head.</p>\n<p>The <b>parasympathetic</b> division supports rest and digest. Its preganglionic cell bodies lie in the brainstem nuclei of cranial nerves (CN) III, VII, IX and X and in the lateral horn of sacral 2 to 4 (S2–S4), so it is called craniosacral. Its ganglia are small and sit at or inside the target: the four head ganglia (ciliary, pterygopalatine, otic and submandibular) and tiny <b>enteric (intramural) ganglia</b> in the walls of the viscera.</p>\n<p>Fiber length follows ganglion position. Sympathetic ganglia lie near the cord, so the preganglionic fiber is <b>short</b> and the postganglionic fiber is <b>long</b>. Parasympathetic ganglia lie at the organ, so the preganglionic fiber is <b>long</b> and the postganglionic fiber is <b>short</b>.</p>\n<p>The divisions also differ in reach. Sympathetic fibers go everywhere: to every blood vessel, to the skin, and to the sweat glands, which only sympathetic fibers supply. Parasympathetic fibers stay with the viscera and never reach the skin, sweat glands or limbs. Both divisions are involuntary, and they work in tandem on smooth muscle, glands and the heart's conducting system.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G008-5.jpg\" alt=\"Craniosacral parasympathetic and thoracolumbar (T1–L2) sympathetic outflow\"></div><figcaption>Craniosacral parasympathetic and thoracolumbar (T1–L2) sympathetic outflow <small>· 2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 5</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Somatic: voluntary; skin, joints, skeletal muscle; 1 neuron (anterior horn); withdrawal reflex</li>\n<li>Autonomic: involuntary; smooth muscle, heart muscle, glands; 2 neurons in a row</li>\n<li>Preganglionic: cell body in CNS gray matter (neural tube), myelinated, ends in ganglion</li>\n<li>Postganglionic: cell body in ganglion (neural crest), unmyelinated, ends on target</li>\n<li>Ganglion = cell bodies outside CNS; sensory cell bodies = dorsal root ganglia</li>\n<li>White and gray rami = fibers only, no cell bodies</li>\n<li>Sympathetic: T1–L2; paravertebral + prevertebral ganglia (postganglionic bodies); short pre, long post</li>\n<li>Parasympathetic: CN III, VII, IX, X + S2–S4; head or enteric (wall) ganglia; long pre, short post</li>\n<li>Head ganglia = parasympathetic; highest sympathetic = superior cervical (neck)</li>\n<li>Only sympathetic reaches skin, sweat glands, every vessel</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The plan of the autonomic system","sn":1,"pn":"1.1"},{"l":"ANAT-G6","h":"From the lateral horn through the chain","p":"<h4>Where sympathetic outflow starts</h4>\n<p>Every sympathetic preganglionic cell body lies in the <b>lateral horn</b> of the spinal cord, also called the intermediolateral cell column. It is a small wedge of gray matter between the anterior (motor) horn and the posterior (sensory) horn. The lateral horn exists only from thoracic 1 to lumbar 2 (T1–L2). Twelve of those fourteen segments are thoracic, so almost all sympathetic cell bodies sit in the thoracic cord. The cervical, sacral and coccygeal cord send out no sympathetic fibers. The autonomic nuclei of the brainstem are parasympathetic, and so are the cell bodies of sacral 2 to 4.</p>\n<p>Inside the lateral horn the cells are arranged by target (somatotopically). The head and neck are served by T1–T4, the upper limb by T1–T4, the thoracic viscera by T1–T5, the abdomen by T5–T12, the pelvis and perineum by T11–L2, and the lower limb by T12–L2. Sweat glands get only sympathetic fibers. So destroying the lateral horn of T1–T4 stops the arm from sweating. Damage to the posterior horn, the posterior roots or the dorsal root ganglia only removes sensation, and the dorsal rami of T1–T4 go to the back, not the arm.</p>\n<h4>The path: white in, gray out</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>The preganglionic axon leaves the cord through the <b>ventral (anterior) root</b>; the dorsal (posterior) root carries only sensory fibers.</li>\n<li>It runs a short way in the spinal nerve, then turns off through the <b>white ramus communicans</b> into a chain (paravertebral) ganglion. The ramus looks white because these preganglionic fibers are myelinated, and it exists only at T1–L2.</li>\n<li>In the chain the fiber synapses, or it moves on to synapse elsewhere.</li>\n<li>Postganglionic axons from the chain return to the spinal nerve through the <b>gray ramus communicans</b>. They are unmyelinated, so this ramus looks gray, and every spinal nerve has one.</li>\n<li>They rejoin the spinal nerve, not the cord, and travel in both its dorsal and ventral rami, and along blood vessels, to sweat glands, vessel walls and the tiny muscles that raise hairs.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Parasympathetic fibers never pass through the rami or the chain. Somatic motor axons simply stay in the spinal nerve.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G008-11.jpg\" alt=\"Spinal nerve with its white and gray rami joining a sympathetic chain ganglion\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G008-11c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Spinal nerve with its white and gray rami joining a sympathetic chain ganglion\"></div></div><figcaption>Spinal nerve with its white and gray rami joining a sympathetic chain ganglion <small>· 2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 11</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Four routes inside the chain</h4>\n<p>Once a preganglionic fiber reaches the chain, it can take any of four routes. It can synapse in the ganglion at its own level, which serves the body wall at T1–L2. It can climb the chain and then synapse, because outflow starts at T1 but the head and neck also need sympathetic fibers. It can run down the chain and then synapse, because outflow stops at L2 but the pelvis, perineum and lower limb need fibers too. Or it can pass through without synapsing, as a <b>splanchnic nerve</b>, to a prevertebral ganglion in front of the aorta. That last route serves the abdominal and pelvic organs (2.3).</p>\n<p>Each chain runs from the superior cervical ganglion, just below the skull base, to the <b>ganglion impar</b> in front of the coccyx, where the two chains join.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G008-12.jpg\" alt=\"Sympathetic chain: fibers ascending, descending, synapsing at their level or passing through\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G008-12c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Sympathetic chain: fibers ascending, descending, synapsing at their level or passing through\"></div></div><figcaption>Sympathetic chain: fibers ascending, descending, synapsing at their level or passing through <small>· 2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 12</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Sympathetic preganglionic bodies: lateral horn (intermediolateral column), T1–L2 only</li>\n<li>12 of 14 segments thoracic; none from cervical, sacral, coccygeal cord or brain</li>\n<li>Head/neck T1–T4; upper limb T1–T4; thorax T1–T5; abdomen T5–T12; pelvis T11–L2; leg T12–L2</li>\n<li>No arm sweating: lateral horn T1–T4 lesion (posterior horn, roots, dorsal root ganglia = sensory)</li>\n<li>Lateral horn → ventral root → white ramus → chain → gray ramus → both rami</li>\n<li>White ramus: preganglionic, myelinated, T1–L2 only</li>\n<li>Gray ramus: postganglionic, unmyelinated, every spinal nerve; back to nerve, not cord</li>\n<li>Chain routes: synapse same level, ascend, descend, or pass through as splanchnic → prevertebral</li>\n<li>Chain: superior cervical ganglion → ganglion impar (coccyx)</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The sympathetic division","sn":2,"pn":"2.1"},{"l":"ANAT-G6","h":"Sympathetics to the head: Horner syndrome","p":"<h4>The route to the head</h4>\n<p>The head gets its sympathetic supply from the top of the thoracic outflow. The preganglionic cell bodies lie in the lateral horn of thoracic 1 and 2 (T1–T2). Their axons leave through the <b>ventral (anterior) roots</b> of T1–T2, enter the spinal nerves and pass through the white rami into the chain. They then <b>climb</b> the cervical part of the chain. The cervical cord adds nothing on the way, because it has no lateral horn.</p>\n<p>On the way up, the fibers pass the cervicothoracic (stellate) ganglion, which sits at the apex of the lung near the first rib. They synapse in the <b>superior cervical ganglion</b>, just below the base of the skull. The postganglionic fibers then wrap the internal and external carotid arteries as the <b>carotid plexuses</b> and ride the arterial branches to their targets. The main targets are the sweat glands of the face, the dilator pupillae muscle, and the <b>superior tarsal muscle</b>, a small smooth muscle that helps hold up the upper eyelid.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G008-17.jpg\" alt=\"Cervical sympathetic chain along the carotid arteries, from superior cervical to cervicothoracic ganglion\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G008-17c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Cervical sympathetic chain along the carotid arteries, from superior cervical to cervicothoracic ganglion\"></div></div><figcaption>Cervical sympathetic chain along the carotid arteries, from superior cervical to cervicothoracic ganglion <small>· 2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 17</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Horner syndrome</h4>\n<p>If this pathway is cut anywhere on one side, the face on that side loses its sympathetic supply. Three signs follow, and each comes from one lost target. The upper lid droops a little (<b>ptosis</b>) because the superior tarsal muscle goes slack. The pupil becomes small (<b>miosis</b>) because the dilator pupillae stops pulling and the parasympathetic constrictor acts unopposed. The face stops sweating (<b>anhidrosis</b>) because its sweat glands lose their only nerve supply.</p>\n<p>There are three main causes: a lesion of the cervical sympathetic chain, a <b>Pancoast tumor</b> (a lung cancer at the apex that presses on the chain), and injury to the carotid arteries, which carry the postganglionic plexus. A break earlier in the path gives the same picture. Cutting the T1–T2 anterior roots stops the preganglionic fibers before they ever reach the chain. Cervical anterior roots carry no sympathetic fibers, and posterior roots and rootlets carry only sensory fibers, so damage to those does not cause Horner syndrome.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G008-18.jpg\" alt=\"Horner syndrome: drooping upper lid and small pupil on one side\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G008-18c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Horner syndrome: drooping upper lid and small pupil on one side\"></div></div><figcaption>Horner syndrome: drooping upper lid and small pupil on one side <small>· 2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 18</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Neighbors that do other jobs</h4>\n<p>Several nerves near the lung apex and in the neck can be hurt by the same disease but give different signs. The vagus nerve is parasympathetic; damage to it, or to its recurrent laryngeal branch, causes hoarseness. The phrenic nerve, from cervical 3 to 5 (C3–C5), is motor to the diaphragm, so its loss paralyzes half of the diaphragm. The cardiopulmonary plexus serves the heart and lungs, not the face. The posterior (dorsal) rami supply the muscles and skin of the back.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Head sympathetics: lateral horn T1–T2 → anterior roots → white rami → chain</li>\n<li>Ascend chain past stellate ganglion (lung apex) → synapse in superior cervical ganglion</li>\n<li>Postganglionic → internal + external carotid plexuses → along carotid branches</li>\n<li>Targets: facial sweat glands, dilator pupillae, superior tarsal muscle</li>\n<li>Horner (same side): ptosis + miosis + anhidrosis</li>\n<li>Causes: cervical chain lesion, Pancoast tumor, carotid injury, T1–T2 anterior roots</li>\n<li>No Horner: cervical anterior roots (no outflow), posterior roots/rootlets (sensory)</li>\n<li>Vagus → hoarseness; phrenic (C3–C5) → diaphragm; posterior rami → back</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The sympathetic division","sn":2,"pn":"2.2"},{"l":"ANAT-G6","h":"Splanchnic nerves, plexuses and visceral pain","p":"<h4>What a splanchnic nerve is</h4>\n<p>A <b>splanchnic nerve</b> carries fibers to and from the viscera of a body cavity. The name alone does not say what kind of fibers it holds, so each one has to be learned. The <b>cardiopulmonary</b> splanchnic nerves leave the cervical and upper thoracic chain after synapsing there, so they carry <b>postganglionic</b> sympathetic fibers to the heart, lungs and esophagus. The abdominopelvic splanchnic nerves (greater, lesser, least, lumbar and sacral) carry <b>preganglionic sympathetic</b> fibers that passed through the chain without synapsing. The pelvic splanchnic nerves are the exception: they are preganglionic parasympathetic (3.2).</p>\n<h4>The route to an abdominal organ</h4>\n<p>The fiber starts in the lateral horn of the organ's segments and leaves by the ventral root and white ramus. It passes straight through the chain as a splanchnic nerve and synapses in a <b>prevertebral ganglion</b> on the front of the aorta. The postganglionic fibers then form plexuses around the aortic branches and ride those arteries to the organ.</p>\n<p>The <b>greater splanchnic</b> nerve comes from thoracic 5 to 9 (T5–T9). It synapses in the celiac ganglion and serves the foregut: stomach, liver, gallbladder, pancreas and upper duodenum. The <b>lesser splanchnic</b> (T10–T11) synapses in the aorticorenal and superior mesenteric ganglia and serves the midgut: small intestine, appendix, and ascending and transverse colon. The <b>least splanchnic</b> (T12) goes to the renal plexus ganglia for the kidney. The <b>lumbar splanchnics</b> (lumbar 1 to 2, L1–L2) go to the inferior mesenteric ganglion and the superior hypogastric plexus, for the hindgut and pelvis. The sacral splanchnics leave the sacral chain for the inferior hypogastric plexus.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G008-19.jpg\" alt=\"Sympathetic recap: splanchnic nerves from the chain to the prevertebral ganglia and organs\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G008-19c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Sympathetic recap: splanchnic nerves from the chain to the prevertebral ganglia and organs\"></div></div><figcaption>Sympathetic recap: splanchnic nerves from the chain to the prevertebral ganglia and organs <small>· 2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 19</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The plexuses on the aorta</h4>\n<p>Each prevertebral plexus is named for the artery it surrounds and sits at that artery's level. The celiac plexus surrounds the celiac trunk at the twelfth thoracic vertebra (T12). The superior mesenteric plexus surrounds the superior mesenteric artery at the first lumbar vertebra (L1). The renal plexus lies on the renal arteries at L1–L2. The inferior mesenteric plexus surrounds the inferior mesenteric artery at L3. Below the aortic bifurcation, in front of the fifth lumbar vertebra (L5) and the sacral promontory, lies the <b>superior hypogastric plexus</b>. It splits into right and left hypogastric nerves, which run down into the pelvis to supply the pelvic viscera.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G008-14.jpg\" alt=\"Splanchnic nerves on the posterior wall joining the plexuses on the aorta\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G008-14c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Splanchnic nerves on the posterior wall joining the plexuses on the aorta\"></div></div><figcaption>Splanchnic nerves on the posterior wall joining the plexuses on the aorta <small>· 2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 14</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Visceral pain runs the sympathetic route backward</h4>\n<p>Pain fibers from an organ travel back with its sympathetic fibers: through the plexus, the splanchnic nerve, the chain and the white ramus into the spinal nerve. Their cell bodies sit in the dorsal root ganglia of the organ's segments. The brain therefore feels the pain in the skin of those segments, and the pain is dull, poorly localized and near the midline.</p>\n<p>Each organ's pain lands in the skin of its own segments. The stomach, liver, gallbladder and pancreas (T7–T9) send pain through the celiac plexus and greater splanchnic nerves to the epigastrium. Liver and gallbladder pain is also felt deep under the right costal margin. The small intestine (T8–T10) and the appendix and colon (T10–T11) send pain through the superior mesenteric plexus and lesser splanchnic nerve to the skin around the umbilicus, the T10 dermatome. The kidney (T11–L1) sends pain to the flank and toward the groin. The rectum, bladder and uterus (T12–L2) send pain above the pubis.</p>\n<p>Appendicitis shows both kinds of pain. At first the inflamed appendix gives dull pain around the umbilicus, carried by the lesser splanchnic nerve. Once it inflames the <b>parietal peritoneum</b>, which has somatic nerves, the pain turns sharp and moves to the right lower quadrant, right over the organ.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G008-9.jpg\" alt=\"Spinal segments that supply each organ: the map for visceral pain\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G008-9c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Spinal segments that supply each organ: the map for visceral pain\"></div></div><figcaption>Spinal segments that supply each organ: the map for visceral pain <small>· 2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 9</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Splanchnic name ≠ fiber type; cardiopulmonary = postganglionic sympathetic</li>\n<li>Greater, lesser, least, lumbar, sacral = preganglionic sympathetic; pelvic = parasympathetic</li>\n<li>Greater T5–T9 → celiac ganglion → foregut</li>\n<li>Lesser T10–T11 → aorticorenal + superior mesenteric ganglia → midgut</li>\n<li>Least T12 → renal plexus; lumbar L1–L2 → inferior mesenteric + superior hypogastric</li>\n<li>Plexus levels: celiac T12, superior mesenteric L1, renal L1–L2, inferior mesenteric L3</li>\n<li>Superior hypogastric: below bifurcation, in front of L5 → right + left hypogastric nerves</li>\n<li>Visceral pain: back along sympathetics → dorsal root ganglia → dull, midline</li>\n<li>Foregut T7–T9 epigastric (liver/gallbladder also right subcostal); appendix T10 periumbilical</li>\n<li>Kidney T11–L1 flank; parietal peritoneum → sharp, local pain (appendix → right lower quadrant)</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The sympathetic division","sn":2,"pn":"2.3"},{"l":"ANAT-G6","h":"Cranial outflow and the vagus","p":"<h4>Where parasympathetic neurons sit</h4>\n<p>The parasympathetic division is <b>craniosacral</b>. Its preganglionic cell bodies lie in two places: the brainstem nuclei of four cranial nerves (CN), III, VII, IX and X, and the lateral horn of sacral segments 2 to 4 (S2–S4). No other cranial nerve, such as the trigeminal (CN V) or the accessory (CN XI), has a parasympathetic nucleus. The sacral cell bodies sit in the lateral horn, not in the posterior (dorsal) horn, which is sensory. Any autonomic cell body inside the spinal cord is preganglionic, so the gray matter of S2–S4 holds preganglionic parasympathetic neurons. The postganglionic cell bodies lie in the four head ganglia or in small ganglia in or next to the organ walls.</p>\n<p>Several structures sound parasympathetic but are sympathetic. The paravertebral ganglia (the chain) and the prevertebral ganglia in front of the vertebral column and aorta (celiac and mesenteric) are sympathetic. The white rami carry sympathetic preganglionic fibers; parasympathetic fibers never use the rami. And activity during stress is the sympathetic fight-or-flight response.</p>\n<h4>The four cranial nerves</h4>\n<p>The oculomotor nerve (CN III) synapses in the ciliary ganglion. It <b>constricts the pupil</b> and contracts the ciliary muscle for near vision. The facial nerve (CN VII) synapses in the pterygopalatine and submandibular ganglia and drives the lacrimal, nasal and palatine glands and the submandibular and sublingual glands. The glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) synapses in the otic ganglion and drives the parotid gland. It also carries sensory input from the carotid sinus, which senses blood pressure. The <b>vagus</b> (CN X) carries sensory input from the carotid body, which senses blood oxygen. Its parasympathetic fibers supply the heart, the lungs, the esophagus and the gut down to the <b>left colic (splenic) flexure</b>.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G008-25.jpg\" alt=\"Parasympathetic outflow: CN III, VII, IX, X and pelvic splanchnics from S2–S4\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G008-25c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Parasympathetic outflow: CN III, VII, IX, X and pelvic splanchnics from S2–S4\"></div></div><figcaption>Parasympathetic outflow: CN III, VII, IX, X and pelvic splanchnics from S2–S4 <small>· 2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 25</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The vagus to the gut</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>The preganglionic cell bodies lie in the brainstem (medulla).</li>\n<li>Long preganglionic fibers run down the vagus through the neck and thorax.</li>\n<li>They join postganglionic sympathetic fibers in mixed plexuses: the cardiac, pulmonary and esophageal plexuses, then the prevertebral plexuses on the aorta.</li>\n<li>They pass through those plexuses, and along the arteries, without synapsing. Passing the celiac plexus is not synapsing there, because the celiac ganglion is sympathetic.</li>\n<li>They synapse in tiny <b>enteric ganglia</b> inside the wall of the organ, in the myenteric and submucosal plexuses.</li>\n<li>Very short postganglionic fibers reach the smooth muscle and glands.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Because the postganglionic neurons live inside the gut wall, any vagal fiber cut outside the wall of the small intestine is a preganglionic fiber. The vagus also has its own inferior (nodose) ganglion below the skull, but it holds sensory cell bodies, and no autonomic synapse happens there. The left colic flexure is where the embryonic midgut meets the hindgut. Beyond it, the pelvic splanchnic nerves take over (3.2).</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G008-22.jpg\" alt=\"Vagus nerves joining sympathetic fibers in the cardiac, pulmonary and esophageal plexuses\"></div><figcaption>Vagus nerves joining sympathetic fibers in the cardiac, pulmonary and esophageal plexuses <small>· 2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 22</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The vagus in the thorax</h4>\n<p>In the thorax the vagus slows the heart. Through the pulmonary plexus it also <b>constricts bronchial smooth muscle</b> and increases bronchial secretion, as in the bronchospasm of an asthma attack. Sympathetic fibers do the opposite and dilate the bronchi. Other nerves of the region have different jobs. The phrenic nerve is motor to the diaphragm, the intercostal nerves supply the muscles and skin of the chest wall, and the greater and lesser thoracic splanchnic nerves carry sympathetic fibers down to the abdomen (2.3).</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Parasympathetic = craniosacral: CN III, VII, IX, X + lateral horn S2–S4 (never CN V or XI, never dorsal horn)</li>\n<li>S2–S4 gray matter = preganglionic parasympathetic cell bodies</li>\n<li>Postganglionic bodies: 4 head ganglia or organ-wall ganglia; never chain or prevertebral</li>\n<li>Never in white or gray rami; stress response = sympathetic</li>\n<li>CN III → ciliary ganglion → pupil constricts, near vision</li>\n<li>CN VII → pterygopalatine + submandibular; CN IX → otic → parotid, carotid sinus</li>\n<li>CN X: carotid body; heart, lungs, gut to left colic (splenic) flexure</li>\n<li>Vagal preganglionic fibers pass celiac plexus → synapse in enteric ganglia (myenteric, submucosal) → very short postganglionic</li>\n<li>Inferior (nodose) vagal ganglion = sensory, no synapse</li>\n<li>Vagus: slows heart; constricts bronchi, more secretion (asthma bronchospasm)</li>\n<li>Phrenic: diaphragm; intercostal: chest wall; thoracic splanchnics: sympathetic to abdomen</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The parasympathetic division","sn":3,"pn":"3.1"},{"l":"ANAT-G6","h":"Sacral outflow: the pelvic splanchnic nerves","p":"<h4>The path from S2–S4</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>The preganglionic cell bodies lie in the lateral horn of sacral 2 to 4 (S2–S4).</li>\n<li>Their axons leave through the anterior roots into the anterior rami of S2–S4.</li>\n<li>They branch off those rami as the <b>pelvic splanchnic nerves</b>, which carry preganglionic parasympathetic fibers.</li>\n<li>They join the <b>inferior hypogastric (pelvic) plexus</b> beside the rectum and bladder.</li>\n<li>They pass through it to the pelvic organs, and some fibers climb out of the pelvis to the descending and sigmoid colon.</li>\n<li>They synapse in small ganglia in the organ walls, and very short postganglionic fibers finish the path.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Because the synapse is in the organ wall, an injury to the pelvic splanchnic nerves themselves, as in a pelvic fracture, is a preganglionic injury.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G008-23.jpg\" alt=\"Pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2–S4) and hypogastric nerves forming the inferior hypogastric plexus\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G008-23c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2–S4) and hypogastric nerves forming the inferior hypogastric plexus\"></div></div><figcaption>Pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2–S4) and hypogastric nerves forming the inferior hypogastric plexus <small>· 2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 23</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>What they do</h4>\n<p>The pelvic splanchnics supply the gut beyond the left colic flexure: the descending colon, sigmoid colon and rectum. There they increase peristalsis, contract the rectum and relax the internal anal sphincter, which allows defecation. In the bladder they contract the detrusor muscle and relax the internal sphincter, which empties it. In the external genitalia they cause erection. When S2–S4 outflow is lost, from a sacral cord injury or a pelvic fracture, the bladder and rectum cannot empty. The patient has urinary retention and constipation, and peristalsis of the descending colon weakens.</p>\n<p>Some gut functions do not depend on these nerves. Peristalsis of the jejunum and enzyme secretion in the stomach are driven by the vagus. Contraction of the internal anal sphincter and constriction of the colon's blood vessels are sympathetic jobs.</p>\n<h4>The inferior hypogastric plexus is mixed</h4>\n<p>Three inputs meet in this plexus. The pelvic splanchnic nerves bring preganglionic parasympathetic fibers from S2–S4. The right and left <b>hypogastric nerves</b> come down from the superior hypogastric plexus, in front of the fifth lumbar vertebra (L5), where lumbar splanchnic fibers have synapsed, so they carry postganglionic sympathetic fibers. The <b>sacral splanchnic nerves</b> bring preganglionic sympathetic fibers from the sacral part of the chain. Do not confuse this autonomic plexus with the <b>sacral plexus</b>, the somatic nerve plexus that forms the sciatic and pudendal nerves.</p>\n<h4>Who supplies which part of the gut</h4>\n<p>The gut's parasympathetic supply switches once, at the left colic flexure, while its sympathetic supply changes with each gut segment. The foregut (esophagus, stomach, upper duodenum, liver and pancreas) gets parasympathetic fibers from the vagus and sympathetic fibers from the greater splanchnic nerve through the celiac ganglion. The midgut (the rest of the small intestine, the appendix, and the ascending and transverse colon) also gets the vagus, with sympathetic fibers from the lesser splanchnic nerve through the superior mesenteric ganglion. The hindgut beyond the left colic flexure (descending and sigmoid colon and rectum) gets parasympathetic fibers from the <b>pelvic splanchnics</b> and sympathetic fibers from the lumbar splanchnics through the inferior mesenteric ganglion.</p>\n<p>Two names are easy to swap. The <b>pelvic</b> splanchnics are parasympathetic and never sympathetic. The <b>sacral</b> splanchnics are sympathetic. Every other abdominopelvic splanchnic nerve (greater, lesser, least and lumbar) is preganglionic sympathetic.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Pelvic splanchnics: S2–S4 anterior rami, preganglionic parasympathetic</li>\n<li>→ inferior hypogastric plexus → synapse in organ walls; pelvic fracture = preganglionic injury</li>\n<li>Supply: gut beyond left colic flexure, bladder, erectile tissue</li>\n<li>Actions: ↑ peristalsis, rectum contracts, internal anal sphincter relaxes; detrusor contracts</li>\n<li>S2–S4 loss → urinary retention + constipation, weak descending colon peristalsis</li>\n<li>Internal anal sphincter contraction, colonic vasoconstriction = sympathetic; jejunum, stomach = vagus</li>\n<li>Inferior hypogastric (mixed) inputs: pelvic splanchnics + hypogastric nerves (from L5 plexus) + sacral splanchnics; ≠ sacral plexus (somatic)</li>\n<li>Hypogastric nerves: postganglionic sympathetic</li>\n<li>Foregut: vagus + greater → celiac; midgut: vagus + lesser → superior mesenteric</li>\n<li>Hindgut: pelvic splanchnics + lumbar → inferior mesenteric; pelvic = parasympathetic, sacral = sympathetic</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The parasympathetic division","sn":3,"pn":"3.2"},{"l":"ANAT-G6","h":"What each division does, and its transmitters","p":"<h4>Fight or flight</h4>\n<p>The easiest way to recall each effect is to picture the situation. The sympathetic division readies the body for <b>fight or flight</b>. The pupil <b>dilates</b> to let in more light for distance vision. The heart beats <b>faster and harder</b>, and its coronary vessels dilate. The bronchi dilate and secrete less, so air moves freely. The gut is put on hold: <b>peristalsis slows</b>, the gut vessels constrict so that blood goes to skeletal muscle, and the internal anal sphincter contracts to keep continence. The liver breaks glycogen down to glucose for energy. In the urinary tract the renal vessels constrict, which slows urine formation, and the internal sphincter of the bladder contracts. Salivary and lacrimal secretion falls a little and turns thick. In the skin, sweat glands switch on, hairs stand up (goose bumps) and vessels constrict. Sympathetic fibers cause ejaculation and end an erection, and they make the adrenal medulla release adrenaline into the blood.</p>\n<h4>Rest and digest</h4>\n<p>The parasympathetic division does the reverse, for <b>rest and digest</b>: it conserves energy and promotes digestion. The pupil <b>constricts</b>, and the ciliary muscle contracts so the lens thickens for near vision. The heart <b>slows</b> and beats less forcefully, and the coronary vessels constrict to match the lower demand. The bronchi constrict and secrete more. The gut speeds up: <b>peristalsis and digestive secretions increase</b>, including gastric enzymes, the rectum contracts and the internal anal sphincter relaxes for defecation. The liver stores glycogen and bile secretion rises. The detrusor contracts and the internal sphincter relaxes, so the bladder empties. Salivary and lacrimal glands give abundant, watery secretion, and the erectile tissue of the external genitals fills (erection). Parasympathetic fibers do not reach the skin, the sweat glands or the adrenal medulla.</p>\n<p>Two general rules help. Sympathetic fibers reach every blood vessel and constrict it, except the coronaries, which they dilate. They are the only supply to sweat glands, and they reduce the secretion of every other gland. Sympathetic action is diffuse, spreading through the whole body, while parasympathetic action is focal.</p>\n<h4>Transmitters, synapse by synapse</h4>\n<p>Acetylcholine (ACh) is the transmitter almost everywhere. Every preganglionic fiber, sympathetic or parasympathetic, releases ACh onto the postganglionic neuron in its ganglion. Parasympathetic postganglionic endings release ACh onto the target. Somatic motor neurons also release ACh, at the motor end plate of skeletal muscle.</p>\n<p>The main exception is the sympathetic postganglionic ending, which releases <b>norepinephrine (NE)</b> onto the target. A drug that blocks NE release therefore acts at postganglionic sympathetic terminals and leaves every other synapse working. Even here there is one exception: the sympathetic fibers to sweat glands release ACh.</p>\n<p>The adrenal (suprarenal) medulla is the odd one out. Some greater splanchnic preganglionic fibers pass through the celiac ganglion without synapsing and end directly on the medulla's cells. These cells act like postganglionic neurons, but they release adrenaline (epinephrine) and noradrenaline into the blood, which spreads a body-wide sympathetic response.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G008-15.jpg\" alt=\"Greater splanchnic preganglionic fibers passing the celiac ganglion to the adrenal medulla\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G008-15c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Greater splanchnic preganglionic fibers passing the celiac ganglion to the adrenal medulla\"></div></div><figcaption>Greater splanchnic preganglionic fibers passing the celiac ganglion to the adrenal medulla <small>· 2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 15</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Sympathetic = fight or flight; parasympathetic = rest and digest (conserves energy, promotes digestion)</li>\n<li>Sympathetic: pupil dilates, heart faster + stronger, coronaries dilate, bronchi dilate</li>\n<li>Sympathetic gut: ↓ peristalsis, gut vasoconstriction, internal anal sphincter contracts</li>\n<li>Sympathetic: glycogen → glucose, bladder sphincter contracts, sweating, ejaculation, adrenaline</li>\n<li>Parasympathetic: pupil constricts + near vision, heart slows, bronchi constrict + secrete</li>\n<li>Parasympathetic gut: ↑ peristalsis + gastric enzymes, rectum contracts, internal anal sphincter relaxes</li>\n<li>Parasympathetic: detrusor contracts (urination), watery saliva, more bile, erection</li>\n<li>Only sympathetic: skin, sweat glands, adrenal medulla; constricts all vessels except coronaries</li>\n<li>ACh: every ganglion, parasympathetic endings, somatic motor end plate</li>\n<li>NE: sympathetic postganglionic endings (sweat glands = ACh) → NE blocker acts here</li>\n<li>Adrenal medulla: preganglionic fibers end directly → adrenaline + noradrenaline into blood</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The two divisions side by side","sn":4,"pn":"4.1"},{"l":"ANAT-G7","h":"Chest film views and the heart borders","p":"<h4>How a film is read</h4>\n<p>An X-ray film is a shadow picture. Dense tissue stops the beam and looks white, and air lets it through and looks black. From dark to bright the order is air, fat, soft tissue and fluid, bone, then metal and contrast. The heart and the blood inside it are both soft tissue, so no edge shows between them. A heart border appears only where air-filled lung touches the side of the heart or a vessel. Films are hung as if you were facing the patient, so the patient's right is on the image's left.</p>\n<h4>PA and AP films</h4>\n<p>The standard chest study is an erect <b>PA (posteroanterior)</b> film plus a lateral film. In a PA film the beam enters the patient's back, and the front of the chest lies against the detector. The heart sits in front, close to the detector, so it is shown at true size. The patient rolls the shoulders forward, so the scapulae move out of the lung fields, and the clavicles angle downward toward the midline.</p>\n<p>Patients in bed get an <b>AP (anteroposterior)</b> film instead, with the beam entering the front. Now the heart is far from the detector, so it looks <b>magnified</b>, and a wide heart on an AP film may be normal. The scapulae overlie the lung fields and the clavicles lie flatter.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G009-7.jpg\" alt=\"PA and AP films side by side: the heart looks wider on AP\"></div><figcaption>PA and AP films side by side: the heart looks wider on AP <small>· 2026 lecture: Radiology of the Thorax, slide 7</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The borders on the PA film</h4>\n<p>Trace the <b>right border</b> from top to bottom. The upper part is a straight vertical edge made by the <b>superior vena cava (SVC)</b>. At the angle between the trachea and the right main bronchus, the azygos vein can add a small bump. Below that, the curved border that runs down to the diaphragm is the <b>right atrium</b>, which makes almost all of the right heart border. The inferior vena cava (IVC) adds at most a small piece at the right cardiophrenic angle.</p>\n<p>Now trace the <b>left border</b>. At the top is the rounded <b>aortic knob</b>, the arch seen end-on. Just below it is a second bulge made by the pulmonary trunk and the <b>left pulmonary artery</b>. Next comes the left atrial appendage, which is flat unless the left atrium is enlarged. The lowest and largest curve, ending at the apex, is the <b>left ventricle</b>. The right ventricle faces forward, between the two borders, so it makes no side border at all. The esophagus and the main bronchi lie inside the mediastinum and make no border either.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G009-11.jpg\" alt=\"PA film: SVC and right atrium right; aorta, pulmonary artery, left ventricle left\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G009-11c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: PA film: SVC and right atrium right; aorta, pulmonary artery, left ventricle left\"></div></div><figcaption>PA film: SVC and right atrium right; aorta, pulmonary artery, left ventricle left <small>· 2026 lecture: Radiology of the Thorax, slide 11</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The borders on the lateral film</h4>\n<p>On a lateral film, look just behind the sternum. The front border of the heart there is the <b>right ventricle</b>, the most anterior chamber. The back border is made by the left atrium above and the left ventricle below.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G009-13.jpg\" alt=\"Lateral film: right ventricle in front, left atrium and left ventricle behind\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G009-13c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Lateral film: right ventricle in front, left atrium and left ventricle behind\"></div></div><figcaption>Lateral film: right ventricle in front, left atrium and left ventricle behind <small>· 2026 lecture: Radiology of the Thorax, slide 13</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The same chambers on CT</h4>\n<p>On computed tomography (CT) the patient's right is also on the image left. On an axial slice, find the sternum at the top. The chamber pressed directly behind it is the <b>right ventricle</b>. Inside it, the <b>moderator band</b> (septomarginal trabecula) is a muscle bar running from the interventricular septum to the anterior papillary muscle, carrying part of the right bundle branch. The chamber on the patient's right (image left), making the right heart edge, is the <b>right atrium</b>. The thick-walled chamber running toward the apex on the patient's left is the <b>left ventricle</b>. The chamber at the back, just in front of the esophagus and descending aorta, is the <b>left atrium</b>.</p>\n<p>A four-chamber view shows the same pattern: right ventricle on top, right atrium behind it on the image left, the long left ventricle running to the apex, and the left atrium at the back. On a coronal slice, the right atrium makes the lower right heart edge and the left ventricle the apex, with the SVC above on the patient's right and the aortic arch, brachiocephalic trunk and pulmonary trunk toward the left.</p>\n<h4>Counting ribs</h4>\n<p>Each rib shows twice on a PA film. Its <b>posterior</b> part runs almost flat outward from the spine and is the easiest part to count. Its <b>anterior</b> part slopes down toward the sternum and fades into cartilage. Count the posterior ribs from the top, starting beside the spine; on a good breath in, about ten lie above the diaphragm.</p>\n<h4>Where the valves are heard</h4>\n<p>The <b>aortic area</b> is the right upper sternal border, the <b>pulmonary area</b> the left upper sternal border, and the <b>tricuspid area</b> the lower sternal border. The <b>mitral (bicuspid) valve</b>, the two-cusp valve between the left atrium and the left ventricle, is heard at the <b>apex</b>, the left fifth intercostal space at the midclavicular line, so mitral stenosis is loudest there. A congenitally bicuspid aortic valve is a different valve, heard in the aortic area.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>White = dense, black = air; border only where lung touches; image left = patient's right</li>\n<li>PA: erect, heart true size, scapulae out of lungs, clavicles angled down</li>\n<li>AP: bedbound, heart magnified, scapulae over lungs, clavicles flatter</li>\n<li>PA right border: SVC (straight) → right atrium (curved, to diaphragm); azygos bump; IVC tiny</li>\n<li>PA left border: aortic knob → left pulmonary artery bulge → left atrial appendage → left ventricle</li>\n<li>Lateral: right ventricle = front border (no PA border); left atrium + left ventricle = back border</li>\n<li>CT: right ventricle behind sternum; right atrium image left; left ventricle to apex; left atrium at back</li>\n<li>Moderator band: right ventricle, septum → anterior papillary muscle, right bundle branch</li>\n<li>Ribs: count the flat posterior parts from the top; anterior parts slope down to the sternum</li>\n<li>Listening: aortic right upper sternal border, pulmonary left upper, tricuspid lower, mitral (bicuspid) at apex</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"The heart on the chest film","sn":1,"pn":"1.1"},{"l":"ANAT-G7","h":"The thoracic inlet and the airways","p":"<h4>How to read a chest CT</h4>\n<p>Axial slices of computed tomography (CT) are viewed from the patient's feet, with the front of the chest at the top. The patient's right is therefore on the image left. Coronal slices are viewed from the front, with the same left–right rule. Air is black, fat dark gray, muscle and blood gray, and contrast and bone white. A lung window shows the lungs gray and the airways black. A soft-tissue window turns the lungs black and spreads the mediastinum into shades of gray.</p>\n<h4>The thoracic inlet, front to back</h4>\n<p>At the top of the chest, find the bones first. The flat bone in the midline at the very front is the <b>manubrium of the sternum</b>, and the clavicles meet it at the sides. The vertebral body is the midline bone at the back, and the ribs curve around the sides. Now read the space between them from front to back.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The brachiocephalic veins lie just behind the manubrium. The right one sits at the front on the patient's right, and the left one crosses in front of the arteries to join it.</li>\n<li>Behind the veins lie the three branches of the aortic arch: the <b>brachiocephalic trunk</b> in front of and right of the trachea, the <b>left common carotid</b> left of the trachea, and the <b>left subclavian</b> furthest left and furthest back.</li>\n<li>The <b>trachea</b> is the round tube in the midline, black because it holds air.</li>\n<li>The esophagus is the small soft-tissue tube just behind the trachea.</li>\n<li>The vertebral body comes next, with the spinal cord behind it.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>A short way to hold the order is veins, arteries, airway, esophagus, spine. The aortic arch itself appears a little lower, as a curved vessel on the left of the trachea. A bronchoscope passes through the larynx into the <b>trachea</b>, so it enters the black midline ring. An endoscope for the gut goes into the esophagus behind it instead.</p>\n<p>The chest wall wraps around this. In front, pectoralis major lies over pectoralis minor. Serratus anterior covers the side of the ribs. At the back, the scapula has subscapularis on its front surface and supraspinatus and infraspinatus behind it. Rhomboid major runs between the scapula and the spine, and erector spinae lies beside the spine.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G009-33.jpg\" alt=\"Axial CT at the thoracic inlet in a soft-tissue window\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G009-33c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Axial CT at the thoracic inlet in a soft-tissue window\"></div></div><figcaption>Axial CT at the thoracic inlet in a soft-tissue window <small>· 2026 lecture: Radiology of the Thorax, slide 33</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The airways</h4>\n<p>Follow the trachea down a coronal slice. It is one midline tube until the <b>carina</b>, the ridge where it splits into the two main bronchi. The <b>right main bronchus</b> is wider, shorter and more vertical. It ends almost at once, where the right upper lobe bronchus leaves. The air tube that carries on below that point is the <b>bronchus intermedius</b>, which runs behind the right pulmonary artery to the middle and lower lobes. On an axial slice below the carina, look on the patient's right (image left) for an air tube just behind the right pulmonary artery: that is the bronchus intermedius. The <b>left main bronchus</b> is longer, narrower and more horizontal, pushed aside by the aortic arch and the heart.</p>\n<p>The right lung has three lobes, separated by the horizontal and oblique fissures. The left lung has two lobes and only an oblique fissure, and the lingula belongs to its upper lobe.</p>\n<p>An inhaled object follows the widest, straightest path, so it usually lodges in the <b>right main bronchus</b>. An object small enough to pass the larynx seldom stops in the oropharynx or trachea. A swallowed object enters the esophagus instead and causes trouble swallowing, not choking.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G009-22.jpg\" alt=\"Coronal CT: carina, main bronchi and the bronchus intermedius (arrow)\"></div><figcaption>Coronal CT: carina, main bronchi and the bronchus intermedius (arrow) <small>· 2026 lecture: Radiology of the Thorax, slide 22</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Lobes on the chest film: opacities and the silhouette sign</h4>\n<p>A white patch in the lung is an <b>opacity</b>. In <b>consolidation</b>, as in pneumonia, fluid fills the air spaces, so the patch is dense and hides the vessel markings; a ground-glass opacity is a fainter haze that still shows them. A heart border shows only where air touches it, so a consolidation lying against the heart erases that border: the <b>silhouette sign</b>. On a posteroanterior (PA) chest film the <b>right middle lobe</b> lies in the lower right zone against the right atrium, so a right middle lobe pneumonia blurs the right heart border. The right lower lobe lies behind it, on the diaphragm, and blurs the diaphragm instead. Other problems look different: a pneumothorax is a black space with no lung markings, tuberculosis favors the upper lobes, and an ascending aortic aneurysm widens the upper right mediastinum.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Axial CT: viewed from feet, front at top, patient's right = image left</li>\n<li>Air black, fat dark gray, soft tissue gray, bone and contrast white</li>\n<li>Inlet front → back: manubrium → brachiocephalic veins → arch branches → trachea → esophagus → vertebra</li>\n<li>Brachiocephalic trunk front-right of trachea; left common carotid left; left subclavian furthest left/back</li>\n<li>Right brachiocephalic vein front-right; left one crosses in front of the arteries</li>\n<li>Bronchoscope → trachea (black midline ring); gut endoscope → esophagus behind it</li>\n<li>Pectoralis major over minor; scapula: subscapularis in front, supra/infraspinatus behind</li>\n<li>Right main bronchus: wide, short, vertical → inhaled objects lodge here</li>\n<li>Bronchus intermedius: below right upper lobe bronchus, behind right pulmonary artery</li>\n<li>Right lung 3 lobes (2 fissures); left 2 lobes, lingula in upper lobe</li>\n<li>Consolidation hides markings; right middle lobe pneumonia blurs right heart border (silhouette sign)</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Chest CT and heart angiography","sn":2,"pn":"2.1"},{"l":"ANAT-G7","h":"Great vessels above the heart","p":"<h4>Arch level</h4>\n<p>A little below the thoracic inlet, the <b>aortic arch</b> appears on axial computed tomography (CT) as a curved, sausage-shaped vessel running from front-right to back-left beside the trachea. The <b>superior vena cava (SVC)</b> is a round vessel on the patient's right of the trachea.</p>\n<h4>Pulmonary trunk level</h4>\n<p>At and just below the carina, the great vessels sit in a tight group at the front of the mediastinum. Find the <b>pulmonary trunk</b> first: it leaves the heart at the front, on the patient's left, and divides into the right and left pulmonary arteries. The large round vessel just to its right, in the middle of the front of the mediastinum, is the <b>ascending aorta</b>. The smaller round vessel just right of the ascending aorta and slightly behind it is the <b>SVC</b>. The right pulmonary artery is a horizontal tube passing behind the ascending aorta and the SVC, so it never looks like a round dot. The left pulmonary artery curves backward over the left main bronchus.</p>\n<p>Now move to the back of the chest. The round bright vessel just left of the vertebral body, and slightly in front of it, is the <b>descending aorta</b>. Directly behind the trachea or carina, and directly in front of the vertebral body, lies the <b>esophagus</b>, a small soft-tissue tube. The azygos vein lies to the right of the esophagus, against the spine. The thoracic duct is tiny and lies behind the esophagus, between the aorta and the azygos vein. The left main bronchus is black with air and sits to the left of the carina, not behind it.</p>\n<p>So this level has two round vessels in front (the ascending aorta, with the SVC to its right), one round vessel at the back by the spine (the descending aorta), and the esophagus between the airway and the spine.</p>\n<p>The SVC receives both brachiocephalic veins, so it drains both arms, the head and the neck. A block in the SVC, from a clot or a tumor, makes both arms, the face and the neck swell (<b>SVC syndrome</b>). A block in one brachiocephalic vein swells only one arm.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G009-47.jpg\" alt=\"Axial CT at the pulmonary trunk level; the arrow marks the pulmonary trunk\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G009-47c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Axial CT at the pulmonary trunk level; the arrow marks the pulmonary trunk\"></div></div><figcaption>Axial CT at the pulmonary trunk level; the arrow marks the pulmonary trunk <small>· 2026 lecture: Radiology of the Thorax, slide 47</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The aorta on an aortogram</h4>\n<p>An aortogram fills the aorta with contrast. The ascending aorta rises from the heart and turns into the arch. The arch gives three branches, in order from the patient's right: the brachiocephalic trunk, the left common carotid artery and the left subclavian artery. The descending aorta then runs down beside the spine.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G009-106.jpg\" alt=\"Aortogram: ascending aorta, arch with its three branches, descending aorta\"></div><figcaption>Aortogram: ascending aorta, arch with its three branches, descending aorta <small>· 2026 lecture: Radiology of the Thorax, slide 106</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Coarctation of the aorta</h4>\n<p>In <b>coarctation of the aorta</b> the aorta is narrowed just past the origin of the left subclavian artery. The arms are supplied before the narrowing and the legs after it. Pulse and blood pressure in the arms are therefore high, while the femoral pulses are weak or absent and the legs are cold. Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) or angiography shows the narrowing on the descending aorta just beyond the arch. Other congenital problems look different. Tetralogy of Fallot causes cyanosis, with no gap between arm and leg pressure. An atrial septal defect is a hole between the atria, and the pulses stay normal. Esophageal atresia causes feeding problems, and a hiatal hernia is stomach pushed up through the diaphragm.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Arch: curved vessel, front-right → back-left beside trachea</li>\n<li>Pulmonary trunk: front, patient's left; splits into right + left pulmonary arteries</li>\n<li>Ascending aorta: large, round, mid-front, right of pulmonary trunk</li>\n<li>SVC: smaller, round, right of and slightly behind ascending aorta</li>\n<li>SVC block → both arms, face and neck swell; one brachiocephalic vein → one arm</li>\n<li>Right pulmonary artery: horizontal tube behind ascending aorta + SVC</li>\n<li>Descending aorta: round, left of and slightly in front of vertebral body</li>\n<li>Esophagus: behind trachea/carina, in front of vertebra; azygos to its right; thoracic duct behind</li>\n<li>Arch branches: brachiocephalic trunk → left common carotid → left subclavian</li>\n<li>Coarctation: past left subclavian → high arm pressure, weak femoral pulses, cold legs</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Chest CT and heart angiography","sn":2,"pn":"2.2"},{"l":"ANAT-G7","h":"The heart on CT and angiograms","p":"<h4>The heart on axial CT</h4>\n<p>On an axial computed tomography (CT) slice through the heart, every chamber lies behind the sternum, so position inside the heart must sort them. The chamber pressed directly against the back of the sternum is the <b>right ventricle</b>, the most anterior chamber. The chamber on the patient's right (image left), making the right heart edge, is the <b>right atrium</b>. The thick-walled chamber on the patient's left, running toward the apex, is the <b>left ventricle</b>. The most posterior chamber is the <b>left atrium</b>.</p>\n<p>At the base of the heart, look in the center for the round <b>aortic root</b>. The root of the pulmonary trunk lies in front of it and to its left, leaving the right ventricle; of the two roots, the pulmonary one is the more anterior. The right atrium wraps the right side of the heart at this level. The round descending aorta sits left of the spine, behind the left atrium.</p>\n<h4>The left atrium and the esophagus</h4>\n<p>The left atrium lies <b>directly in front of the esophagus</b>, at about the seventh thoracic vertebra (T7), and its auricle (appendage) points forward on the left. This contact has two practical results. An enlarged left atrium pushes the esophagus backward. And a sharp object driven straight forward through the esophageal wall at T7 enters the left atrium, not any other chamber. The coronary sinus, also at the back of the heart, is a vein in the posterior groove, not a chamber.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G009-65.jpg\" alt=\"Axial CT at the heart base: arrow on the esophagus behind the left atrium\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G009-65c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Axial CT at the heart base: arrow on the esophagus behind the left atrium\"></div></div><figcaption>Axial CT at the heart base: arrow on the esophagus behind the left atrium <small>· 2026 lecture: Radiology of the Thorax, slide 65</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Four-chamber view and cardiac CT</h4>\n<p>A four-chamber view cuts all four chambers in one slice. The right ventricle is on top, nearest the chest wall. The right atrium sits behind it on the image left. The long, thick-walled left ventricle runs to the apex on the image right. The left atrium lies at the bottom of the picture, which is the back of the heart. Cardiac CT, with slices angled along the heart, also shows the aortic and pulmonary valves, the papillary muscles and the trabeculae carneae. The apex is the tip, pointing forward and to the left. The base is the back of the heart, where the left atrium and the great vessels are.</p>\n<h4>Angiograms</h4>\n<p>In an angiogram, contrast injected through a catheter fills a chamber or vessel and makes it stand out. In a <b>left ventriculogram</b>, the large contrast-filled chamber lies at the lower left of the heart shadow, toward the apex, and the ascending aorta rises from it. The right ventricle would lead to the pulmonary trunk instead, and the left atrium sits high and at the back.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G009-104.jpg\" alt=\"Left ventriculogram: catheter looped in the left ventricle, ascending aorta above\"></div><figcaption>Left ventriculogram: catheter looped in the left ventricle, ascending aorta above <small>· 2026 lecture: Radiology of the Thorax, slide 104</small></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The coronary arteries arise from the aortic root: the right coronary from its right side and the left coronary from its left. In a <b>left coronary angiogram</b>, the catheter comes up the aorta and its tip sits in the short first segment of the left coronary artery, the <b>left main</b> coronary artery. The left main soon splits in two. The <b>left anterior descending (LAD)</b> artery runs straight down the anterior interventricular groove to the apex, through the middle of the heart shadow, and gives diagonal branches. Its blockage causes a large anterior heart attack (myocardial infarction), nicknamed the widow maker. The <b>circumflex</b> artery curves along the outer left border in the atrioventricular groove and gives marginal branches. In a right coronary angiogram, the right coronary artery runs in the right atrioventricular groove and gives the posterior descending artery.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G009-105.jpg\" alt=\"Aortic root angiogram: right and left coronary arteries arising from the aortic root\"></div><figcaption>Aortic root angiogram: right and left coronary arteries arising from the aortic root <small>· 2026 lecture: Radiology of the Thorax, slide 105</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Pulmonary veins and pulmonary embolus</h4>\n<p>The <b>pulmonary veins</b> carry oxygenated blood from the lungs into the left atrium. On an axial slice through the left atrium, they enter its back corners; the left pulmonary vein lies on the patient's left, just in front of the descending aorta. A <b>pulmonary embolus</b> is usually a clot from the leg veins, for example after a long flight. It travels up the inferior vena cava, through the right atrium and right ventricle into the pulmonary trunk, and lodges in the <b>pulmonary arteries</b>, never in the pulmonary veins. A computed tomography pulmonary angiogram fills those arteries with contrast, and the clot shows as a dark filling defect inside them.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>All chambers behind sternum; right ventricle directly against it (most anterior)</li>\n<li>Right atrium: right heart edge (image left); left ventricle: thick wall, to apex</li>\n<li>Left atrium: most posterior, directly in front of esophagus at T7</li>\n<li>Enlarged left atrium pushes esophagus back; object forward through esophagus at T7 → left atrium</li>\n<li>Heart base: aortic root central; pulmonary root in front and left of it</li>\n<li>Descending aorta: left of spine, behind left atrium</li>\n<li>Four-chamber: right ventricle top, right atrium image left, left ventricle to apex, left atrium back</li>\n<li>Ventriculogram: lower-left chamber with ascending aorta rising from it = left ventricle</li>\n<li>Left coronary angiogram: catheter tip in left main → LAD + circumflex</li>\n<li>LAD: anterior interventricular groove, diagonals, widow maker; circumflex: marginals</li>\n<li>Pulmonary veins → left atrium (left one in front of descending aorta); embolus lodges in pulmonary arteries</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Chest CT and heart angiography","sn":2,"pn":"2.3"},{"l":"ANAT-G7","h":"Orientation, the great veins and the kidneys","p":"<h4>Reading a slice</h4>\n<p>Axial computed tomography (CT) and magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) slices are viewed from the feet, with the front of the body at the top. So the patient's right is on the image left, and the patient's left on the image right. Coronal slices are viewed from the front, and the same rule holds. Ask three things of every slice. What level is it? Is there contrast (a bright aorta and bright kidneys mean intravenous contrast)? And why is this slice being shown?</p>\n<h4>Finding the structures of the upper abdomen</h4>\n<p>Start with the vertebra at the bottom center, then work outward. The <b>aorta</b> is the round vessel on the front of the vertebral body, just left of the midline. The right and left crura of the diaphragm hug it from each side. The <b>inferior vena cava (IVC)</b> is the large vein on the right side of the spine, to the right of the aorta. Higher up, at the liver dome, the IVC sits in a groove on the back of the liver, to the right of the esophagus and aorta, about to pierce the diaphragm and enter the right atrium. The hepatic veins run through the liver toward it. The right adrenal gland is a thin line just behind the IVC.</p>\n<p>The <b>liver</b> is the largest solid organ and fills the patient's right side. The falciform ligament is the fat-filled cleft at its front, between the large right lobe and the left lobe, which lies in front and reaches across the midline. The caudate lobe sits between the portal vein and the IVC.</p>\n<p>The <b>stomach</b> lies in front in the left upper abdomen. The best clue is the <b>gastric air bubble</b>, which is black, because no other organ there normally holds air. Being on the left is a weaker clue, since the spleen, colon and kidney are also on the left. Being gray proves little, as almost every soft tissue is gray, and the liver shows on so many slices that its presence proves nothing. The body of the stomach is on the left, and the pyloric antrum crosses toward the right.</p>\n<p>The <b>spleen</b> is the solid organ at the far left and back (posterolateral), behind the stomach and under ribs 9 to 11. The tail of the pancreas points to it. The left kidney lies lower and closer to the spine, and the lower lobe of the left lung, black with air, lies above the diaphragm.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G010-66.jpg\" alt=\"Upper abdominal CT: the arrow marks the stomach&#x27;s air bubble on the patient&#x27;s left\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G010-66c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Upper abdominal CT: the arrow marks the stomach&#x27;s air bubble on the patient&#x27;s left\"></div></div><figcaption>Upper abdominal CT: the arrow marks the stomach&#x27;s air bubble on the patient&#x27;s left <small>· 2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen I, slide 66</small></figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G011-20.jpg\" alt=\"Axial CT at the portal vein level: star on the IVC beside the spine\"></div><figcaption>Axial CT at the portal vein level: star on the IVC beside the spine <small>· 2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen II, slide 20</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Kidneys and renal vessels</h4>\n<p>The kidneys are bean-shaped organs beside the spine, behind the peritoneum. They lie from about the twelfth thoracic to the third lumbar vertebra (T12–L3). On an axial slice, the kidney on the image right is the <b>left kidney</b>, and the one on the image left, below the liver, is the right kidney. The round muscles on each side of the vertebral body are the psoas major muscles.</p>\n<p>On a coronal slice, the IVC is the wider vertical vessel on the patient's right (image left), and the aorta runs on the patient's left (image right). The right kidney lies below the liver, lateral to the IVC. A vessel that runs from the kidney's hilum straight into the IVC is a vein. The <b>right renal vein</b> is short and enters the IVC directly. The left renal vein is long and crosses in front of the aorta, just below the superior mesenteric artery. The renal arteries come from the aorta and lie behind the veins, and the right renal artery passes behind the IVC.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G012-60.jpg\" alt=\"Coronal CT: arrows on the right and left renal veins running to the IVC\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G012-60c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Coronal CT: arrows on the right and left renal veins running to the IVC\"></div></div><figcaption>Coronal CT: arrows on the right and left renal veins running to the IVC <small>· 2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen III, slide 60</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Axial + coronal: patient's right = image left</li>\n<li>Bright aorta + kidneys = intravenous contrast</li>\n<li>IVC: right of spine and aorta; at liver dome in groove on back of liver</li>\n<li>Aorta: just left of midline on vertebra, hugged by crura</li>\n<li>Right adrenal: thin line behind IVC; caudate lobe between portal vein + IVC</li>\n<li>Falciform ligament: fat cleft between right + left lobes</li>\n<li>Stomach: air bubble is the key clue (left side, gray, liver = weak clues)</li>\n<li>Spleen: far left, posterolateral, behind stomach, ribs 9–11; pancreatic tail points to it</li>\n<li>Kidneys T12–L3; left kidney = image right</li>\n<li>Right renal vein short, straight into IVC; left renal vein crosses in front of aorta</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Abdominal CT and MRI","sn":4,"pn":"4.1"},{"l":"ANAT-G7","h":"Liver, gallbladder, pancreas and the portal vein","p":"<h4>Liver</h4>\n<p>The liver is built of segments. Each segment has a central portal triad: branches of the portal vein, the hepatic artery and the bile duct. Blood leaves at the segment's edge through the hepatic veins, which drain into the inferior vena cava (IVC). The caudate lobe drains straight into the IVC, so it is spared in early cirrhosis.</p>\n<h4>Gallbladder</h4>\n<p>The gallbladder is a pear-shaped sac in the gallbladder fossa on the underside of the liver. It stores bile and joins the biliary tree through the <b>cystic duct</b>. It has three parts. The <b>fundus</b> is the rounded tip. The <b>body</b> is the main sac. The <b>neck</b> is the narrow end that tapers into the cystic duct. On computed tomography (CT), the fluid-filled sac in the fossa is the gallbladder, and most of the sac is its body. On ultrasound, bile is black, and a stone is a bright echo that casts a black shadow behind it.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G011-24.jpg\" alt=\"Gallbladder under the liver: fundus, body and neck, with the cystic and bile ducts\"></div><figcaption>Gallbladder under the liver: fundus, body and neck, with the cystic and bile ducts <small>· 2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen II, slide 24</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Pancreas</h4>\n<p>The pancreas is a lobulated gland, outlined by the fat around it. Its head sits inside the duodenal C-loop, and its uncinate process hooks behind the superior mesenteric vein (SMV). The <b>body</b> crosses in front of the aorta and lies <b>behind the stomach</b>, across the lesser sac. The <b>splenic vein</b> runs along the back of the body. So on an axial slice, the organ between the stomach in front and the splenic vein behind is the pancreatic body. The tail reaches the hilum of the spleen.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G011-56.jpg\" alt=\"Pancreatic body and tail (thick line), splenic vein along its back (thin line)\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G011-56c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Pancreatic body and tail (thick line), splenic vein along its back (thin line)\"></div></div><figcaption>Pancreatic body and tail (thick line), splenic vein along its back (thin line) <small>· 2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen II, slide 56</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>MRCP</h4>\n<p>Magnetic resonance cholangiopancreatography (MRCP) shows fluid-filled ducts as white. It is noninvasive and as accurate as endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography (ERCP), without its risks. Read the image from the top down. The intrahepatic ducts branch inside the liver. The gallbladder is a white sac high on the patient's right, and the <b>cystic duct</b> joins its neck to the bile duct, high up. The <b>common bile duct (CBD)</b> is the long tube running down toward the duodenum. The main pancreatic duct runs across from the patient's left to meet it. At the very bottom of the CBD, where it enters the duodenal wall together with the pancreatic duct, lie the <b>major papilla and the sphincter of Oddi</b>. The <b>minor papilla</b> is higher and drains the accessory pancreatic duct. A stone in the CBD (choledocholithiasis) shows as a dark filling defect.</p>\n<h4>The portal vein</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>The <b>SMV</b> rises vertically from below, to the right of the superior mesenteric artery.</li>\n<li>The <b>splenic vein</b> runs horizontally from the patient's left, behind the pancreas, and the smaller inferior mesenteric vein usually drains into it.</li>\n<li>The two join behind the neck of the pancreas to form the <b>portal vein</b>.</li>\n<li>The portal vein runs up to the porta hepatis and divides into right and left branches inside the liver.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>On a venogram, the big trunk that enters the liver and splits into right and left branches is the portal vein, and the large vein climbing vertically into it from below is the SMV. The hepatic veins are different: they leave the top of the liver into the IVC, which lies behind the liver and does not branch inside it. Small veins such as the right gastric vein drain straight into the portal vein. In portal hypertension, the veins around the umbilicus swell (caput medusae), and a portal venogram maps the system.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G012-54.jpg\" alt=\"Portal vein formed behind the pancreas by the splenic vein and SMV\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G012-54c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Portal vein formed behind the pancreas by the splenic vein and SMV\"></div></div><figcaption>Portal vein formed behind the pancreas by the splenic vein and SMV <small>· 2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen III, slide 54</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Liver segment: central portal triad; hepatic veins at edge → IVC; caudate drains straight to IVC</li>\n<li>Gallbladder: pear sac in fossa; fundus = tip, body = main sac, neck → cystic duct</li>\n<li>Ultrasound: bile black; stone bright with black shadow</li>\n<li>Pancreas: head in C-loop; uncinate behind SMV; tail at splenic hilum</li>\n<li>Pancreatic body: behind stomach, in front of splenic vein and aorta</li>\n<li>MRCP: fluid white; CBD runs down; pancreatic duct comes from the left</li>\n<li>Major papilla + sphincter of Oddi: lowest CBD in duodenal wall; minor papilla higher</li>\n<li>SMV (vertical) + splenic vein (horizontal) → portal vein behind pancreatic neck</li>\n<li>Portal vein → porta hepatis → right + left branches; hepatic veins → IVC</li>\n<li>Portal hypertension → caput medusae; inferior mesenteric vein → splenic vein</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Abdominal CT and MRI","sn":4,"pn":"4.2"},{"l":"ANAT-G7","h":"The aorta and its branches by level","p":"<h4>Ways to see the arteries</h4>\n<p>Computed tomography (CT) angiography uses intravenous contrast timed to fill the arteries. A maximum intensity projection (MIP) stacks the slices so the vessels look like an angiogram. Catheter angiography with digital subtraction angiography (DSA) takes one image before contrast and one after, then subtracts the first from the second so that only the vessels remain.</p>\n<h4>Branches from top to bottom</h4>\n<p>The abdominal aorta runs down in front of the vertebral bodies, slightly left of the midline. Levels here are vertebrae: T for thoracic, L for lumbar. Each branch is recognized by three things: its level, whether it is single or paired, and which way it leaves the aorta.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>At T12, just below the diaphragm, the small paired <b>inferior phrenic</b> arteries run up to the diaphragm, and the single <b>celiac trunk</b> leaves the front as a short trunk pointing forward.</li>\n<li>At L1, about 1 cm lower, the single <b>superior mesenteric artery (SMA)</b> leaves the front and runs down in front of the aorta.</li>\n<li>At L1–L2 the large paired <b>renal arteries</b> leave the sides and run sideways to each kidney; accessory renal arteries are common.</li>\n<li>At L2 the thin paired gonadal arteries leave the front and run down to the gonads.</li>\n<li>At L3 the smaller single <b>inferior mesenteric artery (IMA)</b> leaves the front-left and runs down and to the left.</li>\n<li>From L1 to L4, four pairs of small <b>lumbar arteries</b> leave the back of the aorta to supply the posterior body wall.</li>\n<li>At <b>L4</b>, near the level of the umbilicus and the iliac crests, the aorta ends by splitting into the right and left <b>common iliac arteries</b>, while the thin median sacral artery continues down the midline.</li>\n</ol>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G012-4.jpg\" alt=\"Coronal MIP of the abdominal aorta: celiac branches above, renal arteries to the sides\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G012-4c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Coronal MIP of the abdominal aorta: celiac branches above, renal arteries to the sides\"></div></div><figcaption>Coronal MIP of the abdominal aorta: celiac branches above, renal arteries to the sides <small>· 2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen III, slide 4</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Below the bifurcation</h4>\n<p>Each common iliac artery divides in front of the sacroiliac joint. The <b>internal iliac</b> artery runs medially into the pelvis, and the <b>external iliac</b> artery runs laterally toward the leg. The external iliac continues as the common femoral artery, which splits into the superficial femoral artery and the profunda femoris. So on an aortogram, the two big vessels that begin where the aorta ends are the common iliacs, and the internal and external iliacs begin lower, from them.</p>\n<h4>Celiac trunk or SMA on a side view</h4>\n<p>On a sagittal CT or a lateral aortogram, the front of the aorta gives two branches close together. The upper one, short and pointing forward just below the diaphragm at T12, is the <b>celiac trunk</b>. It supplies the foregut organs and, through its largest branch, the splenic artery, the spleen. The lower one, about 1 cm below at L1, running downward in front of the aorta, is the <b>SMA</b>. It supplies the midgut. The IMA arises much lower, at L3. The common hepatic artery is a branch of the celiac trunk, not of the aorta. The <b>greater splanchnic nerves</b>, from the fifth to ninth thoracic segments (T5–T9), end in the celiac ganglia, which surround the celiac trunk.</p>\n<p>The <b>left renal vein</b> and the third part of the duodenum pass between the SMA and the aorta. The left renal vein runs from the left kidney across the front of the aorta, just below the SMA, to the inferior vena cava. So on a sagittal view it is the small round vessel caught in the angle between the SMA in front and the aorta behind. The other veins nearby sit elsewhere. The splenic vein passes in front of the SMA, behind the pancreas. The portal vein lies higher, behind the neck of the pancreas. The inferior mesenteric vein runs up on the left into the splenic vein, and the left gonadal vein drains into the left renal vein.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G012-9.jpg\" alt=\"Sagittal CT: the arrow marks the celiac trunk leaving the front of the aorta\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G012-9c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Sagittal CT: the arrow marks the celiac trunk leaving the front of the aorta\"></div></div><figcaption>Sagittal CT: the arrow marks the celiac trunk leaving the front of the aorta <small>· 2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen III, slide 9</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>CT angiography + MIP looks like an angiogram; DSA subtracts the pre-contrast image</li>\n<li>T12: celiac trunk (single, front); inferior phrenics (paired, run up)</li>\n<li>L1: SMA, about 1 cm below celiac, runs down in front of aorta</li>\n<li>L1–L2: renal arteries, large, paired, sideways; accessory renals common</li>\n<li>L2: gonadal (thin, front); L3: IMA (single, front-left)</li>\n<li>Lumbar arteries: 4 pairs, small, from the back, to posterior body wall</li>\n<li>Left renal vein: in the angle between SMA (front) and aorta (behind); splenic vein in front of SMA</li>\n<li>L4 (umbilicus): bifurcation → common iliacs; median sacral continues in midline</li>\n<li>Common iliac → internal (medial, pelvis) + external (lateral, leg) at sacroiliac joint</li>\n<li>External iliac → common femoral → superficial femoral + profunda femoris</li>\n<li>Side view: upper short forward branch = celiac (→ splenic → spleen); lower = SMA (midgut)</li>\n<li>Greater splanchnic nerves (T5–T9) → celiac ganglia around the celiac trunk</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Abdominal arteries on angiograms","sn":5,"pn":"5.1"},{"l":"ANAT-G7","h":"Celiac trunk branches","p":"<p>The celiac trunk leaves the front of the aorta at the twelfth thoracic vertebra (T12), just below the diaphragm. Most people (86%) have the usual three branches; 12% have only two main branches, and 2% have no celiac trunk at all.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G012-10.jpg\" alt=\"Celiac trunk variants: three branches in 86%, two in 12%, none in 2%\"></div><figcaption>Celiac trunk variants: three branches in 86%, two in 12%, none in 2% <small>· 2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen III, slide 10</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The three branches on an angiogram</h4>\n<p>With the catheter tip in the short celiac trunk, look at which way each branch runs. The <b>left gastric artery</b> is the smallest. It runs up and to the left toward the esophagus and supplies the lesser curvature and the lower esophagus. The <b>splenic artery</b> is the largest, and it is wavy (tortuous). It runs to the patient's left (image right) along the top of the pancreas to the spleen. On the way it gives small pancreatic branches, the short gastric arteries to the fundus, and the left gastro-omental artery to the greater curvature. The <b>common hepatic artery</b> runs to the patient's right (image left), toward the liver. A celiac injection fills only these branches; the superior mesenteric artery is a separate aortic branch lower down and stays empty.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G012-11.jpg\" alt=\"The usual celiac trunk: left gastric, common hepatic and splenic arteries and their branches\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G012-11c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: The usual celiac trunk: left gastric, common hepatic and splenic arteries and their branches\"></div></div><figcaption>The usual celiac trunk: left gastric, common hepatic and splenic arteries and their branches <small>· 2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen III, slide 11</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Following the common hepatic artery</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>The common hepatic artery gives off the <b>gastroduodenal artery (GDA)</b>.</li>\n<li>The GDA runs straight down behind the first part of the duodenum, on the patient's right. It gives the superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries, then turns left along the greater curvature as the <b>right gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery</b>.</li>\n<li>Past the GDA, the common hepatic continues as the <b>proper hepatic artery</b>. The right gastric artery, to the lesser curvature, usually leaves the proper hepatic.</li>\n<li>Near the liver the proper hepatic divides into the <b>right hepatic artery</b>, which supplies the right lobe and gives the cystic artery, and the <b>left hepatic artery</b>, which supplies the left lobe. A middle hepatic artery, when present, supplies segment 4.</li>\n</ol>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G012-21.jpg\" alt=\"Celiac MIP: the arrow marks the gastroduodenal artery running down from the common hepatic\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G012-21c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Celiac MIP: the arrow marks the gastroduodenal artery running down from the common hepatic\"></div></div><figcaption>Celiac MIP: the arrow marks the gastroduodenal artery running down from the common hepatic <small>· 2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen III, slide 21</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Territories</h4>\n<p>Each branch has its own territory, so a blocked branch starves a predictable region. Blocking the right hepatic artery cuts the arterial supply of the right lobe of the liver. The lesser curvature is supplied by an arcade of the right and left gastric arteries. The fundus depends on the short gastric arteries from the splenic artery, and the first part of the duodenum on the GDA and its branches. Around the head of the pancreas, the superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries from the celiac side meet the inferior pancreaticoduodenal arteries from the superior mesenteric artery.</p>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>Celiac trunk: T12; 3 branches 86%, 2 branches 12%, none 2%</li>\n<li>Left gastric: smallest, runs up-left to esophagus; lesser curvature</li>\n<li>Splenic: largest, tortuous, to patient's left along pancreas</li>\n<li>Splenic branches: pancreatic, short gastrics (fundus), left gastro-omental</li>\n<li>Common hepatic: to patient's right → GDA + proper hepatic; celiac injection: SMA stays empty</li>\n<li>GDA: down behind 1st part of duodenum → superior pancreaticoduodenals → right gastro-omental</li>\n<li>Proper hepatic → right hepatic (right lobe, cystic artery) + left hepatic; middle → segment 4</li>\n<li>Right hepatic blocked → right lobe ischemia</li>\n<li>Superior (celiac) + inferior (superior mesenteric) pancreaticoduodenals meet at pancreatic head</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Abdominal arteries on angiograms","sn":5,"pn":"5.2"},{"l":"ANAT-G7","h":"Mesenteric arteries and colon collaterals","p":"<h4>Superior mesenteric artery: branches in order</h4>\n<p>The superior mesenteric artery (SMA) leaves the aorta at the first lumbar vertebra and runs down into the root of the mesentery. On an SMA angiogram, take its branches from top to bottom.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery is the first, a small branch near the origin.</li>\n<li>The <b>middle colic</b> artery is the first large branch on the right, running up to the transverse colon.</li>\n<li>Many jejunal and ileal branches leave the left side and are joined by arcades; the first jejunal branch leaves high up.</li>\n<li>The <b>right colic</b> artery leaves the right side and runs to the patient's right (image left), to the ascending colon.</li>\n<li>The <b>ileocolic</b> artery is the lowest branch on the right, heading for the cecum in the right lower quadrant. It supplies the terminal ileum, cecum and appendix.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The <b>appendicular artery</b> is a branch of the ileocolic artery and is tied during an appendectomy. If it bleeds after surgery, tying the ileocolic artery controls the bleeding. An SMA injection fills only SMA branches, because the inferior mesenteric artery is a separate aortic branch. Veins do not fill on an arterial injection.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G012-28.jpg\" alt=\"SMA branches: middle colic, right colic, ileocolic, jejunal and ileal arteries\"></div><figcaption>SMA branches: middle colic, right colic, ileocolic, jejunal and ileal arteries <small>· 2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen III, slide 28</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Inferior mesenteric artery: branches in order</h4>\n<p>The inferior mesenteric artery (IMA) leaves the front-left of the aorta at the third lumbar vertebra. Its first branch is the <b>left colic</b> artery, which runs up and to the patient's left (image right) to the <b>descending colon</b>. Next come the <b>sigmoid arteries</b>, which run down and to the patient's left (image right) into the left lower quadrant, to the sigmoid colon. The IMA then continues down into the pelvis as the superior rectal artery, to the rectum. On any of these angiograms the catheter is a smooth, straight tube of even width coming up from the femoral artery in the groin; it is not a branch.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G012-40.jpg\" alt=\"IMA branches: left colic, sigmoid and superior rectal arteries\"></div><figcaption>IMA branches: left colic, sigmoid and superior rectal arteries <small>· 2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen III, slide 40</small></figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Collaterals along the colon</h4>\n<p>The colic arteries are linked, so the colon has backup routes. The <b>marginal artery of Drummond</b> runs close along the mesenteric edge of the colon. It links all the colic arteries of the SMA and IMA, and they feed it. On an IMA angiogram it is the vessel running along the colon edge, parallel to the bowel, near the splenic flexure; the left colic artery, by contrast, runs from the IMA toward the colon. The <b>arc of Riolan</b> is a more central loop, nearer the root of the mesentery, joining the middle colic (SMA) to the left colic (IMA). The arc of Bühler is a rare link between the celiac trunk and the SMA, near the pancreas.</p>\n<p>The marginal artery is weakest where the superior and inferior mesenteric territories meet. This weak link, the watershed, is at the splenic flexure, so ischemia of the colon shows up there first.</p>\n<figure class=\"sf\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../sl/G012-45.jpg\" alt=\"Marginal artery along the colon edge; arc of Riolan nearer the mesentery root\"></div><div class=\"cuw\"><span class=\"cul\">Close-up of the picture</span><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" class=\"cuimg\" src=\"../sl/G012-45c.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up: Marginal artery along the colon edge; arc of Riolan nearer the mesentery root\"></div></div><figcaption>Marginal artery along the colon edge; arc of Riolan nearer the mesentery root <small>· 2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen III, slide 45</small></figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul>\n<li>SMA order: inferior pancreaticoduodenal → middle colic → jejunal/ileal (left) → right colic → ileocolic</li>\n<li>Middle colic: first large right branch, to transverse colon; right colic: to ascending colon</li>\n<li>Ileocolic: lowest right branch → terminal ileum, cecum, appendix</li>\n<li>Appendicular artery from ileocolic → appendectomy bleed: tie ileocolic</li>\n<li>SMA injection: no IMA filling, no veins</li>\n<li>IMA (L3): left colic (first, up-left, descending colon) → sigmoid → superior rectal</li>\n<li>Sigmoid arteries: down and to patient's left into left lower quadrant; catheter = straight tube from groin</li>\n<li>Marginal artery of Drummond: along colon edge, links all colic arteries</li>\n<li>Arc of Riolan: central, middle colic ↔ left colic; arc of Bühler: celiac ↔ SMA</li>\n<li>Watershed: splenic flexure</li>\n</ul></div>","s":"Abdominal arteries on angiograms","sn":5,"pn":"5.3"},{"l":"ANAT-Kahoot","sn":null,"s":"","pn":"K","h":"Faculty Kahoot: GI development, in the order of the game","p":""}]};